window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old sprinter with anterior thigh tightness is tested prone. As the examiner passively flexes the right knee toward the buttock, the right hip rises into flexion and the pelvis tilts anteriorly before the heel reaches the buttock. Which muscle shortening does this Ely test finding most directly indicate?", "options": {"A": "Shortened iliopsoas crossing the lumbar spine and the hip on the tested side", "B": "Shortened biceps femoris crossing the hip and knee on the posterior thigh", "C": "Shortened rectus femoris crossing both the hip and the knee on the tested side", "D": "Shortened vastus intermedius lying deep to the rectus femoris on the tested side"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shortened rectus femoris crossing both the hip and the knee on the tested side): In the Ely test, knee flexion pulls on the rectus femoris, the only quadriceps head that crosses the hip, so shortening lifts the hip into flexion and tilts the pelvis. The vasti cross only the knee and cannot affect the hip. The iliopsoas is assessed with the Thomas test. The hamstrings lie on the posterior thigh and would limit knee extension, not flexion."}, {"id": 2, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractic student performs a side plank with the body in a straight line, supported on the elbow and feet. The instructor holds a hand over the lateral trunk on the supported side and asks which muscle is primarily challenged to keep the pelvis from sagging toward the floor. Which muscle is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "The iliopsoas, with assistance from the rectus femoris on the unsupported side", "B": "The quadratus lumborum, with assistance from the lateral abdominal obliques", "C": "The erector spinae at the thoracolumbar junction, with assistance from the latissimus dorsi", "D": "The rectus abdominis, with assistance from the transversus abdominis on the opposite side"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The quadratus lumborum, with assistance from the lateral abdominal obliques): The side plank resists frontal plane sag of the pelvis, which mainly challenges the quadratus lumborum and the lateral abdominal obliques on the supported side. The rectus abdominis acts in the sagittal plane and contributes little. The erector spinae are mainly extensors. The hip flexors are not the main limiters of lateral sag."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "Ten minutes after a heel lock and stirrup ankle taping before practice, an athlete reports tingling in the toes, and the toes look dusky and feel cool. The trainer is deciding how to respond to these findings. What should be done?", "options": {"A": "Apply an ice pack over the tape to calm the paresthesia", "B": "Loosen only the top anchor and keep the rest of the tape on", "C": "Remove the tape immediately and recheck the toes", "D": "Elevate the foot for ten minutes with the tape left in place"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the tape immediately and recheck the toes): Tingling with dusky, cool toes signals circulatory compromise or nerve compression from the tape, and the tape should come off at once and circulation and sensation be reassessed. Waiting under elevation, loosening one strip or icing over the tape all leave the constriction in place. Capillary refill and sensation should be rechecked after any ankle taping job."}, {"id": 4, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 70-year-old man has absent right arm swing, a resting tremor in the right hand, and short steps that speed up when he walks. The arm hangs by his side rather than held flexed, and he turns in one piece. Which diagnosis best fits the gait?", "options": {"A": "Cerebellar ataxic gait with a wide base and irregular, lurching steps", "B": "Hemiplegic gait after a left hemisphere stroke with circumduction of the right leg", "C": "Parkinsonian gait, with rigidity and reduced arm swing on the right", "D": "Frontal magnetic gait with a wide base and feet that stick to the floor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Parkinsonian gait, with rigidity and reduced arm swing on the right): Resting tremor, short steps that speed up, loss of arm swing and turning in one piece are the classic parkinsonian picture. A hemiplegic pattern holds the arm flexed and swings the stiff leg outward, which this man does not show. Ataxic gait is wide and irregular rather than short and hurried, and frontal magnetic gait has a wide base and sticking feet without a resting tremor."}, {"id": 5, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with weak hip extensors performs a double-leg bridge in hooklying while the therapist palpates the buttocks and posterior thigh during the lift. The clinician explains that one muscle acts as the primary hip extensor driving the hips upward, with others assisting. Which muscle is the prime mover in this bridging movement?", "options": {"A": "The gluteus maximus, working with the hamstrings as secondary assistants", "B": "The hamstrings, working with the gluteus maximus as secondary assistants", "C": "The adductor magnus, working with the gluteus medius as secondary assistants", "D": "The erector spinae, working with the multifidus as secondary assistants"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The gluteus maximus, working with the hamstrings as secondary assistants): In a bridge the hips rise by hip extension against gravity, and the gluteus maximus is the main driver of that motion. The hamstrings assist but are shortened at the knee in hooklying, so they cannot lead. The adductor magnus has an extensor portion yet contributes less force, and the erector spinae mainly hold the trunk steady. These muscles are active in the bridge, which is why they tempt, but none is the prime mover."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A college athlete is tested 24 hours after a concussion. Her Balance Error Scoring System total is 22 errors, compared with a preseason baseline of 11. Her symptoms have resolved at rest. What is the best decision about return to play?", "options": {"A": "Clear her for gradual return after a repeat test one week later shows improvement", "B": "Clear her for light drills, because a score under 30 is normal in athletes", "C": "Clear her for full contact, because her symptoms at rest have resolved", "D": "Withhold her from play, because balance remains worse than her baseline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold her from play, because balance remains worse than her baseline): Her balance score is double her own preseason baseline, which shows persistent postural control impairment even though symptoms have settled at rest. Return to play needs symptoms, balance and graded exertion all back to baseline. A population cutoff does not replace her own baseline, and resting symptom relief alone does not clear her. Scheduling a retest for later still leaves a current deficit unaddressed."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old man has chronic low back pain and a measured structural left leg length discrepancy of one half inch, with equal findings on repeated tape measurements. He has lived with the difference for decades. How should a heel lift be started for him?", "options": {"A": "Overcorrect with three quarters of an inch to unload the compensating segments", "B": "Correct the full half inch at once so the pelvis levels immediately and the back unloads", "C": "Place the half inch lift in the shoe on the longer limb to level the pelvis", "D": "Begin with a partial correction of about one quarter inch, then increase gradually"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin with a partial correction of about one quarter inch, then increase gradually): Patients who have adapted to a structural difference for years tolerate a partial correction better, so a lift of about one quarter inch is added and raised gradually. Full immediate correction can provoke new back and hip pain. The lift always goes under the short side, which here is the left. Overcorrecting beyond the measured difference creates a new asymmetry."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A new clinic assistant proposes setting every patient's first UVB exposure from a Fitzpatrick skin type chart. A type I patient and a type IV patient both receive the chart dose, and the type I patient burns. The supervisor wants a safer method. Which approach should set each patient's initial dose?", "options": {"A": "A dose estimated from the patient's body weight and height", "B": "A chart dose adjusted by 25 percent for each lighter skin type", "C": "A timed MED test performed on each individual patient before treatment", "D": "The average dose from the last ten patients with similar skin types"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A timed MED test performed on each individual patient before treatment): Skin type predicts a trend, but individual sensitivity varies with pigmentation, medications and prior sun exposure. A timed MED test measures the patient's own threshold, and every later dose is calculated from it. Chart adjustments, averages from other patients and body size estimates all substitute a guess for a measurement. The MED test is the safest way to start."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "Four patients are referred to a clinic for possible mechanical lumbar traction. The clinician reviews each history and examination and must select the patient for whom traction is both appropriate and safe to begin this week. Which patient is the best candidate for lumbar traction?", "options": {"A": "A 38-year-old with leg pain, positive straight leg raise, and a disc protrusion on imaging", "B": "A 30-year-old at 22 weeks of pregnancy with low back and pelvic girdle pain", "C": "A 66-year-old with severe osteoporosis and a vertebral compression fracture found last month", "D": "A 59-year-old with a pulsatile abdominal mass and constant low back pain at rest"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A 38-year-old with leg pain, positive straight leg raise, and a disc protrusion on imaging): A radicular presentation with a positive straight leg raise and a posterolateral protrusion is the classic indication for lumbar traction. Severe osteoporosis with a recent compression fracture is a contraindication because of fracture risk. Pregnancy is a contraindication. A pulsatile abdominal mass with constant rest pain suggests an aortic aneurysm and requires medical referral rather than traction."}, {"id": 10, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 36-year-old had dry needling of the upper trapezius and rhomboids the day before. He calls to say that since last night he has sharp pleuritic chest pain on the same side and feels short of breath at rest. What advice is best?", "options": {"A": "Rest the shoulder and monitor symptoms for two days, since delayed needling soreness is expected", "B": "Seek emergency evaluation today for a possible pneumothorax on that side", "C": "Reassess in clinic tomorrow and switch to superficial needling with a shorter needle", "D": "Use a home pulse oximeter and call back if the reading drops below ninety percent"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seek emergency evaluation today for a possible pneumothorax on that side): Pleuritic chest pain with dyspnea at rest after needling near the thorax suggests pneumothorax, which needs urgent imaging and emergency evaluation. Ordinary soreness is local and does not cause breathlessness at rest. Waiting two days, returning for gentler needling, or relying on home oximetry all delay care, and oxygen saturation can look normal early in a pneumothorax."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 23-year-old basketball player has rolled the same ankle five times in 18 months. The anterior drawer is positive, he feels it give way on cutting, and single leg balance with eyes closed is poor. Which program best targets his deficits?", "options": {"A": "Resistance band peroneal strengthening progressed weekly, with static two-leg balance on firm ground only", "B": "Referral for lateral ligament reconstruction first, with rehabilitation deferred until after healing", "C": "Early return to cutting drills in ankle taping, with gradual progression of running volume and speed", "D": "Progressive balance training on unstable surfaces with peroneal strengthening and sport specific agility drills"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive balance training on unstable surfaces with peroneal strengthening and sport specific agility drills): Recurrent sprains with a positive drawer and poor eyes-closed balance reflect mechanical laxity plus lost proprioception and weak dynamic stabilizers. Balance work on unstable surfaces, peroneal strengthening and sport-specific agility drills retrain protective reflexes and cutting control. Strengthening on firm ground with two-leg balance omits the sensorimotor challenge this athlete lacks. Early surgery is reserved for failed conservative care, and taped cutting drills do not correct the deficit."}, {"id": 12, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In supine, a 36-year-old swimmer's right acromion rests clearly above the table surface while the left rests near flat. The clinician is judging muscle length on both sides. Which muscle's shortness does this finding most directly suggest, and which plan addresses it?", "options": {"A": "Shortened upper trapezius, treated by stretching it and strengthening the levator scapulae", "B": "Shortened subscapularis, treated by stretching it and strengthening the infraspinatus", "C": "Shortened pectoralis minor, treated by stretching it and strengthening scapular retractors", "D": "Shortened latissimus dorsi, treated by stretching it and strengthening the pectoralis major"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shortened pectoralis minor, treated by stretching it and strengthening scapular retractors): A shoulder that rides forward and up off the table in supine points to a short pectoralis minor pulling the coracoid forward and down. Stretching it and strengthening the scapular retractors restores position. The other muscles named do not tilt the scapula forward in this way."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 44-year-old man is two days after open appendectomy through a right lower quadrant incision covered by a sterile dressing. He has incisional pain and requests TENS. The surgeon has approved electrical stimulation. The dressing must stay in place. Which electrode placement is most appropriate for conventional TENS in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Electrodes directly over the dressing, using extra gel to improve contact through the gauze.", "B": "Electrodes on the right upper arm, to use a distant site for heterosegmental pain relief.", "C": "Electrodes on intact skin bordering the dressing, within the same dermatomes as the incision.", "D": "Electrodes on intact skin bordering the dressing, placed two segments above the incision level."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Electrodes on intact skin bordering the dressing, within the same dermatomes as the incision.): Conventional TENS works best when pads sit on intact skin in the same dermatomes as the pain, so A-beta input reaches the segments carrying the incisional pain. Bordering the dressing meets that goal without disturbing the wound. Pads over gauze lose conduction and break the sterile field, and distant arm placement suits low rate acupuncture like TENS instead. Bordering pads set at the wrong segmental level miss the spinal segments that carry the pain."}, {"id": 14, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old electrician has right leg pain radiating along the L5 dermatome from a lumbar disc protrusion. A conventional TENS unit with two channels is available, and the clinician wants electrode placement consistent with segmental pain control. Which placement is most appropriate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Paraspinal electrodes at the L4 to S1 root levels, plus a pair along the dermatome.", "B": "Electrodes over the thoracic and sacral spine to cover every cord level feeding the leg.", "C": "Electrodes over the common peroneal and tibial nerves at the knee and ankle only.", "D": "Electrodes over the L5 dermatome with a second pair across the opposite lumbar paraspinals."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Paraspinal electrodes at the L4 to S1 root levels, plus a pair along the dermatome.): Segmental placement puts electrodes over the root levels supplying the painful area, here the paraspinals at L4 to S1 plus a pair along the dermatome, so A-beta input gates nociception at the involved segment. Nerve-trunk placement at the knee and ankle is a real technique but does not address the root level. Opposite-side paraspinal placement lies off the affected segment. Spanning thoracic to sacral levels dilutes input across unrelated segments."}, {"id": 15, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old programmer reports neck tension and says her shoulders are relaxed, while a surface electromyographic unit over the upper trapezius shows high resting activity during sitting. The therapist is deciding how to use the device in neuromuscular re-education for this problem. What is the best use of the biofeedback?", "options": {"A": "Train endurance by rewarding a steady signal while she carries a loaded bag", "B": "Train relaxation by rewarding reduction of the resting signal during sitting tasks", "C": "Train joint range by cueing her to reach the signal peak at end range", "D": "Train strengthening by rewarding increases in the signal during sustained shrugging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Train relaxation by rewarding reduction of the resting signal during sitting tasks): High resting upper trapezius activity marks an overactive muscle, so the best use is downtraining, rewarding a lower resting signal so she learns to release tension during sitting. Uptraining with shrugs suits a weak or poorly recruited muscle, which is not her problem. A loaded carry adds demand to a muscle already working too much. Cueing a signal peak at end range trains recruitment at the limits of motion, a different goal."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old cashier has arm heaviness and forearm paresthesia when working overhead. A clinician reviews the anatomy of the interscalene triangle, a common site of neurovascular compression in thoracic outlet syndrome. Which structures pass through the interscalene triangle between the anterior and middle scalene muscles?", "options": {"A": "The subclavian artery and vein together, with the brachial plexus passing in front of them", "B": "The axillary artery with the cords of the brachial plexus beneath the pectoralis minor", "C": "The brachial plexus trunks together with the subclavian vein, but not the subclavian artery", "D": "The brachial plexus trunks together with the subclavian artery, but not the subclavian vein"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The brachial plexus trunks together with the subclavian artery, but not the subclavian vein): The brachial plexus trunks and the subclavian artery pass between the anterior and middle scalenes. The subclavian vein runs in front of the anterior scalene, so it is not in the interscalene gap. The axillary vessels and cords belong to the retropectoral space under the pectoralis minor."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 54-year-old bookkeeper with type 2 diabetes reports three months of shoulder pain that wakes her at night and hurts at rest. Her active and passive motion in every plane is steadily getting worse, and the capsule feels tight at end range. Which stage of adhesive capsulitis best fits this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Freezing stage, with escalating pain, night symptoms, and worsening restriction of shoulder motion", "B": "Pre-adhesive stage, where pain occurs only at end range while passive motion is fully preserved", "C": "Thawing stage, where motion gradually returns as the capsular restriction slowly resolves over months", "D": "Frozen stage, where pain settles but stiffness persists without further progressive loss of motion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Freezing stage, with escalating pain, night symptoms, and worsening restriction of shoulder motion): The freezing stage is the painful, progressive stage in which pain is present at rest and at night while motion is steadily lost. The frozen stage has less pain but a stable, firm restriction. The thawing stage is marked by motion returning, and a pre-adhesive picture would still show full passive motion, which this patient has lost."}, {"id": 18, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old assembly line worker with subacute low back pain is issued a flexible lumbosacral corset by the clinic. The patient asks the therapist how the garment is thought to help. Which mechanism is described in standard orthotics references as a primary effect of a flexible lumbar corset?", "options": {"A": "It raises intra-abdominal pressure and gives a tactile reminder about trunk posture and motion", "B": "It stretches the thoracolumbar fascia so the erector spinae lengthen over several weeks", "C": "It strengthens the trunk extensors because they work against the stiff fabric stays", "D": "It holds every lumbar segment in a fixed position so intersegmental motion stops"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It raises intra-abdominal pressure and gives a tactile reminder about trunk posture and motion): A flexible corset compresses the abdomen to raise intra-abdominal pressure and provides skin contact that cues the wearer about posture and gross motion. It does not hold individual lumbar segments rigid, and measured segmental motion is only modestly reduced. It does not lengthen the erector spinae or fascia. It also does not strengthen the trunk, and prolonged use can contribute to deconditioning."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with recurrent low back pain lies hooklying and is learning the abdominal drawing-in maneuver to activate the transversus abdominis. The therapist wants the patient to avoid any visible pelvic movement or rib depression during the contraction and to keep the effort low. Which verbal cue best matches the standard instruction for this maneuver?", "options": {"A": "Tighten all of the abdominal muscles together as if bracing for a punch to the stomach", "B": "Gently draw the lower abdomen up and in toward the spine without moving the pelvis", "C": "Flatten the lower back firmly into the table by tilting the pelvis backward", "D": "Pull the navel as hard as you can toward the spine and hold your breath"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gently draw the lower abdomen up and in toward the spine without moving the pelvis): The drawing-in maneuver is a low effort, gentle inward and upward draw of the lower abdomen that selectively recruits the transversus abdominis while the pelvis and spine stay still. Maximal effort and breath holding recruit the superficial muscles and rib cage instead. A brace is a global co-contraction, which is a different strategy. A posterior pelvic tilt is a movement of the pelvis, not an isolated deep contraction."}, {"id": 20, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "Researchers compare force production in a small hand muscle and the large quadriceps. In the hand muscle, nearly all motor units are already recruited by about half of maximal force, while the quadriceps keeps recruiting units to much higher force. Which conclusion about increasing force above 60 percent of maximum is best?", "options": {"A": "Neither muscle increases force above 60 percent because motor unit firing rates have reached their ceiling", "B": "Both muscles rely only on recruitment, since rate coding raises force just at very low loads", "C": "The hand muscle relies mainly on rate coding, while the quadriceps still uses recruitment", "D": "The quadriceps relies mainly on rate coding, while the hand muscle still adds new motor units"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The hand muscle relies mainly on rate coding, while the quadriceps still uses recruitment): Small muscles such as the hand muscles finish recruiting motor units at roughly half of maximal force, so higher forces come mainly from faster firing, or rate coding. Large muscles like the quadriceps continue recruiting new units to much higher force levels. Rate coding is not limited to low loads, the muscles are not reversed, and force certainly rises above 60 percent."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 24-year-old recreational soccer player is 6 weeks out from anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction and has a quadriceps strength deficit of 40 percent. The clinician starts electrical muscle stimulation to the quadriceps during isometric exercise. Which set of parameters best fits an initial strengthening protocol using Russian stimulation?", "options": {"A": "10 second contraction, 50 second rest, 3 second ramp, intensity at the sensory threshold", "B": "10 second contraction, 50 second rest, 3 second ramp, highest tolerable intensity", "C": "2 second contraction, 2 second rest, no ramp, intensity at the sensory threshold", "D": "60 second contraction, 10 second rest, 3 second ramp, highest tolerable intensity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (10 second contraction, 50 second rest, 3 second ramp, highest tolerable intensity): Strengthening with Russian stimulation uses about 10 seconds of contraction followed by 50 seconds of rest, a 1 to 5 ratio, with a ramp of about 3 seconds. Intensity is raised to the highest tolerable level because torque gains relate to the force of the stimulated contraction. A 60 second contraction with 10 seconds rest would fatigue the muscle quickly. Sensory-level intensity does not produce a training effect."}, {"id": 22, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 24-year-old student with a documented history of fainting at blood draws has a painful trigger point in the upper trapezius that limits neck rotation. She states she is terrified of needles. Her neck is otherwise normal. Which treatment is the best initial choice?", "options": {"A": "High-velocity cervical thrust manipulation with the patient positioned supine on the table", "B": "Dry needling with the patient supine after explaining that the needle is very fine", "C": "Manual ischemic compression with the patient positioned supine", "D": "Dry needling with the patient seated to allow observation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Manual ischemic compression with the patient positioned supine): Ischemic compression treats the trigger point without any needle exposure, and lying supine protects a patient with a fainting history. Cervical thrust manipulation is a real technique but the neck is otherwise normal and the problem is a soft tissue point. Dry needling after reassurance, even when supine, still exposes a needle phobic patient to the trigger for syncope. Seated needling adds fall risk, so observation does not make it safer than a needle free option."}, {"id": 23, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old marathon runner has lateral knee pain after five miles and a positive Ober test with the knee extended. Hip abduction strength is graded 4 out of 5 on the painful side. The clinician designs a rehabilitation plan targeting the likely contributing impairments. Which plan fits best?", "options": {"A": "Stretch the tensor fasciae latae in hip adduction and extension, and strengthen the gluteus medius", "B": "Stretch the iliopsoas in half-kneeling, and strengthen the quadriceps with deep knee flexion squats", "C": "Stretch the gluteus medius and the hip adductors, and strengthen the tensor fasciae latae", "D": "Stretch the hamstrings in a seated forward fold, and strengthen the hip adductor group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stretch the tensor fasciae latae in hip adduction and extension, and strengthen the gluteus medius): A positive Ober test points to a tight tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band, which is lengthened with the hip adducted and extended. Weak hip abductors, especially the gluteus medius, let the thigh drift into adduction and load the lateral knee, so strengthening them completes the plan. Reversing the roles, targeting the hamstrings, or loading the knee with deep squats does not address these impairments."}, {"id": 24, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old smoker reports calf cramping after 200 yards that stops within a minute of standing still, without needing to sit or flex. Both pedal pulses are weak, the feet are cool with hair loss, and lumbar movements do not change symptoms. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for vascular assessment with ankle-brachial index testing first", "B": "Start a flexion-based stenosis program with stationary cycling and posterior pelvic tilts", "C": "Begin a McKenzie extension protocol and reassess the calf pain after one week", "D": "Prescribe lumbar traction and moist heat, then progress to walking on an incline"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for vascular assessment with ankle-brachial index testing first): Relief by standing still, weak pulses, cool skin, and hair loss point to peripheral arterial disease rather than spinal stenosis. Neurogenic claudication would ease with sitting or flexion and often vary with spinal posture. The clinician should arrange vascular testing. Spinal programs and modalities do not address arterial insufficiency."}, {"id": 25, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "In a controlled laboratory study, healthy volunteers receive an injection of naloxone, an opioid receptor antagonist, before a 30-minute session of low-rate TENS at 4 pps with visible muscle twitching. The investigators measure pressure pain threshold afterward. What result would the endorphin theory predict compared with a saline injection?", "options": {"A": "Loss of the visible muscle twitching, because naloxone blocks acetylcholine release at the motor endplate.", "B": "A smaller rise in pain threshold than the saline condition would be seen.", "C": "A larger rise in pain threshold, because naloxone sensitizes opioid receptors to stimulation-released endorphins.", "D": "An identical rise in pain threshold, because the analgesia is explained by gate control, not opioids."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A smaller rise in pain threshold than the saline condition would be seen.): Low-rate acupuncture-like TENS is thought to release endogenous opioids such as beta-endorphin, so occupying opioid receptors with naloxone blunts the rise in pain threshold. Gate control does explain high-rate TENS, but it would not be reversed by naloxone, which is why it fails to fit this low-rate protocol. Naloxone is an antagonist, so it cannot sensitize receptors or enhance the effect. It also has no action at the neuromuscular junction, so twitching continues."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "At the end of a symptom-limited treadmill test, a 62-year-old man reaches a peak heart rate of 142. He cools down by walking slowly, and one minute after stopping his heart rate is 133. The therapist reviews the recovery response. How should this heart rate recovery be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "Indeterminate, since recovery is judged only at five minutes after exercise ends", "B": "Abnormal, because recovery should reach resting heart rate within one minute of stopping", "C": "Normal, since a recovery drop of 9 beats shows the heart returned toward baseline", "D": "Abnormal, a 9-beat drop reflecting blunted parasympathetic reactivation and higher mortality risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Abnormal, a 9-beat drop reflecting blunted parasympathetic reactivation and higher mortality risk): Heart rate recovery is the fall from peak to one minute into recovery, and a drop of 12 beats or less is an abnormal response linked to reduced vagal reactivation and higher mortality. His 9-beat drop falls in that range. Returning fully to resting within a minute is not expected in anyone. Recovery at one minute is the standard point of assessment."}, {"id": 27, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 70-year-old man with moderate emphysema becomes short of breath climbing a flight of stairs in the clinic. His therapist coaches him to inhale through the nose and exhale slowly through lips held in a whistling position. What is the main physiologic benefit of this pursed-lip breathing technique during exertion?", "options": {"A": "It increases the inspiratory volume by strengthening the diaphragm during each effort", "B": "It raises the arterial carbon dioxide level so the drive to breathe is reduced", "C": "It speeds exhaled airflow so the lungs empty fully before the next breath", "D": "It creates back pressure that keeps small airways open and reduces air trapping"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It creates back pressure that keeps small airways open and reduces air trapping): In emphysema the small airways collapse during exhalation because elastic recoil is lost. Exhaling through pursed lips adds resistance at the mouth, which transmits back pressure into the airways and holds them open, so less air is trapped. This slows exhalation, which lowers respiratory rate and breathlessness. It does not strengthen the diaphragm or raise carbon dioxide."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 24 year old basketball player rolls an ankle six hours earlier and asks for a hot pack because heat feels better to him. The ankle is warm and visibly swollen. The therapist declines heat and explains the physiological reason. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Heat raises capillary permeability and blood flow, increasing swelling in fresh injury", "B": "Heat raises nerve conduction velocity, which amplifies pain signals from the injured ankle", "C": "Heat slows nerve conduction, which hides worsening injury from the athlete", "D": "Heat constricts local arterioles, which traps inflammatory fluid in the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heat raises capillary permeability and blood flow, increasing swelling in fresh injury): Heat dilates vessels and increases capillary permeability, so it feeds fluid into a fresh, already swollen injury. Faster nerve conduction is a real heat effect but it is not why heat is withheld here. Slowed conduction and arteriolar constriction are effects of cold, so they describe the wrong modality."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 42-year-old electrician with lateral epicondylalgia has pain with gripping but no night pain or swelling, and his symptoms have lasted ten weeks. He has finished relative rest and is ready for a loading program for the common extensor origin. Which exercise has the best support for this chronic tendinopathy?", "options": {"A": "Isometric wrist flexor strengthening with the elbow flexed to ninety degrees on a table", "B": "Rapid concentric wrist curls with maximum weight performed to fatigue every session", "C": "Static wrist extensor stretching held thirty seconds for ten repetitions as the main treatment", "D": "Slow eccentric wrist extension with a light weight or flexible bar, progressively loaded"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Slow eccentric wrist extension with a light weight or flexible bar, progressively loaded): Slow, progressively loaded eccentric wrist extension is the best supported exercise for chronic lateral epicondylar tendinopathy. Stretching alone does not load the tendon. Flexor work targets the wrong muscles, and maximal rapid curls to fatigue overload an irritable tendon."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 52-year-old electrician reports radiating left arm pain and tingling in the thumb and index finger. During the examination he rests his left hand on top of his head and says the arm pain eases within seconds. This shoulder abduction relief sign points toward which problem?", "options": {"A": "Cervical nerve root compression, usually involving the C5, C6 or C7 root", "B": "Rotator cuff impingement with subacromial bursitis during overhead elevation", "C": "Carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve compression beneath the flexor retinaculum", "D": "Thoracic outlet syndrome with costoclavicular compression of the subclavian vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cervical nerve root compression, usually involving the C5, C6 or C7 root): Placing the hand on the head slackens the lower cervical nerve roots and often relieves radicular arm pain, which supports root compression at C5 to C7. Thoracic outlet syndrome tends to worsen when the arm is elevated. Carpal tunnel syndrome would not change with shoulder position, and impingement pain usually increases with overhead elevation."}, {"id": 31, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old with C6 radiculopathy has arm pain of 7 of 10 that flares with small movements and lingers for an hour afterward, disturbing her sleep most nights. The therapist, early in the plan of care, plans neural mobilization. Which approach fits this irritable presentation?", "options": {"A": "Postponing neural work and traction until the biceps reflex returns to normal", "B": "Tensioner techniques with large oscillations near end range, repeated until the arm pain eases", "C": "Sustained end-range median nerve stretch for two minutes performed daily at home", "D": "Gentle sliders that glide the nerve without added tension, in short sessions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gentle sliders that glide the nerve without added tension, in short sessions): Pain of 7 of 10 that lingers an hour and disturbs sleep marks a highly irritable nerve root, so loading must stay low. Sliders move the nerve through its bed without adding tension and fit this stage. Tensioners and sustained end-range stretches are reserved for low irritability and can flare this patient. Reflex normalization is not a prerequisite for starting gentle neural mobilization."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 78-year-old woman reports one fall in the past year. Her Timed Up and Go takes 14 seconds without a device, and she holds a Romberg stance with eyes closed for 30 seconds without sway. Which interpretation and plan is most appropriate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "A TUG of 14 seconds is normal for her age, so advise general walking only", "B": "The score meets the 12 second screening cut-off, so start gait and balance training", "C": "The negative Romberg excludes a balance deficit, so no balance program is needed", "D": "The TUG cut-off for her age is 20 seconds, so monitor and retest in six months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The score meets the 12 second screening cut-off, so start gait and balance training): A Timed Up and Go of 12 seconds or more is a commonly used screening threshold for increased fall risk, and her fall in the past year adds concern. A static Romberg does not test dynamic balance or gait, so a normal result does not rule out a deficit. A much longer cut-off belongs to frailer groups, and calling the time normal for age overlooks the positive screen. Starting gait and balance training addresses the risk now."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old man received 8 minutes of stationary continuous ultrasound at 2.0 W/cm2 over the calf from a student. The next day he has localized bruising and soreness. The machine was working normally. Which mechanism is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Cavitation of dissolved gas in the muscle ruptured capillaries through violent bubble collapse", "B": "Reflected energy at the tibia periosteum overheated the bone surface and bruised the adjacent calf muscle", "C": "Standing waves from the stationary head caused blood cell stasis and endothelial injury", "D": "Air pockets in the coupling gel concentrated energy and ruptured superficial capillaries beneath the head"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Standing waves from the stationary head caused blood cell stasis and endothelial injury): A stationary sound head lets reflected waves combine into standing waves, whose pressure nodes stack red blood cells and injure vessel walls, producing bruising and soreness. Moving the head continuously prevents this. Cavitation needs far higher intensities or very low frequencies, periosteal heating would produce bone pain rather than calf bruising, and gel air pockets mainly reduce transmission instead of concentrating injury."}, {"id": 34, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 36-year-old competitive swimmer trains six days weekly in the pool and wants to know her aerobic capacity. A treadmill test returns a VO2 max noticeably lower than the value measured during tethered swimming. The clinician reviews training principles to explain the discrepancy between the two results. Which principle best accounts for the lower treadmill value?", "options": {"A": "Specificity, because adaptations are largest in the muscles and movement pattern trained", "B": "Individual variability, because genetic ceilings differ between athletes and between testing modes", "C": "Progressive overload, because the treadmill protocol exceeded her prior training load", "D": "Reversibility, because her pool training lost its effect during recent months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Specificity, because adaptations are largest in the muscles and movement pattern trained): Specificity states that adaptations are greatest in the muscles and movement pattern used in training, so a swimmer tested on a treadmill uses less trained muscle mass and records a lower VO2. She trains six days a week, so reversibility does not apply. Progressive overload concerns raising training stress over time, not test mode. Genetic variability differs between people and does not explain two modes in the same athlete."}, {"id": 35, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with right hip osteoarthritis leans her trunk over the right hip every time she loads the right leg, and the right stance time is short. She says the lean eases her pain. What does this lateral trunk lean mainly reduce at the right hip?", "options": {"A": "The plantarflexor demand for push-off during terminal stance", "B": "The quadriceps demand at the knee during the loading response", "C": "The hip extensor demand needed at initial contact", "D": "Abductor force and joint compression needed to level the pelvis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Abductor force and joint compression needed to level the pelvis): Moving the trunk over the painful hip shifts the body's center of mass toward the joint, which shortens the lever arm the weight has on the hip. The abductors then need less force to hold the pelvis level, and the compressive load on the arthritic joint falls. The lean does not unload the knee, ankle, or hip extensors."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old sprained his thumb collateral ligament nine days ago. Tissue at the injury is now filling with new capillaries and fibroblasts, and weak type III collagen is being laid down in a disorganized pattern to form granulation tissue. Which phase of tissue healing is this tissue in?", "options": {"A": "Remodeling phase, when collagen realigns along stress lines and type III is replaced by type I", "B": "Proliferative phase, when fibroblasts build granulation tissue and early scar", "C": "Inflammatory phase, when vasodilation, edema, and phagocytosis clear debris from the injury site", "D": "Coagulation phase, when a platelet plug and fibrin clot form within minutes of injury"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Proliferative phase, when fibroblasts build granulation tissue and early scar): New capillaries, fibroblasts, granulation tissue, and disorganized type III collagen mark the proliferative phase. The inflammatory phase is dominated by edema and phagocytosis, and coagulation happens in the first minutes. Remodeling comes later, when type III is replaced by stronger type I collagen aligned to stress."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 66-year-old man with stable angina places a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet under his tongue before a physical therapy session as prescribed by his cardiologist. He asks what physiologic effect to expect during activity. Which response is the most likely effect of the drug that the clinician should monitor for after he stands up?", "options": {"A": "Bronchodilation that increases airflow and raises resting oxygen saturation before exercise begins", "B": "Direct blunting of sinoatrial node firing that holds heart rate low at every workload", "C": "Increased myocardial contractility that raises stroke volume and systolic blood pressure during effort", "D": "Peripheral vasodilation that can lower blood pressure and cause lightheadedness or a headache"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Peripheral vasodilation that can lower blood pressure and cause lightheadedness or a headache): Nitroglycerin relaxes vascular smooth muscle, mainly venous, which reduces preload and can lower blood pressure. Reflex tachycardia, headache, and orthostatic lightheadedness are common, so pressure should be watched on standing. Slowing the sinus node is the action of beta blockers, not nitrates. The drug does not dilate airways or increase contractility."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 28-year-old collegiate pitcher in rehabilitation needs to restore rapid shoulder internal rotation. His isokinetic program has been performed only at 60 degrees per second. Retesting is planned at a much faster speed. Based on velocity specificity of strength training, which result is most likely at the faster speed?", "options": {"A": "Gains will be largest at the faster speed because fast twitch fibers were recruited", "B": "Improvement will carry over only partly and appear smaller at the faster test speed", "C": "Gains will carry over equally to every speed up to 180 degrees per second, then decline", "D": "Torque at the faster speed will match the slow speed gain because dynamometers equalize resistance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Improvement will carry over only partly and appear smaller at the faster test speed): Strength gains are greatest at or near the trained velocity, so a program done at 60 degrees per second transfers only partly to a much faster test speed. Larger gains at the faster speed or equal carryover across speeds ignore this specificity. The dynamometer holds velocity constant but does not make adaptations transfer between velocities."}, {"id": 39, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A community health clinic wants to reduce falls among residents older than 70 who live independently and have no acute illness. The medical director asks which single intervention category has the strongest evidence for lowering fall rates in this group. Which should the physical therapist recommend as the core of the program?", "options": {"A": "A single home environment hazard assessment visit offered to every resident living independently", "B": "Daily vitamin D supplements for all residents, including those with normal serum levels", "C": "A single yearly review of eyesight alone, with no other changes for each resident", "D": "A supervised program of progressive balance and lower limb strengthening exercise"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A supervised program of progressive balance and lower limb strengthening exercise): Across community dwelling adults over 70, supervised exercise that progresses balance and leg strength has the most consistent evidence for fewer falls. A single home hazard visit helps mainly people with a fall history and does not improve capacity. Vitamin D supplements help those who are deficient, not residents with normal levels. A multifactorial package is a bundle of several measures and is not the single category with the strongest evidence for this general group."}, {"id": 40, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 17-year-old swimmer has bilateral shoulder looseness, a positive sulcus sign, and apprehension in several directions without any traumatic event. She has generalized joint laxity, hypermobile elbows, and no prior treatment, and her parents ask about the best first approach. Which initial management is best?", "options": {"A": "Capsular stretching with end-range mobilization to increase glenohumeral range of motion and reduce apprehension", "B": "Surgical capsular shift before any trial of a structured rehabilitation program lasting several months", "C": "Immobilization in a sling for six weeks followed by passive range of motion exercises", "D": "Cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening with neuromuscular control training for several months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening with neuromuscular control training for several months): Atraumatic multidirectional instability is treated first with dynamic stabilizer strengthening and neuromuscular control, because the capsule is already lax. Stretching and mobilization add laxity. Surgery is reserved for failure of a long rehabilitation trial, and a sling does not improve dynamic stability."}, {"id": 41, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 33-year-old runner has a chronic Achilles tendon thickening, 10 weeks old. Swelling has resolved, the tendon is stiff and ankle dorsiflexion is limited by tightness at end range. The tendon is about 1 cm deep. Which plan best fits this stage?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz continuous at 2.0 W/cm2, with a stationary head, followed by ice for ten minutes", "B": "3 MHz continuous at about 1.0 W/cm2, with a moving head, followed by stretching", "C": "3 MHz pulsed at 20 percent duty cycle at 0.5 W/cm2, with no stretching afterward", "D": "1 MHz pulsed at 20 percent at 0.5 W/cm2, head moving over the tendon"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (3 MHz continuous at about 1.0 W/cm2, with a moving head, followed by stretching): In the chronic stage the goal is to raise collagen extensibility, which needs continuous thermal ultrasound at a frequency matching the shallow depth and a moving head. Stretching follows right away. Pulsed low doses are nonthermal, and 1 MHz held still or followed by ice defeats the goal."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 52 year old woman with plantar fasciitis volunteers a history of paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria, noting dark urine and chills after past cold exposure. The therapist considers ice massage to the heel because of localized tenderness. Which plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Use a cold pack over a damp towel instead, to soften the exposure to the heel", "B": "Avoid cryotherapy and use a nonthermal option such as stretching or taping", "C": "Use ice massage on the tender heel but limit total exposure to five minutes", "D": "Use a cold whirlpool at 60 degrees F for the foot and ankle for ten minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid cryotherapy and use a nonthermal option such as stretching or taping): Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria is a recognized contraindication to cryotherapy, because cold can trigger hemolysis. Shorter or milder cold still delivers the trigger. A nonthermal approach such as stretching or taping treats the heel without cold exposure."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old man arrives at a physical therapy clinic with new, unexplained abdominal and flank pain that has worsened over two weeks. He has not had a medical evaluation and asks for TENS because a friend used it successfully for back pain. The clinician considers his request. What is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": {"A": "Apply conventional TENS over the lumbar paraspinals, away from the abdominal organs, pending evaluation.", "B": "Apply low-intensity sensory TENS to the flank, which is safe because it cannot damage organs.", "C": "Withhold TENS and refer him for medical evaluation until a diagnosis explains the pain.", "D": "Apply acupuncture-like TENS to the painful area for one session, then refer if pain persists."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold TENS and refer him for medical evaluation until a diagnosis explains the pain.): Unexplained, worsening abdominal and flank pain may signal visceral disease, and TENS can dampen a warning symptom and delay diagnosis. Withholding it and referring is correct. Paraspinal placement still reduces perceived pain from the same problem. Low-intensity sensory settings are less risky electrically but do not change the diagnostic concern. Treating once before referral postpones evaluation."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 34-year-old recreational athlete stretches the hamstrings only on Sundays, then reports no lasting improvement in range. The athlete has no injury and trains four days each week. The clinician reviews stretching frequency recommendations for improving and maintaining flexibility in healthy adults. Which frequency should the clinician recommend?", "options": {"A": "Three to four sessions per week, with each stretch held for only five seconds", "B": "One session per week of two or three stretches held thirty seconds each", "C": "Daily bouncing ballistic stretches of one to two seconds to maximize lasting range", "D": "At least two to three days per week, with daily stretching giving the greatest gains"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At least two to three days per week, with daily stretching giving the greatest gains): Guidelines for healthy adults call for stretching at least two to three days per week, with daily stretching giving the largest gains in range. Frequent but five-second holds are too short to change tissue length. A single weekly session is the pattern that already failed him, since gains fade between sessions. Ballistic bouncing is a different technique and does not give the sustained stretch used for flexibility."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 64-year-old man on metoprolol after a stent is cycling in the third week of rehabilitation. His heart rate is 92 beats per minute, well below the 110 to 125 range his chart lists as the training zone, but he rates his effort at 16 and breathes heavily. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the workload to a perceived exertion near 12 to 13 despite the low heart rate", "B": "Hold the current workload since heart rate is safely low and perceived exertion is subjective", "C": "Stop the session and request an urgent ECG because the heart rate is abnormally low", "D": "Increase the resistance until heart rate reaches the charted zone of 110 to 125"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce the workload to a perceived exertion near 12 to 13 despite the low heart rate): The beta blocker holds his heart rate down, so the heart rate zone no longer reflects real effort. His rating of 16 means he is working hard, above the usual 12 to 13 target, so workload should come down. Raising resistance to hit the zone would overload him. A heart rate of 92 on a beta blocker is expected and not a reason for an urgent ECG."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 71-year-old man scores 54 out of 56 on the Berg and completes the Timed Up and Go in 9.5 seconds with no device. He still reports stumbling while walking and talking, or carrying shopping bags. Which assessment is most useful next?", "options": {"A": "A Romberg test repeated on a foam surface with the eyes open", "B": "Repeating the Berg Balance Scale with the stance items done first", "C": "Computerized dynamic posturography across six different sensory conditions", "D": "A dual task walking test, such as Timed Up and Go while counting backward"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A dual task walking test, such as Timed Up and Go while counting backward): His scores are near the ceiling on single tasks, but his problems appear when attention is divided. A dual task test such as the cognitive Timed Up and Go can reveal that deficit. Repeating the Berg or adding a static test does not stress divided attention."}, {"id": 47, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 19-year-old volleyball player performs a single-leg squat and the knee collapses medially into valgus with the pelvis dropping on the unsupported side. The therapist wants to address the proximal contribution to this movement pattern. Which strengthening plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Isolated quadriceps strengthening with seated knee extension at heavy load in clinic", "B": "Gastrocnemius strengthening with heavy calf raises on a step", "C": "Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening in closed chain such as band walks", "D": "Hip adductor and internal rotator strengthening with resisted cable adduction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening in closed chain such as band walks): Medial knee collapse with a contralateral pelvic drop reflects weak hip abductors and external rotators, so they are trained in closed chain to carry over to single-leg tasks. Isolated knee extension and calf work do not address the hip. Strengthening adductors and internal rotators reinforces the faulty alignment."}, {"id": 48, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A woman at 34 weeks of pregnancy has low back pain and requests massage. She feels dizzy and light-headed when lying flat on her back for more than a minute. Her blood pressure is normal when seated. Which positioning best matches safe practice for her treatment?", "options": {"A": "Prone with a firm pillow under the abdomen to flatten the back", "B": "Flat supine with a small pillow under the knees", "C": "Supine with the head of the table raised slightly for comfort", "D": "Side-lying with pillows between the knees and supporting the abdomen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Side-lying with pillows between the knees and supporting the abdomen): In late pregnancy, the weight of the uterus can compress the vena cava when supine, lowering venous return and causing dizziness, so side-lying with pillow support is used. Flat supine and slightly raised supine both retain that compression, and prone positioning with a pillow places pressure on the abdomen. Pressure and stroke choices should also be gentle over the calves and lower limbs."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats a pressure ulcer on the heel with high voltage pulsed current. The student must choose electrode sizes. The active electrode will lie in the wound bed, and the dispersive electrode will go on intact skin of the same limb. Which size relationship follows standard practice for this setup?", "options": {"A": "Dispersive electrode much smaller than the wound, giving sharply concentrated current at the pad", "B": "Dispersive electrode smaller than the active electrode, giving higher current density under the dispersive pad", "C": "Dispersive electrode larger than the active electrode, giving lower current density under the dispersive pad", "D": "Both electrodes equal in size, giving equal current density under each pad"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dispersive electrode larger than the active electrode, giving lower current density under the dispersive pad): The treatment effect is meant to occur at the active electrode, so the dispersive electrode is larger. A larger pad spreads the same current over more skin, lowering density and sensation there. A smaller dispersive pad would concentrate current and risk discomfort at the return site. Equal sizes give equal density and defeat the purpose of the active and dispersive roles."}, {"id": 50, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician measures a strength-duration curve on a wrist extensor muscle three months after a nerve injury. Rheobase is elevated, and chronaxie measures about 12 ms, compared with a normal innervated muscle chronaxie well under 1 ms on the opposite arm. What do these findings suggest about the muscle?", "options": {"A": "Upper motor neuron lesion with intact nerve supply and the curve shifted toward shorter durations.", "B": "Lower motor neuron denervation, with the curve shifted toward longer pulse durations.", "C": "Disuse atrophy alone, with an intact nerve supply and an unchanged chronaxie.", "D": "Normal innervated muscle, because chronaxie values below 20 ms lie within the expected range."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower motor neuron denervation, with the curve shifted toward longer pulse durations.): Denervated muscle has an elevated rheobase and a markedly prolonged chronaxie, so the strength-duration curve shifts upward and to the right. Normal innervated muscle has a chronaxie well under 1 ms, so 12 ms is clearly abnormal. An upper motor neuron lesion leaves the lower motor neuron intact and does not shift the curve. Disuse atrophy alone does not prolong chronaxie."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During a bodyweight squat, a 19-year-old soccer player's knees collapse inward over the second toes as she descends, while both feet stay flat on the floor. Manual muscle testing shows weak hip abductors and external rotators with normal quadriceps strength. Which corrective strengthening choice best addresses this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Hip adductor squeezes with a ball between the knees to build medial control", "B": "Eccentric single leg decline squats to strengthen the quadriceps against knee valgus", "C": "Foam rolling and stretching of the iliotibial band and tensor fasciae latae", "D": "Side-lying and banded exercises for the gluteus medius and gluteus maximus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Side-lying and banded exercises for the gluteus medius and gluteus maximus): Dynamic valgus in a squat with weak hip abductors and external rotators calls for strengthening the gluteus medius and maximus. Adductor squeezes build a muscle group that pulls the knee inward and does not address the weak abductors. Decline squats target the quadriceps, which tested normal here. Iliotibial band stretching is a real treatment for tightness, but the finding is weakness that needs loading."}, {"id": 52, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 55-year-old man 6 months after a mild stroke walks the clinic hallway with a symmetrical pattern, talks freely and shows few errors across repeated trials. He reports needing no concentration on walking in the clinic. The therapist wants to keep challenging his motor system. Which progression best fits his stage of learning?", "options": {"A": "Return to simple verbal step by step cueing for every stride to restore accuracy", "B": "Provide manual guidance for each step so errors cannot develop during higher speed walking", "C": "Add varied environments and dual tasks, such as outdoor uneven surfaces while carrying objects", "D": "Repeat the same hallway walk with the same pace and surface to build consistency further"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Add varied environments and dual tasks, such as outdoor uneven surfaces while carrying objects): A patient who walks accurately with little attention is at or near the autonomous stage. Progress then comes from variability and added attention demands, such as uneven outdoor terrain and carrying objects, which test whether the skill holds under new conditions. Step by step cues and manual guidance suit early learning, and repeating the same hallway walk adds no new challenge."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician counts the steps a healthy 35-year-old woman takes in one minute during a gait screen, walking at a self-selected comfortable pace on a level hallway. The clinician compares the count with accepted adult norms. What cadence is typical for healthy adults at this pace?", "options": {"A": "Roughly 100 to 120 steps per minute for a healthy adult walking freely", "B": "Roughly 60 to 80 steps per minute, typical of slow or cautious walking in older adults", "C": "Roughly 130 to 150 steps per minute, typical of very fast walking or slow jogging", "D": "Roughly 85 to 95 steps per minute, typical of walking with a short stride"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Roughly 100 to 120 steps per minute for a healthy adult walking freely): Cadence is the number of steps per minute, and healthy adults walking at a comfortable self-selected pace average about 100 to 120. Values near 60 to 80 appear in frail or cautious walkers. Rates around 85 to 95 are seen with shortened strides but fall below the usual adult norm. Cadences of 130 to 150 belong to very fast walking or jogging."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 38-year-old man starts a 60-minute moderate run at 6 a.m. after an overnight fast and feels weak and light-headed at minute 40. He ate dinner at 7 p.m. His physiotherapist considers which store was most reduced by the fast. Which explanation best fits his early fatigue and light-headedness?", "options": {"A": "Phosphocreatine stores fall during the fast, leaving too little ATP at moderate intensity", "B": "Triglyceride stores in fat cells were exhausted overnight, leaving no fatty acids to burn", "C": "Liver glycogen was substantially reduced overnight, limiting glucose release as demand rose", "D": "Muscle glycogen was emptied overnight, since resting muscle burns carbohydrate to maintain posture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Liver glycogen was substantially reduced overnight, limiting glucose release as demand rose): The liver maintains blood glucose overnight by glycogenolysis, so liver glycogen is substantially reduced by morning. During a run, glucose demand rises and the liver may be unable to keep up, which causes weakness and light-headedness. Muscle glycogen is largely preserved, phosphocreatine is not affected by fasting, and fat stores are far from exhausted."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 60 year old man with chronic stroke has elbow flexor spasticity that limits passive extension. The therapist applies a cold pack to the biceps for 15 minutes and then moves directly into passive stretching and active reaching practice. What neurophysiological change from cooling makes this sequence useful?", "options": {"A": "Reduced muscle spindle sensitivity, giving a short window of lower spasticity", "B": "Temporary reduction in muscle fiber stiffness from collagen warming, allowing longer tissue elongation", "C": "Long-lasting resetting of motor neuron excitability that carries over to later sessions", "D": "Increased nerve conduction velocity that improves voluntary motor drive during reaching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced muscle spindle sensitivity, giving a short window of lower spasticity): Cooling lowers muscle spindle sensitivity and slows afferent conduction, giving a short window of reduced spasticity in which stretching and reaching can be practiced. Collagen warming and extensibility belong to heat, not cold. The effect lasts minutes rather than resetting motor neuron excitability, and cold slows conduction instead of speeding it."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 19-year-old gymnast with tight gastrocnemius muscles performs rapid bouncing toe touches before practice and reports that the calves feel tighter afterward. The therapist plans to teach a safer technique. Which instruction best accounts for the muscle spindle response and reduces the protective contraction?", "options": {"A": "Precede the bouncing with a ten second maximal contraction so autogenic inhibition replaces spindle firing", "B": "Hold a slow sustained stretch for about 30 seconds so spindle firing adapts and fades", "C": "Warm the calf with light jogging, then keep bouncing since heat reduces spindle sensitivity", "D": "Perform the bounces at a lower amplitude and higher frequency to reduce the stretch reflex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold a slow sustained stretch for about 30 seconds so spindle firing adapts and fades): Rapid bouncing stretches the muscle quickly, so the spindle fires and the calf contracts reflexively, which feels like tightness. A slow sustained hold lets spindle firing adapt and fade, allowing the muscle to lengthen. A contraction beforehand is a real facilitation method but does not make ballistic bouncing safe. Warming helps tissue extensibility yet does not remove the reflex, and smaller faster bounces still stimulate spindles."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 70-year-old man with weak right knee extensors holds a rail in the clinic stairwell. The therapist teaches a safe sequence for descending one step at a time, since he has no balance deficits otherwise. Which stair descent sequence is correct?", "options": {"A": "Alternate feet in a reciprocal pattern without using the rail", "B": "Lead down with the right leg first, then bring the left leg down", "C": "Descend sideways with both feet placed together on each step", "D": "Lead down with the left leg first, then bring the right leg down to meet it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lead down with the right leg first, then bring the left leg down): When descending, the leading weaker leg lowers the body while the stronger leg controls the descent, summarized as up with the good, down with the bad. Leading with the strong leg makes the weak knee do the eccentric work. A reciprocal pattern without a rail is unsafe for this patient."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist sets up a pressure biofeedback unit under a patient's lumbar spine in hooklying to monitor control during a lower abdominal stabilization test. Before the patient attempts any movement, the cuff must be inflated and the dial read to establish a reference level. What baseline pressure setting is standard for this test?", "options": {"A": "Forty millimeters of mercury, with the patient resting in lumbar neutral", "B": "Eighty millimeters of mercury, with the patient holding a gentle abdominal brace", "C": "Sixty millimeters of mercury, with the patient in a posterior pelvic tilt", "D": "Twenty millimeters of mercury, with the patient pressing the spine flat"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Forty millimeters of mercury, with the patient resting in lumbar neutral): The standard baseline for the lumbar pressure biofeedback unit is 40 mmHg with the spine resting in neutral, so later changes in the dial show movement of the lumbar spine against the cuff. Other starting pressures or starting from a flattened spine or a brace would change the reference. The patient should rest quietly before the test begins."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old has a one-sided headache beginning at the base of the skull and triggered by sustained neck posture. There is reduced neck motion, no aura or nausea, and the flexion-rotation test is limited to 28 degrees toward the painful side. Which diagnosis and treatment pairing is best supported?", "options": {"A": "Tension-type headache, treated with progressive relaxation and heat to the temples and scalp", "B": "Cluster headache, treated with high-flow oxygen and rest from exertion during attacks", "C": "Cervicogenic headache, treated with upper cervical mobilization and deep flexor training", "D": "Migraine without aura, treated with dark room rest and a cold pack to the neck"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cervicogenic headache, treated with upper cervical mobilization and deep flexor training): A flexion-rotation range under about 32 degrees points to C1-C2 dysfunction, and the posture-triggered unilateral pattern with neck restriction fits cervicogenic headache. Trials support upper cervical mobilization combined with craniocervical flexion training. Migraine, tension-type and cluster headaches lack the reproducible neck findings seen here."}, {"id": 60, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 56-year-old man finishes twelve weeks of supervised cardiac rehabilitation after a stent. His ejection fraction is 58 percent, his capacity is 9 METs, and he had no ischemia or arrhythmia on recent testing. He asks about his next step. Which plan best fits his status?", "options": {"A": "Stop all structured exercise and resume activity as tolerated without further guidance", "B": "Transition to a maintenance program with self-monitoring and periodic follow-up in a community setting", "C": "Repeat the supervised program for another twelve weeks with continuous ECG monitoring", "D": "Return to Phase I education with inpatient-level mobilization and heart rate limits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transition to a maintenance program with self-monitoring and periodic follow-up in a community setting): A low-risk patient has an ejection fraction of 50 percent or more, capacity above 7 METs, and no ischemia or arrhythmia. After the supervised outpatient phase he can move to a maintenance program with self-monitoring and periodic follow-up. Repeating supervised sessions with continuous ECG adds no benefit. Stopping structured exercise forfeits the maintenance goal, and the inpatient phase is far behind him."}, {"id": 61, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Just before heel contact the swinging leg's knee extends quickly and the thigh is still moving forward. The therapist wants to know which muscles brake this motion and prepare the limb for initial contact in the final part of swing phase. Which muscles act?", "options": {"A": "The gluteus medius, contracting concentrically to lift the pelvis on the swing side", "B": "The quadriceps, contracting concentrically to speed up the extending knee", "C": "The iliopsoas, contracting concentrically to keep flexing the hip into stance", "D": "The hamstrings, contracting eccentrically to brake the thigh and the extending knee"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The hamstrings, contracting eccentrically to brake the thigh and the extending knee): In terminal swing the limb is moving forward fast, and the hamstrings lengthen under tension to decelerate hip flexion and knee extension and position the foot for initial contact. Concentric quadriceps or iliopsoas activity would speed the limb further, and the gluteus medius works mainly in stance."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient slowly lowers a heavy box from a shelf to a table during a rehabilitation session while the biceps controls the descent. The student asks which type of contraction this represents and what is true of the force capacity of that contraction compared with a concentric contraction of the same muscle.", "options": {"A": "Eccentric contraction, in which the muscle lengthens but generates noticeably less force than a concentric contraction", "B": "Concentric contraction, in which the muscle shortens while lowering the load against gravity", "C": "Eccentric contraction, in which the muscle lengthens while tension can exceed concentric maximum force", "D": "Isometric contraction, in which the muscle length stays constant while the box moves down"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eccentric contraction, in which the muscle lengthens while tension can exceed concentric maximum force): Controlling the descent of a load means the biceps produces tension while being lengthened, which is an eccentric action. Eccentric force capacity is higher than concentric, which is why eccentric work is efficient for loading tissue. A concentric action shortens the muscle. An isometric action has no change in length, so the box could not be moving."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A physiotherapist explains to a recreational runner that blood lactate stays near resting values at easy paces and then climbs sharply once the pace passes a certain point. The runner asks what that turning point is called and what it marks in physiologic terms. Which description of the lactate threshold is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The exercise intensity above which lactate appears in blood faster than the body can clear it", "B": "The workload at which heart rate stops rising and begins to plateau despite higher pace", "C": "The intensity at which muscle phosphocreatine stores are depleted and contraction fails", "D": "The maximal intensity at which oxygen uptake continues to rise with each added workload"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The exercise intensity above which lactate appears in blood faster than the body can clear it): The lactate threshold is the intensity beyond which production by working muscle outpaces oxidation and removal, so blood concentration climbs abruptly. It is a submaximal marker of endurance capacity. A heart rate plateau and the VO2max plateau are different markers, and phosphocreatine depletion is a separate energy system event. None of those describe the point where lactate entry exceeds clearance."}, {"id": 64, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old patient performs a floor bridge with neutral spine and 20-second holds, with no pain and no pelvic drop. The therapist wants to raise the stabilization demand by increasing surface instability while keeping the same basic movement pattern for the hips and trunk. Which progression is most appropriate next for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Perform the bridge on the floor with a single leg lifted and the pelvis held level", "B": "Bridge on the floor with a resistance band around the knees and the thighs abducted", "C": "Extend the bridge hold to sixty seconds and add a second daily set", "D": "Bridge with both heels resting on a Swiss ball, holding the pelvis level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bridge with both heels resting on a Swiss ball, holding the pelvis level): Placing the heels on a Swiss ball keeps the same hip and trunk pattern but makes the base unstable, so the stabilizers work harder to hold the pelvis level. A single-leg bridge is a real progression but changes the pattern and increases load rather than instability. The banded bridge adds resisted abduction, and longer holds raise endurance demand without changing surface stability."}, {"id": 65, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 21-year-old football player had a severe quadriceps contusion three weeks ago and received repeated deep massage and forceful passive knee flexion stretching. Pain is increasing, knee flexion is falling, and a firm, warm, tender mass is now palpable in the anterior thigh. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Apply cold and compression, rest the thigh, and recheck knee range of motion in one week", "B": "Switch to ultrasound and gentle massage over the mass, and resume stretching when pain settles", "C": "Stop forceful stretching and deep massage, and refer for imaging to assess for heterotopic ossification", "D": "Refer for aspiration of a presumed hematoma, then resume stretching once the swelling subsides"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop forceful stretching and deep massage, and refer for imaging to assess for heterotopic ossification): Worsening pain, falling flexion and a firm warm mass after vigorous massage and passive stretching suggest myositis ossificans, a form of heterotopic ossification. Provocative stretching and deep massage must stop and imaging should confirm. Cold and compression suit the acute phase, not week three. Heat, ultrasound or massage may aggravate ectopic bone, and a firm mass is not an aspirable hematoma."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 13-year-old premenarchal girl, Risser stage 1, has a thoracic curve of 30 degrees on her first standing radiograph. She is otherwise healthy, has no pain and plays soccer. Which management is most consistent with accepted guidelines for adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?", "options": {"A": "Observation only with a repeat radiograph once each year at routine visits", "B": "Bracing, scoliosis-specific exercise and scheduled radiographic monitoring by orthopedics", "C": "Exercise alone with radiographs ordered only if pain or deformity develops later", "D": "Immediate spinal fusion surgery to prevent further progression of the curve"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bracing, scoliosis-specific exercise and scheduled radiographic monitoring by orthopedics): A skeletally immature adolescent with a 25 to 40 degree curve is the classic indication for bracing, usually paired with scoliosis-specific exercise and close radiographic follow-up. Observation fits curves under about 25 degrees, and fusion is considered when curves approach 45 to 50 degrees. Waiting for pain is inappropriate because idiopathic curves are often painless."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician programs electrical muscle stimulation for a patient with quadriceps weakness after a tibial plateau fracture. The device is set to a contraction time of 10 seconds followed by a rest time of 50 seconds. What are the duty cycle and the on to off ratio?", "options": {"A": "Duty cycle of about 17 percent, an on to off ratio of 1 to 5", "B": "Duty cycle of 50 percent, an on to off ratio of 1 to 1", "C": "Duty cycle of 83 percent, an on to off ratio of 5 to 1", "D": "Duty cycle of 20 percent, an on to off ratio of 1 to 5"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Duty cycle of about 17 percent, an on to off ratio of 1 to 5): Duty cycle is on time divided by the total of on plus off time, so 10 divided by 60 equals about 17 percent. The on to off ratio is 10 to 50, or 1 to 5. Dividing on time by off time gives 20 percent, which ignores the on time in the denominator. The 83 percent figure is the rest fraction, and 50 percent would need equal on and off times."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with a resting heart rate of 70 beats per minute begins aerobic training. Her clinician estimates maximum heart rate as 220 minus age and prescribes a minimum intensity of 60 percent of heart rate reserve using the Karvonen method. What is her lower target training heart rate?", "options": {"A": "96 beats per minute, calculated as 60 percent of the age-predicted maximum alone", "B": "54 beats per minute, calculated as 60 percent of the reserve without the resting rate", "C": "124 beats per minute, from the reserve fraction plus the resting rate", "D": "142 beats per minute, calculated with 80 percent of reserve added to the resting rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (124 beats per minute, from the reserve fraction plus the resting rate): Maximum heart rate is 220 minus 60, or 160. Reserve is 160 minus 70, or 90. Sixty percent of 90 is 54, and adding the resting 70 gives 124 beats per minute. Taking 60 percent of 160 gives 96, leaving out the resting rate gives 54, and using 80 percent gives 142."}, {"id": 69, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 41-year-old nurse with chronic low back pain uses conventional TENS and gets good relief while the unit is on, but her pain returns within minutes of turning it off. She wants relief that lasts for hours after each session and can tolerate stronger sensations. Which change in TENS settings best addresses her goal?", "options": {"A": "Use 2 to 4 pps with visible twitching intensity for 20 to 30 minutes.", "B": "Use brief intense mode at 100 pps with long pulses for 10 minutes before activity.", "C": "Use burst mode at 2 bursts per second at sensory intensity for 30 minutes.", "D": "Raise the conventional rate to 150 pps and extend each session to two hours."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use 2 to 4 pps with visible twitching intensity for 20 to 30 minutes.): Low-rate acupuncture-like TENS at 2 to 4 pps with motor-level intensity is thought to release endogenous opioids, giving a slower onset of 20 to 30 minutes but relief that outlasts the session. Conventional gate control relief tends to fade soon after the unit is stopped. Raising conventional rate or using brief intense or sensory-level burst settings does not create the prolonged opioid-mediated effect."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old recreational runner has two months of pain and thickening in the midportion of the Achilles tendon, worse after rest. The clinician chooses the classic eccentric heel drop protocol described for midportion Achilles tendinopathy and wants to prescribe it with the standard dosage. Which dosage matches that standard protocol?", "options": {"A": "Two sets of twenty repetitions every other day for eight weeks on flat ground", "B": "Three sets of fifteen, twice daily for twelve weeks, with knee straight and bent", "C": "Five sets of five heavy repetitions three times weekly for six weeks with a heel lift", "D": "Three sets of ten repetitions once daily for four weeks with the knee straight only"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Three sets of fifteen, twice daily for twelve weeks, with knee straight and bent): The Alfredson protocol uses eccentric heel drops from a step, three sets of fifteen, twice daily for twelve weeks. It is done once with the knee straight to load the gastrocnemius and once with the knee bent to load the soleus. Shorter or less frequent programs do not match the studied dosage, and training on flat ground removes the eccentric drop below neutral."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old completed four weeks of daily hamstring stretching, and passive range increased by 12 degrees. Measures of passive muscle stiffness and resistance torque at the old end-range angle were unchanged. A student asks which adaptation most likely explains the range gain. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Permanent plastic elongation of the tendon, meaning the passive stiffness has been reduced for good", "B": "Addition of sarcomeres in series, meaning the muscle fibers have structurally lengthened within four weeks", "C": "Increased stretch tolerance, meaning the patient accepts greater tension before sensing discomfort", "D": "Increased muscle spindle sensitivity, meaning the muscle relaxes faster during each passive stretch"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased stretch tolerance, meaning the patient accepts greater tension before sensing discomfort): When range improves without a change in passive stiffness or torque at the old end-range angle, the gain reflects greater tolerance of stretch, a sensory change in how much tension the patient accepts. Structural adaptations such as added sarcomeres or tendon elongation would change stiffness and generally take longer. Higher spindle sensitivity would increase reflex resistance and work against range."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient has weak shoulder external rotators and abductors after an overuse injury. The therapist wants to strengthen flexion combined with abduction and external rotation, finishing overhead and out to the side. Two upper extremity diagonals involve flexion and external rotation. Which pattern should the therapist select?", "options": {"A": "D2 extension, because it combines extension with adduction and internal rotation", "B": "D1 extension, because it combines extension with abduction and internal rotation", "C": "D1 flexion, because it combines flexion with adduction and external rotation", "D": "D2 flexion, because it combines flexion with abduction and external rotation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (D2 flexion, because it combines flexion with abduction and external rotation): D2 flexion combines flexion, abduction, and external rotation and finishes overhead and out to the side, matching the goal. D1 flexion shares flexion and external rotation but uses adduction. The two extension diagonals move in the opposite direction. Abduction is the detail that separates the correct pattern from D1 flexion."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist is starting a DeLorme progressive resistance program for a patient recovering from a knee injury. Before any training sets are prescribed, the patient must first be tested to establish the reference load for the program. Which definition describes the reference value, called the ten repetition maximum (10RM), used to set the training loads?", "options": {"A": "The average load lifted across ten consecutive sessions of the prescribed exercise program", "B": "Ten percent of the maximum weight the patient can lift for one single repetition", "C": "The lightest load that still allows ten repetitions over three consecutive sets without rest", "D": "The heaviest load lifted through full range exactly ten times with correct technique"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The heaviest load lifted through full range exactly ten times with correct technique): The 10RM is the greatest load a patient can move through the full range for ten repetitions with good form, and DeLorme percentages are applied to it. It is a repetition-based maximum, not a percentage of the one repetition maximum. It is found in a single set, not by averaging across sessions or by comparing three rested sets."}, {"id": 74, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 42-year-old accountant finishes a heavy 60-minute whole-body resistance session at 6 p.m. and wants to maximize muscle protein synthesis and glycogen recovery. He usually skips food until the next day because he is not hungry in the evening. Based on standard sports nutrition guidance, which post-exercise approach is most appropriate for him?", "options": {"A": "Eat a very large high protein meal of 60 g protein with no carbohydrate", "B": "Consume carbohydrate alone, about 1 g per kg body mass, within the first hour", "C": "Consume carbohydrate with roughly 20 to 25 g of protein within one to two hours", "D": "Drink 20 to 25 g of protein now and delay carbohydrate until breakfast"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Consume carbohydrate with roughly 20 to 25 g of protein within one to two hours): After heavy resistance work, carbohydrate refills glycogen while roughly 20 to 25 g of protein supplies amino acids for muscle protein synthesis, and both work best when taken within a couple of hours. A very large protein meal without carbohydrate leaves glycogen low and adds little beyond the useful dose. Carbohydrate by itself restores glycogen but gives muscle repair less support. Delaying carbohydrate until morning wastes the early window for glycogen resynthesis."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with unilateral vestibular hypofunction reports blurred vision and dizziness when she turns her head. The therapist prescribes keeping the eyes fixed on a stationary letter while moving the head side to side, increasing speed gradually. What is the primary goal of this exercise?", "options": {"A": "Adaptation of the vestibulo-ocular reflex to reduce retinal image slip", "B": "Canalith repositioning of debris out of the posterior canal", "C": "Habituation of positional vertigo through repeated provocation of the symptom", "D": "Substitution of cervical proprioception for lost labyrinthine input"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Adaptation of the vestibulo-ocular reflex to reduce retinal image slip): Fixating a target while moving the head creates retinal slip that drives adaptive change in the vestibulo-ocular reflex gain, which improves gaze stability. Habituation uses repeated provocation of a symptom, substitution uses other senses, and canalith repositioning moves loose debris. None of these is the purpose of this drill."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 59-year-old man after a stroke has a peak knee flexion in swing of only about 25 degrees, compared with the normal 60 degrees, and he circumducts the limb to clear the floor. Hip flexion strength and ankle dorsiflexion are normal. Which cause is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Rectus femoris overactivity limiting knee flexion in swing", "B": "Tibialis anterior weakness limiting the foot lift needed in midswing", "C": "Hamstring weakness limiting the knee flexion needed in initial swing", "D": "Iliopsoas weakness limiting the hip flexion needed in initial swing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rectus femoris overactivity limiting knee flexion in swing): Stiff-knee gait is a common poststroke pattern in which the rectus femoris stays active in preswing and initial swing and resists knee flexion, so peak flexion falls and the patient circumducts. Hip flexion strength and dorsiflexion are normal here, and weak hamstrings would not stiffen the knee."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "Starting with the arm overhead, abducted and externally rotated, a patient pulls down and across the body toward the opposite hip while the therapist applies resistance, like returning a sword to its sheath. Following standard proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation naming, which shoulder motions does this upper extremity D2 extension pattern combine?", "options": {"A": "Shoulder flexion, adduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the hand near the opposite shoulder", "B": "Shoulder extension, adduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm across the opposite hip", "C": "Shoulder extension, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the palm facing away at the hip", "D": "Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm down and out"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shoulder extension, adduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm across the opposite hip): D2 extension is the reverse of D2 flexion, so the arm travels from flexion, abduction, and external rotation into extension, adduction, and internal rotation. The arm ends across the opposite hip. Finishing down and out to the same side is D1 extension. Retaining external rotation or a flexion component would not reverse the starting position."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 69-year-old woman with severe COPD walks on a treadmill during pulmonary rehabilitation. Her resting oxygen saturation is 93 percent on room air. After four minutes at a modest pace, the pulse oximeter reads 85 percent and she reports marked breathlessness. What is the most appropriate immediate action by the therapist?", "options": {"A": "Continue at the same pace for another two minutes to allow the saturation to recover", "B": "Stop the walk, seat her, coach pursed-lip breathing, and apply prescribed supplemental oxygen", "C": "Lower the speed slightly and recheck the saturation after five more minutes of walking", "D": "Raise the incline to recruit accessory muscles and improve ventilation during the effort"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the walk, seat her, coach pursed-lip breathing, and apply prescribed supplemental oxygen): In COPD, exercise is generally stopped when saturation drops below about 88 percent or symptoms are marked. Seating the patient, coaching pursed-lip breathing, and giving prescribed oxygen restores saturation safely. Continuing at the same pace or only slowing slightly leaves her desaturated. Raising the incline increases the metabolic and ventilatory demand and worsens the problem."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient lifts a 25 lb dumbbell through the full range of elbow flexion using a standard isotonic curl. Her therapist explains why the load she can use is limited even though the muscle could produce more force at other points in the arc. What explains this limitation of conventional isotonic loading?", "options": {"A": "The weight lifted is limited by the weakest point in the range of motion", "B": "The load is set by the angle with the longest moment arm, where torque peaks", "C": "Resistance adjusts to effort through the arc, so peak load falls near mid range", "D": "Muscle length stays constant during the lift, so tension cannot vary through the arc"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The weight lifted is limited by the weakest point in the range of motion): A fixed weight must be light enough to move at the weakest point of the arc, so the muscle is under-loaded where leverage and length-tension are more favourable. Strongest-angle loading would stall the lift at weaker angles. Accommodating resistance belongs to isokinetic dynamometers. A fixed muscle length describes an isometric action, not a full-range curl."}, {"id": 80, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 65-year-old man has vertigo when rolling in bed. The Dix-Hallpike test is negative for nystagmus, but a supine head roll test to the right produces strong horizontal nystagmus beating toward the floor, stronger than on the left. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Meclizine as the main treatment for the positional vertigo", "B": "Gaze stabilization exercises to adapt the vestibulo-ocular reflex gain", "C": "The Epley maneuver for debris in the posterior semicircular canal", "D": "Horizontal canal repositioning such as the barbecue roll"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Horizontal canal repositioning such as the barbecue roll): A negative Dix-Hallpike with a positive roll test showing horizontal geotropic nystagmus indicates horizontal canal BPPV. The barbecue roll or similar maneuver moves the debris out of that canal. The Epley maneuver targets the posterior canal, and exercises or suppressant medication do not remove the debris."}, {"id": 81, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old patient 10 weeks after a knee injury has a painful arc near mid range during resisted knee extension. Fixed weight isotonic exercise provokes pain at that point. Which exercise mode allows maximal effort while the resistance adjusts to pain-limited effort through the arc?", "options": {"A": "Plyometric jumping to use the stretch-shortening cycle through the painful range", "B": "Isokinetic exercise, which accommodates resistance to effort at a set angular velocity", "C": "Isotonic exercise with a heavier fixed weight to overload the painful range of motion", "D": "Isometric exercise at the painful angle with a maximal sustained contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Isokinetic exercise, which accommodates resistance to effort at a set angular velocity): Isokinetic devices fix the speed and match the resistance to the patient's effort at every point, so the resistance falls when effort drops at the painful arc. A heavier fixed isotonic weight raises the load right where pain occurs. A maximal isometric hold at the painful angle and plyometric jumping both increase stress there."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 56-year-old man has a six-month history of shoulder stiffness. Pain is now mild and occurs mainly with forced motion, while capsular restriction limits external rotation to 10 degrees with a firm end feel. He is in the frozen stage of adhesive capsulitis. What treatment emphasis is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Heat followed by sustained stretching into the capsular restriction and joint mobilization to regain motion", "B": "Gentle grade I oscillations short of pain with ice and avoidance of stretching", "C": "Referral for manipulation under anesthesia as the first treatment for this stiffness", "D": "Scapular stabilization and cuff strengthening to improve motion before mobilizing the capsule"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heat followed by sustained stretching into the capsular restriction and joint mobilization to regain motion): In the frozen stage pain is low and stiffness dominates, so heat followed by sustained stretching and mobilization to the restriction is the emphasis. Gentle pain-limited oscillations with ice fit the painful freezing stage instead. Manipulation under anesthesia is reserved for cases that fail a trial of conservative care. Strengthening alone does not release a capsular restriction that blocks external rotation."}, {"id": 83, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 22 year old rugby player has a quadriceps contusion from a tackle 8 hours ago. The thigh is swelling rapidly, tense, and painful, and girth is increasing between measurements 30 minutes apart. Heat seems tempting for muscle relaxation. Which management choice is best?", "options": {"A": "Deep friction massage to disperse the collecting blood and restore early knee motion", "B": "Moist heat for 20 minutes to loosen the muscle before knee flexion", "C": "Cold with compression and elevation to limit bleeding, with no heat or massage", "D": "Contrast bath to flush the thigh and pump out fluid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cold with compression and elevation to limit bleeding, with no heat or massage): Rapid girth increase signals ongoing bleeding, so cold with compression and elevation limits hemorrhage. Heat and massage increase blood flow and can enlarge the hematoma. A contrast bath includes a warm phase that vasodilates the injured muscle."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "After one non-injurious fall in her kitchen, a 76-year-old woman stopped leaving her home, citing fear of another fall. Her Berg score is 50 out of 56, her strength is normal for age, and she has no new neurological symptoms. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Home safety modification and removal of kitchen hazards as the main intervention", "B": "Graded community exercise and balance training to rebuild confidence", "C": "Vitamin D and calcium supplementation to lower fall related fracture risk", "D": "Clinic based high intensity leg strengthening to correct the weakness behind the fear"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Graded community exercise and balance training to rebuild confidence): Fear of falling with a good Berg score and normal strength is a confidence and avoidance problem, so graded community exercise and balance training rebuild activity. Home hazard removal is useful but does not reverse avoidance. Vitamin D and calcium support bone health and fracture risk, not fear. Intensive strengthening targets a deficit she does not have."}, {"id": 85, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old patient sits on a Swiss ball with feet flat and performs alternating marches while keeping the trunk upright. The therapist explains why this task trains the trunk stabilizers more than the same march performed on a firm chair. What principle accounts for the difference between the two conditions?", "options": {"A": "The ball raises hip height above the knees, which increases hamstring activation", "B": "The unstable base forces continuous small trunk muscle adjustments to keep the body centered", "C": "The ball raises the center of mass slightly, which increases hip flexor demand during each march", "D": "The ball reduces ground reaction force, so the trunk muscles work less during each march"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The unstable base forces continuous small trunk muscle adjustments to keep the body centered): A ball moves under the patient, so the trunk muscles must adjust continuously to keep the body centered, which raises stabilizer activation compared with a firm chair. Hip height changes may alter hamstring or hip flexor work but do not explain the added trunk demand. Ground reaction force is not meaningfully reduced and does not lower the need for trunk muscle contraction. The instability itself is the training stimulus."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 36 year old carpenter has localized pain over the common extensor origin at the lateral elbow, with no swelling. The therapist wants cold analgesia before a short session of active wrist exercises and considers a bag of ice or ice massage. Which choice is best, and why?", "options": {"A": "Ice massage, because it cools a small area quickly and allows ongoing movement", "B": "Cold whirlpool for the whole arm, since immersion covers the painful area evenly", "C": "Ice bag for 30 minutes, since longer cooling gives deeper analgesia", "D": "Vapocoolant spray over the epicondyle held in place until skin frosts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ice massage, because it cools a small area quickly and allows ongoing movement): Ice massage cools a small area to numbness in about 5 to 10 minutes and lets the patient move during treatment, which suits an epicondylar origin before exercise. Prolonged bagging and whole arm immersion cool more than needed. Holding vapocoolant in place frosts the skin."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has complete denervation of the tibialis anterior from a peroneal nerve injury, and the chiropractor wants to stimulate the muscle directly. High voltage pulsed current with brief pulses fails to produce contraction at maximal tolerable intensity. What explains this, and what is the better approach?", "options": {"A": "Denervated muscle needs a larger dispersive pad, so enlarge the return electrode", "B": "Denervated muscle needs a higher pulse rate, so raise the rate to the maximum available", "C": "Denervated muscle needs a negative active electrode, so switch to cathodal output", "D": "Denervated muscle needs very long pulse durations, so use a long-duration stimulator"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Denervated muscle needs very long pulse durations, so use a long-duration stimulator): Denervated muscle fibers respond poorly to short pulses and need very long pulse durations, often in the range of tens or hundreds of milliseconds. High voltage pulsed current is far too brief to excite them. Raising the rate, changing polarity, or enlarging the pad does not lengthen the pulse."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist begins the arm in full D1 flexion, flexed, adducted, and externally rotated across the face, then asks the patient to pull down and out toward the same-side hip against resistance. A student asks which shoulder motions combine in this return movement, named the D1 extension pattern in proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation. What is the answer?", "options": {"A": "Shoulder flexion, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm overhead and the palm forward", "B": "Shoulder extension, adduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm across the opposite hip", "C": "Shoulder extension, abduction, and external rotation, finishing with the palm turned outward behind the body", "D": "Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm down and away from the body"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm down and away from the body): D1 extension reverses D1 flexion, so the shoulder moves into extension, abduction, and internal rotation and the arm finishes down and away from the body. Finishing across the opposite hip is D2 extension, which uses adduction. Keeping external rotation fails to reverse the rotation component. A flexion finish overhead does not fit an extension diagonal."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 49-year-old swimmer is nine weeks into treatment of rotator cuff tendinopathy. She is pain-free in daily activities, has full range, and tolerates light resistance. Tissue is considered to be in the remodeling phase. Which progression best supports tendon adaptation now?", "options": {"A": "Progressive heavy slow resistance for the cuff and scapular muscles, three sessions weekly", "B": "Therapeutic ultrasound over the tendon, with rest from loading until tenderness resolves", "C": "Continued submaximal isometrics at the same load to protect the remodeling tendon for several weeks", "D": "Plyometric overhead ball drops at high speed before heavy strength is rebuilt"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Progressive heavy slow resistance for the cuff and scapular muscles, three sessions weekly): In the remodeling phase tendon adapts to progressively greater slow, heavy loading of the cuff and scapular muscles. Ultrasound with rest offers no loading stimulus. Staying with unchanged isometrics suits an earlier phase, and high speed plyometrics belong later, after a strength base has been rebuilt."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 47-year-old presents with neck pain and right arm paresthesia. Rotation toward the right measures 45 degrees, the median nerve upper limb tension test reproduces symptoms, Spurling's test is positive, and manual distraction relieves them. The clinician weighs cervical radiculopathy against shoulder pathology. What is the best interpretation and plan?", "options": {"A": "Radiculopathy is highly likely, so begin traction, deep neck flexor work and neural mobilization", "B": "A rotator cuff tear is likely, so order shoulder MRI before treating the neck", "C": "Cervical myelopathy is likely, so refer urgently for a surgical opinion", "D": "Thoracic outlet syndrome is likely, so start first rib mobilization and scalene stretching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Radiculopathy is highly likely, so begin traction, deep neck flexor work and neural mobilization): Rotation under 60 degrees toward the involved side, a positive tension test, positive Spurling and symptom relief with distraction form the cluster that makes radiculopathy highly likely. Conservative care with traction, deep neck flexor training and nerve mobility is appropriate when neurological signs are stable. Myelopathy would show upper motor neuron findings, which are absent."}, {"id": 91, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 36-year-old woman has early L4 radiculopathy with right ankle dorsiflexion of 4 out of 5. A clinician watches her walk down a hallway and wants to predict what a mild dorsiflexor deficit looks like on observation. Which finding is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Toe drag in swing with a high steppage knee lift on the right", "B": "Knee hyperextension in midstance with the heel held off the floor", "C": "An audible foot slap at loading response with normal clearance in swing", "D": "Vaulting on the left limb with a rise onto the left toes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An audible foot slap at loading response with normal clearance in swing): A grade of 4 out of 5 means the dorsiflexors can lift the foot in swing, so clearance is normal, but they cannot fully control the forefoot's eccentric lowering after heel contact, producing foot slap. Toe drag and steppage appear when strength falls to roughly 3 out of 5 or less."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 41-year-old has pain around the eye and forehead and a tender trigger point in the sternal division of the sternocleidomastoid. The therapist wants to treat it manually and avoid structures deep to the muscle. He has no neurologic findings. Which technique is safest?", "options": {"A": "Use hacking over the muscle to release the point", "B": "Press a thumb flat against the muscle to compress it toward the cervical spine", "C": "Lift and squeeze the muscle in a pincer grasp away from the deeper vessels", "D": "Apply sustained deep pressure at the muscle's midpoint with the thumb"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lift and squeeze the muscle in a pincer grasp away from the deeper vessels): The sternocleidomastoid lies over the carotid sheath, so it is lifted and held in a pincer grasp, which keeps pressure within the muscle and away from the vessels and nerves. Pressing the muscle toward the spine or applying deep thumb pressure risks compressing the carotid. Percussion on the neck is also unsafe."}, {"id": 93, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 27-year-old rower has trained twice daily for six weeks and now reports persistent fatigue, poor sleep and slower times. Resting heart rate is up 10 beats per minute, and his testosterone to cortisol ratio is lower than at baseline. Three weeks of reduced training did not help. Which interpretation best fits this picture?", "options": {"A": "Short-term overreaching that resolves with a taper of three to five days", "B": "Iron deficiency anemia from heavy sweating, needing supplements rather than reduced training load", "C": "Acute muscle glycogen depletion that a carbohydrate meal will correct within hours", "D": "Overtraining syndrome, with a catabolic hormonal shift requiring an extended reduction in training load"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Overtraining syndrome, with a catabolic hormonal shift requiring an extended reduction in training load): Six weeks of heavy training with fatigue, poor sleep, a raised resting heart rate and a falling testosterone to cortisol ratio that persist after three weeks of reduced load point to overtraining syndrome. Simple overreaching recovers within days to a couple of weeks, so it does not fit. Anemia can cause fatigue but does not explain the hormonal shift or the training history. Glycogen depletion would respond to food within hours, so it cannot explain these findings."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 40-year-old office worker has a chronic upper trapezius spasm that is painful but not limited by a contracture. The goal is to relieve pain and spasm and to increase local blood flow, not to stretch dense collagen. Which tissue temperature rise is the appropriate target?", "options": {"A": "A rise of about 6 degrees Celsius, which maximizes blood flow quickly", "B": "A rise of about 1 degree Celsius, the mild heating level", "C": "A rise of about 2 to 3 degrees Celsius, the moderate heating level", "D": "A rise of about 4 degrees Celsius or more, the vigorous heating level"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A rise of about 2 to 3 degrees Celsius, the moderate heating level): Moderate heating of 2 to 3 degrees C is associated with reduced pain and spasm and increased blood flow. Vigorous heating at 4 degrees C or more is reserved for collagen extensibility, and mild heating of 1 degree C affects metabolism and mild inflammation. A 6 degree rise would damage tissue."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old cashier stands for long shifts and shows increased lumbar lordosis with an anterior pelvic tilt and a protruding abdomen. She has low back ache by the end of each day and no leg symptoms. Using the Janda lower crossed pattern, which description correctly pairs the tight and weak muscle groups?", "options": {"A": "Tight hip flexors and abdominals, weak lumbar extensors and hamstrings", "B": "Tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors; weak abdominals and gluteals", "C": "Tight abdominals and gluteus maximus, weak hip flexors and lumbar extensors", "D": "Tight hamstrings and abdominals, weak hip flexors and gluteals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors; weak abdominals and gluteals): The lower crossed pattern pairs tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors with weak abdominals and gluteals. The imbalance tips the pelvis anteriorly and increases lordosis. Reversing the groups or calling the abdominals tight would predict a flat back posture rather than the lordosis seen here."}, {"id": 96, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old athlete receives pulsed electrical stimulation to the quadriceps at 80 pps with a 10 second on time and a 20 second off time. The torque falls sharply after the fourth contraction, and he reports the muscle feels exhausted. The clinician wants to keep the strengthening effect while reducing this rapid fatigue. Which change is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Deliver continuous stimulation without rest so motor units adapt to the repeated load.", "B": "Raise the pulse rate to 100 pps and shorten the off time further.", "C": "Lower the pulse rate to 35 to 50 pps and lengthen the off time.", "D": "Shorten the pulse duration to 50 microseconds and remove the ramp-up."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lower the pulse rate to 35 to 50 pps and lengthen the off time.): Higher pulse rates and short rest periods fatigue stimulated muscle quickly. Lowering the rate to 35 to 50 pps still produces a tetanic contraction, and a longer off time allows recovery between contractions, so torque is better maintained. Raising the rate or shortening rest increases fatigue. Pulse duration and ramp do not address the cause, and continuous stimulation removes recovery entirely."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 57-year-old man with low back pain and an untreated resting blood pressure of 168 over 102 asks whether he can use inversion boots at home for gravity traction. He has no history of glaucoma. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise against inversion because high blood pressure raises risk when inverted", "B": "Allow brief inversion at 30 degrees since hypertension affects only supine traction", "C": "Permit inversion for ten minutes if his blood pressure is first checked and stays under 180", "D": "Recommend a gradual progression from partial inversion, since he lacks glaucoma or eye disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise against inversion because high blood pressure raises risk when inverted): Inversion raises blood pressure and venous pressure in the head, so untreated hypertension makes it unsafe. A shallow angle still raises pressure, and the risk is not limited to supine traction. A single reading under a high cut-off does not make an unmanaged resting pressure of 168 over 102 acceptable. Lacking glaucoma removes one contraindication, but hypertension remains."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old man felt a sudden snap in his calf while sprinting for a rebound and now struggles to push off. With him lying prone and the knee flexed, the examiner squeezes the calf muscle belly. What finding on this squeeze test indicates a complete Achilles tendon rupture?", "options": {"A": "No passive plantarflexion of the foot occurs when the calf muscle belly is squeezed", "B": "The foot inverts as the tibialis posterior tendon is pulled by the squeeze", "C": "The foot plantarflexes briskly but pain increases over the medial gastrocnemius belly", "D": "The foot dorsiflexes slightly because the soleus slackens during the calf squeeze"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No passive plantarflexion of the foot occurs when the calf muscle belly is squeezed): In a healthy leg, squeezing the calf pulls on the intact Achilles tendon and produces passive plantarflexion. When the tendon is completely ruptured, the force is not transmitted and the foot stays still, which is a positive Thompson test. Pain, dorsiflexion, or inversion are not the diagnostic response."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 25-year-old runner with patellofemoral pain has pain at the 10th repetition of a step-down. The therapist applies a medial glide patellar tape and has the patient repeat the step-down. What finding best indicates that the taping is appropriate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "The patient reports that the tape feels tight along the lateral border", "B": "The patient has less swelling at the knee one hour after the tape", "C": "The step-down becomes pain-free or markedly less painful immediately after the taping", "D": "The patella sits visibly higher in the groove at rest after taping"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The step-down becomes pain-free or markedly less painful immediately after the taping): The standard clinical test of a taping technique is an immediate reduction in pain during the provocative task, such as a step-down or squat. A snug feeling, resting patellar position or later swelling changes do not show that the correction has reduced symptoms during function. Static appearance and the athlete's description of tape tightness do not predict whether symptoms will improve during activity."}, {"id": 100, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 59-year-old man with long-standing hypertension arrives for an outpatient exercise session. After five minutes seated, the clinician measures 214 over 108 mmHg on two readings. He feels well and has no chest pain or headache. According to standard exercise screening guidelines, what is the appropriate decision for today's session?", "options": {"A": "Proceed after a longer 15-minute warm-up, since the pressure should settle with gradual activity", "B": "Proceed with light cycling at 40 percent of reserve while checking pressure every three minutes", "C": "Postpone the session and refer him for medical review of his blood pressure", "D": "Proceed with resistance training only, since isometric work places less strain than aerobic effort"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Postpone the session and refer him for medical review of his blood pressure): A resting systolic pressure above 200 mmHg or diastolic above 110 mmHg is a relative contraindication to exercise testing and training. The absence of symptoms does not remove the risk. Lighter cycling still raises systolic pressure. Resistance work raises pressure sharply, and a warm-up does not reliably lower such a high reading, so referral comes first."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 61-year-old retired teacher is three weeks after arthroscopic repair of a full-thickness supraspinatus tear. The surgeon's protocol protects the repair during early tendon healing. She feels good and asks to start lifting a light dumbbell overhead at home. Which exercise approach is most appropriate at this point?", "options": {"A": "Continue pendulums and therapist-guided passive range of motion within limits, avoiding active elevation", "B": "Begin active-assisted overhead pulley exercises with light isotonic scaption against dumbbell resistance", "C": "Begin closed-chain weight bearing push-ups on a bench to restore scapular control", "D": "Start progressive resisted external rotation with elastic tubing three times weekly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue pendulums and therapist-guided passive range of motion within limits, avoiding active elevation): Early after cuff repair the tendon is weak, so motion is passive and pendulum based and active elevation is withheld. Pulleys with dumbbell scaption, resisted tubing work, and push-ups all load the repair too soon. Active and resisted work follows once healing allows, per the surgeon's protocol."}, {"id": 102, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old soccer player has a tight posterior calf and limited dorsiflexion. The clinician wants a wall stretch that targets the gastrocnemius more than the soleus. Considering which muscle crosses the knee joint, how should the knee and ankle be positioned during the stretch?", "options": {"A": "Knee flexed with the heel raised on a step and the ankle plantarflexed", "B": "Knee extended with the foot turned outward and the ankle held in neutral", "C": "Knee flexed about 45 degrees with the heel flat on the floor", "D": "Knee fully extended, heel flat on the floor, ankle dorsiflexed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Knee fully extended, heel flat on the floor, ankle dorsiflexed): The gastrocnemius crosses both the knee and the ankle, so it is stretched with the knee extended and the ankle dorsiflexed. Bending the knee slackens the gastrocnemius and shifts the stretch to the soleus, which crosses only the ankle. Plantarflexing the ankle shortens the calf. A neutral ankle with an outward foot does not dorsiflex the ankle, so it does not lengthen the muscle."}, {"id": 103, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old triathlete has lateral knee pain at about 30 degrees of flexion during runs, relieved by rest. His hip abduction strength is graded 4 minus out of 5 on the painful side with a pelvic drop on stance, and foam rolling the band has not helped. Which treatment plan best addresses the cause?", "options": {"A": "Iliotibial band stretching and foam rolling with continued training", "B": "A corticosteroid injection at the lateral epicondyle followed by return to full mileage within a week", "C": "Quadriceps strengthening with patellar taping to unload the patellofemoral joint during running", "D": "Hip abductor strengthening with reduced running volume and gait retraining"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hip abductor strengthening with reduced running volume and gait retraining): Lateral knee pain near 30 degrees of flexion with weak hip abductors and a pelvic drop on stance points to iliotibial band syndrome driven by poor proximal control. Strengthening the abductors, trimming running volume, and retraining gait address the cause. Stretching and foam rolling have already failed, an injection with full mileage ignores the overload, and patellofemoral taping treats a different condition."}, {"id": 104, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a demand cardiac pacemaker implanted in the left upper chest has chronic left shoulder and upper trapezius pain. A student wants to place the sound head on the anterior shoulder close to the device. Which statement about therapeutic ultrasound and implanted cardiac pacemakers is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ultrasound is contraindicated over the chest and device area because it may disturb pacemaker function", "B": "Ultrasound is acceptable over the shoulder when the pacemaker is a demand type", "C": "Ultrasound is contraindicated only over the generator itself, so the anterior shoulder can be treated", "D": "Ultrasound is safe near pacemakers because it is mechanical energy, unlike shortwave diathermy which is contraindicated"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ultrasound is contraindicated over the chest and device area because it may disturb pacemaker function): Therapeutic ultrasound is avoided over the chest and the pacemaker region because it may interfere with device function. Restricting the caution to the generator alone leaves the anterior shoulder too close. Demand type does not change that. Shortwave diathermy is indeed contraindicated, but that fact does not make ultrasound safe near the device."}, {"id": 105, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "During a case discussion, a chiropractic student is asked to describe the mechanism by which low-intensity pulsed ultrasound is thought to speed soft tissue repair in an acute wound without meaningfully heating the tissue. Which mechanism best explains this nonthermal effect?", "options": {"A": "Stable cavitation and acoustic streaming that alter cell membrane permeability", "B": "Direct depolarization of motor nerve axons causing visible muscle contraction", "C": "Conversion of sound energy to heat that raises tissue temperature by about 4 degrees", "D": "Unstable cavitation with bubble collapse that releases energy into the tissue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stable cavitation and acoustic streaming that alter cell membrane permeability): Nonthermal effects are attributed to stable cavitation, in which gas bubbles oscillate, and to acoustic streaming, which moves fluid near cell membranes and changes their permeability. Heating is the thermal mechanism, and nerve depolarization belongs to electrical stimulation. Unstable cavitation is a hazard that is avoided."}, {"id": 106, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "During a metabolic cart test, a 50-year-old man cycling steadily at a moderate workload produces a respiratory exchange ratio of 0.72. His physiotherapist reviews what fuel mix this ratio reflects. Carbon dioxide produced divided by oxygen consumed is recorded every 30 seconds, and the ratio has been stable for five minutes. Which interpretation is best supported by that value?", "options": {"A": "Energy is supplied mainly by anaerobic glycolysis, with buffered carbon dioxide inflating the ratio", "B": "Energy is supplied mostly by fat oxidation, with a small carbohydrate contribution", "C": "Nearly all of the energy comes from carbohydrate oxidation at this workload", "D": "Energy is supplied about equally by carbohydrate and fat oxidation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Energy is supplied mostly by fat oxidation, with a small carbohydrate contribution): The respiratory exchange ratio is about 0.70 when fat is the only fuel burned and 1.0 when carbohydrate is. A steady value of 0.72 therefore points to mostly fat, with a small carbohydrate share. A midpoint near 0.85 would indicate an even mix. Anaerobic metabolism pushes the ratio above 1.0 through buffered carbon dioxide, which is not seen here."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with a peripheral nerve injury attempts hip flexion in a supine gravity-eliminated position. The examiner sees no movement of the limb but palpates a flicker of contraction in the muscle when the patient tries to move. What manual muscle test grade is recorded?", "options": {"A": "Grade 0, zero, because no joint motion occurred during the attempt", "B": "Grade 1, trace, because a contraction is palpable without joint movement", "C": "Grade 3, fair, because a contraction was palpated against gravity", "D": "Grade 2, poor, because the muscle contracted in a gravity eliminated position"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Grade 1, trace, because a contraction is palpable without joint movement): A palpable or visible contraction without joint motion is grade 1 (trace). Grade 0 means no contraction can be felt at all, and here a flicker was palpated. Grade 2 requires full range in the gravity-eliminated position, which did not happen. Grade 3 requires full range against gravity."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinician examines a 42-year-old accountant with low back and left leg pain using repeated end-range movements. During repeated lumbar extension in lying, the pain in the calf retreats toward the midline of the lower back while the back ache remains. Which term describes this response in the McKenzie system?", "options": {"A": "Centralization, a favorable response supporting continued use of that movement direction", "B": "Peripheralization, an unfavorable response signaling that the movement direction should be abandoned", "C": "Dysfunction syndrome, mechanical end-range pain that does not change location with repeated movement", "D": "Lateral shift, a frontal plane deviation that must be corrected before extension"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Centralization, a favorable response supporting continued use of that movement direction): Centralization is the movement of the most distal referred symptoms toward the spine during repeated movements, and it predicts a good outcome. It identifies the direction of preference to prescribe. Peripheralization is the opposite pattern, with symptoms spreading distally. A lateral shift is a visible postural finding, and dysfunction syndrome produces end-range pain that stays in one place."}, {"id": 109, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist plans a progression for a patient with poor trunk control using the Sullivan and Rood framework of motor control stages. The therapist wants to confirm the order of the four stages before charting goals for the next several weeks of care. Which sequence lists the stages from earliest to most advanced?", "options": {"A": "Stability first, then mobility, then skill, and finally controlled mobility", "B": "Mobility first, then stability, then controlled mobility, and finally skill", "C": "Mobility first, then controlled mobility, then stability, and finally skill", "D": "Stability first, then controlled mobility, then mobility, and finally skill"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mobility first, then stability, then controlled mobility, and finally skill): The stages run from mobility, which is available range and initiation, to stability, which is co-contraction holding in a posture, to controlled mobility, which is moving within a weight-bearing posture, and finally skill, which is coordinated distal movement. Each stage builds on the one before it. Controlled mobility cannot come before stability because moving in a posture requires holding it first. Skill is last."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "At the end of a patient's passive hamstring range, the therapist asks for a concentric hamstring contraction against moderate resistance for six seconds, then relaxation. The patient then actively contracts the quadriceps while the therapist takes up the new range. Which PNF technique is being used?", "options": {"A": "Contract-relax followed by an agonist contraction, sometimes called contract-relax-agonist-contract", "B": "Slow reversal hold followed by alternating concentric contractions of the hamstrings and quadriceps", "C": "Rhythmic stabilization followed by sustained passive stretching without further muscle contraction", "D": "Hold-relax followed by a repeated isometric contraction of the hamstring muscle group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contract-relax followed by an agonist contraction, sometimes called contract-relax-agonist-contract): A concentric contraction of the tight muscle against resistance, followed by relaxation, is contract-relax, and the added quadriceps contraction adds the agonist contraction phase that uses reciprocal inhibition. Hold-relax would use an isometric hamstring contraction, and slow reversal hold alternates contractions without a passive stretch. Rhythmic stabilization uses isometric alternating resistance and is not used to take up new range."}, {"id": 111, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old secretary with eight months of neck stiffness, no night pain and no inflammatory signs wants to restore motion before a family wedding. Her session starts with a modality chosen to prepare tissue for movement. Which sequence is best?", "options": {"A": "Moist heat for fifteen minutes, then gentle stretching and active motion exercises", "B": "Cold pack for fifteen minutes, then gentle stretching and active motion exercises", "C": "Moist heat for fifteen minutes, then complete rest to let the warmed tissue settle", "D": "Low intensity pulsed ultrasound over the neck, then gentle stretching and active motion exercises"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Moist heat for fifteen minutes, then gentle stretching and active motion exercises): A chronic stiff neck without inflammation responds to heat that raises tissue extensibility, followed right away by gentle stretching and active motion. Cold before stretching lowers extensibility and suits acute irritation instead. Heat followed by rest wastes the window of increased extensibility. Low intensity pulsed ultrasound is largely nonthermal, so it does not prepare the tissue for movement as moist heat does."}, {"id": 112, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient wants to return to recreational running after a hamstring strain. The clinician designs the final phase of training so that the exercises reflect the speed, muscle action, and movement patterns of running. Which training principle is the clinician applying when selecting exercises that resemble the target activity?", "options": {"A": "Progressive overload, meaning the load is gradually increased beyond current capacity", "B": "Specificity of training, meaning adaptations reflect the demands imposed", "C": "Reversibility, meaning gains are lost when the training stimulus is withdrawn", "D": "Individuality, meaning each person responds differently to the same training stimulus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Specificity of training, meaning adaptations reflect the demands imposed): Specificity, or the SAID principle, states that the body adapts specifically to the demands placed on it, so training should resemble the speed, contraction type, and pattern of the goal activity. Overload concerns how much stress is applied. Reversibility concerns detraining. Individuality concerns differing responses between people."}, {"id": 113, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old distance runner reports deep groin pain that has built over three weeks and now hurts at night. Hopping on the right leg reproduces pain, and hip internal rotation and the fulcrum test are painful. She wants a modified running plan. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Begin hip abductor strengthening and taper mileage gradually over several weeks while still running", "B": "Stop weight bearing activity and arrange urgent imaging for suspected femoral neck stress fracture", "C": "Apply thermal ultrasound to the groin and retest hopping after one week of rest", "D": "Treat as a hip flexor strain with stretching and continue running with ice applied afterward"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop weight bearing activity and arrange urgent imaging for suspected femoral neck stress fracture): Groin pain with night pain, pain on hopping, and a positive fulcrum test in a runner suggests a femoral neck stress fracture, which can displace and cause avascular necrosis if loaded. Protection from weight bearing and urgent imaging are required. Strain treatment, gradual tapering, and modalities delay the diagnosis."}, {"id": 114, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old man is two weeks after open carpal tunnel release. The sutures are out, the incision is healing, and he has mild tenderness over the scar and palm. He asks whether he should keep the wrist in a splint. Which program is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Scar massage, early active finger and wrist motion, and tendon and nerve gliding exercises", "B": "Continuous wrist immobilization in a splint for six more weeks to protect the released ligament", "C": "Aggressive passive wrist flexion stretching to stretch the incision and prevent scar tethering", "D": "Heavy putty grip strengthening started immediately to restore strength lost during early recovery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Scar massage, early active finger and wrist motion, and tendon and nerve gliding exercises): Two weeks after release, scar massage with early active motion and tendon and nerve gliding limits adhesions and stiffness. The transverse carpal ligament is divided, not repaired, so long immobilization adds stiffness without protecting anything. Heavy putty grip stresses a tender palm too soon. Aggressive flexion stretching can irritate the incision and provoke scar reaction."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "During a physiology lecture on carbohydrate metabolism, a student asks which enzyme acts as the main regulatory, rate-limiting step of glycolysis and is stimulated by rising ADP, AMP and inorganic phosphate in working muscle. The instructor wants the enzyme tied to the committed step of the pathway. Which enzyme is it?", "options": {"A": "Hexokinase, the enzyme that traps glucose and commits it to the pathway", "B": "Pyruvate kinase, the enzyme that catalyzes the final step and produces ATP", "C": "Phosphofructokinase, the enzyme catalyzing the committed step of glycolysis", "D": "Glycogen phosphorylase, the enzyme that releases glucose units from stored glycogen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Phosphofructokinase, the enzyme catalyzing the committed step of glycolysis): Phosphofructokinase converts fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, the committed and rate-limiting step, and is stimulated by ADP, AMP and phosphate. Hexokinase acts earlier and is inhibited by its own product but does not commit the molecule to glycolysis. Pyruvate kinase acts at the last step and is not the main controller. Glycogen phosphorylase supplies glucose from glycogen and belongs to a separate pathway."}, {"id": 116, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with knee osteoarthritis presents with a flare: the knee is warm with a moderate effusion, pain is 7 out of 10, and morning stiffness lasts under 30 minutes. She has been walking daily. Which plan best fits this flare?", "options": {"A": "Rigid brace immobilization for several weeks until the effusion has fully cleared", "B": "Cryotherapy, relative rest from impact, and gentle pain free motion with isometric quadriceps work", "C": "Moist heat followed by progressive resisted open chain knee extension to the limit of motion", "D": "Continue the daily walking program and add stair climbing to maintain function through the flare"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cryotherapy, relative rest from impact, and gentle pain free motion with isometric quadriceps work): A warm effused knee in a flare needs cooling, relative rest from impact, and gentle pain free motion with isometrics to keep quadriceps activation. Prolonged rigid bracing promotes stiffness and weakness. Heat and heavy resisted extension aggravate an inflamed joint. Adding stair climbing to the walking program keeps loading a flaring knee, which suits stable osteoarthritis but not this flare."}, {"id": 117, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A UV lamp delivers the target dose in 4 minutes when its beam strikes a flat skin surface perpendicularly. The therapist must treat a sloped area of the shoulder where the beam meets the skin at 60 degrees from the perpendicular, with the lamp distance unchanged. Using the cosine law, what exposure time delivers the same dose?", "options": {"A": "Increase the exposure to 8 minutes at the unchanged lamp distance", "B": "Increase the exposure to 6 minutes at the unchanged lamp distance", "C": "Keep the exposure at 4 minutes at the unchanged lamp distance", "D": "Decrease the exposure to 2 minutes at the unchanged lamp distance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Increase the exposure to 8 minutes at the unchanged lamp distance): At 60 degrees the cosine is 0.5, so the surface receives half the perpendicular intensity. Time must therefore be divided by the cosine: 4 minutes divided by 0.5 equals 8 minutes. Keeping 4 minutes underdoses by half. Multiplying by the cosine gives 2 minutes, which makes the underdose worse, and adding 50 percent gives 6 minutes, which is still short."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A chiropractic clinic wants to estimate aerobic fitness in a 58-year-old sedentary woman who is uncomfortable with running and with maximal testing. The clinician selects the Rockport one-mile walk test. Besides the time taken to finish the mile, which additional measurements are entered into the Rockport prediction equation?", "options": {"A": "Age, sex, height, and resting blood pressure before the walk begins", "B": "Age, sex, body weight, and heart rate immediately at the end of the walk", "C": "Sex, height, and the rating of perceived exertion reported after the walk", "D": "Age, body weight, and the heart rate taken one minute before the walk starts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Age, sex, body weight, and heart rate immediately at the end of the walk): The Rockport equation uses age, sex, body weight, walking time, and the heart rate measured right at the finish of the mile. The end of walk heart rate reflects how hard the heart worked at that pace. Resting blood pressure, height, and RPE are not variables in the equation. A pre-test heart rate would not capture the response to the walk."}, {"id": 119, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 19 year old with moderate acne completed an MED test 3 weeks ago. Since then her physician started doxycycline 100 mg daily. At today's session the therapist plans to use the dose calculated from the earlier MED. The patient reports no skin changes. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Increase the earlier dose because the drug thickens the epidermis", "B": "Proceed with the earlier dose and watch for redness after treatment", "C": "Repeat the MED test, because the drug may have lowered her MED", "D": "Use the earlier dose but shorten the exposure by one half"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the MED test, because the drug may have lowered her MED): Tetracycline class antibiotics such as doxycycline are photosensitizers and can lower the MED substantially. The earlier MED no longer describes her skin, so it should be measured again. A guessed reduction might still burn or underdose, and waiting for redness risks an avoidable burn. The drug does not thicken the epidermis or raise tolerance."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 49-year-old nurse has medial heel pain on her first steps each morning. Ankle dorsiflexion measures 3 degrees with the knee straight but improves to 14 degrees with the knee flexed. Which stretch addresses the restriction identified by this finding?", "options": {"A": "Anterior tibialis stretching with the ankle held in plantarflexion", "B": "Soleus stretch with the back knee bent, held for thirty seconds twice daily", "C": "Towel toe curls to strengthen the intrinsic muscles of the foot", "D": "Standing gastrocnemius stretch with the back knee straight and heel down"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Standing gastrocnemius stretch with the back knee straight and heel down): Dorsiflexion that improves when the knee is flexed shows the two joint gastrocnemius is tight, because flexing the knee slackens it. A straight knee calf stretch targets that muscle, and tight gastrocnemius increases plantar fascia strain. The soleus is a single joint muscle and would not limit motion differently with knee position, and toe curls and tibialis stretching do not address the finding."}, {"id": 121, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician programs pulsed ultrasound at a duty cycle of 20 percent with a spatial average peak intensity of 2.0 W/cm2 during the on-time. The student records the temporal average intensity delivered over the entire pulse period for the chart. What average intensity should be recorded?", "options": {"A": "1.0 W/cm2, found by halving the peak intensity for pulsed output", "B": "2.0 W/cm2, because the unit displays the same intensity whether pulsed or continuous", "C": "10 W/cm2, found by dividing the peak intensity by the duty cycle", "D": "0.4 W/cm2, found by multiplying the peak intensity by the duty cycle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (0.4 W/cm2, found by multiplying the peak intensity by the duty cycle): Averaged across on and off periods, intensity is the peak value times the fraction of time the sound is on, so 2.0 times 0.20 equals 0.4 W/cm2. Dividing by 0.20 would inflate the figure to 10. The displayed peak intensity overstates the energy actually delivered per unit time."}, {"id": 122, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A physical therapy clinic stocks a germicidal quartz lamp for wound care. A new assistant asks which part of the spectrum gives the strongest bacteria killing action, with a peak near 254 nm, and why the lamp is kept away from intact skin. Which band does the germicidal lamp emit?", "options": {"A": "UVA, which spans roughly 320 to 400 nanometers, the longest ultraviolet band", "B": "UVC, which spans roughly 200 to 290 nanometers, the shortest ultraviolet band", "C": "Near infrared, which spans roughly 780 to 1,400 nanometers, beyond visible red light", "D": "UVB, which spans roughly 290 to 320 nanometers, the sunburn producing band"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (UVC, which spans roughly 200 to 290 nanometers, the shortest ultraviolet band): Germicidal lamps emit mostly UVC, roughly 200 to 290 nanometers, with a strong peak near 254 nanometers that damages bacterial nucleic acids. UVB produces erythema and is used for psoriasis but is not the bacteria killing band. UVA is weakly erythemal and needs a photosensitizer for therapy. Near infrared is a heating band and has no germicidal action."}, {"id": 123, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 30-year-old recreational runner with no injuries wants a simple static stretching routine for the hamstrings to improve flexibility. Following common exercise guidelines for healthy adults, a chiropractic student is asked to prescribe the hold time and number of repetitions for each stretch. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Hold each stretch about 5 seconds and repeat ten to fifteen times in a bouncing rhythm", "B": "Hold each stretch for 60 seconds and repeat six to eight times for permanent lengthening", "C": "Hold each stretch about 10 seconds and repeat five to six times per muscle group", "D": "Hold each stretch about 30 seconds and repeat two to four times per muscle group"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hold each stretch about 30 seconds and repeat two to four times per muscle group): Exercise guidelines for healthy adults advise holding static stretches roughly 15 to 30 seconds, with about 30 seconds common, repeated two to four times per muscle group. Very short or bouncing holds do not allow enough creep and stress relaxation to build range. Ten-second holds are closer to guidelines but still fall short of the accepted dose. Sixty-second holds repeated many times add time and fatigue without extra benefit."}, {"id": 124, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 63-year-old anxious man has lumbar paraspinal spasm after lifting and is tense and guarded when touched. The therapist wants a massage plan that will reduce muscle tone and calm the patient. He has no neurologic findings. Which plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Slow, rhythmic effleurage and petrissage, using a calm tempo", "B": "Deep transverse friction to the spasmed muscle, applied until the tissue releases", "C": "Firm, rapid tapotement with cupping over the paraspinals to stimulate the muscle", "D": "Rapid, light feather-touch stroking over the back to distract from the pain"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow, rhythmic effleurage and petrissage, using a calm tempo): Slow, rhythmic effleurage and petrissage lower muscle tone and calm an anxious, guarded patient. Deep friction suits localized adhesions and would increase guarding here. Tapotement is stimulating, and fast light stroking is too superficial and too quick to relax a spasmed muscle."}, {"id": 125, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 49-year-old man bends forward and reaches only to mid shin. Straight leg raise measures 85 degrees on both sides, the hips flex well, and the lumbar curve stays flat with very little flexion during the bend. Which finding best explains the limited reach, and what is the best focus?", "options": {"A": "Short hamstrings, so add prolonged hamstring stretching three times daily", "B": "Weak hip flexors, so add loaded hip flexion strengthening exercises", "C": "Reduced lumbar flexion mobility, so add lumbar flexion mobility drills", "D": "Reduced hip flexion mobility, so mobilize the hip capsule posteriorly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduced lumbar flexion mobility, so add lumbar flexion mobility drills): A raise of 85 degrees is normal and the hips flex well, so neither the hamstrings nor the hip limits the reach. The flat lumbar curve shows the missing motion is lumbar flexion, which is the target of the mobility work. Strengthening hip flexors does not address a motion limit."}, {"id": 126, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient in a sports clinic is using the Daily Adjustable Progressive Resistance Exercise (DAPRE) technique for the quadriceps. The therapist reviews how the program decides each next session's working weight, since it differs from the fixed percentages of DeLorme. What determines the adjustment?", "options": {"A": "The first warm-up set performed at half the working weight, guiding the next load", "B": "Performance in the fourth working set, which guides the load for the next session", "C": "Repetitions completed in the second set at three quarters of the working weight", "D": "A fixed ten percent increase whenever every prescribed repetition is completed that day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Performance in the fourth working set, which guides the load for the next session): In DAPRE the repetitions achieved in the third set decide the weight used for the fourth set, and the repetitions achieved in the fourth set decide the load for the next session. This makes the program responsive to daily performance rather than fixed percentages. The first warm-up set is submaximal and gives little information, a second-set count is not the deciding set, and a fixed increment ignores how the patient performed that day."}, {"id": 127, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats a 29 year old violinist's jaw pain over the temporomandibular joint with an 810 nm class 3B probe. The patient asks whether keeping both eyelids tightly closed is enough protection, so goggles are not needed. Which response reflects correct laser safety practice for this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Goggles are required, since eyelids still transmit near-infrared light to the retina", "B": "Goggles are required only within 5 cm of the orbit at power above 500 mW", "C": "Closed eyelids suffice when the probe stays in contact with the skin near the eye", "D": "A white cloth over the eyes suffices because it diffuses the beam before it reaches them"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Goggles are required, since eyelids still transmit near-infrared light to the retina): Eyelids transmit a meaningful fraction of near-infrared light, so closing them leaves the retina exposed. Treatment near the face makes scattered light likely whatever the probe position. No distance or power rule waives eyewear for a class 3B device, and cloth is not a rated barrier."}, {"id": 128, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "An above-knee prosthesis wearer swings the prosthetic limb outward in an arc during every swing phase. His pelvis stays level and the opposite heel stays on the floor throughout stance. The therapist labels the deviation. Which label is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Circumduction, an outward arc of the swing limb with no pelvic elevation", "B": "Hip hiking, elevating the pelvis on the swing side to shorten the limb", "C": "Vaulting, rising on the toes of the sound limb during stance", "D": "Steppage, lifting the knee excessively to avoid dragging the foot"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Circumduction, an outward arc of the swing limb with no pelvic elevation): The three compensations all give floor clearance to a limb that does not shorten well. Here the pelvis is level and the sound heel stays on the floor, which excludes hip hiking and vaulting, and the limb arcs outward, which defines circumduction. Steppage uses extra hip and knee flexion."}, {"id": 129, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old has diffuse bruising and swelling over the anterior thigh two weeks after a contusion. The therapist wants to apply kinesiology tape in a fan pattern to assist fluid clearance. How much stretch should be applied to the fan strips?", "options": {"A": "Moderate stretch of 50 percent, with the tails fanned away from the node basin", "B": "Very light stretch, roughly 0 to 15 percent, with the tails fanned toward a node basin", "C": "Light stretch of 25 percent on the base, with the tails fanned across the bruise", "D": "No stretch on the tails, with one straight strip applied over the bruise center"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Very light stretch, roughly 0 to 15 percent, with the tails fanned toward a node basin): Lymphatic applications use very light tension, about 0 to 15 percent, so the skin is lifted slightly and the tails direct fluid toward a lymph node basin. Moderate stretch is a mechanical correction tension and a fan pointed away from the nodes sends fluid the wrong way. A quarter stretch is too tight for drainage, and a single straight strip does not create a fan channel for fluid."}, {"id": 130, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A patient asks how to take off kinesiology tape at home after four days because the skin is sensitive. The student wants the method that is least likely to tear skin or pull hair. How should the tape be removed?", "options": {"A": "Lift one end and pull it straight up and away from the skin perpendicular", "B": "Pull off quickly against hair growth, so the adhesive releases in one motion", "C": "Soak the area in hot water until the tape falls off without peeling", "D": "Peel slowly in the direction of hair growth while holding the skin down"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Peel slowly in the direction of hair growth while holding the skin down): Slow removal, with the tape folded back on itself and parallel to the skin, along the direction of hair growth, while the other hand supports the skin, lowers the chance of skin tears and hair pulling. Fast removal against hair growth and pulling straight up both increase skin trauma. Prolonged hot soaking can macerate the skin."}, {"id": 131, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 63 year old man with controlled hypertension has acute low back muscle spasm after yard work. The therapist plans a large cold pack over the lumbar and flank area for 15 minutes in sitting. What precaution is most appropriate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Apply for only five minutes and skip monitoring, since brief cooling cannot change pressure", "B": "Protect the skin with a thick towel, since frostbite is the main risk in hypertension", "C": "Screen for cold intolerance and Raynaud signs first, since cold sensitivity is the key risk", "D": "Monitor blood pressure, since extensive cold can cause a sympathetic pressor response"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Monitor blood pressure, since extensive cold can cause a sympathetic pressor response): Cooling a large area triggers sympathetic vasoconstriction that can raise blood pressure, so a patient with hypertension needs monitoring. A brief exposure still stimulates cold receptors and does not remove the need for checks. Towel protection and screening for cold intolerance are sensible, but they address skin injury and cold hypersensitivity rather than the pressor response that matters in this patient."}, {"id": 132, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 75 kg male triathlete has a laboratory-measured absolute VO2 max of 4.5 liters per minute. A chiropractor wants to compare him with published normative tables that report relative oxygen uptake rather than absolute uptake. What is his relative VO2 max in mL/kg/min?", "options": {"A": "60 mL/kg/min, from 4,500 mL per minute divided by 75 kg", "B": "0.06 mL/kg/min, from 4.5 liters per minute divided by 75 kg", "C": "27 mL/kg/min, from 4,500 mL per minute divided by his body weight of 165 pounds", "D": "80 mL/kg/min, from 4,500 mL per minute divided by 56 kg estimated lean body mass"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (60 mL/kg/min, from 4,500 mL per minute divided by 75 kg): Relative VO2 max is absolute uptake in milliliters per minute divided by body mass in kilograms: 4,500 divided by 75 gives 60. Dividing liters by kilograms leaves the wrong magnitude. Using pounds understates the result, and dividing by lean mass is a different metric from the per-kilogram body mass norms in the tables."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 62-year-old retired smoker has shoulder and medial arm pain in a C8-T1 pattern, weakness of the hand intrinsics, a drooping right eyelid with a small pupil, and unexplained weight loss of 15 lb over two months. Neck exercises have not helped. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Cervical traction trial for three weeks with C8 nerve gliding and chin tuck exercises", "B": "Urgent referral for chest imaging and evaluation of a possible apical lung tumor", "C": "Thoracic outlet program with scalene stretching and first rib mobilization twice weekly", "D": "Rotator cuff strengthening with therapeutic ultrasound to the supraspinatus tendon daily"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urgent referral for chest imaging and evaluation of a possible apical lung tumor): Shoulder and medial arm pain with C8-T1 deficits, Horner syndrome and weight loss in a smoker suggest a Pancoast tumor invading the lower brachial plexus and sympathetic chain. This is a red flag requiring urgent chest imaging, not a trial of cervical or shoulder rehabilitation. Delay worsens prognosis."}, {"id": 134, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 55-year-old woman cannot elevate her right arm beyond 90 degrees. The scapula elevates early in a shrug, and passive glenohumeral elevation is also limited to about 90 degrees with a firm end feel. Which plan fits best at this stage of her care?", "options": {"A": "Glenohumeral capsule mobilization and stretching to restore passive glenohumeral elevation", "B": "Rotator cuff strengthening in the scaption plane at progressive loads", "C": "Lower trapezius strengthening to correct the shrug and encourage scapular depression", "D": "Serratus anterior strengthening with wall slides to increase scapular upward rotation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glenohumeral capsule mobilization and stretching to restore passive glenohumeral elevation): A firm limit of passive glenohumeral motion shows the shoulder joint itself restricts elevation, and the early shrug is a compensation for it. Restoring capsular motion is the priority. Strengthening scapular or cuff muscles cannot overcome a passive restriction."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 49-year-old woman has a chronic elbow flexion contracture after a fracture. Continuous 3 MHz ultrasound has been used to heat the capsule. The clinician plans to apply a prolonged stretch. When should the stretch be applied to take advantage of the thermal effect?", "options": {"A": "During the heating or immediately after it ends, while collagen extensibility remains high", "B": "Before the ultrasound, to prime the collagen for heating", "C": "After thirty minutes, once the tissue has cooled fully to baseline", "D": "After an ice pack, so that the tissue is held in a cooled elongated state"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (During the heating or immediately after it ends, while collagen extensibility remains high): Heated collagen is more extensible, and the effect fades as the tissue cools, so the stretch is applied during or right after heating. Waiting 30 minutes wastes the effect. Stretching first gives no thermal advantage, and cooling beforehand stiffens the tissue."}, {"id": 136, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 29-year-old recreational runner has medial knee pain and a navicular drop of 15 mm. On gait observation her right foot stays pronated through terminal stance and the arch remains flat as the heel rises. What gait finding does this represent?", "options": {"A": "The foot slaps down at loading response from weak dorsiflexors", "B": "The foot fails to resupinate in terminal stance, leaving a flexible lever", "C": "The heel rises early in midstance from a tight gastrocnemius", "D": "The foot supinates too much at loading response, leaving a rigid shock absorber"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The foot fails to resupinate in terminal stance, leaving a flexible lever): Normally the subtalar joint supinates in terminal stance so the midfoot locks into a rigid lever for push-off. In this runner the foot stays pronated and flat while the heel rises, so the lever stays flexible, which stresses the arch and the medial knee. Cavus supination, foot slap, and early heel rise do not fit the findings."}, {"id": 137, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A UVB unit is measured with a radiometer at 5 mW/cm2 at the treatment distance. A patient with a psoriatic plaque on the knee is to receive an exposure lasting 60 seconds. Remember that 1 mW/cm2 equals 1 mJ per second per square centimeter. What dose does this exposure deliver to the plaque?", "options": {"A": "A delivered dose of 5 millijoules per square centimeter at the plaque", "B": "A delivered dose of 30 millijoules per square centimeter at the plaque", "C": "A delivered dose of 300 millijoules per square centimeter at the plaque", "D": "A delivered dose of 65 millijoules per square centimeter at the plaque"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A delivered dose of 300 millijoules per square centimeter at the plaque): Dose equals intensity times time. Here 5 mJ per second per square centimeter times 60 seconds equals 300 mJ/cm2. Adding the two numbers gives 65, which is meaningless dimensionally. Reporting 5 ignores the exposure time, and 30 is a decimal slip."}, {"id": 138, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "During a school screening, a 12-year-old girl at Risser stage 0 shows a right rib hump on the Adams forward bend test, and the scoliometer reads 8 degrees of trunk rotation. She has no pain or neurological signs. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for a standing posteroanterior spine radiograph to measure the Cobb angle", "B": "Observe with repeat scoliometer screening every six months until skeletal maturity is reached", "C": "Start Schroth exercise therapy right away and defer radiographs until the curve progresses", "D": "Order an MRI of the whole spine to look for a neural axis abnormality"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for a standing posteroanterior spine radiograph to measure the Cobb angle): A rib hump with a scoliometer reading of 7 degrees or more calls for a standing radiograph, since the Cobb angle drives every later decision. Risser 0 means much growth remains, so six months of observation risks missed progression. Exercise therapy can be useful but should follow measurement of the curve. MRI is kept for pain, neurological signs or atypical curves, none of which are present here."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with hip osteoarthritis walks with a lateral trunk lean over the right hip during right stance, keeping the pelvis level. This compensated gait reduces the demand on the abductors. Which muscle should the rehabilitation plan target to correct the underlying weakness?", "options": {"A": "Right quadratus lumborum, strengthened in side bridges with the left side down", "B": "Right gluteus medius, strengthened in side-lying and standing hip abduction exercises", "C": "Right hip adductors, strengthened with resisted adduction against a cable", "D": "Left gluteus medius, strengthened in side-lying and standing hip abduction exercises"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right gluteus medius, strengthened in side-lying and standing hip abduction exercises): Leaning the trunk over the stance hip moves the center of mass closer to the joint, which reduces the abductor torque needed, so the lean signals a weak stance-side gluteus medius. The right side is the one to strengthen. The left gluteus medius is on the swing side, quadratus lumborum is not the primary pelvic stabilizer in stance, and adductors oppose the needed action."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient has weak shoulder flexors after a nerve injury, and the therapist applies maximal resistance to the strong trunk and scapular muscles within a diagonal pattern, noticing improved recruitment in the weaker muscles. Which proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation principle explains this effect?", "options": {"A": "Autogenic inhibition, in which tension relaxes the contracting muscle itself", "B": "Irradiation, in which strong resisted contractions spread overflow to weaker muscles", "C": "Reciprocal inhibition, in which agonist contraction relaxes the antagonist muscle", "D": "Successive induction, in which contraction of one muscle group facilitates its antagonist"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Irradiation, in which strong resisted contractions spread overflow to weaker muscles): Irradiation is the spread of facilitation from strong resisted muscles to weaker ones within the same pattern. Successive induction facilitates the antagonist after the agonist contracts, and reciprocal inhibition relaxes the antagonist during agonist contraction. Autogenic inhibition relaxes the tensed muscle itself. Only irradiation describes the weaker muscles improving."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with sarcopenia has lost fast twitch fiber size, which contributes to her slow rise from a chair. The therapist wants a resistance program that recruits Type II motor units, in line with the size principle. Which prescription best meets this goal?", "options": {"A": "Heavy loads near 80 percent of one repetition maximum, lifted with controlled but forceful effort", "B": "Moderate loads near 40 percent of one repetition maximum, ten slow repetitions well short of fatigue", "C": "Prolonged walking at 50 percent of heart rate reserve for 45 minutes on most days", "D": "Light loads near 20 percent of one repetition maximum, lifted for 15 repetitions without reaching fatigue"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Heavy loads near 80 percent of one repetition maximum, lifted with controlled but forceful effort): By the size principle, Type II units are recruited when force demand is high, so heavy loads near 80 percent of one repetition maximum with forceful intent are needed. Light loads stopped short of fatigue stay mostly within slow units. Moderate loads stopped well short of fatigue also leave Type II units largely unrecruited. Walking is mainly Type I work."}, {"id": 142, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "During a manual muscle test, a clinician explains to a student that a sensory receptor in the musculotendinous junction responds to tension generated by the contracting muscle and, when its firing is high, reduces motor neuron drive to the same muscle. Which receptor is being described, and what is the reflex response called?", "options": {"A": "Golgi tendon organ, producing autogenic inhibition of the muscle that is generating the tension", "B": "Pacinian corpuscle, producing a flexor withdrawal reflex in response to deep pressure", "C": "Muscle spindle, producing the monosynaptic stretch reflex in the muscle being lengthened", "D": "Golgi tendon organ, producing reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist muscle group across the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Golgi tendon organ, producing autogenic inhibition of the muscle that is generating the tension): The Golgi tendon organ lies in series with muscle fibers at the musculotendinous junction and monitors tension. Its Ib afferents inhibit the alpha motor neurons of the same muscle, which is autogenic inhibition. The spindle monitors length and excites the muscle, reciprocal inhibition acts on the antagonist, and a Pacinian corpuscle senses vibration and deep pressure, not muscle tension."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old weekend tennis player strained the wrist flexor mass ten days ago. Pain is 3 of 10 with use, swelling has resolved, and early scar is forming. The therapist wants a loading choice that suits this stage of healing. Which exercise is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Aggressive passive stretching to end range to lengthen the new scar and restore flexibility", "B": "Heavy eccentric wrist flexion at near-maximal load to stimulate collagen alignment in the scar", "C": "Submaximal pain-free isometric wrist flexion at several joint angles, then light active motion", "D": "Complete immobilization in a cast until pain is absent with all activities of daily use"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Submaximal pain-free isometric wrist flexion at several joint angles, then light active motion): Ten days after injury the tissue is in the proliferative phase with weak, immature collagen. Submaximal pain-free isometrics and light active motion stimulate healing without overloading it. Heavy eccentric work belongs to remodeling, aggressive stretching can disrupt the scar, and prolonged casting causes stiffness."}, {"id": 144, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old gymnast with a hamstring strain stretches in supine with the leg raised and the ankle dorsiflexed. She feels pulling behind the knee and tingling in the sole. Plantarflexing the ankle immediately eases the tingling without changing the hamstring stretch sensation. What does this finding suggest, and what is the best adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Hamstring tendon scarring is present, so apply transverse friction massage during the stretch", "B": "Joint capsule restriction limits the motion, so add grade III posterior hip mobilization", "C": "Sciatic nerve tension contributes, so stretch with the ankle plantarflexed to isolate hamstring length", "D": "Tight gastrocnemius limits the stretch, so add calf stretching with the knee extended first"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sciatic nerve tension contributes, so stretch with the ankle plantarflexed to isolate hamstring length): Tingling that eases with ankle plantarflexion while the muscle sensation stays the same is a neural tension response, since dorsiflexion adds tension to the sciatic nerve. Stretching with the ankle plantarflexed removes neural tension so the hamstring length can be addressed. Calf shortness, scar tissue, or a capsular restriction would not produce a symptom that changes with ankle position alone."}, {"id": 145, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 46-year-old teacher reports sharp heel pain with her first steps out of bed each morning that eases after a few minutes of walking, then returns after long periods of standing. Palpation finds tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle. Which mechanism explains why passive great toe extension reproduces her pain?", "options": {"A": "Toe extension stretches the tibial nerve through the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus", "B": "Toe extension winds the plantar fascia around the metatarsal heads and tightens it at the heel", "C": "Toe extension compresses the flexor hallucis longus tendon against the posterior talus bone", "D": "Toe extension loads the Achilles tendon insertion by pulling the calcaneus into plantarflexion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Toe extension winds the plantar fascia around the metatarsal heads and tightens it at the heel): Extending the great toe shortens the distance between the metatarsal heads and the heel through the windlass mechanism, raising tension in the plantar fascia at the calcaneal origin. That reproduces pain in plantar fasciitis and supports the diagnosis. The flexor hallucis longus, tibial nerve, and Achilles tendon are not loaded by this motion in the same way."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 41-year-old driver is rear-ended at a stoplight and develops neck pain that same afternoon. Examination shows reduced cervical motion and point tenderness over the paraspinals, while reflexes, strength and sensation in both arms are normal. Using the Quebec Task Force classification of whiplash associated disorders, which grade describes this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Grade I, a neck complaint with pain or stiffness but no physical signs found", "B": "Grade IV, a neck complaint with fracture or dislocation confirmed on imaging", "C": "Grade II, a neck complaint with objective musculoskeletal findings but no neurological deficit", "D": "Grade III, a neck complaint with neurological signs such as diminished reflexes, weakness or sensory deficit"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade II, a neck complaint with objective musculoskeletal findings but no neurological deficit): Grade II requires neck pain plus musculoskeletal signs such as decreased range of motion and point tenderness. Grade I has symptoms only, Grade III adds neurological findings such as reduced reflexes or weakness, and Grade IV is reserved for fracture or dislocation. This patient has objective musculoskeletal signs and a normal neurological exam, so Grade II fits."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old man with treated hypertension and a resting blood pressure of 148 over 92 starts a lower extremity strengthening program. He tends to hold his breath when lifting. Which instruction best protects him from excessive blood pressure spikes during resistance exercise?", "options": {"A": "Take a deep breath before each lift and hold it through the effort", "B": "Use light loads with high repetitions and hold each contraction for ten seconds", "C": "Lift the maximal load for three to five repetitions to shorten each set", "D": "Exhale during the lifting effort and avoid sustained maximal isometric holds"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Exhale during the lifting effort and avoid sustained maximal isometric holds): Exhaling during the lifting effort avoids the Valsalva maneuver, and avoiding maximal sustained isometric holds limits blood pressure spikes in a man whose pressure is already elevated. Holding the breath before and during the lift raises intrathoracic and arterial pressure. Long isometric holds, even at light load, raise pressure through continuous muscle compression of vessels. Near maximal loads strain the cardiovascular system and are poorly suited to him."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 5-year-old boy walks on his toes at home and in the clinic. He can place his heels down when asked, passive ankle dorsiflexion reaches 15 degrees past neutral, and reflexes and tone are normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Spastic diplegic cerebral palsy with equinus from gastrocnemius spasticity", "B": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy with early calf shortening and weakness", "C": "Fixed Achilles tendon contracture limiting ankle dorsiflexion range", "D": "Idiopathic toe walking, with preserved ankle motion and no neurological signs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Idiopathic toe walking, with preserved ankle motion and no neurological signs): A child who can walk with the heels down on request, has normal tone and reflexes, and has full passive dorsiflexion most likely has idiopathic toe walking. Cerebral palsy would show increased tone or hyperreflexia, a contracture would limit passive range, and dystrophy would show proximal weakness."}, {"id": 149, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with chronic ankle pain is being treated at a lateral joint line with a class 3B probe emitting a continuous 250 mW. The treatment plan specifies a delivered energy of 5 J at this point. A student asks the chiropractor how long the probe should stay on that point. What exposure time is correct?", "options": {"A": "50 s, found by dividing 5 J by 0.1 W", "B": "1.25 s, found by multiplying 5 J by 0.25 W", "C": "20 s, found by dividing 5 J by 0.25 W", "D": "200 s, found by dividing 5 J by 0.025 W"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (20 s, found by dividing 5 J by 0.25 W): Time equals energy divided by power. With 5 J and 0.25 W the result is 20 s. Multiplying instead of dividing gives a meaningless 1.25, a decimal slip gives ten times too long, and a 100 mW assumption does not match the stated output."}, {"id": 150, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 9-year-old girl with spastic diplegia from cerebral palsy walks with stiff legs and tight hip adductors. As each leg swings forward it passes across the midline so the knees and thighs touch or cross. Which gait pattern is this?", "options": {"A": "Circumduction gait, with the swing leg arcing outward around a stiff knee", "B": "Waddling gait, with the pelvis dropping alternately from bilateral abductor weakness", "C": "Scissoring gait, with the swing limbs crossing the midline", "D": "Crouch gait, with persistent hip and knee flexion throughout stance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Scissoring gait, with the swing limbs crossing the midline): Tight hip adductors drag each swinging limb across the midline, which defines scissoring gait. Circumduction is an outward arc seen after stroke. Waddling comes from abductor weakness and a dropping pelvis. Crouch gait is common in diplegia but shows persistent flexion in stance rather than crossing limbs."}, {"id": 151, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with a lumbar T-score of -3.0 has increased thoracic kyphosis and a recent bone density report. She has no fracture history and wants posture exercises for her upper back and a stronger posture. Which exercise choice is best?", "options": {"A": "Seated weighted twisting to mobilize the thoracic spine", "B": "Forward bending toe touches to stretch the hamstrings in standing", "C": "Weighted crunches to strengthen the abdominal muscles with a resistance band", "D": "Standing or prone thoracic extension with scapular retraction, avoiding loaded spinal flexion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Standing or prone thoracic extension with scapular retraction, avoiding loaded spinal flexion): Patients with osteoporosis should avoid loaded spinal flexion and twisting because they raise the risk of vertebral compression fractures. Extension and scapular retraction strengthen the back extensors and counter the kyphotic posture. Crunches, toe touches, and weighted twists load the spine in the wrong direction."}, {"id": 152, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 61-year-old man is on hospital day three after an uncomplicated anterior myocardial infarction treated with stenting. He is hemodynamically stable on telemetry and is walking the corridor with nursing staff. In the standard phased model of cardiac rehabilitation, which phase describes the program he is currently receiving?", "options": {"A": "Phase I, the inpatient stage built around early mobilization, low-level activity, and discharge education", "B": "Phase III, a later outpatient stage with reduced supervision and greater independence", "C": "Phase II, the supervised outpatient stage with continuous ECG telemetry and monitored exercise sessions", "D": "Phase IV, the long-term community maintenance stage focused on lifelong self-directed training"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Phase I, the inpatient stage built around early mobilization, low-level activity, and discharge education): Phase I covers the hospital stay and centers on early, low-level mobilization, risk factor education, and discharge planning. The patient is still an inpatient, so any outpatient phase is premature. Telemetry is used in hospital too, so its presence does not make this the supervised outpatient stage. Later phases focus on progressive conditioning and finally long-term maintenance."}, {"id": 153, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old carpenter with chronic nonspecific low back pain has good flexibility but poor trunk endurance on testing. His clinician selects exercises that build spinal stability while limiting compressive load on the lumbar discs. Which combination best matches the McGill stabilization exercises for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Seated hamstring stretch, prone press-ups, and lumbar flexion on a stability ball", "B": "Full sit-ups with feet anchored, double leg raises, and weighted trunk twists", "C": "Modified curl-up, side bridge, and bird dog with brief isometric holds", "D": "Loaded back extensions, Russian twists, and bent-over rows performed to fatigue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Modified curl-up, side bridge, and bird dog with brief isometric holds): The McGill big three train the abdominal wall, lateral stabilizers, and extensors using low spinal load and short isometric holds with a neutral spine. This builds endurance without repeated spinal flexion under compression. Full sit-ups and weighted twists load the discs heavily. Flexibility drills and fatigue-level loaded extensions do not match the stabilization goal."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old woman, 28 weeks pregnant, reports deep pain over the posterior pelvis and sacroiliac region that worsens with stair climbing, rolling in bed, and single-leg stance. Lumbar range of motion is pain free. A pelvic girdle pain protocol is started. Which device is commonly used to reduce her pain during weight-bearing activities?", "options": {"A": "A semirigid thoracolumbar brace extending from the sternal notch to the sacrum", "B": "An abdominal binder fitted high over the lower ribs", "C": "A pelvic support belt fitted low, below the anterior superior iliac spines", "D": "A rigid lumbar corset with rear steel stays fitted at the waist"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A pelvic support belt fitted low, below the anterior superior iliac spines): A belt placed low around the pelvis compresses the sacroiliac joints and improves force closure during loading. That reduces pain with stairs and single-leg tasks. Spinal braces and corsets act on the lumbar and thoracic spine, not the pelvic ring. A binder high over the ribs also gives no sacroiliac compression."}, {"id": 155, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old man sways about equally with eyes open and closed, walks with a wide base, and cannot walk heel to toe. Romberg sway does not increase when he closes his eyes, and ankle reflexes are normal. Which lesion localization best fits this?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral vestibular hair cell loss from ototoxic drugs", "B": "Midline cerebellar involvement producing truncal ataxia and sway", "C": "Peripheral neuropathy with absent ankle reflexes and distal sensory loss", "D": "Posterior column dysfunction impairing joint position sense in both legs"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Midline cerebellar involvement producing truncal ataxia and sway): Sway that is equal with eyes open and closed, a wide based gait, and failure on tandem walking point to a cerebellar midline lesion rather than a sensory deficit. Posterior column disease or neuropathy worsens with eye closure. Vestibular loss typically produces unsteadiness mainly when vision and surface cues are removed."}, {"id": 156, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 47-year-old bookkeeper with mild lower cervical radiculopathy improved with clinic traction and wants an over-the-door unit with a water bag for home use. The therapist reviews the setup and warning signs. Which instruction is most appropriate for the patient to follow during home cervical traction?", "options": {"A": "Sit slightly flexed, use a light weight, and stop if symptoms spread", "B": "Sit with the neck extended and begin with 25 lb to reach separation quickly", "C": "Lie supine with the neck flexed and begin at 25 lb for a supervised-level pull", "D": "Sit slightly flexed, use a light weight, and keep pulling through any increase in arm symptoms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sit slightly flexed, use a light weight, and stop if symptoms spread): Home cervical traction should use a seated posture with slight neck flexion, a light weight, and short sessions, and the patient should stop if arm symptoms spread or dizziness occurs. Extension narrows the foramina and a 25 lb start is too heavy without supervision, even in a flexed supine position. Continuing through worsening arm symptoms ignores the key warning sign that the pull is aggravating the nerve root."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist examines a 38-year-old whose elbow was fractured years ago and now lacks 25 degrees of extension. Passive overpressure meets an abrupt, hard stop with no give and no pain. Which interpretation and plan best fits this end feel?", "options": {"A": "A bony block, so stretching will not increase range and should not be forced", "B": "A shortened biceps muscle, so contract-relax stretching should restore the missing extension", "C": "Muscle guarding, so heat and gentle stretching should allow the elbow to reach full extension", "D": "A tight joint capsule, so graded mobilization and sustained stretching should restore the range"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A bony block, so stretching will not increase range and should not be forced): An abrupt, hard stop with no give is a bony end feel, and here it reflects old fracture changes that physically block motion. Stretching or forceful overpressure cannot lengthen bone and can injure the joint. A capsular end feel is firm and leathery with some give. A muscular end feel is springy and elastic, and guarding feels like a muscle spasm stopping motion, so neither fits."}, {"id": 158, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 42-year-old patient with back pain is learning a hip hinge using a dowel along the spine to avoid lumbar flexion while bending to lift. The therapist wants the correct contact points for the drill so that neutral spine is preserved. Which three points should the dowel contact throughout the movement?", "options": {"A": "The back of the head, the lumbar spine at its curve, and the sacrum", "B": "The back of the head, the thoracic spine, and the sacrum", "C": "The upper thoracic spine, the lumbar spine, and the posterior thighs", "D": "The sacrum, the posterior thighs, and the calves throughout the bend"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The back of the head, the thoracic spine, and the sacrum): The dowel touches the head, the thoracic spine, and the sacrum, with a small gap at the lumbar curve that shows neutral is kept. Pressing the dowel against the lumbar spine flattens the curve. Contacts on the thighs and calves do not monitor the spine. If the gap at the lumbar curve closes or the contacts are lost, the spine has flexed."}, {"id": 159, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic is comparing a class 3B laser probe with a cluster of light-emitting diodes that both emit near 660 nm. The sales representative says the two sources are physically identical in their light. A chiropractor wants to name the property that distinguishes the laser output from the diode cluster output. Which property is it?", "options": {"A": "Laser light diverges more quickly, which is why it needs contact with the skin", "B": "Laser light carries more energy per photon at the same wavelength than diode light", "C": "Laser light is coherent, meaning its waves travel in phase, unlike the diode output", "D": "Laser light has a wider bandwidth covering many wavelengths, unlike the narrow diode output"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Laser light is coherent, meaning its waves travel in phase, unlike the diode output): A laser produces coherent light, with waves in phase, along with narrow bandwidth and low divergence. Diodes emit incoherent light over a wider bandwidth with greater divergence. Photon energy depends only on wavelength, so two sources at 660 nm give equal energy per photon."}, {"id": 160, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old nurse has chronic low back pain and a pelvis that drops on the right side when she stands on her left leg during gait observation. The clinician wants to strengthen the muscle responsible for holding the pelvis level in single-leg stance. Which muscle is the primary target?", "options": {"A": "Right gluteus medius, assisted by gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae", "B": "Left gluteus maximus, assisted by the hamstrings and erector spinae", "C": "Left gluteus medius, assisted by gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae", "D": "Left adductor magnus, assisted by the adductor longus and gracilis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left gluteus medius, assisted by gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae): In single-leg stance the abductors of the stance hip hold the pelvis level against the weight of the body. The pelvis drops on the right when the left stance abductors are weak, which is the left gluteus medius with help from gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae. The right side is the swing limb here. Adductors and extensors do not control frontal plane pelvic drop."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 41-year-old carpenter has chronic tendinopathy at the elbow, and the clinician plans phonophoresis to drive a topical anti-inflammatory through intact skin with ultrasound. Which agent is the one most commonly used in phonophoresis for this inflammatory purpose, and in what form is it applied?", "options": {"A": "Lidocaine ointment at 5 percent, applied as a topical anesthetic under the sound head", "B": "Trolamine salicylate cream, applied as a counterirritant under the sound head", "C": "Menthol gel, applied as a cooling counterirritant under the sound head", "D": "Hydrocortisone, usually prepared as a 10 percent cream applied under the sound head"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hydrocortisone, usually prepared as a 10 percent cream applied under the sound head): Hydrocortisone cream, usually at 10 percent, is the classic corticosteroid driven by phonophoresis for inflammatory conditions such as tendinopathy. Lidocaine is a real phonophoresis agent but aims at anesthesia. Salicylate and menthol preparations act as counterirritants rather than anti-inflammatory steroids, so they do not match the stated purpose."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old woman has patellofemoral pain that worsens on stairs. Patellofemoral compressive forces rise with knee flexion in closed chain exercise and rise toward extension in open chain exercise. Which exercise choice keeps joint stress lowest while strengthening the quadriceps?", "options": {"A": "Closed chain deep squats to ninety degrees or more of knee flexion", "B": "Closed chain squats limited to about forty five degrees of knee flexion", "C": "Open chain knee extension through the final forty five degrees with heavy resistance", "D": "Full range open chain knee extension with an ankle weight for twenty repetitions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Closed chain squats limited to about forty five degrees of knee flexion): In closed chain work patellofemoral stress rises as the knee flexes, so the range is limited to roughly zero to forty five degrees. In open chain work stress is greatest near extension, so heavy resistance in the last forty five degrees is the worst choice. Deep squats and full range extension both enter high stress zones."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has trained leg press three times weekly for three weeks, and her one-repetition maximum rose 25 percent. Thigh girth measured by tape and ultrasound thickness are unchanged. She asks whether the training is working. Which explanation of her strength gain is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Hyperplasia of muscle fibers, which adds contractile units before girth changes appear", "B": "Early hypertrophy of type I fibers that is too small to measure by ultrasound", "C": "Increased tendon and bone density producing greater force transmission within three weeks", "D": "Improved motor unit recruitment and coordination, with little change in muscle size so far"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Improved motor unit recruitment and coordination, with little change in muscle size so far): During the first several weeks of resistance training, strength rises mainly through neural adaptations such as greater motor unit recruitment, firing rate and coordination, before measurable hypertrophy occurs. This fits a 25 percent increase with unchanged girth. Hyperplasia is minor in humans, and bone and tendon adapt too slowly to account for this change."}, {"id": 164, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 45 year old swimmer with chronic supraspinatus tendinopathy improved over three laser sessions at a moderate dose. Hoping for faster progress, the treating chiropractor discusses raising the dose several fold at the next visit. Which principle of dose response predicts that an excessive dose may reduce the benefit rather than increase it?", "options": {"A": "The Grotthuss-Draper law, which states that light must be absorbed to produce an effect", "B": "The Bunsen-Roscoe law, which states that effect depends on the product of intensity and time", "C": "The inverse square law, which describes falling beam intensity as distance from the source increases", "D": "The Arndt-Schulz principle, which describes a biphasic response with inhibition at excessive dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Arndt-Schulz principle, which describes a biphasic response with inhibition at excessive dose): The Arndt-Schulz principle holds that weak stimuli do little, moderate stimuli promote activity, and strong stimuli inhibit it. That biphasic curve is why laser dose has an optimal window. The inverse square law concerns distance, the Grotthuss-Draper law concerns absorption, and the Bunsen-Roscoe reciprocity law concerns intensity multiplied by time."}, {"id": 165, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old has deep low back pain treated with four-pole interferential current. Channel one reads 30 mA and channel two reads 10 mA, and the patient feels weak pulsing despite a 100 Hz beat. The cables and electrodes are intact. Which correction best restores strong amplitude modulation at the crossing point?", "options": {"A": "Reverse the electrode polarity on channel two to strengthen the beat", "B": "Balance the two channel amplitudes so the currents interfere with comparable strength", "C": "Raise both carrier frequencies to 5000 Hz to deepen the interference field", "D": "Widen the sweep range so the beat alternates between 80 and 150 Hz"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Balance the two channel amplitudes so the currents interfere with comparable strength): Interference forms a strong beat only where the two currents have comparable amplitude, so a 30 mA and 10 mA mismatch gives weak pulsing. Balancing the channels restores deep modulation. Polarity is irrelevant for alternating currents, and a higher carrier changes comfort and penetration rather than beat depth. Changing the sweep alters the beat frequency but leaves the amplitude imbalance in place."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student asks why a 4000 Hz interferential carrier is usually tolerated at higher intensities than a 50 Hz current delivered through the same electrodes on the same patient. The instructor wants an answer based on the electrical properties of skin. Which statement best explains the difference in comfort between the two currents?", "options": {"A": "Frequencies above 1000 Hz bypass cutaneous nerves, so the patient feels almost no sensation", "B": "Skin capacitive reactance falls as frequency rises, so less resistance occurs at 4000 Hz", "C": "Medium-frequency current carries a net direct component that buffers skin chemical changes", "D": "Medium-frequency current is absorbed by superficial fat, which lowers the charge reaching nerves"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Skin capacitive reactance falls as frequency rises, so less resistance occurs at 4000 Hz): Skin behaves partly like a capacitor, and its reactance is inversely proportional to frequency. At 4000 Hz the skin offers far less impedance than at 50 Hz, so more current passes with less stinging. Superficial nerves still respond to the beat, so sensation is not abolished. Interferential current is alternating and carries no net direct component."}, {"id": 167, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old warehouse supervisor has increased lumbar lordosis, an anterior pelvic tilt, and a protruding abdomen. Testing shows a positive Thomas test, tight lumbar erector spinae, and poor gluteal and abdominal activation. Which exercise program best addresses this pattern in the first phase of rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "Stretch hip flexors and lumbar extensors, and activate gluteus maximus and abdominals", "B": "Strengthen hip flexors and lumbar extensors, and stretch gluteus maximus and abdominals", "C": "Stretch hamstrings and gluteus maximus, and strengthen iliopsoas and lumbar erector spinae", "D": "Stretch hip flexors and lumbar extensors, then perform full sit-ups with the feet anchored"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stretch hip flexors and lumbar extensors, and activate gluteus maximus and abdominals): The lower crossed pattern pairs tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors with weak gluteus maximus and abdominals, so treatment lengthens the tight group and activates the weak one with pelvic control. Reversing the roles deepens the lordosis. Stretching hamstrings and gluteals targets the wrong muscles. Anchored full sit-ups recruit the hip flexors strongly, so they reinforce the anterior tilt."}, {"id": 168, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 63-year-old man with a known metastatic lesion in a lumbar vertebra requests ultrasound for persistent low back pain at his visit. The student notes that the lumbar paraspinals could be treated with 1 MHz. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply pulsed ultrasound at low intensity, because pulsing avoids tumor heating", "B": "Apply 1 MHz continuous at 1.5 W/cm2, because deep heating relieves tumor pain", "C": "Do not apply ultrasound over the area, and use another modality or refer", "D": "Apply 3 MHz continuous at 1.0 W/cm2, because the shallow beam avoids the lesion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not apply ultrasound over the area, and use another modality or refer): Ultrasound over known malignancy is contraindicated because heating and cavitation may stimulate tumor growth or spread and may mask worsening disease. Changing frequency or duty cycle does not remove the concern. Pain relief here belongs to the oncology team or a modality not directed over the lesion."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "Two 40-year-old women both receive a prescription of 60 percent of heart rate reserve using an age-predicted maximum of 180. Patient one has a resting heart rate of 52 and patient two has a resting heart rate of 84. The clinician compares their calculated targets. Which statement about the two targets is correct?", "options": {"A": "Patient one's target is higher, about 142 versus about 129 beats per minute", "B": "Patient two's target is higher, about 142 versus about 129 beats per minute", "C": "Both targets are 108 beats per minute because intensity is a fixed fraction of maximum", "D": "Patient one's target is higher because her larger reserve produces a larger fraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Patient two's target is higher, about 142 versus about 129 beats per minute): Patient one has a reserve of 128, so 60 percent is about 77, plus 52 gives about 129. Patient two has a reserve of 96, so 60 percent is about 58, plus 84 gives about 142. The higher resting rate is added back, which outweighs the smaller reserve. A fixed 108 beats per minute would result only from using percent of maximum, which is not the Karvonen method."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 74 year old woman has a sacral pressure ulcer with purulent drainage and a culture positive for Pseudomonas. The physician orders ultraviolet irradiation with the goal of reducing the bacterial load on the wound surface. The wound has been cleansed and debrided. Which ultraviolet source is selected?", "options": {"A": "Broadband UVB delivered at a wavelength near 300 nanometers", "B": "UVA delivered at a wavelength near 365 nanometers from a mercury lamp", "C": "UVC delivered at a wavelength near 254 nanometers", "D": "Narrowband UVB delivered at a wavelength near 311 nanometers"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (UVC delivered at a wavelength near 254 nanometers): UVC near 254 nanometers is strongly bactericidal and is the band selected for infected, colonized wounds. Narrowband and broadband UVB are used for psoriasis and for stimulating healing, not for killing surface bacteria. UVA has weak bactericidal action. UVC penetrates only the surface, which suits a superficial bacterial load."}, {"id": 171, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old woman is eight weeks out from a distal radius fracture treated in a cast. After removal she has burning pain out of proportion to the injury, swelling, skin color and temperature changes in the hand, and pain from light touch. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Edema control, desensitization, graded motor imagery, and pain-free active motion", "B": "Passive wrist stretching and mobilization to prevent contracture", "C": "Cortisone injection into the carpal tunnel and a night splint for presumed median nerve entrapment", "D": "Contrast baths alternating hot and cold with vigorous massage to flush swelling"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Edema control, desensitization, graded motor imagery, and pain-free active motion): Burning pain out of proportion, swelling, skin color and temperature changes, and allodynia after a fracture indicate complex regional pain syndrome. Management combines edema control, desensitization, graded motor imagery, and pain-free active use. Passive stretching and vigorous massage provoke the sensitized limb, and an injection for median nerve entrapment treats a different problem even though carpal tunnel symptoms can follow distal radius fracture."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 62-year-old man with hemiparesis after a stroke cannot bend the right knee well during swing. To get the foot past the floor he moves the whole right leg out to the side and then forward along a curved path. What is the name of this gait deviation?", "options": {"A": "Hip hiking, elevating the pelvis on the swing side to clear the foot", "B": "Vaulting, rising on the stance limb toes to raise the swing limb", "C": "Circumduction, an outward curved path of the swing limb", "D": "Steppage gait, exaggerated hip and knee flexion to lift the toes clear"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Circumduction, an outward curved path of the swing limb): Circumduction is an outward arc of the swing leg used when knee flexion and foot clearance are inadequate, as in hemiparesis. Hip hiking lifts the pelvis instead of swinging the leg out, and vaulting raises the body on the stance limb. Steppage is a high-lift pattern for foot drop, not an outward curved path."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinician uses a continuous 60 mW probe and holds a 0.5 cm2 spot for 50 seconds, delivering 6 J/cm2 to a small tender point. For a larger plaque, the same probe is fitted with a lens that spreads the beam over 1.5 cm2. To still deliver 6 J/cm2 over this larger spot, how long must the probe be held?", "options": {"A": "17 s, found by dividing the original 50 s by the tripled area", "B": "50 s, found by keeping the original exposure time", "C": "450 s, found by multiplying 150 s by the tripled area", "D": "150 s, found by dividing 9 J by 0.06 W"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (150 s, found by dividing 9 J by 0.06 W): A 1.5 cm2 spot at 6 J/cm2 needs 9 J of energy. Dividing 9 J by 0.06 W gives 150 s, which is three times the original because the area tripled. Keeping 50 s would drop the dose to a third, dividing by three goes the wrong direction, and tripling again counts the area twice."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 17-year-old basketball player is cleared to begin plyometrics after a patellar tendinopathy flare settled. He plays three games weekly. He has pain-free full range and good single-leg squat control. How should the therapist progress the jump training in the first sessions?", "options": {"A": "Begin with bilateral jumps from a moderate height box and add depth jumps within two weeks", "B": "Begin with low intensity single-leg hops in place, since he shows good single-leg control", "C": "Begin with low intensity bilateral jumps in place and progress to single-leg hops later", "D": "Begin with moderate intensity bounding over short distances and add hurdle hops after one week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin with low intensity bilateral jumps in place and progress to single-leg hops later): Plyometric training progresses from low to high intensity and from bilateral to unilateral, so in place bilateral jumps come first after a tendon flare. Good single-leg squat control does not replace the low load introduction that lets the tendon adapt. Box jumps with early depth work and bounding impose high eccentric load too soon. Frequent games add to the weekly tendon load, which supports a conservative start."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 20-year-old soccer player is nine months after ACL reconstruction, has full range of motion and no effusion, and wants clearance for games. Isokinetic testing shows quadriceps strength at 82 percent of the other leg, and single leg hop distance is 86 percent. Which decision best matches current return to sport criteria?", "options": {"A": "Clear her for full competition because nine months have passed since surgery and range is full", "B": "Restrict her from all cutting sports for the next two years while strength is rebuilt", "C": "Continue strengthening and plyometrics until limb symmetry reaches about ninety percent or better", "D": "Clear her for practice only, since symmetry above eighty percent is adequate for her sport"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue strengthening and plyometrics until limb symmetry reaches about ninety percent or better): Return to sport is based on function: quadriceps strength and hop tests near ninety percent symmetry, along with confidence and movement quality. Her 82 and 86 percent values fall short even though nine months is a typical time point. Lower symmetry is linked to higher reinjury risk, but a two year restriction is not criterion based, so targeted progression is best."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 34-year-old recreational cyclist completes twelve weeks of supervised endurance training three times per week. At follow-up, the clinician compares her resting measurements with her baseline values and expects a typical cardiovascular adaptation to have occurred. Which resting change is the expected result of regular endurance training in a healthy adult?", "options": {"A": "Lower resting stroke volume with a compensatory rise in resting cardiac output", "B": "Unchanged resting heart rate with a larger stroke volume and a higher cardiac output", "C": "Higher resting heart rate with a smaller stroke volume and unchanged output", "D": "Lower resting heart rate accompanied by a larger stroke volume"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower resting heart rate accompanied by a larger stroke volume): Endurance training enlarges ventricular and plasma volume, raising stroke volume so a slower resting heart rate maintains the same cardiac output. Resting cardiac output stays about the same, so a higher output is wrong. A smaller stroke volume with faster rate describes deconditioning. Falling stroke volume is the reverse of the expected adaptation."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist plans proprioceptive training for a patient three weeks after a lateral ankle sprain who now walks without pain. The aim is to follow the principle of advancing from the easiest to the most challenging balance tasks. Which sequence best applies this progression?", "options": {"A": "Single-leg floor stance, double-leg floor stance, single-leg foam stance, finally wobble board with eyes closed", "B": "Double-leg floor stance, then single-leg foam stance, single-leg floor stance, finally wobble board with eyes closed", "C": "Double-leg floor stance, then single-leg floor stance, single-leg foam stance, finally wobble board with eyes closed", "D": "Double-leg floor stance, then single-leg floor stance, wobble board with eyes closed, finally single-leg foam stance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Double-leg floor stance, then single-leg floor stance, single-leg foam stance, finally wobble board with eyes closed): Balance training advances by narrowing the base, reducing surface stability and then removing vision, one demand at a time. The correct order starts with the widest, firmest stance and ends with the unstable board under eyes-closed conditions. Starting single-leg skips the basic double-leg stage. Using foam before single-leg floor stance adds a surface change before the base is narrowed. Placing the eyes-closed board before foam makes the last task easier than the one before it."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old runner has unilateral sacroiliac pain with a positive cluster of provocation tests and excessive pelvic motion during single-leg loading. Mobilization has not helped. The clinician plans to improve force closure across the joint through the posterior oblique sling. Which muscle pairing makes up this functional sling?", "options": {"A": "External oblique and the contralateral adductor longus, linked by the abdominal fascia", "B": "Gluteus medius and the ipsilateral quadratus lumborum, linked by the iliolumbar ligament", "C": "Gluteus maximus and the ipsilateral latissimus dorsi, linked by the thoracolumbar fascia", "D": "Gluteus maximus and the contralateral latissimus dorsi, linked by the thoracolumbar fascia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gluteus maximus and the contralateral latissimus dorsi, linked by the thoracolumbar fascia): The posterior oblique sling crosses the sacroiliac region by linking the gluteus maximus on one side with the opposite latissimus dorsi through the thoracolumbar fascia. Its tension compresses the joint during gait. The same muscles on one side do not form a crossing sling. External oblique with the opposite adductors forms the anterior oblique sling."}, {"id": 179, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old runner has 5 degrees of passive ankle dorsiflexion with the knee fully extended but 18 degrees with the knee flexed to 90 degrees. The end feel is elastic in both positions. Which interpretation and stretching plan best fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Achilles tendon adhesion, treated with ballistic heel drops off a step edge", "B": "Gastrocnemius shortening, treated with calf stretching performed with the knee fully extended", "C": "Soleus shortening, treated with calf stretching performed with the knee flexed", "D": "Posterior talocrural capsule restriction, treated with grade III anterior talar mobilization"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gastrocnemius shortening, treated with calf stretching performed with the knee fully extended): Dorsiflexion that increases when the knee is flexed shows that the gastrocnemius, which crosses the knee, is limiting motion, because flexing the knee slackens it. The treatment is calf stretching with the knee extended. If the soleus were the cause, range would stay limited with the knee flexed. A capsular problem would give a firm end feel and limitation in both positions, and ballistic heel drops do not lengthen a shortened muscle."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 67 year old man is referred for UV treatment of a chronic leg ulcer. His medication list includes levothyroxine, acetaminophen, famotidine and hydrochlorothiazide. During screening the therapist considers which drug could lower his MED and make a standard dose cause an unexpected burn. Which medication is the likely photosensitizer?", "options": {"A": "Levothyroxine, since thyroid hormone replacement changes skin sensitivity to ultraviolet exposure", "B": "Famotidine, since acid reducing drugs deposit in the skin and absorb ultraviolet light", "C": "Hydrochlorothiazide, the thiazide diuretic listed among his medications", "D": "Acetaminophen, since regular analgesic use thins the epidermis and lowers the MED"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hydrochlorothiazide, the thiazide diuretic listed among his medications): Thiazide diuretics such as hydrochlorothiazide are well known photosensitizers that lower the MED. Levothyroxine replaces a hormone and does not change ultraviolet response at a replacement dose. Famotidine is not recognized as photosensitizing. Acetaminophen does not thin the epidermis or lower the MED."}, {"id": 181, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old rugby player had whiplash ten days ago and now has full active range with mild end-range pain at 2 of 10 and a normal neurological exam. She has been doing gentle range of motion exercises daily. Which progression is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Return to collision sports immediately to restore her confidence in the neck", "B": "Add low-load craniocervical flexion and scapular endurance work with gradual progression", "C": "Begin maximal isometric neck strengthening at 80 percent effort every day starting this week", "D": "Continue gentle range of motion alone until the pain is fully absent before adding load"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add low-load craniocervical flexion and scapular endurance work with gradual progression): Once full motion returns with mild end-range pain and a normal neurological exam, the subacute stage moves to low-load deep flexor and scapular endurance exercises with gradual progression. Maximal effort strength training is too aggressive at ten days. Waiting for zero pain with motion alone delays recovery and conditioning. Return to contact sport needs functional criteria, not just confidence."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic is choosing a lamp to treat chronic plaque psoriasis in a 52 year old man. The clinician reviews the ultraviolet spectrum and wants the band that produces the strongest erythemal response and is the standard range for conventional UVB therapy. Which wavelength range defines the UVB band?", "options": {"A": "200 to 290 nanometers, the shortest band used for germicidal lamps in clinics", "B": "400 to 500 nanometers, the violet and blue band just beyond UVA", "C": "290 to 320 nanometers, the band between UVC and UVA", "D": "320 to 400 nanometers, the longest band used with oral psoralen"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (290 to 320 nanometers, the band between UVC and UVA): UVB spans about 290 to 320 nanometers and sits between UVC and UVA. This band produces the strongest erythemal response and is the standard range for psoriasis lamps. The 200 to 290 band is UVC, which is germicidal, and 320 to 400 is UVA, which is paired with psoralen. The 400 to 500 band is visible light and is not ultraviolet."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A chiropractic student times a healthy adult walking at a comfortable pace and wants to confirm the textbook split of one complete gait cycle on a single limb between time with the foot on the ground and time with the foot off the ground. Which split describes normal walking for that limb?", "options": {"A": "Roughly forty percent of the cycle in stance and sixty percent in swing", "B": "Roughly seventy percent of the cycle in stance and thirty percent in swing", "C": "Roughly fifty percent of the cycle in stance and fifty percent in swing", "D": "Roughly sixty percent of the cycle in stance and forty percent in swing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Roughly sixty percent of the cycle in stance and forty percent in swing): In normal free-speed walking one limb spends about 60 percent of the cycle in stance and 40 percent in swing. The swing percentage is smaller because the other limb carries the body during that time. A 70 to 30 split appears with slow, cautious walking, and the reversed or equal splits do not describe walking at all."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During a gait observation a clinician hears the left foot slap down loudly right after the heel touches the floor, although the toes clear the floor well in swing. In which phase does the deficit occur, and which muscle control is inadequate?", "options": {"A": "Midstance, with weak eccentric control by the left soleus", "B": "Loading response, weak eccentric control of the left tibialis anterior", "C": "Terminal swing, with weak eccentric control by the left hamstrings", "D": "Preswing, with weak concentric action of the left gastrocnemius"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loading response, weak eccentric control of the left tibialis anterior): Foot slap happens in the loading response when the forefoot falls to the floor because tibialis anterior cannot lengthen under control. The toes clear in swing, so the dorsiflexors are strong enough concentrically. Soleus works in midstance, the hamstrings brake the knee in terminal swing, and gastrocnemius contributes at push-off."}, {"id": 185, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Janda classified muscles by their typical response to pain, overload, or immobilization. A therapist reviewing a chart for a 50-year-old office worker with a tight lateral hip wants the muscle that, in this scheme, is a tonic postural muscle with a tendency to shorten and tighten. Which muscle fits that description?", "options": {"A": "The gluteus medius at the lateral hip and pelvis", "B": "The tensor fasciae latae at the lateral hip", "C": "The lower trapezius at the thoracic spine and scapula", "D": "The vastus medialis at the anterior thigh and knee"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The tensor fasciae latae at the lateral hip): Janda lists the tensor fasciae latae among the tonic muscles that tend toward hypertonicity and shortening, alongside the iliopsoas, rectus femoris, piriformis and erector spinae. The gluteus medius, vastus medialis and lower trapezius are phasic muscles that tend toward inhibition and weakness. The lateral hip position of the gluteus medius makes it a tempting neighbor, but it weakens rather than tightens."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old graphic designer presents with forward head posture, rounded shoulders and neck ache after long hours at a desk. Applying Janda's upper crossed syndrome pattern, a therapist plans strengthening for the muscles that are typically inhibited and weak rather than the muscles that are tight. Which group should the strengthening program target?", "options": {"A": "Deep cervical flexors, lower trapezius, serratus anterior and rhomboids", "B": "Upper trapezius, levator scapulae, pectoralis major and sternocleidomastoid", "C": "Suboccipitals, pectoralis minor, upper trapezius and the cervical extensors", "D": "Sternocleidomastoid, scalenes, pectoralis major and the anterior deltoid fibers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deep cervical flexors, lower trapezius, serratus anterior and rhomboids): Upper crossed syndrome pairs tight upper trapezius, levator scapulae, pectorals and suboccipitals with weak deep neck flexors, lower trapezius, serratus anterior and rhomboids. Strengthening goes to the weak, inhibited side while the tight side is stretched or released. Training the tight muscles would deepen the postural imbalance."}, {"id": 187, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 15-year-old competitive diver is diagnosed with a unilateral L5 pars stress reaction after six weeks of extension-related pain. Pain is now absent at rest and with walking. The team physician allows progressive rehabilitation. Which element belongs in the first rehabilitation phase before any return to diving?", "options": {"A": "Repeated end-range lumbar extension to restore full arch mobility", "B": "Plyometric landings from a platform to rebuild impact tolerance in the lumbar spine", "C": "Hyperextension back raises over a Roman chair to strengthen the lumbar extensors", "D": "Neutral spine core stabilization along with hamstring flexibility work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neutral spine core stabilization along with hamstring flexibility work): The first phase of pars stress reaction rehabilitation uses neutral spine core stabilization with hamstring flexibility work, avoiding repeated extension. Repeated end-range extension and loaded Roman chair extension both stress the pars. Plyometric landings are impact loads that belong in a late phase just before return to diving."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist places fingers beside the L4 spinous process of a prone patient and cues a gentle swelling beneath the fingers with no pelvic or lumbar movement. A student asks which muscle is being isolated and what response confirms that the patient is contracting it correctly. What is the best answer?", "options": {"A": "The lumbar multifidus, confirmed by slow deep swelling under the fingers without motion", "B": "The transversus abdominis, confirmed by the abdominal wall drawing away from the table", "C": "The quadratus lumborum, confirmed by hip hiking toward the ribs on the same side", "D": "The lumbar erector spinae, confirmed by lumbar extension lifting the trunk off the table"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The lumbar multifidus, confirmed by slow deep swelling under the fingers without motion): Palpation beside the spinous process in prone lets the therapist feel the deep multifidus swell slowly during a gentle contraction while the spine stays still. A global extension or hip hiking is a movement, not an isolated contraction. Drawing the abdominal wall away from the table describes the transversus abdominis. The spinous process location points to the multifidus."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 28-year-old runner has just had a cast removed after a tibial stress injury and the calf feels stiff and doughy. The therapist plans to lift, roll and squeeze the bulk of the gastrocnemius between both hands with a rhythmic compressive pattern. Which category of massage stroke is being used?", "options": {"A": "Effleurage, a long gliding stroke over the calf made with the palm toward the heart", "B": "Petrissage, a kneading stroke that lifts, rolls and squeezes the muscle belly between the hands", "C": "Deep friction, a small circular stroke that mobilizes adhesions within the calf muscle belly", "D": "Tapotement, a percussive stroke using alternating cupping or hacking blows over the muscle belly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Petrissage, a kneading stroke that lifts, rolls and squeezes the muscle belly between the hands): Lifting, rolling and squeezing the muscle belly between the hands is the pattern of petrissage. Effleurage glides over the surface without lifting tissue, and friction works small focal areas deeply. Tapotement uses rhythmic percussion rather than compression. Each is a real stroke, but only kneading matches this description."}, {"id": 190, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A massage therapy clinic keeps a list of conditions for which massage of the affected region must be avoided. A student is asked which of four listed presentations is a contraindication to massaging the limb. Which one belongs on that list?", "options": {"A": "Suspected deep vein thrombosis in the calf of the affected leg", "B": "Residual stiffness around a fully healed ankle fracture after clearance", "C": "Chronic muscle tension in the upper back from desk work", "D": "Mild delayed onset muscle soreness in the thigh two days after training"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Suspected deep vein thrombosis in the calf of the affected leg): Massage can dislodge a clot, so suspected deep vein thrombosis in a limb is a contraindication until it has been excluded. Chronic tension, ordinary soreness and stiffness after a healed fracture are usual indications for massage. A clinic list separates conditions needing medical clearance from those that benefit from treatment."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "During dry needling of a gluteus medius trigger point in a 46-year-old runner with lateral hip pain, the clinician inserts a fine filiform needle into a taut band and looks for a sign that the needle has reached the active point. Which response is the expected confirming sign?", "options": {"A": "A sharp electric-like paresthesia radiating from the buttock down the leg", "B": "A firm bony endpoint felt as the needle contacts the greater trochanter", "C": "A brief local twitch response of the taut band", "D": "A distinct fascial give felt during needle insertion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A brief local twitch response of the taut band): A brief local twitch response of the taut band is the expected sign that the needle has reached an active trigger point. Radiating paresthesia suggests nerve contact, so the needle should be redirected. Bony contact means the needle is too deep and risks periosteal irritation, and a fascial give only shows the needle passed through a layer without confirming anything about the taut band."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old accountant sits ten hours daily and has an anterior pelvic tilt with a positive Thomas test for tight iliopsoas. The clinician teaches a hip flexor stretch for the right side that targets the muscle rather than moving the lumbar spine. Which technique is best?", "options": {"A": "Half-kneeling with the trunk leaning backward to deepen the stretch across the front hip", "B": "Half-kneeling with the pelvis tilted posteriorly while shifting weight forward", "C": "Standing quadriceps stretch holding the ankle, with the knee pulled behind the body line", "D": "Lying supine with both knees pulled to the chest to flex the lumbar spine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Half-kneeling with the pelvis tilted posteriorly while shifting weight forward): Stretching the iliopsoas requires the pelvis to stay posteriorly tilted while the hip moves toward extension, which a half-kneeling position with weight shift achieves. Leaning back lets the lumbar spine extend and hides the shortness. Knee-pulled-behind quadriceps stretching mainly loads the rectus femoris, and double knee to chest stretches the lumbar extensors rather than the hip flexors."}, {"id": 193, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient's MED was found to be 80 seconds. By mistake the therapist exposed the patient's back for 400 seconds at the same distance with the same lamp. The therapist now counsels the patient about what to expect over the next day. Which reaction does this dose most likely produce?", "options": {"A": "A third degree erythema with blistering and marked tenderness", "B": "No visible erythema, since the dose stays below the MED", "C": "A first degree erythema with mild redness and little tenderness that fades within a day", "D": "A second degree erythema with edema, tenderness and later peeling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A second degree erythema with edema, tenderness and later peeling): The dose of 400 seconds is five times the 80 second MED, which defines an E2 reaction. E2 produces marked erythema with edema, tenderness and later peeling. An E1 reaction needs only about 2.5 times the MED, and E3 with blistering needs about ten times. A dose well above the MED cannot leave the skin unchanged."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 40-year-old with neck pain and radiating arm symptoms has never had traction, and the clinician is unsure how he will respond. The therapist wants to trial the treatment before committing to a mechanical program. Which approach best allows a brief trial with immediate adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Manual traction applied briefly with adjustment of angle and force by feel", "B": "A home over-the-door unit sent with him for trial use before the next visit", "C": "Inversion traction on a table at 60 degrees for five minutes", "D": "Mechanical traction at 50 percent of body weight for thirty minutes to test tolerance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Manual traction applied briefly with adjustment of angle and force by feel): Manual traction lets the therapist change angle, force, and hand position moment by moment and stop immediately if symptoms change, so it is a good screening trial. A heavy mechanical dose of 50 percent of body weight would be too strong at the start of the cervical spine. Inversion cannot be graded or stopped quickly. Sending a home unit before the response is known removes supervision."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 68-year-old man on warfarin with a recent INR of 3.8 has a tender, tight lumbar paraspinal area and requests a firm sports-style massage. He also has mild unexplained ecchymosis on his forearm, but no history of trauma. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Light, gentle effleurage only, avoiding deep pressure and percussive strokes", "B": "Moderate pressure petrissage with slow strokes, avoiding friction and percussive strokes over the lumbar area", "C": "Light effleurage to the lumbar area, followed by firm stripping strokes along the paraspinal muscles", "D": "Myofascial release with sustained moderate traction on the lumbar fascia, avoiding all percussion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Light, gentle effleurage only, avoiding deep pressure and percussive strokes): An INR of 3.8 with unexplained bruising means fragile vessels and a real risk of hematoma, so light gliding strokes are the safe choice and the prescriber should be told. Moderate kneading still compresses tissue enough to bruise. Firm stripping after a light start adds the same risk. Sustained moderate myofascial traction also stresses vulnerable tissue, and avoiding percussion alone does not make it safe."}, {"id": 196, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with a demand-type cardiac pacemaker has chronic knee pain from osteoarthritis. She asks about TENS because her neighbor found it helpful. Her knee is far from her chest, and she has no skin lesions. The clinician must choose the safest approach to her pain management. Which course of action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply conventional TENS around the knee at sensory intensity, routing leads away from the chest.", "B": "Apply Russian stimulation to the quadriceps instead, since its burst pattern differs from TENS.", "C": "Withhold electrical stimulation, contact her cardiologist, and use a nonelectrical modality meanwhile.", "D": "Apply brief intense TENS for only 10 minutes to limit time spent near the device."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold electrical stimulation, contact her cardiologist, and use a nonelectrical modality meanwhile.): TENS and other electrical stimulation are contraindicated with demand pacemakers because the current may interfere with sensing and pacing. Distance from the chest, a different waveform, or a short exposure does not remove that risk. The clinician should defer electrical agents, involve the cardiologist, and use a nonelectrical option in the meantime."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist performs the Dix-Hallpike test to check for right posterior canal BPPV in a seated 60-year-old patient who has brief vertigo when lying down. Before the move, the patient is seated lengthwise on the table. How should the head be positioned as the patient is brought from sitting to supine?", "options": {"A": "Head turned 45 degrees right, then lowered supine with the neck extended about 20 degrees", "B": "Head turned 90 degrees right, then lowered supine with the neck in neutral", "C": "Head turned straight ahead, then lowered side lying with the neck flexed 30 degrees", "D": "Head turned 45 degrees left, then lowered supine with the neck extended about 20 degrees"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Head turned 45 degrees right, then lowered supine with the neck extended about 20 degrees): To test the right posterior canal, the head is turned about 45 degrees to the right and the patient is lowered quickly to supine with the head hanging about 20 degrees below horizontal. This aligns the canal with the plane of gravity. Turning left tests the opposite ear, and the other positions fail to align the canal."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old soccer player is eight weeks after ACL reconstruction with full range of motion and no effusion. She holds single-leg stance on firm floor with eyes open for 30 seconds on both legs, and her surgeon has cleared graded balance work. Which progression is best next?", "options": {"A": "Single-leg foam stance with a ball toss, then with eyes closed", "B": "Bilateral stance on a wobble board with the eyes open and arms free", "C": "Single-leg stance on firm floor for 60 seconds with the eyes open", "D": "Single-leg hops on the surgical limb with a landing hold on firm floor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Single-leg foam stance with a ball toss, then with eyes closed): She holds single-leg firm-floor stance for 30 seconds, so the next step adds an unstable surface and a secondary task, then removes vision. Bilateral board work is easier than her current level. Longer firm-floor holding adds little challenge. Hopping at eight weeks adds impact load before dynamic stability is shown."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractor reviews a lateral thoracic radiograph of a 45-year-old accountant with mild upper back stiffness, and the radiologist reports the sagittal Cobb measurement. Before labeling the posture as hyperkyphosis, the chiropractor wants the accepted normal range for adult thoracic kyphosis. Which range is generally considered normal?", "options": {"A": "Roughly 50 to 70 degrees, meaning the upper limit of normal for older adults", "B": "Roughly 10 to 20 degrees, meaning a mild curve that is rarely exceeded in adults", "C": "Roughly 20 to 40 degrees of posterior convexity in the sagittal plane", "D": "Roughly 0 to 10 degrees, meaning a nearly flat thoracic spine in the sagittal plane"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Roughly 20 to 40 degrees of posterior convexity in the sagittal plane): Normal thoracic kyphosis measures about 20 to 40 degrees by the Cobb method. Values above roughly 40 to 45 degrees are called hyperkyphosis, and values below 20 degrees are described as hypokyphosis or flat back. Mild increases with age are common, but the normal range itself does not shift to 50 to 70 degrees."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old man receives four-pole interferential current for deep hip pain. He feels strong tingling beneath each electrode but nothing in the joint. The chiropractor notes both electrode pairs run parallel along the thigh, side by side. What adjustment is most likely to correct the problem?", "options": {"A": "Reposition the pairs diagonally so both current paths cross over the hip joint", "B": "Raise the carrier frequency to 10,000 Hz to push current deeper", "C": "Increase intensity until the patient feels the joint despite the strong skin tingling", "D": "Switch to premodulated mode and keep the electrodes parallel along the thigh"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reposition the pairs diagonally so both current paths cross over the hip joint): In a four-pole setup the interference occurs only where the two circuits cross, so the crossing point must lie on the painful joint. Parallel pairs never cross, so the patient feels only the surface. Premodulated mode would give a modulated current but no tissue crossing. A higher carrier does not move the crossing, and a higher intensity increases discomfort under the pads."}, {"id": 201, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinic's high voltage unit allows adjustment of pulse duration at a fixed amplitude and pulse rate. A student increases the pulse duration from 40 to 80 microseconds without touching the intensity control. What happens to the charge delivered in each phase?", "options": {"A": "It roughly doubles, because charge depends on amplitude multiplied by duration", "B": "It quadruples, because charge rises with the square of pulse duration", "C": "It halves, because longer pulses spread the same charge over more time", "D": "It stays equal, because charge depends only on the amplitude setting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It roughly doubles, because charge depends on amplitude multiplied by duration): Phase charge is the product of current amplitude and pulse duration. With amplitude fixed, doubling the duration from 40 to 80 microseconds doubles the charge. Charge is not diluted by longer pulses, and duration does matter alongside amplitude. The relationship is linear, not squared, so quadrupling is wrong."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A clinician is converting treadmill results into metabolic equivalents to counsel a patient about safe daily activities. She explains that activity intensity in cardiac rehabilitation is often described in MET units. A MET is a standard unit of energy cost, so what resting value does one MET represent in oxygen uptake terms?", "options": {"A": "1.0 liter of oxygen per minute for any adult, measured during quiet sitting", "B": "7.0 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute during slow walking", "C": "35 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute at rest", "D": "3.5 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute at quiet rest"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3.5 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute at quiet rest): One MET is the resting oxygen uptake, defined as 3.5 mL per kg per minute. Dividing an exercise oxygen uptake by 3.5 gives the workload in METs. The value is scaled to body mass, so an absolute liters-per-minute figure does not define it. A reading of 35 is ten times too high and 7.0 is the cost of roughly two METs."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 44 year old landscaper has sciatica-like pain from suspected irritation of the sciatic nerve as it passes beneath the gluteus maximus, roughly 5 cm below the skin. The chiropractor has a 660 nm, 50 mW probe and a higher-output 810 nm probe. Which choice best allows meaningful energy to reach the target depth?", "options": {"A": "The 660 nm 50 mW probe, because red light is absorbed least by superficial tissue", "B": "The 660 nm probe, because shorter wavelengths scatter less and so reach deeper structures", "C": "A lower-output 810 nm probe held in contact, because less power reduces superficial absorption losses", "D": "The higher-output 810 nm probe, giving more surface energy so enough remains at depth"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The higher-output 810 nm probe, giving more surface energy so enough remains at depth): Only a small fraction of surface energy reaches 5 cm, so a deep target needs a near-infrared wavelength with low absorption and enough output to leave a useful dose at depth. The 810 nm probe at higher output does both. Red light is absorbed strongly by superficial pigment and blood, so little reaches the nerve. Shorter wavelengths scatter more, not less. Lowering output reduces what remains at depth."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 62-year-old man with coronary artery disease is progressing through a graded exercise session on a cycle ergometer while a clinician tracks blood pressure each stage. Workload increases every three minutes. Which response to the increasing workload is an accepted criterion for stopping the session rather than continuing?", "options": {"A": "A systolic pressure that rises about 10 mmHg with each added MET of effort", "B": "A systolic pressure that falls by more than 10 mmHg while effort keeps climbing", "C": "A heart rate that climbs steadily in step with the increase in cycling resistance", "D": "A rating of perceived exertion of 13 reported at a moderate stage of the test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A systolic pressure that falls by more than 10 mmHg while effort keeps climbing): Systolic pressure normally rises about 10 mmHg per MET. A drop of more than 10 mmHg despite increasing load suggests the heart cannot raise output and is a standard reason to end exercise. A steady heart rate rise and a moderate exertion rating are expected normal responses. The proper systolic rise per MET is also normal and not a reason to stop."}, {"id": 205, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A high voltage pulsed current unit is set to a pulse rate of 100 pulses per second, and each twin-peaked pulse lasts 100 microseconds in total. A student wants to know how much of each second the current is actually flowing. What percentage of each second is the current on?", "options": {"A": "10 percent, treating each pulse as 1000 microseconds instead of 100", "B": "0.1 percent, dropping a factor of ten in the conversion to seconds", "C": "1 percent, since 100 pulses of 100 microseconds equal 10,000 microseconds per second", "D": "100 percent, assuming the pulses arrive back to back with no gaps"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (1 percent, since 100 pulses of 100 microseconds equal 10,000 microseconds per second): There are 100 pulses each second, and each lasts 100 microseconds, so the current flows 100 times 100, or 10,000 microseconds per second. One second is 1,000,000 microseconds, so the on time is 10,000 divided by 1,000,000, which is 1 percent. The other values come from a pulse ten times too long, a unit slip, or ignoring the gaps between pulses."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 42-year-old firefighter is three weeks after L4-L5 microdiscectomy with no leg pain and a healed incision. He asks what loading he may begin for return to duty. His surgeon's protocol lists no lifting over 10 pounds until week six. Which plan is best now?", "options": {"A": "Light deadlifts with 40 pounds using strict form to rebuild back extensor strength", "B": "Sustained loaded lumbar flexion stretching to improve mobility around the incision", "C": "Weighted sit-ups on an incline bench to restore abdominal strength quickly", "D": "Walking, neutral-spine abdominal bracing, and hip hinge practice with no external load"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Walking, neutral-spine abdominal bracing, and hip hinge practice with no external load): Until the surgeon clears heavier loading, rehabilitation stays within the lifting limit and avoids repeated flexion under load. Walking, bracing, and unloaded hip hinge patterning prepare him for later strengthening. Loaded deadlifts exceed the 10 pound limit despite strict form. Weighted sit-ups and loaded flexion stretches stress the healing disc."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 25-year-old rugby player has a grade 1 hamstring strain from six days ago. Passive straight leg raise is limited to 60 degrees by pain, and any resisted knee flexion reproduces sharp posterior thigh pain. Contract-relax and hold-relax were considered. Which PNF technique is most appropriate here?", "options": {"A": "Contract-relax: a concentric hamstring contraction against resistance, then relaxation and passive stretching", "B": "Hold-relax: an isometric hamstring contraction at the end of range, then passive stretching", "C": "Slow reversal: alternating hamstring and quadriceps contractions through the full range with resistance", "D": "Agonist contraction: the patient contracts the quadriceps while the therapist stretches the hamstrings"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Agonist contraction: the patient contracts the quadriceps while the therapist stretches the hamstrings): Agonist contraction relies on reciprocal inhibition from quadriceps activity, so the injured hamstrings are never contracted against resistance. Contract-relax and hold-relax both need a hamstring contraction, which is painful here. Slow reversal also loads the hamstrings against resistance, so it carries the same problem."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractic physician reads the specification sheet of a therapeutic laser purchased to treat chronic tendon pain in a busy clinic. The sheet lists a continuous output of 250 mW at the probe tip. Under the standard laser safety classification used for therapy devices, which class does this output place the device in?", "options": {"A": "Class 3B, which covers continuous outputs between 5 mW and 500 mW", "B": "Class 4, which covers continuous outputs above 500 mW at the beam", "C": "Class 2, which covers visible outputs below 1 mW with blink protection", "D": "Class 3R, which covers continuous outputs between 1 mW and 5 mW"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Class 3B, which covers continuous outputs between 5 mW and 500 mW): An output of 250 mW lies between 5 mW and 500 mW, which is the class 3B band. Class 3R tops out at 5 mW, so it is too low for this device. Class 4 begins above 500 mW, and class 2 is limited to visible beams under 1 mW that rely on the blink reflex."}, {"id": 209, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student selects continuous ultrasound and sets the output power to 6 watts. The transducer has an effective radiating area of 4 cm2. To document the treatment, the student must compute the intensity delivered at the transducer face. What intensity in W/cm2 results from these settings, and how is it found?", "options": {"A": "0.67 W/cm2, found by dividing the effective radiating area by total power", "B": "10 W/cm2, found by adding the total power to the effective radiating area", "C": "24 W/cm2, found by multiplying total power by the effective radiating area", "D": "1.5 W/cm2, found by dividing total power by the effective radiating area"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (1.5 W/cm2, found by dividing total power by the effective radiating area): Intensity in W/cm2 is power in watts divided by the ERA in cm2, so 6 W divided by 4 cm2 equals 1.5 W/cm2. Multiplying gives 24, inverting gives 0.67 and adding gives 10, none of which have the correct units. Check: 1.5 times 4 returns 6 W."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 54 year old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has a flare: her second and third metacarpophalangeal joints are warm, red, and swollen, with morning stiffness lasting two hours. She asks whether she should use moist heat or cold. Which choice is best for this stage?", "options": {"A": "Cold applied briefly to the joints, since active inflammation and warmth are present", "B": "Contrast bath ending with heat to promote movement", "C": "A hot whirlpool for the hands, since it softens the swollen joints before exercise", "D": "Moist heat for 20 minutes, since arthritis responds best to warmth"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cold applied briefly to the joints, since active inflammation and warmth are present): Warm, red, swollen joints indicate active inflammation, so brief cold is the safer choice during a flare. Heat suits chronic stiffness without inflammation, not an active flare. A contrast bath that ends in heat still raises temperature in inflamed joints."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with a bone density T-score of minus 3.1 at the lumbar spine has low back pain without leg symptoms and asks about lumbar traction. She has no prior fracture, is otherwise healthy, and walks daily. What is the best response to her request?", "options": {"A": "Avoid lumbar traction because distraction forces carry a fracture risk with severe osteoporosis", "B": "Allow inversion traction, since gravity based pull is gentler than a motorized pull", "C": "Allow traction once her pain has settled for two weeks without medication", "D": "Use traction at 25 percent of body weight, since low forces spare the vertebral bodies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid lumbar traction because distraction forces carry a fracture risk with severe osteoporosis): A T-score of minus 3.1 indicates severe osteoporosis, and traction forces applied through a harness and rib belt raise the risk of vertebral or rib fracture. Lower force does not remove the risk because the compromised bone is the problem. Inversion also loads the spine. A pain free interval does not change bone density."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old man wants to increase quadriceps muscle size after rehabilitation and has no injuries. He currently performs three sets of 25 repetitions with a light load at the end of each session. Which change best matches evidence-based guidelines for muscle hypertrophy?", "options": {"A": "Use loads of about 85 to 95 percent of 1RM for two to five repetitions", "B": "Use loads of about 50 to 60 percent of 1RM for fifteen to twenty repetitions", "C": "Use loads of about 67 to 85 percent of 1RM for eight to twelve repetitions", "D": "Use loads of about 40 to 50 percent of 1RM moved explosively for six repetitions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use loads of about 67 to 85 percent of 1RM for eight to twelve repetitions): Hypertrophy is best driven by moderate to heavy loads, about 67 to 85 percent of 1RM, for eight to twelve repetitions. Loads above 85 percent for few repetitions favor maximal strength. Loads near 50 to 60 percent for fifteen to twenty repetitions remain an endurance stimulus similar to what he does now. Light loads moved explosively train power."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old violinist has an overactive, tender upper trapezius on the left and reports neck tightness after long rehearsals. The therapist wants a kinesiology taping application intended to reduce tone in that muscle. There is no history of neck injury. Which application best matches the technique?", "options": {"A": "Apply a perpendicular strip across the muscle belly with strong tension", "B": "Anchor at the acromion and lay the strip toward the occiput with light tension", "C": "Apply a fan strip from the acromion toward the axilla with no stretch", "D": "Anchor at the occipital origin and lay the strip toward the acromion with light tension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Anchor at the acromion and lay the strip toward the occiput with light tension): In the standard teaching, inhibition tape runs from insertion toward origin with light tension, which here means starting at the acromion and finishing near the occiput. The origin-to-insertion direction is the facilitation pattern and would raise tone. A fan strip is used for swelling, and a perpendicular tensioned strip is a space or correction technique. None of these match the goal of reducing tone."}, {"id": 214, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 42-year-old office manager has a reduced craniovertebral angle, forward head posture, and neck fatigue by midday. The clinician selects a strengthening exercise aimed at the muscle group most likely inhibited in upper crossed syndrome and wants to avoid training the already overactive superficial muscles. Which exercise fits best?", "options": {"A": "Resisted shrugs with dumbbells to train the upper trapezius", "B": "Prone neck extension lifts to train the cervical erector spinae", "C": "Resisted forward head protrusion to train the sternocleidomastoid", "D": "Low-load chin nod holds that train the deep cervical flexors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low-load chin nod holds that train the deep cervical flexors): Low-load chin nods target the deep cervical flexors, which are inhibited in upper crossed syndrome, without recruiting the superficial flexors. Shrugs and extension lifts train muscles that are already tight. Resisted protrusion loads the sternocleidomastoid, which takes over when the deep flexors are weak."}, {"id": 215, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "During continuous 3 MHz ultrasound at 1.5 W/cm2 over the lateral malleolus, a patient reports a sharp, deep ache at the bone. The clinician is moving the head slowly with good gel contact. Which immediate adjustment is most appropriate for this report?", "options": {"A": "Lower the intensity and keep the head moving until the ache resolves", "B": "Hold the head still briefly so the heat can settle into the deeper bone tissue", "C": "Switch to 1 MHz continuous at the same intensity and continue", "D": "Add more gel and continue unchanged at the same intensity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower the intensity and keep the head moving until the ache resolves): A deep, sharp ache over a bony surface suggests periosteal heating, because bone absorbs much of the beam and reflects the rest back at the interface. The fix is to reduce intensity and keep the head moving so that no spot accumulates energy. Stopping movement or leaving intensity unchanged would worsen the pain."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "During a supervised aerobic session, a 54-year-old man is asked to rate how hard he is working using the original Borg scale that runs from 6 to 20. He points to the number 13 on the chart. According to the verbal anchors printed on that scale, what does a rating of 13 describe?", "options": {"A": "Somewhat hard, a moderate effort within the usual range for aerobic training", "B": "Light effort, comparable to an easy stroll that barely raises breathing or heart rate", "C": "Very hard effort, near the upper limit most people can sustain for a few minutes", "D": "Hard effort, where talking in full sentences becomes difficult and fatigue builds quickly"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Somewhat hard, a moderate effort within the usual range for aerobic training): On the 6 to 20 Borg scale, 11 is light, 13 is somewhat hard, 15 is hard, and 17 is very hard. The numbers approximate heart rate in healthy young adults when multiplied by ten. A rating of 13 sits in the moderate range commonly prescribed for aerobic conditioning and cardiac rehabilitation. The other descriptions match 11, 15, and 17."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 35-year-old man was kicked in the thigh three hours ago and has a swelling that is enlarging, with firm tense tissue and bruising spreading distally toward the knee. He asks for ultrasound to treat the injury. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply continuous 1 MHz ultrasound to speed reabsorption of the hematoma", "B": "Apply pulsed 3 MHz ultrasound to break up the clotted blood early and aid recovery", "C": "Apply ultrasound moving at a steady rate to avoid heating a single spot", "D": "Withhold ultrasound over the site until bleeding has stopped, then reassess"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold ultrasound over the site until bleeding has stopped, then reassess): An expanding swelling shortly after trauma suggests ongoing hemorrhage, and ultrasound can increase blood flow and bleeding. It is contraindicated over active hemorrhage regardless of the pulse setting or technique. The area is treated with rest and monitoring first, with ultrasound reconsidered later."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old man has chronic anterolateral thigh pain and numbness from a prior nerve injury. He cannot reliably distinguish warm from cool over the area when tested with test tubes. The clinician is considering thermal ultrasound. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use continuous 1 MHz at 2.0 W/cm2, because deep heating is not felt by the skin", "B": "Use a stationary technique to hold the head in one spot until warmth is reported", "C": "Use a pulsed, low-intensity setting or another modality, because the patient cannot report excessive heat", "D": "Use continuous 3 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 and rely on the patient to report heat"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a pulsed, low-intensity setting or another modality, because the patient cannot report excessive heat): Patient feedback is the main safeguard against overheating in thermal ultrasound, so numb skin is a precaution. Lower, pulsed doses or another approach are safer. High-intensity continuous settings or a stationary head would risk a burn that the patient cannot detect."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A therapist reviews a slow-motion recording of an adult walking and notes the moments when both feet are on the ground together. Across one complete gait cycle, counting both such periods, what share of the cycle is normally spent in double limb support in healthy free-speed walking?", "options": {"A": "About five percent in total, only as the heel first meets the floor", "B": "About ten percent in total, as a single short period at initial contact", "C": "About forty percent in total, equal in length to the swing phase", "D": "About twenty percent, made of two periods of about ten percent each"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About twenty percent, made of two periods of about ten percent each): Double limb support occurs twice per cycle, once after initial contact and once in preswing, each lasting roughly 10 percent. Together they make about 20 percent of the cycle. Counting only one period gives 10 percent, and as walking speed rises the double support share shrinks until running removes it."}, {"id": 220, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A student is asked to choose an exercise to train the deep neck flexors, longus colli and longus capitis, in a patient with chronic neck pain. The exercise should recruit these stabilizing muscles while keeping the superficial neck flexors quiet. Which exercise is the standard choice?", "options": {"A": "Isometric upper trapezius shrug against a heavy dumbbell held in each hand", "B": "Resisted cervical flexion with a hand pressing firmly against the forehead", "C": "Supine head lift off the table holding the chin toward the chest", "D": "Craniocervical flexion, a gentle nodding motion as if saying yes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Craniocervical flexion, a gentle nodding motion as if saying yes): Craniocervical flexion is a low-effort nod that activates the deep flexors, which attach to the cervical vertebrae and the base of the skull. Forceful resisted flexion and supine head lifts are dominated by the sternocleidomastoid. A shrug trains the upper trapezius, which is not a neck flexor."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "Two runners of equal body mass and running economy have identical VO2max values of 60 mL/kg/min. Runner A's blood lactate rises sharply at 60 percent of VO2max, while Runner B's rises sharply at 80 percent. Both race a 10 km event. Which prediction is best supported by these findings?", "options": {"A": "Runner B should hold a faster steady pace, with a higher fraction of VO2max usable", "B": "Both runners should finish together, since equal VO2max and economy fix 10 km performance", "C": "Runner A should hold a faster pace because her earlier lactate rise reflects stronger buffering", "D": "Runner B should slow sooner, because a late lactate rise shows weak glycolytic power"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Runner B should hold a faster steady pace, with a higher fraction of VO2max usable): Lactate threshold sets the fraction of VO2max a runner can sustain, so with equal VO2max and economy the runner whose lactate rises later should hold the faster steady pace. VO2max alone does not fix race time. An early lactate rise reflects a lower sustainable fraction, not better buffering, and a late rise reflects a strength, not weak glycolytic power."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 21-year-old sprinter is about to run a 100 m final in 30 minutes and asks for a massage to feel ready. The therapist wants a technique appropriate for that setting, not one used for recovery. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Slow effleurage with long gliding strokes to relax the legs before the sprint", "B": "A moderate session of deep stripping to the hamstrings and quadriceps to restore length", "C": "Slow sustained compression with joint stretching to increase hamstring range before the sprint", "D": "A short, brisk session of petrissage and tapotement to stimulate the muscles"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A short, brisk session of petrissage and tapotement to stimulate the muscles): A short pre-event session should raise readiness, so brisk petrissage and tapotement stimulate the muscles without lowering tone. Slow effleurage and sustained compression relax tissue, which suits recovery rather than a sprint in 30 minutes. Deep stripping and long sessions can leave the muscles sore or less responsive. The stimulating approach fits the time and the goal."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 47-year-old secretary with chronic neck pain notices that the tingling from a TENS unit reduces her pain within a few minutes of starting. She asks the clinician how it works. The clinician refers to the theory proposed by Melzack and Wall in 1965. Which statement best summarizes the mechanism that theory proposes for conventional TENS?", "options": {"A": "Repetitive depolarization exhausts small C fibers until conduction fails, producing a temporary physiological nerve block.", "B": "Current raises tissue temperature, increasing local blood flow that clears pain-producing chemicals from tissue.", "C": "Large afferent input activates inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn, reducing onward nociceptive transmission.", "D": "Stimulation releases beta-endorphin from the pituitary, which binds opioid receptors and blocks pain perception centrally."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Large afferent input activates inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn, reducing onward nociceptive transmission.): Gate control theory holds that activity in large A-beta afferents, generated by the sensory-level tingling of conventional TENS, excites inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn. These interneurons close the gate on nociceptive input carried by small fibers. The opioid explanation belongs to the endorphin theory of low-rate stimulation. Conventional TENS produces little heat and does not block C fiber conduction."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old man with diabetic peripheral neuropathy has insensate feet, a rigid high arched foot, and a callus under the second and third metatarsal heads. The clinician is choosing an orthotic type to reduce plantar pressure and protect the skin. Which type is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "A semi-rigid orthosis with a heavy forefoot varus post to realign the forefoot", "B": "A rigid functional orthosis with an aggressive medial post to control pronation and arch collapse", "C": "A thin firm insert with a raised metatarsal bar placed under the callus", "D": "A soft accommodative insole with full contact and cushioning to spread plantar pressure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A soft accommodative insole with full contact and cushioning to spread plantar pressure): An insensate foot needs a soft, full-contact accommodative insole that spreads pressure over a large area and protects the skin. A rigid orthosis with a strong post concentrates force on skin that cannot report pain. A heavy forefoot post is intended to realign motion and may create pressure points. A raised bar placed under a callus increases pressure at the very site that needs relief."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 67-year-old retired welder has progressive low back pain for two months that is worse at night and unrelieved by lying still. He has lost 15 pounds without dieting and has a prior prostate cancer history. Lumbar movements are painful in all directions. A colleague suggests starting extension exercises and moist heat. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for urgent medical evaluation and imaging to rule out spinal metastasis", "B": "Start a progressive core stabilization program and monitor weight monthly", "C": "Begin a four-week trial of directional preference exercises and reassess pain at each visit", "D": "Obtain lumbar radiographs through his primary clinic within three months and continue exercise meanwhile"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for urgent medical evaluation and imaging to rule out spinal metastasis): Prior cancer, unexplained weight loss, age over 50 and constant night pain together make spinal metastasis a serious possibility. Prompt medical referral with imaging is the appropriate action. A trial of core work or directional exercise would delay diagnosis, and a routine imaging request three months away is too slow for these red flags. Painful motion in all directions also argues against a simple mechanical pattern."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 35-year-old woman completes six months of endurance training. At follow-up, a clinician reviews respiratory function expecting typical adaptations. At the same submaximal workload her breathing is easier than before. Which respiratory change is expected after endurance training in a healthy person?", "options": {"A": "Lower minute ventilation at a given submaximal workload", "B": "Increased forced vital capacity due to stronger respiratory muscles after training", "C": "Increased resting diffusing capacity from greater alveolar surface area in the lungs", "D": "Greater ventilatory response to carbon dioxide at rest, raising the resting breathing rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower minute ventilation at a given submaximal workload): Endurance training improves ventilatory efficiency, so minute ventilation is lower at the same submaximal workload even though maximal ventilation rises. Vital capacity and resting diffusing capacity change little in healthy adults because lung structure does not adapt to training. The ventilatory response to carbon dioxide tends to be blunted rather than increased, so a higher resting rate is not expected."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician compares two stimulators for quadriceps strengthening: one delivers pulsed current at 50 pps, and the other delivers a 2,500 Hz alternating current in bursts. A patient wants to reach strong contractions with less discomfort at the skin. Which statement best explains the advantage of the medium-frequency alternating current?", "options": {"A": "Each cycle lasts longer at 2,500 Hz, so each pulse recruits more motor units.", "B": "Skin impedance rises as frequency rises, so current is spread over more tissue depth.", "C": "Skin impedance falls as frequency rises, so more current reaches motor nerves with less skin discomfort.", "D": "Medium frequency current produces a polar chemical effect that softens skin and reduces its sensitivity."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Skin impedance falls as frequency rises, so more current reaches motor nerves with less skin discomfort.): Skin behaves partly as a capacitor, so its impedance falls as frequency rises. At 2,500 Hz less of the current is lost crossing the skin, so deeper motor nerves can be stimulated with less sensation at the electrodes. Cycle duration is shorter, not longer, at higher frequency. Alternating current has no net charge, so there is no polar chemical effect."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinician screens a 19-year-old ballet dancer with recurrent shoulder subluxations for generalized joint hypermobility. The clinician uses the Beighton scale, a standardized screen made of brief passive maneuvers at several joints and the spine. Which description of the Beighton scoring structure is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ten points: one point for each thumb, little finger, elbow, knee, and ankle", "B": "Five points: one for each of five joint tests, scored on the dominant side only", "C": "Twelve points: one for each shoulder, hip, elbow, knee, thumb, and little finger", "D": "Nine points: one for each thumb, little finger, elbow, and knee, plus trunk flexion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nine points: one for each thumb, little finger, elbow, and knee, plus trunk flexion): The Beighton scale awards one point for each side for thumb, little finger, elbow, and knee maneuvers, which gives eight points, plus one point for forward trunk flexion with palms to the floor, for a total of nine. It does not include ankles, hips, or shoulders. Scoring only the dominant side would give five points and would miss bilateral hypermobility."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old nurse has numbness in the thumb, index, and middle fingers that wakes her at night and improves when she shakes her hand. Neck motion does not change symptoms, Spurling's test is negative, and Phalen's test reproduces tingling within 40 seconds. Which plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Elbow padding with an extension night splint for cubital tunnel syndrome", "B": "Stretching of the scalenes and pectoralis minor with postural retraining for thoracic outlet syndrome", "C": "Mechanical cervical traction with deep neck flexor strengthening for C6 radiculopathy", "D": "Neutral wrist night splint, activity modification, and tendon and median nerve gliding exercises"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neutral wrist night splint, activity modification, and tendon and median nerve gliding exercises): Nocturnal median-distribution numbness relieved by shaking and a positive Phalen's test with a negative Spurling's test indicates carpal tunnel syndrome. A neutral splint at night, activity changes, and gliding exercises are the usual conservative plan. Neck traction, scalene stretching, and elbow padding target other sites."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 72-year-old retired teacher has been cleared for exercise by her physician, has normal strength, and has mild fear of falling after a friend's fall. She wants an enjoyable group activity. Which activity has trial evidence for reducing falls in community-dwelling older adults?", "options": {"A": "Tai Chi classes with slow controlled weight shifting", "B": "Walking club sessions at a moderate pace on level ground three times weekly", "C": "Group aerobic dance classes with light hand weights focused on cardiovascular conditioning", "D": "Yoga classes emphasizing seated stretching and floor-based flexibility work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tai Chi classes with slow controlled weight shifting): Tai Chi, with slow controlled weight shifting, has randomized trial evidence for lowering falls in community-dwelling older adults. Walking clubs and aerobic dance improve fitness but do not target balance or reduce falls consistently. Seated or floor-based flexibility yoga lacks the dynamic standing balance demand."}, {"id": 231, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 52-year-old woman with no cardiac disease has been told to walk at a moderate intensity. During her session she rates her effort as 17 on the 6 to 20 Borg scale, described as very hard, and she is breathing heavily and cannot speak in sentences. What adjustment is best?", "options": {"A": "Slow the pace until her rating falls to about 9 to 10", "B": "Slow the pace until her rating falls to about 12 to 13", "C": "Maintain the pace but add a two minute rest break every ten minutes", "D": "Stop the session and refer for a cardiac stress test before any further walking"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slow the pace until her rating falls to about 12 to 13): Moderate intensity matches roughly 12 to 13 on the 6 to 20 Borg scale, and a rating of 17 with heavy breathing and no ability to speak shows she is well above it. Slowing to the moderate range corrects the prescription. A rating of 9 to 10 is very light and would under dose her. Added rest breaks leave the pace too high, and stopping for a stress test is unnecessary in a woman with no cardiac disease and no symptoms."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old man eight weeks after a right middle cerebral artery stroke stands with about 70 percent of weight on his right leg and avoids loading the left. He reaches poorly to the left. Which training is best to improve his standing balance?", "options": {"A": "Seated reaching to the right to keep weight off the left leg while building trunk control", "B": "Lateral weight shifting toward the left leg with mirror feedback", "C": "Single-leg stance on the right leg to build confidence before loading the left", "D": "Wide stance static standing with most weight kept on the right leg"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lateral weight shifting toward the left leg with mirror feedback): Asymmetric weight bearing reinforces itself, so training should drive loading of the paretic left leg through controlled weight shifts with visual feedback. Seated work builds trunk control but not standing symmetry. Right single-leg stance trains the limb that already works. Static wide standing with weight on the right repeats the faulty pattern."}, {"id": 233, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 20 year old sprinter ices her sore hamstring for 20 minutes during halftime, and the coach sends her back to full sprinting immediately afterward. The athletic trainer objects to returning her to maximal effort so soon. Which physiological concern best supports the objection?", "options": {"A": "Cooling lowers conduction and proprioception, raising reinjury risk at maximal effort", "B": "Cooling increases local blood flow, which causes swelling", "C": "Cooling raises tissue metabolism, which causes early fatigue", "D": "Cooling increases tissue extensibility, which allows excessive stretch during the sprint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cooling lowers conduction and proprioception, raising reinjury risk at maximal effort): Cold slows nerve conduction and dulls proprioception and protective reflexes for a time. Returning to full effort too soon therefore risks reinjury. Cold lowers rather than raises metabolism and blood flow. It also reduces collagen extensibility rather than increasing it."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student prepares an interferential current unit for a patient with chronic low back pain. The device delivers two alternating currents through separate channels, and the manual calls each one a carrier. Which description fits the carrier frequency range that interferential current traditionally uses so the current crosses skin with relatively low impedance?", "options": {"A": "A low frequency near 50 Hz delivered on each of the two channels", "B": "A medium frequency near 4000 Hz delivered on each of the two channels", "C": "A medium frequency near 100 Hz delivered on each of the two channels", "D": "A radiofrequency near 27 MHz delivered on each of the two channels"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A medium frequency near 4000 Hz delivered on each of the two channels): Interferential current uses medium-frequency carriers, traditionally about 4000 Hz, because skin impedance falls as frequency rises and the current crosses skin comfortably. A 50 Hz current is a typical low-frequency value and meets much more skin resistance. A 100 Hz rate is also low and resembles a beat or pulse rate, not a carrier. A 27 MHz current belongs to shortwave diathermy, a heating agent rather than an electrical stimulation current."}, {"id": 235, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 40 year old sprinter suffers a grade 1 hamstring strain and begins treatment on day 1 with cold, compression, and relative rest. On day 4, bruising is stable, swelling is minimal, and tenderness is improving. When is it generally appropriate to introduce moist heat before gentle stretching for this subacute strain?", "options": {"A": "After the first 48 to 72 hours, once acute inflammation has subsided", "B": "Within the first 24 hours, to speed removal of the hematoma", "C": "At the time of injury, alternating immediately with cold packs", "D": "Not until 6 weeks, after remodeling is finished"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (After the first 48 to 72 hours, once acute inflammation has subsided): Heat is generally introduced once the acute inflammatory phase has settled, typically after about 48 to 72 hours, as in this patient on day 4. Heat in the first day risks more bleeding and swelling. Waiting six weeks delays a useful treatment, and alternating heat with cold at injury confuses the two phases."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 71-year-old woman with a bone density T-score of minus 3.1 and a prior thoracic vertebral compression fracture requests a vigorous hacking and cupping massage of her thoracic spine. She takes calcium and vitamin D. What is the best approach for the therapist to take?", "options": {"A": "Use hacking at light pressure, since it stimulates blood flow without stressing the spine", "B": "Use deep thumb friction over each spinous process to assess tenderness", "C": "Use cupping over the ribs and spine for a short period", "D": "Avoid percussive strokes and use only gentle gliding strokes over the thoracic region"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid percussive strokes and use only gentle gliding strokes over the thoracic region): Percussion and deep focal pressure transmit force into bone, and with severe osteoporosis and a prior compression fracture they risk another fracture. Gentle gliding strokes give the comfort and circulatory effect without loading the spine. Bone density findings should guide the choice of technique whenever percussion is considered."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 46-year-old with plantar heel pain has first-step pain in the morning, tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle, and a flexible, mildly pronated foot. She wants a prefabricated insert that reduces strain on the plantar fascia. Which design is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "A one inch heel lift with a flat top cover and no arch contour", "B": "A rigid carbon plate insert with a forefoot rocker to limit great toe extension", "C": "A firm lateral wedged insert with a high lateral arch to unload the medial foot", "D": "A contoured insert with medial arch support and a cushioned heel cup"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A contoured insert with medial arch support and a cushioned heel cup): Plantar fasciitis in a flexible pronated foot responds to a contoured insert with medial arch support and a cushioned heel cup that offloads the fascia and heel. A plain heel lift with no arch contour leaves the fascia strained. A rigid rocker plate suits great toe stiffness. A lateral wedge shifts load away from the medial side and does not support the arch."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "An instructor contrasts two electrical agents in a modality lab. For interferential current the students need not label electrodes as positive or negative, but for high voltage pulsed current they must. A student asks why polarity matters only for the second agent. Which statement correctly explains this?", "options": {"A": "Its higher peak voltage reverses the direction of ion flow on alternate pulses", "B": "Its pulses are monophasic, so net charge accumulates under each electrode with distinct effects", "C": "Its carrier frequency is lower, so each electrode takes on one fixed charge", "D": "Its pulses are biphasic, but the phases are unequal in duration"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Its pulses are monophasic, so net charge accumulates under each electrode with distinct effects): High voltage pulsed current is monophasic, so current flows one direction and each electrode keeps a defined polarity with its own tissue effects. Interferential current alternates and its net charge averages to zero, so electrode labels carry no meaning. High voltage pulsed current has no carrier. The pulse is not biphasic, and voltage level does not reverse ion flow."}, {"id": 239, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "During a slow resisted elbow curl performed by a healthy volunteer, the biceps shortens substantially, yet muscle spindle afferents keep signaling length changes rather than going silent. A researcher asks which mechanism keeps the spindle sensitive while the extrafusal fibers shorten. What is it?", "options": {"A": "Reciprocal inhibition, in which the triceps drives alpha motor neurons to keep the spindle taut", "B": "Alpha gamma coactivation, in which gamma motor neurons shorten the intrafusal fibers during contraction", "C": "Presynaptic inhibition, in which spindle afferents are blocked from reaching the spinal cord", "D": "Autogenic inhibition, in which Golgi tendon organs lengthen intrafusal fibers during contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alpha gamma coactivation, in which gamma motor neurons shorten the intrafusal fibers during contraction): When alpha motor neurons contract the extrafusal fibers, gamma motor neurons are activated at the same time and shorten the polar ends of the intrafusal fibers. This keeps the central sensory region taut so the spindle continues to report length. The Golgi tendon organ does not act on intrafusal fibers, and blocking afferent input would silence the spindle rather than preserve it."}, {"id": 240, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 38-year-old data analyst spends nine hours each day at a monitor and presents with rounded shoulders, a forward head posture, and neck fatigue by late afternoon. Applying Janda's upper crossed syndrome model to this presentation, a clinician would expect which group of muscles to be inhibited and weak rather than shortened and overactive?", "options": {"A": "The deep cervical flexors together with the lower trapezius and serratus anterior", "B": "The upper trapezius together with the levator scapulae and sternocleidomastoid", "C": "The suboccipitals together with the cervical erector spinae and the scalenes", "D": "The pectoralis major together with the pectoralis minor and anterior deltoid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The deep cervical flexors together with the lower trapezius and serratus anterior): In upper crossed syndrome the deep cervical flexors, lower trapezius, serratus anterior and rhomboids are inhibited and lengthened, while the upper trapezius, levator scapulae, sternocleidomastoid and pectoral muscles become tight. The upper trapezius group and the pectorals are the tight arm of the cross, so they would be stretched rather than strengthened. The suboccipitals, cervical extensors and scalenes also shorten in a forward head posture."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with chronic knee osteoarthritis tolerates neither a steady tingling sensation nor strong muscle twitching from earlier TENS trials. The clinician wants a mode that groups high-frequency pulses into packets delivered at a low repetition rate, hoping to combine benefits of both settings. Which mode should be selected?", "options": {"A": "Brief intense mode, with pulses near 100 pps of long duration at tolerable intensity.", "B": "Acupuncture-like mode, with continuous pulses near 4 pps at intensity producing visible twitches.", "C": "Burst mode, with pulses near 100 pps grouped into bursts about 2 times per second.", "D": "Conventional mode, with a continuous train of pulses near 100 pps at tingling intensity."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Burst mode, with pulses near 100 pps grouped into bursts about 2 times per second.): Burst mode delivers packets of high-frequency pulses, near 100 pps, repeated at a low rate of about 1 to 4 bursts per second. It combines a high-frequency component with a low-frequency burst envelope and can be more comfortable than twitching at motor level. Conventional and brief intense modes deliver continuous trains without burst grouping. Acupuncture-like mode is a continuous low-rate train."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "On the sensory organization test, a 55-year-old woman scores normally when the platform is fixed with her eyes open or closed. She loses balance only when the platform sways and vision is absent or sway-referenced. Her hearing is normal. Which interpretation best fits this pattern?", "options": {"A": "A vestibular loss pattern, since only vestibular input remains on those conditions", "B": "A somatosensory loss pattern, since she is steady on the fixed platform", "C": "A normal pattern, since failures on sway-referenced conditions are expected", "D": "A visual preference pattern, since she follows misleading visual cues"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A vestibular loss pattern, since only vestibular input remains on those conditions): A person with vestibular loss compensates with vision and somatosensation when they are accurate, but loses balance when both are unreliable and only the labyrinth can help. Healthy people stay upright in those conditions. A visual preference pattern fails when vision is misleading, and somatosensory loss would affect the surface conditions with the eyes closed."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 50-year-old man is prescribed training at 70 percent of age-predicted maximum heart rate, with no reserve calculation, for his first aerobic session. The clinician uses the traditional 220 minus age equation to estimate his maximum. What target heart rate should be recorded in beats per minute?", "options": {"A": "154 beats per minute, calculated as 0.70 times 220", "B": "119 beats per minute, calculated as 0.70 times 170", "C": "126 beats per minute, calculated as 0.70 times 180", "D": "136 beats per minute, calculated as 0.80 times 170"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (119 beats per minute, calculated as 0.70 times 170): Age-predicted maximum is 220 minus 50, or 170 beats per minute. Seventy percent of 170 is 119 beats per minute. Multiplying 220 by 0.70 gives 154 and skips the age subtraction. A maximum of 180 gives 126, and 80 percent of 170 gives 136, which does not match the prescribed intensity."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with low back pain attempts the bird dog on hands and knees. When he lifts the opposite arm and leg together his trunk rotates and the lumbar spine sags. The therapist wants a regression that preserves the quadruped position and neutral spine. What should the therapist change first in the program?", "options": {"A": "Move to a prone position on the table and perform the same lifts", "B": "Add a Swiss ball under the abdomen to increase the instability challenge", "C": "Reduce the lift to a small range while keeping both limbs lifted together", "D": "Lift only one limb at a time until the trunk stays quiet, then combine them"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lift only one limb at a time until the trunk stays quiet, then combine them): Lifting a single limb halves the perturbation to the trunk, letting the patient learn to hold neutral before the two limbs are combined. Shortening the range while lifting both limbs still produces the same rotational challenge. Prone lifts train the extensors in a different posture and do not regress the demand for anti-rotation control. A Swiss ball makes the task less stable and harder."}, {"id": 245, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A therapist prepares a moist heat treatment for a patient with chronic upper trapezius tightness. The pack comes out of the tank wet and very hot, and the patient will lie against the plinth during the session. What toweling arrangement between the pack and the skin is conventionally taught for a standard hydrocollator pack without a commercial terry cover?", "options": {"A": "A single thin dry towel so the heat reaches the muscle quickly", "B": "One layer of plastic wrap, with the pack applied directly over it", "C": "Two layers of damp toweling to improve heat conduction into the muscle", "D": "Six to eight layers of dry toweling, with the pack placed over them"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Six to eight layers of dry toweling, with the pack placed over them): A hydrocollator pack straight from the tank is far hotter than skin tolerates, so about six to eight layers of dry toweling provide the insulation. Dry layers trap air and slow conduction, whereas a thin towel would transmit too much heat. Damp toweling conducts heat better than dry toweling and increases burn risk. Plastic wrap adds no meaningful insulation."}, {"id": 246, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 38-year-old dental hygienist with right C6 radiculopathy is positioned supine for mechanical cervical traction using an occipital halter. The clinician wants to maximize opening of the posterior intervertebral foramina at the lower cervical levels during the pull. Which neck position and line of pull best meets that goal during the treatment?", "options": {"A": "Neck flexed about 60 degrees with the chin tucked, with the pull directed downward", "B": "Neck flexed about 20 to 30 degrees, with the pull applied along that line", "C": "Neck extended about 20 degrees, with the pull directed upward and slightly posterior", "D": "Neck held at neutral zero degrees, with the pull applied straight along the table"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neck flexed about 20 to 30 degrees, with the pull applied along that line): Flexion of roughly 20 to 30 degrees opens the posterior elements and the intervertebral foramina at the lower cervical segments, so the line of pull should follow that angle. A neutral position places relatively more stress on anterior structures and gives less foraminal opening. Extension narrows the foramina and tends to aggravate radicular symptoms. Flexion near 60 degrees goes far beyond the range used clinically and over-stretches the posterior ligaments."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old man with a healed quadriceps tendon repair wants to isolate and measure quadriceps strength without help from the hip extensors and calf. He is eight months out and cleared for full activity. Leg press and squats let other muscles assist. Which exercise best isolates the quadriceps?", "options": {"A": "Front squat to parallel holding a barbell across the shoulders", "B": "Seated open chain knee extension against a lever arm at the ankle", "C": "Seated leg press emphasizing the quadriceps with feet low on the platform", "D": "Bulgarian split squat with the rear foot elevated and a dumbbell in each hand"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seated open chain knee extension against a lever arm at the ankle): Seated open chain knee extension fixes the hip and moves only the knee against the lever arm, so the quadriceps work with little help from other muscles. Front squats, leg presses and split squats are closed chain lifts in which the gluteals, hamstrings and calf share the load. They build the quadriceps but cannot isolate it."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 7-year-old boy with calf enlargement uses his hands to climb up his own legs when rising from the floor. He walks with a wide base, lumbar lordosis, and the pelvis dropping alternately on each side. What is the best description of his gait?", "options": {"A": "Trendelenburg gait from unilateral gluteus medius weakness typical of superior gluteal nerve injury", "B": "Antalgic gait with a shortened stance on a painful hip typical of transient synovitis", "C": "Cerebellar ataxic gait with a wide base and irregular steps typical of vermis disease", "D": "Waddling gait from bilateral hip abductor weakness typical of muscular dystrophy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Waddling gait from bilateral hip abductor weakness typical of muscular dystrophy): A wide base, lordosis and alternating pelvic drop reflect weak hip abductors on both sides, giving a waddling gait. With calf pseudohypertrophy and climbing the legs with the hands, this fits muscular dystrophy. A Trendelenburg gait is one-sided, and an antalgic gait is a limp driven by pain. Ataxia also has a wide base but shows irregular steps without the pelvic drop or Gowers maneuver."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient after a stroke can hold the quadruped posture with co-contraction for thirty seconds but loses trunk control whenever she shifts her weight forward. Using the Rood and Sullivan stages of motor control, the therapist must decide which stage to train next. Which choice is best for her?", "options": {"A": "Mobility, practiced through passive range of motion in supine", "B": "Controlled mobility, practiced through graded weight shifts within the quadruped posture", "C": "Skill, practiced through reaching tasks in standing with a moving target", "D": "Stability, practiced through longer isometric holds in the same posture"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Controlled mobility, practiced through graded weight shifts within the quadruped posture): She has achieved stability in quadruped and now needs controlled mobility, which is moving within a weight-bearing posture while keeping control. Graded weight shifts train this. More holds repeat a stage she has mastered, and passive range of motion is a regression. Skill tasks in standing are a later step."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old with a cervical sprain reports jaw soreness after her first session of seated cervical traction with a chin and occiput halter set at 12 lb. The therapist suspects the harness rather than the neck is responsible for the new complaint. Which structure is most likely being stressed by this type of halter?", "options": {"A": "The temporomandibular joint, through pressure of the chin strap against the mandible", "B": "The sternoclavicular joint, through downward force transmitted from the cervical fascia", "C": "The hyoid bone, through pressure of the halter against the anterior neck", "D": "The atlantoaxial joint, through tension placed on the transverse ligament of the atlas"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The temporomandibular joint, through pressure of the chin strap against the mandible): A chin strap transmits part of the pull through the mandible, so the temporomandibular joint can become sore, especially when the jaw is clenched or the strap is tight. The transverse ligament of the atlas is a concern in rheumatoid instability, not a typical result of halter pressure. The hyoid lies behind the chin strap and is not loaded by a properly placed halter. The sternoclavicular joint is not meaningfully loaded by a cervical pull."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student practices massage on a classmate who has mild ankle swelling after a long flight. The instructor asks for a long, smooth stroke made with the whole palm, light to moderate pressure, ending proximally on the limb. Which stroke is the instructor describing, and what is its main effect on the tissues?", "options": {"A": "Petrissage, which lifts and rolls the muscle belly deeply", "B": "Vibration, which shakes the limb rhythmically to promote relaxation and venous drainage", "C": "Friction, which uses small deep circles to break up local adhesions and scar tissue", "D": "Effleurage, which assists venous and lymphatic return toward the heart"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Effleurage, which assists venous and lymphatic return toward the heart): A long, smooth, whole-palm stroke with light to moderate pressure directed toward the heart is effleurage, which assists venous and lymphatic return. Petrissage lifts and rolls the muscle belly rather than gliding. Vibration is a fine rhythmic shaking, and friction is a small deep circular stroke aimed at local adhesions, so neither fits the description or suits mild ankle swelling."}, {"id": 252, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "An 83-year-old woman fell last month and has weak quadriceps with a Berg score of 36. Her physician asks for a home program that matches the Otago Exercise Programme model, which has fall prevention trial support. Which description fits that model?", "options": {"A": "Individually prescribed leg strengthening and balance exercises with a walking plan, delivered at home", "B": "Group classes at a community center, using the same balance and strengthening exercises", "C": "Individually prescribed upper limb strengthening and flexibility exercises at home with walking", "D": "Supervised clinic balance training twice weekly on a platform with an assistant"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Individually prescribed leg strengthening and balance exercises with a walking plan, delivered at home): The Otago Exercise Programme is individually tailored to each person at home, combining leg strengthening, balance retraining and a walking plan. Group classes share one program, upper limb work misses the leg and balance deficits, and clinic-based platform training is not the home model."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old retired teacher reports buttock and bilateral thigh heaviness after walking two blocks. The symptoms ease within a minute when she leans on a shopping cart or sits down, and they worsen walking downhill. Her imaging confirms central lumbar canal narrowing. Which posture or movement typically relieves neurogenic claudication in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Lateral flexion toward the painful side held at end range", "B": "Lumbar extension, such as arching backward or lying prone on the elbows", "C": "Lumbar rotation, such as twisting the trunk while standing upright", "D": "Lumbar flexion, such as forward leaning or hugging the knees"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lumbar flexion, such as forward leaning or hugging the knees): Flexion increases canal and foramen dimensions and tightens the ligamentum flavum, which relieves pressure on the neural elements. That is why patients with stenosis tolerate sitting and leaning forward and flare with standing, extension, and downhill walking. Extension narrows the canal further. Rotation and side bending do not open the canal in a useful way."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A certified athletic trainer sideline-tests a high school soccer player for a suspected concussion using the Balance Error Scoring System. The athlete performs double-leg, single-leg, and tandem stances with eyes closed, first on firm ground and then on a foam pad. How long is each of the six trials, and what is the maximum error count per trial?", "options": {"A": "Twenty seconds per trial, with a maximum of 5 errors per trial", "B": "Thirty seconds per trial, with a maximum of 6 errors per trial", "C": "Twenty seconds each, with at most 10 errors per trial", "D": "Thirty seconds per trial, with a maximum of 10 errors per trial"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Twenty seconds each, with at most 10 errors per trial): Each BESS trial lasts 20 seconds with the eyes closed and the hands on the iliac crests, and the examiner counts errors up to a maximum of 10 for that trial. Three stances on two surfaces give six trials and a possible total of 60 errors. The 30 second duration and the lower error ceilings do not belong to this test."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 43-year-old swimmer lies supine with the knees bent and the lumbar spine flat, but the arms cannot reach overhead to the table without the low back arching. Thomas test is negative on both sides. Which structure most likely limits overhead reach, and which approach fits?", "options": {"A": "Shortened iliopsoas, so perform kneeling hip flexor stretches before each swim", "B": "Weak rhomboids, so strengthen scapular retraction at high loads", "C": "Weak serratus anterior, so strengthen with push-up plus exercises", "D": "Shortened latissimus dorsi, so stretch it while maintaining a flat lumbar spine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shortened latissimus dorsi, so stretch it while maintaining a flat lumbar spine): A short latissimus dorsi limits overhead reach and pulls the lumbar spine into extension to compensate, and the negative Thomas test rules out the iliopsoas. Stretching it with the lumbar spine held flat isolates the muscle. The rhomboids and serratus anterior do not limit passive overhead reach."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A rehabilitation team is introducing surface electromyographic biofeedback to patients who have difficulty sensing weak muscle contractions during neuromuscular re-education. The team lead wants new staff to understand what the device actually does for the patient during exercise practice. Which statement best describes the purpose of electromyographic biofeedback in this setting?", "options": {"A": "It records joint angle during movement and sounds a tone when the target range is reached", "B": "It converts muscle activity into sound or visual signals so patients learn to modify it", "C": "It uses a current to produce a contraction that the patient watches and then copies", "D": "It reports force from a dynamometer so the patient can reach a target load"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It converts muscle activity into sound or visual signals so patients learn to modify it): Surface electromyographic biofeedback records muscle electrical activity and shows it as a tone or display, so the patient can sense and adjust a weak contraction. Joint angle feedback uses goniometric sensors and reports range, not muscle activity. A device that produces the contraction with current is electrical stimulation. Dynamometry reports force output, which is a result rather than the muscle activation the patient needs to feel."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 44-year-old runner has lateral thigh and leg aching that mimics sciatica but has a negative straight leg raise, normal reflexes and normal strength. Palpation of the gluteus minimus reproduces the lateral thigh pain exactly. She has no back pain. Which conclusion is best?", "options": {"A": "An L5 radiculopathy is present, since the pain travels below the knee along the leg", "B": "An active gluteus minimus trigger point is referring pain to the lateral thigh and leg", "C": "Iliotibial band friction syndrome is the cause, since pain is lateral", "D": "Piriformis syndrome is present, since the pain begins in the buttock"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An active gluteus minimus trigger point is referring pain to the lateral thigh and leg): A negative straight leg raise, normal reflexes and reproduction of the familiar pain with palpation point to gluteus minimus trigger point referral, which typically spreads down the lateral thigh and leg. Radiculopathy would usually show neurologic findings, and iliotibial band syndrome and piriformis syndrome have their own distinct examination patterns. Palpation that reproduces the familiar pain is the key finding that links the muscle to the complaint."}, {"id": 258, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 66-year-old man is two weeks past coronary artery bypass grafting through a median sternotomy and is cleared for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation. He asks to add the barbell bench press he performed before surgery. His walking tolerance is good and his incision is healing well. What is the best guidance about upper-body resistance work at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Begin the bench press with a light bar and about 15 repetitions, avoiding breath holding", "B": "Substitute push-ups against the floor, since body weight is less stressful than a barbell", "C": "Use rapid overhead medicine ball throws to restore chest wall mobility before strength work", "D": "Defer loaded pushing and pulling with the arms until the surgeon clears the sternum"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defer loaded pushing and pulling with the arms until the surgeon clears the sternum): After a median sternotomy the divided sternum needs about six to eight weeks to heal, and pushing, pulling, or lifting with the arms creates tension across it. Walking and lower-body work can proceed. Light barbell pressing and push-ups both load the chest wall and arms substantially. Rapid overhead throws add dynamic stress, so loaded arm work waits for surgical clearance."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old distance runner with no injuries wants the largest immediate gain in passive hamstring range before a testing session next week. A clinician is comparing static stretching with PNF contract-relax. The runner has a willing partner and no pain with resisted knee flexion. Which approach is most likely to produce the largest short-term range gain?", "options": {"A": "One passive static hold of 30 seconds without any muscle contraction beforehand", "B": "PNF contract-relax with partner resistance followed by a passive stretch of about 30 seconds", "C": "Ballistic bouncing for 30 seconds, using rapid repetitions at the end of range", "D": "Brief 5-second static holds repeated many times without contraction or partner assistance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (PNF contract-relax with partner resistance followed by a passive stretch of about 30 seconds): PNF techniques that include a contraction before the passive stretch, such as contract-relax, tend to produce slightly larger acute range gains than passive static stretching alone. They also give the runner a partner-assisted method with no pain on contraction. A single static hold gives less range, and ballistic bouncing provokes the stretch reflex. Very brief static holds do not create enough creep to change range."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 25-year-old male lifter eats 0.8 g of protein per kg daily and trains four times weekly, yet gains little lean mass. He asks whether he must time protein around workouts to fix this. Which recommendation is best supported by sports nutrition evidence?", "options": {"A": "Replace protein with carbohydrate after workouts, which drives muscle protein synthesis more than amino acids", "B": "Keep intake at 0.8 g per kg and take it all within 30 minutes post-workout", "C": "Increase intake to 3.5 g per kg daily to speed muscle growth beyond the usual range", "D": "Raise total daily intake to about 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg, spread across meals"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Raise total daily intake to about 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg, spread across meals): For people lifting to gain lean mass, total daily protein of about 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg is the best supported target, with timing a smaller refinement. At 0.8 g per kg he is at the sedentary recommendation and timing cannot make up the shortfall. Intakes far above the range add little, and carbohydrate does not substitute for amino acids in synthesis."}, {"id": 261, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 74-year-old woman has positional vertigo. She has severe cervical stenosis with signs of myelopathy, so neck extension is unsafe. The therapist still needs to examine her for posterior canal BPPV today in the clinic. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use standard Dix-Hallpike with the neck extended and held for 60 seconds", "B": "Use the supine roll test, which provokes the horizontal canal without neck extension", "C": "Use Brandt-Daroff exercises first and examine only if symptoms persist", "D": "Use the side-lying test, which avoids neck extension, in place of standard Dix-Hallpike"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the side-lying test, which avoids neck extension, in place of standard Dix-Hallpike): The side-lying test moves her from sitting to side-lying and provokes the posterior canal without sustained neck extension, protecting the myelopathic cord. Standard Dix-Hallpike needs extension. The roll test examines the horizontal canal. Brandt-Daroff is a treatment, so using it first skips diagnosis."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A student asks why a quick, bouncing stretch of the calf produces a brisk contraction of the same muscle. The instructor says a receptor lying parallel to the extrafusal fibers monitors both the length of the muscle and how fast that length is changing. Which receptor and response does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Muscle spindle, whose primary afferents trigger alpha motor neuron contraction through the myotatic reflex", "B": "Muscle spindle, whose afferents trigger relaxation of the muscle through autogenic inhibition", "C": "Golgi tendon organ, whose Ib afferents trigger relaxation of the muscle being stretched", "D": "Ruffini ending, whose slow adapting afferents trigger contraction through the crossed extensor reflex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Muscle spindle, whose primary afferents trigger alpha motor neuron contraction through the myotatic reflex): The muscle spindle sits parallel to the extrafusal fibers, and its primary afferents respond to both stretch and the velocity of stretch. They excite the alpha motor neuron of the same muscle, the myotatic reflex, which explains the brisk contraction after a bounce. The Golgi tendon organ lies in series and senses tension, and autogenic inhibition belongs to it rather than the spindle."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with osteoporosis and a lumbar spine T-score of minus 3.1 wants to strengthen her trunk muscles. She is otherwise healthy and walks daily. She has no fractures but has a mild forward stoop. Which exercise best fits her condition?", "options": {"A": "Standing trunk rotation against cable resistance with a neutral spine at moderate load", "B": "Prone or standing back extension with a neutral spine, avoiding loaded forward flexion", "C": "Abdominal crunches on a stability ball, holding the end position to build endurance", "D": "Quadruped cat-camel stretches through full spinal flexion to reduce the stoop"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prone or standing back extension with a neutral spine, avoiding loaded forward flexion): Loaded or repeated forward flexion raises compressive force on the anterior vertebral bodies and is linked with compression fractures when the T-score is this low. Back extension with a neutral spine strengthens the extensors and counters the stoop without that risk. Loaded rotation adds torsion to weakened bone. Crunches and end range cat-camel flexion both repeatedly load the spine in flexion."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old warehouse worker with low back pain must lift 40 lb boxes from the floor. The therapist wants to teach a cue that stiffens the spine during the lift using the co-contraction of the abdominal wall that McGill favors for loaded tasks. Which cue should the therapist give the worker to use during lifting?", "options": {"A": "Gently contract the abdominal wall in all directions as if bracing, without pulling the belly in", "B": "Exhale fully through pursed lips and keep the abdomen soft during the lift", "C": "Tuck the pelvis backward and flatten the lumbar curve before gripping the box and lifting", "D": "Pull the navel firmly toward the spine to hollow the abdomen before starting each lift"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gently contract the abdominal wall in all directions as if bracing, without pulling the belly in): McGill favors abdominal bracing, a co-contraction of the whole abdominal wall that increases spinal stiffness without drawing the belly in, for loaded tasks like lifting. Hollowing activates the transversus abdominis in isolation and gives less stiffness. Flattening the lumbar curve moves the spine toward flexion under load. A soft abdomen with a full exhale leaves the spine less stiff."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old with low back pain has a straight leg raise of 50 degrees bilaterally. In long sitting he reaches his toes by rounding the lumbar spine and flexing the neck while the knees stay straight. The therapist needs a hamstring stretch that targets the muscle rather than lumbar flexion. Which exercise is best?", "options": {"A": "Supine strap-assisted hamstring stretch with the opposite leg flat and the lumbar spine neutral", "B": "Standing hamstring stretch with the heel on a stool and the trunk folded toward the knee", "C": "Prone knee flexion stretch for the quadriceps with a strap, since hip flexor tightness limits reach", "D": "Long-sitting toe touch with a contract-relax cycle while reaching forward with the trunk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Supine strap-assisted hamstring stretch with the opposite leg flat and the lumbar spine neutral): Stretching supine with a strap, the opposite leg flat and the lumbar spine neutral isolates the hamstrings and removes lumbar flexion. Folding the trunk over a raised heel lets the spine round instead of lengthening the muscle. A quadriceps stretch treats a different muscle. A forward reach in long sitting repeats the pattern he already uses, even with contract-relax."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has a right leg that is 3 cm shorter than the left from an old femur fracture. A clinician views her from behind during walking. Which finding is expected during right stance in this structural short leg gait?", "options": {"A": "The right foot slaps down hard at loading from weak dorsiflexors", "B": "The pelvis rises on the right side as the body stays upright", "C": "The pelvis and trunk drop toward the short right side during stance", "D": "The trunk leans sharply to the left to move over the longer leg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The pelvis and trunk drop toward the short right side during stance): With a shorter limb the body drops toward the short side whenever that limb bears weight, so the pelvis and trunk dip on the right during right stance. The longer limb may be managed by hip hiking, vaulting, or circumduction in swing. Foot slap is a dorsiflexor problem, not a limb length problem."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 52-year-old man with hip pain stands on his right leg during a single leg stance test, and his left pelvis drops sharply while his trunk shifts slightly. The therapist plans strengthening for the weak muscle responsible for this finding. Which muscle is weak or inhibited, and on which side?", "options": {"A": "Right adductor longus, the adductor on the supporting limb side", "B": "Right gluteus medius, the abductor on the stance limb", "C": "Left gluteus maximus, the hip extensor on the lifted leg side", "D": "Left gluteus medius, the abductor on the side of the dropped pelvis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Right gluteus medius, the abductor on the stance limb): In single leg stance the abductors of the stance hip hold the pelvis level against gravity. When they are weak, the opposite pelvis drops, so a left pelvic drop while standing on the right indicates right gluteus medius weakness. The left side muscles are not the ones supporting the pelvis, and adductors or extensors do not provide frontal plane pelvic control."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 57-year-old man completes a symptom-limited treadmill test after a stent and reaches a peak oxygen uptake of 24.5 milliliters per kilogram per minute. His therapist wants to express this peak capacity in METs to select appropriate home activities. Using the standard resting value, what is his peak capacity in METs?", "options": {"A": "7 METs, found by dividing 24.5 by the resting value of 3.5", "B": "12.3 METs, found by dividing 24.5 by 2.0 as the resting value", "C": "5.0 METs, found by dividing 24.5 by 4.9 as the resting value", "D": "8.4 METs, found by dividing 24.5 by 2.9 as the resting value"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (7 METs, found by dividing 24.5 by the resting value of 3.5): One MET equals 3.5 mL per kg per minute, so METs equal oxygen uptake divided by 3.5. Dividing 24.5 by 3.5 gives 7.0, and multiplying 7 by 3.5 returns 24.5 to confirm it. Using a divisor of 2.0 or 2.9 inflates the result to 12.3 or 8.4, while 4.9 deflates it to 5.0. Only the standard resting value of 3.5 gives the correct conversion."}, {"id": 269, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 36-year-old marathon runner must travel for 6 weeks with only brief gym access. Before leaving, she can leg press 250 lb for 8 repetitions. Her goal is to keep her current leg strength with limited time. Which plan best maintains strength during the trip?", "options": {"A": "Lower the load to 50 percent and keep the same weekly volume and sessions", "B": "Stop lifting and rely on daily walking, since aerobic work preserves leg strength", "C": "Perform high volume light circuits daily, since volume is the main driver of maintaining strength", "D": "Keep the load heavy but cut volume to one or two short weekly sessions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the load heavy but cut volume to one or two short weekly sessions): Research on detraining and maintenance shows strength can be held with much less volume and frequency as long as training intensity stays high. A heavy load once or twice a week fits her limited time. Lowering the load to 50 percent loses the key stimulus, walking does not preserve strength, and high volume light work is not what maintains it."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 36 year old software developer has chronic neck pain with tenderness over the cervical facet joints and the anterior neck muscles. The chiropractor plans an 810 nm laser course. A thyroid ultrasound last year showed a small nodule that is being monitored. Which treatment plan is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Defer all laser treatment to the neck until the nodule is surgically removed", "B": "Treat the anterior neck with a red 660 nm probe so the thyroid is spared", "C": "Treat both anterior and posterior neck, using a lower dose over the anterior thyroid", "D": "Treat the posterior facet region and avoid direct irradiation over the anterior thyroid gland"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the posterior facet region and avoid direct irradiation over the anterior thyroid gland): Direct irradiation over the thyroid gland is avoided as a standard precaution, so the anterior neck is left untreated. The painful facet joints can still be treated from the posterior side. Lowering the dose or switching to red light still delivers energy to the gland, and the posterior neck does not require waiting for surgery."}, {"id": 271, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A strength coach explains that a muscle will not gain strength if the weekly workload stays equal to the athlete's habitual activity. The coach states that adaptation requires a stimulus that exceeds what the tissue is accustomed to handling. Which training principle is the coach describing?", "options": {"A": "Individuality, tailoring the training dose to the genetics and baseline fitness of each person", "B": "Overload, applying a training stress that is greater than the tissue's current capacity to adapt", "C": "Reversibility, losing training adaptations once the training stimulus is reduced or stopped", "D": "Specificity, matching the training stimulus to the movement pattern and energy system of the task"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Overload, applying a training stress that is greater than the tissue's current capacity to adapt): The overload principle says a tissue adapts only when loaded beyond what it is used to, whether by resistance, volume, frequency or duration. Specificity concerns the match between training and task. Reversibility describes loss of gains when training stops, and individuality concerns tailoring the program to the person, none of which states the requirement to exceed habitual load."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old man stretches his hamstrings until he feels sharp, burning pain, believing more discomfort produces faster gains. He holds each stretch 30 seconds and bounces slightly at the end. The clinician corrects the technique. Which instruction describes the appropriate stretch intensity?", "options": {"A": "Stretch to a point of mild tension or discomfort and hold it still, without sharp pain", "B": "Use a very light position with no tension and extend each hold to several minutes", "C": "Stretch to a point of strong pain, and add small bounces to gain extra range", "D": "Stretch to a strong pull rated about 8 of 10 and hold with gentle pulses"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stretch to a point of mild tension or discomfort and hold it still, without sharp pain): An effective static stretch reaches mild tension or discomfort and is held still, without sharp pain. A position with no tension gives too little stimulus however long it is held. Strong pain with bouncing, or a near-painful pull with pulses, risks triggering the stretch reflex and tissue injury."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 26 year old man receives an ice pack over a sore elbow. Within five minutes he reports itching, and raised red welts appear on the skin under the pack, spreading slightly beyond its edges. His breathing is normal. What is the best interpretation and next action?", "options": {"A": "Early frostbite: apply a hot pack right away and resume cooling later", "B": "Probable cold urticaria: remove the cold, document, and avoid cryotherapy later", "C": "Reactive hyperemia: shorten the session to five minutes and continue treatment with monitoring", "D": "Normal vascular response to cold: continue the treatment for ten minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probable cold urticaria: remove the cold, document, and avoid cryotherapy later): Itching with raised welts that develop under and around a cold source is the typical picture of cold urticaria, so cold is removed, the reaction documented, and later cold avoided. Normal cold responses cause uniform redness without welts. Frostbite looks white and firm, and reactive hyperemia does not itch or form welts."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 52-year-old woman bends forward and the lumbar spine flexes through almost the whole movement while the pelvis barely rotates. Her straight leg raise measures 55 degrees bilaterally with a tight posterior thigh feeling. Which statement best explains her lumbopelvic rhythm and a sensible corrective choice?", "options": {"A": "Tight lumbar erector spinae block spinal flexion, so stretch them before any hinge training", "B": "Shortened hip flexors tilt the pelvis forward, so stretch the iliopsoas and add crunches", "C": "Shortened hamstrings limit anterior pelvic rotation, so lengthen them and train hip hinging", "D": "Weak gluteus maximus allows excess pelvic rotation, so add heavy hip thrusts only"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shortened hamstrings limit anterior pelvic rotation, so lengthen them and train hip hinging): Short hamstrings restrict anterior pelvic rotation, so the lumbar spine supplies the motion instead. Lengthening the hamstrings and retraining hip flexion addresses the cause. The hip flexor explanation does not fit a limited pelvic tilt, and the erector spinae are not blocking lumbar flexion since the lumbar spine already flexes freely."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A technician sets up an 810 nm class 3B probe and reasons that the beam would be visible, so a flash would make anyone look away before eye injury. The technician therefore skips goggles for a quick 30 second treatment of a thumb joint. Which statement correctly evaluates this reasoning?", "options": {"A": "The reasoning is flawed because 810 nm is invisible, so no blink or aversion response occurs", "B": "The reasoning is flawed, and standard sunglasses provide adequate protection for this wavelength", "C": "The reasoning holds because class 3B beams are visible and trigger blinking quickly", "D": "The reasoning holds because treatments under one minute deliver too little energy to injure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The reasoning is flawed because 810 nm is invisible, so no blink or aversion response occurs): An 810 nm beam lies outside the visible spectrum, so the eye gives no warning and the aversion response never occurs. A brief exposure can still injure the retina at class 3B power. Sunglasses do not filter this wavelength to a rated level, so matched goggles are needed."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician measures a patient's walking pattern on a marked walkway by recording where each heel lands. The therapist needs the measure that spans one full gait cycle, from a heel contact of the right foot to the next heel contact of that same right foot. What is this measure called?", "options": {"A": "Step length, the distance from one heel contact to the opposite heel contact", "B": "Cadence, the number of steps taken per minute along the marked walkway", "C": "Stride length, which equals the sum of one right step and one left step", "D": "Step width, the mediolateral distance between the midlines of both feet during walking"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stride length, which equals the sum of one right step and one left step): A measure running from one heel contact to the next contact of the same foot spans a full gait cycle, which is stride length. It equals one right step plus one left step. Step length covers only half of that distance. Cadence is a rate in steps per minute, and step width is a side-to-side distance, so neither describes the distance of a full cycle."}, {"id": 277, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 39-year-old warehouse worker with acute neck pain two days after a lifting injury wants pain relief before a gentle motion session. His pain is 6 of 10 at rest. The therapist applies conventional TENS to the paraspinal muscles for sensory level analgesia. Which parameter set fits conventional TENS?", "options": {"A": "Medium frequency 4000 Hz carrier with a 100 Hz beat and sensory intensity", "B": "High rate near 100 Hz, pulse near 100 microseconds, comfortable tingling intensity", "C": "High rate near 100 Hz, pulse near 400 microseconds, strong muscle contraction", "D": "Low rate near 4 Hz, pulse near 250 microseconds, visible muscle twitch"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (High rate near 100 Hz, pulse near 100 microseconds, comfortable tingling intensity): Conventional TENS uses high frequency of about 80 to 120 Hz, short pulse duration and intensity that produces tingling without contraction, targeting the gate control mechanism. The low rate with twitch describes acupuncture-like TENS. Strong contraction and carrier-frequency beats describe motor level stimulation and interferential current."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A strength coach asks a chiropractor how to estimate aerobic capacity for a group of healthy college athletes using only a track, a stopwatch, and no laboratory equipment. The chiropractor recommends a field test in which each athlete covers as much distance as possible during a fixed period. Which test does this description fit?", "options": {"A": "The Rockport one-mile walk test, with VO2 max predicted from finishing time and heart rate", "B": "The Cooper 12-minute run test, with VO2 max predicted from distance covered", "C": "The YMCA submaximal cycle test, with VO2 max extrapolated from two heart rate values", "D": "The Bruce protocol, with VO2 max predicted from total treadmill time to exhaustion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Cooper 12-minute run test, with VO2 max predicted from distance covered): The Cooper test fixes the time at 12 minutes and measures how far the athlete runs, then converts distance to estimated VO2 max. The Rockport test fixes distance at one mile and uses time plus heart rate. The Bruce protocol needs a treadmill and staged increases in workload. The YMCA test uses a cycle ergometer, so none of these fit a track-only setting."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 36-year-old accountant has right arm pain and paresthesia into the thumb. The Spurling test reproduces the arm symptoms, and manual neck distraction by the examiner relieves them. The therapist is deciding whether a trial of mechanical cervical traction is reasonable. What does relief with manual distraction suggest about the likely response to traction?", "options": {"A": "Upper cervical ligaments are lax, so a rigid collar should be used instead of traction", "B": "Pain is mostly muscular, so traction would offer no more than soft tissue massage", "C": "Mechanical traction is likely to reduce the radicular symptoms, so a trial is reasonable", "D": "Traction is contraindicated because the pain shows the nerve root is compressed beyond relief"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mechanical traction is likely to reduce the radicular symptoms, so a trial is reasonable): When manual distraction lessens arm pain, widening the foramina is probably relieving root irritation, so traction is likely to help. Root irritation that responds to distraction is an indication for traction, not a contraindication. A reproduced Spurling pain points to a radicular source, not a muscular one. Relief with a pull does not suggest ligament laxity, which is tested with stability tests."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Eight weeks after an ACL reconstruction, a 24-year-old man has a knee effusion and walks with only 3 degrees of knee flexion in loading response, compared with about 15 degrees on the other side. Quadriceps activation is poor. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Hamstring weakness from graft harvest, causing hyperextension of the knee in early stance", "B": "Gastrocnemius tightness forcing the knee straight in midstance", "C": "Weak gluteus maximus allowing the trunk to lean backward over the stance hip", "D": "Quadriceps avoidance from inhibition after surgery with effusion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quadriceps avoidance from inhibition after surgery with effusion): Effusion and pain inhibit the quadriceps, so the patient keeps the knee nearly straight in loading response instead of flexing about 15 degrees. The effusion and poor quadriceps activation fit quadriceps avoidance directly. Hamstring weakness and calf tightness can alter stance mechanics but do not explain missing flexion at contact with an effusion, and a backward trunk lean is a hip extensor pattern."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old is three weeks after Achilles tendon repair and asks the chiropractor to add calf stretching to reduce tightness. The surgeon's protocol limits dorsiflexion to neutral until week eight. The patient reports the posterior leg feels extremely tight at night. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use PNF hold-relax into dorsiflexion with half-effort contractions to ease calf tightness", "B": "Decline passive dorsiflexion stretching and teach protected motion within the surgeon's limits", "C": "Begin gentle gastrocnemius stretching with the knee extended to prevent a shortened repair of the tendon", "D": "Apply ballistic heel drops off a step edge to restore calf length quickly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline passive dorsiflexion stretching and teach protected motion within the surgeon's limits): A fresh tendon repair needs protected range, and stretching into dorsiflexion beyond neutral can elongate or rupture the repair. The therapist follows the surgeon's protocol and teaches motion and positioning within the allowed range. Gentle stretching, half-effort PNF, and heel drops all lengthen the repaired tendon too early. Night tightness is addressed by positioning and the allowed exercises."}, {"id": 282, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During a plank push-up, a 27-year-old woman's pelvis sags and the lumbar spine hyperextends after 20 seconds, while her shoulders and hips test strong in isolated manual testing. Her pain is absent and the elbows and wrists are stable throughout. Which deficit and training emphasis best fit?", "options": {"A": "Weak gluteus maximus, so add hip thrusts and single-leg bridges first", "B": "Poor scapular stabilizer endurance, so train serratus plus holds with bent elbows", "C": "Poor rotational control, so train Pallof presses and side planks first", "D": "Poor anti-extension trunk endurance, so train planks with a maintained posterior pelvic tilt"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Poor anti-extension trunk endurance, so train planks with a maintained posterior pelvic tilt): Pelvic sag and lumbar hyperextension with strong isolated hips and shoulders point to poor anti-extension endurance of the trunk. Planks with a maintained posterior pelvic tilt train that control. Hip thrusts and scapular holds address regions that tested strong. Pallof presses train anti-rotation, which is not the observed fault."}, {"id": 283, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old gymnast twisted her knee two days ago and reports it catching. Today it will not straighten, resting about 25 degrees short of extension with a firm block at end range, and passive pressure is painful. The effusion is mild. Which plan is most appropriate for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Apply heat and begin gentle active extension exercises, progressing as tolerated for pain", "B": "Perform grade IV tibiofemoral extension glide mobilization to reduce the fragment and restore full extension today", "C": "Immobilize in a long leg cast for four weeks, then restart extension range exercises", "D": "Avoid forcing extension and refer promptly for orthopedic evaluation of a possible displaced meniscal tear"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid forcing extension and refer promptly for orthopedic evaluation of a possible displaced meniscal tear): A knee stuck short of extension with a firm end range block after a twisting injury and catching suggests a displaced meniscal fragment. Forcing motion can worsen the tear, so the joint should be protected and the patient referred for orthopedic evaluation. Heat with exercise treats soft tissue tightness, not a mechanical block. Strong mobilization risks damaging the meniscus, and casting delays the diagnosis the block calls for."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 35-year-old office worker has unilateral pain behind the ear, spreading from the side of the neck to the temple. Palpation finds a tender taut band in the upper trapezius that reproduces the headache. Which referral pattern is typical of an active upper trapezius trigger point?", "options": {"A": "Deep pain in the posterior shoulder and lateral arm, with a spread toward the forearm", "B": "Pain in the posterolateral neck, behind the ear and into the temple", "C": "Aching low back pain over the iliac crest and greater trochanter", "D": "Pain in the lateral hip and thigh extending down toward the calf"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pain in the posterolateral neck, behind the ear and into the temple): Active upper trapezius trigger points refer to the posterolateral neck, behind the ear and to the temple, which is why they feed tension-type headache. Infraspinatus points refer to the shoulder and arm. Quadratus lumborum points refer to the iliac crest and trochanter. Gluteus minimus points refer along the lateral thigh and calf."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 32-year-old man trains his whole body with heavy resistance exercises six days per week for 8 weeks after an elbow injury, and he hopes to return to sport. His strength has plateaued and his elbow aches. Which change best reflects standard strength training recommendations?", "options": {"A": "Train each muscle group once per week with a single heavy set", "B": "Train each major muscle group two to three nonconsecutive days per week", "C": "Train each muscle group four consecutive days then rest for three days", "D": "Train each major muscle group every day using lighter loads and higher repetitions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Train each major muscle group two to three nonconsecutive days per week): Muscle needs about 48 hours of recovery between hard sessions for the same group, so two to three nonconsecutive days per week is the standard recommendation. A single weekly session is closer to maintenance than progression. Four consecutive days and daily training both leave no recovery, which fits his plateau and aching elbow. Lighter loads reduce but do not remove the need for rest days."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "After a 20-minute interval session, a patient's oxygen uptake stays above resting levels for some time while he rests on the plinth. He asks the physiotherapist what this lingering extra oxygen use pays for. Which statement best describes a recognized contributor to excess post-exercise oxygen consumption?", "options": {"A": "Oxidation of lactate within the same muscle fibers, which accounts for the full excess consumption", "B": "Down regulation of mitochondrial enzymes that lowers the energy cost of resting after training", "C": "Resynthesis of phosphocreatine and ATP plus the cost of a raised body temperature", "D": "Repayment of an oxygen loan taken from the lungs during the exercise bout"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resynthesis of phosphocreatine and ATP plus the cost of a raised body temperature): Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption reflects resynthesis of phosphocreatine and ATP plus the cost of a raised body temperature, among other recovery processes. Lactate oxidation contributes but cannot explain the whole excess. Enzyme down regulation does not occur, since recovery raises metabolic demand. The old oxygen loan from the lungs idea has been replaced by this multifactor view."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "On lateral standing view a plumb line passes through the acromion and greater trochanter, but the ear lobe sits 3 centimeters in front of the line in a 36-year-old office worker with neck ache. Which muscle impairment best explains the deviation, and which exercise addresses it?", "options": {"A": "Tight deep neck flexors, stretched with sustained neck flexion", "B": "Weak deep neck flexors, trained with chin tucks", "C": "Weak upper trapezius, trained with heavy shoulder shrugs", "D": "Weak thoracic extensors, trained with prone lifts to restore the upright trunk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weak deep neck flexors, trained with chin tucks): A forward ear position relative to the line is a forward head posture, usually with weak deep cervical flexors and tight upper trapezius and suboccipitals. Craniocervical flexion training with a chin tuck strengthens the weak group. Stretching the deep flexors worsens their weakness, and shrugs load the already overactive upper trapezius. Thoracic extensor work helps kyphosis but does not address the head position here."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old painter has weakness when turning the arm outward after a rotator cuff strain. The therapist wants to strengthen the infraspinatus and teres minor with the arm in a safe, low-stress position beside the body. Which exercise is best for these muscles?", "options": {"A": "Bent-over rows with elbows close to the torso toward the ribs", "B": "Resisted shoulder internal rotation with the elbow against the side", "C": "Standing empty can raises with thumbs down to ninety degrees of elevation", "D": "Side-lying external rotation with a small towel roll under the elbow"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Side-lying external rotation with a small towel roll under the elbow): Infraspinatus and teres minor are the main external rotators, and side-lying external rotation with a towel roll trains them with high activation while keeping the arm at the side. Empty can raises target the supraspinatus in a stressful position. Resisted internal rotation works the subscapularis. Rows work the scapular retractors and latissimus."}, {"id": 289, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist plans to start ultraviolet treatment for a patient with moderate acne and first tests a small patch of skin using several timed exposures. The test is meant to find the lowest exposure that produces a defined skin response. How is the minimal erythemal dose, or MED, defined?", "options": {"A": "The smallest exposure that produces faint, just visible redness on the tested patch", "B": "The exposure that produces blistering limited to the tested patch of skin", "C": "The exposure that produces redness with edema and later peeling of the skin", "D": "The largest exposure the skin tolerates without redness or tenderness over the next day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The smallest exposure that produces faint, just visible redness on the tested patch): The MED is the shortest exposure at a set distance that produces minimal, barely perceptible erythema on the tested skin. Doses below it are sub-erythemal. Redness with edema and peeling is a second degree reaction at about five times the MED. Blistering is a much higher reaction and is not used to define the MED."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 20-year-old football player with dense leg hair needs rigid zinc oxide tape for an ankle before practice and sweats heavily. The trainer wants to protect his skin during removal while keeping the tape secure on the leg. Which preparation is best?", "options": {"A": "Wrap the leg in a thick elastic bandage and tape over the bandage", "B": "Apply adherent spray to the hair and skin, then tape directly over the hair", "C": "Apply skin lubricant over the hair so removal glides, then tape over it", "D": "Clean and dry the skin, trim or shave the hair and apply underwrap"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Clean and dry the skin, trim or shave the hair and apply underwrap): Clean, dry, hair-free skin covered by underwrap protects the skin on removal while letting the tape anchor securely. Taping over an elastic bandage loosens as the player sweats. Adherent spray over hair still pulls the hair when the tape is removed, and lubricant prevents the tape from sticking."}, {"id": 291, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A clinical exercise physiologist explains to a chiropractic student how oxygen consumption is determined during maximal treadmill testing in a healthy 28-year-old runner. The student asks which two physiologic variables are multiplied together in the Fick equation to yield whole-body oxygen uptake. Which pair of variables does the Fick equation multiply to calculate VO2?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac output multiplied by the arteriovenous oxygen difference across the working tissues", "B": "Stroke volume multiplied by heart rate, giving the oxygen delivered to working muscle", "C": "Minute ventilation multiplied by the difference between inspired and expired oxygen fractions", "D": "Heart rate multiplied by arterial oxygen content measured at peak exercise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cardiac output multiplied by the arteriovenous oxygen difference across the working tissues): The Fick equation states that oxygen uptake equals cardiac output times the arteriovenous oxygen difference. It combines delivery of blood with the extraction of oxygen by the tissues. Stroke volume times heart rate only gives cardiac output. Ventilation times the oxygen fraction difference is how gas analyzers calculate VO2 in practice, but that is a different method, and heart rate times arterial content leaves out extraction."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "Two clients burn the same 300 kcal on a bike, one at a steady moderate pace for 40 minutes and the other with hard intervals for 20 minutes. Afterward, the interval client's oxygen uptake stays elevated longer. The physiotherapist must explain the greater excess post-exercise oxygen consumption after intense work. Which factor best accounts for it?", "options": {"A": "Larger disturbances in temperature, catecholamines, phosphocreatine and ion balance that take longer to restore", "B": "Intervals reduce blood volume for hours, forcing the heart to consume more oxygen at rest", "C": "Interval work creates a debt of oxygen repaid mainly by converting lactate back into glucose", "D": "Intervals shift fuel use to carbohydrate only, so oxygen uptake stays elevated while glycogen is restored"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Larger disturbances in temperature, catecholamines, phosphocreatine and ion balance that take longer to restore): Intense exercise produces larger and longer lasting disturbances, including higher core temperature, more circulating catecholamines, greater phosphocreatine depletion and ion shifts, so recovery needs more oxygen for longer. Lactate conversion to glucose is only a small part of recovery. Glycogen resynthesis contributes but does not explain the full elevation, and blood volume is restored within hours."}, {"id": 293, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 27-year-old man receives pulsed electrical stimulation to the quadriceps at 10 pps with strong amplitude. The muscle shows repeated individual twitches rather than a sustained contraction, and the knee extension torque gain is small. The clinician wants a smooth, sustained tetanic contraction for strengthening. Which change is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lower the pulse rate to 2 pps and lengthen the pulse duration.", "B": "Increase the ramp-up time to 5 seconds while keeping the same pulse rate.", "C": "Raise the pulse rate to about 35 to 50 pps.", "D": "Reduce the amplitude to sensory level and keep the pulse rate at 10 pps."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise the pulse rate to about 35 to 50 pps.): At low rates such as 10 pps, each pulse produces a separate twitch. At about 35 to 50 pps successive twitches summate and fuse into a smooth tetanic contraction, which is needed for strengthening. Lowering the rate separates twitches further. Ramp time and sensory-level amplitude do not change fusion."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old has a stiff, non-irritable glenohumeral joint with a firm capsular end feel. Pain appears only at the end of range and settles quickly. The therapist selects a mobilization grade to stretch the capsule. According to the Maitland system, which grade is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Grade I oscillations, small amplitude at the beginning of the available range", "B": "Grade I and II oscillations alternating with sustained traction to relieve pain at rest", "C": "Grade III or IV oscillations, large amplitude into resistance or small amplitude at the limit", "D": "Grade II oscillations, large amplitude within the free part of range, not reaching resistance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade III or IV oscillations, large amplitude into resistance or small amplitude at the limit): In the Maitland system, grades I and II are used to modulate pain because they stay in the free part of range, while grades III and IV move into resistance or sit at the limit and are used to stretch stiff tissue. This non-irritable stiff capsule needs stretch, so grade III or IV fits. Grade I, grade II, or traction-based pain relief would not reach the restricting tissue."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old pianist who is 20 weeks pregnant and otherwise healthy has first dorsal compartment tenosynovitis at the wrist, with the tendon sheath about 1 cm deep. The clinician considers 3 MHz ultrasound to the wrist. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Ultrasound to the wrist is acceptable, because the pregnancy contraindication concerns fields over the uterus", "B": "Ultrasound to the wrist is acceptable only after the twentieth week, once the fetus is formed", "C": "Ultrasound to the wrist is acceptable only at 1 MHz, because it avoids the uterus", "D": "Ultrasound is contraindicated anywhere on the body during pregnancy, including the wrist"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ultrasound to the wrist is acceptable, because the pregnancy contraindication concerns fields over the uterus): The concern in pregnancy is exposing the fetus, so the contraindication is for fields over the gravid uterus, including the abdomen and low back and pelvis. A wrist field is far from the fetus. Frequency does not change the distance to the uterus and no gestational cutoff applies."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient lies in hooklying with a pressure cuff under the lumbar spine inflated to forty millimeters of mercury. During a single leg lowering test the reading rises to fifty-two millimeters of mercury. The therapist must interpret what the pelvis and lumbar spine did during the movement. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "The pelvis tilted anteriorly and the lumbar spine extended, lifting away from the cuff", "B": "The trunk rotated toward the lowering leg, shifting load to one side of the cuff", "C": "The pelvis tilted posteriorly and the lumbar spine flexed, pressing harder into the cuff", "D": "The transversus abdominis contracted well, so control of the lumbar spine was excellent"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The pelvis tilted posteriorly and the lumbar spine flexed, pressing harder into the cuff): A rise above baseline means the lumbar spine pressed down on the cuff, which occurs with posterior pelvic tilt and lumbar flexion as the leg lowers. A fall below baseline would indicate anterior tilt and extension. Rotation is not reported by the cuff reading. Good control keeps the reading near baseline."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 44-year-old man has followed the same fixed 3 sets of 8 program for 12 weeks with progressive load increases, and progress has stalled for the last 4 weeks. Sleep, nutrition and stress are normal, and he has no pain. Which modification is most appropriate to restart adaptation?", "options": {"A": "Keep the same load and add two weekly sessions at identical intensity and volume", "B": "Vary the load and volume in planned phases, then raise the heavy phase intensity gradually", "C": "Change the exercise selection at every session to prevent any familiarity with the pattern", "D": "Increase the load by ten percent every session until the plateau breaks"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vary the load and volume in planned phases, then raise the heavy phase intensity gradually): A plateau in a well recovered trainee on a fixed program means the stimulus has become too familiar, so planned variation of load and volume in phases with gradual rises in heavy phase intensity restarts adaptation. Adding sessions at the same demand adds little. Changing exercises at every session removes progressive structure, and pushing linear increases on a stalled lifter risks overload without a new stimulus."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 44-year-old man fractured his fibular head three weeks ago and now cannot lift the right forefoot. During walking he lifts the right thigh and bends the knee higher than normal in swing and then the toes slap the floor. What gait compensation is this pattern producing?", "options": {"A": "Excess hip and knee flexion in swing to lift the toes clear", "B": "The trunk leans over the stance hip as the weight is accepted", "C": "The stance foot rises onto its toes to lengthen the standing leg", "D": "The whole limb swings out in a curved arc around the body"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excess hip and knee flexion in swing to lift the toes clear): Foot drop from common peroneal nerve injury leaves the dorsiflexors unable to lift the foot, so the patient raises the knee and hip higher to prevent toe drag, a steppage gait. Circumduction, vaulting, and lateral trunk lean are compensations for stiff knees, long swing limbs, and abductor problems respectively."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a hairy, dry thigh is receiving electrical muscle stimulation to the quadriceps. Even at a high amplitude the contraction is weak, and the patient feels a sharp sensation at the pad edges. The pads are old and slightly dry. The clinician suspects high skin impedance. Which action best corrects the problem?", "options": {"A": "Raise the pulse duration to 1 ms until the contraction appears at the same amplitude.", "B": "Switch to direct current, because skin impedance to direct current is lower than to alternating.", "C": "Clean and moisten the skin, clip the hair, and apply fresh conductive pads.", "D": "Move the pads closer together to shorten the conducting path through the thigh tissue."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Clean and moisten the skin, clip the hair, and apply fresh conductive pads.): Dry skin, hair, and dried electrodes raise impedance and concentrate current at the pad edges. Cleaning and moistening the skin, clipping hair, and using fresh conductive pads lower the impedance and spread current evenly. Lengthening the pulse or moving pads closer does not address contact. Skin impedance to direct current is actually higher than to alternating current at higher frequencies."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 71-year-old woman has a venous leg ulcer that has not healed over 12 weeks despite compression therapy. A clinician proposes adding microcurrent applied through the wound bed and surrounding skin. The patient asks what the current is supposed to do inside the wound. Which rationale is the most accurate for this treatment?", "options": {"A": "It heats the wound bed, raising metabolic rate and dilating capillaries to deliver oxygen.", "B": "It imitates the natural current of injury and encourages repair cells to migrate into the wound.", "C": "It blocks nociceptor transmission, relaxing the tissue so granulation can proceed without pain.", "D": "It produces rhythmic muscle pumping that increases venous return and reduces leg edema."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It imitates the natural current of injury and encourages repair cells to migrate into the wound.): Injured skin generates an endogenous electrical current, and microcurrent is thought to mimic it, supporting migration of cells involved in repair toward the wound. Sub-sensory amplitude is too low to cause muscle pumping or measurable heating. Pain gating is the rationale for TENS, not for wound-healing microcurrent."}, {"id": 301, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A student watches a healthy adult walk from behind and focuses on right single limb support. The student wants to describe the normal frontal plane pelvic motion in this phase of the cycle. Which description is correct for the normal pelvis at that moment?", "options": {"A": "The right pelvis drops as the right hip adducts under load", "B": "The left pelvis rises as the hip hikes to clear the foot", "C": "Left pelvis drops slightly, held by the right hip abductors", "D": "The pelvis stays perfectly level with no obliquity in normal gait"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left pelvis drops slightly, held by the right hip abductors): In right single support the unsupported left side of the pelvis normally drops a few degrees, about 5 degrees total obliquity, and the right gluteus medius restrains it. A pelvis that rises on the swing side indicates hip hiking. A completely level pelvis is not normal, and a drop on the stance side is not the normal pattern."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 34-year-old man begins a resistance program and returns the morning after his first leg session reporting mild stiffness, with worse aching expected. He worries about injury. The physiotherapist explains the typical time course of delayed onset muscle soreness. When does soreness usually reach its peak intensity after an unaccustomed bout?", "options": {"A": "Between 5 and 7 days after the bout, persisting for about two weeks", "B": "Within 4 to 6 hours of finishing, resolving by the next morning", "C": "Between 24 and 72 hours after the bout, commonly around the second day", "D": "During the exercise itself, easing within the first hour afterward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Between 24 and 72 hours after the bout, commonly around the second day): Delayed onset muscle soreness develops several hours after unaccustomed exercise, peaks between about 24 and 72 hours, and resolves within roughly 5 to 7 days. Pain felt during exercise or in the first hours is a different phenomenon. A peak at one week or later suggests a strain or another problem rather than typical DOMS."}, {"id": 303, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 47-year-old plumber with lateral epicondylitis is fitted with a counterforce forearm strap to wear during work. The strap is meant to alter force transmission to the extensor origin during gripping. Where should the strap be positioned on the limb for the intended effect?", "options": {"A": "Around the upper arm just above the elbow over the distal triceps", "B": "Around the forearm over the muscle bellies, about one to two inches distal to the epicondyle", "C": "Around the distal forearm just proximal to the wrist crease at the radius", "D": "Directly over the lateral epicondyle, tight enough to compress the tendon origin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Around the forearm over the muscle bellies, about one to two inches distal to the epicondyle): A counterforce brace is worn over the proximal forearm muscle bellies a short distance below the epicondyle, where it is thought to dissipate contraction force before it reaches the tendon origin. Compressing the epicondyle itself irritates the origin. A wrist or upper arm position does not change extensor loading."}, {"id": 304, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A patient performs an abdominal drawing-in maneuver with a pressure biofeedback unit set at 40 mmHg under the lumbar spine. The pressure rises to 58 mmHg as the patient flexes the trunk and tilts the pelvis. How is this best interpreted and corrected?", "options": {"A": "Substitution by the global trunk flexors, so retrain gentle drawing in", "B": "Good transversus abdominis activation, so progress to heel slides and leg loading", "C": "Substitution by the erector spinae, so retrain with prone extension holds and bracing", "D": "Excess hip flexor tightness, so stretch the iliopsoas before repeating the drawing-in test"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Substitution by the global trunk flexors, so retrain gentle drawing in): A pressure rise from 40 to 58 mmHg with trunk flexion and pelvic tilt shows the global flexors substituting for the deep transversus abdominis. The correction is gentle, low-effort drawing in with the pressure kept near baseline. Progressing exercises would reinforce the compensation, and erector spinae or hip flexor explanations do not produce a pressure rise from trunk curl."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "When a patient actively contracts the quadriceps during a knee extension exercise, the hamstrings relax reflexively through spinal interneurons that lower the excitability of the opposing motor neurons. A physical therapy student must name this neurophysiological principle for the chart. Which term correctly names the principle?", "options": {"A": "Recurrent inhibition, in which Renshaw cells reduce motor neuron output through negative feedback", "B": "Reciprocal inhibition, in which agonist activation reduces antagonist motor neuron excitability", "C": "Autogenic inhibition, in which tension in a muscle reduces that same muscle's motor neuron excitability", "D": "Temporal summation, in which rapid repeated impulses add together to increase muscle tension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reciprocal inhibition, in which agonist activation reduces antagonist motor neuron excitability): Reciprocal inhibition is the spinal reflex in which activation of an agonist, through Ia inhibitory interneurons, reduces activation of its antagonist. Autogenic inhibition acts on the same muscle after Golgi tendon organ firing, and recurrent inhibition is Renshaw cell feedback within a motor pool. Temporal summation concerns adding twitches to raise tension and does not describe antagonist relaxation."}, {"id": 306, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 36-year-old recreational cyclist has a lactate threshold at 70 percent of her VO2max and wants to race 40 km time trials faster. Her physiotherapist designs a training block to push that threshold toward a higher percentage of VO2max. Which training session is best matched to that goal?", "options": {"A": "Short 30 second all out sprints with four minute recoveries to build anaerobic capacity", "B": "Two hour rides at an easy pace well below the lactate threshold", "C": "Sustained 20 to 30 minute tempo efforts near the current threshold pace", "D": "Repeated 4 minute efforts at VO2max pace with equal rest to raise maximal oxygen uptake"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sustained 20 to 30 minute tempo efforts near the current threshold pace): To raise lactate threshold as a share of VO2max, the best stimulus is sustained work at or just below current threshold pace, such as 20 to 30 minute tempo efforts. All out sprints train anaerobic capacity, and easy long rides build aerobic base with little threshold stress. Four minute efforts at VO2max pace mainly raise maximal oxygen uptake, which is a different adaptation from the fraction of it she can sustain."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 52-year-old has adhesive capsulitis with constant night pain and a muscle-guarded end feel at 10 degrees of shoulder external rotation. Pain begins before tissue resistance, and symptoms linger an hour after examination. Grade IV mobilization and prolonged stretching are both being considered. Which approach is most appropriate initially?", "options": {"A": "PNF contract-relax with maximal contraction of the internal rotators at the end of range", "B": "Grade IV oscillations at the limit of external rotation to begin stretching the capsule", "C": "Grade I or II oscillations in the pain-free range to modulate pain, avoiding end-range loading", "D": "Low-load prolonged stretching of external rotation held at the painful limit for twenty minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade I or II oscillations in the pain-free range to modulate pain, avoiding end-range loading): Constant night pain, pain before resistance, and lingering symptoms mark an irritable joint, where treatment should modulate pain rather than stretch tissue. Grade I or II oscillations in the pain-free range do this. Grade IV mobilization, prolonged stretching, or maximal contractions at a painful end range provoke guarding and increase irritability. Stretching progresses later, after pain and irritability settle."}, {"id": 308, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "After a hard interval session, a 35-year-old client asks where the lactate produced in her working muscle goes during the following hour. Her physiotherapist reviews that some lactate moves into the circulation and some is processed locally. Which description of lactate's main fate during recovery is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Most is excreted unchanged in sweat and urine within about thirty minutes", "B": "Most is oxidized for energy in muscle and heart, with a smaller share becoming glucose", "C": "Most is converted to fatty acids in adipose tissue for later storage", "D": "Most is converted to glucose by the liver, with a smaller share oxidized in muscle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Most is oxidized for energy in muscle and heart, with a smaller share becoming glucose): During recovery most lactate is taken up by slow-twitch fibers, the heart and other tissues and oxidized for energy, while a smaller share, roughly 20 percent, returns to glucose through the Cori cycle. Making the liver pathway dominant reverses those proportions. Only trace lactate is lost in sweat and urine. Fatty acid conversion is minor."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A geriatric clinic uses the Tinetti Performance Oriented Mobility Assessment on an 84-year-old resident who previously fractured a hip. The balance items total 16 points and the gait items total 12 points. A combined score in which range is classified as high risk for falls on this tool?", "options": {"A": "A combined score below 45 out of a possible 56 points", "B": "A combined score below 19 out of a possible 24 points", "C": "A combined score of 24 or lower out of a possible 28 points", "D": "A combined score of 18 or lower out of 28 points"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A combined score of 18 or lower out of 28 points): The Tinetti tool totals 28 points, and a score of 18 or lower indicates high fall risk, 19 to 23 moderate risk, and 24 or higher lower risk. A total of 45 out of 56 is the Berg threshold, and 19 out of 24 belongs to the Dynamic Gait Index. Treating 24 as the high risk cutoff would label most moderate and even low risk patients as high risk."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 31-year-old mechanic has L5 radicular pain in the left leg that centralizes with repeated prone press-ups and worsens with sitting and forward bending. The therapist chooses mechanical lumbar traction for a trial. Which patient position best matches this directional preference while her symptoms are being monitored?", "options": {"A": "Supine with the hips and knees at 90 degrees to flatten the lordosis", "B": "Sitting slouched in flexion with a thoracic harness pulling upward", "C": "Side-lying with the top leg drawn fully to the chest and the spine flexed", "D": "Prone with the lumbar spine in a neutral to slightly extended posture"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prone with the lumbar spine in a neutral to slightly extended posture): When symptoms centralize with extension and worsen with flexion, traction is best delivered with the lumbar spine in neutral to slight extension, which is done in prone. The supine 90 degree position flattens the lordosis and would likely aggravate her leg pain. Sitting slouched and side-lying flexion also load the spine in the flexed direction she cannot tolerate."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "During a single-leg stance test, a patient standing on the right leg shows the pelvis dropping on the left side. A chiropractic student must identify what this positive Trendelenburg sign indicates about hip muscle function. The patient has no pain and normal hip range of motion. Which muscle weakness does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "Weakness of the left gluteus medius on the swing side", "B": "Weakness of the right gluteus maximus during hip extension", "C": "Weakness of the right gluteus medius on the stance side", "D": "Weakness of the left tensor fasciae latae on the lifted side"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Weakness of the right gluteus medius on the stance side): In single-leg stance the stance-side gluteus medius must hold the pelvis level. When it is weak, the pelvis drops on the opposite, unsupported side, so a left-side drop while standing on the right means right gluteus medius weakness. The muscle on the swing side is not holding the pelvis. Gluteus maximus controls hip extension."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 34-year-old woman cannot isolate the transversus abdominis during drawing-in, with the rectus abdominis and ribs dominating each attempt. The therapist knows the pelvic floor co-activates with the deep abdominal muscles at low effort. Which cue is likely to help her recruit the transversus abdominis?", "options": {"A": "Take a large breath into the chest to expand the upper ribs", "B": "Squeeze the buttocks together firmly while keeping the pelvis tilted backward", "C": "Gently lift the pelvic floor, as if stopping the flow of urine", "D": "Pull the lower ribs down and forward by curling the trunk upward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gently lift the pelvic floor, as if stopping the flow of urine): The pelvic floor and transversus abdominis co-contract at low effort, so a gentle pelvic floor lift can facilitate the deep abdominal muscle when isolation is poor. A buttock squeeze with a posterior tilt substitutes the gluteals and hip muscles. Curling the trunk recruits the rectus abdominis, the very muscle already dominating. A large chest breath expands the ribs and recruits superficial muscles."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "During a neurologic screen, a clinician taps the patellar tendon of a seated patient and observes a brisk knee extension. A student asks which circuit produces the response. The clinician explains that the pathway has a single synapse in the spinal cord between the sensory neuron and the motor neuron. What is the circuit?", "options": {"A": "A polysynaptic flexor withdrawal arc from nociceptors through interneurons to flexor motor neurons", "B": "A Renshaw cell loop that feeds activity back to inhibit the quadriceps motor neuron pool", "C": "A Golgi tendon organ circuit through Ib afferents and inhibitory interneurons to the quadriceps", "D": "A monosynaptic stretch reflex arc from muscle spindle Ia afferents to alpha motor neurons"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A monosynaptic stretch reflex arc from muscle spindle Ia afferents to alpha motor neurons): The tendon tap briefly stretches the quadriceps, exciting muscle spindles. Ia afferents synapse directly on the alpha motor neurons of the same muscle, forming the only monosynaptic reflex in the body, so the quadriceps contracts. Flexor withdrawal and Golgi tendon organ circuits use interneurons, and a Renshaw loop provides inhibitory feedback rather than a stretch response."}, {"id": 314, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has bilateral vestibular loss from gentamicin toxicity confirmed on testing. She has oscillopsia when walking and cannot walk safely in dim light or on uneven ground. She has no positional vertigo and her cervical spine is normal. Which exercise approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Canalith repositioning with the Epley maneuver at each visit until symptoms stop", "B": "Habituation with repeated Brandt-Daroff positional exercises done at home daily", "C": "Sensory substitution training with gaze stabilization exercises as tolerated", "D": "Vestibular suppressant medication and rest until symptoms subside"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sensory substitution training with gaze stabilization exercises as tolerated): With severe bilateral loss, recovery depends on using remaining senses and any residual vestibulo-ocular function. Substitution training and gaze stabilization are the base of treatment. The Epley maneuver treats debris and habituation addresses positional symptoms. Suppressants with rest delay adaptation."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 55-year-old woman recovering from a knee sprain walks a straight corridor at a steady pace. Her cadence is measured at 108 steps per minute, and her average step length is 0.70 meters. Using speed equals cadence times step length, what is her walking speed in meters per second?", "options": {"A": "About 1.26 meters per second, from 75.6 meters per minute divided by sixty", "B": "About 2.52 meters per second, after doubling step length into stride length", "C": "About 0.63 meters per second, after halving the cadence as if in strides", "D": "About 0.76 meters per second, after dividing by one hundred not sixty"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 1.26 meters per second, from 75.6 meters per minute divided by sixty): Speed equals cadence times step length: 108 steps per minute times 0.70 meters is 75.6 meters per minute. Dividing by 60 seconds gives 1.26 meters per second, rechecked as 0.70 times 108 equals 75.6. Using stride length with a step cadence doubles the answer, and halving cadence or dividing by 100 gives the other wrong values."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 45 year old roofer has widespread plaque psoriasis. Both PUVA and narrowband UVB would be effective for him. He works outdoors all day, often without sunglasses, and says he would forget to cover his skin and eyes on treatment days. Which treatment is the better choice?", "options": {"A": "PUVA with a topical psoralen soak before the UVA exposure", "B": "Broadband UVA alone at 365 nanometers without psoralen", "C": "PUVA with oral psoralen taken two hours before the UVA exposure", "D": "Narrowband UVB at 311 nanometers without any oral photosensitizing drug"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Narrowband UVB at 311 nanometers without any oral photosensitizing drug): Narrowband UVB at 311 nanometers is effective for psoriasis and needs no photosensitizing drug, so it does not extend the sun sensitivity beyond the session. Oral psoralen leaves skin and eyes sensitive to sunlight for hours, which is risky for an outdoor worker who will not protect himself. Topical psoralen also sensitizes the skin. UVA alone without psoralen is not an effective psoriasis treatment."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A weightlifter is 26 years old, trains five days weekly, and has pain with overhead lifts. When he performs a wall push-up, the medial border of the right scapula lifts away from the ribs. Which muscle weakness is most likely, and which exercise best targets it?", "options": {"A": "Serratus anterior, strengthened with progressive push-up plus and wall slide exercises", "B": "Lower trapezius, strengthened with prone Y raises and external rotation", "C": "Pectoralis minor, strengthened with wall push-ups and resisted scapular depression", "D": "Rhomboid major, strengthened with bent-over rows and scapular retraction holds"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Serratus anterior, strengthened with progressive push-up plus and wall slide exercises): The serratus anterior holds the medial border of the scapula against the thorax, and winging during a pushing movement points to its weakness. Push-up plus and wall slides train it. Rhomboids and lower trapezius are retractors and depressors that do not prevent winging in a push, and pectoralis minor tilts the scapula forward."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has chronic lumbar paraspinal tightness limiting trunk extension. The tissues to be heated lie about 4 cm below the skin, under a moderate layer of subcutaneous fat. The clinician plans continuous therapeutic ultrasound before stretching. Which frequency and intensity best fits the target depth and a thermal effect?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz at 0.1 W/cm2 pulsed at a 20 percent duty cycle", "B": "1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 continuous with a slowly moving sound head", "C": "3 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 continuous with a slowly moving sound head", "D": "3 MHz at 0.2 W/cm2 pulsed at a 50 percent duty cycle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 continuous with a slowly moving sound head): A 1 MHz beam penetrates to about 3 to 5 cm, which suits tissue 4 cm deep, and continuous output at around 1.5 W/cm2 produces the heating wanted. A 3 MHz beam is absorbed within 1 to 2 cm and does not reach the target. Low intensity pulsed settings provide a nonthermal effect and will not heat the tissue."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 14-year-old, still growing, with a right thoracic idiopathic curve of 28 degrees begins Schroth therapy with a certified instructor. The therapist coaches rotational angular breathing, directing inhalation toward the concave side of the thoracic curve. What is the purpose of this breathing pattern?", "options": {"A": "To expand the collapsed concave rib area and help derotate the trunk", "B": "To strengthen the convex-side latissimus dorsi through resisted exhalation", "C": "To inflate the convex rib hump and stretch the tight intercostals", "D": "To increase intra-abdominal pressure and lock the pelvis in a neutral position"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (To expand the collapsed concave rib area and help derotate the trunk): Rotational angular breathing sends inhaled air into the collapsed concave regions, expanding the ribs there and encouraging derotation of the trunk. Directing air toward the convex hump would reinforce the prominence. The technique is a corrective breathing pattern, not a bracing or latissimus strengthening drill."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In Sahrmann's movement system impairment model, a body segment that moves more readily in a given direction than its neighbors is treated as the source of repeated stress. A clinician explains the guiding principle to a patient before beginning movement retraining. Which principle describes why that segment moves first?", "options": {"A": "Movement takes the path of least resistance, so the most flexible segment moves first", "B": "Movement takes the path of least resistance, so the segment nearest the force moves first", "C": "Movement takes the path of most muscle activation, so the weakest segment moves first", "D": "Movement takes the path of least effort, so the segment with the shortest lever moves first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Movement takes the path of least resistance, so the most flexible segment moves first): Sahrmann holds that movement follows the path of least resistance, so the most flexible segment moves first and absorbs repeated stress. Nearness to the force does not set which segment moves. Weakness can contribute to faulty patterns but is not the guiding principle. Lever length affects torque yet does not define the principle of relative flexibility."}, {"id": 321, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 36 year old woman requests UVB for psoriasis on her elbows. Her intake lists daily levothyroxine for hypothyroidism, Fitzpatrick type III skin, a family history of psoriasis, and systemic lupus erythematosus diagnosed 5 years ago with a facial rash. Which finding makes ultraviolet therapy unsafe for her?", "options": {"A": "Fitzpatrick type III skin, which burns too readily for therapy", "B": "A family history of psoriasis, a condition that worsens with irradiation", "C": "Levothyroxine use for hypothyroidism, a recognized photosensitizing medication", "D": "Systemic lupus erythematosus, an autoimmune disease with photosensitive skin involvement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Systemic lupus erythematosus, an autoimmune disease with photosensitive skin involvement): Lupus is photosensitive, and ultraviolet exposure can flare systemic disease and the rash, so it is a contraindication. Levothyroxine is not a recognized photosensitizer. Type III skin tolerates dosing once the MED is measured. A family history of psoriasis has no bearing on safety and does not make irradiation worse."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist is giving UVB to a patient's anterior trunk and must stay in the treatment room to adjust the lamp height. The patient already wears UV-opaque goggles. The therapist considers personal protection because the unit emits ultraviolet radiation that can reflect from walls and skin. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Avoid looking directly at the lamp while adjusting its height", "B": "Wear UV-blocking goggles for the whole session, as the patient does", "C": "Wear ordinary dark sunglasses since they absorb ultraviolet radiation", "D": "Rely on the patient's goggles because reflected radiation is too weak to matter"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wear UV-blocking goggles for the whole session, as the patient does): Ultraviolet radiation scatters from walls, equipment and the patient's skin, so a clinician in the room is exposed even when not facing the lamp. UV-blocking goggles protect the clinician's eyes for the full session. Tinted sunglasses may transmit ultraviolet radiation. Looking away does not stop reflected exposure, and the patient's goggles protect only the patient."}, {"id": 323, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 31-year-old with a cervical disc protrusion and a history of temporomandibular joint dysfunction with clicking is scheduled for cervical traction. The standard chin and occiput halter has aggravated her jaw in the past. Which modification is most appropriate for her next session?", "options": {"A": "Tighten the chin strap so the jaw is held firmly and cannot shift under tension", "B": "Keep the same halter and reduce the weight by half for the same pull time", "C": "Switch to a supine mechanical pull with the same chin halter at a lower angle", "D": "Use an occipital-based halter with minimal chin pressure and begin with a lower force"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use an occipital-based halter with minimal chin pressure and begin with a lower force): Loading the occiput instead of the chin reduces force through the temporomandibular joint, and starting lighter lets the jaw tolerate treatment. Tightening the strap increases jaw compression. Halving the weight while keeping the same halter still loads the mandible. Changing to supine does not remove the chin pressure source, so the jaw problem persists."}, {"id": 324, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinician uses a class 4 laser at a continuous 8 W to treat a 20 cm2 area of the lumbar paraspinals in one scanning pass. The protocol calls for 10 J/cm2 across the area. For the dose calculation, the clinician needs the total treatment time for this one area. How long is required?", "options": {"A": "25 s, found by dividing 200 J by 8 W", "B": "40 s, found by dividing 200 J by 5 W", "C": "250 s, found by dividing 200 J by 0.8 W", "D": "1.25 s, found by dividing 10 J by 8 W"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (25 s, found by dividing 200 J by 8 W): Total energy is 10 J/cm2 times 20 cm2, which equals 200 J. Dividing 200 J by 8 W gives 25 s. Ignoring the area gives barely over a second, a decimal slip on power gives ten times too long, and using 5 W does not match the device setting."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist trains the deep neck flexors with a pressure biofeedback unit placed behind the neck. The cuff is inflated to a baseline pressure, and the patient performs craniocervical flexion to progressively higher targets. Which baseline and progression are standard for this test?", "options": {"A": "Baseline of 20 mm Hg, progressing in 2 mm Hg steps up to 30 mm Hg", "B": "Baseline of 40 mm Hg, progressing in 5 mm Hg steps up to 60 mm Hg", "C": "Baseline of 30 mm Hg, progressing in 2 mm Hg steps up to 40 mm Hg", "D": "Baseline of 10 mm Hg, progressing in 2 mm Hg steps up to 20 mm Hg"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Baseline of 20 mm Hg, progressing in 2 mm Hg steps up to 30 mm Hg): The craniocervical flexion test inflates the cuff to a 20 mm Hg baseline and targets five 2 mm Hg steps up to 30 mm Hg. The higher values in the 40 to 60 range are associated with lumbar pressure biofeedback. Starting at 30 or at 10 mm Hg changes both the baseline and the range away from the standard test."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 40 year old man with acne is scheduled for UVB. He says he spent Saturday afternoon at the beach, and today he has mild redness and tenderness across the shoulders and upper back, which are the areas to be treated. The standard session dose is planned. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Treat at half of the planned dose over the red areas", "B": "Reduce the dose by 25 percent and treat all planned areas", "C": "Treat at the planned dose after applying a broad spectrum sunscreen", "D": "Postpone treatment until the sunburn erythema has resolved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Postpone treatment until the sunburn erythema has resolved): Ultraviolet exposure is additive, and skin already showing erythema from sunlight has used up its tolerance. Treating it, even at a reduced dose, risks a more severe burn. Waiting until the erythema resolves lets the therapist dose from a known baseline. Sunscreen would block the intended therapeutic effect of the lamp without removing the burn risk."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes uses a rapid-acting insulin analog and plans a 45-minute brisk walk in the clinic. Her fingerstick glucose before starting reads 82 mg/dL, and her last insulin dose was two hours ago. What is the best step before beginning the walk?", "options": {"A": "Take an additional small insulin dose so the glucose stays stable during activity", "B": "Shorten the walk to 10 minutes and skip any carbohydrate until symptoms appear", "C": "Begin the walk at the planned pace and check glucose again in about 45 minutes", "D": "Ingest 15 to 30 grams of carbohydrate, then recheck glucose before starting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ingest 15 to 30 grams of carbohydrate, then recheck glucose before starting): For a person on insulin, a pre-exercise glucose under about 100 mg/dL leaves little margin, because exercise adds insulin-independent glucose uptake. Taking 15 to 30 grams of carbohydrate and rechecking lowers hypoglycemia risk. Starting without fuel, adding insulin, or waiting for symptoms all invite a low during or after the walk."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old electrician is two weeks out from a lumbar microdiscectomy for an L5-S1 herniation, with leg pain resolved and a clean incision. At the first outpatient visit, the clinician reviews typical early postoperative precautions. Which instruction fits standard guidance for the first several weeks after surgery?", "options": {"A": "Avoid heavy lifting and repeated bending while walking farther each day", "B": "Lift up to 25 pounds with a rounded back once the leg pain has resolved", "C": "Perform lumbar flexion stretching to end range several times daily to prevent scar adhesions", "D": "Keep activity to short household walks and rest, delaying exercise until week twelve"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid heavy lifting and repeated bending while walking farther each day): Standard early guidance after a microdiscectomy limits heavy lifting, repeated bending and prolonged sitting while encouraging progressive walking. This protects the repair and keeps tissue active. Lifting 25 pounds with a rounded back loads the disc too soon, and end-range flexion stretching does the same. Prolonged rest to week twelve is outdated and promotes deconditioning."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 52-year-old man with no cardiac history is starting a walking program, and the clinician wants a quick estimate of his maximal heart rate without ordering a maximal test. The student recalls the traditional age-based prediction equation that appears in most clinic handouts and exercise textbooks. Using that traditional equation, how is the age-predicted maximal heart rate found?", "options": {"A": "Subtract 0.7 times the person's age from 208 beats per minute", "B": "Subtract the person's age in years from 220 beats per minute", "C": "Subtract the person's age in years from 200 beats per minute", "D": "Subtract 0.64 times the person's age from 211 beats per minute"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Subtract the person's age in years from 220 beats per minute): The traditional prediction subtracts age from 220, so this man would be estimated near 168 beats per minute. The 208 minus 0.7 times age and 211 minus 0.64 times age equations are later regressions, not the traditional rule asked about. A constant of 200 is not part of the standard handout formula. All age-based equations carry a standard deviation of roughly 10 beats per minute, so they remain estimates."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student sets interferential channels at 4000 Hz and 3900 Hz for a patient with acute shoulder pain and asks which frequency the nervous system treats as the effective stimulation rate, given that nerves cannot follow a 4000 Hz carrier cycle by cycle. Which frequency is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "The 7900 Hz sum of the carriers, since the currents add together", "B": "The 100 Hz beat created by the interference of the two carriers", "C": "The 4000 Hz carrier, since it has the higher frequency of the two", "D": "The 3950 Hz average of the two carriers, since both reach the tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The 100 Hz beat created by the interference of the two carriers): Nerves cannot respond to each cycle of a medium-frequency carrier, but they follow the amplitude envelope, which pulses at the beat frequency. Here the beat is 4000 minus 3900, or 100 Hz, and that is the effective stimulation rate. Neither carrier alone, nor their average or sum, describes what the nerve experiences."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 19-year-old football player is two weeks after a repair of a vertical tear in the medial meniscus. He is pain free and, seeing good range, asks to start bodyweight squats to 100 degrees of knee flexion. The protocol seeks to protect the repair. Which instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Perform deep passive flexion with overpressure daily to prevent adhesions around the repaired meniscus", "B": "Begin pivoting and twisting drills because the tear was vertical and judged stable by imaging", "C": "Squat to one hundred degrees since loaded deep flexion stimulates meniscal healing in repairs", "D": "Avoid loaded flexion beyond ninety degrees and limit weight bearing per the surgeon's protocol"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid loaded flexion beyond ninety degrees and limit weight bearing per the surgeon's protocol): Deep loaded flexion and rotation compress and shear the meniscus, especially the posterior horn, so repairs are protected from loaded flexion beyond about ninety degrees and from twisting in the early weeks. Being pain free does not mean the repair has healed. Overpressure into flexion and pivot drills put the repair at risk."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 46-year-old teacher with patellar tendinopathy performs a loading program. She rates pain during exercise at 3 out of 10, but the next morning the knee is more painful and stiffer than at baseline. How should the therapist adjust the program?", "options": {"A": "Continue unchanged since exercise pain at three out of ten is acceptable", "B": "Reduce the load or volume until symptoms return to baseline within twenty four hours", "C": "Switch from slow heavy loading to fast plyometric loading to build capacity", "D": "Stop loading for six weeks until the tendon pain settles fully"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the load or volume until symptoms return to baseline within twenty four hours): A tendon loading program is judged by the response over the next 24 hours, and symptoms above baseline the next morning mean the dose was too high. Exercise pain of 3 out of 10 alone does not show that. Plyometric loading is a later stage that adds demand after a flare, and six weeks without loading removes the stimulus the tendon needs."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old violinist reports arm fatigue, heaviness, and ulnar-border hand tingling when holding her arms overhead. Symptoms reproduce within one minute on a three-minute elevated arm stress test with hand opening and closing, and scalene tenderness is present. What is the best initial management?", "options": {"A": "Carpal tunnel splinting with neutral wrist and median nerve gliding at night, plus tendon glides", "B": "Scalene and pectoralis minor stretching with posture correction and scapular strengthening", "C": "Heavy overhead resistance training to build tolerance of the compressed space in the scalene gap", "D": "Early referral for first rib resection after two weeks of symptoms and a positive stress test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Scalene and pectoralis minor stretching with posture correction and scapular strengthening): Positive overhead stress testing with ulnar-sided symptoms suggests thoracic outlet compression, and initial treatment is conservative: stretch the tight scalenes and pectoralis minor, improve posture, strengthen the scapular muscles, and modify work. Surgery is considered only after failed conservative care. Wrist splints and heavy overhead loading do not help."}, {"id": 334, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old marathon trainee has lateral knee pain that begins around mile three of each run, worse on downhill stretches, with no swelling. Palpation reproduces pain over the lateral femoral epicondyle at about 30 degrees of knee flexion. Which structure is irritated in this typical presentation?", "options": {"A": "The lateral meniscus body along the lateral joint line of the tibiofemoral joint", "B": "The popliteus tendon within the popliteal hiatus beneath the lateral collateral ligament", "C": "The distal iliotibial band as it glides over the lateral femoral epicondyle", "D": "The biceps femoris tendon at its insertion on the head of the fibula"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The distal iliotibial band as it glides over the lateral femoral epicondyle): Iliotibial band syndrome produces pain where the distal band passes over the lateral femoral epicondyle, most notably at about 20 to 30 degrees of flexion during running. Downhill running and repeated flexion cycles increase irritation. The popliteus, lateral meniscus, and biceps femoris do not match the epicondyle focus or the absence of swelling."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 63-year-old man has a diabetic heel ulcer. Probing reaches bone, and imaging reported by his physician suggests osteomyelitis that has not yet been treated. The chiropractor considers high voltage pulsed current to speed wound closure. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Do not start electrical stimulation until the bone infection is treated and cleared", "B": "Start stimulation using the lowest pulse rate to reduce microbial spread", "C": "Start anodal stimulation at sensory intensity to avoid stimulating the bone", "D": "Start cathodal stimulation to exploit its bacteriostatic action on the bone infection and the ulcer"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Do not start electrical stimulation until the bone infection is treated and cleared): Untreated osteomyelitis is a contraindication to electrical stimulation for wound healing, because the underlying bone infection must be treated first. Polarity and pulse rate choices do not make stimulation safe over an active bone infection. The treatment should wait until the physician clears it."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A patient walks at a cadence of 120 steps per minute, and the therapist assumes a normal 60 percent stance and 40 percent swing split for each limb. Since each limb completes one cycle every two steps, how long is the swing phase of one limb in each cycle?", "options": {"A": "0.60 seconds, which equals the time of the stance phase", "B": "0.50 seconds, found by taking half of the cycle time", "C": "0.40 seconds, which is forty percent of a one second cycle", "D": "0.20 seconds, found by counting 120 cycles every minute"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0.40 seconds, which is forty percent of a one second cycle): At 120 steps per minute the patient takes 60 cycles per minute, so one cycle lasts 1.0 second. Swing is 40 percent of 1.0 second, which is 0.40 seconds, and stance is 0.60 seconds. Treating 120 as cycles per minute gives a 0.5 second cycle and a 0.20 second swing."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician is setting up conventional (high-rate) TENS for a 38-year-old warehouse worker with chronic shoulder pain. The goal is fast-onset relief using a strong but comfortable sensation, with no visible muscle contraction. Which combination of pulse rate, pulse duration, and intensity best matches the standard conventional TENS setting for this goal?", "options": {"A": "Rate near 100 pps, long pulse duration, intensity high enough for strong visible muscle contraction", "B": "Rate near 4 pps, short pulse duration, intensity giving comfortable tingling without visible muscle contraction", "C": "Rate near 4 pps, long pulse duration, intensity high enough for rhythmic visible muscle twitching", "D": "Rate near 100 pps, short pulse duration, intensity giving comfortable tingling without visible muscle contraction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rate near 100 pps, short pulse duration, intensity giving comfortable tingling without visible muscle contraction): Conventional TENS combines a high pulse rate near 100 pps with short pulses of about 50 to 100 microseconds at sensory intensity, which selectively activates large A-beta fibers and produces tingling without contraction. Long pulses with strong intensity describe brief intense TENS. A low rate with twitching intensity describes acupuncture-like TENS. A low rate at sensory intensity is not a standard TENS mode."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old teacher, four days after a rear-end collision, has Grade II whiplash with pain at 5 of 10 and can turn her head 50 degrees to each side. Her neurological exam is normal. She asks whether to wear a soft collar full time and rest. Which advice best matches current evidence for recovery?", "options": {"A": "Resume usual activity with frequent gentle neck motion performed within pain limits", "B": "Stay on bed rest with the neck supported until the pain has resolved", "C": "Perform passive end range stretching with overpressure twice a day to regain motion quickly", "D": "Wear a rigid collar full time for three weeks to rest the injured tissues"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Resume usual activity with frequent gentle neck motion performed within pain limits): Early return to normal activity and gentle active motion produce better outcomes than rest or prolonged collar use after whiplash. Immobilization beyond a few days promotes stiffness and slows recovery. Forceful end range stretching risks aggravating irritable tissue, so motion stays within comfortable limits."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractic student reviews a classic animal study in which a muscle immobilized in a lengthened position for several weeks developed a longer resting fiber length and a changed length-tension relationship. Which structural adaptation within the muscle fibers best explains this long-term finding?", "options": {"A": "Loss of sarcomeres in series that shifts the length-tension curve toward shorter muscle lengths", "B": "Addition of new myofibrils in parallel that increases the cross-sectional area of the fibers", "C": "Addition of new sarcomeres in series along the length of each muscle fiber", "D": "Raised Golgi tendon organ threshold that changes tension sensing without altering fiber structure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Addition of new sarcomeres in series along the length of each muscle fiber): A muscle held in a lengthened position adds sarcomeres in series, a process called sarcomerogenesis, which lengthens the resting fiber and shifts the optimal length of tension. Immobilization in a shortened position produces the opposite, a loss of series sarcomeres. Parallel myofibril addition raises cross-sectional area and strength, not length. A neural threshold change cannot explain a measured structural fiber length change."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician times side bridge endurance in a 35-year-old warehouse worker and records 90 seconds on the right and 60 seconds on the left. Using McGill's guideline that a left to right difference greater than about 5 percent suggests a deficit, how should this result be interpreted?", "options": {"A": "The difference falls within the 5 percent guideline, so the two sides are balanced", "B": "The left to right ratio is about 0.67, indicating a left lateral core deficit", "C": "The right to left ratio of 1.5 shows normal dominance, so no deficit exists", "D": "The left to right ratio is about 0.67, indicating a right lateral core deficit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The left to right ratio is about 0.67, indicating a left lateral core deficit): Sixty divided by ninety is 0.67, which means the left side lasted about two thirds as long as the right, a difference near 33 percent. That far exceeds the 5 percent guideline and identifies the weaker side as the left. The right side is the stronger side and is not the deficit."}, {"id": 341, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old man with a known history of cervicogenic headache arrives for rehabilitation and reports that today's headache began abruptly within seconds, is the worst of his life, and is accompanied by neck stiffness on flexion. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Refer urgently to his physician within days for imaging of a possible cervical artery dissection", "B": "Stop treatment and arrange emergency evaluation for a possible subarachnoid hemorrhage", "C": "Perform cervical ligament stress tests, then continue treatment if they are normal", "D": "Treat as a migraine variant with dark room rest and refer to his physician next week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop treatment and arrange emergency evaluation for a possible subarachnoid hemorrhage): An abrupt worst-of-life headache with neck stiffness on flexion is a thunderclap pattern concerning for subarachnoid hemorrhage and demands stopping care and emergency evaluation. A referral within days is too slow, ligament testing does not address it, and managing it as migraine or his usual headache risks missing a life-threatening bleed."}, {"id": 342, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient's one repetition maximum on the leg press is 220 lb. The therapist wants to prescribe a strength training load at 85 percent of 1RM for four repetitions per set. What load should be set on the leg press machine, rounded to the nearest pound?", "options": {"A": "198 lb, found by multiplying the 220 lb maximum by 0.90", "B": "33 lb, found by taking fifteen percent of the 220 lb maximum", "C": "154 lb, found by multiplying the 220 lb maximum by 0.70", "D": "187 lb, found by multiplying the 220 lb maximum by 0.85"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (187 lb, found by multiplying the 220 lb maximum by 0.85): The load equals the 1RM times the target fraction, so 220 times 0.85 equals 187 lb. Multiplying 220 by 0.70 gives 154 lb, which uses the wrong percentage. Taking fifteen percent gives 33 lb, the complement of the intended load. Multiplying by 0.90 gives 198 lb, which exceeds the prescribed intensity. Checking again, 220 times 0.85 is 187 lb."}, {"id": 343, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old is five weeks after total knee arthroplasty with limited knee extension. Active and passive knee movement through range is painful, but a muscle contraction without joint movement is comfortable. The therapist chooses a PNF technique. Which technique best fits the patient's pain pattern?", "options": {"A": "Rhythmic initiation, using passive then active assisted movements through the full painful range", "B": "Hold-relax, using an isometric hamstring contraction at the pain-free end of range", "C": "Slow reversal, using repeated alternating concentric contractions of knee flexors and extensors through range", "D": "Contract-relax, using a concentric hamstring contraction that moves the knee through range"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hold-relax, using an isometric hamstring contraction at the pain-free end of range): Hold-relax uses an isometric contraction, so the joint does not move, which makes it the best fit when motion is painful but static effort is not. Contract-relax and slow reversal both require moving through range or repeated concentric work. Rhythmic initiation uses passive and active-assisted movement through the painful range, so these choices load the painful arc."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 44-year-old man had a long flight two days ago. He has a swollen, warm, tender right calf and asks for ultrasound for a calf strain. The student thinks the pain is muscular and wants to start treatment today. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply pulsed ultrasound at low intensity, since pulsed mode is safe over thrombi", "B": "Apply 3 MHz continuous, since the shallow beam will not reach deep vessels", "C": "Withhold ultrasound and refer for evaluation of possible deep vein thrombosis", "D": "Apply ultrasound with fast head movement to avoid dislodging any clot"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold ultrasound and refer for evaluation of possible deep vein thrombosis): Swelling, warmth and calf tenderness after prolonged immobility fit a deep vein thrombosis, and ultrasound over a thrombus is contraindicated because energy and heat could dislodge it or promote clotting. No parameter change makes the treatment safe. The patient needs medical evaluation first."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old recreational lifter returning after a hamstring strain is progressing to heavy resisted work. His therapist sets four sets of five repetitions at about 85 percent of one repetition maximum on the seated leg curl, aiming mainly at maximal strength rather than endurance or size. Which rest interval between sets best fits this goal?", "options": {"A": "Ten minutes between sets so every trace of lactate clears before the next effort", "B": "Fifteen to thirty seconds between sets to keep the heart rate elevated through the session", "C": "Two to three minutes between sets so phosphagen stores recover and load quality holds", "D": "Sixty seconds between sets, the interval usually chosen for hypertrophy and muscular endurance work"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two to three minutes between sets so phosphagen stores recover and load quality holds): Heavy sets near 85 percent of one repetition maximum draw mainly on the phosphagen system, which needs roughly two to three minutes to replenish so the next set can match the load. Very short rests leave the lifter fatigued and force a lighter load, which shifts the stimulus toward endurance. Rests of about 60 seconds suit hypertrophy blocks, and ten minutes adds no strength benefit while consuming most of the session."}, {"id": 346, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractic student prescribes static hamstring stretching: four repetitions of 30 seconds on each leg, five days per week. The patient asks how much total stretching time each single leg receives in one week. Calculate the weekly hold time for one leg, excluding rest periods between repetitions.", "options": {"A": "120 seconds, or 2 minutes of total hold time per leg each week", "B": "600 seconds, or 10 minutes of total hold time per leg each week", "C": "1,200 seconds, or 20 minutes of total hold time per leg each week", "D": "300 seconds, or 5 minutes of total hold time per leg each week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (600 seconds, or 10 minutes of total hold time per leg each week): Each session gives 4 repetitions times 30 seconds, which is 120 seconds per leg. Over five days the total is 120 times 5, or 600 seconds, which equals 10 minutes. Checking the work, 600 divided by 60 is 10. Using 120 seconds counts a single day, and 1,200 seconds doubles the total by adding the second leg."}, {"id": 347, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 21-year-old soccer player sprained the lateral ankle ligaments 36 hours ago. The joint is warm, swollen and tender, and the anterior talofibular ligament lies about 1 cm below the skin. The clinician wants to use ultrasound. Which choice best fits this stage?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz pulsed at 20 percent duty cycle held stationary over the ligament", "B": "1 MHz continuous at 1.5 W/cm2 to maximize heating and clear the edema", "C": "3 MHz pulsed at 20 percent duty cycle at low intensity, with the head moving", "D": "3 MHz continuous at 1.0 W/cm2 for eight minutes to speed tissue warming and repair"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3 MHz pulsed at 20 percent duty cycle at low intensity, with the head moving): In the acute phase, heat increases blood flow and edema, so the goal is nonthermal pulsed ultrasound at low intensity. The target is superficial, so 3 MHz fits, and the head keeps moving. Both continuous options deliver thermal doses, and 1 MHz held still reaches too deep and concentrates energy."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 24-year-old recreational runner has anterior knee pain with stair descent. The knee examination is unremarkable. Hip abduction and external rotation strength each measure 4 minus out of 5, and a single-leg squat shows knee valgus. Which plan best reflects regional interdependence?", "options": {"A": "Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening added to the knee program", "B": "Quadriceps strengthening with emphasis on terminal knee extension exercises for the vastus medialis oblique", "C": "Patellar taping combined with a foam rolling routine for the iliotibial band before running", "D": "Foot orthoses to control pronation, with hip strength deficits addressed later if pain persists"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening added to the knee program): Regional interdependence says a remote impairment can drive local pain, and here weak hip abductors and external rotators allow knee valgus under load. Adding hip strengthening to the knee program addresses that contributor directly. Quadriceps work treats the symptomatic region only. Taping with foam rolling lowers symptoms without correcting the weakness, and orthoses with a delayed hip focus leave the measured deficit untreated."}, {"id": 349, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "An interferential unit has channel one fixed at 4000 Hz and channel two set to 3920 Hz. The therapist wants to confirm the frequency of the amplitude modulation the patient will feel in the tissue where the two currents cross. What beat frequency results from these two settings?", "options": {"A": "A beat frequency of 3920 Hz, equal to the lower of the two carriers", "B": "A beat frequency of 7920 Hz, found by adding the two carriers together", "C": "A beat frequency of 4000 Hz, equal to the fixed carrier on channel one", "D": "A beat frequency of 80 Hz, found by subtracting one carrier from the other"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A beat frequency of 80 Hz, found by subtracting one carrier from the other): The beat frequency equals the difference between the two carriers: 4000 Hz minus 3920 Hz is 80 Hz. Adding the carriers gives 7920 Hz, which the tissue does not feel as modulation. Either carrier alone is an input to the crossing, not the beat. The 80 Hz modulation is the frequency the patient experiences."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "During testing, a 35-year-old patient holds the right side bridge for 72 seconds and the left side bridge for 41 seconds. The therapist recalls McGill's guidance that left and right side bridge endurance should be within about five percent of each other. How should the therapist interpret this finding and respond in the program?", "options": {"A": "A meaningful asymmetry, so extra side bridge work is added for the stronger right side", "B": "A meaningful asymmetry, so both sides are trained equally to preserve overall endurance", "C": "A meaningful asymmetry, so extra side bridge work is added for the weaker left side", "D": "A meaningful asymmetry, so the program switches to dynamic side raises instead of holds"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A meaningful asymmetry, so extra side bridge work is added for the weaker left side): A difference of 72 versus 41 seconds is far beyond five percent and shows a lateral trunk endurance deficit on the left. The program should add work for the weaker left side to restore balance. Extra work for the stronger side widens the gap. Equal training keeps the deficit in place. Swapping holds for dynamic raises changes the task but does not target the deficient side."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 61 year old farmer with extensive plaque psoriasis requests UVB treatment. During intake he reports a basal cell carcinoma removed from his forearm 3 years ago, takes no photosensitizing medication, and has Fitzpatrick type II skin. His dermatologist has not mentioned phototherapy. Which action is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Begin narrowband UVB at a sub-erythemal dose with close checks of his skin each visit", "B": "Begin UVA treatment alone at a low dose with the scar site shielded", "C": "Defer ultraviolet treatment and refer him back to the dermatologist for clearance", "D": "Start UVB after an MED test with the dermatologist informed afterward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer ultraviolet treatment and refer him back to the dermatologist for clearance): A history of skin cancer is a recognized contraindication to ultraviolet therapy because ultraviolet radiation is carcinogenic. The therapist should hold treatment and ask the dermatologist to clear it or choose another approach. Lower doses, a different band or shielding the scar do not remove the added carcinogenic risk. Treatment should not begin before the physician has weighed in."}, {"id": 352, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old has a knee flexion contracture after prolonged immobilization, with a firm, elastic end feel and no pain. The therapist wants the greatest amount of lasting tissue elongation from a stretching session. Which combination of heat and stretch loading best supports permanent lengthening of the collagenous tissue?", "options": {"A": "Heat the tissue, then apply a brief high-load stretch released at once at end range", "B": "Heat the tissue, then perform brief ballistic stretches at the end of the range", "C": "Stretch with a high load first, then apply heat afterward to reduce soreness", "D": "Heat the tissue, then apply a low-load prolonged stretch held while it cools"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heat the tissue, then apply a low-load prolonged stretch held while it cools): Plastic lengthening of collagen is best achieved by heating the tissue, then applying a low-load prolonged stretch maintained while it cools. A brief high-load stretch produces mostly elastic change and risks tearing. Ballistic bounces cannot hold tissue at length. Heating afterward only relieves soreness and misses the window when tissue is most extensible."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 34-year-old tennis player has chronic lateral epicondylitis. The common extensor tendon lies roughly 1 to 2 cm beneath the skin. A chiropractic student plans therapeutic ultrasound and must choose the frequency that concentrates absorption in this superficial tendon rather than passing through it. Which frequency and depth pairing should guide the selection?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz, with most energy absorbed in the superficial 1 to 2 centimeters", "B": "1 MHz, with most energy absorbed at depths of 3 to 5 centimeters below the skin", "C": "3 MHz, with most energy absorbed in the superficial 1 to 2 centimeters", "D": "3 MHz, with most energy absorbed at depths of 3 to 5 centimeters"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3 MHz, with most energy absorbed in the superficial 1 to 2 centimeters): Higher frequencies are absorbed faster, so 3 MHz deposits most of its energy in the first 1 to 2 cm, which is where the extensor tendon sits. The 1 MHz beam travels farther and is chosen for tissue 3 to 5 cm deep. Reversing the frequency and depth pairing is the common error when a student remembers that the two frequencies differ but not which way."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old graphic designer presents with forward head posture, rounded shoulders, and winged scapulae. Janda describes an upper crossed pattern of muscle imbalance in such presentations. A student plans a program that addresses both tight and weak muscle groups. Which plan matches the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Stretch rhomboids and middle trapezius, and strengthen pectoralis minor and levator scapulae", "B": "Stretch deep neck flexors and lower trapezius, and strengthen pectoralis and upper trapezius", "C": "Stretch pectoralis and upper trapezius, and strengthen deep neck flexors and lower trapezius", "D": "Stretch hip flexors and lumbar extensors, and strengthen gluteus maximus and abdominal muscles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stretch pectoralis and upper trapezius, and strengthen deep neck flexors and lower trapezius): The upper crossed pattern combines tight pectorals, upper trapezius, and levator scapulae with weak deep neck flexors, lower trapezius, and serratus anterior. The plan stretches the tight group and strengthens the weak group. Reversing the groups, strengthening tight muscles, or choosing the lower crossed hip and lumbar pattern would worsen the posture."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 67-year-old woman with known metastatic breast cancer to the lumbar spine reports severe low back pain and asks for TENS to help control it between oncology visits. She has not mentioned the idea to her oncologist. The skin over the spine is intact. What is the most appropriate response from the clinician?", "options": {"A": "Apply conventional TENS over the lumbar paraspinals at low intensity because the skin is intact.", "B": "Do not place electrodes over the tumor site, and obtain oncologist approval before any stimulation.", "C": "Apply acupuncture-like TENS over the spine, because endorphin release may suppress tumor pain.", "D": "Apply TENS only to the thighs, keeping the intensity just below the sensory threshold."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do not place electrodes over the tumor site, and obtain oncologist approval before any stimulation.): Electrical stimulation is contraindicated over a known malignancy, because it could affect tumor tissue and hide important pain changes. Spinal metastasis also needs medical oversight. The clinician should avoid placement over the tumor and involve the oncologist before using any stimulation. Intact skin, endorphin rationale, or sub-threshold intensity does not remove the contraindication."}, {"id": 356, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient's ten repetition maximum for seated knee extension is established at 80 lb. The therapist uses the DeLorme protocol of three sets of ten repetitions per session, progressing from lighter to heavier. What load should the patient use for the second set of the first session?", "options": {"A": "40 lb, calculated as fifty percent of 80 lb, which is the first set load", "B": "80 lb, calculated as one hundred percent of 80 lb, which is the third set load", "C": "60 lb, calculated as seventy five percent of 80 lb, for ten repetitions", "D": "55 lb, calculated by subtracting 25 lb from the 80 lb 10RM"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (60 lb, calculated as seventy five percent of 80 lb, for ten repetitions): DeLorme uses 50 percent of 10RM in set one, 75 percent in set two, and 100 percent in set three. For a 10RM of 80 lb, the second set is 0.75 times 80, which equals 60 lb. The 40 lb value is the first set, since 0.50 times 80 equals 40 lb. The 80 lb value is the third set. Subtracting a flat 25 lb has no basis in the protocol."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 40-year-old cyclist has tight hip flexors but a recent minor hip flexor strain still twinges with strong effort. The therapist chooses a hold-relax stretch and must set the isometric contraction and stretch phases. Which parameters best match standard PNF stretching guidance for this situation?", "options": {"A": "Submaximal isometric contraction held 3 to 6 seconds, then a 10 to 30 second assisted stretch", "B": "Maximal isometric contraction held 30 seconds, then a 3 to 6 second assisted stretch", "C": "Rapid ballistic contractions repeated for 2 minutes, then a 10 to 30 second passive stretch", "D": "Maximal effort concentric contraction through range for 10 repetitions, then a brief 3 second stretch"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Submaximal isometric contraction held 3 to 6 seconds, then a 10 to 30 second assisted stretch): PNF stretching commonly uses a submaximal isometric contraction of about 3 to 6 seconds followed by a 10 to 30 second assisted stretch, and submaximal effort fits a recent strain. The other options load the strained muscle maximally, use ballistic efforts, or reverse the short contraction and longer stretch pattern. Dose choices such as these should match the tissue state."}, {"id": 358, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "During the single-leg stance trial of the Balance Error Scoring System on a firm surface, an athlete steps out of position, briefly opens his eyes, and then lifts his forefoot off the ground, all within one twenty-second trial. How many errors are scored for this single trial?", "options": {"A": "Three errors, one for each separate error committed during the trial", "B": "One error, because only the first error is scored in a trial", "C": "Two errors, because opening the eyes is not scored during single-leg stance", "D": "Ten errors, because three errors in one trial earn the maximum"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Three errors, one for each separate error committed during the trial): In the BESS each separate error such as stepping, opening the eyes, or lifting the forefoot or heel is counted, up to 10 per trial. This trial contains three distinct errors, so three are scored. Eye opening is a scored error, so a count of two is wrong. The maximum of 10 is a ceiling for many errors, not an automatic penalty for three, and later errors are not ignored."}, {"id": 359, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A student reads that the lumbar multifidus is a key target in segmental stabilization training after back injury. The instructor asks about its primary role in the lumbar spine, as distinct from the large superficial muscles that generate gross trunk motion and higher forces. Which description of the multifidus is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A superficial muscle that generates large trunk flexion torque during sit-ups and heavy lifting", "B": "A deep muscle that wraps the trunk and raises intra-abdominal pressure to brace the spine", "C": "A deep muscle that provides segmental control and stiffness across individual lumbar vertebrae", "D": "A global extensor that produces large lumbar extension torque through attachments on the ribs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A deep muscle that provides segmental control and stiffness across individual lumbar vertebrae): The multifidus is a deep muscle spanning short segments that gives segmental control and stiffness across individual lumbar vertebrae. Large superficial flexors and global extensors produce gross torque instead. The transversus abdominis is also a deep stabilizer but works by raising intra-abdominal pressure, so it is a different muscle."}, {"id": 360, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 24-year-old soccer player is 14 months out from anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction, has full range of motion, and is cleared to return to cutting sports. The surgeon prescribes a custom knee orthosis for use during play. Which category of knee brace best describes this device and its purpose?", "options": {"A": "Unloader brace, intended to shift joint load away from one compartment of an arthritic knee", "B": "Rehabilitative brace, intended to limit motion and protect a surgical repair in the early weeks", "C": "Prophylactic brace, intended to lower injury risk in a previously uninjured knee during contact sports", "D": "Functional brace, intended to support a ligament deficient or reconstructed knee during sport"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Functional brace, intended to support a ligament deficient or reconstructed knee during sport): A functional brace is used by athletes with an injured or reconstructed ligament to add support while they return to sport. Prophylactic braces are for uninjured knees. Rehabilitative braces with range of motion stops protect a repair in the first weeks, which does not fit a patient 14 months out with full motion. Unloader braces treat compartment osteoarthritis rather than cruciate ligament deficiency."}, {"id": 361, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 39-year-old runner has a painful stress reaction in the left metatarsals. A clinician times stance on each limb with a stopwatch app while he walks down a hallway. Which timing finding is expected in an antalgic gait on the painful side?", "options": {"A": "Both limbs show longer double support, with matching stance times on each side", "B": "The painful limb's swing phase is shortened, so the stance phase lasts longer", "C": "The painful limb's stance phase is prolonged, to protect it from loading", "D": "Painful limb stance phase is shortened, so the other limb steps sooner"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Painful limb stance phase is shortened, so the other limb steps sooner): Antalgic gait is a protective pattern in which the patient spends less time bearing weight on the painful limb, so the stance phase on that side is shortened and the opposite limb advances sooner. A prolonged stance on the painful side would be the reverse of protection. Symmetric slowing is not specific to a painful side."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic adds a class 3B 810 nm laser to treat chronic neck pain. During staff training, the clinic owner asks who in the treatment room must wear protective eyewear during emission and what the eyewear must match. Which statement correctly describes the requirement for the clinician, the patient, and the eyewear selection?", "options": {"A": "The patient wears goggles because the clinician remains outside the direct path of the beam", "B": "Standard tinted sunglasses protect everyone adequately because the beam is low power", "C": "Everyone wears goggles labeled for the laser wavelength with adequate optical density", "D": "The clinician wears goggles while the patient keeps both eyes closed under the treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Everyone wears goggles labeled for the laser wavelength with adequate optical density): Eyewear is wavelength specific, so each pair must be labeled for the laser in use with an optical density that suits its output. Anyone in the room can be exposed to direct or scattered light, including the clinician and bystanders. Closed eyelids transmit some light, and ordinary sunglasses are not rated for the laser wavelength."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinic owner is choosing between stimulators and wants a waveform that causes no polar chemical effects under the electrodes, such as acid or alkaline skin reactions, during long treatments. The vendor lists several waveform types for the devices. Which waveform produces no net charge and therefore no polar chemical reaction under the electrodes?", "options": {"A": "Symmetrical biphasic waveform in which the two phases carry equal and opposite charge.", "B": "Twin-peak monophasic waveform delivering two pulses of the same polarity per cycle.", "C": "Monophasic pulsed waveform in which a single phase repeats at a set rate.", "D": "Continuous direct current in which charge flows in one direction without interruption."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Symmetrical biphasic waveform in which the two phases carry equal and opposite charge.): A symmetrical biphasic waveform sends equal charge in opposite directions, so the net charge is zero and no acid or alkaline products accumulate. Monophasic pulsed current and direct current deposit charge of one polarity and produce polar effects under each electrode. A twin-peak monophasic waveform still has a single polarity, so it also leaves a net charge."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old man with a history of intravenous drug use reports three weeks of worsening mid and low back pain, night sweats, and a temperature of 101.2 degrees F. Percussion over L3 is exquisitely tender, and movement does not change the pain. Which action is most appropriate for the clinician to take?", "options": {"A": "Refer for routine physician review within a few weeks and continue the current exercise program", "B": "Begin thoracolumbar manipulation after screening, because percussion tenderness suggests a segmental joint restriction", "C": "Stop treatment and arrange same-day medical evaluation for possible spinal infection", "D": "Order lumbar flexion radiographs for the next visit and hold exercise until results arrive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop treatment and arrange same-day medical evaluation for possible spinal infection): IV drug use, fever, night sweats, progressive pain and exquisite percussion tenderness suggest vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis. Mechanical-type treatment is inappropriate, and the patient needs medical evaluation the same day. Waiting weeks risks neurological harm and sepsis. Manipulation could spread infection, and deferring to a later imaging visit delays the urgent workup."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old nurse with chronic neck pain, working twelve-hour shifts, reaches 22 mmHg on the craniocervical flexion test with the pressure unit, then substitutes with visible sternocleidomastoid bulging and a chin poke. She begins a training program. Which first exercise prescription fits her current ability?", "options": {"A": "Ten 10-second nodding holds at the 30 mmHg level using maximal effort", "B": "Chin tuck holds with the head lifted off the table for thirty seconds", "C": "Resisted head lifts in supine against manual resistance for three sets of fifteen", "D": "Ten 10-second nodding holds at the 22 mmHg level with minimal sternocleidomastoid activity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ten 10-second nodding holds at the 22 mmHg level with minimal sternocleidomastoid activity): Training starts at the highest pressure she can sustain with the deep flexors and without superficial muscle compensation, here 22 mmHg, using 10-second holds for about ten repetitions. Aiming for 30 mmHg or adding resisted head lifts encourages sternocleidomastoid substitution. The goal is motor control first, then endurance and strength."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 22-year-old man develops painful muscle cramps and dark urine within minutes of starting hard exercise, but feels better after a brief rest and can then continue at a steady pace. A forearm ischemic exercise test shows no rise in venous lactate while ammonia rises normally. Which defect best explains this picture?", "options": {"A": "Mitochondrial complex I deficiency causing early lactic acidosis with exercise intolerance", "B": "Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency, blocking long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria", "C": "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, blocking glycogen breakdown to glucose-1-phosphate", "D": "Myoadenylate deaminase deficiency, which blocks purine nucleotide cycle ammonia production during exercise"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, blocking glycogen breakdown to glucose-1-phosphate): Absence of myophosphorylase prevents glycogen breakdown, so lactate does not rise on ischemic exercise, and cramps with myoglobinuria and a second wind phenomenon occur as blood glucose and fatty acids are delivered. Normal ammonia shows effort was adequate and rules out myoadenylate deaminase deficiency. Complex I defects give an excessive lactate rise, and carnitine palmitoyltransferase deficiency causes symptoms after prolonged exercise."}, {"id": 367, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician preparing electrical muscle stimulation for a weak vastus medialis muscle wants the lowest possible intensity to produce a contraction. A pen electrode is available for locating the best electrode position. Which description defines the motor point, the site the clinician is searching for with the pen electrode?", "options": {"A": "The musculotendinous junction, where tension receptors give the strongest reflex contraction at low current.", "B": "The geometric center of the muscle belly, where the cross section of fibers is largest.", "C": "The site where the motor nerve enters the muscle, where the stimulation threshold is lowest.", "D": "The muscle origin on bone, where the periosteum conducts current best to underlying fibers."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The site where the motor nerve enters the muscle, where the stimulation threshold is lowest.): The motor point is the skin site over the place where the motor nerve branch enters the muscle, so the least current is needed to depolarize the nerve and recruit fibers. A pen electrode probe finds the site giving the strongest twitch at the lowest amplitude. The belly center may be nearby but is not defined by fiber bulk. Tendon junctions and bony origins do not have the lowest nerve threshold."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician watches the left foot of a 30-year-old walker just after heel contact in the loading response. The forefoot descends to the floor smoothly rather than dropping suddenly, and no slapping sound is heard. Which muscle action controls this lowering of the forefoot during loading response?", "options": {"A": "Tibialis anterior working eccentrically to lower the forefoot", "B": "Tibialis posterior contracting concentrically to flatten the medial arch", "C": "Gastrocnemius contracting concentrically to push the forefoot down", "D": "Tibialis anterior contracting concentrically to raise the toes upward"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tibialis anterior working eccentrically to lower the forefoot): After heel contact the ground reaction force pulls the foot into plantarflexion, and tibialis anterior lengthens under tension to lower the forefoot gently. Weakness of this eccentric control produces foot slap. Concentric work by the gastrocnemius or tibialis posterior would not slow the descent of the forefoot."}, {"id": 369, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student is asked to describe the standard Russian stimulation waveform used for quadriceps strengthening after anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction. The device delivers a medium-frequency alternating current that is interrupted into bursts rather than delivered continuously. Which description gives the correct carrier frequency and burst timing of the original Russian protocol?", "options": {"A": "2,500 Hz carrier in 10 bursts per second, 50 ms on and 50 ms off", "B": "2,500 Hz carrier in 50 bursts per second, 10 ms on and 10 ms off", "C": "250 Hz carrier in 100 bursts per second, 5 ms on and 5 ms off", "D": "4,000 Hz carrier in 50 bursts per second, 10 ms on and 10 ms off"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (2,500 Hz carrier in 50 bursts per second, 10 ms on and 10 ms off): Russian stimulation uses a 2,500 Hz alternating current interrupted into 50 bursts per second, each lasting 10 ms with a 10 ms gap, a 50 percent burst duty cycle. Each burst therefore holds 25 carrier cycles. The 4,000 Hz carrier belongs to interferential current. Swapping burst rate and duration, or dropping the carrier into the low-frequency range, no longer describes the Russian waveform."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with stage II posterior tibial tendon dysfunction has a flexible flatfoot, medial ankle pain, and difficulty performing a single-leg heel rise. The clinician is selecting a foot orthosis to support the medial arch and rearfoot. Which design is best?", "options": {"A": "A lateral heel wedge with a forefoot cushion to shift load toward the lateral border", "B": "A heel lift of one half inch to shorten the calf tendon", "C": "A soft cushioned insole with no arch contour to spread plantar pressure", "D": "A rigid or semi-rigid orthosis with a deep heel cup and medial arch support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A rigid or semi-rigid orthosis with a deep heel cup and medial arch support): Stage II dysfunction has a flexible deformity, and a rigid or semi-rigid orthosis with a deep heel cup and medial arch support unloads the posterior tibial tendon by controlling the rearfoot and arch. A soft insole does not provide control. A lateral wedge shifts load in the wrong direction. A heel lift shortens the calf but leaves the arch and rearfoot unsupported."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 43-year-old patient receiving interferential current for sensory-level relief at a 100 Hz beat reports rhythmic muscle pulling beneath the electrodes. The therapist had raised intensity twice during the session to counter fading tingling. What is the best interpretation and adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Switch electrode polarity to relieve muscle pulling beneath the pads", "B": "Raise the carrier frequency so the contraction stops while sensation remains", "C": "Keep the intensity unchanged, because a visible muscle contraction improves analgesia at a 100 Hz beat", "D": "Reduce intensity to the sensory level, and consider beat sweep to counter the fading tingling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce intensity to the sensory level, and consider beat sweep to counter the fading tingling): Raising intensity to overcome accommodation pushed the current past the motor threshold, which is why the muscle pulls. The remedy is to return to the sensory level and use a beat sweep to limit accommodation. Contraction is not the goal in a sensory-level plan. A carrier change would not selectively stop contraction, and interferential current has no polarity."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient performs three sets of ten repetitions of dumbbell shoulder presses at 15 lb and now completes the last set with ease. The therapist applies the principle of progressive overload to keep the muscle adapting. Which guideline is commonly used to decide when to increase the load?", "options": {"A": "Raise the load slightly after two extra repetitions in the last set across two sessions", "B": "Hold the same load until the patient reports no soreness for two full weeks", "C": "Add repetitions only, up to thirty per set, before any increase in weight", "D": "Increase the load by 25 percent after each session where the final set felt easy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Raise the load slightly after two extra repetitions in the last set across two sessions): The two-for-two rule says to increase the load by a small amount once the patient can perform two or more repetitions beyond the target in the last set on two consecutive sessions. A quarter jump after one easy session is too large. Waiting for soreness to vanish ignores performance. Adding repetitions to thirty shifts training toward endurance, which does not keep overloading strength."}, {"id": 373, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 52-year-old woman performing rotator cuff strengthening with 8 lb dumbbells completes 3 sets of 12 repetitions with correct form and minimal effort on two consecutive sessions. Her goal is gradual strength gain without flaring her shoulder pain. Which progression follows the principle of progressive overload?", "options": {"A": "Add a small increment of 1 to 2 lb and keep the repetition range the same", "B": "Switch to 30 repetitions per set at the same 8 lb load to build more endurance", "C": "Jump to 16 lb so the first set is performed near maximal effort with fewer repetitions", "D": "Keep the same load and repetitions and wait two more weeks before changing anything"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add a small increment of 1 to 2 lb and keep the repetition range the same): When a patient completes the target repetitions with good form and low effort on consecutive sessions, the load can be raised by a small increment, generally about 2 to 10 percent. Dumbbells come in coarse steps, so on an 8 lb weight the smallest practical increase of 1 to 2 lb is used and the repetition range stays the same. Moving to 30 repetitions changes the goal toward endurance, doubling the load is too large a jump for an irritable shoulder, and waiting longer withholds the stimulus she is ready for."}, {"id": 374, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 72-year-old woman who has taken oral prednisone for rheumatoid arthritis for eight years develops sudden sharp low back pain after a forceful sneeze. Pain is worse standing and relieved lying down, and there is point tenderness over L1. Which red flag condition should the clinician suspect first?", "options": {"A": "Lumbar disc herniation with an acute annular tear and nerve root irritation", "B": "Acute lumbar muscle strain from sudden contraction of the paraspinal muscles", "C": "Vertebral compression fracture related to steroid-induced bone loss", "D": "Lumbar facet joint syndrome with referred pain into the posterior thigh"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Vertebral compression fracture related to steroid-induced bone loss): Older age, long-term corticosteroid use, and sudden pain after minimal force make osteoporotic compression fracture the leading concern. Focal bony tenderness supports it. These patients need imaging and medical management before loading exercise or manual force. Disc, facet, and strain explanations are common but do not account for the bone risk."}, {"id": 375, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 38-year-old sedentary man starts a supervised resistance program with the leg press. After 3 weeks his one repetition maximum has risen markedly, yet thigh circumference and muscle cross sectional area have not changed. Which mechanism best accounts for the early strength gain seen in these first weeks of training?", "options": {"A": "Muscle fiber hypertrophy from added myofibrillar protein within the first two weeks", "B": "Conversion of Type I fibers into Type IIx fibers, which raises peak force output", "C": "Improved motor unit recruitment, firing rate and synchronization from neural adaptation", "D": "Hyperplasia, the splitting of muscle fibers to create new fibers during the first weeks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Improved motor unit recruitment, firing rate and synchronization from neural adaptation): Strength gains in the first several weeks of resistance training occur mostly because the nervous system learns to recruit more motor units, fire them faster and coordinate them better. Measurable hypertrophy lags behind, which is why girth did not change. Fiber splitting is not an established main mechanism in humans, and resistance training shifts Type IIx toward Type IIa rather than creating Type I into Type IIx conversion."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A chiropractic student is asked which clinical test measures trunk extensor muscle endurance in a person lying prone with the upper body hanging over the end of a table, hips and legs strapped down, and the body held horizontal for as long as possible. Which test fits that description?", "options": {"A": "The prone plank test, held on both forearms and the toes", "B": "The McGill curl-up test, held with one knee bent and hands under the lumbar spine", "C": "The McGill side bridge test, held on one forearm and the feet", "D": "The Biering-Sorensen test, held with the trunk horizontal off the table edge"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Biering-Sorensen test, held with the trunk horizontal off the table edge): The Biering-Sorensen test times how long the person holds the unsupported upper body horizontal, which loads the trunk extensors. The side bridge tests lateral trunk muscles. The curl-up and plank load the flexor side of the trunk."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 49-year-old woman weighing 155 lb has a C6 radiculopathy, and her arm pain eases when the neck is slightly flexed. The therapist sets up intermittent mechanical cervical traction with her supine for the first session. Which setup is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Neck flexed about 25 degrees with a starting force of 10 to 15 lb", "B": "Neck flexed about 25 degrees with a starting force of 50 lb", "C": "Neck in full extension with a starting force of 10 to 15 lb", "D": "Neck flexed to about 45 degrees with a starting force of 10 to 15 lb"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neck flexed about 25 degrees with a starting force of 10 to 15 lb): Moderate flexion of roughly 20 to 30 degrees opens the posterior intervertebral foramina, and a first-session force near 10 to 15 lb is conservative for a 155 lb patient. Extension closes the foramen and may reproduce arm symptoms. Forces such as 50 lb are excessive for an initial trial, and flexion beyond about 30 degrees shifts stress away from the target."}, {"id": 378, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinic handout classifies tissue heating as mild, moderate or vigorous according to temperature rise. A patient with a chronic joint contracture needs collagen extensibility increased before stretching. Which tissue temperature rise defines the vigorous heating category that this goal requires?", "options": {"A": "A rise of 2 to 3 degrees Celsius above baseline, sustained during the treatment", "B": "A rise of about 4 degrees Celsius above baseline, sustained during the treatment", "C": "A rise of about 1 degree Celsius above baseline, sustained during the treatment", "D": "A rise of 8 to 10 degrees Celsius above baseline, sustained throughout the full treatment session"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A rise of about 4 degrees Celsius above baseline, sustained during the treatment): Mild heating (about 1 degree C) speeds metabolism and mild inflammation, while moderate heating (2 to 3 degrees C) reduces pain and spasm and raises blood flow. Vigorous heating (4 degrees C or more) is needed to increase the extensibility of dense collagen, which is this patient's goal. A rise of 8 to 10 degrees would burn tissue."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old man felt a pop at the back of his ankle while playing tennis. He has a palpable gap about 4 cm above the calcaneus, the Thompson test shows no plantarflexion, and he cannot rise onto his toes. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Start gentle calf stretching and eccentric heel drops to promote tendon healing", "B": "Splint the ankle in plantarflexion, protect from weight bearing, and refer", "C": "Apply a compression wrap and ice, advise relative rest, and reassess in one week", "D": "Order an MRI and delay protection until imaging confirms the rupture next week"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Splint the ankle in plantarflexion, protect from weight bearing, and refer): A palpable gap, a positive Thompson test, and inability to rise onto the toes indicate a complete Achilles rupture. The immediate step is to splint in plantarflexion to approximate the ends, protect weight bearing, and refer for operative or functional management. Stretching and eccentric loading stress a torn tendon, a compression wrap with delayed review leaves the ends separated, and waiting for imaging leaves the tendon unprotected."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 30 year old woman received facial UVB for acne, and the therapist placed folded gauze over her closed eyes instead of goggles. Six hours later she calls with severe eye pain, gritty irritation, tearing and light sensitivity in both eyes. She wears no contact lenses. Which cause is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Dry eye disease from prolonged lid closure during the session", "B": "Infrared cataract formation from the heat produced by the lamp during a long session", "C": "Photokeratitis from ultraviolet exposure of the cornea during the session", "D": "Allergic conjunctivitis from a reaction to the cleansing wipes used"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Photokeratitis from ultraviolet exposure of the cornea during the session): Unprotected ultraviolet exposure burns the corneal surface, and symptoms of photokeratitis appear several hours later with pain, gritty irritation, tearing and light sensitivity in both eyes. Gauze is not a reliable ultraviolet barrier, so goggles were needed. Allergic conjunctivitis typically itches and does not start hours after a session. Infrared cataract is a chronic lens change, and lid closure does not cause acute painful irritation."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with low back pain performs a split squat pain-free but loses a neutral spine when the therapist adds trunk rotation and a 10 lb dumbbell at the same time. The therapist wants to keep his progress and apply a sound progression principle. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Return to the split squat and add only one new challenge at a time", "B": "Keep trunk rotation, drop the dumbbell, and add an unstable surface to the split squat", "C": "Regress to bilateral squats with lighter load, then add rotation and weight together later", "D": "Continue the combined challenge for the session, using verbal cues to maintain neutral spine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Return to the split squat and add only one new challenge at a time): He lost neutral spine only when rotation and load were added together, so the sound principle is to progress one variable at a time from the pain-free split squat. Swapping variables while adding a surface still changes several things at once. Going back to bilateral squats then re-adding both repeats the error, and cueing through the failure keeps training with poor control."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "During iontophoresis with dexamethasone at 4 mA, a patient with lateral epicondylitis reports burning under the drug electrode after 6 minutes. The skin beneath it is red but intact. Skin was cleaned before the treatment. The remaining dose would require 4 more minutes. What should the clinician do next?", "options": {"A": "Reverse the polarity of the electrodes to neutralize the burning under the drug pad.", "B": "Raise the current to finish the dose faster while the sensation is still tolerable.", "C": "Continue unchanged for the remaining 4 minutes, since mild redness is an expected finding.", "D": "Stop the current, inspect the skin, and resume at a lower amplitude only if comfortable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop the current, inspect the skin, and resume at a lower amplitude only if comfortable.): Burning under a direct current electrode signals a developing chemical skin reaction, so the current should be stopped and the skin inspected. Treatment can resume at a lower amplitude only if the skin is intact and the patient is comfortable. Mild redness alone can be expected, but burning is a reason to stop. Raising current worsens the injury, and reversing polarity changes which pad is the acid or alkaline site without removing the risk."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student compares how tissues absorb therapeutic ultrasound. The patient has a thickened Achilles tendon, a bulky gastrocnemius muscle and a layer of subcutaneous fat over the calf. The student asks which tissue type absorbs ultrasound energy most readily and therefore heats fastest at a given intensity. Which tissue is it?", "options": {"A": "Blood and other body fluids with their very high water content and low protein", "B": "Dense collagenous tissue such as tendon, ligament and joint capsule", "C": "Subcutaneous fat with its high lipid content and low protein content", "D": "Muscle tissue with its high water content and rich circulating blood supply"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dense collagenous tissue such as tendon, ligament and joint capsule): Absorption rises with protein and collagen content, so tendon, ligament, fascia, scar and capsule absorb the most. Fat and fluid absorb little, and muscle is cooled by its blood supply. This is why ultrasound is chosen to heat the dense connective tissue of joints and tendons selectively."}, {"id": 384, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 60-year-old man with cervical radiculopathy is scheduled for TENS. The clinician has electrode pads available and is reviewing placements with a student. Several placements are discussed for the neck and shoulder region. Which placement should be avoided because of the risk of triggering a reflex drop in heart rate and blood pressure?", "options": {"A": "Electrodes placed across the chest, with one pad on the sternum and one on the back.", "B": "Electrodes placed over the anterior throat, on the thyroid cartilage and the laryngeal muscles.", "C": "Electrodes placed on the anterior neck, directly over the carotid sinus on either side.", "D": "Electrodes placed on the temples and forehead, with current crossing the head."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Electrodes placed on the anterior neck, directly over the carotid sinus on either side.): Pads over the carotid sinus can trigger the baroreceptor reflex, causing bradycardia, hypotension or fainting, so anterior neck placement over the sinus is the one to avoid in this question. Transthoracic placement is also avoided but because of the risk of arrhythmia. Placement over the throat is avoided for laryngeal spasm and airway compromise. Current across the head is avoided for cerebral risks, and none of these is the carotid reflex."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old carpenter has acute neck pain from a muscle strain. The chiropractor selects high voltage pulsed current for gate control analgesia, with electrodes placed over the painful region. Which pulse rate and intensity are most appropriate for this goal?", "options": {"A": "Pulse rate near 1 pps with subsensory intensity that the patient cannot feel", "B": "Pulse rate near 100 pps with intensity high enough for tetanic contraction", "C": "Pulse rate near 100 pps with sensory intensity and no visible contraction", "D": "Pulse rate near 10 pps with intensity high enough for visible twitching"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pulse rate near 100 pps with sensory intensity and no visible contraction): Gate control analgesia relies on a relatively high rate, about 80 to 120 pps, at a sensory intensity that produces comfortable tingling. A low rate of about 10 pps with twitching is the endorphin-oriented method for chronic pain. Tetany at 100 pps would fatigue an acutely strained muscle. A subsensory 1 pps setting gives the patient no afferent input to gate the pain."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 34-year-old graphic designer with neck pain performs the craniocervical flexion test with a pressure biofeedback unit inflated to 20 mmHg. She reaches 24 mmHg with a visible chin jut and strong sternocleidomastoid activity, and cannot hold 26 mmHg. Which training approach best addresses this finding?", "options": {"A": "Cervical traction followed by passive extension stretching of the neck", "B": "Resisted neck flexion with a head strap to build sternocleidomastoid strength", "C": "Chin tuck holds at high effort against a wall to build superficial flexor endurance", "D": "Low-load craniocervical flexion with biofeedback, progressed in small steps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Low-load craniocervical flexion with biofeedback, progressed in small steps): A chin jut with strong sternocleidomastoid activity shows superficial substitution for the deep cervical flexors, so training uses low load with biofeedback and builds pressure in small steps. Resisted flexion strengthens the substituting muscle. High-effort chin tucks also recruit superficial flexors. Traction and passive stretching do not retrain deep flexors."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 47-year-old man has a stiff right knee after a fusion in extension. Throughout the left stance phase he plantarflexes the left ankle and lifts his whole body so the right foot can pass over the floor. Which compensation is he using?", "options": {"A": "Steppage, bending the hip and knee higher than normal in swing", "B": "Hip hiking, raising the pelvis on the swing side with trunk muscles", "C": "Vaulting, lengthening the stance limb to give swing clearance", "D": "Circumduction, swinging the stiff limb outward along a curved path"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Vaulting, lengthening the stance limb to give swing clearance): Vaulting is a rise onto the toes of the stance limb that effectively lengthens it, giving the stiff opposite limb room to swing through. The stem describes plantarflexion of the stance ankle and rising of the body, not elevation of the pelvis or an outward arc. Steppage needs a knee that can flex."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 38-year-old man's right straight leg raise stops at 55 degrees with pulling pain at the back of the thigh. The pain eases when the ankle is plantar flexed and rises when the neck is flexed, with no focal weakness. Which interpretation and plan fit best?", "options": {"A": "Neural sensitivity rather than hamstring shortness, so use gentle neurodynamic mobilization", "B": "Hamstring tear, so rest and apply ice before any movement is allowed", "C": "Hamstring shortening, so use aggressive static stretching with the ankle dorsiflexed", "D": "Hip flexor shortening, so use a Thomas position stretch on the right"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neural sensitivity rather than hamstring shortness, so use gentle neurodynamic mobilization): Symptoms that change with ankle position and neck flexion, which alter neural tension but not hamstring length, point to neural tissue sensitivity. Gentle neurodynamic mobilization is the better fit, and aggressive stretching can aggravate the nerve. A tear or hip flexor shortening would not respond to those distant movements."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old teacher is six weeks past her first episode of acute low back pain. Pain has resolved and she returned to normal activities without any exercise instruction. The therapist explains that ultrasound studies show the multifidus on the painful side does not recover by itself. What does this finding imply for her care?", "options": {"A": "She needs targeted multifidus exercise even though the pain has already resolved", "B": "She can rely on general walking, since the multifidus recovers on its own after pain", "C": "She needs only stretching of the lumbar extensors, since the multifidus is shortened after injury", "D": "She needs general aerobic conditioning and a return to sport to restore the multifidus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She needs targeted multifidus exercise even though the pain has already resolved): Ultrasound research on first-episode low back pain showed the multifidus does not recover spontaneously when pain resolves, so specific exercise is needed to restore it. Walking and other general activity do not retrain the deep segmental muscle. The problem is inhibition and atrophy, not shortening, so stretching misses it. General aerobic conditioning and sport return lack the targeted recruitment the muscle needs."}, {"id": 390, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist treats a psoriatic plaque on the lateral thigh of a patient lying supine. The lamp is directly above the table, but the curved lateral thigh slopes so the plaque edge lies about 60 degrees away from the beam. The next day that edge shows much less erythema than the plaque center. Which correction is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the exposure for the whole plaque until the edge reddens", "B": "Raise the lamp higher so the beam spreads over the sloped edge", "C": "Move the lamp closer to the plaque to raise the dose reaching the edge", "D": "Reposition the patient or lamp so the beam meets the plaque perpendicularly"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reposition the patient or lamp so the beam meets the plaque perpendicularly): Dose falls with the cosine of the angle of incidence, so a surface 60 degrees off the beam receives far less energy. Positioning the lamp or patient so the beam meets the plaque perpendicularly corrects the edge. Longer exposure for the whole plaque overdoses the center. Raising the lamp lowers intensity everywhere, and moving it closer raises the center dose sharply."}, {"id": 391, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A patient receiving microcurrent electrical therapy for a slow-healing ankle sprain asks why she feels nothing even though the unit shows it is running. The clinician explains the usual output range of this device type compared with a TENS unit. Which statement correctly describes microcurrent output?", "options": {"A": "Amplitude is below 1,000 microamperes, typically under the sensory threshold of the patient.", "B": "Current is delivered in bursts that activate only deep tissue and bypass cutaneous nerve endings.", "C": "Amplitude is 1 to 10 milliamperes, which stays under the threshold of most patients.", "D": "Pulse duration is under 10 microseconds, too brief to depolarize any sensory fiber membrane."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Amplitude is below 1,000 microamperes, typically under the sensory threshold of the patient.): Microcurrent delivers current below 1 milliampere, usually tens to hundreds of microamperes, which is below the sensory threshold of most patients. The patient therefore feels nothing, unlike with TENS. A 1 to 10 milliampere output exceeds the microcurrent definition and is often felt. Sensation depends on amplitude and fiber excitation, so neither burst timing nor pulse duration explains the lack of sensation."}, {"id": 392, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "In a Thomas test of a 30-year-old runner, the left thigh rests flat on the table but the left knee extends and the lower leg swings forward as the right knee is held to the chest. A clinician interprets this result and chooses a matching stretch. Which choice fits?", "options": {"A": "Iliopsoas shortening, stretched with the hip extended and the knee kept straight", "B": "Hamstring shortening, stretched with the hip flexed and the knee fully extended", "C": "Rectus femoris shortening, stretched with the hip extended and the knee flexed", "D": "Tensor fasciae latae shortening, stretched with the hip abducted and externally rotated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rectus femoris shortening, stretched with the hip extended and the knee flexed): When the thigh stays flat but the knee extends, the rectus femoris is shortened, because it crosses both the hip and the knee. It is lengthened with the hip extended and the knee flexed. Iliopsoas shortening would lift the thigh with the knee flexed. The tensor fasciae latae is stretched with adduction and extension, and the hamstrings are not tested by this finding."}, {"id": 393, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 28 year old runner has chronic hamstring tightness limiting straight leg raise to 60 degrees, with no pain, swelling, or recent injury. The goal is to increase hamstring length through sustained passive stretching in today's visit. How should the therapist sequence moist heat and stretching to take advantage of thermal effects?", "options": {"A": "Stretch first, then apply moist heat afterward to reduce soreness", "B": "Apply moist heat while stretching, using a thin single towel layer", "C": "Apply the moist heat first, then stretch while the tissue is still warm", "D": "Apply ice for 20 minutes first, since cold increases collagen extensibility"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply the moist heat first, then stretch while the tissue is still warm): Heat increases collagen extensibility and reduces resistance for a short time, so stretching right after heating gives more length gain. Stretching first wastes the effect. Cold does not increase collagen extensibility, and stretching through a pack with thin toweling risks a burn and is not practical."}, {"id": 394, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 68-year-old man with peripheral arterial disease has chronic foot and ankle tendon pain. His pedal pulses are weak and the foot is cool to touch with slow capillary refill. The clinician wants to use thermal ultrasound. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use a lower intensity and shorter duration with close monitoring, because heat clears slowly", "B": "Use a stationary sound head, because less movement protects fragile skin from shear", "C": "Use the standard dose, because ultrasound heating does not depend on circulation", "D": "Use a higher dose, because impaired circulation means the tissue needs stronger heating"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a lower intensity and shorter duration with close monitoring, because heat clears slowly): Poor circulation clears heat slowly, so thermal ultrasound needs lower intensity, shorter duration and careful monitoring. A stationary head concentrates heat and risks burns. Heating does depend on blood flow, and raising the dose in poorly perfused tissue invites overheating."}, {"id": 395, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 12-year-old gymnast has heel pain from calcaneal apophysitis, and the calcaneal growth plate is still open on recent radiographs of the foot. The clinician considers continuous 3 MHz ultrasound at 1.5 W/cm2 over the heel. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply continuous 1 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 with a moving head over the heel", "B": "Avoid continuous thermal doses over the open growth plate and use a gentle option instead", "C": "Apply continuous 3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 with a moving head for ten minutes", "D": "Apply continuous 1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 with a stationary head to reach the plate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid continuous thermal doses over the open growth plate and use a gentle option instead): An open growth plate is a relative precaution for continuous thermal ultrasound, so the clinician should choose a gentle option such as relative rest, activity modification or cooling. Changing frequency or lowering intensity in a continuous mode still heats tissue near the physis. A stationary head concentrates energy and adds hotspot risk. The safest step is to avoid continuous thermal doses over the plate altogether."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A clinician compares blood pressure responses of four patients who each progress from rest to 6 METs on a graded treadmill protocol, with readings recorded as systolic over diastolic in mmHg. One pattern is an abnormal hypertensive response that calls for medical review. Which pattern is abnormal?", "options": {"A": "118 over 76 at rest rising to 154 over 70 at peak effort", "B": "130 over 84 at rest rising to 168 over 86 at peak effort", "C": "124 over 78 at rest rising to 176 over 82 at peak effort", "D": "126 over 80 at rest rising to 170 over 112 at peak effort"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (126 over 80 at rest rising to 170 over 112 at peak effort): Systolic pressure normally rises with workload while diastolic stays about the same or changes by a few mmHg. A diastolic increase of more than 10 mmHg, or a value above 110, is an abnormal hypertensive response that needs medical review. The other three show appropriate systolic rises with stable or slightly lower diastolic readings."}, {"id": 397, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "While observing a patient from the side, a clinician marks the phase that starts at the instant the right heel touches the floor and ends when the left foot leaves the floor. During this first double support interval the right limb accepts body weight. Which stance subphase is being described?", "options": {"A": "Terminal stance, when the heel rises and the body moves ahead of the foot", "B": "Midstance, the single support period when the body passes over the foot", "C": "Loading response, the weight acceptance period at the start of stance", "D": "Preswing, the second double support interval that prepares the limb for toe-off"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Loading response, the weight acceptance period at the start of stance): Loading response begins at initial contact and ends when the opposite foot lifts off, about the first 10 percent of the cycle. It is the first double support interval and the period of weight acceptance and shock absorption. Midstance follows as single limb support, terminal stance begins with heel rise, and preswing is the second double support interval."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old hairdresser with de Quervain tenosynovitis has pain aggravated by repetitive gripping and pinching with the wrist moving into ulnar deviation. Initial conservative care is planned for the first three weeks before any injection is considered. Which splint and activity advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Thumb spica splint holding the thumb and wrist still, with less repeated pinching", "B": "Elbow extension night splint that holds the forearm in full supination", "C": "Volar wrist cock-up splint that leaves the thumb free for continued pinching tasks", "D": "Circumferential elastic wrist wrap worn during sleep with the thumb left free"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thumb spica splint holding the thumb and wrist still, with less repeated pinching): The first dorsal compartment tendons move with thumb and wrist motion, so a thumb spica splint rests them and reduced pinching limits irritation. A cock-up splint that frees the thumb leaves the tendons working. An elbow night splint treats nerve problems at the elbow, and a wrap with free thumb gives little rest."}, {"id": 399, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old sprinter is two weeks after a grade 2 hamstring strain. Walking and jogging are pain free and he has full passive range. Manual resistance with the knee at 90 degrees of flexion causes mild pain, and he is eager to start sprinting. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Begin submaximal sprint drills now because jogging is pain free and range is full", "B": "Begin sprinting at half speed since mild pain on testing is acceptable", "C": "Delay sprinting and continue eccentric strengthening until testing is pain free and symmetrical", "D": "Switch to static stretching only until range exceeds that of the other leg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delay sprinting and continue eccentric strengthening until testing is pain free and symmetrical): Return to sprinting requires pain free resisted testing and near symmetrical strength, because sprinting loads the hamstring eccentrically at high speed. Pain on manual resistance shows the tissue is not ready, even though jogging and range look good. Stretching alone does not rebuild the strength needed to protect against reinjury."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 32-year-old cashier stands with a pronounced lumbar lordosis, an anterior pelvic tilt, and a protruding abdomen, and reports tightness in the front of the hips. Palpation shows weak lower abdominals and gluteal muscles. Which exercise strategy best addresses these findings in a stabilization program?", "options": {"A": "Anterior pelvic tilts in standing, plus lumbar extension stretching over a Swiss ball", "B": "Resisted sit-ups with the feet anchored, plus seated hip flexor strengthening", "C": "Prone press-ups to end range, plus hamstring stretching and light bridging", "D": "Posterior pelvic tilts with drawing-in and gluteal activation, plus hip flexor stretching"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Posterior pelvic tilts with drawing-in and gluteal activation, plus hip flexor stretching): An anterior tilt with lordosis goes with tight hip flexors and weak abdominals and gluteals, so the plan lengthens the hip flexors and activates the lower abdominals and gluteals with posterior tilting. Anterior tilting and extension stretches reinforce the posture. Press-ups add extension. Anchored sit-ups and hip flexor strengthening shorten the tight muscles further."}, {"id": 401, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 62-year-old man is 5 days after total knee replacement and has been receiving electrical muscle stimulation to the quadriceps. Today he reports increasing left calf pain, and the calf is swollen, warm, and tender compared with the right. The clinician was planning to add stimulation to the calf muscles. What is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Withhold calf stimulation and arrange urgent medical evaluation for possible deep vein thrombosis.", "B": "Continue the calf stimulation but add a compression stocking to protect the vein.", "C": "Apply conventional TENS to the calf, since it does not produce a muscle contraction.", "D": "Apply stimulation to the calf at low intensity to promote venous return and reduce swelling."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold calf stimulation and arrange urgent medical evaluation for possible deep vein thrombosis.): Unilateral calf pain, swelling, warmth, and tenderness after knee replacement suggest deep vein thrombosis. Muscle contraction could dislodge a clot, so calf stimulation is withheld and the patient is sent for urgent medical evaluation. Lower intensity or compression stockings do not remove the risk. Electrical stimulation of any kind over a suspected thrombus is avoided."}, {"id": 402, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old patient with chronic low back pain completes bilateral glute bridges for 15 repetitions with perfect pelvic control and no pain. The clinician wants to increase the demand on the gluteus maximus and the lumbopelvic stabilizers without adding external load. Which progression is most appropriate next?", "options": {"A": "Prone trunk extensions from the floor held at end-range lordosis", "B": "Single-leg bridges while keeping the pelvis level and the trunk still", "C": "Supine straight leg raises with the lumbar spine allowed to arch", "D": "Bilateral bridges with the feet placed far from the hips to emphasize the hamstrings"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Single-leg bridges while keeping the pelvis level and the trunk still): Removing one foot from the floor doubles the load on the working gluteus maximus and forces the stabilizers to resist rotation and pelvic drop. That meets the goal of greater demand without added weight. Placing the feet far away shifts work to the hamstrings. Straight leg raises and prone extensions load the lumbar spine rather than train the target muscles."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A neurology note states that a patient's one-sided headache is cervicogenic. A chiropractic student asks which spinal segments are classically implicated, noting that afferent input from them converges with trigeminal pathways in the trigeminocervical nucleus. Which cervical levels most commonly act as the source of pain in this headache type?", "options": {"A": "The upper three cervical segments, chiefly the C1-C2 and C2-C3 joints", "B": "The cervicothoracic junction, chiefly the C7-T1 and T1-T2 joints", "C": "The lower cervical segments, chiefly the C6-C7 joint and first rib articulation", "D": "The mid cervical segments, chiefly the C4-C5 and C5-C6 joints"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The upper three cervical segments, chiefly the C1-C2 and C2-C3 joints): Cervicogenic headache is referred from the upper cervical joints, muscles and nerves, mostly the C1-C3 segments. Their afferents converge with the trigeminal nucleus caudalis, so neck pain is felt in the head. The lower levels commonly produce arm symptoms or shoulder pain, not head pain."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A powerlifter's muscle biopsy is reviewed in a lab after 16 weeks of heavy squatting. The physiotherapist asks which fiber types typically show the greatest increase in cross-sectional area with heavy resistance training. The athlete trained mainly with loads of 3 to 8 repetitions at high effort. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Neither fiber type, since strength gains come from added fiber number only", "B": "Type I and type II fibers, which grow equally with matching percent increases", "C": "Slow-twitch type I fibers, which grow more than fast-twitch fibers", "D": "Fast-twitch type II fibers, which grow more than type I fibers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Fast-twitch type II fibers, which grow more than type I fibers): Heavy resistance training produces greater hypertrophy in type II fibers, particularly type IIa, than in type I fibers. Both types can grow, but not equally. Hyperplasia contributes little in humans, so growth in fiber size is the main structural change."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 48-year-old patient is confined to bed for two weeks after abdominal surgery, and the clinician anticipates a fall in her aerobic capacity. The clinician wants to explain which change accounts for the earliest loss of VO2 max during prolonged bed rest. Which physiologic change is most responsible for that early decline?", "options": {"A": "Decline in arterial saturation from reduced lung diffusion capacity", "B": "Reduced plasma volume and stroke volume, lowering cardiac output at maximal effort", "C": "Fall in maximal heart rate of about 20 beats per minute during maximal work", "D": "Rapid regression of muscle capillary density within the first few days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced plasma volume and stroke volume, lowering cardiac output at maximal effort): Bed rest quickly reduces plasma volume and stroke volume, which lowers maximal cardiac output and therefore VO2 max. Muscle capillary density and mitochondrial enzymes change more slowly over weeks. Maximal heart rate stays roughly the same, and lung diffusion capacity is not the limiting factor in this early loss."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A physical therapist is screening four new referrals before starting aerobic conditioning in an outpatient cardiac and medical fitness clinic. Each patient has a different cardiovascular history. According to standard exercise testing and prescription guidelines, which condition is an absolute contraindication to beginning an exercise session today?", "options": {"A": "Resting systolic pressure of 205 mmHg with stable symptoms after a recent medication change", "B": "Known left main coronary stenosis with controlled symptoms and a normal resting electrocardiogram", "C": "Moderate aortic valve stenosis with no symptoms during daily activities", "D": "Unstable angina that has not yet been stabilized with medical therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Unstable angina that has not yet been stabilized with medical therapy): Unstable angina that has not been stabilized signals active plaque instability and a high risk of infarction, so it is an absolute contraindication to exercise. Resting systolic pressure above 200 mmHg, left main stenosis, and asymptomatic moderate valve stenosis are relative contraindications, where benefits may outweigh risks under supervision. A therapist can still proceed with careful monitoring in those cases."}, {"id": 407, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A strength coach explains why box jumps are classified as plyometric exercise for a patient returning to basketball. The explanation centers on the sequence of muscle actions that lets the muscle produce more force than a concentric action alone. Which sequence defines the stretch-shortening cycle used in plyometric training?", "options": {"A": "Rapid concentric contraction, a brief amortization pause, then forceful eccentric loading", "B": "Rapid eccentric loading, a prolonged isometric hold of several seconds, then slow concentric contraction", "C": "Rapid eccentric loading, a brief amortization pause, then forceful concentric contraction", "D": "Slow eccentric lowering, a brief amortization pause, then forceful concentric contraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rapid eccentric loading, a brief amortization pause, then forceful concentric contraction): The stretch-shortening cycle is a rapid eccentric stretch, a very brief amortization phase, then a forceful concentric contraction that uses stored elastic energy and the stretch reflex. Reversing the order removes the stretch. A long hold lets stored energy dissipate, and a slow eccentric phase does not engage the stretch reflex."}, {"id": 408, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 57-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has lost protective sensation in both feet and has a healed plantar callus. He wants an aerobic program of 30 minutes, four days weekly. Which exercise mode best fits his foot status for the first several weeks of training?", "options": {"A": "Stationary cycling or seated arm and leg ergometry with well-fitted shoes", "B": "Barefoot walking on varied surfaces to retrain foot sensation and improve proprioception", "C": "Step aerobics with hopping patterns to build bone density and balance together", "D": "Treadmill jogging at a moderate pace on a padded belt with daily foot checks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stationary cycling or seated arm and leg ergometry with well-fitted shoes): Loss of protective sensation means repetitive impact or friction can cause unnoticed ulcers. Non-weight-bearing modes such as cycling or seated ergometry deliver aerobic work without that stress, with properly fitted shoes. Jogging and hopping repeat impact loads on the foot. Barefoot walking exposes insensate skin to injury with no warning pain."}, {"id": 409, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old man fell on an outstretched arm two weeks ago and has since been unable to lift his arm. He shows marked weakness in abduction, a positive drop arm test, and cannot hold his arm at 90 degrees. Passive range is nearly full. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue pendulums and wall slides as a home program for another month before reassessing", "B": "Start a thermal ultrasound and stretching program for presumed adhesive capsulitis", "C": "Refer for imaging and orthopedic evaluation for suspected full-thickness rotator cuff tear", "D": "Begin progressive resisted abduction with tubing to rebuild deltoid strength over six weeks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for imaging and orthopedic evaluation for suspected full-thickness rotator cuff tear): A traumatic onset, inability to hold abduction, and a positive drop arm test with preserved passive motion point to a full-thickness cuff tear that needs imaging and surgical opinion. Resisted work could aggravate it. Passive range is not restricted, so adhesive capsulitis does not fit, and waiting a month delays care."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 52-year-old man with hypertension controlled on medication, resting at 134 over 86 mmHg, wants to begin resistance training for general fitness. He proposes sets at his maximum weight with very few lifts. He has no known heart disease, trains at a community gym, and reports no symptoms in daily activity. Which resistance prescription is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Loads of 60 to 70 percent of maximum for 10 to 12 repetitions with continuous breathing", "B": "Sustained isometric holds at maximum effort for ten seconds with a long exhale afterward", "C": "Loads of 80 to 90 percent of maximum for 4 to 6 repetitions and paced breathing", "D": "Loads of 40 to 60 percent of maximum for 12 to 15 repetitions holding the breath"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loads of 60 to 70 percent of maximum for 10 to 12 repetitions with continuous breathing): Hypertensive clients should use moderate loads for higher repetitions with continuous breathing, because heavy lifting and breath holding produce large pressure surges. Sixty to 70 percent of maximum for 10 to 12 repetitions fits this. Near-maximal loads and sustained maximal isometrics both drive pressure up sharply. Holding the breath, even with lighter loads, adds a Valsalva effect that is the problem to avoid."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 19-year-old football player with an anterior cruciate ligament deficient knee wears a functional brace and says the knee feels stable, so he wants to skip his neuromuscular training and return to cutting drills. How should the therapist interpret the role of the brace in his plan?", "options": {"A": "It controls rotational instability at high speed, so training can focus on conditioning", "B": "It supplements but cannot replace dynamic muscular control, so training should continue", "C": "It strengthens the hamstrings through resistance from its hinges, so extra training is redundant", "D": "It restores the mechanical restraint of the ligament, so cutting drills can resume without training"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It supplements but cannot replace dynamic muscular control, so training should continue): Functional braces can add some restraint at low loads and may improve proprioception, but they do not control high speed rotation and they do not restore the ligament. Dynamic stability depends on neuromuscular control, so training continues. A brace does not strengthen the hamstrings, and conditioning alone does not address the instability."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist works with a patient who lacks shoulder stability in quadruped and wants a manual contact that enhances the joint stabilizers and weight-bearing responses. Which proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation manual cue should the therapist apply through the shoulders during the quadruped posture?", "options": {"A": "Approximation, a compressive force through the joint to stimulate the stabilizing muscles", "B": "Traction, a distraction force along the limb to ease motion and facilitate pulling", "C": "Stretch reflex, a quick lengthening of the muscle before the voluntary effort", "D": "Resisted rolling, a manual force applied to the trunk during segmental rolling from supine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Approximation, a compressive force through the joint to stimulate the stabilizing muscles): Approximation compresses the joint surfaces and stimulates joint receptors and stabilizers, which improves weight-bearing responses in quadruped. Traction separates the surfaces and facilitates mobility and pulling. A quick stretch initiates movement. Resisted rolling is a different task and does not compress the joint."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A patient using a home TENS unit with a symmetrical biphasic waveform notices that the red lead is on the pad above his painful elbow and the black lead is below it. He asks whether swapping the leads would make the treatment stronger or more effective at reducing his pain. How should the clinician answer?", "options": {"A": "Alternate the polarity every few minutes, which prevents a chemical burn under one pad.", "B": "Make the electrode over the pain positive, which produces an acid reaction that relieves pain.", "C": "Make the electrode over the pain negative, which attracts positive ions and reduces inflammation.", "D": "Polarity makes no meaningful difference with a balanced biphasic waveform; amplitude and placement matter more."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Polarity makes no meaningful difference with a balanced biphasic waveform; amplitude and placement matter more.): A balanced biphasic waveform alternates direction each phase and has no net charge, so there is no true anode or cathode and no polar chemical effect. Swapping leads changes nothing meaningful. The effect depends on amplitude, pulse parameters, and electrode placement. Ion attraction and acid or alkaline reactions belong to direct current, and burn prevention is not an issue here."}, {"id": 414, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A heavy-set patient has chronic piriformis spasm, with the muscle estimated 5 cm beneath the skin and a thick subcutaneous fat layer. The goal is to heat the muscle. Which parameter set is most appropriate for the clinician to start with?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz, continuous, about 1.5 W/cm2, with a moving technique", "B": "1 MHz, pulsed at 20 percent, 0.5 W/cm2, with a stationary technique", "C": "3 MHz, continuous, about 1.5 W/cm2, with a moving technique", "D": "3 MHz, pulsed at 20 percent, 0.5 W/cm2, with a stationary technique"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 MHz, continuous, about 1.5 W/cm2, with a moving technique): A muscle 5 cm deep needs the penetration of 1 MHz, and heating needs a continuous output at roughly 1.5 W/cm2 with the head kept moving to avoid hot spots. A 3 MHz beam would be absorbed in the superficial tissues. Pulsed 20 percent settings produce mainly nonthermal effects."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 76-year-old woman recently received progressive multifocal lenses and now misjudges curbs and the edges of stairs when she walks outside. She is otherwise healthy and wants to keep her glasses. Which advice from the therapist best lowers her fall risk when walking outdoors and on stairs?", "options": {"A": "Remove the glasses on stairs so the distortion does not interfere", "B": "Keep the multifocals on and look down through the reading segment", "C": "Switch to bifocals with a wide near segment for walking tasks", "D": "Wear single-vision distance glasses when walking outdoors and using stairs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wear single-vision distance glasses when walking outdoors and using stairs): Multifocal lenses blur the floor and reduce contrast and depth cues at the lower field, which raises tripping risk on curbs and stairs. Single-vision distance lenses give a clear view of the walking surface during these tasks. Looking through the reading segment, removing glasses, or using bifocals keeps either blur or uncorrected vision in the critical area."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 66-year-old with treated hypertension, resting blood pressure 138/86 mmHg, is stretching tight hamstrings with PNF hold-relax. During earlier sessions she held her breath and strained during maximal contractions, and her blood pressure rose sharply. What modification is most appropriate for subsequent sessions?", "options": {"A": "Continue maximal contractions but cue exhaling during the contraction and shorten the hold to five seconds", "B": "Use submaximal isometric contractions with continuous breathing and no Valsalva maneuver", "C": "Continue maximal contractions and record blood pressure before each repetition, stopping above 160/100 mmHg", "D": "Replace the PNF technique with ballistic bouncing stretches that need no sustained contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use submaximal isometric contractions with continuous breathing and no Valsalva maneuver): Maximal isometric contractions with breath holding produce a Valsalva effect and a sharp pressor response, so the fix is submaximal contractions with continuous breathing. Cueing exhalation helps, but maximal effort still raises pressure considerably. Monitoring before each repetition records the problem without removing its cause. Ballistic bouncing avoids the contraction but adds strain risk, and her resting 138/86 mmHg does not call for stopping stretching."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinician measures passive straight leg raise on a 28-year-old with the opposite leg flat and the pelvis stabilized. The right hip reaches 55 degrees before the pelvis begins to tilt posteriorly. The knee is fully extended and there are no neurological symptoms. How should the clinician interpret this result?", "options": {"A": "Hip capsular restriction, because passive straight leg raise normally reaches about 120 degrees", "B": "Hamstring shortening, because normal passive straight leg raise reaches roughly 80 degrees", "C": "Sciatic nerve tension, because passive straight leg raise normally reaches about 90 degrees", "D": "Normal hamstring length, because passive straight leg raise normally reaches about 50 degrees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hamstring shortening, because normal passive straight leg raise reaches roughly 80 degrees): With the pelvis stabilized and the knee straight, passive straight leg raise normally reaches about 80 degrees, so a limit at 55 degrees indicates hamstring shortening. The 120 degree figure applies to hip flexion with the knee bent. Neural tension needs symptoms or response to ankle or neck position, which are absent. A 50 degree norm is not the accepted range."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A therapist introduces a new standing balance exercise to a 70-year-old man who has never performed it. He makes large, variable errors and cannot yet describe what he is doing wrong. His fatigue is low and he is motivated. Which teaching approach best fits his current stage of learning?", "options": {"A": "Demonstrate the whole movement, give simple cues, and provide frequent specific feedback on each trial", "B": "Withhold feedback during the session so the patient discovers errors from internal sensation alone", "C": "Introduce distracting dual task challenges immediately so the movement becomes automatic more quickly", "D": "Randomize several unrelated balance tasks each minute to build adaptable movement strategies early"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Demonstrate the whole movement, give simple cues, and provide frequent specific feedback on each trial): A learner in the cognitive stage needs to understand what the task is, so a demonstration, simple cues and frequent specific feedback help him build the movement plan. Dual task challenges, no feedback and heavy randomization suit later stages when errors are small, intrinsic feedback can be used and attention demand has fallen. The same approach is not the best fit once the movement is learned."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "Two ultrasound units were tested in a lab at the same average output. Unit A has a beam nonuniformity ratio of 2:1 and unit B has a ratio of 7:1. A student must decide which unit delivers energy more evenly and carries the lower risk of hot spots under the head. Which conclusion is correct?", "options": {"A": "Unit A, because a lower ratio means peak intensity sits closer to the average intensity", "B": "Unit B, because a higher ratio means a higher average intensity delivered", "C": "The units are equal, because both were tested at the same average output", "D": "Unit B, because a higher ratio indicates a larger effective radiating area and cooler beam"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Unit A, because a lower ratio means peak intensity sits closer to the average intensity): Beam nonuniformity ratio is the spatial peak intensity divided by the spatial average intensity. A ratio near 1:1 means a uniform beam, so Unit A at 2:1 is safer. Unit B at 7:1 has peaks seven times the average, creating hot spots even when the displayed output looks modest. Equal average output does not mean equal distribution. A larger ratio does not mean a bigger or cooler beam."}, {"id": 420, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student holds the sound head still on the calf of a 38-year-old runner for eight minutes at 1.5 W/cm2 continuous. The instructor stops the treatment and corrects the technique. Which problem does the stationary technique create at this intensity?", "options": {"A": "Excessive broadening of the beam, which spreads the energy over too large an area", "B": "Hot spots at the beam peaks and standing waves, which can injure tissue and cause pain", "C": "Reduced absorption of the beam, which limits tissue heating in the calf muscle", "D": "Excessive reflection from the skin, which overheats the transducer face"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hot spots at the beam peaks and standing waves, which can injure tissue and cause pain): A stationary head concentrates the beam peaks in the same tissue and builds standing waves, with blood cell stasis, hot spots and periosteal pain. Slowly moving the head spreads the energy through the field. The beam does not broaden or absorb less when the head is still."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 15-year-old competitive rower has mid-thoracic pain after practice and a fixed rounded upper back that does not flatten when he extends over a bolster. Radiographs are ordered to confirm Scheuermann disease rather than postural round back. Which radiographic finding is the classic diagnostic criterion?", "options": {"A": "A thoracic kyphosis above 20 degrees with normally shaped vertebral bodies", "B": "A lateral curve with a Cobb angle of at least 10 degrees and vertebral rotation", "C": "Anterior wedging of at least 5 degrees in three or more adjacent vertebrae", "D": "Loss of vertebral body height of 50 percent after an acute compression fracture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anterior wedging of at least 5 degrees in three or more adjacent vertebrae): Sorensen's criteria define Scheuermann disease by anterior wedging of 5 degrees or more in at least three consecutive vertebrae, usually with endplate irregularity. Postural kyphosis shows normal vertebral shapes and flexes freely, and a Cobb angle of 10 degrees describes scoliosis. Fracture produces acute height loss, not gradual wedging across several levels."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 37-year-old with a temperature of 101 degrees F, chills and body aches arrives for a scheduled massage. He says massage always helps his aches. He has no localized injury and has been ill for two days. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Give a short stimulating massage to promote immune circulation", "B": "Defer the massage until the fever and infection have resolved", "C": "Give light lymphatic drainage strokes only, to support the immune response", "D": "Give gentle effleurage only, since the aches are muscular"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Defer the massage until the fever and infection have resolved): A systemic infection with fever is a contraindication to massage, so deferral until recovery is correct. Stimulating massage will not boost immunity and may worsen malaise. Lymphatic drainage strokes still circulate fluid during acute infection. Gentle effleurage also raises circulation, and the muscular aches are part of the illness."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old nurse who stands for twelve hour shifts has burning pain between the third and fourth metatarsal heads consistent with Morton neuroma. The therapist adds a metatarsal pad to her insole. Where should the pad be positioned relative to the metatarsal heads to unload them?", "options": {"A": "Under the navicular and sustentaculum tali, to support the medial longitudinal arch", "B": "Just distal to the heads, under the proximal phalanges of the toes", "C": "Just proximal to the metatarsal heads, so it spreads and relieves the heads", "D": "Directly under the metatarsal heads, so it cushions them from the ground"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Just proximal to the metatarsal heads, so it spreads and relieves the heads): A metatarsal pad sits just behind the metatarsal heads so it lifts and spreads them and takes pressure off the interdigital nerve. Placed directly under the heads it increases pressure on the painful area. Placed distal to the heads it loads the toes and does nothing for the heads. An arch support under the navicular addresses the midfoot, not the forefoot."}, {"id": 424, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 36-year-old warehouse supervisor is 12 weeks after Achilles tendon repair. Swelling has resolved, but adhesions limit tendon gliding and dorsiflexion. The tendon is about 1 cm below the skin. Which plan is best for this goal in this patient at this stage?", "options": {"A": "3 MHz pulsed at 20 percent at 0.5 W/cm2 to avoid heating the tendon", "B": "1 MHz continuous at 2.0 W/cm2 with a stationary head, then rest for the day", "C": "1 MHz pulsed at 20 percent at 0.5 W/cm2 for deep nonthermal effect", "D": "3 MHz continuous at about 1.0 W/cm2 with a moving head, then stretching"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3 MHz continuous at about 1.0 W/cm2 with a moving head, then stretching): Adhesions in a shallow, chronic tendon respond to heating that increases extensibility, so continuous 3 MHz at about 1.0 W/cm2 with a moving head is appropriate, followed immediately by stretching. A stationary head risks hot spots, and pulsed low-intensity settings do not heat enough. A 1 MHz beam would pass beyond a tendon this shallow."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student examines the oscilloscope display of a high voltage pulsed current unit while setting it up for a clinic demonstration. The instructor points out that the waveform differs from a rounded monophasic pulse. Which description correctly identifies the characteristic waveform of high voltage pulsed current?", "options": {"A": "A monophasic pulse made of two very brief peaks separated by a short interval", "B": "A long-duration rectangular monophasic pulse lasting several hundred milliseconds", "C": "A symmetrical biphasic square pulse with a net charge of zero per cycle", "D": "A sinusoidal alternating wave with an amplitude that rises and falls at the beat rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A monophasic pulse made of two very brief peaks separated by a short interval): High voltage pulsed current delivers a monophasic pulse with two extremely short peaks, called the twin-peaked waveform. The symmetrical biphasic square wave is typical of some low-voltage stimulators. The amplitude-modulated sinusoid is interferential current. Long rectangular monophasic pulses are used to stimulate denervated muscle."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 31 year old woman arrives 36 hours after a rear end collision with neck muscle guarding, mild local warmth over the right upper trapezius, and tenderness. Her goal is relief of spasm before a work trip. Both heat and cold seem plausible to her. Which approach best matches her stage and findings?", "options": {"A": "Hot pack first to relax the muscle, then a cold pack afterward to limit swelling", "B": "Moist heat for 20 minutes, because spasm responds best to relaxation", "C": "Alternating hot and cold packs every 5 minutes to pump fluid", "D": "Cold pack with gentle positioning, because warmth indicates ongoing acute inflammation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cold pack with gentle positioning, because warmth indicates ongoing acute inflammation): Local warmth at 36 hours shows inflammation is still active, so cold remains the better choice and may ease the spasm. Heat would raise permeability and swelling in an inflamed area. Alternating hot and cold packs, or heating first, does not respect the acute stage."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 57 year old man with knee osteoarthritis is scheduled for laser therapy to the medial joint line. During skin inspection, the chiropractor notices an irregular dark mole on the medial thigh, just proximal to the planned treatment points, which the patient says has grown over several months. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Cancel all treatment and prohibit laser therapy to the knee for the entire course", "B": "Cover the mole with an opaque bandage and treat every planned point as scheduled", "C": "Irradiate the mole briefly with a low dose to see whether it responds", "D": "Avoid the mole, refer for dermatologic evaluation, and proceed with the joint line points"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoid the mole, refer for dermatologic evaluation, and proceed with the joint line points): An irregular, enlarging dark lesion needs medical evaluation for melanoma and should not receive laser energy. The joint line is a separate site and can be treated while the referral proceeds, taking care to keep the beam off the mole. A bandage is not a reliable barrier to scatter, and irradiating the lesion is inappropriate even at low dose."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 30-year-old man develops painless medial scapular winging after a viral illness. The winging worsens at 90 degrees of forward flexion, he cannot raise the arm above shoulder height, and there is visible wasting over the serratus anterior. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer for neurological assessment of long thoracic nerve function before strengthening", "B": "Begin progressive resisted push-up plus exercises to rebuild serratus strength", "C": "Start trapezius strengthening with prone Y raises at heavy load", "D": "Apply scapular taping and stretch the pectoralis minor before any further testing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer for neurological assessment of long thoracic nerve function before strengthening): Painless winging after a viral illness with wasting and loss of elevation suggests long thoracic nerve involvement, which needs neurological assessment before exercise. Strengthening a denervated muscle will not rebuild it, and other muscle work does not address the nerve. Taping and stretching treat posture, not a suspected nerve lesion. Early referral also guards against loading an unrecovered nerve."}, {"id": 429, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A patient receives electrical muscle stimulation from a constant current device to the quadriceps at 45 mA. Midway through treatment one edge of a pad peels away from the skin, and the patient suddenly reports a sharp, intense sensation under that pad. The contraction remains strong. What best explains this sudden sensation?", "options": {"A": "The pad polarity reversed automatically, producing a brief direct current effect under that pad.", "B": "Skin impedance fell beneath the lifted edge, so the device delivered additional current.", "C": "Motor nerve fibers adapted to the stimulus and began firing at a higher frequency.", "D": "Reduced contact area raised the current density beneath the pad edge that stayed attached."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced contact area raised the current density beneath the pad edge that stayed attached.): With a constant current device, total current stays at 45 mA when contact area falls. The same current through a smaller area raises current density, which causes a sharp sensation and risk of burn. The pad should be reattached or replaced and intensity reduced. Impedance changes do not add current in this device type, polarity does not reverse, and accommodation reduces sensation rather than sharpening it."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 70 year old man has an infected heel ulcer with a purulent base surrounded by intact, fragile skin. UVC irradiation is ordered to reduce bacteria. The wound has been cleansed. To prevent unwanted burns to the surrounding tissue, which step should the therapist add before turning on the lamp?", "options": {"A": "Cover the ulcer with a clear dressing so the lamp output is softened", "B": "Drape the surrounding intact skin so only the ulcer is exposed", "C": "Expose a wider skin margin to treat the tissue around the ulcer too", "D": "Apply a moist saline gauze to the ulcer to carry radiation deeper"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Drape the surrounding intact skin so only the ulcer is exposed): UVC is absorbed in the outer layers and burns healthy skin, so intact surrounding tissue should be draped to expose only the ulcer. A dressing or wet gauze over the wound would absorb the radiation and defeat the treatment. Widening the margin exposes fragile skin that has no need of irradiation. Draping keeps the dose on the target."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A treatment protocol for psoriasis describes graded erythema reactions as multiples of the patient's MED, from a first degree reaction up to blistering. A student must memorize the multiples used in the standard system. Which dose, expressed as a multiple of the MED, produces a second degree, or E2, erythema?", "options": {"A": "A dose equal to two and one half times the minimal erythemal dose", "B": "A dose equal to twice the minimal erythemal dose", "C": "A dose equal to five times the minimal erythemal dose", "D": "A dose equal to ten times the minimal erythemal dose"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A dose equal to five times the minimal erythemal dose): The standard grading uses about 2.5 times the MED for E1, 5 times for E2 and 10 times for E3. An E2 reaction is therefore five times the MED. Two and one half times is the E1 dose and ten times is the E3 dose. A simple doubling is not part of the system."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 40 year old woman with multiple sclerosis has hamstring spasticity and notes her symptoms and fatigue worsen on hot days and after hot showers. The therapist wants a modality to ease tone before gait training. Which choice fits her condition?", "options": {"A": "Brief cooling of the legs, while avoiding hot packs that could worsen symptoms", "B": "Moist heat to the hamstrings, since warmth reduces spindle activity in spasticity", "C": "A contrast bath starting with a long warm immersion, then brief cold dips", "D": "Continuous thermal ultrasound over the hamstrings to relax tone before gait training"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brief cooling of the legs, while avoiding hot packs that could worsen symptoms): People with multiple sclerosis often worsen with heat, so brief cooling of the legs can ease tone and fatigue before gait training. Moist heat, a long warm immersion at the start of a contrast bath, and thermal ultrasound all raise tissue temperature. Any of them can aggravate her symptoms despite their general use for spasm."}, {"id": 433, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 52 year old teacher has plantar fasciitis. The clinic's protocol calls for 4 J/cm2 at each point. The probe emits a continuous 50 mW and the spot area measures 0.5 cm2. The chiropractor needs the exposure time per point to meet this protocol. How long should each point be treated?", "options": {"A": "0.4 s, found by dividing 2 J by 5 W", "B": "160 s, found by dividing 8 J by 0.05 W", "C": "40 s, found by dividing 2 J by 0.05 W", "D": "80 s, found by dividing 4 J by 0.05 W"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (40 s, found by dividing 2 J by 0.05 W): The energy needed per point is 4 J/cm2 times 0.5 cm2, which equals 2 J. Dividing 2 J by 0.05 W gives 40 s. Skipping the area gives 80 s, dividing by the area gives 160 s, and misreading the output as 5 W gives less than a second."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 29-year-old man believes his sore legs 36 hours after a heavy squat session come from lactic acid left in the muscle. His blood lactate was 15 mmol/L right after the session, and he asks for massage to flush it out. Which statement about the cause of his soreness is best?", "options": {"A": "Acidosis from hydrogen ions that lingers in the muscle until deep tissue massage flushes it", "B": "Residual lactic acid crystals that dissolve slowly and irritate nociceptors for up to two days", "C": "Persistent phosphocreatine depletion that triggers pain receptors until stores are fully replenished", "D": "Microscopic fiber and connective tissue damage with an inflammatory response in the sore muscles"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Microscopic fiber and connective tissue damage with an inflammatory response in the sore muscles): Lactate and hydrogen ions return to baseline within about an hour of exercise, so they cannot explain soreness at 36 hours. Delayed onset muscle soreness follows mechanical damage to fibers and connective tissue with an inflammatory response. Phosphocreatine recovers within minutes, and lactic acid crystals do not exist in muscle."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "Intramuscular recordings from an experienced weightlifter and a sedentary adult of similar size show that, during a maximal grip, the lifter's motor units discharge in closer temporal alignment, and the lifter produces more force despite equal muscle cross sectional area. Which neural adaptation does this finding most directly reflect?", "options": {"A": "Lower motor neuron firing rate, which prolongs each twitch and fuses force at lower frequency", "B": "Increased motor unit synchronization, which can improve force production and rate of force development", "C": "Reduced spindle afferent input, which removes reflex inhibition of the agonist muscle during effort", "D": "Greater muscle fiber hypertrophy, which raises the force generated by each active motor unit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increased motor unit synchronization, which can improve force production and rate of force development): The recordings show motor units firing closer together in time, which is synchronization. Trained lifters are thought to show more of it, and it may help force production and rate of force development independent of muscle size. The equal cross sectional area rules out hypertrophy. Training generally raises, not lowers, firing rates, and spindle afferents excite rather than inhibit the agonist."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 52-year-old office worker tries a side plank supported on the elbow and feet, but her hips drop toward the floor within five seconds, and she reports fatigue along the lateral trunk without pain. The therapist wants a regression that keeps the side bridge pattern and the same muscle focus. What is the best modification?", "options": {"A": "Perform the side bridge with the knees bent and the lower legs on the table", "B": "Perform the side bridge with the top foot lifted off the bottom foot", "C": "Switch to a prone plank on the elbows to train the abdominal wall instead", "D": "Perform the side bridge with a hand-held weight in the top hand to build endurance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perform the side bridge with the knees bent and the lower legs on the table): Her hips drop within five seconds, so she needs less lever arm while keeping the same lateral trunk muscle focus. Bending the knees shortens the lever and lets her hold a true side bridge. Lifting the top foot and adding weight increase demand. A prone plank changes the pattern and moves emphasis away from the lateral trunk."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with chronic heart failure arrives for her regular session. She reports gaining 4 pounds in two days, new difficulty breathing when lying flat, and swelling over both ankles. Her resting oxygen saturation is 94 percent and her heart rate is 88. What is the most appropriate decision?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the usual session because her oxygen saturation and heart rate are acceptable", "B": "Perform seated limb exercises only and ask her to log weight daily at home", "C": "Withhold exercise and contact her cardiology team the same day about possible decompensation", "D": "Run a shortened low-intensity session at perceived exertion of 11 and monitor symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold exercise and contact her cardiology team the same day about possible decompensation): A gain of several pounds in days, orthopnea, and new ankle edema point to fluid overload and worsening heart failure. Exercise should be withheld and the cardiology team contacted promptly. Shortened or seated sessions still stress a decompensating heart. Normal saturation and heart rate do not exclude congestion."}, {"id": 438, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 33 year old woman with Achilles tendinopathy has taken oral doxycycline for acne for two months. She reports that she sunburns easily on this medication. The chiropractor plans a first laser session over the Achilles tendon with a 660 nm probe. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the standard dose because laser therapy produces no ultraviolet radiation", "B": "Decline laser treatment because photosensitizing drugs rule out phototherapy of any kind", "C": "Begin with a conservative test dose, check the skin reaction, and then progress", "D": "Use the highest tolerated dose on the first session to complete treatment sooner"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin with a conservative test dose, check the skin reaction, and then progress): Photosensitizing drugs can raise skin reactivity to visible light as well as ultraviolet, so a cautious first dose with a skin check is the standard approach. The fact that a laser emits no ultraviolet does not remove the risk. The condition is a precaution rather than an absolute contraindication, and a high first dose adds needless risk."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "During evaluation of a 40-year-old assembly worker who has nighttime tingling in the thumb, index, and middle fingers, the examiner decides to perform Phalen's test to provoke median nerve symptoms. The patient is seated comfortably with elbows on the table. Which procedure describes the correct position and duration for this test?", "options": {"A": "Maximal wrist extension held about 60 seconds with the palms pressed together in a prayer position", "B": "Light percussion over the volar wrist crease with a reflex hammer along the median nerve", "C": "Direct thumb pressure over the volar carpal tunnel held thirty seconds with the wrist neutral", "D": "Maximal wrist flexion held about 60 seconds with the backs of the hands pressed together"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Maximal wrist flexion held about 60 seconds with the backs of the hands pressed together): Phalen's test holds the wrists in full flexion with the dorsal surfaces together for up to about 60 seconds, and reproduced tingling suggests median nerve compression. The prayer position uses extension. Percussion over the wrist is Tinel's sign, and direct pressure over the tunnel is the carpal compression test."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A therapist examines the infraspinatus of a patient with deep shoulder aching and finds a localized spot of marked tenderness. To justify calling it a myofascial trigger point rather than a general area of soreness, which clinical finding is the most characteristic?", "options": {"A": "Widespread tenderness at 11 of 18 paired sites with no localized palpable band", "B": "Tenderness confined to the bony tendon insertion, without any palpable band in the muscle", "C": "Joint line tenderness with crepitus and pain that stops when the muscle is relaxed", "D": "A palpable taut band containing a hypersensitive spot that refers pain on compression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A palpable taut band containing a hypersensitive spot that refers pain on compression): The classic criteria are a taut band in a skeletal muscle with a hypersensitive nodule that, when pressed, reproduces or refers pain. The 11 of 18 pattern is the older fibromyalgia tender point criterion and has no referred pain. Insertional or joint line tenderness points to tendon or joint pathology."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 29-year-old man completes 16 weeks of heavy resistance training with 4 to 6 repetition sets at about 85 percent of one repetition maximum. A repeat biopsy of the vastus lateralis is compared with baseline for fiber cross sectional area. Which result is most expected?", "options": {"A": "Greatest hypertrophy in Type II fibers, with a smaller increase in Type I fiber area", "B": "Equal hypertrophy of all fiber types because loading recruits every fiber at 85 percent", "C": "Hypertrophy confined to Type I fibers, with Type II fibers unchanged in area", "D": "Selective hypertrophy of Type I fibers with Type II fibers decreasing in area"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Greatest hypertrophy in Type II fibers, with a smaller increase in Type I fiber area): Heavy resistance training recruits high threshold motor units, and the Type II fibers they supply show the largest increases in cross sectional area, with smaller increases in Type I fibers. Selective Type I growth reverses this pattern. Equal growth ignores the recruitment difference, and Type I only growth fits endurance training better. Fiber type specific growth is a well described adaptation."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 40-year-old man with a measured resting heart rate of 70 beats per minute begins an aerobic program. The clinician uses the age-predicted maximum heart rate of 180 and the Karvonen method to set his starting target at 60 percent of heart rate reserve. What starting target heart rate should the clinician record?", "options": {"A": "178 beats per minute, from 60 percent of the maximum plus the resting rate", "B": "136 beats per minute, from the reserve of 110 times 0.60 plus 70", "C": "66 beats per minute, from 60 percent of the reserve alone", "D": "108 beats per minute, from 60 percent of the age-predicted maximum alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (136 beats per minute, from the reserve of 110 times 0.60 plus 70): Heart rate reserve is 180 minus 70, or 110. Sixty percent of 110 is 66, and adding the resting rate of 70 gives 136 beats per minute. The value 108 is simple 60 percent of maximum, a different method. The value 66 omits the resting rate, and 178 mixes the two methods and exceeds a sensible target."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old woman returns from an 11-hour flight with a tight, swollen left calf that is warm and tender to squeeze. She asks for a deep calf massage to loosen it up. The calf measures 3 cm larger than the right. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Apply a warm pack and advise stretching of the gastrocnemius", "B": "Use deep petrissage because swelling after a long flight is usually muscular", "C": "Give light effleurage toward the heart and recheck the calf size", "D": "Withhold massage and arrange urgent evaluation for deep vein thrombosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold massage and arrange urgent evaluation for deep vein thrombosis): Unilateral calf swelling with warmth, tenderness and a recent long flight is a classic risk picture for deep vein thrombosis, and massage could dislodge a clot. The patient needs prompt imaging and medical evaluation. Even light effleurage, heat or stretching is not safe until the diagnosis is excluded."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old pitcher has a flare of posterior shoulder pain 24 hours after a long outing. The shoulder is warm, mildly swollen, and tender over the infraspinatus tendon insertion at the greater tuberosity. Which modality is most appropriate in this acute inflammatory stage?", "options": {"A": "Continuous ultrasound at 1.5 W/cm2 for eight minutes over the tender insertion", "B": "Cryotherapy for ten to fifteen minutes with compression and relative rest", "C": "Continuous shortwave diathermy at a thermal dose for twenty minutes over the whole shoulder", "D": "A moist hot pack for twenty minutes before gentle stretching of the posterior cuff"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cryotherapy for ten to fifteen minutes with compression and relative rest): A warm, swollen tendon within a day of overuse is in the acute inflammatory stage, and cryotherapy with relative rest limits swelling and pain. Hot packs, thermal ultrasound, and thermal diathermy all increase blood flow and can worsen swelling. These heating options fit later subacute or chronic stages."}, {"id": 445, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 19-year-old cross country runner who tripled mileage in three weeks has diffuse aching along the distal posteromedial tibial border over about eight centimeters. There is no focal bony point tenderness, and she has no night pain. What is the best initial management for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Use crutches with non weight bearing for six weeks and obtain an immediate MRI scan", "B": "Stop all running for two weeks, then resume the previous mileage immediately", "C": "Reduce running volume, cross train with low impact exercise, and increase load gradually once pain free", "D": "Switch to a cushioned shoe and tape the tibia while continuing the same weekly mileage"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce running volume, cross train with low impact exercise, and increase load gradually once pain free): Diffuse posteromedial tibial pain without focal tenderness or night pain after a sudden mileage jump fits medial tibial stress syndrome, a load problem managed by reducing volume, cross training, and building load gradually once pain free. Crutches and urgent MRI are for suspected stress fracture. A brief rest followed by the same mileage repeats the overload, and shoes or taping alone leave the load unchanged."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "During lumbar traction with a thoracic belt, a 24-year-old man becomes pale and sweaty and says he feels faint. His pulse is 48 beats per minute and his blood pressure is 88 over 52. He has no chest pain. What is the most appropriate immediate action?", "options": {"A": "Release the traction, sit him upright with his head between his knees, and recheck pulse", "B": "Stop the traction, keep him supine, give sugary fluids, and resume at lower force later", "C": "Reduce the force by half, keep him supine, and continue the session while monitoring his pulse", "D": "Release the traction, keep him supine, raise his legs, and monitor vital signs"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Release the traction, keep him supine, raise his legs, and monitor vital signs): Pallor, sweating, faintness, bradycardia and low pressure suggest a vasovagal reaction to traction. The belt must be released and the patient kept supine with legs raised while vitals are monitored. Sitting up risks a faint, and continuing at lower force or resuming soon exposes him to the same trigger."}, {"id": 447, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 35 year old data analyst has a taut band with a referred headache pattern in the upper trapezius, no swelling, and normal sensation. The therapist chooses vapocoolant spray with stretch rather than a long cold pack. Which sequence best matches the technique?", "options": {"A": "Apply moist heat first for 30 minutes, then use spray with stretching", "B": "Spray the skin repeatedly without stretching, since cooling alone releases the band", "C": "Spray while the muscle is passively lengthened, then follow with moist heat", "D": "Hold ice on the band for 20 minutes, then stretch the muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Spray while the muscle is passively lengthened, then follow with moist heat): In spray and stretch, brief skin cooling is paired with passive lengthening of the muscle so the stretch is tolerated, and moist heat afterward restores comfort. Prolonged ice is a different treatment. Spraying without stretching omits the key step, and heating first reverses the usual sequence."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 24-year-old healthy rugby player runs 2,500 meters in the Cooper 12-minute test. The clinician applies the published prediction equation, in which VO2 max in mL/kg/min equals the distance in meters minus 504.9, divided by 44.73. What is the estimated VO2 max for this athlete?", "options": {"A": "55.9 mL/kg/min, from 2,500 meters divided by 44.73", "B": "67.2 mL/kg/min, from (2,500 plus 504.9) divided by 44.73", "C": "39.9 mL/kg/min, from (2,500 minus 504.9) divided by 50", "D": "44.6 mL/kg/min, from (2,500 minus 504.9) divided by 44.73"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (44.6 mL/kg/min, from (2,500 minus 504.9) divided by 44.73): Subtracting 504.9 from 2,500 leaves 1,995.1, and dividing by 44.73 gives 44.6 mL/kg/min. Dividing 2,500 alone by 44.73 gives 55.9 and ignores the intercept. Adding the constant gives 67.2, and a divisor of 50 gives 39.9, so neither follows the published equation."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 32-year-old lifter asks which signal most directly switches on muscle protein synthesis after heavy lifting. His friend, an ultramarathoner, says endurance work seems to blunt hypertrophy through an energy-sensing pathway. Which pathway is most directly activated by mechanical tension and amino acids such as leucine to increase protein synthesis?", "options": {"A": "The myostatin pathway, which promotes fiber growth when its signaling increases", "B": "The mTORC1 signaling pathway, which increases translation initiation in muscle fibers", "C": "The AMPK pathway, which stimulates protein synthesis when cellular energy is high", "D": "The cAMP-dependent glycogenolysis pathway, which directly drives contractile protein translation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The mTORC1 signaling pathway, which increases translation initiation in muscle fibers): Mechanical tension and leucine activate mTORC1, which increases translation initiation and ribosome activity and so raises muscle protein synthesis. AMPK is activated by low energy and inhibits mTORC1, which is why heavy endurance work can blunt hypertrophy. Myostatin limits muscle growth, and cAMP signaling mobilizes glycogen rather than driving translation."}, {"id": 450, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old man is 3 weeks after an arthroscopic rotator cuff repair. He reports little pain and asks to start resisted external rotation with a band to regain strength quickly. His surgeon's protocol protects the repair, and tendon-to-bone healing is incomplete. Which plan is most appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Continue protected passive motion and delay resisted strengthening until later healing", "B": "Begin closed chain weight bearing push-ups on the table to load the repair", "C": "Begin isometric rotator cuff contractions at maximum effort in all planes", "D": "Begin light band resisted external rotation now, because pain is minimal and range is good"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Continue protected passive motion and delay resisted strengthening until later healing): At three weeks the tendon-to-bone interface is still weak, and absence of pain does not mean the repair is strong. Protocols emphasize protected passive and assisted motion and delay resisted strengthening until later, often after about ten to twelve weeks. Band work, maximal isometrics, and weight bearing push-ups load the repair too early."}, {"id": 451, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 22-year-old soccer player presents the day after a grade II ankle sprain with marked swelling. The chiropractor applies high voltage pulsed current for edema control with the ankle and electrodes in a basin of cool water. Which parameters best match the usual protocol for acute edema?", "options": {"A": "Negative active electrode, pulse rate about 120 pps, intensity below the motor threshold", "B": "Positive active electrode, pulse rate about 120 pps, intensity above the motor threshold", "C": "Positive active electrode, pulse rate about 10 pps, intensity producing strong contraction", "D": "Negative active electrode, pulse rate about 10 pps, intensity producing strong contraction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Negative active electrode, pulse rate about 120 pps, intensity below the motor threshold): Acute edema control uses a negative active electrode, a rate near 120 pps and an intensity below the motor threshold so no contraction increases bleeding or swelling. Motor-level, low-rate stimulation is a pumping method for later, chronic swelling. Positive polarity is not the one chosen to limit protein leakage. Only the stated set keeps both polarity and intensity appropriate for a one-day-old injury."}, {"id": 452, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 40-year-old with low back pain has tape measurements from the anterior superior iliac spine to the medial malleolus that are equal on both sides, yet the left iliac crest sits lower in standing. The clinician is deciding whether a heel lift is the right first step. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Place a heel lift under the left foot, since a low iliac crest shows structural shortening", "B": "Fit a full length orthotic with a rearfoot post on the left to correct the difference", "C": "Place a lift under the right foot to level the pelvis and relieve the low side", "D": "Treat the pelvic and hip muscle imbalance first, since the bone lengths are equal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the pelvic and hip muscle imbalance first, since the bone lengths are equal): Equal tape measured lengths mean the bones are the same length, so the low iliac crest probably reflects a functional pelvic or hip asymmetry, and the cause should be treated first. A lift under the left foot would assume a structural shortening that was not found. A lift on the right foot would worsen the asymmetry, and an orthotic does not correct muscular pelvic tilt."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "Before beginning cervical rotation exercises and mobilization, a clinician screens a 46-year-old woman with sudden severe neck pain and headache for cervical arterial dysfunction. Which symptom cluster, often taught as the five Ds and three Ns, raises the strongest concern for vertebrobasilar insufficiency?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral hand clumsiness, gait disturbance, hyperreflexia, positive Hoffmann sign and bladder urgency", "B": "Arm paresthesia, thumb weakness, reduced biceps reflex, positive Spurling test and shoulder tenderness", "C": "Fever, night sweats, weight loss, focal bone tenderness and a history of cancer", "D": "Dizziness, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia and drop attacks with nausea, numbness and nystagmus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dizziness, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia and drop attacks with nausea, numbness and nystagmus): The five Ds are dizziness, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia and drop attacks, and the three Ns are nausea, numbness and nystagmus. They reflect posterior circulation ischemia and call for stopping manual therapy and arranging urgent evaluation. The other clusters describe radiculopathy, systemic disease and myelopathy, each with a different next step."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A therapist explains to a student why a healthy adult's knee flexes about 15 degrees right after heel contact. The student asks what this early stance knee flexion accomplishes and which muscle action produces it. How should the therapist answer?", "options": {"A": "It absorbs impact, with eccentric quadriceps work in early stance", "B": "It lets the foot clear the floor, with the hamstrings contracting concentrically after heel contact", "C": "It absorbs impact, with concentric hamstring work to control the descending body weight", "D": "It lowers the foot to the floor, with the tibialis anterior contracting eccentrically in early stance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It absorbs impact, with eccentric quadriceps work in early stance): Knee flexion of about 15 degrees in loading response absorbs the shock of heel contact, and the quadriceps lengthen under load, an eccentric action. Foot clearance happens in swing, not stance. Concentric hamstring work would not resist the knee bending under body weight. The tibialis anterior does act eccentrically to lower the forefoot, but that controls the ankle and not what knee flexion accomplishes."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A clinician reviewing metabolism for a deconditioned patient notes that during quiet rest in the post-absorptive state the body does not burn fuels in equal proportions. A student asks which substrate supplies the largest share of energy in a healthy adult lying at complete rest. Which substrate is the main contributor at rest?", "options": {"A": "Muscle protein, broken down continuously to fuel baseline energy needs", "B": "Phosphocreatine, mobilized steadily from muscle stores to cover resting ATP demand", "C": "Fatty acids, providing the majority of energy for the resting muscle and heart", "D": "Blood glucose alone, which supplies nearly all resting energy for skeletal muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fatty acids, providing the majority of energy for the resting muscle and heart): At rest, oxidative metabolism relies mostly on free fatty acids, which supply roughly half to well over half of resting energy, with carbohydrate making up most of the rest. Protein contributes very little in a fed or fasted resting state. Phosphocreatine is held in reserve for brief high-power efforts rather than steady resting demand."}, {"id": 456, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old volleyball player has anterior knee pain on stairs and squatting. Her patella sits laterally with a tight lateral retinaculum, and her pain drops from 6 to 2 out of 10 when the clinician manually glides the patella medially during a squat. Which taping approach follows from this finding to allow exercise?", "options": {"A": "Lateral glide taping of the patella to stretch the medial retinaculum during squats", "B": "Medial glide taping of the patella, to reduce pain and permit quadriceps exercise", "C": "Circular compression taping around the knee to limit all knee flexion during stairs", "D": "Superior glide taping of the patella to pull it upward toward the quadriceps"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Medial glide taping of the patella, to reduce pain and permit quadriceps exercise): The pain improvement with a manual medial glide acts as a test of the corrective direction, so medial glide taping is chosen to mimic it and allow pain free strengthening. Lateral glide would worsen the lateral position. Circular compression does not correct patellar position, and a superior glide does not match the finding."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During a push-up, a 24-year-old swimmer's right scapula lifts away from the rib cage, with the medial border and inferior angle prominent as the body lowers. The clinician suspects a muscle imbalance. Which muscle weakness most directly explains medial border winging in this test?", "options": {"A": "Serratus anterior, which holds the scapula flat against the thorax", "B": "Supraspinatus, which initiates abduction of the humerus at the shoulder", "C": "Rhomboids, which retract and elevate the scapula toward the spine", "D": "Upper trapezius, which elevates the scapula on the thorax"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Serratus anterior, which holds the scapula flat against the thorax): Serratus anterior protracts the scapula and pins its medial border to the rib cage, so weakness lets the border lift during push-up loading. Rhomboid weakness changes scapular retraction and can contribute to a different scapular posture, but not the classic medial winging. The upper trapezius elevates the scapula and the supraspinatus abducts the arm."}, {"id": 458, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats a patient's low back with a class 3B probe set to 150 mW, applying it at six separate points for 30 seconds per point. The chart requires the total energy delivered during the visit, summed across all points. What total energy should be recorded?", "options": {"A": "162 J, from 0.9 W of combined power for 30 s at six points", "B": "2.7 J, from 0.015 W per point for 30 s at six points", "C": "27 J, from 4.5 J at each of the six points", "D": "4.5 J, from the energy delivered to one point"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (27 J, from 4.5 J at each of the six points): Each point receives 0.15 W times 30 s, which is 4.5 J. Six points deliver 6 times 4.5 J, which totals 27 J. Reporting a single point misses the other five, counting six twice inflates the total, and a decimal slip shrinks it tenfold."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 65-year-old man two months after a bypass operation is cycling on telemetry during a monitored rehabilitation session. At 5 METs, the monitor shows a run of four consecutive wide ventricular beats at a rate of 150 before sinus rhythm returns. He feels nothing unusual and blood pressure is stable. What is the best action?", "options": {"A": "Lower the workload to 3 METs and continue while counting additional ventricular beats", "B": "Stop the session, have him sit, and notify the physician before any further exercise", "C": "Begin an extended cool-down walk and repeat the same workload at the next visit", "D": "Continue at 5 METs because he is asymptomatic and the rhythm returned to sinus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the session, have him sit, and notify the physician before any further exercise): Three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rapid rate is nonsustained ventricular tachycardia, a recognized reason to stop exercise and notify the physician. Absence of symptoms and spontaneous return to sinus rhythm do not make it safe to continue. Reducing the workload or deferring the issue risks sustained arrhythmia. Further exercise waits for medical review."}, {"id": 460, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old carpenter has lateral elbow pain that increases when he grips a hammer and extends his wrist against resistance. Palpation reproduces tenderness just distal to the lateral epicondyle. Textbooks describe one wrist tendon as the primary site of degenerative change in this condition. Which tendon is most commonly involved?", "options": {"A": "The extensor carpi radialis brevis tendon at its common extensor origin", "B": "The extensor carpi ulnaris tendon where it crosses the dorsal ulnar side of the wrist", "C": "The brachioradialis tendon at its distal insertion on the radial styloid process", "D": "The common flexor tendon at its origin on the medial epicondyle of the humerus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The extensor carpi radialis brevis tendon at its common extensor origin): Lateral epicondylitis most often involves degeneration of the extensor carpi radialis brevis origin on the lateral epicondyle. Extensor carpi ulnaris tendinopathy occurs at the ulnar wrist. The brachioradialis inserts far distally at the radial styloid, and the common flexor tendon is the medial elbow structure involved in golfer's elbow."}, {"id": 461, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 42-year-old man with S1 radiculopathy cannot rise on his right toes more than twice. When he walks, the right heel rises late and the left step is shorter than the right. He has no sensory loss. Which gait finding explains this pattern best?", "options": {"A": "Weak push-off at terminal stance shortening the opposite step", "B": "Foot slap at loading response from weak eccentric dorsiflexor control", "C": "Contralateral pelvic drop at midstance from weak gluteus medius", "D": "Backward trunk thrust at initial contact from weak gluteus maximus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weak push-off at terminal stance shortening the opposite step): The S1 root supplies gastrocnemius and soleus, which produce push-off in terminal stance and preswing. When they are weak, the heel rise is delayed, the forward drive is lost, and the opposite limb takes a shorter step. Foot slap, pelvic drop, and trunk thrust belong to other muscle groups."}, {"id": 462, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A jumper in a plyometric session lands from a box, pauses for about a second with the knees deeply bent, and then jumps. His height gain is poor compared with when he rebounds immediately. What explains the reduced jump height?", "options": {"A": "Muscle spindle activation was increased by the pause, inhibiting the concentric action", "B": "Excessive eccentric loading that damaged the muscle fibers during the landing", "C": "A long amortization phase lets the stored elastic energy dissipate as heat", "D": "Insufficient concentric strength because the muscle shortened during the pause"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A long amortization phase lets the stored elastic energy dissipate as heat): Elastic energy stored in the eccentric phase is lost as heat if the transition to the concentric phase takes too long. A very short amortization phase preserves that energy and the stretch reflex contribution. The pause does not change concentric strength, the landing does not by itself cause damage that reduces height, and spindle activity would not be inhibited."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "Students compare the pulse durations of several electrical stimulators in a lab. The high voltage pulsed current unit shows a peak voltage above 150 V, yet volunteers tolerate it comfortably at sensory intensity. The instructor asks which pulse feature, together with a low average current, accounts for that comfort. Which answer fits?", "options": {"A": "Each pulse reverses direction, so the net charge under the electrode cancels", "B": "Each peak lasts several milliseconds, so deeper fibers adapt before the pulse ends", "C": "Each peak lasts only microseconds, so the charge delivered per pulse remains small", "D": "Each peak repeats above 10,000 Hz, so skin receptors cannot detect the current"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Each peak lasts only microseconds, so the charge delivered per pulse remains small): The pulse of high voltage pulsed current is extremely short, on the order of microseconds, so each pulse delivers very little charge despite a high peak voltage. That small charge, repeated at a modest rate, gives a low average current and good comfort. The pulse is monophasic, so it does not reverse direction. Pulse rates are tens to a hundred or so per second, not above 10,000 Hz."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old rugby player is rehabilitating an anterior glenohumeral subluxation treated nonoperatively. He has full pain-free range and a negative apprehension test at rest, and he wants to add strengthening to his routine this week. Which position should the therapist avoid early in the program because it stresses the anterior capsule?", "options": {"A": "Scapular plane elevation with a light weight and the thumb pointing upward", "B": "Internal and external rotation with the elbow at the side using tubing", "C": "Prone horizontal abduction with external rotation using a light dumbbell", "D": "Combined end-range abduction and external rotation under load"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Combined end-range abduction and external rotation under load): Abduction with external rotation at end range is the position of anterior dislocation and places the greatest stress on the anterior capsule, so it is delayed. Scaption with the thumb up strengthens the cuff at much lower stress. Prone horizontal abduction and neutral rotation with tubing also load the cuff and scapular muscles without stressing the anterior restraints."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student reads the specifications for a high voltage pulsed current stimulator: voltage in the hundreds of volts, with current in milliamperes. A classmate worries such a voltage must be hazardous to skin. The instructor asks the student to restate the defining relationship between peak voltage and average current for this waveform. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Peak voltage above about 150 V, but average current stays low as pulses are brief", "B": "Peak voltage above 150 V, and average current is high because pulses are long", "C": "Peak voltage below 50 V, but average current stays low as pulses are brief", "D": "Peak voltage above 150 V, with average current high enough to cause polar skin burns"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Peak voltage above about 150 V, but average current stays low as pulses are brief): High voltage pulsed current is defined by a peak voltage above roughly 150 V. The pulses last only microseconds with long gaps between them, so average current stays low, usually well under a few milliamperes. That low average current makes the current comfortable and carries little risk of chemical skin damage. A low peak voltage does not define this waveform, and high average current describes continuous galvanic output."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A graduate student calculating energy yield for a sprint conditioning project notes that a runner's working muscle fibers draw on stored glycogen rather than blood glucose. Each glucose unit is released from glycogen by phosphorylase without spending the first ATP of the investment phase. What is the net ATP yield of anaerobic glycolysis per glycogen glucose unit?", "options": {"A": "Three net ATP per glycogen glucose unit processed anaerobically", "B": "Thirty-two net ATP per unit, assuming complete oxidation in mitochondria", "C": "Two net ATP per glycogen unit, the same as free blood glucose", "D": "Four net ATP per unit, the gross yield counting both phases"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Three net ATP per glycogen glucose unit processed anaerobically): Glycolysis from free glucose produces four gross ATP and spends two, netting two. Starting from glycogen, phosphorylase uses inorganic phosphate rather than ATP for the first step, so only one ATP is invested: 4 gross minus 1 invested equals three net ATP. The figure of four ignores the investment, and the large figure applies to full aerobic oxidation."}, {"id": 467, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In side-lying hip abduction testing, a 29-year-old runner's top thigh flexes and internally rotates as it lifts. The tensor fasciae latae contracts visibly at the front of the hip while the gluteus medius feels soft on palpation. Which interpretation and cue best correct this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Quadratus lumborum substitution, so cue a lateral trunk hike while lifting the leg", "B": "Tensor fasciae latae substitution, so cue hip extension and external rotation", "C": "Adductor substitution, so cue a squeeze of the knees before lifting the leg", "D": "Gluteus maximus overactivity, so cue added hip flexion while lifting the leg"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tensor fasciae latae substitution, so cue hip extension and external rotation): Flexion and internal rotation during abduction show the tensor fasciae latae taking over for a weak gluteus medius. Cueing slight extension with external rotation shifts the work back to the gluteus medius. A trunk hike, adductor squeeze, or more flexion do not remove the tensor substitution."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old with neck tightness performs a self-stretch for the right upper trapezius by side bending left, rotating right, and adding overpressure with the hand. After ten seconds she reports tingling down the right arm that fades when she returns to neutral. What is the best instruction?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the overpressure and hold the stretch for sixty seconds to let symptoms settle", "B": "Continue the same stretch but add a median nerve glide to desensitize the arm", "C": "Stop the stretch and examine the cervical spine and brachial plexus before repeating it", "D": "Replace stretching with a soft cervical collar for several days to settle the irritation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the stretch and examine the cervical spine and brachial plexus before repeating it): Arm tingling that appears with a neck stretch suggests neural tension or cervical involvement, so the stretch should stop and the cervical spine and brachial plexus be examined. Lowering overpressure but holding longer still provokes symptoms. Adding a nerve glide treats the symptom before the cause is found. A collar delays assessment and weakens neck muscles."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old cashier with chronic low back pain for 18 months wants a home electrical option that can be used during work. The clinician selects conventional TENS aimed at gate control for symptomatic relief. Which parameter set is most appropriate for this approach?", "options": {"A": "Frequency 80 to 100 pps, short phase duration, intensity at strong comfortable sensory tingling", "B": "Frequency 20 pps, long phase duration, intensity set below the level of perception", "C": "Frequency 100 pps, long pulses, intensity raised to a painful noxious level", "D": "Frequency 2 to 5 pps, long phase duration, intensity high enough to produce visible muscle twitching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Frequency 80 to 100 pps, short phase duration, intensity at strong comfortable sensory tingling): Conventional TENS stimulates large diameter afferents with high frequency, short pulses, and sensory-level intensity that is strong but comfortable, closing the pain gate. This allows wear during daily work. Low frequency, high intensity settings with twitching are acupuncture-like TENS. Subsensory stimulation does not engage the gate mechanism, and painful intensity belongs to brief intense TENS."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 64-year-old man with diabetic peripheral neuropathy and plantar fasciitis is referred for iontophoresis with dexamethasone to the plantar heel. Sensation in the foot is reduced to light touch and pinprick. The physician has prescribed a standard dose. Which precaution is most important for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Increase the current to overcome reduced sensation and drive the drug toward the heel.", "B": "Reduce the current and inspect the skin frequently, because he may not sense burning.", "C": "Apply the drug pad as the positive electrode, because neuropathy raises tissue acidity.", "D": "Use smaller electrodes to concentrate current, because neuropathy limits drug penetration."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce the current and inspect the skin frequently, because he may not sense burning.): Direct current can cause chemical burns under the electrodes, and a patient with impaired sensation may not report the warning sensation. Lowering current, using adequate electrode size, and checking the skin often reduce the risk. Raising current or using smaller electrodes increases current density in an insensate foot. Drug polarity is determined by the ion charge, not by neuropathy."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A student reviews soft tissue repair after a muscle strain. During the late remodeling phase, which may continue for up to a year, scar tissue gradually gains strength in response to mechanical load. Which change in collagen best describes what occurs during this phase of healing?", "options": {"A": "Fibroblast numbers increase sharply while new capillary buds form throughout the wound bed", "B": "Type III collagen is progressively replaced by stronger type I collagen aligned along lines of stress", "C": "Macrophages lay down the initial fibrin clot matrix that is later converted into elastin fibers", "D": "Type I collagen is progressively replaced by weaker type III collagen as the scar thickens"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Type III collagen is progressively replaced by stronger type I collagen aligned along lines of stress): Remodeling is marked by replacement of the early type III collagen with stronger type I collagen, and by reorientation of fibers along the lines of applied stress. Controlled loading therefore guides scar quality. The clot and macrophage events belong to inflammation, and fibroblast proliferation with capillary budding belongs to the proliferative phase."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old developer has forward head posture. Testing shows short pectoralis minor and upper trapezius, weak lower trapezius and markedly reduced deep neck flexor endurance. The clinician plans the order of the program using a Janda approach. Which sequence is best?", "options": {"A": "Start with functional lifting tasks and posture cues, delaying stretching until strength normalizes", "B": "Strengthen the weak muscles first while leaving tight muscles untouched, then add stretching later", "C": "Begin with heavy resisted shrugs and rows, then add stretching if pain persists", "D": "Release tight muscles first, then activate weak ones, then integrate into functional tasks"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Release tight muscles first, then activate weak ones, then integrate into functional tasks): The Janda sequence lengthens or inhibits overactive tight muscles first, then retrains the inhibited weak ones, then integrates the pattern into movement. Strengthening before releasing leaves the overactive antagonists dominating. Heavy shrugs overload the tight upper trapezius."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "After a 12 minute cold pack on the anterior thigh, a patient's skin looks uniformly pink to red and feels warm for several minutes. There are no welts, no pain, no blanching, and sensation is normal when tested. Which interpretation fits these findings?", "options": {"A": "Cold burn from dry toweling, requiring wound care today", "B": "Expected cutaneous vasodilation after cooling, resolving within minutes without injury", "C": "Early frostbite, requiring gentle rewarming and referral for assessment of tissue damage", "D": "Cold urticaria, requiring removal of cold from future plans"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Expected cutaneous vasodilation after cooling, resolving within minutes without injury): Uniform redness and warmth that fade within minutes, with normal sensation and no welts, reflects the usual vasodilation after cooling. Frostbite appears white or waxy with sensory loss. Urticaria forms itchy raised welts, and a burn would show injury rather than uniform transient color."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 54-year-old has a clean stage two leg ulcer treated with high voltage pulsed current at 10 pps. Each pulse causes a painful calf twitch, and the patient stops the session. The chiropractor wants to continue wound stimulation. Which change fits a typical wound protocol?", "options": {"A": "Lower the pulse rate to 1 pps and keep the same intensity", "B": "Raise the rate to about 100 pps with intensity below the motor threshold", "C": "Raise the pulse rate to 100 pps and set intensity above the motor threshold", "D": "Keep 10 pps and reduce intensity until the twitch is barely visible"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raise the rate to about 100 pps with intensity below the motor threshold): Wound protocols commonly use a pulse rate near 100 pps at an intensity below the motor threshold, which gives stimulation without painful contractions. A low rate such as 10 pps produces individual twitches, and 1 pps produces even more distinct ones. Staying above the motor threshold at 100 pps would cause tetany. Raising the rate and lowering intensity addresses the problem directly."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 27-year-old distance runner increased weekly mileage by 40 percent over 8 weeks. She now has a resting heart rate 12 beats above her usual, poor sleep, low mood and slower race times despite 2 weeks of lighter training. Blood tests are normal. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continue current mileage but add a carbohydrate supplement after each run to restore glycogen", "B": "Add interval sessions twice weekly to restore the training stimulus and break the plateau", "C": "Extended relative rest with sharply reduced volume and intensity until performance and mood recover", "D": "Take 3 days off and then resume the previous peak weekly mileage immediately"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Extended relative rest with sharply reduced volume and intensity until performance and mood recover): Persistent performance decline, raised resting heart rate, sleep and mood disturbance that continue after 2 weeks of lighter training point to overtraining syndrome rather than ordinary fatigue. Recovery needs prolonged relative rest with markedly lower volume and intensity, often weeks. More intervals add stress, a carbohydrate supplement does not fix the syndrome, and a 3 day break is too short."}, {"id": 476, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old patient performs a prone plank on the elbows and toes. Photographs show the lumbar spine sagging into extension and the pelvis dropping below the shoulder line after twenty seconds. The therapist wants a progression decision that fits this finding. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Add a weight plate on the back to improve trunk strength", "B": "Continue the plank to fatigue so the abdominals adapt to the sagging position", "C": "Regress to an incline plank with the hands on a bench, keeping the trunk straight", "D": "Progress to a plank with the feet on a Swiss ball to improve endurance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Regress to an incline plank with the hands on a bench, keeping the trunk straight): A lumbar sag shows the trunk cannot resist extension at this lever length, so load should be reduced with an incline plank where the hands are on a bench. Adding weight or placing the feet on a ball raises the demand and worsens the fault. Continuing to fatigue rehearses the faulty pattern and builds compensation. Control comes before load."}, {"id": 477, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "During a routine output check using a power meter, a unit set to 5 watts reads only 3 watts. The clinic's next patient needs treatment for a chronic tendon problem and is waiting in the treatment room. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Continue using the unit, because a patient report of warmth confirms adequate dose", "B": "Raise the displayed setting to 8 watts to compensate and treat the patient", "C": "Treat the patient now and schedule calibration at the next routine annual service", "D": "Remove the unit from service for calibration, because the actual output is below the displayed setting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove the unit from service for calibration, because the actual output is below the displayed setting): A unit delivering 3 watts at a 5 watt setting is out of calibration and must leave service until it is calibrated. Patient warmth is not a reliable dose measure. Raising the setting guesses at an unverified output, and treating now with calibration deferred still uses a known faulty device."}, {"id": 478, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 58 year old woman has greater trochanteric pain syndrome with tenderness over a bursa lying about 3 to 4 cm beneath the skin of the lateral hip. The chiropractor owns several class 3B probes of different wavelengths. Which probe is the best choice to reach this target depth?", "options": {"A": "A 660 nm visible red probe applied perpendicular to the lateral hip skin", "B": "A 405 nm visible violet probe applied perpendicular to the lateral hip skin", "C": "An 810 nm near-infrared probe applied perpendicular to the lateral hip skin", "D": "A 532 nm visible green probe applied perpendicular to the lateral hip skin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An 810 nm near-infrared probe applied perpendicular to the lateral hip skin): A target 3 to 4 cm deep needs a wavelength inside the optical window where absorption is lowest, which favors near-infrared such as 810 nm. Red at 660 nm is absorbed more in superficial layers and suits wounds. Green and violet light are absorbed strongly by hemoglobin and melanin and barely get past the skin."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 41-year-old man completes 12 weeks of progressive aerobic training for a half marathon, and a repeat vastus lateralis biopsy is compared with baseline. His physician asks about the expected change among his fast twitch fibers. Which finding is most consistent with endurance training?", "options": {"A": "Conversion of Type I fibers into Type IIa fibers to increase peak contraction velocity", "B": "A shift of Type IIx fibers toward Type IIa fibers with greater mitochondrial content", "C": "Loss of capillary density around Type I fibers as aerobic work lowers oxygen demand", "D": "A shift of Type IIa fibers toward Type IIx fibers with greater glycolytic enzyme content"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A shift of Type IIx fibers toward Type IIa fibers with greater mitochondrial content): Endurance training increases mitochondria, capillaries and oxidative enzymes, and the fast fiber population shifts from the most glycolytic Type IIx toward the more oxidative Type IIa. The reverse shift is seen with detraining. Slow fibers do not convert into fast fibers with training, and capillary density increases rather than falls."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 44-year-old man has jogged 45 minutes at 65 percent of heart rate reserve three days weekly for four months, and his five-kilometer time has stopped improving. A new work schedule now allows only three 30-minute sessions per week. Which change best restores a training stimulus within these limits?", "options": {"A": "Replace one session with a long slow walk at 40 percent of reserve for recovery", "B": "Keep the same pace but split each session into two 15-minute bouts with a break", "C": "Convert one session to intervals, alternating hard efforts with easy recovery periods", "D": "Swap one session for steady cycling at 65 percent of reserve to reduce impact"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Convert one session to intervals, alternating hard efforts with easy recovery periods): A plateau after four months at the same intensity means the body has adapted, so the stimulus needs a new challenge. With only 30 minutes available, intervals raise intensity within the time limit. Lower intensity, split bouts or a change of mode at the same pace do not add overload. Those choices would likely maintain or reduce fitness rather than improve the five-kilometer time."}, {"id": 481, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 48-year-old painter has deep pain at the front of the shoulder, has trouble reaching overhead and cannot sleep on that side. Passive glenohumeral motion is full and painless. Firm compression of a taut band below the scapular spine reproduces the anterior pain. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Infraspinatus trigger point referring pain to the anterior shoulder", "B": "Glenohumeral osteoarthritis, since the pain is deep and worse at night", "C": "Bicipital tendinopathy, since pain is anterior and worse on overhead reaching", "D": "Adhesive capsulitis, since overhead motion is difficult and sleep is disturbed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Infraspinatus trigger point referring pain to the anterior shoulder): Reproduction of anterior shoulder pain from a posterior infraspinatus band, with full painless passive motion, indicates trigger point referral. Joint disease and adhesive capsulitis limit passive motion, and bicipital tendinopathy would be tender over the bicipital groove rather than reproduced from the infraspinatus. Palpating the muscle and finding the familiar pain is what separates referral from joint disease."}, {"id": 482, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A patient walks 10 meters in 8 seconds and the therapist counts a cadence of 100 steps per minute. The therapist wants the average step length for the chart, not the stride length. Using speed and cadence, what is the average step length for this patient?", "options": {"A": "0.375 meters, found by halving the step length twice", "B": "0.75 meters, from 75 meters per minute over 100 steps", "C": "1.50 meters, which is the stride length of two steps", "D": "1.25 meters, the same number as the gait speed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.75 meters, from 75 meters per minute over 100 steps): Speed is 10 meters over 8 seconds, which is 1.25 meters per second, or 75 meters per minute. Dividing 75 by a cadence of 100 steps per minute gives 0.75 meters per step, checked by 0.75 times 100 equals 75. The stride length would be twice that, 1.50 meters."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 57-year-old with 20 years of rheumatoid arthritis has neck pain, occipital headache, a clunk with neck flexion and new hand numbness. She wears no collar and was told by her rheumatologist to avoid heavy lifting. She asks for her usual neck traction session. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Withhold traction and manipulation, and refer for flexion-extension imaging to assess atlantoaxial instability", "B": "Apply moist heat and teach gentle end-range neck stretching into flexion", "C": "Proceed with gentle cervical traction at low force and monitor symptoms", "D": "Start deep neck flexor training supine with a pressure biofeedback unit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold traction and manipulation, and refer for flexion-extension imaging to assess atlantoaxial instability): Long-standing rheumatoid arthritis can erode the transverse ligament and dens, producing atlantoaxial instability with clunking and neurological symptoms. Traction and manipulation risk cord injury, so they are withheld pending flexion-extension imaging and specialist review. Gentle traction still pulls on lax upper cervical ligaments. Exercise or end-range flexion stretching would still stress the upper cervical segments."}, {"id": 484, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A therapist sets an interferential unit to a 10,000 Hz carrier instead of the usual 4000 Hz, hoping to improve comfort in a sensitive patient. The patient then reports little sensation at an amplitude that normally gives clear tingling. What best accounts for the weak sensation at the higher carrier?", "options": {"A": "Higher carriers are absorbed by skin, so less current reaches the nerves", "B": "Higher carriers raise skin impedance, so the delivered amplitude drops", "C": "Higher carriers cancel the beat frequency, so no amplitude modulation occurs", "D": "Shorter phase duration is less effective at depolarizing nerves, so more amplitude is required"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shorter phase duration is less effective at depolarizing nerves, so more amplitude is required): At 10,000 Hz each phase is very short, and short phases are less efficient at depolarizing nerve membranes, so the patient needs more amplitude to feel the same effect. Skin impedance actually falls as frequency rises, so impedance does not explain the weak sensation. The beat frequency still depends on the difference between carriers, not on their absolute value."}, {"id": 485, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A physician prescribes iontophoresis with a total dose of 80 milliampere-minutes for a patient with plantar fasciitis. The clinician sets the current to 4 mA, which the patient tolerates comfortably under the electrode. The clinician must program the treatment timer. For how long must the current run to deliver the prescribed dose?", "options": {"A": "Run the current for 20 minutes at 4 mA.", "B": "Run the current for 40 minutes at 4 mA.", "C": "Run the current for 76 minutes at 4 mA.", "D": "Run the current for 320 minutes at 4 mA."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Run the current for 20 minutes at 4 mA.): Dose equals current times time, so time equals dose divided by current. Dividing 80 milliampere-minutes by 4 milliamperes gives 20 minutes. Dividing the dose by two ignores the 4 milliampere setting, subtracting 4 from 80 gives 76 with no basis in the formula, and multiplying 80 by 4 gives 320, which inverts the relationship."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with weakness after a nerve injury lies supine with the arm supported on a powdered board for testing shoulder abduction. She completes the full range of motion with gravity eliminated but cannot lift her arm against gravity when tested sitting. What manual muscle test grade is recorded?", "options": {"A": "Grade 1, which is a visible or palpable contraction without joint motion", "B": "Grade 3, which is full motion against gravity without resistance", "C": "Grade 2, which is full motion with gravity eliminated", "D": "Grade 0, which is complete absence of any palpable muscle contraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade 2, which is full motion with gravity eliminated): Completing the full range in a gravity-eliminated position but not against gravity is grade 2 (poor). Grade 3 requires the full range against gravity, which she cannot do. Grade 1 means a contraction without motion, and she moves the entire range. Grade 0 means no contraction at all."}, {"id": 487, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 63-year-old woman is ten days after coronary bypass surgery and arrives for her first outpatient rehabilitation visit. She feels a clicking in her sternum, the lower incision has yellow drainage, and her temperature is 100.8 degrees F. Her walking tolerance has been good. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Withhold today's session and contact the surgeon the same day to evaluate the wound", "B": "Proceed with walking and add sternal splinting with a pillow during coughing and transfers", "C": "Proceed with lower-body exercise only and recheck the wound at the next visit", "D": "Apply a moist heat pack over the sternum to relieve the clicking and soften drainage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold today's session and contact the surgeon the same day to evaluate the wound): Sternal instability, purulent drainage, and fever after sternotomy suggest deep sternal wound infection, which can progress to mediastinitis. These findings need the surgeon's evaluation promptly, so exercise is held. Splinting and lower-body work address routine precautions, not an infection. Heat can worsen infection and does not stabilize the sternum."}, {"id": 488, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 30-year-old beginner lifter trains his chest once a week and asks whether he could stimulate growth more often. His physiotherapist notes that muscle protein synthesis after a hard resistance session stays elevated for a limited time. How long does the elevation typically persist in a beginner after a hard session?", "options": {"A": "About 10 to 12 hours, which fits one overnight rest period after training", "B": "About 7 to 10 days, matching the time needed for new contractile protein turnover", "C": "About 4 to 5 days, matching the full period of delayed soreness", "D": "About 24 to 48 hours after the session, then returning toward baseline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 24 to 48 hours after the session, then returning toward baseline): After a hard session, muscle protein synthesis rises and typically stays elevated for roughly 24 to 48 hours before returning toward baseline. Ten to twelve hours is too short, while four to five days or a week or more overstates the window and mistakes soreness duration or fiber turnover for the synthesis response."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist stretches a patient's tight hamstrings using a PNF agonist contraction technique. At the end of the available range, the patient actively contracts the quadriceps while the therapist maintains the position. A student asks which neurophysiological mechanism is intended to allow the hamstrings to relax further. What is it?", "options": {"A": "Stretch reflex facilitation, where muscle spindles in the quadriceps excite the hamstring motor neurons", "B": "Reciprocal inhibition, where quadriceps activity reduces motor neuron drive to the hamstrings", "C": "Viscoelastic creep, where the hamstring tissue lengthens under the sustained force of quadriceps contraction", "D": "Autogenic inhibition, where hamstring tension activates the Golgi tendon organs and relaxes the muscle"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reciprocal inhibition, where quadriceps activity reduces motor neuron drive to the hamstrings): When the agonist, here the quadriceps, contracts, spinal circuits reduce activation of the antagonist hamstrings, which is reciprocal inhibition. This lets the hamstrings relax and permits a greater passive stretch. Autogenic inhibition is the proposed mechanism of hold-relax and contract-relax, where the stretched muscle itself contracts. Spindle excitation of the quadriceps does not facilitate the hamstrings, and creep is a mechanical property, not a neural mechanism."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student treats the lateral aspect of the elbow using gel and tilts the sound head so its edge contacts the skin and the face is angled away. The patient's skin is curved and the unit shows a high reflected-energy reading. What correction is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Press the edge firmly into the skin so the beam reaches deeper tissue below the curve", "B": "Switch to pulsed mode, which lowers reflected energy by shortening each burst of sound", "C": "Keep the face tilted, because angling spreads the beam over a larger area", "D": "Keep the face flat against the surface, perpendicular to the skin, with ample gel"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the face flat against the surface, perpendicular to the skin, with ample gel): Sound reflects when it meets skin at an angle or across an air gap, so the face should lie flat and perpendicular with ample gel. Pulsed mode changes the duty cycle but not coupling, so reflection remains. Tilting does not enlarge the field, and pressing an edge in does not drive energy deeper. If flat contact is impossible on a curve, a water path or smaller head is the alternative."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old cyclist is 8 weeks after a grade II hamstring strain. Pain is minimal, range of motion is full, and strength is 4 out of 5. Which type of strengthening is most appropriate in this remodeling phase of tissue healing to restore load tolerance and prepare for sprinting?", "options": {"A": "Active range of motion without added load until strength reaches five out of five", "B": "Gentle passive stretching and ice, with strengthening delayed until twelve weeks", "C": "Submaximal isometric contractions in the shortened position to protect the healing muscle tissue", "D": "Progressive heavy resistance including eccentric hamstring loading, advancing toward sprint specific work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive heavy resistance including eccentric hamstring loading, advancing toward sprint specific work): In the remodeling phase the tissue needs progressive loading to regain strength and tolerate sport demands, so heavy resistance with eccentric emphasis and then sprint-specific work is appropriate. Submaximal isometrics and unloaded motion belong to early phases. Delaying strengthening to twelve weeks with passive stretching leaves the muscle underloaded."}, {"id": 492, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A therapist performs ice massage over a patient's lateral epicondyle using a frozen paper cup, and warns the patient what to expect. Textbooks describe a predictable sequence of sensations as the skin cools during the treatment. Which sequence should the therapist describe so that the patient can recognize when analgesia has been reached?", "options": {"A": "Numbness, then cold, then burning, then a deep aching", "B": "Burning, then numbness, then aching, then a cold feeling", "C": "Cold first, then burning, then aching, and finally numbness", "D": "Aching, then tingling, then cold, then a pressure sensation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cold first, then burning, then aching, and finally numbness): The usual progression is cold, then burning, then aching, and finally numbness (analgesia). Numbness marks the endpoint, so treatment is stopped there. Any sequence that starts with numbness reverses the physiology."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old teaches herself a runner's wall stretch for tight gastrocnemius. As she leans forward, the back heel lifts slightly and the foot turns outward while the arch collapses. She feels the stretch mainly in the arch rather than the upper calf. How should she correct the stretch?", "options": {"A": "Lift the heel slightly and push through the forefoot to increase the calf stretch", "B": "Allow the foot to roll inward so the arch stretches along with the calf", "C": "Bend the back knee and turn the toes outward to stretch the soleus more", "D": "Keep the heel down with the toes pointing straight ahead and the arch supported"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the heel down with the toes pointing straight ahead and the arch supported): Pronation and a lifted heel let the stretch escape to the arch, so the correction keeps the heel down, toes straight ahead and arch supported with the knee extended. Bending the knee shifts the stretch to the soleus and the turned-out foot keeps the fault. Lifting the heel removes the load. Rolling inward reinforces the compensation."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient is 4 weeks after an anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction with a patellar tendon graft. The therapist wants quadriceps strengthening that limits anterior tibial translation and stress on the graft. Which exercise is most appropriate for this early stage?", "options": {"A": "Closed chain mini squats within a limited range of knee flexion", "B": "Isokinetic knee extension at slow speed from 90 degrees to full extension", "C": "Open chain resisted knee extension from 45 degrees of flexion to full extension", "D": "Plyometric box jumps with single-leg landings on a firm surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Closed chain mini squats within a limited range of knee flexion): Closed chain squats in a limited range produce co-contraction of the hamstrings and quadriceps and limit anterior tibial shear, so they are favored early. Open chain resisted extension in the last 45 degrees increases anterior tibial translation and loads the graft. Plyometric jumping is a late-stage activity and is not appropriate at four weeks."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old office worker with low back pain shows increased lumbar lordosis, an anterior pelvic tilt, tight hip flexors, and weak abdominals and gluteals in standing posture assessment. The clinician diagnoses a lower crossed pattern. Which pair of interventions best addresses the muscle imbalance?", "options": {"A": "Stretch the iliopsoas and rectus femoris, and strengthen the abdominals and gluteus maximus", "B": "Stretch the erector spinae and hamstrings, and strengthen the iliopsoas and rectus femoris", "C": "Strengthen the erector spinae and tensor fasciae latae, and stretch the gluteus medius", "D": "Stretch the abdominals and gluteus maximus, and strengthen the iliopsoas and erector spinae"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stretch the iliopsoas and rectus femoris, and strengthen the abdominals and gluteus maximus): Janda's lower crossed pattern pairs tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors with weak abdominals and gluteals, which tilts the pelvis forward. Treatment lengthens the tight hip flexors and strengthens the inhibited abdominals and gluteus maximus. Stretching weak muscles or strengthening tight ones would worsen the imbalance."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old man had sudden severe vertigo, vomiting, and horizontal nystagmus without hearing loss, diagnosed as vestibular neuritis 36 hours ago, with imaging excluding stroke. He is now able to sit and walk with assistance. Which approach is best for rehabilitation at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Avoiding head movement exercises for six weeks to protect the labyrinth", "B": "Repeated Epley maneuvers each session to clear canal debris", "C": "Vestibular suppressant medication continued for several weeks to control the dizziness", "D": "Early gradual mobilization with walking and gaze exercises"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Early gradual mobilization with walking and gaze exercises): Vestibular compensation depends on movement, so after the acute phase of vestibular neuritis early mobilization and gaze stabilization exercises speed recovery. Avoiding head movement delays compensation. The Epley maneuver treats displaced canal debris, which is not the problem in neuritis. Suppressant drugs help the first days but prolonged use slows compensation."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 55-year-old man has a drooping right shoulder after neck surgery. He cannot shrug well, has difficulty abducting above 90 degrees, and the scapula sits low and shifted laterally with winging that is worse in abduction. A wall push-up looks normal. What is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Strengthen the serratus anterior with push-up plus exercises for long thoracic nerve winging", "B": "Refer for neurological evaluation of suspected spinal accessory nerve injury", "C": "Stretch the pectoralis minor and begin a posture program for scapular anterior tilt", "D": "Mobilize the glenohumeral joint inferiorly to correct a presumed capsular restriction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for neurological evaluation of suspected spinal accessory nerve injury): Shoulder droop, weak shrug, lateral scapular displacement, and winging worse in abduction fit trapezius weakness from spinal accessory injury, especially after neck surgery. The normal wall push-up argues against serratus palsy. A neurological evaluation is needed, and posture and capsular approaches do not address the deficit."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician applies two electrodes to a patient's forearm for a pulsed stimulation treatment. One electrode measures 2 square centimeters and the other measures 20 square centimeters, and both carry the same total current. The patient reports that the stimulation feels much stronger under one pad. Which electrode carries the higher current density?", "options": {"A": "The larger electrode carries the higher current density and produces the stronger stimulation.", "B": "The smaller electrode carries the higher current density and produces the stronger stimulation.", "C": "Both carry equal density because the same total current flows through each electrode.", "D": "The electrode nearer the muscle belly carries the higher density and produces the stronger stimulation."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The smaller electrode carries the higher current density and produces the stronger stimulation.): Current density is total current divided by electrode area, so the same current spread over 2 square centimeters is ten times denser than over 20. The smaller electrode therefore feels stronger and is used as the active electrode. Equal total current does not mean equal density. Proximity to the muscle belly changes which tissue is reached, not the density at the skin."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old programmer has a tight right upper trapezius with neck pain. The upper trapezius extends and side bends the neck to the same side while rotating it toward the opposite side. A student is asked to position the neck to place the right upper trapezius on stretch. Which position is best?", "options": {"A": "Neck flexed, side bent to the left, and rotated to the right", "B": "Neck extended, side bent to the right, and rotated to the left", "C": "Neck flexed, side bent to the left, and rotated to the left", "D": "Neck extended, side bent to the left, and rotated to the right"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neck flexed, side bent to the left, and rotated to the right): To stretch a muscle, the position must oppose all of its actions. The right upper trapezius extends, side bends right, and rotates left, so stretch is created by flexion, side bending left, and rotation right. Positions that extend the neck or bend toward the tight side bring the attachments closer together. Rotating left while bending left leaves rotation in the muscle's own direction and reduces the stretch."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 20-year-old 400 m runner slows markedly over the final 100 m and reports burning, heavy legs. Blood lactate drawn right after the race is 14 mmol/L, and the race lasted about 55 seconds, far longer than phosphocreatine alone can sustain. Which mechanism best explains the fatigue in this effort?", "options": {"A": "Oxygen debt from the first 300 m prevents mitochondria from resynthesizing ATP until the race ends", "B": "Lactate molecules directly poison the contractile proteins and block calcium release", "C": "Falling muscle pH from hydrogen ion accumulation impairs glycolytic enzymes and cross-bridges", "D": "Phosphocreatine is exhausted and no other system can supply ATP in this effort"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Falling muscle pH from hydrogen ion accumulation impairs glycolytic enzymes and cross-bridges): Rapid glycolysis releases hydrogen ions along with lactate, lowering muscle pH, which inhibits phosphofructokinase and interferes with calcium handling and cross-bridge cycling. Lactate itself is not the toxic agent. Phosphocreatine is depleted early but glycolysis supplies ATP in this effort, and mitochondria keep producing ATP during the race."}, {"id": 501, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 21-year-old dancer has a grade II lateral ankle sprain three days old, with moderate swelling, bruising, and tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament. She can bear weight with a limp. Which approach is supported by evidence for managing her during the first weeks?", "options": {"A": "Early protected weight bearing with a brace and pain free motion", "B": "A rigid cast with no weight bearing for six weeks", "C": "Aggressive stretching of the ligament into inversion to prevent laxity", "D": "Rest with no ankle movement until swelling has resolved"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early protected weight bearing with a brace and pain free motion): Functional treatment with a brace or semirigid support, early protected weight bearing, and pain free motion gives faster return to activity than prolonged immobilization. Long cast immobilization and delayed motion lead to stiffness and weakness. Stretching an injured lateral ligament into inversion works against healing and adds instability."}, {"id": 502, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A patient with hemiparesis reaches for a cup and can see whether she grasped it, but she uses excessive trunk lean to do so. The therapist says, 'Your trunk moved forward about 10 degrees, try to keep it upright.' What type of augmented feedback did the therapist give, and why is it suited here?", "options": {"A": "Intrinsic feedback, since the information comes from the patient's own sensory systems during reaching", "B": "Knowledge of performance, since the patient already knows the outcome but not the movement pattern", "C": "Knowledge of results, since the therapist is reporting whether the reach achieved its goal", "D": "Knowledge of results, since trunk angle is a measurable outcome of the finished movement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Knowledge of performance, since the patient already knows the outcome but not the movement pattern): Knowledge of performance describes the movement pattern itself, such as trunk lean. It suits this patient because she can already see the outcome, a grasped cup, so outcome feedback adds little. Knowledge of results reports goal achievement. Intrinsic feedback comes from the patient's own senses, whereas the therapist's statement is augmented feedback."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 34-year-old delivery driver with a mild cervical strain after a low speed rear-end collision is given a soft foam cervical collar to wear for several days. He asks whether it will hold his neck still. Which statement best describes what a soft foam collar actually accomplishes?", "options": {"A": "It unloads the cervical discs by transferring the weight of the head to the shoulders", "B": "It limits flexion and extension about as much as a Philadelphia collar does", "C": "It restricts motion very little, serving mainly as a warmth and reminder cue", "D": "It restricts the upper cervical spine enough to protect an unstable odontoid fracture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It restricts motion very little, serving mainly as a warmth and reminder cue): Studies of cervical orthoses show a soft foam collar permits most of the normal range of motion, so its main value is warmth, comfort, and a reminder to move less. A Philadelphia collar restricts flexion and extension noticeably more. A soft collar does not meaningfully unload the discs, and it cannot protect an unstable fracture, which needs a rigid device."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 27-year-old woman training for a half marathon has a maximal oxygen uptake well below expected for her mileage. Laboratory work shows hemoglobin of 8.6 g/dL from iron deficiency, with normal heart function and normal lung function. Which Fick equation component is most directly reduced by her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Muscle capillary density, because hemoglobin concentration governs capillary growth", "B": "The arteriovenous oxygen difference, because arterial oxygen content is lowered", "C": "Maximal stroke volume, because low red cell mass reduces ventricular contractility", "D": "Maximal heart rate, because anemia lowers the sinoatrial node firing ceiling"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The arteriovenous oxygen difference, because arterial oxygen content is lowered): Hemoglobin carries most of the oxygen in blood, so low hemoglobin reduces arterial oxygen content and limits how much oxygen the muscles can extract, which narrows the arteriovenous difference. Her heart and lungs are normal, so stroke volume and maximal heart rate are not primarily affected. Capillary density is a structural training adaptation and does not respond to hemoglobin level in this way."}, {"id": 505, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student sets a pulsed ultrasound unit so that sound is emitted for 2 milliseconds, followed by 8 milliseconds of silence, repeating throughout the treatment. The student must record the duty cycle on the treatment note. Which duty cycle describes this pulse pattern and how is it calculated?", "options": {"A": "80 percent, calculated as off-time divided by the full pulse period", "B": "20 percent, calculated as on-time divided by the sum of on-time and off-time", "C": "50 percent, because the unit alternates evenly between sound on and sound off", "D": "25 percent, calculated as on-time divided by off-time alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20 percent, calculated as on-time divided by the sum of on-time and off-time): Duty cycle is the percentage of the pulse period during which sound is emitted. Here 2 ms divided by the 10 ms total (2 on plus 8 off) gives 0.20, or 20 percent. Dividing by the off-time alone gives 25 percent, and using the silent portion gives 80 percent."}, {"id": 506, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist wants to challenge the vestibular system specifically in a 66-year-old patient recovering from vestibular neuritis. The patient is safe in standing with a gait belt and a countertop nearby. Which standing condition best increases the reliance on vestibular input?", "options": {"A": "Standing on firm floor with eyes open while wearing usual glasses", "B": "Standing on a foam pad with eyes open, fixating a wall target", "C": "Standing on a thick foam pad with eyes closed and head still", "D": "Standing on firm floor with eyes closed and arms folded across the chest"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Standing on a thick foam pad with eyes closed and head still): Foam degrades ankle and foot input and closing the eyes removes vision, so the vestibular system becomes the main reliable source. On a firm floor with eyes closed the somatosensory system still supplies accurate information. Eyes-open conditions leave vision available to compensate."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient has chronic Achilles tendinopathy with the tendon about 1 cm below the skin. After a 5-minute course at 1 MHz and 1.0 W/cm2 continuous, no warmth is reported. The goal is thermal. What is the most likely explanation and fix?", "options": {"A": "The frequency is too high for the target, so lower it to 0.5 MHz instead", "B": "The 1 MHz beam heats superficial tissue slowly, so change to 3 MHz", "C": "The gel layer was too thick, so the gel should be removed to improve transmission", "D": "The tendon is too dense to absorb ultrasound, so a different modality should be used"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The 1 MHz beam heats superficial tissue slowly, so change to 3 MHz): At 1.0 W/cm2, 3 MHz heats superficial tissue about three times faster than 1 MHz, so a shallow tendon warms slowly with 1 MHz. Collagen absorbs ultrasound readily, and gel is needed for coupling. Therapeutic units offer 1 and 3 MHz, not 0.5 MHz."}, {"id": 508, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old carpenter has a chronic tendon problem at the elbow and the therapist wants to use deep friction massage to limit adhesions in the healing collagen. The student asks how the friction stroke should run relative to the tendon fibers and what outcome is intended. What is the correct description?", "options": {"A": "Across the fibers, to mobilize scar and keep collagen mobile", "B": "Parallel to the fibers, to reduce adhesions along the length of the tendon", "C": "Along the fibers with deep pressure, to stretch and lengthen the shortened tendon", "D": "Across the fibers at high speed, to deliberately stimulate muscle spindles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Across the fibers, to mobilize scar and keep collagen mobile): Deep transverse friction runs across the fibers to mobilize scar and keep healing collagen from adhering. A longitudinal stroke glides along the fibers and does not break cross links between them. Deep lengthening along the fibers is a stretching goal, not friction. Rapid strokes aimed at muscle spindles belong to tone techniques, not tendon friction."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 17-year-old basketball player has pain at the inferior pole of the patella with jumping, diagnosed as patellar tendinopathy. The therapist suggests an infrapatellar strap for use during play. What is the commonly proposed mechanism by which the strap may reduce his pain?", "options": {"A": "It alters force transmission through the tendon, offloading the painful insertion", "B": "It holds the patella laterally so the patellofemoral joint cannot subluxate", "C": "It blocks anterior tibial translation so the cruciate ligaments carry less load during landing", "D": "It limits terminal knee extension so the quadriceps contracts less"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It alters force transmission through the tendon, offloading the painful insertion): An infrapatellar strap compresses the tendon below the patella, which is thought to change how force is transmitted and reduce stress at the painful inferior pole. It does not hold the patella laterally, which is the job of a patellar stabilizing brace. It does not limit terminal extension, and it does not control tibial translation, which is the function of a functional ACL brace."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 45-year-old marathon runner is cruising at roughly 75 percent of VO2max and feels his legs become heavy and his pace collapse near mile 20, about two and a half hours into the race. He took no fuel during the race. Which fatigue mechanism most likely explains this late-race slowing?", "options": {"A": "Build-up of lactic acid to levels that block cross-bridge cycling", "B": "Depletion of muscle and liver glycogen stores limiting carbohydrate oxidation", "C": "Exhaustion of phosphocreatine stores in the active leg muscles", "D": "Failure of neuromuscular transmission from loss of acetylcholine at the junction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Depletion of muscle and liver glycogen stores limiting carbohydrate oxidation): At about 75 percent of VO2max, glycogen is a major fuel, and stores typically run low after roughly 90 to 150 minutes, so carbohydrate oxidation falls and pace collapses. Phosphocreatine is depleted within the first minute of hard effort and recovers quickly. Lactate remains steady at a sustainable pace, and acetylcholine supply does not fail in this setting."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "Four patients begin UVB therapy for psoriasis on the same day. The therapist reviews Fitzpatrick skin types to anticipate how easily each patient will burn before any test dosing is done. Based on skin type alone, which patient is expected to have the lowest MED and the greatest risk of burning?", "options": {"A": "A patient with type V skin who rarely burns and tans darkly", "B": "A patient with type I skin who burns easily and does not tan", "C": "A patient with type II skin who burns easily and tans minimally", "D": "A patient with type III skin who sometimes burns mildly and tans gradually"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A patient with type I skin who burns easily and does not tan): Type I skin has little melanin, burns readily and does not tan, so it has the lowest MED. Type II also burns easily but tans minimally, so its MED is slightly higher. Type III tolerates more exposure before burning, and type V has more protective pigmentation and a much higher MED. Skin type only predicts the trend, and an individual MED test still sets the dose."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 57-year-old woman six weeks after a myocardial infarction has an ejection fraction of 44 percent, a symptom-limited capacity of 6 METs, no complex arrhythmias, and 1.5 mm of exercise ST depression without symptoms. Using standard cardiac rehabilitation risk stratification, which category fits her and what supervision follows?", "options": {"A": "Low risk, with no monitoring needed because her capacity exceeds 5 METs", "B": "Low risk, because she has no symptoms despite the ST depression noted on testing", "C": "High risk, with continuous ECG monitoring and physician presence during every session", "D": "Moderate risk, supported by ECG monitoring for a limited number of early sessions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moderate risk, supported by ECG monitoring for a limited number of early sessions): An ejection fraction of 40 to 49 percent, a capacity between 5 and 7 METs, and exercise ST depression under 2 mm are moderate-risk features. Moderate risk usually warrants ECG monitoring for a limited early period. Low risk requires an ejection fraction of 50 percent or more, capacity above 7 METs, and no ischemia. Her findings do not meet high-risk criteria such as an ejection fraction below 40 percent or ST depression of 2 mm or more."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with extensive psoriasis took oral psoralen 2 hours before her UVA session and finished treatment at 1 pm. She plans to walk 20 blocks home in bright afternoon sun. She has her own sunscreen but no sunglasses. Which discharge instruction is best?", "options": {"A": "Wear UV-blocking sunglasses and cover exposed skin until the drug effect clears", "B": "Apply sunscreen and go outdoors since the session has already ended", "C": "Wear dark sunglasses if the sky is clear and skip skin coverage", "D": "Skip protection because the clinic dose was controlled and measured"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wear UV-blocking sunglasses and cover exposed skin until the drug effect clears): Oral psoralen leaves the skin and the lens of the eye sensitive to ultraviolet radiation for many hours after the session. She needs UV-blocking sunglasses and clothing that covers exposed skin until the effect fades. Sunscreen alone is not adequate protection. The controlled clinic dose does not shorten the period of sensitivity."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 25 year old volleyball player has a fresh moderate ankle sprain with marked swelling, and the clinic offers either a cold whirlpool with the leg hanging or an ice pack. The therapist wants to avoid worsening edema. Which choice best limits swelling?", "options": {"A": "Cold whirlpool at 55 degrees F with the leg hanging down for 20 minutes", "B": "Moist heat in elevation to speed removal of fluid from the joint", "C": "Contrast bath to alternately open and close vessels around the joint", "D": "Cold pack with compression and elevation, as dependent immersion can raise swelling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cold pack with compression and elevation, as dependent immersion can raise swelling): In a fresh sprain, cold with compression and elevation limits swelling. A hanging leg in a whirlpool lets gravity pull fluid into the ankle, which can raise edema despite the cold. A contrast bath and moist heat both include warming that increases blood flow and swelling."}, {"id": 515, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A first-year chiropractic student reviewing exercise biochemistry is asked how many net ATP molecules anaerobic glycolysis produces from one molecule of blood glucose, ending in two pyruvate. Her notes list a gross production of four ATP and an investment phase that consumes some ATP first. What is the net yield per glucose molecule?", "options": {"A": "Two net ATP per glucose molecule, with no oxygen required for this pathway", "B": "Thirty-eight net ATP per glucose molecule, the older textbook figure for complete aerobic oxidation", "C": "Four net ATP per glucose molecule, counting only the phase that produces ATP directly", "D": "Three net ATP per glucose molecule, when glycogen rather than glucose is the starting substrate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two net ATP per glucose molecule, with no oxygen required for this pathway): Anaerobic glycolysis produces four ATP gross and spends two in the investment phase, giving a net of two per glucose without oxygen. The older figure of 38 belongs to complete aerobic oxidation. Four ignores the investment cost, and three is the true net only when glycogen supplies the glucose unit."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist gently nudges a standing 25-year-old client at the sternum with just enough force to sway the body forward a few degrees. The client stays on a firm floor with both feet flat and recovers using muscles of the lower leg. Which balance strategy is being used, and when is it normally the dominant strategy?", "options": {"A": "Stepping strategy, dominant for small slow sway on a firm surface", "B": "Hip strategy, dominant for small slow sway on a firm wide surface", "C": "Hip strategy, dominant for fast large pushes on a narrow surface", "D": "Ankle strategy, dominant for small slow sway on a firm surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ankle strategy, dominant for small slow sway on a firm surface): The ankle strategy shifts the body as a single inverted pendulum by activating muscles about the ankle from distal to proximal, and it dominates when sway is small and the surface is firm and wide. The hip strategy takes over with faster or larger perturbations or a narrow or compliant surface. A step is reserved for displacements that carry the center of mass beyond the base of support."}, {"id": 517, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 58 year old man with a total hip arthroplasty four years ago and normal sensation has chronic lumbar muscle tightness. A student worries that the metal hip prosthesis makes a hydrocollator pack unsafe and suggests skipping heat altogether. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Use a hotter pack, since metal absorbs heat and blocks penetration", "B": "A hot pack is acceptable, since metal implants do not heat selectively with it", "C": "Use the pack, but limit it to five minutes because of the prosthesis", "D": "Avoid the pack, since metal conducts heat into surrounding bone and soft tissue quickly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A hot pack is acceptable, since metal implants do not heat selectively with it): A hot pack heats by conduction at the skin, so metal implants do not concentrate heat as they can with shortwave diathermy. The usual precautions of sensation, circulation and skin checks still apply. A time cut or a hotter pack is unnecessary and the second adds burn risk."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During an overhead squat, a 33-year-old's arms drift forward in front of the ears while the ankles and torso look fine. In supine, passive shoulder flexion to 170 degrees is full and painless. Which muscle impairment best explains the arm drift?", "options": {"A": "Tight gastrocnemius and soleus limiting dorsiflexion, with weak anterior tibialis", "B": "Tight hip flexors and weak gluteus maximus, producing excessive anterior pelvic tilt", "C": "Weak deltoids and rotator cuff unable to hold the arms overhead against gravity", "D": "Shortened latissimus dorsi and pectoral muscles with weak lower and middle trapezius"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shortened latissimus dorsi and pectoral muscles with weak lower and middle trapezius): Arms drifting forward during an overhead squat with normal ankles, torso and full passive flexion suggest short latissimus dorsi and pectoral muscles with weak lower and middle trapezius. The shortened muscles pull the arms forward and the weak scapular muscles cannot correct it. The lower-leg and pelvic patterns are real faults but are normal here. Full passive range rules out capsular restriction, and deltoid weakness does not explain the drift."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A previously sedentary 37-year-old man shows a lower heart rate at a fixed submaximal workload after only ten days of daily endurance sessions, long before muscle biopsies would show new mitochondria. The clinician asks which early adaptation best accounts for this quick change. Which adaptation explains it?", "options": {"A": "Expanded plasma volume that increases venous return and stroke volume", "B": "Increased capillary density around the trained muscle fibers of the legs", "C": "Increased skeletal muscle mitochondrial number and oxidative enzyme size", "D": "Structural thickening of the left ventricular wall with a larger cavity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Expanded plasma volume that increases venous return and stroke volume): Plasma volume expands within days of starting endurance training, which raises venous return and stroke volume and lowers submaximal heart rate. Mitochondrial growth and capillary proliferation need several weeks. Structural changes in the ventricle also take weeks to months, so they cannot explain a response at ten days."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist is explaining normal scapular mechanics to a 30-year-old volleyball player who has shoulder pain during overhead hitting. The scapula must rotate upward to clear the acromion as the arm elevates. Which muscle group forms the primary force couple that produces upward rotation of the scapula?", "options": {"A": "Latissimus dorsi, teres major, and posterior deltoid working together to rotate the scapula", "B": "Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor working together to rotate the scapula", "C": "Upper trapezius, lower trapezius, and serratus anterior working together", "D": "Rhomboids, levator scapulae, and pectoralis minor working together to rotate the scapula"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Upper trapezius, lower trapezius, and serratus anterior working together): The upper and lower trapezius and the serratus anterior pull in different directions and together produce scapular upward rotation. The rhomboids, levator, and pectoralis minor rotate it downward. The latissimus, teres major, and posterior deltoid act at the humerus, and the cuff muscles centre the humeral head."}, {"id": 521, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with a stable low back condition has completed a strengthening phase and now needs to improve local muscular endurance of the trunk extensors for a job that requires prolonged standing and bending. Which general exercise prescription best targets muscular endurance rather than maximal strength or power?", "options": {"A": "Heavy loads near eighty five percent of 1RM for six or fewer repetitions per set", "B": "Light to moderate loads performed for fifteen or more repetitions with short rest periods", "C": "Light loads moved at maximal intended velocity for three to five explosive repetitions with full recovery", "D": "Moderate loads of about seventy percent of 1RM for eight to twelve repetitions with long rests"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Light to moderate loads performed for fifteen or more repetitions with short rest periods): Endurance training uses lower loads with high repetitions, commonly fifteen or more, and short rests so the muscle works against fatigue. Heavy loads for six or fewer repetitions develop maximal strength. Eight to twelve repetitions at moderate load with longer rests fit hypertrophy. Explosive low repetition sets with full recovery develop power."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A student physical therapist documents that a patient keeps an upright stance on a firm floor with feet together for 30 seconds without stepping or reaching for support. The therapist wants to label this observation correctly in the chart. Which category of balance does it represent?", "options": {"A": "Reactive balance, responding to an unexpected external perturbation of stance", "B": "Dynamic balance, controlling the center of mass during voluntary movement", "C": "Static balance, holding the center of mass over a stationary base", "D": "Anticipatory balance, preparing posture before a planned limb movement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Static balance, holding the center of mass over a stationary base): Static balance is the ability to keep the center of mass within a stationary base of support, as in quiet standing. Dynamic balance requires control while moving, reactive balance answers an unexpected disturbance, and anticipatory balance prepares posture before a voluntary movement. Nothing moved or disturbed this patient during the 30 seconds."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old swimmer has pain at the base of the neck with deep inhalation. Palpation shows the right first rib sitting higher than the left and tender, with tight muscles at the side of the neck. Which muscles most directly pull the first rib upward in this dysfunction?", "options": {"A": "The serratus posterior inferior and the lower external intercostals", "B": "The levator scapulae and the upper trapezius at the back of the neck", "C": "The pectoralis minor, attaching to the third through fifth ribs", "D": "The anterior and middle scalene muscles on the right side"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The anterior and middle scalene muscles on the right side): The anterior and middle scalenes attach to the first rib and elevate it during inspiration, so tightness holds the rib elevated. The levator scapulae and upper trapezius are tight in neck pain but move the scapula, not the first rib. Pectoralis minor acts on ribs three to five, and serratus posterior inferior acts on the lower ribs. Treatment often releases the scalenes and mobilizes the first rib."}, {"id": 524, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old recreational tennis player has completed ten weeks of rehabilitation for lateral epicondylitis. Grip is pain-free, eccentric loading is tolerated well, and she has no tenderness at the common extensor origin. She asks to resume match play this weekend. Which plan best supports her return to activity?", "options": {"A": "Full match play at the previous volume within one week of clearance", "B": "Graded return with shorter sessions, plus technique, grip size, and string tension changes", "C": "Immediate full play with a counterforce brace to offload the extensor origin", "D": "Restrict play to forehand strokes only for the remainder of the season"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Graded return with shorter sessions, plus technique, grip size, and string tension changes): A graded return with shorter sessions lets the tendon adapt, and changes to technique, grip size, and string tension reduce load on the extensor origin. Jumping to full volume risks recurrence. A counterforce brace can offload the origin but does not replace graded exposure, and limiting play to forehand strokes does not rebuild tolerance for all strokes."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 58-year-old man with long-standing diabetes walks with feet apart, stamping each heel down hard while watching the floor. He sways badly when he closes his eyes, and vibration sense is lost at both ankles. Which gait pattern fits best?", "options": {"A": "Vestibular gait from inner ear disease, with veering to one side", "B": "Sensory ataxia from loss of position sense, worse with eyes closed", "C": "Cerebellar ataxic gait from a lesion of the vermis, unchanged by vision", "D": "Parkinsonian gait from basal ganglia disease, with reduced arm swing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sensory ataxia from loss of position sense, worse with eyes closed): Stamping steps, a wide base, visual dependence, and loss of vibration sense at the ankles indicate sensory ataxia from dorsal column or large fiber loss. A cerebellar ataxia is not improved by looking at the floor or worsened by closing the eyes. Parkinsonian gait features short shuffling steps."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 64-year-old patient two weeks after total knee arthroplasty cannot voluntarily recruit the quadriceps and shows a quad lag during straight leg raises. The therapist wants to use surface electromyographic biofeedback during quad sets. Where should the electrode be placed and what feedback goal should be set for the patient?", "options": {"A": "Over the rectus femoris, with the goal of keeping the signal below a relaxation threshold", "B": "Over the vastus lateralis, with feedback encouraging lower amplitude to spare the medialis", "C": "Over the hamstrings, with feedback encouraging higher amplitude to improve cocontraction", "D": "Over the vastus medialis, with visual feedback encouraging higher signal amplitude on each set"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Over the vastus medialis, with visual feedback encouraging higher signal amplitude on each set): Surface EMG over the vastus medialis, with feedback rewarding higher amplitude, helps a patient who cannot voluntarily recruit the quadriceps. A relaxation threshold suits overactive muscles, not weak ones. Lowering vastus lateralis signal does not address recruitment, and hamstring recording reflects the antagonist."}, {"id": 527, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 22-year-old recreational runner has anterior knee pain on stairs, and examination shows a patella that sits and tracks laterally during active extension. The therapist plans McConnell-style patellar taping to correct this positioning before the next training week. In which direction should the tape pull the patella?", "options": {"A": "Posteriorly on the inferior pole, using an anteroposterior tilt correction for fat pad irritation", "B": "Toward the medial side, using a medial glide correction", "C": "Superiorly along the quadriceps line, using a superior glide correction for a low riding patella", "D": "With a lateral rotation correction, pulling the inferior pole laterally to realign the patella"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Toward the medial side, using a medial glide correction): Lateral patellar displacement is corrected by anchoring tape on the lateral border and pulling the patella medially, giving a medial glide. An anteroposterior tilt correction treats fat pad irritation and does not change lateral position. A superior glide is used for a low riding patella. A lateral rotation correction treats a medially rotated patella, so none of them addresses lateral displacement."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 74-year-old man with a demand cardiac pacemaker has mid-thoracic muscle pain. The chiropractor plans four-pole interferential current with electrodes around the upper thoracic spine and anterior chest wall. He asks whether lowering intensity to sensory level would make this setup acceptable. Which response is best?", "options": {"A": "No, avoid electrodes across the thorax and choose a non-electrical modality instead", "B": "Yes, if the carrier is raised above 8000 Hz to bypass the device", "C": "Yes, if premodulated mode replaces four-pole to keep current confined", "D": "Yes, sensory intensity is too weak to interfere with the sensing circuit of the pacemaker"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, avoid electrodes across the thorax and choose a non-electrical modality instead): Electrical current across the thorax can be sensed by a demand pacemaker and may inhibit or disturb its output. Lowering intensity, changing mode, or raising the carrier does not remove the risk. The safe approach is to avoid electrical stimulation near the heart and choose a non-electrical modality."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 37-year-old performs a kneeling hip flexor stretch for a tight iliopsoas but feels the stretch in the low back and no pull at the front of the hip. His lumbar spine arches and the pelvis tilts anteriorly as he leans forward. What is the best correction?", "options": {"A": "Place the front foot closer to the knee to shorten the stretch", "B": "Increase lunge depth and lean farther forward to force more hip extension", "C": "Tilt the pelvis posteriorly and squeeze the glute before leaning forward", "D": "Hold the stretch twice as long while keeping the lower back arched"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tilt the pelvis posteriorly and squeeze the glute before leaning forward): An arched back and anterior tilt let the pelvis slide forward so the iliopsoas never reaches end range. A posterior tilt with a gluteal squeeze fixes the pelvis and loads the hip flexor. Leaning farther adds range but keeps the compensation. Longer holds with the arch repeat the error. Moving the foot closer simply shortens the stretch."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 33-year-old cyclist is tested side-lying with the top hip extended and abducted, then the examiner releases the leg. The thigh stays suspended above the table rather than dropping toward adduction. A student interprets this Ober test result. What structure is most likely shortened?", "options": {"A": "Piriformis and the deep lateral hip rotators of the tested hip", "B": "Gluteus maximus fibers that insert onto the gluteal tuberosity of the femur", "C": "Tensor fasciae latae and the iliotibial band on the tested side", "D": "Adductor longus and gracilis muscles of the tested lower extremity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tensor fasciae latae and the iliotibial band on the tested side): The Ober test lets gravity adduct an abducted, extended hip, and a leg that stays up indicates shortened tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band. Adductors shortened would limit abduction, not adduction. Piriformis and the deep rotators restrict rotation and are assessed with other tests. Gluteus maximus fibers that insert onto the iliotibial band are less relevant than the tensor fasciae latae and band itself."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student prepares high voltage pulsed current for a clean sacral wound. The therapist explains the active electrode setup, noting that a bare metal electrode must not rest directly on the wound surface. Which active electrode preparation matches standard technique?", "options": {"A": "Electrode coated in ultrasound gel placed in the wound bed beneath plastic film", "B": "Electrode wrapped in dry gauze placed over the wound and held with adhesive tape", "C": "Electrode placed on intact skin two inches from the wound with no packing", "D": "Saline-moistened gauze around the electrode, packed into the wound bed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Saline-moistened gauze around the electrode, packed into the wound bed): Saline-moistened gauze gives a conductive, moist interface that conforms to the wound bed and spreads current evenly. A sterile cover holds it in place and keeps the wound clean. Dry gauze is a poor conductor and can cause a sharp sensation. Gel is not the usual interface for a wound, and an electrode on intact skin away from the wound does not stimulate the wound tissue."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 68 year old man two weeks after hip surgery reports calf tightness and asks for a hot pack. The left calf is swollen, warm, and tender to deep palpation, and the left leg is larger than the right. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply moist heat for 20 minutes to relax the calf muscles", "B": "Gentle calf stretching and walking to reduce tightness, then recheck swelling", "C": "Withhold heat and refer promptly for evaluation of possible deep vein thrombosis", "D": "Apply a compression wrap and elevate the leg, then reassess the swelling in a day"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold heat and refer promptly for evaluation of possible deep vein thrombosis): Unilateral swelling, warmth, tenderness and a larger leg after surgery point to possible deep vein thrombosis, so heat is withheld and the patient is referred promptly. Heat treats it as muscle tightness and delays diagnosis. Stretching and walking could disturb a clot. Compression and elevation address swelling but wait a day while thrombosis remains unexamined."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old walker has pain at the posterior heel directly where the Achilles tendon attaches to the calcaneus, with a tender bony prominence and pain when the heel drops below a step edge. Her therapist adapts the eccentric loading program for insertional tendinopathy. How should the heel drop be changed?", "options": {"A": "Replace loading with deep static calf stretching in maximal dorsiflexion each hour", "B": "Add a ballistic bounce at the bottom to increase the rate of tendon strain", "C": "Perform the heel drops from flat ground so the heel stays at or above neutral", "D": "Perform heel drops off a step edge so the heel falls well below neutral"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Perform the heel drops from flat ground so the heel stays at or above neutral): At the insertion, dorsiflexion past neutral compresses the tendon against the posterior calcaneus and aggravates symptoms. Loading is therefore done from flat ground or with a heel lift so the range stays at or above neutral. A step drop, deep dorsiflexion stretching, and ballistic bouncing all increase compressive stress at the attachment."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A strength coach asks a chiropractic student to name the three exercises Stuart McGill recommends as the foundation of a stabilization program to build spinal endurance while limiting compressive load. The student must choose the set exactly as McGill describes it for patients with low back pain. Which set of exercises is correct?", "options": {"A": "The modified curl-up, the dead bug, and the Swiss ball hamstring curl with straight legs", "B": "The modified curl-up, the side bridge, and the quadruped bird dog exercise", "C": "The full sit-up, the prone back extension, and the bird dog exercise on hands and knees", "D": "The side bridge, the prone plank, and the supine straight leg raise with both knees locked"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The modified curl-up, the side bridge, and the quadruped bird dog exercise): McGill's big three are the modified curl-up for the anterior wall, the side bridge for the lateral stabilizers, and the bird dog for the extensors, chosen for endurance with low spinal compression. Full sit-ups load the spine through the hip flexors and are avoided. The dead bug and the hamstring curl are useful exercises but are not part of the big three. The plank and the straight leg raise are also outside the set."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 32-year-old runner has marked quadriceps weakness and cannot fully activate the muscle after patellar surgery, with a voluntary activation deficit shown on testing. The clinician wants electrical stimulation to produce the greatest strengthening effect during isometric knee extension. Which strategy is best?", "options": {"A": "Superimpose stimulation on a maximal voluntary quadriceps contraction at the highest tolerable intensity.", "B": "Stimulate with the muscle fully relaxed, so voluntary effort cannot interfere with the current.", "C": "Alternate stimulation and voluntary contractions every minute, keeping the two separate in time.", "D": "Use sensory-level stimulation during voluntary contraction, keeping intensity below visible contraction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Superimpose stimulation on a maximal voluntary quadriceps contraction at the highest tolerable intensity.): Adding stimulation on top of a maximal voluntary contraction recruits motor units the patient cannot activate alone, increasing torque and training effect and improving voluntary activation. Voluntary effort adds to the current rather than interfering with it. Sensory-level stimulation adds little force, and alternating separately gives up the combined effect."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 60-year-old man holds the standard Romberg position for 30 seconds with eyes closed without sway. The clinician suspects mild dorsal column impairment from long-standing alcohol use and wants a more sensitive static test. Which test is the best choice for this purpose?", "options": {"A": "Single leg stance with the eyes open on a firm surface for thirty seconds", "B": "Sharpened Romberg in tandem stance with the eyes closed", "C": "Timed Up and Go at a comfortable pace", "D": "Standard Romberg repeated with the eyes open and head turns"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sharpened Romberg in tandem stance with the eyes closed): A sharpened Romberg in tandem stance with the eyes closed narrows the base and removes vision, making it more sensitive for mild dorsal column loss. Single leg stance with the eyes open lets vision mask the deficit. Timed Up and Go measures dynamic mobility. Repeating the standard Romberg with the eyes open and head turns adds visual input and does not challenge proprioception."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 31-year-old distance runner increased weekly mileage by 40 percent over three weeks. His morning resting heart rate has climbed from 52 to 64 beats per minute, his pace at the usual effort has slowed, and he reports poor sleep and irritability. Which next step is most appropriate for this runner?", "options": {"A": "Increase the long run further to restore the previous training base", "B": "Repeat a maximal treadmill test this week to confirm that VO2 max has fallen", "C": "Continue current mileage but add easy cross-training to improve recovery capacity", "D": "Reduce training volume and intensity for several days while tracking resting heart rate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduce training volume and intensity for several days while tracking resting heart rate): A rising morning resting heart rate, slower pace, poor sleep, and mood change after a rapid mileage jump are classic signs of overreaching or early overtraining. The remedy is a reduction in load with recovery while resting heart rate is monitored. Adding long running increases stress, and a maximal test places further demand on a fatigued athlete. Keeping mileage unchanged with cross-training does not reduce the total load."}, {"id": 538, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 26-year-old sprinter has a strain of the adductor longus near its pubic origin, and the clinician plans therapeutic ultrasound for it this week. The sound head would approach the groin close to the testes. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Treat the origin with pulsed ultrasound, because pulsing makes the testes safe from heating", "B": "Treat the origin with 3 MHz, because the shallow beam cannot reach the testes", "C": "Treat the origin at reduced intensity with the head angled toward the pubic bone", "D": "Treat the muscle with the beam directed away from the testes, or choose another modality"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Treat the muscle with the beam directed away from the testes, or choose another modality): The testes are sensitive to ultrasound, so the beam should be directed away from them or another modality chosen for an origin this close. Pulsing lowers average heating but does not remove the risk. A 3 MHz beam is shallow yet still affects nearby superficial tissue, and angling toward the pubic bone at lower intensity still aims energy at the groin."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old runner attempts the drawing-in maneuver in hooklying while the therapist palpates just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine. The therapist feels strong bulging of the rectus abdominis and sees the pelvis tilt posteriorly on each attempt, with little deep tension under the fingers. Which adjustment best corrects this performance?", "options": {"A": "Reduce effort to a gentle contraction and cue a slow lower abdominal draw in", "B": "Add a posterior pelvic tilt to anchor the spine while the abdomen is drawn in", "C": "Increase the effort so the deeper muscle can overpower the rectus abdominis during the contraction", "D": "Switch to a full sit-up so the strong rectus abdominis can be trained first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce effort to a gentle contraction and cue a slow lower abdominal draw in): The transversus abdominis works at low effort, and high effort recruits the rectus abdominis and tilts the pelvis. Cueing a gentle, slow draw lets the deep muscle become the main contributor. Increasing effort worsens substitution, and a posterior tilt is the compensation being observed. A sit-up trains the dominant muscle instead."}, {"id": 540, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes notices his blood glucose falls after a 30-minute brisk walk even though he injected no insulin and his insulin levels were low. His physiotherapist is asked how muscle takes up glucose under these conditions. Which mechanism best explains this?", "options": {"A": "Exercise raises circulating insulin sharply, which then drives GLUT4 to the membrane", "B": "Exercise increases receptor sensitivity so the small insulin level drives GLUT4 as usual", "C": "Contraction moves GLUT4 transporters to the membrane through an insulin-independent pathway", "D": "Increased muscle blood flow alone delivers more glucose, and transport proteins play no role"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Contraction moves GLUT4 transporters to the membrane through an insulin-independent pathway): Muscle contraction moves GLUT4 to the membrane through an insulin-independent pathway involving AMPK and calcium signaling. That explains glucose uptake with low insulin. Exercise lowers rather than raises insulin, and increased sensitivity is a later effect rather than the mechanism during the walk. Greater blood flow helps delivery but transport still depends on GLUT4."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 38-year-old man with morning back stiffness and reduced chest expansion walks with a rigid trunk, almost no counter-rotation of the shoulders against the pelvis, and reduced arm swing. He has no tremor and normal leg strength. What best explains his gait?", "options": {"A": "Loss of pelvis and trunk counter-rotation from axial spine stiffness", "B": "Cerebellar dysfunction causing loss of reciprocal limb coordination", "C": "Parkinsonian rigidity causing reduced arm swing and reduced step size", "D": "Bilateral hip abductor weakness causing a restricted pelvic motion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of pelvis and trunk counter-rotation from axial spine stiffness): Fusion or stiffness of the thoracolumbar spine from ankylosing spondylitis blocks the normal rotation between pelvis and shoulders, which also reduces arm swing. The early morning stiffness, limited chest expansion, and age support this cause. Parkinsonism would add tremor and short steps, and strong legs rule out abductor weakness."}, {"id": 542, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 48-year-old mail carrier has subacute lumbar paraspinal muscle guarding after a strain 10 days ago, with no radicular signs and intact sensation. Pain limits trunk motion before an exercise session. The clinician selects a superficial thermal agent to reduce guarding and improve tolerance. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A hydrocollator pack heated to 212 degrees F and applied without toweling for 10 minutes", "B": "An ice pack applied directly to bare skin for 45 minutes over the lumbar muscles", "C": "A chemical cold spray and stretch technique over the muscles for several minutes", "D": "A hot moist pack with towel layers applied for 20 minutes to the lumbar muscles"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A hot moist pack with towel layers applied for 20 minutes to the lumbar muscles): In the subacute stage, moist heat with several towel layers for about 20 minutes increases superficial blood flow and reduces guarding. Hydrocollator units are kept near 160 to 165 degrees F, and toweling prevents burns. Prolonged bare skin ice stiffens muscle and risks injury. Cold spray is a different technique and does not provide the heating effect wanted."}, {"id": 543, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 44-year-old programmer has low back pain that increases with prolonged sitting and forward bending. During bending the lumbar spine flexes early while the hips move little, and the pain eases with standing extension. Which movement impairment and strategy fit best?", "options": {"A": "Lumbar flexion syndrome, so train hip flexion while holding a neutral lumbar spine", "B": "Lumbar extension syndrome, so train abdominal bracing during standing activities", "C": "Lumbar flexion syndrome, so stretch the lumbar erectors to improve forward bending", "D": "Lumbar rotation syndrome, so train unilateral trunk twisting with a resistance band"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lumbar flexion syndrome, so train hip flexion while holding a neutral lumbar spine): Pain that rises with sitting and flexion and eases with extension, plus early lumbar flexion with little hip motion, fits a flexion pattern. Training hip flexion with a neutral lumbar spine reduces repeated lumbar flexion. Stretching the erectors would add more flexion motion to the already mobile lumbar segments."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 33-year-old cross-country runner has a rigid high-arched foot with a clawed toe posture, recurrent lateral ankle sprains, and pain under the metatarsal heads. She has poor shock absorption at heel strike on hard surfaces. Which orthotic design best fits her presentation?", "options": {"A": "A rigid orthosis with a strong medial rearfoot post to block pronation", "B": "A cushioned full-contact insole with arch fill and a metatarsal pad", "C": "A thin flat insert that matches the shoe and adds no cushioning under the forefoot", "D": "A medial arch wedge that raises the navicular to push the foot toward eversion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A cushioned full-contact insole with arch fill and a metatarsal pad): A rigid cavus foot has little natural shock absorption and concentrates load under the heel and metatarsal heads, so a cushioned insole that fills the arch spreads pressure and a metatarsal pad unloads the heads. A strong medial post targets a pronated foot. A medial wedge that raises the navicular adds stress to an already rigid arch. A thin flat insert gives no cushioning."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "In an exercise laboratory, a healthy 30-year-old woman performing a maximal cycle test has a cardiac output of 18 liters per minute and an arteriovenous oxygen difference of 16 milliliters of oxygen per 100 milliliters of blood. Using the Fick equation, what is her oxygen consumption in milliliters per minute?", "options": {"A": "288 mL/min, from 1,800 mL multiplied by 0.16", "B": "1,125 mL/min, from 18,000 mL divided by 16", "C": "2,880 mL/min, from 18,000 mL multiplied by 0.16", "D": "2.88 mL/min, from 18 liters multiplied by 0.16 per minute"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (2,880 mL/min, from 18,000 mL multiplied by 0.16): Cardiac output of 18 L/min is 18,000 mL/min, and 16 mL per 100 mL equals 0.16. Multiplying gives 18,000 times 0.16, or 2,880 mL/min. Dividing by 16 gives 1,125, which is the wrong operation. Using 1,800 mL loses a factor of ten, and 2.88 is the answer in liters but is mislabeled as milliliters."}, {"id": 546, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient wearing a long leg cast after a fracture is taught quadriceps setting, in which she tightens the thigh muscle without moving the knee. A student asks what type of contraction this is and how far the strength gain carries across the joint range after training at one angle.", "options": {"A": "Isometric, with strength gains that transfer equally across the entire range of motion", "B": "Isometric, with strength gains mostly confined to joint angles near the trained position", "C": "Isotonic concentric, with strength gains confined to joint angles near the trained position", "D": "Isokinetic, with strength gains that follow the preset angular velocity of the limb"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Isometric, with strength gains mostly confined to joint angles near the trained position): Quadriceps setting produces tension with no change in muscle length, which is an isometric contraction. Isometric strength gains are angle specific, so they appear mostly near the angle trained. For that reason multiple angles are used when the joint can move. Isotonic and isokinetic work both involve joint motion, which the cast prevents."}, {"id": 547, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 39-year-old carpenter reports pain with overhead reaching at work and no neurologic symptoms. The scapula shows reduced upward rotation during elevation, and pain falls markedly when the examiner manually assists scapular upward rotation. Which treatment emphasis is best supported by this finding?", "options": {"A": "Stretching the posterior shoulder capsule as the first priority of care", "B": "Rotator cuff external rotation strengthening at moderate loads as the sole emphasis", "C": "Strengthening the serratus anterior and lower trapezius as scapular upward rotators", "D": "Stretching the pectoralis minor as the sole emphasis of the plan"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Strengthening the serratus anterior and lower trapezius as scapular upward rotators): Relief when the scapula is assisted into upward rotation points to deficient scapular upward rotation, driven by the serratus anterior and the upper and lower trapezius. Training the serratus and lower trapezius addresses it. Capsule stretching does not explain the relief with scapular assist. Cuff strengthening and pectoralis minor stretching help in some shoulders but leave the weak upward rotators untrained."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 26-week pregnant woman with low back and pelvic girdle pain asks about lumbar traction because it helped her sister. Her examination shows no neurologic deficit, she is otherwise healthy, and she wants a non-drug option. Which plan should the therapist select?", "options": {"A": "Apply low force supine traction with the legs elevated to protect the fetus", "B": "Withhold lumbar traction and offer a pelvic support belt with positioning advice", "C": "Apply inversion traction at 10 degrees for short periods with the belly supported", "D": "Use prone traction with a pillow under the abdomen at 25 percent of body weight"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold lumbar traction and offer a pelvic support belt with positioning advice): Pregnancy is a contraindication to lumbar traction because the belts and pull stress the abdomen and pelvis, so a pelvic support belt and positioning advice are better options. Lower force, leg elevation, pillow support, or a small inversion angle do not remove the risk and some of these positions are unsafe at 26 weeks. The belt gives support without adding distraction forces to a pregnant abdomen."}, {"id": 549, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 19-year-old volleyball player has anterior knee pain on stairs. The patella sits lateral at rest and tracks laterally during active extension. Manually gliding the patella medially during a squat eliminates the pain completely. Which taping choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Medial glide tape pulling the patella toward the medial femoral condyle", "B": "Fat pad unloading tape lifting the infrapatellar tissue to relieve anterior knee pain", "C": "Superior glide tape pulling the patella toward the quadriceps tendon to lift it from the trochlea", "D": "Circumferential compression tape around the knee without patellar direction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Medial glide tape pulling the patella toward the medial femoral condyle): The manual medial glide removed her pain, so taping should reproduce that correction by pulling the patella medially. Fat pad unloading tape suits infrapatellar fat pad irritation rather than lateral maltracking. A superior glide does not match the corrective direction that relieved symptoms, and nondirectional compression gives no positional correction."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 48-year-old patient in an intensive care unit has been sedated and bedbound for ten days, with the feet resting in plantarflexion against the foot of the bed. Passive dorsiflexion is now limited with a soft tissue stretch end feel. The team wants to prevent progressive calf shortening. Which intervention best fits?", "options": {"A": "Daily calf stretching by the therapist in short sessions, with feet left resting against the footboard", "B": "Positioning splints holding the ankles near neutral, plus regular passive dorsiflexion", "C": "Resting splints holding the ankles in slight plantarflexion for comfort, plus regular passive dorsiflexion", "D": "Foot boards pressed firmly against the soles to maintain pressure and encourage active dorsiflexion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Positioning splints holding the ankles near neutral, plus regular passive dorsiflexion): In a sedated, bedbound patient the calf shortens in plantarflexion, so prolonged positioning near neutral with splints plus regular passive dorsiflexion prevents contracture. Brief stretching leaves the feet in shortening for most of the day. Splinting in plantarflexion preserves the shortened range, and a footboard cannot drive active dorsiflexion in a sedated patient."}, {"id": 551, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with confirmed lumbar canal stenosis stands in marked lordosis, has tight hip flexors, and develops thigh heaviness after 100 yards of walking that eases in forward-flexed positions. Her physician has excluded red flags. Which exercise prescription best addresses her impairments and symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Posterior pelvic tilts, hip flexor stretching, and abdominal strengthening in flexion", "B": "Repeated prone press-ups, hip flexor stretching, and lumbar extension strengthening in standing", "C": "Prone lying with a pillow under the hips and passive extension stretching over a ball", "D": "Back extension machine strengthening and anterior pelvic tilt drills to build lordosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Posterior pelvic tilts, hip flexor stretching, and abdominal strengthening in flexion): Reducing lordosis with posterior pelvic tilts, lengthening tight hip flexors, and strengthening the abdominals widen the canal and improve walking tolerance. This matches the flexion bias of stenosis. Extension exercises and passive extension stretching narrow the canal and provoke symptoms. Building lordosis counters the goal of the program."}, {"id": 552, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 47-year-old nurse has diffuse aching over a broad area of the lower back that she cannot localize to one spot. The chiropractor uses four-pole interferential current but cannot find a single crossing point that covers the whole region. Which feature of the unit best addresses the large, poorly localized area?", "options": {"A": "A higher carrier frequency, which spreads the interference over a wider zone", "B": "Premodulated mode, which concentrates the beat between two electrodes", "C": "Beat frequency sweep, which varies the beat rate across a range", "D": "Vector scan, which shifts the field orientation so maximal interference sweeps across the area"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vector scan, which shifts the field orientation so maximal interference sweeps across the area): Vector scan alters channel amplitudes so the zone of maximal interference moves, covering a larger or poorly localized area without moving pads. Beat sweep changes the rate, not the area. Premodulated mode concentrates current between its two electrodes. Carrier frequency affects skin impedance, not the size of the interference field."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 24-year-old sprinter performs repeated maximal 100 meter efforts. During the session, the coach explains that the fibers producing the highest peak force and fastest contraction speed also fatigue the quickest. Which fiber type is the coach describing, and which energy systems does it depend on most during this kind of effort?", "options": {"A": "Type IIx fibers, which depend mainly on anaerobic glycolysis and the phosphagen system", "B": "Type IIa fibers, which are fast but oxidative and resist fatigue through high mitochondrial content", "C": "Type I fibers, which depend mainly on aerobic oxidation of fats and carbohydrate", "D": "Type I fibers, which depend mainly on anaerobic glycolysis because of their large motor neurons"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Type IIx fibers, which depend mainly on anaerobic glycolysis and the phosphagen system): Type IIx fibers have the fastest myosin ATPase and the greatest peak force, and they rely on stored phosphagen and anaerobic glycolysis, so they fatigue in seconds to a minute. Type I fibers are slow and oxidative. Type IIa fibers are fast but have more mitochondria and resist fatigue better, and Type I fibers are small motor unit fibers with oxidative metabolism, not glycolytic ones."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 21-year-old runner has had shin pain for five weeks. Tenderness is limited to a one centimeter spot on the mid tibia, the area hurts when walking and at night, and single leg hopping reproduces sharp pain. Which interpretation and plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Medial tibial stress syndrome, so continue running with calf stretching and ice after each run", "B": "Suspected tibial stress fracture, so stop impact, protect the leg, and obtain imaging", "C": "Shin splints, so add therapeutic ultrasound and resume full mileage after a week of rest", "D": "Anterior compartment syndrome, so begin forceful eccentric tibialis anterior loading each day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Suspected tibial stress fracture, so stop impact, protect the leg, and obtain imaging): Focal point tenderness, pain at night and with walking, and a positive hop test indicate a stress fracture, not the diffuse pain of medial tibial stress syndrome. The plan is to stop impact, protect the bone, and confirm with imaging. Compartment syndrome gives exertional tightness, and a quick return to mileage risks a complete fracture."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old patient performs the bird dog and lifts the leg well above the level of the trunk, and the therapist observes lumbar hyperextension with pelvic rotation. The patient reports mild low back pain at the top of each lift. What is the best correction for this exercise?", "options": {"A": "Lift the leg to horizontal while cueing a deliberate lumbar arch to engage the extensors", "B": "Lift the leg higher still to increase the gluteal contraction against gravity", "C": "Add an ankle weight so the lumbar muscles work harder during the leg lift", "D": "Lift the leg only to the horizontal level of the trunk, keeping the pelvis level"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lift the leg only to the horizontal level of the trunk, keeping the pelvis level): Lifting above trunk level produces lumbar extension and pelvic rotation, so the leg should rise only to horizontal while the pelvis stays level. Cueing a deliberate lumbar arch trains the very extension that is the fault. Higher lifts increase the extension, and an ankle weight adds load to a faulty pattern."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old has limited ankle dorsiflexion measuring 6 degrees with the knee extended and 7 degrees with the knee flexed. The end feel is firm and capsular, and posterior glide of the talus on the tibia is reduced. Calf muscle length testing is otherwise normal. Which treatment addresses the primary restriction?", "options": {"A": "Anterior glide of the talus on the fixed tibia to promote dorsiflexion range", "B": "Soleus stretching with the knee flexed and the heel held flat on the floor", "C": "Talocrural joint mobilization with a posterior glide of the talus on the fixed tibia", "D": "Gastrocnemius stretching with the knee extended, the heel flat, and the trunk leaning forward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Talocrural joint mobilization with a posterior glide of the talus on the fixed tibia): Dorsiflexion limited equally with the knee flexed or extended, with a firm capsular end feel and a reduced posterior talar glide, points to a joint restriction rather than gastrocnemius shortening. Dorsiflexion needs the talus to glide posteriorly on the tibia, so posterior glide mobilization is the match. Calf stretching targets muscle length, which is not the main problem, and an anterior glide is the wrong direction."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician plans phonophoresis for a 46-year-old teacher with chronic plantar fasciitis and needs a drug-containing medium that transmits ultrasound efficiently into the heel tissue beneath the skin. Which preparation is best suited to carry the drug while still transmitting the beam?", "options": {"A": "A thick petroleum-based ointment of the drug applied in a heavy layer over the heel", "B": "A drug powder sprinkled directly on dry skin before applying the sound head", "C": "A water-based gel formulation of the drug mixed with a transmission gel", "D": "A whipped foam preparation that spreads easily and leaves air in the layer"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A water-based gel formulation of the drug mixed with a transmission gel): Media with high water content transmit ultrasound with little loss, so a gel formulation is the standard carrier. Thick ointments attenuate the beam. Powder leaves an air gap and foams contain air, both of which reflect energy before it reaches the tissue."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old sprinter is tested on an isokinetic dynamometer. The clinician wants to train quadriceps strength and peak torque in the early phase, then later train at speeds closer to those of running. Which angular velocities match the strength and functional phases?", "options": {"A": "Slow speeds around sixty degrees per second for strength, faster speeds above two hundred for function", "B": "Zero degrees per second for strength, and sixty degrees per second for function", "C": "Fast speeds around three hundred degrees per second for strength, slow speeds for function", "D": "Slow speeds around sixty degrees per second for both strength and function phases"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slow speeds around sixty degrees per second for strength, faster speeds above two hundred for function): Slow isokinetic speeds near 60 degrees per second yield high peak torque for strength, while speeds above about 200 degrees per second approximate functional limb speeds and fit velocity specificity. Reversing the order fails the same principle. Keeping slow speeds for function ignores the speed demand of sprinting. Zero degrees per second is an isometric setting."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A patient with chronic stroke has learned three transfers (bed to chair, chair to toilet, car to chair) separately, with fewer errors each session. The therapist now wants the best retention and carryover at home. Which practice structure should be used for the next several sessions?", "options": {"A": "Blocked practice, completing all repetitions of one transfer before moving to the next transfer", "B": "Whole task practice with continuous manual guidance on each repetition to prevent any errors", "C": "Random practice that mixes the three transfers in unpredictable order within each session", "D": "Mental rehearsal only between sessions, with physical practice limited to one transfer per week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Random practice that mixes the three transfers in unpredictable order within each session): Once a patient can perform each task, random practice that interleaves tasks forces the learner to retrieve a plan each trial. Performance in the clinic looks worse, but retention and transfer to home are better. Blocked practice helps early acquisition, continuous guidance prevents error detection, and mental rehearsal with one transfer weekly gives too little physical practice."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old soccer player is five days after patellar tendon autograft ACL reconstruction. He walks with crutches, the knee rests in about 15 degrees of flexion because of pain, and he asks what to prioritize during this first week. Which goal should receive the highest priority in early rehabilitation?", "options": {"A": "Regaining ninety degrees of flexion first and delaying extension work for several weeks", "B": "Starting open chain extension from ninety to zero degrees with ankle weights", "C": "Single-leg wobble board drills to restore proprioception before extension is restored", "D": "Full passive knee extension equal to the other knee plus early quadriceps activation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Full passive knee extension equal to the other knee plus early quadriceps activation): Early extension loss is hard to recover and is linked to arthrofibrosis and poor gait, so full passive extension is the first priority, along with quadriceps activation and effusion control. Flexion progresses gradually and should not come before extension. Weighted open chain extension strains the new graft. Single-leg wobble board work is premature while the knee rests in flexion."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A physical therapy aide asks why a patient lifting a 2 lb cuff weight with the elbow flexors seems to use different muscle fibers than during a maximal single repetition. According to Henneman's size principle, which statement correctly describes the order of motor unit recruitment as the force required from the muscle increases?", "options": {"A": "Fast motor units fire first when the movement is rapid, and slow units are added later.", "B": "Large fast glycolytic motor units fire first, then smaller units are added to fine tune force.", "C": "Small slow oxidative motor units fire first, and larger units are added as force rises.", "D": "Motor units are recruited by distance from the motor neuron cell body, nearest fibers first."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Small slow oxidative motor units fire first, and larger units are added as force rises.): Henneman's size principle says small slow oxidative units with low thresholds fire first, and larger units join as force demand rises. Tying recruitment to movement speed confuses the size principle with ballistic strategies. Putting large fast units first reverses the order. Cell body distance plays no role in recruitment order."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with a 15-year history of rheumatoid arthritis has neck pain and occasional arm tingling. The Sharp-Purser test is positive, with a clunk and relief of symptoms as the head is translated posteriorly. The therapist is considering cervical traction. What is the best course of action?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with supine mechanical traction at 10 lb since arm tingling indicates a root problem", "B": "Apply low force intermittent traction with the neck held in about 10 degrees of extension", "C": "Withhold traction and refer for imaging because of suspected atlantoaxial instability", "D": "Use gentle manual traction with rotation to retest the clunk before deciding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Withhold traction and refer for imaging because of suspected atlantoaxial instability): A positive Sharp-Purser test suggests laxity of the transverse ligament, which is a recognized risk in long standing rheumatoid disease, and traction or rotation could endanger the cord. The right step is to hold traction and obtain imaging. Low force traction, manual pull with rotation, or supine mechanical traction all load an unstable upper cervical segment."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old recreational runner has recovered from an acute lumbar strain, with full pain-free motion, normal strength testing, and no neurological findings. She wants to resume running three weeks after the injury. Which return-to-activity plan best reflects a graded progression for her?", "options": {"A": "Wait for a full month of complete rest from all exercise before any jogging", "B": "Alternate walking and jogging intervals on alternate days, building running time gradually", "C": "Run on consecutive days at race pace to test whether the back tolerates load", "D": "Resume half her previous weekly mileage in continuous runs on consecutive days"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Alternate walking and jogging intervals on alternate days, building running time gradually): Graded exposure uses short running intervals broken by walking, with rest days between sessions, and advances volume only when symptoms stay quiet. This rebuilds tolerance while monitoring the response. Further complete rest causes deconditioning in a patient who has already recovered. Race pace on consecutive days is an excessive early test, and half mileage in continuous runs still skips the interval progression."}, {"id": 564, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 42-year-old walker has 8 degrees of ankle dorsiflexion with the knee extended and 9 degrees with the knee flexed to 90 degrees. The end feel is firm and elastic, and talar glides are normal. The patient reports tightness deep in the lower calf. Which muscle and stretch position best address the restriction that persists with the knee flexed?", "options": {"A": "Gastrocnemius, stretched with the knee extended so the muscle crosses both joints", "B": "Tibialis posterior, stretched with the ankle dorsiflexed and the foot everted", "C": "Soleus, stretched with the knee flexed so the gastrocnemius is slackened", "D": "Plantaris and gastrocnemius together, stretched with the knee hyperextended and the ankle dorsiflexed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Soleus, stretched with the knee flexed so the gastrocnemius is slackened): With the knee flexed, the gastrocnemius is slack, so a limit that persists points to the soleus, which crosses only the ankle. The soleus is stretched with the knee flexed and the ankle dorsiflexed. Normal talar glides and an elastic end feel make a joint restriction unlikely. Stretching with the knee extended or hyperextended loads the gastrocnemius instead."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 48-year-old man with a common peroneal nerve injury at the fibular head has weak ankle dorsiflexion, catches his toes during swing, and has no spasticity or mediolateral instability at the ankle. Which brace best prevents toe drag while allowing a normal push-off?", "options": {"A": "A solid ankle foot orthosis fixed at 15 degrees of plantar flexion to block ankle motion", "B": "A plastic posterior leaf spring orthosis that holds the foot near neutral in swing", "C": "A medial heel wedge and arch support that controls rearfoot eversion in stance", "D": "A lace-up ankle stabilizer with figure-eight straps that limits inversion and eversion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A plastic posterior leaf spring orthosis that holds the foot near neutral in swing): A posterior leaf spring orthosis flexes during stance to allow push-off and recoils to hold the foot near neutral during swing, which prevents toe drag from dorsiflexor weakness. A lace-up stabilizer does not lift the foot. A solid orthosis fixed in plantar flexion blocks push-off and does not correct the foot position. A medial wedge addresses pronation, not foot drop."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 26-year-old distance runner with a flexible flat foot develops medial shin pain, and the clinician places a medial rearfoot post in each running shoe. In terms of foot mechanics, which motion is a medial rearfoot post chiefly intended to limit during stance?", "options": {"A": "Excessive ankle dorsiflexion during the late stance push-off phase", "B": "Excessive calcaneal eversion that accompanies pronation after heel strike", "C": "Excessive calcaneal inversion that accompanies supination at the push-off phase", "D": "Excessive tibial external rotation that accompanies supination during midstance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excessive calcaneal eversion that accompanies pronation after heel strike): A medial rearfoot post limits excessive calcaneal eversion that accompanies pronation after heel strike, which loads the medial shin. Excessive inversion and tibial external rotation are supination motions, the problem addressed by a lateral post. Ankle dorsiflexion is a sagittal plane motion that the post is not designed to control."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 41-year-old carpenter with chronic low back pain is assessed with electromyography during rapid arm raises. Research by Hodges and Richardson found that patients like him show delayed transversus abdominis activation before limb movement. How should this finding shape the therapist's training focus for this muscle in his program?", "options": {"A": "Train the timing of anticipatory activation during limb movements, not only the strength", "B": "Train the strength of the rectus abdominis with weighted curl-ups until the abdomen fatigues", "C": "Train sustained endurance of the muscle with prolonged supine drawing-in holds", "D": "Train flexibility of the hip flexors and erector spinae with sustained stretching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Train the timing of anticipatory activation during limb movements, not only the strength): Hodges and Richardson showed a delay in anticipatory transversus abdominis activation before limb movement in people with low back pain. The deficit is feedforward timing, so training should rehearse timed activation during limb movements. Strength work for the rectus abdominis misses the target muscle, and endurance holds train capacity rather than timing. Stretching addresses length, not the activation delay."}, {"id": 568, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A therapist applies 80 lb of mechanical lumbar traction to a 200-lb patient lying on a standard one-piece table and the patient reports no change in symptoms. The therapist wants to lower the friction between the lower body and the table so more of the applied force reaches the spine. Which table change addresses this directly?", "options": {"A": "Use a split table so the lower section glides with the patient", "B": "Add a thick lumbar roll to flatten the lordotic curve during pull", "C": "Raise the head end of the table about 30 degrees to tilt the patient", "D": "Tighten the pelvic belt another notch against the iliac crests"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a split table so the lower section glides with the patient): Much of the applied pull is used up by friction between the lower body and the table, so a table split into sections that glide on rollers lets more force reach the spine. Raising the head end changes gravity components but not table friction. A lumbar roll alters lordosis and does not reduce friction. A tighter pelvic belt improves grip but leaves table friction unchanged."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "After a lumbar disc herniation, a 49-year-old man has right ankle dorsiflexion of 2 out of 5, plantarflexion 5 out of 5, and normal hip abductor strength. He lifts the right knee high in swing and the foot slaps down at heel contact. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Circumduction gait from spasticity of the right plantarflexor muscles", "B": "Vaulting gait from a stiff right knee and a long swing limb", "C": "Trendelenburg gait from superior gluteal nerve weakness at the L5 level", "D": "Steppage gait from dorsiflexor weakness at the L4 and L5 roots"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Steppage gait from dorsiflexor weakness at the L4 and L5 roots): Weak dorsiflexors cause foot slap in the loading response and toe drag in swing, and the patient compensates with extra hip and knee flexion, a steppage gait. L4 and L5 supply tibialis anterior, and the other options need abductor weakness, a stiff knee, or spasticity, none of which are present."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In prone, the clinician passively flexes a 28-year-old's right knee toward the buttock. Before the heel reaches the buttock, the right hip rises into flexion and the pelvis tilts forward. The left side moves freely to the buttock. Which structure does this Ely test result implicate as shortened?", "options": {"A": "The iliopsoas, a single-joint hip flexor acting on the lumbar spine", "B": "The biceps femoris, a two-joint hip extensor and knee flexor", "C": "The rectus femoris, a two-joint hip flexor and knee extensor", "D": "The tensor fasciae latae, a hip abductor that tracks along the iliotibial band"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The rectus femoris, a two-joint hip flexor and knee extensor): Flexing the knee in prone stretches the rectus femoris across both joints, and a short muscle pulls the hip into flexion and the pelvis into anterior tilt. The iliopsoas crosses only the hip. The biceps femoris is slackened by knee flexion, and the tensor fasciae latae is tested differently."}, {"id": 571, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 30-year-old man has a swollen, painful knee after a meniscus repair and the surgeon allows only isometric work for now. Because isometric strength gains carry over only to angles near the training position, how should quadriceps isometrics be performed?", "options": {"A": "Only at the angle of greatest weakness, using one maximal hold daily", "B": "At one knee angle only, using the position with the least pain and swelling during contraction", "C": "At several knee angles within the allowed range, holding each contraction several seconds", "D": "At full extension only, since this angle gives the greatest carryover"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (At several knee angles within the allowed range, holding each contraction several seconds): Isometric strength increases mostly near the trained angle, so the quadriceps should be trained at multiple angles within the surgeon's allowed range, submaximally and pain free. Using one angle leaves the rest of the range untrained. Full extension does not give universal carryover, and a daily maximal hold at one angle is neither comprehensive nor appropriate for a swollen joint."}, {"id": 572, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old man with known mid-thoracic stiffness reports mid-back tightness spreading to the left jaw for twenty minutes while climbing stairs, with sweating, relieved by rest. Thoracic motion is full and palpation does not reproduce the pain. What should the clinician do?", "options": {"A": "Mobilize the thoracic spine and recheck the pain", "B": "Order thoracic radiographs for the stiffness and continue the exercise program meanwhile", "C": "Refer for a dental examination and continue stair training while awaiting the appointment", "D": "Withhold exercise and refer for urgent cardiac evaluation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold exercise and refer for urgent cardiac evaluation): Exertional mid-back and jaw pain with sweating that eases with rest is angina until proven otherwise, and full thoracic motion with no pain on palpation argues against a musculoskeletal source. Exercise is withheld and urgent cardiac evaluation arranged. Mobilizing first, imaging the spine, or sending him for a dental opinion while he keeps exerting all delay recognition of a possible cardiac event."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 33-year-old guitarist has pain over the lateral epicondyle. Space on the forearm allows placement of only two small electrodes, and no site remains for a second circuit. The chiropractor wants interferential current for pain relief. Which mode is most practical?", "options": {"A": "Four-pole mode, with electrode pairs set diagonally across the elbow", "B": "Four-pole mode, with a pair on each side of the forearm", "C": "Vector scan on four poles, with the two electrodes shared between both channels on the forearm", "D": "Two-pole premodulated mode, with both electrodes on one circuit near the epicondyle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Two-pole premodulated mode, with both electrodes on one circuit near the epicondyle): Premodulated mode needs just one circuit and two electrodes because the beat is created inside the unit. That fits a small area such as the lateral elbow. A four-pole setup requires two independent pairs, and vector scan requires four poles as well. Electrodes cannot be shared between channels."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old software developer has acute central low back pain with referral to the right posterior thigh that worsens after sitting and forward bending. Repeated lumbar extension in standing and prone reduces the thigh symptoms and increases lumbar lordosis comfortably. Neurological screening is normal. Which exercise should the clinician prescribe first for home use?", "options": {"A": "Sustained long-sitting hamstring stretch held for several minutes before every activity", "B": "Repeated lumbar flexion with knees to chest several times daily to stretch the back", "C": "Repeated prone press-ups several times daily, with a lumbar roll when sitting", "D": "Side-gliding movements against the wall in standing, repeated each hour"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeated prone press-ups several times daily, with a lumbar roll when sitting): A response of centralization with extension defines an extension directional preference. Prone press-ups repeated frequently, along with maintaining lordosis in sitting, apply that direction at home. Flexion exercises and sustained long-sitting stretches load the posterior disc in the aggravating direction. Side gliding treats a lateral shift, which this patient does not show."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In the double straight leg lowering test, a 35-year-old woman lowers both legs from vertical in supine, and her lumbar spine arches off the table at about 60 degrees of hip flexion. What does this indicate, and what is the best training choice?", "options": {"A": "Limited lower abdominal control, so train slow leg lowering with core bracing", "B": "Weak erector spinae, so perform loaded back extensions to stop the arching", "C": "Strong hip flexors, so advance to full sit-ups with straight legs", "D": "Short hip flexors only, so stretch the iliopsoas and avoid abdominal work"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Limited lower abdominal control, so train slow leg lowering with core bracing): When the legs lower, the abdominals must hold the pelvis and lumbar spine against the pull of the hip flexors, and lumbar arching at 60 degrees shows control is lost early. Training leg lowering with a held posterior tilt addresses it. Stretching alone, back extensions, or straight leg sit-ups do not."}, {"id": 576, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 66-year-old man has 2 out of 5 right quadriceps strength after a femoral nerve injury and his knee buckles whenever he loads the leg. He has full hip strength. Which gait compensation helps him keep the right knee from collapsing during early stance?", "options": {"A": "Leaning the trunk backward behind the hip axis at heel contact", "B": "Leaning the trunk forward so the force line passes in front of the knee", "C": "Leaning the trunk sideways toward the swinging limb during stance", "D": "Landing flat-footed with the knee deliberately flexed thirty degrees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Leaning the trunk forward so the force line passes in front of the knee): A forward trunk lean puts the ground reaction force line anterior to the knee axis, creating an extension moment that keeps the knee locked without quadriceps work. A posterior lean is the compensation for weak gluteus maximus, and a lateral lean is for abductor problems. Flexing the knee would raise the quadriceps demand and increase buckling."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A reference table lists tissue heating of 0.2 degrees C per minute for 1 MHz and 0.6 degrees C per minute for 3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 continuous. A chronic superficial tendon needs about a 4 degree C rise using 3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2. About how long will the heating take?", "options": {"A": "About 20 minutes, found by dividing 4 degrees by 0.2 degrees per minute", "B": "About 10 minutes, found by dividing 4 degrees by the 0.4 average of both rates", "C": "About 2.4 minutes, found by multiplying 4 degrees by 0.6 degrees per minute", "D": "About 7 minutes, found by dividing 4 degrees by 0.6 degrees per minute"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 7 minutes, found by dividing 4 degrees by 0.6 degrees per minute): Time equals target rise divided by rate: 4 degrees divided by 0.6 degrees per minute is 6.7, about 7 minutes. Using the 1 MHz rate of 0.2 would give 20 minutes, the multiplication yields 2.4, and averaging the rates gives 10. The 3 MHz beam heats a superficial tendon about three times faster at the same intensity."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a venous leg ulcer needs sharp debridement lasting about 10 minutes, and a previous TENS session at tingling intensity gave only partial relief. The clinician wants rapid analgesia during the procedure itself, delivered around the wound edges. Which TENS mode and parameters best match this need?", "options": {"A": "Conventional mode: rate near 100 pps, short pulse, sensory intensity applied around the wound edges.", "B": "Brief intense mode: rate near 100 pps, long pulse, highest tolerable intensity for short periods.", "C": "Burst mode: bursts delivered about twice per second, intensity at the sensory threshold for 30 minutes.", "D": "Acupuncture-like mode: rate near 4 pps, long pulse, intensity producing rhythmic visible twitching for 30 minutes."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Brief intense mode: rate near 100 pps, long pulse, highest tolerable intensity for short periods.): Brief intense TENS uses a high rate with long pulses at the highest tolerable intensity for short periods, such as a 10 to 15 minute painful procedure. It produces rapid analgesia that can last through the procedure. Acupuncture-like TENS has a delayed onset of 20 to 30 minutes. Conventional and sensory-level burst settings are weaker than brief intense stimulation for acute procedural pain."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A physiotherapist advises four athletes about creatine monohydrate supplementation to raise muscle phosphocreatine. A marathoner, a 2000 m rower, a 100 m sprinter in a speed session and a golfer ask who benefits most. Which athlete's event demands best match the benefit of increased phosphocreatine stores?", "options": {"A": "The golfer walking the course at low intensity for four hours", "B": "The sprinter performing six all-out 10-second accelerations with brief rests", "C": "The rower whose 2000 m race lasts roughly six to seven minutes", "D": "The marathoner holding a steady aerobic pace for over three hours"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The sprinter performing six all-out 10-second accelerations with brief rests): Creatine supplementation raises phosphocreatine stores, which helps repeated efforts of maximal power lasting about 10 seconds with short recovery. Marathon running and golf rely on oxidative metabolism and gain little. A 2000 m rowing race is mostly aerobic, so the gain is smaller than for repeated all-out sprints."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 28-year-old man with type 1 diabetes completed a 90-minute afternoon cycling session with a stable glucose of 130 mg/dL at the end. He plans to sleep in three hours and asks what to watch for tonight. Which advice best addresses his main risk after prolonged exercise?", "options": {"A": "Check glucose within one hour of finishing the ride and then resume normal routine", "B": "Rely on the normal end-of-ride reading and skip further checks before sleeping", "C": "Check glucose at bedtime and again overnight, and treat or adjust intake as needed", "D": "Expect glucose to rise overnight from stress hormones and add extra rapid-acting insulin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Check glucose at bedtime and again overnight, and treat or adjust intake as needed): Prolonged exercise depletes muscle and liver glycogen, and the body replenishes it for hours afterward, increasing insulin sensitivity and the risk of hypoglycemia that night. Checking at bedtime and overnight, with carbohydrate or intake adjustments as needed, addresses that risk. Adding insulin would worsen it. A normal reading at the end does not rule out a later drop."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A physical therapy student measures passive straight leg raise on a supine 22-year-old with a flat opposite leg, a stabilized pelvis, and the knee held in full extension. The student is asked to state the benchmark for normal hamstring length in Kendall's muscle length testing. Which range of hip flexion is considered normal?", "options": {"A": "About 100 degrees of hip flexion before the pelvis begins to rotate", "B": "About 80 degrees of hip flexion before the pelvis begins to rotate", "C": "About 60 degrees of hip flexion before the pelvis begins to rotate", "D": "About 120 degrees of hip flexion before the pelvis begins to rotate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 80 degrees of hip flexion before the pelvis begins to rotate): Kendall's norm for hamstring length is about 80 degrees of hip flexion with the knee extended and the pelvis kept still. Less than that suggests shortness. The 120 degree figure belongs to passive hip flexion with the knee bent, which is not limited by the hamstrings."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 46-year-old patient wants a simple intensity check without monitoring equipment. While walking briskly at her chosen pace, she can speak in full sentences but cannot sing a song comfortably. The clinician applies the talk test as described in ACSM guidance. Which intensity category does her response indicate for this walking session?", "options": {"A": "Moderate intensity, the zone recommended for most beginning aerobic programs", "B": "Maximal intensity, the zone reached at volitional exhaustion during a graded exercise test", "C": "Vigorous intensity, the zone where only a few words fit between breaths", "D": "Light intensity, the zone used for warm up and recovery periods"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Moderate intensity, the zone recommended for most beginning aerobic programs): The talk test places moderate intensity where a person can talk but not sing, which fits this patient. Light intensity allows singing, and vigorous intensity limits speech to a few words between breaths. Maximal effort is far above her current level. The talk test is a practical check that correlates with moderate heart rate zones."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with plaque psoriasis has a measured MED of 2 minutes at 30 inches with the clinic's UVB lamp. The therapist wants to produce a first degree erythema, or E1, reaction at the same distance using the standard multiple of the MED for E1. Which exposure time should the therapist set?", "options": {"A": "Five minutes of exposure at 30 inches from the same lamp", "B": "Four minutes of exposure at 30 inches from the same lamp", "C": "Ten minutes of exposure at 30 inches from the same lamp", "D": "Twenty minutes of exposure at 30 inches from the same lamp"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Five minutes of exposure at 30 inches from the same lamp): E1 is produced by about 2.5 times the MED. With an MED of 2 minutes, 2 minutes times 2.5 equals 5 minutes. Ten minutes is the E2 dose at five times the MED, and twenty minutes is the E3 dose at ten times. Four minutes is a simple doubling and falls below the E1 multiple."}, {"id": 584, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic educator reviews the physics of radiant energy and asks how wavelength relates to the energy carried by each photon. Photon energy equals Planck's constant times frequency, and frequency is inversely related to wavelength. Which of the following radiations carries the most energy per photon?", "options": {"A": "Ultraviolet A radiation with a wavelength near 365 nanometers, the longest ultraviolet band", "B": "Near infrared radiation with a wavelength near 905 nanometers, a wavelength used by pulsed lasers", "C": "Visible red light with a wavelength near 660 nanometers, a wavelength used by some lasers", "D": "Ultraviolet C radiation with a wavelength near 254 nanometers"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ultraviolet C radiation with a wavelength near 254 nanometers): Because frequency rises as wavelength falls, the shortest wavelength carries the most energy per photon. Of the listed radiations the 254 nanometer UVC is the shortest. UVA at 365 nanometers carries less, red light at 660 less again, and near infrared at 905 the least. Longer wavelengths penetrate more deeply, but depth is not the same as photon energy."}, {"id": 585, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old soccer player has had three hamstring strains in two seasons, each during the late swing phase of sprinting when the hamstrings work while being lengthened. He is otherwise healthy and returned to play each time after rest and stretching. Which training emphasis best reduces recurrence risk?", "options": {"A": "Isometric hamstring holds at full knee extension for ten seconds", "B": "Static hamstring stretching held for sixty seconds before every sprint", "C": "Eccentric hamstring strengthening such as Nordic hamstring curls", "D": "Concentric hamstring curls at light load for high repetitions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eccentric hamstring strengthening such as Nordic hamstring curls): Hamstring strains in late swing occur while the muscle is lengthening under load, so eccentric strengthening such as Nordic curls matches the injury mechanism and reduces recurrence. Light concentric curls do not load the lengthening action. Static stretching does not build eccentric strength, and brief isometric holds at one angle do not carry over to the swing phase."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A cardiology clinic uses a standardized treadmill exercise test for an adult patient, raising the workload at set intervals until the patient reaches exhaustion. A chiropractic student shadowing the clinic asks how the stages are structured. In the Bruce protocol, how long does each stage last and what changes between stages?", "options": {"A": "Each stage lasts two minutes, then only the treadmill speed increases", "B": "Each stage lasts five minutes, then both speed and grade increase", "C": "Each stage lasts three minutes, then both speed and grade increase", "D": "Each stage lasts one minute, then only the treadmill grade increases"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Each stage lasts three minutes, then both speed and grade increase): The Bruce protocol uses three minute stages, and each new stage raises both speed and incline, which makes the workload jump relatively steeply. One minute and two minute stages belong to other ramp or modified protocols. Five minute stages resemble steady state cycle tests. Changing only speed or only grade does not describe the Bruce design."}, {"id": 587, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 17-year-old football player performs repeated 10-second maximal sprints during a speed session. His coach wants almost full restoration of phosphocreatine between sprints so that power output stays high on each repetition. The physiotherapist advises on the rest interval. Which recovery period best restores most phosphocreatine stores between efforts?", "options": {"A": "About 20 seconds of light jogging between each maximal sprint effort", "B": "About 10 seconds of slow walking between each maximal sprint effort", "C": "About 45 seconds of rest between each maximal sprint effort", "D": "About 3 minutes of passive rest between each maximal sprint effort"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 3 minutes of passive rest between each maximal sprint effort): Phosphocreatine resynthesis has a half-time of about 30 seconds, so recovery is roughly 50 percent at 30 seconds, 75 percent at 60 seconds and nearly complete at 3 minutes. Passive rest restores it fastest because resynthesis is oxygen dependent. Intervals of 10 to 45 seconds leave phosphocreatine only partly restored and power output declines on later sprints."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "During a spray and stretch session for a stiff upper trapezius, the student sprays vapocoolant in circles directly over the trigger point and then lets the patient rest. The supervisor corrects the technique and asks for the standard method. How is the vapocoolant properly applied?", "options": {"A": "In parallel sweeps toward the referral zone while the muscle is passively stretched", "B": "In sweeps across the fibers before the stretch, with the muscle left slack", "C": "In circles over the trigger point, followed by stretching after the skin warms", "D": "As a continuous spray held in one spot until the skin turns white"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (In parallel sweeps toward the referral zone while the muscle is passively stretched): The vapocoolant is applied in slow, parallel sweeps in the direction of the muscle fibers and toward the referral zone while the muscle is stretched gently, so that the cold distracts from stretch discomfort. Circular spraying or holding in one spot does not link the stimulus with the stretch and can chill the skin excessively. Spraying a slack muscle gives no stretch benefit."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 55-year-old woman after a stroke has right quadriceps strength of 4 plus out of 5. In midstance the knee snaps back into hyperextension, the heel stays off the floor, and passive ankle dorsiflexion is limited by tone. What is the most likely cause?", "options": {"A": "Hamstring tightness pulling the knee into extension during stance", "B": "Quadriceps weakness allowing the knee to buckle backward in stance", "C": "Gluteus maximus weakness shifting the force line behind the knee", "D": "Spastic plantarflexors blocking tibial advance of the shank"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Spastic plantarflexors blocking tibial advance of the shank): When the plantarflexors are spastic or short, the tibia cannot advance over the planted foot, so the force line pushes the knee back into hyperextension. The quadriceps are strong at 4 plus out of 5, tight hamstrings would tend to flex the knee, and gluteus maximus weakness produces a trunk thrust rather than knee recurvatum."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old with recurrent low back pain performs prone hip extension. The clinician observes the hamstrings contract first, followed by lumbar erector spinae on the same side, with the gluteus maximus firing late and weakly. Which cue and exercise best correct the pattern?", "options": {"A": "Prone extension lifts of the trunk and legs to strengthen the erector spinae", "B": "Heavy hamstring curls to fatigue to raise hamstring strength before any glute work", "C": "Isolated glute squeezes before the lift, then bridges or quadruped hip extension with the knee bent", "D": "Stretching the gluteus maximus in a deep seated figure-four position held for several minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Isolated glute squeezes before the lift, then bridges or quadruped hip extension with the knee bent): In Janda's description the gluteus maximus should fire first, and a late glute with dominant hamstrings and erectors marks substitution. Retraining begins with isolated activation and then low load patterns such as bridges and quadruped extension with the knee bent to reduce hamstring involvement. Strengthening the dominant muscles or stretching the weak glute reinforces the fault."}, {"id": 591, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old woman at 24 weeks of pregnancy has sacroiliac pain. The chiropractor considers interferential current with four-pole placement across the low back and pelvis because home exercises gave limited relief. She has no other contraindication and no skin problems. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Avoid electrodes across the abdomen, low back and pelvis during pregnancy and use another approach", "B": "Proceed with a beat above 100 Hz to avoid uterine muscle stimulation", "C": "Proceed with electrodes at sensory intensity, since amniotic fluid protects the fetus from the current", "D": "Proceed with premodulated mode confined to two electrodes over the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid electrodes across the abdomen, low back and pelvis during pregnancy and use another approach): Electrical stimulation is avoided over the abdomen, lumbar region and pelvis in pregnancy because the effect on the fetus and uterus is not established. Sensory intensity, a single circuit, or a high beat does not remove that concern. Another approach should be chosen for the sacroiliac pain."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic is converting a spare room for a 12 W class 4 laser. The room contains a stainless steel instrument tray, a wall mirror, and a glossy metal exam table. The practice owner asks which preparation step best addresses the specific hazard created by these items during emission. Which step is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Require goggles for the patient alone because reflection concerns the treated tissue", "B": "Lower the room lighting so the reflected beam becomes visible and easier to avoid", "C": "Increase probe distance to one meter so reflected light spreads before reaching staff", "D": "Remove or cover reflective surfaces and keep the beam from striking them"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Remove or cover reflective surfaces and keep the beam from striking them): Shiny surfaces can redirect a class 4 beam into eyes elsewhere in the room, so they are removed or covered and the beam is kept off them. Dimming the lights dilates pupils and increases retinal exposure. Eyewear is required for everyone present, and a class 4 beam stays hazardous at a meter."}, {"id": 593, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with symptomatic medial knee osteoarthritis weighs 212 pounds and has been advised to lose 15 pounds. Using the commonly cited estimate that each pound of body weight lost removes about four pounds of load across the knee with every step, how much knee load per step does this weight loss remove?", "options": {"A": "About 30 pounds less compressive load across the knee with each step", "B": "About 120 pounds less compressive load across the knee with each step", "C": "About 60 pounds less compressive load across the knee with each step", "D": "About 15 pounds less compressive load across the knee with each step"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 60 pounds less compressive load across the knee with each step): The estimate is a four pound reduction in knee load per step for each pound lost, so 15 pounds multiplied by 4 equals 60 pounds per step, and a recheck gives 4 times 15 equals 60 again. Over thousands of steps a day this is a large cumulative reduction, which is why weight loss is a core part of knee osteoarthritis care. The other values come from using the wrong multiplier."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 17 year old with acne has an MED test using patches exposed for 20, 40, 60, 80 and 100 seconds. At the 24 hour review none of the five patches shows any redness. The therapist wants to begin treatment today. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Set the MED at 100 seconds and begin treatment at that exposure", "B": "Repeat the test with longer exposures until minimal erythema appears", "C": "Begin treatment at 250 seconds, 2.5 times the longest tested exposure", "D": "Begin treatment at 50 seconds, the midpoint of the tested range"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the test with longer exposures until minimal erythema appears): The MED is the shortest exposure that produces minimal redness, and none of these patches did. The true MED is therefore above 100 seconds and still unknown. The test should be repeated with longer exposures. Using 100 seconds, a midpoint, or a multiple of a non-reddening patch all rest on a value that was never measured."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician wants to deliver 1.5 W/cm2 with a sound head whose ERA is 3 cm2. The unit's controls are set in watts. The display currently reads 6 watts, which would exceed the intended intensity. What power setting should the clinician use?", "options": {"A": "9.0 watts, found by multiplying 6 watts by the 1.5 W/cm2 intensity", "B": "1.5 watts, found by keeping the intensity value as the power setting", "C": "2.0 watts, found by dividing 6 watts by the 3 cm2 ERA", "D": "4.5 watts, found by multiplying 1.5 W/cm2 by the 3 cm2 ERA"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4.5 watts, found by multiplying 1.5 W/cm2 by the 3 cm2 ERA): Power equals intensity times ERA, so 1.5 W/cm2 times 3 cm2 equals 4.5 W. The current 6 W over 3 cm2 gives 2.0 W/cm2, which is higher than intended. Check: 4.5 W divided by 3 cm2 returns 1.5 W/cm2."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 26-year-old begins a hard interval session and within seconds his heart rate and blood glucose both rise. His physiotherapist attributes part of the fuel mobilization to a catecholamine from the adrenal medulla. Which action of epinephrine contributes most directly to fuel availability during this session?", "options": {"A": "Stimulating glucagon release from the pancreas to raise blood glucose through hepatic gluconeogenesis", "B": "Activating glycogenolysis in liver and muscle plus hormone-sensitive lipase in fat", "C": "Increasing cortisol release to mobilize amino acids for gluconeogenesis within the first seconds", "D": "Activating glycogen synthase in liver and lipoprotein lipase in adipose tissue to supply fatty acids"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Activating glycogenolysis in liver and muscle plus hormone-sensitive lipase in fat): Epinephrine acts directly and quickly by activating glycogenolysis in liver and muscle and hormone-sensitive lipase in fat, raising fuel availability within seconds. Glucagon and cortisol act through other routes or more slowly, and glycogen synthase and lipoprotein lipase favor storage rather than mobilization."}, {"id": 597, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 30-year-old lifter performs a hip hinge while holding a dowel along the spine. The dowel loses contact with the sacrum as the lumbar spine rounds before the hips travel backward, and no pain is reported. Which interpretation and corrective drill fit best?", "options": {"A": "Excess thoracic extension causes the fault, so add thoracic flexion mobility drills", "B": "Lumbar flexion substitutes for hip flexion, so practice a dowel hinge with a neutral spine", "C": "Tight hip flexors restrict the hinge, so perform prolonged lunge stretches before each set", "D": "Weak rectus abdominis causes the rounding, so add high repetition crunches before lifting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lumbar flexion substitutes for hip flexion, so practice a dowel hinge with a neutral spine): A dowel that leaves the sacrum shows the lumbar spine flexing first, a movement pattern error in which spinal motion replaces hip motion. Motor practice of the hinge with a neutral spine addresses it directly. Hip flexor stretching, crunches and thoracic flexion do not train the hip dominant pattern."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A therapist is explaining midstance to a student, the phase when the tibia advances forward over the planted foot while the other limb swings through. Which muscle group controls the forward progress of the tibia over the foot during this period?", "options": {"A": "Soleus and gastrocnemius contracting eccentrically to slow the advancing tibia", "B": "Tibialis anterior contracting eccentrically to lower the forefoot to the floor", "C": "Quadriceps contracting eccentrically to control knee flexion as body weight loads the limb", "D": "Gluteus medius contracting isometrically to hold the pelvis level over the stance limb"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Soleus and gastrocnemius contracting eccentrically to slow the advancing tibia): In midstance the tibia advances over the fixed foot while the soleus and gastrocnemius lengthen under tension to decelerate it, which delays heel rise. The tibialis anterior lowers the forefoot earlier, in loading response. Eccentric quadriceps work controls knee flexion at loading, not tibial progression. The gluteus medius does hold the pelvis level in stance, but that is a different task from controlling the tibia."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "An instructor demonstrates a muscle fiber testing activity on a patient's biceps and explains that the muscle can generate its greatest active tension at one particular length. According to the length-tension relationship, at which muscle length is maximal active tension produced?", "options": {"A": "Near resting length, where actin and myosin filament overlap is optimal", "B": "When fully shortened, because the filaments have the greatest cross bridge contact", "C": "At one half of resting length, where the thick filaments are compressed against the Z lines", "D": "When fully stretched, because passive elastic elements add the most force"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Near resting length, where actin and myosin filament overlap is optimal): Active tension is greatest near resting length because the thick and thin filaments overlap so the most cross bridges can form. When fully stretched the overlap is poor, and passive elastic tension is not active force. When very short, filaments collide and tension falls. Both extremes reduce active force."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old woman at 28 weeks of pregnancy has neck and upper trapezius pain from poor desk posture. Her obstetrician has no objection to physical therapy. The clinician considers TENS for her neck pain and must decide how to proceed. Which approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Apply conventional TENS over the lumbar paraspinals to block pain signals from the neck.", "B": "Apply TENS to the neck with electrodes crossing over the upper thoracic spine and abdomen.", "C": "Conventional TENS to the neck region, keeping electrodes away from the abdomen and lumbar area.", "D": "Withhold all electrical stimulation for the pregnancy and use only thermal modalities."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Conventional TENS to the neck region, keeping electrodes away from the abdomen and lumbar area.): Electrical stimulation is avoided over the uterus and generally over the lumbar and pelvic regions, but neck pain can be treated with TENS placed on the cervical area. Lumbar placement does not suit neck pain. Electrodes spanning toward the abdomen risk current near the uterus. A blanket ban on electrical agents is not required."}, {"id": 601, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old runner has completed double-leg wobble board standing for 60 seconds with good control and no pain, and she has no ankle history. The therapist wants to progress the exercise without a large jump in difficulty. Which is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Single-leg stance on the board with eyes closed and head turns", "B": "Seated board tilts on a stability chair with the feet unsupported", "C": "Single-leg stance on the board with eyes open, rail nearby", "D": "Double-leg standing on a larger board for ten continuous minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Single-leg stance on the board with eyes open, rail nearby): A safe progression changes a single variable, here narrowing the base from two legs to one while keeping vision and a safety rail. Combining single-leg stance with eye closure and head turns changes three variables at once. Longer holding time or a seated task does not add a meaningful new balance demand."}, {"id": 602, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 63-year-old man with stable chronic heart failure and a reduced ejection fraction is cleared to start a monitored walking program. He takes a beta blocker and his heart rate response is unreliable. Which target range on the Borg 6 to 20 scale is an appropriate intensity for him during the early training phase?", "options": {"A": "Between 17 and 19, spanning very hard to extremely hard effort for peak adaptation", "B": "Between 15 and 17, spanning hard to very hard effort for greater cardiac stimulus", "C": "Between 6 and 9, spanning no exertion to very light effort throughout the session", "D": "Between 11 and 14, spanning light to somewhat hard effort"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Between 11 and 14, spanning light to somewhat hard effort): Patients with stable heart failure train at light to moderate effort, roughly 11 to 14 on the 6 to 20 scale, which suits a blunted heart rate response. Ratings of 15 to 17 impose more cardiac load than early training should. Ratings of 17 to 19 approach maximal effort. Ratings of 6 to 9 are too light to produce a training effect."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 47-year-old landscaper has chronic stiffness in the posterior thigh muscles with a tissue depth near 3 to 4 cm. The goal is a significant temperature rise to improve tissue extensibility before stretching. Which ultrasound setting is most likely to produce a meaningful thermal effect in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Pulsed output at 20 percent duty cycle, 3 MHz, 0.5 W/cm2 peak, head kept stationary", "B": "Pulsed output at 20 percent duty cycle, 1 MHz, 1.5 W/cm2 peak, head kept moving", "C": "Continuous output at 1 MHz, about 1.5 W/cm2, with the head kept moving", "D": "Continuous output at 1 MHz, 0.1 W/cm2, with the head kept moving over the field"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continuous output at 1 MHz, about 1.5 W/cm2, with the head kept moving): Significant heating needs a continuous output so that energy is delivered all of the time, with enough intensity (about 1.0 to 2.0 W/cm2 at 1 MHz) and a frequency that reaches the depth. A 20 percent duty cycle cuts the average intensity to one fifth of the peak, which keeps temperature rise small. An intensity of 0.1 W/cm2 is also far below the thermal range."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A student tests gluteus medius strength in side-lying with the examiner resisting at the ankle. The patient flexes the hip and rolls the pelvis forward as she lifts the leg, using a different muscle to perform the movement. Which positioning correction best isolates the gluteus medius and limits that substitution?", "options": {"A": "Keep the hip in slight extension and avoid internal rotation while abducting", "B": "Roll the pelvis further forward so the gluteus medius fibers are placed on stretch", "C": "Position the patient supine and abduct with the hip flexed against knee resistance", "D": "Place the resistance at the thigh instead of the ankle to reduce force"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Keep the hip in slight extension and avoid internal rotation while abducting): Keeping the hip in slight extension without internal rotation shifts work from the tensor fasciae latae to the gluteus medius. Rolling the pelvis forward is the substitution itself. Hip flexion during abduction favors the tensor fasciae latae. Moving resistance to the thigh changes the force but leaves the substituting position unchanged."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 46-year-old man has a stable cervical strain with facet injury after a fall, and the physician wants moderate restriction of flexion and extension while allowing him to be mobile. A soft foam collar would allow too much movement for this need. Which device best meets this requirement?", "options": {"A": "A two-piece Philadelphia-type collar with rigid front and back supports", "B": "A soft foam collar worn day and night with a second layer of foam padding added", "C": "A halo vest with four skull pins fixed to a rigid thoracic jacket", "D": "A cervical traction harness with a home weight bag worn through the day"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A two-piece Philadelphia-type collar with rigid front and back supports): A Philadelphia-type collar uses a rigid front and back with chin and occiput support and restricts flexion and extension much more than foam while remaining wearable. Extra padding does not make a soft collar restrictive. A halo vest is for unstable injuries and is far more restriction than a stable strain needs. A traction harness delivers a pull and is not a motion limiting orthosis."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with right hip pain leans her trunk over the right hip in right stance, and the pain eases with the lean. Hip abduction strength is 5 out of 5 on both sides, and the Trendelenburg test is negative. Which explanation of the lean is best supported?", "options": {"A": "A functional short leg pattern from a structural leg length difference", "B": "A gluteus maximus gait from weakness of the right hip extensors", "C": "A compensated Trendelenburg gait from weakness of the right gluteus medius", "D": "Antalgic lean to unload the joint, with normal abductor strength"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antalgic lean to unload the joint, with normal abductor strength): Both an antalgic lean and a compensated Trendelenburg lean move the trunk over the stance hip, but they differ in cause. The abductors test 5 out of 5 and the Trendelenburg test is negative, so weakness does not explain it, while relief of pain with the lean points to joint protection. Extensor weakness produces a backward trunk thrust instead."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 31-year-old with documented cold urticaria has an upper trapezius trigger point and a neck that rotates poorly. The therapist usually uses spray and stretch, but the patient reports hives after previous cold exposure. The skin over the point is intact. Which alternative is best?", "options": {"A": "Spray and stretch after applying a moist heat pack to warm the area first", "B": "Manual ischemic compression combined with passive stretching, without vapocoolant", "C": "Ice massage over the point for a few seconds, then immediate passive stretch", "D": "Spray and stretch with the vapocoolant held farther away for a briefer application"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Manual ischemic compression combined with passive stretching, without vapocoolant): Cold urticaria makes any cold exposure risky, so manual ischemic compression with passive stretching avoids vapocoolant altogether. Warming first does not stop the cold spray from triggering hives. Ice massage is direct cold, and a briefer spray from farther away still applies cold to the skin."}, {"id": 608, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 62-year-old man completes a submaximal exercise test, and the laboratory reports his peak oxygen uptake as 28 mL/kg/min. The clinician wants to express his capacity in metabolic equivalents so that it can be compared with the energy cost of daily tasks around his home. How many METs does this value represent?", "options": {"A": "Approximately 6 METs, from 28 divided by 4.5, allowing for age-related resting changes", "B": "Approximately 7 METs, from 28 divided by 4.0", "C": "Approximately 8 METs, from 28 divided by 3.5", "D": "Approximately 28 METs, since laboratory values are already reported in METs"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Approximately 8 METs, from 28 divided by 3.5): One MET equals 3.5 mL of oxygen per kilogram per minute, so 28 divided by 3.5 gives 8 METs. The result is not converted automatically, because mL/kg/min is a different unit. Using 4.0 or 4.5 as a divisor ignores the standard resting value and understates capacity. Age does not change the defined MET constant."}, {"id": 609, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with acne received a routine UVB session. Afterward she developed painful erythema with edema and began peeling. The therapist finds the lamp had been moved from 30 inches to 15 inches without changing the exposure time. Which change in intensity at the skin most likely explains this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Intensity at the skin increased about twofold at the closer lamp distance", "B": "Intensity at the skin increased about fourfold at the closer distance", "C": "Intensity at the skin increased about eightfold at the closer distance", "D": "Intensity at the skin stayed the same, and only the beam angle changed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intensity at the skin increased about fourfold at the closer distance): Halving the distance multiplies intensity by two squared, which equals four. With unchanged time the dose rose fourfold, which is enough to turn a routine exposure into a second degree reaction. A linear estimate of twofold would understate the change, and eightfold overstates it. The beam angle was not described as altered."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats a 38 year old cyclist's patellar tendon with a continuous 810 nm probe set to 100 mW. The probe spot area is 1 cm2 and the exposure at each point lasts 60 seconds. The treatment protocol states dose in J/cm2. What dose does each point receive?", "options": {"A": "0.1 J/cm2, from 0.1 W for 1 s over 1 cm2", "B": "6 J/cm2, from 0.1 W for 60 s over 1 cm2", "C": "60 J/cm2, from 1 W for 60 s over 1 cm2", "D": "1.67 J/cm2, from 100 mW divided by 60 s over 1 cm2"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (6 J/cm2, from 0.1 W for 60 s over 1 cm2): Energy is 0.1 W times 60 s, which gives 6 J. Dividing 6 J by the 1 cm2 spot gives 6 J/cm2. Reading the output as 1 W inflates the result tenfold, dividing by time gives the wrong operation, and ignoring the 60 seconds leaves only a tiny value."}, {"id": 611, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 60-year-old man has lost hip extension in terminal stance on the right, with an increased lumbar lordosis and anterior pelvic tilt, and the left step length is shortened. The Thomas test shows the right thigh rising off the table. What is the best explanation?", "options": {"A": "Right hamstring tightness limiting knee extension in terminal swing", "B": "Right gluteus maximus weakness limiting extension at initial contact", "C": "Right hip flexor tightness limiting hip extension in terminal stance", "D": "Right gastrocnemius tightness limiting heel rise in terminal stance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right hip flexor tightness limiting hip extension in terminal stance): A positive Thomas test shows a shortened hip flexor group, which stops the thigh from extending behind the trunk in terminal stance, so the pelvis tilts forward, the lordosis increases, and the opposite step shortens. Gastrocnemius or hamstring tightness would not lift the thigh in the Thomas test. Gluteus maximus weakness causes a trunk thrust."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student calculates how skin capacitive reactance changes between a 50 Hz current and a 4000 Hz interferential carrier, using the fact that reactance is inversely proportional to frequency. Assuming skin capacitance remains constant, how does impedance at 4000 Hz compare with impedance at 50 Hz?", "options": {"A": "About equal at both frequencies, since the skin capacitance has not changed", "B": "About 4 times lower at 4000 Hz, since the frequency rises fourfold", "C": "About 80 times higher at 4000 Hz, since the frequency is 80 times higher", "D": "About 80 times lower at 4000 Hz, since the frequency is 80 times higher"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 80 times lower at 4000 Hz, since the frequency is 80 times higher): Reactance equals 1 divided by 2 pi times frequency times capacitance. Since 4000 divided by 50 equals 80, and capacitance is unchanged, reactance at 4000 Hz is one eightieth of that at 50 Hz. That 80-fold drop explains why medium-frequency current passes through skin so readily. A direct relationship or equal impedance would contradict the formula."}, {"id": 613, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 39-year-old machinist has a swollen, painful hand with irregular bony contours, so the sound head cannot maintain flat gel contact on the surface during treatment. The clinician decides on a water immersion method. Which description correctly describes the underwater technique?", "options": {"A": "Immerse hand and head in degassed water, holding the head a short distance from skin", "B": "Place the head in contact with the skin underwater, pressing firmly against the bony contours", "C": "Immerse both in water and hold the head tilted at about 45 degrees to the skin", "D": "Immerse the hand but keep the head out of the water, using gel on its face"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Immerse hand and head in degassed water, holding the head a short distance from skin): In the immersion method the water is the coupling medium, so the head stays 1 to 2 cm from the skin, perpendicular to the surface. Degassed water is used to avoid bubbles that scatter the beam. The head should not press on the part, and tilting it causes reflection."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A muscle biopsy from a 26-year-old man after 12 weeks of heavy resistance training shows fewer type IIx fibers and more type IIa fibers than at baseline, while the percentage of type I fibers is unchanged. His physiotherapist is asked what this indicates. Which interpretation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Loss of fast-twitch fibers from damage, replaced by newly formed type I fibers", "B": "Conversion of type II fibers into type I fibers, which is the usual result of lifting", "C": "Hypertrophy of type IIx fibers, because their area rose while the count stayed the same", "D": "A transition toward fatigue-resistant, more oxidative type IIa fibers within the fast-twitch group"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A transition toward fatigue-resistant, more oxidative type IIa fibers within the fast-twitch group): Resistance training commonly shifts type IIx fibers toward type IIa, which have greater oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance, while the type I percentage changes little. Conversion between type I and type II is rare with resistance training alone. The biopsy shows fewer type IIx fibers, so growth of that type is not what happened. Muscle damage does not explain the pattern."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A patient stands on the right leg only for 30 seconds while a therapist watches from behind. The left side of the pelvis drops below the level of the right side, and the patient cannot hold the pelvis level. Which muscle weakness best explains this positive Trendelenburg sign?", "options": {"A": "Left gluteus medius, the hip abductor of the swinging limb", "B": "Right gluteus maximus, the hip extensor of the supporting limb", "C": "Right hip adductors, which hold the pelvis level from below", "D": "Right gluteus medius, the hip abductor of the supporting limb"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Right gluteus medius, the hip abductor of the supporting limb): In single limb stance the abductors of the supporting hip hold the pelvis level against the weight of the swinging side. When the right gluteus medius is weak, the left pelvis drops. The left abductors are not working in this task, and gluteus maximus acts mainly in the sagittal plane."}, {"id": 616, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with good strength wants to improve her ability to rise quickly from a chair and catch herself when she stumbles. Her therapist decides to emphasize muscle power instead of maximal strength or size. Which prescription best develops muscle power?", "options": {"A": "Heavy loads near ninety percent of 1RM lifted slowly for two repetitions", "B": "Light to moderate loads lifted as fast as possible with controlled lowering", "C": "Very light loads lifted for thirty repetitions with minimal rest between sets", "D": "Moderate loads lifted slowly for eight to twelve repetitions to volitional fatigue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Light to moderate loads lifted as fast as possible with controlled lowering): Power is force times velocity, so power training moves a light to moderate load with maximal intended speed. Slow heavy lifts develop maximal strength, slow moderate sets to fatigue develop hypertrophy, and thirty repetitions with brief rest develop endurance. None of these emphasize the speed of movement she needs for the chair rise."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 66-year-old man had a left middle cerebral artery stroke. His right arm is held flexed against his chest, and in swing the right leg stays stiff, swings out in an arc, and the foot scrapes the floor. Which gait classification fits best?", "options": {"A": "Steppage gait, with exaggerated knee lift and a slapping foot", "B": "Hemiplegic gait, with a circumducting stiff leg and a flexed upper limb", "C": "Spastic diplegic gait, with scissoring and both knees crossing", "D": "Parkinsonian gait, with a flexed posture and short shuffling steps"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hemiplegic gait, with a circumducting stiff leg and a flexed upper limb): Unilateral spasticity after a middle cerebral artery stroke leaves the arm flexed and the leg extended, so the patient circumducts the leg to clear the floor. Steppage gait produces high knee lift to clear a dropped foot but has no arm posture. Spastic diplegia affects both legs with scissoring, and parkinsonian gait features short shuffling steps and reduced arm swing without circumduction."}, {"id": 618, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A patient beginning an aerobic program asks what happens to circulating insulin and glucagon while he walks briskly for 45 minutes. The physiotherapist wants him to understand how blood glucose is kept stable when the muscles are using more fuel. Which hormonal pattern occurs during moderate prolonged exercise in a healthy adult?", "options": {"A": "Insulin falls while glucagon rises, favoring liver glucose release", "B": "Both insulin and glucagon rise, matching glucose uptake to the higher demand", "C": "Both insulin and glucagon fall, so liver glucose output is suppressed", "D": "Insulin rises while glucagon falls, driving glucose into the liver for storage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Insulin falls while glucagon rises, favoring liver glucose release): During sustained exercise insulin secretion is suppressed while glucagon, epinephrine and cortisol rise, which increases liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis and lipolysis. This keeps blood glucose stable while muscle uptake rises through contraction-driven pathways. The fed-state pattern and the both-fall or both-rise patterns do not match the physiologic response."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 50-year-old man completes 6 weeks of leg extension training. Surface EMG now shows less hamstring activity during maximal knee extension than at baseline, while quadriceps activation is unchanged. His isometric knee extension torque has risen. Which neural adaptation best explains the increased torque?", "options": {"A": "Reduced antagonist coactivation, so less opposing hamstring torque works against the quadriceps during extension", "B": "Increased muscle spindle gain, which amplifies reflex drive to the quadriceps during contraction", "C": "Greater hamstring coactivation, which stabilizes the knee and allows higher quadriceps torque output", "D": "Increased Golgi tendon organ sensitivity, which removes inhibition from the quadriceps motor neurons"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced antagonist coactivation, so less opposing hamstring torque works against the quadriceps during extension): The EMG shows the hamstrings, the antagonist, are working less against the quadriceps. Lower antagonist coactivation reduces opposing torque and raises net knee extension torque without any change in quadriceps drive. The finding is the opposite of greater coactivation, and higher Golgi tendon organ sensitivity would increase inhibition rather than remove it."}, {"id": 620, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has brief spinning vertigo lasting under a minute when she rolls over in bed or looks up at a shelf. A right Dix-Hallpike maneuver reproduces her symptoms with upbeating torsional nystagmus that fatigues after several seconds. Which procedure is the treatment that matches this finding?", "options": {"A": "Cawthorne style head and eye exercises to substitute for lost labyrinth function", "B": "The log roll maneuver, moving displaced debris out of the horizontal canal", "C": "Gaze stabilization exercises at one times viewing to retrain the vestibulo-ocular reflex", "D": "The Epley maneuver, moving displaced debris out of the posterior semicircular canal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Epley maneuver, moving displaced debris out of the posterior semicircular canal): Brief positional vertigo with upbeating torsional nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike points to debris in the posterior canal, and the Epley canalith repositioning maneuver is the matching treatment. The log roll is for horizontal canal involvement. Gaze stabilization and Cawthorne style exercises target unilateral or bilateral vestibular hypofunction, not loose debris."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During the Functional Movement Screen, a 31-year-old man's shoulder mobility reach looks excellent, but the shoulder impingement clearing exam provokes sharp pain at the front of the shoulder. How is this pattern scored, and what is the appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Score the pattern one and prescribe daily shoulder mobility drills for home use", "B": "Score the pattern zero and refer for clinical evaluation of the painful shoulder", "C": "Score the pattern three because the reach was excellent, and add corrective stretching", "D": "Score the pattern two and retest in four weeks after relative rest"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Score the pattern zero and refer for clinical evaluation of the painful shoulder): A positive clearing exam with pain scores zero on that pattern regardless of how the movement itself looks, and the pain source needs clinical evaluation. Scoring the reach alone would hide a painful structure. Drills and delayed retesting do not address the pain."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old drafter has tingling in the ring and small fingers that is worse when the elbow rests flexed on a desk, with a positive Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel. Resisted wrist flexion and pronation do not reproduce pain at the medial epicondyle. What intervention is best?", "options": {"A": "Eccentric wrist flexor and pronator loading to treat medial epicondylitis at the origin", "B": "Neutral wrist night splint with median nerve gliding and tendon gliding exercises", "C": "Elbow padding, avoiding prolonged flexion, and a night splint at mild elbow flexion", "D": "Counterforce strap placed over the flexor mass one inch distal to the medial epicondyle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Elbow padding, avoiding prolonged flexion, and a night splint at mild elbow flexion): Ring and small finger tingling with elbow flexion and a positive Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel indicates ulnar nerve irritation. Padding, avoiding prolonged flexion, and a night splint reduce traction and compression. The medial tendon tests are negative, and wrist splinting treats the median nerve."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist is teaching proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation diagonals to a chiropractic student and demonstrates the upper extremity D1 flexion pattern with the patient supine, starting with the arm down and out to the side. The student must name the shoulder motions that make up the completed D1 flexion pattern. Which combination is correct?", "options": {"A": "Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the arm down and away from the body", "B": "Shoulder flexion, abduction, and external rotation, finishing with the arm overhead and out to the side", "C": "Shoulder flexion, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the arm across the face", "D": "Shoulder flexion, adduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the hand across the opposite shoulder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shoulder flexion, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the arm across the face): D1 flexion moves the shoulder into flexion, adduction, and external rotation, so the hand finishes across the face. The overhead and out to the side finish belongs to D2 flexion, which uses abduction instead of adduction. Internal rotation does not occur in either flexion diagonal for the shoulder. The extension, abduction, and internal rotation combination is the opposite diagonal, D1 extension."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old paramedic with a history of disc-related back pain asks when to schedule his stabilization program. Considering McGill's discussion of disc hydration and tolerance to repeated spinal flexion, which advice about exercises such as full sit-ups and toe touches is best?", "options": {"A": "Perform repeated lumbar flexion late at night, when the discs are most hydrated", "B": "Avoid repeated lumbar flexion soon after rising, when the discs are most hydrated", "C": "Perform repeated lumbar flexion soon after rising, when the discs are most stiff", "D": "Avoid repeated lumbar flexion late at night, when the discs have lost the most height"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid repeated lumbar flexion soon after rising, when the discs are most hydrated): Discs are most hydrated after a night of rest, and McGill advises avoiding repeated spinal flexion in the first period after rising because the hydrated disc tolerates bending stress less well. Late in the day the discs have lost height and are stiffer. Training flexion in the morning is the opposite of the advice."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A physical therapist is teaching a patient with disc-related back pain the McGill curl-up. The patient lies supine, and the therapist must set a starting position that keeps lumbar load low and preserves the neutral curve while the head and shoulders lift only slightly. Which starting position matches the McGill curl-up description?", "options": {"A": "Both knees bent to ninety degrees, feet flat, and hands clasped behind the head", "B": "Both legs straight, toes pointed upward, and the arms crossed over the chest", "C": "One knee bent, the other leg straight, and hands clasped behind the head with elbows wide", "D": "One knee bent, the other leg straight, and both hands placed under the low back curve"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (One knee bent, the other leg straight, and both hands placed under the low back curve): The McGill curl-up starts with one knee bent and the other leg straight, with both hands under the low back curve so the neutral lumbar spine is preserved. Hands behind the head, crossed arms and both knees bent change the load or neck and lumbar posture. The hand placement under the lumbar curve is the specific feature."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 20-year-old college baseball pitcher is eight weeks into rehabilitation for rotator cuff tendinopathy. He has full pain-free range of motion and a normal shoulder at rest, and he asks to begin an interval throwing program. Which criterion should be met first before throwing begins?", "options": {"A": "A normal shoulder at rest and no pain on palpation of the supraspinatus tendon", "B": "Pain-free full motion and a pitching velocity within ten percent of his preinjury level", "C": "Full pain free motion and a negative impingement sign, with strength testing deferred until throwing", "D": "Pain-free full motion, strength near that of the opposite arm, and a normal scapular pattern"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pain-free full motion, strength near that of the opposite arm, and a normal scapular pattern): Interval throwing should start only when range is full and pain free, strength nears the other arm and scapular control is normal. A normal shoulder at rest and a nontender tendon are findings he already has, so they do not show readiness for load. Velocity near preinjury level is the end point of the throwing program, not a starting condition. Deferring strength testing risks loading a weak cuff and scapula."}, {"id": 627, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A dermatology referral asks the therapist about different ultraviolet bands. While explaining the choice to the patient, the therapist compares how deeply each band penetrates into the layers of the skin. Which ultraviolet band penetrates deepest, reaching the dermis, and is the longest wavelength band of the group?", "options": {"A": "Broadband UVB, at 290 to 320 nanometers, which reaches the dermis more than UVA does", "B": "Narrowband UVB at 311 nanometers, a band that passes through to the dermis", "C": "UVA, at 320 to 400 nanometers, the longest wavelength ultraviolet band", "D": "UVC, at 200 to 290 nanometers, the highest energy ultraviolet band"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (UVA, at 320 to 400 nanometers, the longest wavelength ultraviolet band): Penetration into skin increases with wavelength among the ultraviolet bands, so UVA at 320 to 400 nanometers reaches the dermis. UVB, including narrowband 311 nanometers, is absorbed mostly in the epidermis. UVC has the highest photon energy but is absorbed in the outermost layers of the epidermis. Energy per photon and depth of penetration are separate properties."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A clinician uses the ACSM level walking equation, in which VO2 in mL/kg/min equals 0.1 times speed in meters per minute plus 3.5. A 55-year-old patient walks at 80 meters per minute on a level treadmill belt with no handrail support. What is the estimated energy cost of this walk in METs?", "options": {"A": "About 3.3 METs, from 11.5 mL/kg/min divided by 3.5", "B": "About 2.3 METs, from 8.0 mL/kg/min divided by 3.5", "C": "About 5.6 METs, from 19.5 mL/kg/min divided by 3.5", "D": "About 11.5 METs, from reporting 11.5 mL/kg/min as METs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 3.3 METs, from 11.5 mL/kg/min divided by 3.5): The walking equation gives 0.1 times 80, which is 8.0, plus 3.5 for rest, a total of 11.5 mL/kg/min. Dividing by 3.5 yields about 3.3 METs. Omitting the resting 3.5 gives 2.3, and reporting 11.5 treats units as METs. The running coefficient of 0.2 would give 19.5 and 5.6 METs, which does not apply to walking."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has unilateral SI pain 6 months after delivery with a stiff, restricted left sacroiliac joint on motion palpation, no signs of pelvic instability, and good lumbopelvic muscle control. No red flags are present. Which treatment is most likely to help?", "options": {"A": "A rigid sacroiliac belt worn during all waking hours to restrict joint motion", "B": "Sacroiliac mobilization or muscle energy technique, then active exercise", "C": "Prolonged bed rest with the hips flexed to quiet the joint for several weeks", "D": "High-load unilateral squatting to force closure across the joint"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sacroiliac mobilization or muscle energy technique, then active exercise): A hypomobile sacroiliac joint with good muscle control calls for restoring motion through mobilization or muscle energy technique and then active movement. Belts and force closure training are for hypermobile or unstable joints. Restricting motion further, loading heavily, or resting for weeks would not restore the missing movement."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student is reviewing a list of intake cases before choosing therapeutic ultrasound. Each patient has chronic pain near the planned treatment field. Therapeutic ultrasound is considered contraindicated for one of these cases at the planned site. Which patient presentation represents a contraindication to ultrasound over the planned treatment field?", "options": {"A": "A patient with a healed tibial fracture and an intramedullary rod, treated at the shin", "B": "A pregnant patient whose planned field lies directly over the gravid uterus", "C": "A patient with a stainless steel plate at a healed wrist fracture, treated at the forearm", "D": "A patient with a total knee replacement with metal components, treated at the quadriceps"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A pregnant patient whose planned field lies directly over the gravid uterus): Ultrasound should not be directed over the gravid uterus because of fetal exposure risk. Metal implants such as rods, plates and joint components are not contraindications, because metal does not absorb the beam in the way that bone and collagen do, although the plastic or cement parts of an implant deserve care. The three metal cases are acceptable fields."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician reviews four adult patients to find the one for whom therapeutic ultrasound is best indicated at the planned site. All four have knee region complaints and are otherwise medically stable and healthy. Which patient is the best candidate for ultrasound?", "options": {"A": "A patient with a swollen, tender calf following a long car trip", "B": "A patient with a new knee effusion that is warm and rapidly increasing", "C": "A patient with a chronic knee flexion contracture from capsular tightness after immobilization", "D": "A patient with a knee near a known bone tumor causing night pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A patient with a chronic knee flexion contracture from capsular tightness after immobilization): Chronic capsular contracture is a classic indication, since heating dense collagen increases extensibility before stretching. An acute warm effusion is a poor candidate for heat. A swollen calf after travel raises concern for thrombosis, and a known tumor is a contraindication."}, {"id": 632, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 26-year-old runner lies supine with the opposite leg flat and the pelvis fixed. The clinician raises the right straight leg and the pelvis begins to tilt posteriorly at 60 degrees of hip flexion, with the knee held extended and no nerve symptoms. Using Kendall's norm of about 80 degrees, what is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Shortened right iliopsoas, limiting hip flexion by about 20 degrees", "B": "Normal hamstring length, because the pelvis moved before the leg reached 90 degrees", "C": "Shortened right hamstrings, limiting hip flexion by about 20 degrees", "D": "Weak right hip flexors, limiting the active range of motion to 60 degrees"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shortened right hamstrings, limiting hip flexion by about 20 degrees): The test is passive, and the pelvis starting to rotate at 60 degrees shows the hamstrings are limiting the motion roughly 20 degrees short of the 80 degree norm. The iliopsoas lengthens during hip flexion and does not limit it. Weak hip flexors would not matter because the examiner supplies the motion."}, {"id": 633, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 45 year old man falls asleep with an ice bag held against the lateral thigh by an elastic wrap, and the therapist finds the bag 50 minutes later. The skin beneath looks white and waxy, feels firm, and the patient cannot feel light touch there. What is the best interpretation?", "options": {"A": "Normal reactive hyperemia: resume cooling at the next scheduled session with the same pack", "B": "Superficial frostbite: stop the cold, rewarm gently, and arrange medical evaluation", "C": "Cold urticaria: give an antihistamine and continue with a shorter session", "D": "Expected analgesia: massage the skin vigorously to restore circulation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Superficial frostbite: stop the cold, rewarm gently, and arrange medical evaluation): White, waxy, firm, insensate skin after prolonged ice indicates freezing injury, so cold is stopped, the area is rewarmed gently, and medical evaluation is arranged. Reactive hyperemia is red and warm with normal sensation, and urticaria forms welts. Vigorous massage can further damage injured tissue."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "An 80-year-old woman on long-term oral steroids has paper-thin skin and a recently healed skin tear on the forearm. She needs shoulder support during rehabilitation and asks about tape. Her skin tore easily last year with a bandage. Which approach is best?", "options": {"A": "Use a hypoallergenic tape with minimal tension and remove it with great care", "B": "Apply tape directly over the healed tear to protect the scar", "C": "Use kinesiology tape at high tension for faster results in a short time", "D": "Use rigid zinc oxide tape directly on the skin for strong support"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a hypoallergenic tape with minimal tension and remove it with great care): Steroid-thinned skin tears easily with strong adhesive, so a gentle hypoallergenic product with minimal tension and careful removal is the safer choice. Zinc oxide tape and high-tension strips both increase skin trauma, and a recent tear should not be covered with adhesive. Skin inspection should be repeated at each visit while tape is in use."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 70 year old woman with type 2 diabetes and knee osteoarthritis reports morning stiffness. Monofilament and temperature testing show absent protective sensation over the anterior knee and shin. She requests a hot pack, and the therapist considers extra toweling as a safeguard. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Apply six layers of toweling and check skin every two minutes during treatment", "B": "Use a contrast bath at mild temperatures for one short supervised cycle", "C": "Use a cold pack instead, since cold causes fewer burns than heat on the knee", "D": "Withhold hot and cold packs on the insensate skin and use exercise instead"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold hot and cold packs on the insensate skin and use exercise instead): With absent protective sensation, the patient cannot report burning or excessive cold, so any thermal agent over that skin is unsafe. Extra toweling and frequent checks reduce but do not remove the risk. Cold carries its own injury risk on insensate skin, and a mild contrast bath still exposes it to temperature changes it cannot feel."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist wants to train the hip strategy in a 68-year-old client who has already mastered ankle strategy training on a firm floor. The client has good strength and no vestibular symptoms or recent falls. Which condition best elicits the hip strategy?", "options": {"A": "Standing on firm floor while a large pull moves the center of mass past the feet", "B": "Seated trunk perturbations on a ball with feet unsupported", "C": "Standing on a narrow beam or foam while receiving a quick moderate nudge at the shoulders", "D": "Standing on a firm floor with slow small sway"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Standing on a narrow beam or foam while receiving a quick moderate nudge at the shoulders): The hip strategy takes over when the ankle cannot control sway, as on a narrow or compliant surface, or when the perturbation is faster and larger than the ankle can handle. A slow small sway on firm floor favors the ankle strategy. A large pull that carries the center past the feet requires a step."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 39-year-old delivery driver has deep right buttock pain that increases after long drives. Palpation reproduces tenderness over the right piriformis, and straight-leg raise is normal. The clinician teaches a self-stretch lying down. Which technique stretches the right piriformis most effectively?", "options": {"A": "Lie prone and lift the right leg into hip extension with the knee straight", "B": "Lie supine and pull the right knee toward the left shoulder, flexing and adducting the hip", "C": "Lie supine and pull the right knee toward the right shoulder, flexing and abducting the hip", "D": "Lie on the left side and raise the right leg upward in hip abduction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lie supine and pull the right knee toward the left shoulder, flexing and adducting the hip): Flexing and adducting the hip, with the knee pulled toward the opposite shoulder, lengthens the piriformis. Pulling the knee toward the same shoulder adds abduction, which slackens it. Raising the leg in abduction shortens it. Prone hip extension stretches hip flexors, not the posterior hip rotators."}, {"id": 638, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 46-year-old keyboard user has deep aching over the proximal dorsal forearm, about four centimeters distal to the lateral epicondyle. Pain increases with resisted forearm supination, while the epicondyle itself is not tender, and resisted wrist extension is strong and painless. Which management is best?", "options": {"A": "Eccentric wrist extensor loading with a flexible bar to treat common extensor tendinopathy", "B": "A counterforce strap placed tightly over the lateral epicondyle origin", "C": "Radial nerve gliding and activity changes to reduce repeated forearm rotation near the supinator", "D": "Median nerve gliding with neutral wrist night splinting for carpal tunnel syndrome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Radial nerve gliding and activity changes to reduce repeated forearm rotation near the supinator): Tenderness several centimeters distal to the epicondyle with pain on resisted supination and normal wrist extension suggests radial nerve irritation at the supinator, so gliding and rotation avoidance fit. Eccentric extensor loading and the counterforce strap treat the common extensor origin, which is not tender. Median nerve care misses the radial nerve."}, {"id": 639, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "An 80-year-old man with diabetic neuropathy and early cataracts walks steadily in a bright hallway during the day but becomes unsteady in his dim bedroom at night when he goes to the bathroom. His strength is normal for his age. Which statement best explains this change?", "options": {"A": "Poor foot sensation shifts reliance to vision, which dim light degrades", "B": "Cataracts reduce vestibular function, which becomes worse in low light", "C": "Neuropathy increases his reliance on vestibular input, which dim light disturbs", "D": "Reduced vestibular input makes him depend on vision, which dim light improves"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Poor foot sensation shifts reliance to vision, which dim light degrades): Neuropathy degrades the feet and ankle signals, so the nervous system weights vision more heavily. Low light reduces the quality of visual input, which leaves too little reliable information for balance. Dim light does not act on the vestibular system, and cataracts affect vision rather than the labyrinth."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In prone, the clinician flexes a 31-year-old's knee passively to 90 degrees. At about 60 degrees the pelvis tilts anteriorly and the lumbar spine extends, and the patient reports low back pain. Which impairment fits best, and what is a sound corrective choice?", "options": {"A": "Short rectus femoris, so stretch it with the pelvis held posteriorly tilted", "B": "Weak quadriceps, so add leg extensions at high load for the knee", "C": "Short iliopsoas alone, so perform sustained lunge stretches without lumbar control", "D": "Short hamstrings pulling the pelvis, so stretch the hamstrings in a long sitting position"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Short rectus femoris, so stretch it with the pelvis held posteriorly tilted): Knee flexion in prone stretches the rectus femoris over the hip, and a short muscle tips the pelvis forward and extends the lumbar spine. The corrective stretch must hold the pelvis posteriorly tilted. Hamstrings are slackened by knee flexion, and the test is passive, so quadriceps weakness is not involved."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 33-year-old man has a complete radial nerve injury at the spiral groove, confirmed by electromyography 8 weeks ago, and his wrist extensors are denervated. The clinician wants to elicit contractions to slow muscle atrophy while awaiting reinnervation. Which stimulation approach is appropriate for these denervated muscles?", "options": {"A": "Symmetrical biphasic current with 200 microsecond pulses at 50 pulses per second at the motor point.", "B": "Interrupted direct current with pulse duration above 100 ms at 1 to 10 pulses per second.", "C": "Russian stimulation with a 2,500 Hz carrier in 50 bursts per second over the muscle belly.", "D": "Conventional TENS settings at 100 pulses per second with 80 microsecond pulses and sensory intensity."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interrupted direct current with pulse duration above 100 ms at 1 to 10 pulses per second.): Denervated muscle fibers respond directly to current, but they have a very long chronaxie, so they need long pulses, over 100 ms, at low rates such as 1 to 10 pulses per second. Interrupted direct current provides these long monophasic pulses. Short biphasic pulses and Russian stimulation rely on an intact motor nerve and fail to produce a contraction here. Conventional TENS is designed for sensory pain control."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old with subacromial pain syndrome needs a supraspinatus strengthening exercise that limits subacromial compression, and the therapist chooses elevation in the plane of the scapula. Which description of the arm position best matches this exercise as it is usually performed?", "options": {"A": "Elevation about thirty degrees forward of the frontal plane with the thumb up, below shoulder height", "B": "Elevation behind the frontal plane into extension with the elbow flexed to ninety degrees", "C": "Elevation straight forward in the sagittal plane with the arm internally rotated above ninety degrees", "D": "Elevation in the frontal plane with the arm fully internally rotated and the thumb pointing down"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Elevation about thirty degrees forward of the frontal plane with the thumb up, below shoulder height): Scaption is performed roughly thirty degrees anterior to the frontal plane, which matches the scapula's position on the thorax, with the thumb up so the arm is externally rotated. This lowers subacromial compression. Internal rotation with the thumb down and elevation above ninety degrees narrow the space."}, {"id": 643, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient with chronic low back stiffness is receiving a moist heat treatment from a hydrocollator pack wrapped in toweling. The therapist wants to confirm that the toweling is adequate and that the patient is not feeling excessive heat. When should the therapist first check the patient's skin and ask about comfort?", "options": {"A": "At the end of the session, when the pack is removed", "B": "After about 5 minutes, then at intervals throughout the treatment", "C": "After 10 minutes, once the pack has begun to cool", "D": "At 15 minutes, halfway through the planned treatment period"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (After about 5 minutes, then at intervals throughout the treatment): The pack is hottest early in treatment and heat builds in the skin, so the skin is inspected after the first 5 minutes and the patient is asked about comfort. A first check at 10 or 15 minutes misses the period when a burn is most likely to start. Checking only at removal finds damage after it has occurred."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist is about to give UVB to the face of a 28 year old woman with acne. The patient asks whether closing her eyes tightly is enough protection while the lamp is on. The clinic stocks sunglasses, wet cotton pads, dry towels and UV-opaque goggles. Which eye protection should the therapist apply?", "options": {"A": "A folded dry towel draped over the closed eyes of the patient", "B": "Dark visible light sunglasses worn over the closed eyelids of the patient", "C": "UV-opaque goggles that fit snugly over the eyes of the patient", "D": "Wet cotton pads placed over the closed eyelids of the patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (UV-opaque goggles that fit snugly over the eyes of the patient): Ultraviolet radiation reaches the cornea even through closed eyelids, so goggles made of UV-opaque material are required. Tinted sunglasses are designed for visible light and may transmit ultraviolet radiation. Wet pads and a loose towel can shift and leave gaps. Goggles that fit snugly give a dependable barrier."}, {"id": 645, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "After a marathon, a 34-year-old runner says her previous therapist told her massage works by flushing lactic acid out of her legs. She asks whether that is true. She feels sore in both quadriceps. Which response best reflects current understanding of massage effects?", "options": {"A": "Massage mainly raises muscle temperature enough to burn off lactate", "B": "Massage greatly speeds lactate removal, which explains why the legs feel better", "C": "Massage increases muscle glycogen replenishment more than rest alone does", "D": "Massage can reduce perceived soreness and tension, but it does not speed lactate clearance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Massage can reduce perceived soreness and tension, but it does not speed lactate clearance): Research has not shown that massage increases lactate removal, and lactate also returns to baseline within about an hour on its own. Its benefits are subjective, reduced perceived soreness and tension, along with relaxation. It does not heat muscle meaningfully and does not accelerate glycogen resynthesis."}, {"id": 646, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old man with Parkinson disease has marked rigidity and difficulty initiating movement, particularly rolling in bed. A therapist wants a proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation technique that teaches the pattern first and then moves from passive to active assisted to light resisted motion, with a slow rhythmic cue. Which technique fits his problem best?", "options": {"A": "Rhythmic stabilization, using alternating isometric contractions without any joint motion", "B": "Rhythmic initiation, progressing from passive to active assisted to resisted movement", "C": "Hold relax, using an isometric contraction of the tight antagonist before stretching", "D": "Slow reversal, using alternating concentric contractions of antagonist groups without relaxation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rhythmic initiation, progressing from passive to active assisted to resisted movement): Rhythmic initiation helps with difficulty starting movement and with rigidity by moving the limb passively first, then assisting, then adding light resistance. Rhythmic stabilization uses isometric holds for stability. Slow reversal needs the patient to start active motion. Hold relax is a flexibility technique."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 29-year-old recreational runner holds a constant pace on a treadmill in a warm room for 50 minutes. Her heart rate climbs from 148 to 168 beats per minute although speed and incline never change, and she sweats heavily without drinking. Which mechanism best explains the progressive rise in heart rate?", "options": {"A": "Pace gradually exceeding her lactate threshold as muscle glycogen runs low", "B": "Rising blood pressure that forces the heart to beat faster to keep output", "C": "Rising arteriovenous oxygen difference that increases the cardiac output required", "D": "Falling stroke volume from fluid loss and skin blood flow, offset by a faster rate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Falling stroke volume from fluid loss and skin blood flow, offset by a faster rate): Sweating without fluid replacement lowers plasma volume, and skin blood flow takes a share of cardiac output for cooling, so stroke volume falls over time. The heart compensates with a higher rate to maintain output, which is called cardiovascular drift. The pace is constant, so crossing a threshold is not the cause, and a larger extraction would reduce rather than raise the output needed. Blood pressure does not drive the rise in heart rate."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "During a home program, a 47-year-old woman with a known L4-L5 disc herniation calls the clinic reporting new numbness in her inner thighs and perineum, trouble feeling when her bladder is full, and weakness in both feet since this morning. Which instruction should the clinician give?", "options": {"A": "Continue her extension exercises and apply moist heat, then call back in 48 hours", "B": "Take an anti-inflammatory and perform lumbar flexion stretches, then reassess in one week", "C": "Schedule an urgent outpatient MRI within the week and continue activity as tolerated", "D": "Go to the emergency department now for urgent evaluation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Go to the emergency department now for urgent evaluation): Perineal numbness, loss of bladder sensation and bilateral foot weakness form the cauda equina pattern, which needs emergency imaging and often decompression within hours. Delay risks permanent bladder, bowel and sexual dysfunction. Heat with continued exercise and anti-inflammatory care treat it as routine pain. A scheduled outpatient scan within the week is far too slow for a rapidly progressing deficit."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old data-entry clerk has mild to moderate carpal tunnel syndrome with numbness that wakes her at night. Nerve conduction studies confirm median neuropathy without thenar atrophy, and she wants a conservative option before considering injection or surgery. Which splint position is recommended for night wear?", "options": {"A": "Wrist in ulnar deviation with the thumb held in full palmar abduction", "B": "Wrist in thirty degrees of extension to match the usual position of function", "C": "Wrist in neutral, near zero to five degrees of extension, with fingers left free", "D": "Wrist in thirty degrees of flexion to widen the carpal tunnel space during sleep"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wrist in neutral, near zero to five degrees of extension, with fingers left free): Carpal tunnel pressure is lowest with the wrist near neutral, so night splints hold it at about zero to five degrees of extension. Flexion and extension both raise pressure inside the tunnel. A thumb abduction and ulnar deviation posture is not a carpal tunnel splint position."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 67-year-old man with painful right hip osteoarthritis walks with a lateral trunk lean and a limp that worsens by afternoon. The clinician prescribes a single point cane to reduce joint load during walking. In which hand should he hold the cane to best unload the painful hip?", "options": {"A": "The right hand, on the same side as the painful hip", "B": "Both hands, using a walker to share load symmetrically during stance", "C": "The left hand, on the side opposite the painful hip", "D": "No cane, since a lateral trunk lean toward the painful side already unloads the hip"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The left hand, on the side opposite the painful hip): A cane held opposite the painful hip creates a counter moment that reduces the abductor force needed during stance. The same side hand does not provide that moment and keeps demand high. A walker is more support than a unilateral hip problem requires. A lateral trunk lean unloads somewhat but is a compensation that the cane is meant to replace."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinic has only a symmetrical biphasic muscle stimulator available, and the clinician wants to deliver dexamethasone by iontophoresis to a patient with plantar fasciitis by soaking the drug pad and using the stimulator output. A colleague questions whether this device can drive the drug ions into the tissue. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Use it only after raising the pulse duration above 100 ms to mimic direct current.", "B": "Do not use it; ion transfer requires direct current with fixed polarity at the drug pad.", "C": "Use it, because a high pulse rate carries the ions through the skin to the tendon.", "D": "Use it, since alternating polarity pushes ions into the tissue from both pads equally."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Do not use it; ion transfer requires direct current with fixed polarity at the drug pad.): Iontophoresis moves charged ions by repulsion from an electrode of the same polarity, which needs unidirectional direct current. A symmetrical biphasic waveform reverses direction each phase, so ions are pushed in and pulled back with no net transfer. Pulse rate does not carry ions. Lengthening pulse duration does not make a biphasic waveform monophasic."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old retired teacher with osteoporosis (T-score -3.1) and a 55 degree thoracic kyphosis asks her chiropractor for exercises to improve her posture and reduce upper back fatigue. She has no history of fracture. Which exercise should the clinician avoid prescribing for her?", "options": {"A": "Prone back extension lifts with the arms at the sides and neutral head", "B": "Seated gentle trunk rotation within a comfortable pain free range with no added resistance", "C": "Wall-supported scapular retraction with a gentle chin tuck", "D": "Weighted forward trunk flexion such as sit-ups or loaded toe touches"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Weighted forward trunk flexion such as sit-ups or loaded toe touches): Loaded spinal flexion increases anterior vertebral compression and raises the risk of a compression fracture in osteoporotic bone. Low-load back extension and scapular retraction strengthen postural muscles safely. End-range rotation also stresses osteoporotic vertebrae, so it is a poor choice, but weighted flexion is the highest-risk movement and the one to avoid most clearly in this patient."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 46 year old patient has persistent muscle guarding in the lumbar paraspinals 10 days after a strain, with no swelling, warmth, or sensory loss. Moist heat is chosen for the next treatment. Which neurophysiological effect of heat best explains reduced guarding?", "options": {"A": "Decreased muscle spindle firing, which lowers reflex muscle tone", "B": "Increased spindle sensitivity to stretch, which raises tone", "C": "Slowed motor nerve conduction similar to the effect of cold", "D": "Sympathetic vasoconstriction that quiets reflex spasm in the paraspinal muscles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decreased muscle spindle firing, which lowers reflex muscle tone): Heat reduces spindle firing and lowers reflex tone, which eases guarding in a subacute strain. It does not increase spindle sensitivity. Heat speeds rather than slows conduction, and it dilates rather than constricts vessels."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents with unilateral calf swelling, warmth and tenderness three days after a long flight, and she reports no trauma. The chiropractor had planned motor-level high voltage pulsed current for muscle pumping to reduce the swelling. Which action is best?", "options": {"A": "Withhold treatment and arrange urgent evaluation for deep vein thrombosis", "B": "Treat with sensory-level cathodal current, since no contractions are produced", "C": "Apply elevation and compression, then use motor-level current once swelling decreases", "D": "Treat with 10 pps motor-level current while monitoring calf tenderness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold treatment and arrange urgent evaluation for deep vein thrombosis): Unilateral calf swelling, warmth and tenderness after a long flight suggests deep vein thrombosis, so electrical muscle pumping is withheld and urgent evaluation arranged. Sensory-level current is not a safe alternative while a clot is possible. Compression and later motor-level current, or monitoring tenderness during pumping, delay diagnosis and risk embolization."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old woman had a boot removed five weeks after an ankle fracture and now has tight posterior structures, with dorsiflexion limited to 2 degrees. The fracture is healed and no swelling is present. Which sequence best prepares the tissue for lengthening?", "options": {"A": "Ice massage to the calf followed by ballistic stretching into dorsiflexion with bouncing motions", "B": "High voltage stimulation for edema followed by heavy resisted plantarflexion with ankle weights", "C": "Thermal ultrasound to the posterior ankle followed by sustained low load stretching", "D": "Pulsed low dose ultrasound followed by rest without any stretching for several days afterward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thermal ultrasound to the posterior ankle followed by sustained low load stretching): With healed bone and no swelling, the limiting factor is shortened collagenous tissue, which stretches best when heated to a deeper level and held under low load right after heating. Thermal ultrasound suits the deep capsule and tendon, and stretching must follow while the tissue is warm. Cold with ballistic stretch, nonthermal ultrasound, and edema stimulation do not match the problem."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 55-year-old hiker returns from his first downhill trek with severe quadriceps soreness 36 hours later, though his uphill climbing the previous day caused little discomfort. He asks why the descent was worse. Which feature of downhill walking best explains the greater delayed muscle soreness?", "options": {"A": "Greater lactate production during the descent, which lingers in tissue for days", "B": "Lower muscle temperature during descent, which stiffens connective tissue and tears fibers", "C": "Eccentric contractions generate high force per active fiber, disrupting myofibrils", "D": "Higher oxygen cost per step, which depletes mitochondrial enzymes in the quadriceps"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Eccentric contractions generate high force per active fiber, disrupting myofibrils): During descent the quadriceps lengthen under load, and eccentric contractions produce high tension across relatively few recruited fibers, causing structural disruption and an inflammatory response that peaks after about a day or two. Eccentric work also costs less oxygen and produces less lactate than concentric work. Temperature changes do not explain the pattern."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old athlete asks to begin aggressive passive wrist stretching three weeks after a distal radius fracture treated in a cast. Radiographs show callus only, and the surgeon has not yet cleared motion. Which response best reflects safe stretching principles for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Apply heat and joint mobilization to the wrist capsule to speed recovery of motion", "B": "Defer passive stretching across the fracture site until healing is adequate and the surgeon clears it", "C": "Use gentle isometric hold-relax stretching, because no joint movement occurs across the fracture site", "D": "Begin low-load prolonged stretching now because visible callus shows the bone can bear tension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Defer passive stretching across the fracture site until healing is adequate and the surgeon clears it): Early callus is soft and not strong enough for stretch loads, so passive stretching across the fracture site waits until healing is adequate and the surgeon clears motion. Stretching too soon can displace the fracture or delay union. Isometric contractions still create forces across the site, and joint mobilization applies direct stress to a wrist with unhealed bone."}, {"id": 658, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A student comparing tapes at a clinic asks a practical question about elastic kinesiology tape: how far will it stretch along its length compared with its resting length, because that property is meant to approximate the elasticity of skin? Which statement is correct for standard kinesiology tape?", "options": {"A": "It does not stretch, and its benefit comes from a rigid zinc oxide adhesive", "B": "It stretches lengthwise to about 200 percent, like an elastic adhesive bandage", "C": "It stretches lengthwise to about 140 percent of its resting length", "D": "It stretches lengthwise to about 180 percent, matching the elasticity of human muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It stretches lengthwise to about 140 percent of its resting length): Standard kinesiology tape stretches lengthwise to roughly 140 percent of its resting length, which approximates skin elasticity. Rigid zinc oxide tape has almost no stretch. Elastic adhesive bandage stretches far more than kinesiology tape. A 180 percent stretch exceeds the tape's design."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist is rebuilding postural control in a patient recovering from a long hospital stay. The patient can roll segmentally and hold prone on elbows for two minutes with good scapular control. Following the developmental sequence used in neuromuscular re-education, which posture should the therapist introduce next as the following step in progression?", "options": {"A": "Single-leg stance on a foam pad, holding for thirty seconds with eyes open", "B": "Standing with feet together, adding arm reaches to challenge the limits of stability", "C": "Quadruped on hands and knees, starting with weight shifts before lifting any limb", "D": "Half-kneeling with a pelvic tilt, using a wall for balance while one foot steps forward"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Quadruped on hands and knees, starting with weight shifts before lifting any limb): The developmental sequence moves from rolling and prone on elbows to quadruped, then kneeling, half-kneeling, and standing. Quadruped adds weight bearing through the hands and knees with a smaller base than prone and prepares the trunk and limb girdles for later postures. Half-kneeling and standing each demand more upright control than this patient has shown. Single-leg stance on foam is a late balance progression."}, {"id": 660, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "During a supervised treadmill session, a 66-year-old man with known coronary disease has systolic pressure of 148 mmHg at 4 METs. When speed rises to 5 METs, it reads 132 mmHg. He turns pale, reports lightheadedness, and his stride falters. What should the therapist do first?", "options": {"A": "Stop the treadmill, seat or recline him, monitor pressure and rhythm, and notify the physician", "B": "Walk him slowly around the room for a longer cool-down before any seated rest", "C": "Lower the speed to 4 METs and continue the session while rechecking pressure each minute", "D": "Give a drink of water and continue at the same speed for another stage"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the treadmill, seat or recline him, monitor pressure and rhythm, and notify the physician): A systolic drop with increasing workload, together with pallor and dizziness, signals inadequate cardiac output and is a criterion to stop exercise. He should be seated or reclined, and pressure and rhythm monitored while the physician is notified. Continuing at a lower speed or walking around risks syncope. Water and further staging do not address the cause."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 58-year-old man with right hip osteoarthritis stands on his left leg and the pelvis drops on the right side. The clinician writes the exercise plan to correct this pelvic drop during single-leg stance. Which muscle and side should the plan target for strengthening?", "options": {"A": "The left quadratus lumborum, strengthened with right side-lying hip hikes", "B": "The right tensor fasciae latae, stretched to relieve the pelvic drop", "C": "The left gluteus medius, strengthened with standing and side-lying hip abduction", "D": "The right gluteus medius, strengthened with standing and side-lying hip abduction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The left gluteus medius, strengthened with standing and side-lying hip abduction): When the pelvis drops on the right during left stance, the left gluteus medius is failing to hold the pelvis level, so it is the muscle to strengthen. The right gluteus medius is on the swing side. Stretching the right tensor fasciae latae does not address stance side abductor weakness."}, {"id": 662, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "After an intramuscular injection in the upper outer buttock, a 52-year-old woman has right hip abduction of 2 out of 5, normal hip flexion and extension, and during right stance the left pelvis drops. Which nerve is most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "The right inferior gluteal nerve, supplying gluteus maximus", "B": "The right sciatic nerve, supplying the hamstrings and distal limb", "C": "The right superior gluteal nerve to gluteus medius", "D": "The right femoral nerve, supplying the quadriceps and iliopsoas"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The right superior gluteal nerve to gluteus medius): The superior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae, and its injury produces abductor weakness and a Trendelenburg sign. The inferior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus maximus, which tests normal here. Sciatic and femoral nerve injuries would weaken the hamstrings or quadriceps."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinic intern prepares to treat a patient who has subacute low back muscle tightness with a standard hydrocollator pack and proper toweling. The supervisor asks the intern to state the usual treatment duration for this superficial moist heat application before setting the timer. What is the typical duration?", "options": {"A": "About 45 to 60 minutes of continuous application", "B": "Approximately 20 to 30 minutes of continuous application", "C": "About 5 to 10 minutes with a second pack applied immediately after", "D": "About 40 minutes with the pack re-wrapped in additional towels for deeper heating"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Approximately 20 to 30 minutes of continuous application): A hydrocollator pack stays therapeutically warm for roughly 20 to 30 minutes, which is the usual dose for superficial moist heat. Forty-five to sixty minutes exceeds the time the pack holds heat and raises skin risk. A short exposure gives little warming even with a second pack, and extra toweling only reduces heat transfer without heating deeper tissue."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has central low back pain only at end-range flexion that disappears immediately on release. Repeated flexion neither worsens nor centralizes anything, and no leg symptoms occur. The pain has lasted eight months. Which McKenzie syndrome and strategy best fit?", "options": {"A": "Dysfunction syndrome, treated with repeated flexion stretching at the restricted end range", "B": "Postural syndrome, treated by correcting sustained end-range sitting posture alone", "C": "Derangement syndrome, treated with repeated movements in the direction that centralizes pain", "D": "Derangement with lateral shift, treated by side-glide correction before extension"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dysfunction syndrome, treated with repeated flexion stretching at the restricted end range): Pain that appears only at end range, stops on release, and does not change location with repeated movement defines dysfunction syndrome from shortened tissue. Treatment applies repeated stretching into the painful end range to remodel it. Derangement involves changing symptom location, and postural syndrome occurs only with sustained static positions. No lateral shift is present."}, {"id": 665, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A student is dividing the swing phase of the left limb into initial swing, midswing, and terminal swing using the standard observational system. Midswing starts when the swinging foot is level with the stance foot. Which event marks the end of midswing and the start of terminal swing?", "options": {"A": "The heel of the swinging limb touches the floor again", "B": "The tibia of the swinging limb reaches a vertical position", "C": "The swinging foot leaves the floor at toe-off from the surface", "D": "The swinging foot and the stance foot are side by side"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The tibia of the swinging limb reaches a vertical position): In the Rancho system initial swing runs from toe-off until the feet are side by side, midswing runs from there until the tibia is vertical, and terminal swing runs from vertical tibia until initial contact. Toe-off starts the whole swing phase. Heel contact ends terminal swing and begins the next stance phase."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 50 year old carpenter with lateral epicondylitis improved over four visits at 4 J/cm2 per point, then plateaued. The clinician raised the dose to 40 J/cm2 for the next visit, and the patient returned reporting more pain than before. Which interpretation and next step best fit the usual dose response of photobiomodulation?", "options": {"A": "The probe was underpowered, so raise the dose again to 80 J/cm2 next visit", "B": "The tendon has become tolerant to light, so switch to a higher wavelength", "C": "The higher dose caused a normal healing flare, so continue at 40 J/cm2", "D": "Overdosing likely inhibited tissue response, so return to the earlier 4 J/cm2 dose"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Overdosing likely inhibited tissue response, so return to the earlier 4 J/cm2 dose): Improvement at 4 J/cm2 followed by a worse response at ten times that dose fits the biphasic dose response, where excessive dose inhibits healing. The sensible step is to go back to the dose that worked. Escalating further moves deeper into the inhibitory range, and tolerance to light is not a recognized phenomenon here."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 46-year-old teacher has right-sided neck pain with rotation. Cervical rotation measures 70 degrees each way, but thoracic extension and rotation are markedly stiff, and the neck pain eases after thoracic mobilization in the clinic. According to regional interdependence, what should be added to her treatment plan at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Daily stretching of the cervical rotators to extend rotation beyond 70 degrees", "B": "Craniocervical flexion endurance training to correct deep neck flexor weakness driving the pain", "C": "Thoracic extension and rotation mobility work to reduce load on the cervical region", "D": "Lumbar spine mobilization to improve the overall kinetic chain through the pelvis and trunk"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thoracic extension and rotation mobility work to reduce load on the cervical region): Regional interdependence links the stiff thoracic spine to cervical pain, and the pain eased after thoracic mobilization, so adding thoracic extension and rotation mobility is logical. Stretching cervical rotators targets an already adequate range, deep neck flexor training lacks supporting findings, and lumbar mobilization is too distant from the problem."}, {"id": 668, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractor teaches a 34-year-old office worker with chronic neck pain the craniocervical flexion test, with a pressure biofeedback unit placed behind the suboccipital region. The unit is inflated to a starting pressure before the patient performs a gentle head nod. Which starting pressure and final target pressure define the five graded stages of this test?", "options": {"A": "A 30 mmHg baseline, lowered in 2 mmHg steps to a 20 mmHg target", "B": "A 10 mmHg baseline, raised in 2 mmHg steps to a 20 mmHg target", "C": "A 70 mmHg baseline, raised in 2 mmHg steps to an 80 mmHg target", "D": "A 20 mmHg baseline, raised in 2 mmHg steps to a 30 mmHg target"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A 20 mmHg baseline, raised in 2 mmHg steps to a 30 mmHg target): The test inflates the cuff to 20 mmHg and asks the patient to nod to 22, 24, 26, 28 and 30 mmHg while holding each stage for 10 seconds. The target range reflects the small pressure change produced by the deep neck flexors without superficial muscle substitution. The 70 mmHg value belongs to lumbar stabilizer testing, and starting at 10 mmHg or lowering the pressure misdescribes the staged increase."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinician designs knee exercises for a patient with patellofemoral pain who has symptoms during heavy leg extension machine work. Wanting to protect the joint, she compares open chain and closed chain loading. In which range does resisted open chain knee extension place the greatest compressive stress on the patellofemoral joint?", "options": {"A": "Evenly across the full arc because quadriceps force stays constant through the range", "B": "Between ninety and sixty degrees of flexion, where the resistance lever arm is shortest", "C": "Near terminal extension, in the last thirty degrees before the knee is straight", "D": "Near full flexion, where the patella engages deeply within the trochlear groove"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Near terminal extension, in the last thirty degrees before the knee is straight): In open chain extension the lever arm of the resistance grows as the knee straightens, so quadriceps force rises while patellar contact area is small, and stress peaks near terminal extension. Closed chain squats do the opposite and load the joint more in deeper flexion. For many patients with pain, limiting open chain work to the pain free range of mid flexion is therefore common practice."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 19-year-old swimmer and runner has anterior knee pain, lateral patellar tilt and tracking, and pain on stairs, and an examination confirms patellofemoral pain syndrome with lateral patellar glide. The clinician wants a brace that influences patellar position during activity. Which device is best?", "options": {"A": "A patellar stabilizing sleeve with a lateral buttress to guide the patella", "B": "A patellar tendon strap placed below the kneecap to compress the tendon", "C": "A simple neoprene knee sleeve providing warmth and compression around the whole joint", "D": "A rigid functional brace limiting anterior tibial translation at high speed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A patellar stabilizing sleeve with a lateral buttress to guide the patella): A patellar stabilizing sleeve with a lateral buttress resists lateral glide and improves tracking in the trochlear groove, which matches this presentation. An infrapatellar strap compresses the tendon and is used for patellar tendinopathy, not tracking. A plain neoprene sleeve gives warmth and compression without guiding the patella. A functional brace limiting tibial translation protects a deficient cruciate ligament, which is a different problem."}, {"id": 671, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A massage lab exam asks students to identify which technique belongs to the percussive group of strokes, often called tapotement. The student must choose one maneuver as the best example from four being demonstrated on a model's thigh muscle. Which maneuver is a tapotement technique?", "options": {"A": "Wringing, in which both hands twist the muscle belly in opposite directions", "B": "Circular thumb pressure, in which a fixed point is moved over underlying tissue", "C": "Hacking, in which the ulnar borders of both hands strike the muscle alternately", "D": "Skin rolling, in which the fingers lift a fold of skin and advance it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hacking, in which the ulnar borders of both hands strike the muscle alternately): Tapotement includes hacking, cupping, clapping, beating and pounding, all rhythmic striking strokes. Hacking uses the ulnar borders of the hands alternately. Wringing and skin rolling are petrissage variations, and circular thumb pressure is a friction technique."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old desk worker with shoulder pain has a rounded-forward posture. On observation, the inferior angle of the scapula tilts posteriorly off the thorax at rest, the coracoid is tender, and the pectoralis minor tests short. Which treatment plan is best?", "options": {"A": "Latissimus dorsi strengthening combined with serratus anterior stretching during overhead positions", "B": "Upper trapezius shrug strengthening with levator scapulae stretching", "C": "Pectoralis minor stretching with lower trapezius and serratus anterior strengthening", "D": "Rhomboid stretching combined with pectoralis major strengthening through the anterior chest wall"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pectoralis minor stretching with lower trapezius and serratus anterior strengthening): Inferior angle prominence with a short pectoralis minor reflects anterior tilt of the scapula. The plan lengthens the pectoralis minor and strengthens the lower trapezius and serratus anterior to restore posterior tilt and upward rotation. Upper trapezius shrugs add to elevator dominance, and stretching the rhomboids or serratus muscles worsens the imbalance."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old woman at 24 weeks gestation reports mid-lumbar aching after standing at work. She has no neurological signs or bleeding, and her pregnancy is progressing normally. She asks which in-clinic treatment is acceptable. Which option is the most appropriate and safe choice?", "options": {"A": "Postural education, pillow-supported side-lying rest, and gentle stabilization exercises", "B": "Lumbar mechanical traction in supine with the pelvis strapped across the abdomen", "C": "Prolonged supine hot moist packs over the low back and abdomen for 30 minutes", "D": "Therapeutic ultrasound to the lumbar and sacral region at 1 MHz continuous"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Postural education, pillow-supported side-lying rest, and gentle stabilization exercises): Conservative care for pregnancy back pain focuses on posture, positioning, and gentle exercise. Therapeutic ultrasound near the uterus, prolonged heat to the trunk, and traction with an abdominal strap are avoided. Prolonged supine positioning can also reduce venous return in the second trimester."}, {"id": 674, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In the Thomas test position, a 33-year-old cyclist holds the left knee to the chest. On the right side the thigh lifts clearly off the table and the right knee extends a little from its resting flexion as the thigh is lowered. Which interpretation fits both observations?", "options": {"A": "Short right hamstrings, with additional gluteus maximus tightness", "B": "Short left iliopsoas, with additional left rectus femoris tightness", "C": "Weak right gluteus maximus, with additional right quadriceps weakness", "D": "Short right iliopsoas, with additional rectus femoris tightness"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Short right iliopsoas, with additional rectus femoris tightness): A thigh that cannot rest on the table shows limited hip extension, which implicates the iliopsoas. Knee extension as the thigh is lowered adds the two-joint rectus femoris. The hamstrings do not limit hip extension in this position, and both findings are on the tested right side, not the held left."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats a stage three pressure ulcer over the lateral malleolus of a 68-year-old man using high voltage pulsed current. After placing the active electrode in the wound bed, the student must position the dispersive electrode to complete the circuit. Which location is correct?", "options": {"A": "On intact skin of the same limb, proximal to the wound", "B": "On the wound edge, overlapping the periwound skin", "C": "On the opposite limb, level with the contralateral malleolus", "D": "On the lumbar spine, since current should cross the trunk"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (On intact skin of the same limb, proximal to the wound): The dispersive electrode completes the circuit and belongs on intact skin of the same limb, proximal to the wound, so current passes through the wound region and nearby tissue. A pad on the opposite limb sends current across the trunk, which is undesirable. A pad on the wound edge compromises skin integrity and distribution. A lumbar position also crosses the trunk."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 57-year-old man arrives for a scheduled submaximal exercise test. After five minutes seated, his resting blood pressure reads 208/104 mmHg on two separate measurements, and he feels well with no chest pain. He took his medications this morning. Which decision follows ACSM testing guidelines for this finding?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the test using a low-intensity protocol and continuous monitoring", "B": "Begin the test and stop it only if systolic pressure exceeds 250 mmHg", "C": "Proceed with the test after repeating the pressure with a larger cuff", "D": "Defer the test and refer him for blood pressure management first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defer the test and refer him for blood pressure management first): ACSM lists a resting systolic pressure above 200 or diastolic above 110 mmHg as a relative contraindication to exercise testing. He is asymptomatic, but two consistent high readings still call for deferral and medical management. A gentler protocol does not remove the concern, and a larger cuff would not explain two matching readings. A systolic pressure of 250 is a termination criterion during a test, not a reason to begin."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist watches a patient perform a sit-to-stand transfer smoothly while chatting about her weekend, with no verbal cues and no visible attention to the task. Based on the Fitts and Posner stages of motor learning, which stage has she reached in learning this transfer?", "options": {"A": "Retention stage, where the learned skill is tested after a period of rest", "B": "Autonomous stage, where the movement is performed with minimal conscious attention", "C": "Associative stage, where performance is refined but still requires focused attention", "D": "Cognitive stage, where the patient needs frequent cues and makes large errors"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Autonomous stage, where the movement is performed with minimal conscious attention): The autonomous stage is marked by automatic, consistent performance with little attention, which matches a patient talking during the task. The cognitive stage shows large errors and heavy attention. The associative stage still needs concentration. A retention test is a concept that checks learning after a delay, not one of the stages."}, {"id": 678, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "On computerized dynamic posturography, a 55-year-old woman with suspected peripheral vestibular loss is tested under six sensory conditions. She is steady with a fixed platform. When the platform sways with her body and the visual surround is also unreliable, she falls. Which system must a healthy person rely on in those conditions?", "options": {"A": "The visual system, using optic flow cues from the surrounding room", "B": "The cervical joint receptors, which override the vestibular signal", "C": "The vestibular system, which still reports head position relative to gravity", "D": "The somatosensory system, using ankle joint and muscle spindle input"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The vestibular system, which still reports head position relative to gravity): The sensory organization test is built so that conditions with a swaying surface and misleading or absent vision leave vestibular input as the one accurate source. A patient with vestibular loss therefore fails those conditions. Vision and ankle proprioception are the inputs deliberately made unreliable, and neck receptors do not take over from the labyrinth."}, {"id": 679, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist treating a runner with weak gluteals wants a lower extremity diagonal that pairs hip extension with abduction and internal rotation, moving the leg back and out from a position of flexion, adduction, and external rotation. Which pattern should the therapist use for resisted strengthening of this movement?", "options": {"A": "D2 extension, because it combines hip extension, adduction, and external rotation", "B": "D1 extension, because it combines hip extension, abduction, and internal rotation", "C": "D1 flexion, because it combines hip flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "D": "D2 flexion, because it combines hip flexion, abduction, and internal rotation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (D1 extension, because it combines hip extension, abduction, and internal rotation): D1 extension reverses D1 flexion, so the hip moves from flexion, adduction, and external rotation into extension, abduction, and internal rotation. D2 extension uses adduction and external rotation. The two flexion patterns move the leg forward. The start position given matches the end of D1 flexion."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 34-year-old man sprained his ankle three days ago. The ankle is still warm, red, and visibly swollen, and he asks for a heating pad because it feels stiff in the morning. The therapist must choose a modality for today. Which choice best fits his tissue status?", "options": {"A": "Continuous thermal ultrasound over the ligament to speed collagen deposition", "B": "A hot moist pack for twenty minutes to relieve stiffness and loosen scar tissue", "C": "Ice with compression and elevation, then gentle pain free motion", "D": "A contrast bath that begins with heat and ends on the warm immersion cycle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ice with compression and elevation, then gentle pain free motion): Warmth, redness, and swelling at day three show the inflammatory phase is still active, so cooling with compression and elevation controls edema while motion is kept gentle. Heat and thermal ultrasound increase blood flow and could add swelling. A contrast bath ending in warm water also favors edema, and heat is better saved for the later stiff, non swollen stage."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 40-year-old man with type 1 diabetes arrives for a planned moderate walking session. His fingerstick glucose is 265 mg/dL, his urine ketone test is negative, and he feels well with no nausea or abdominal pain. He is well hydrated. What is the most appropriate decision about today's exercise?", "options": {"A": "Begin vigorous interval training to lower glucose quickly through muscle uptake", "B": "Postpone all exercise until glucose falls below 180 and ketones remain negative", "C": "Give a supplemental rapid-acting insulin dose and begin exercise within ten minutes", "D": "Begin light to moderate exercise, hydrate well, and recheck glucose during the session"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin light to moderate exercise, hydrate well, and recheck glucose during the session): With glucose above 250 mg/dL, the key question is ketones. When ketones are negative and the person feels well, light to moderate exercise with hydration and glucose monitoring is acceptable. Postponing is unnecessary here. Vigorous exercise can raise glucose further through counter-regulatory hormones, and a correction insulin dose just before exercise adds hypoglycemia risk."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 20-year-old sprinter is preparing for a 100-meter race in ten minutes. The coach asks the team chiropractor which stretching approach is most appropriate immediately before explosive sprinting. The athlete has full range of motion and no injury. Which warm-up should the athlete choose?", "options": {"A": "Rapid ballistic toe touches with bouncing at full force to stretch the hamstrings", "B": "Two-minute static holds of each hamstring, quadriceps, and calf muscle group", "C": "Light jogging followed by leg swings and walking lunges through progressively larger ranges", "D": "Partner-assisted PNF contract-relax stretches of each lower extremity muscle group at end range"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Light jogging followed by leg swings and walking lunges through progressively larger ranges): Before explosive work, a general warm-up followed by dynamic movements raises tissue temperature and rehearses the sprint pattern through progressively larger ranges. Long static holds, often longer than 60 seconds per muscle, can transiently reduce force and power. Partner PNF at end range is a therapeutic method and is not specific to warm-up. Forceful bouncing adds injury risk without a movement-specific benefit."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 36-year-old woman has weak wrist extensors 10 weeks after a forearm nerve laceration. Short biphasic pulses of 200 microseconds at 50 pps produce no visible contraction at maximal tolerated amplitude. Interrupted direct current with 200 ms pulses at 2 pps produces a slow, wormlike contraction. What do these findings indicate?", "options": {"A": "The stimulator output has failed, so the biphasic amplitude should be raised until a twitch appears.", "B": "The muscle is denervated, so long-pulse low-rate current is needed to elicit contractions.", "C": "The motor point is mislocated, so repeat the pen search with the same short pulses.", "D": "The muscle is innervated but fatigued, so rest is needed before repeating the same settings."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The muscle is denervated, so long-pulse low-rate current is needed to elicit contractions.): Innervated muscle responds to short pulses through its motor nerve. Denervated fibers do not, but they contract slowly to long-duration pulses at low rates, producing the wormlike contraction seen here. The working long-pulse output rules out a failed device, and a mislocated motor point is not relevant once the muscle responds to direct fiber stimulation. Fatigue would not abolish all response to short pulses."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient with a painful tendon receives a 10 minute cold pack, and afterward reports that the aching is much reduced and that light touch feels slightly dulled. The student is asked which nervous system effect of cooling accounts for the rise in pain threshold during treatment. Which effect is it?", "options": {"A": "Increased local metabolic rate, which clears pain mediators from the tendon", "B": "Slowed nerve conduction velocity in sensory fibers, along with gate control inhibition", "C": "Faster conduction in small pain fibers, which floods the spinal cord with more input", "D": "Increased capillary permeability, which washes out nociceptive chemicals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Slowed nerve conduction velocity in sensory fibers, along with gate control inhibition): Cooling slows conduction velocity in sensory nerves and activates cold receptors that help close the spinal gate, which raises pain threshold. Cold lowers rather than raises metabolic rate and conduction speed. Cold also reduces capillary permeability, so washout of mediators by increased permeability does not apply."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 40-year-old programmer has chronic tightness across the upper trapezius, with no neurologic findings and no acute pain. The plan is a short massage that increases circulation and softens the muscle before a stretching program. Which sequence best fits that goal?", "options": {"A": "Warming effleurage, then petrissage to the muscle belly, then stretching", "B": "Beating with loose fists for ten minutes, ending with a cold pack", "C": "Deep transverse friction over the muscle belly, then a gentle gliding stroke", "D": "Hacking over the muscle, followed by deep effleurage and then stretching"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Warming effleurage, then petrissage to the muscle belly, then stretching): Gliding strokes warm the tissue and increase blood flow, and petrissage then softens and mobilizes the muscle belly, setting up an effective stretch. Hacking or prolonged beating is stimulating and can increase tone in a muscle that is already tight. Deep friction is suited to scar and tendon, not a broad muscle belly."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 43-year-old painter has anterior shoulder pain with overhead work, a positive Neer sign, and a painful arc. Cross-body adduction is limited on the right compared with the left, and internal rotation is reduced by 20 degrees. Which intervention should be prioritized to address the contributing deficit?", "options": {"A": "Upper trapezius shrugs with heavy weight to elevate the acromion", "B": "Subscapularis strengthening in end-range external rotation to improve anterior stability", "C": "Cross-body posterior capsule stretching combined with scapular stabilizer strengthening", "D": "Anterior capsule stretching in external rotation with abduction to improve overhead reach"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cross-body posterior capsule stretching combined with scapular stabilizer strengthening): Limited cross-body adduction and lost internal rotation point to a tight posterior capsule, which pushes the humeral head upward and forward and contributes to impingement. Stretching the posterior capsule and strengthening the scapular stabilizers address this. Stretching the anterior capsule, end-range subscapularis work, and heavy shrugs miss the deficit."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old patient began electrical muscle stimulation to the quadriceps at an on to off ratio of 1 to 5. After three weeks he completes every contraction with stable torque and reports little fatigue by the end of each session. The clinician wants to progress the stimulation. Which change in the duty cycle is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Shorten the rest time to move toward an on to off ratio of 1 to 3.", "B": "Lengthen the rest time to move toward an on to off ratio of 1 to 7.", "C": "Reduce the on time to 2 seconds while keeping the same off time.", "D": "Remove the rest time and deliver continuous contraction for the full session."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shorten the rest time to move toward an on to off ratio of 1 to 3.): As fatigue resistance improves, the training load is increased by reducing rest, for example moving from a 1 to 5 ratio to 1 to 3. Longer rest reduces the stimulus and a shorter contraction time does the same. Continuous contraction without rest would cause rapid fatigue and is not a progression."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A trained runner has about 400 g of stored carbohydrate in muscle and liver, and carbohydrate provides 4 kcal per gram. At his marathon pace he burns 120 kcal per mile, with 75 percent of that energy coming from carbohydrate. Approximately how far can he run before the stored carbohydrate is exhausted?", "options": {"A": "About 18 miles of running before carbohydrate stores are exhausted", "B": "About 13 miles of running before carbohydrate stores are exhausted", "C": "About 22 miles of running before carbohydrate stores are exhausted", "D": "About 27 miles of running before carbohydrate stores are exhausted"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 18 miles of running before carbohydrate stores are exhausted): Stored carbohydrate energy is 400 g times 4 kcal per gram, which is 1,600 kcal. Carbohydrate burned per mile is 120 kcal times 0.75, which is 90 kcal. Distance is 1,600 divided by 90, about 17.8, or roughly 18 miles. Ignoring the carbohydrate share gives 13 miles, and lower shares give longer distances."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 70-year-old man with right hip osteoarthritis has an antalgic gait with a lateral trunk lean over the right hip, and he walks about 100 meters before pain builds. A therapist prescribes a single-point cane. In which hand should he hold it, and why?", "options": {"A": "The left hand, so the cane stops the trunk rotating toward the right", "B": "Either hand, since the unloading effect on the hip is equal on both sides", "C": "The right hand, so the cane sits on the same side as the painful hip", "D": "The left hand, so the cane reduces the abductor force needed at the right hip"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The left hand, so the cane reduces the abductor force needed at the right hip): A cane in the hand opposite the painful hip provides a lever on the far side of the body, so the right abductors need less force to level the pelvis and the joint compression drops. A cane on the painful side does not create that opposing moment. The effect is not equal on both sides, and trunk rotation is not the goal."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student in lab calculates the wavelength of a 1 MHz ultrasound beam in soft tissue, where the speed of sound is 1,540 meters per second, using wavelength equals velocity divided by frequency. What wavelength results from this calculation?", "options": {"A": "15.4 millimeters, from 1,540 meters per second divided by a frequency of 100,000 hertz", "B": "154 millimeters, from 1,540 meters per second divided by 10,000 hertz", "C": "1.54 millimeters, from 1,540 meters per second divided by 1,000,000 hertz", "D": "0.51 millimeters, from 1,540 meters per second divided by 3,000,000 hertz"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (1.54 millimeters, from 1,540 meters per second divided by 1,000,000 hertz): At 1 MHz, 1,540 m/s divided by 1,000,000 Hz equals 0.00154 m, which is 1.54 mm. The 0.51 mm figure belongs to 3 MHz, and the other two result from dividing by frequencies far below 1 MHz. Check: 1.54 mm times 1,000,000 per second returns 1,540 m/s."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician tracks ankle joint angle across the stance phase on a video analysis of a healthy walker and wants to find when the ankle reaches its peak dorsiflexion of about 10 degrees. In which phase of gait does this peak occur?", "options": {"A": "Terminal stance, just before the heel rise, as the tibia advances", "B": "Loading response, when the forefoot is lowered after heel contact", "C": "Midstance, when the tibia first passes over the foot and the leg is vertical", "D": "Preswing, when the heel rises and the toes push off the floor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Terminal stance, just before the heel rise, as the tibia advances): As the tibia advances over the planted foot the ankle dorsiflexes to about 10 degrees by terminal stance, just before the heel rises. In loading response the ankle plantarflexes after heel contact. Midstance has dorsiflexion still building. In preswing the ankle moves rapidly toward plantarflexion."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old man's right leg is removed from a long leg cast after 6 weeks. The calf and thigh are visibly smaller. He is otherwise healthy and was non-weight-bearing throughout. Which muscle fiber type and muscle group show the greatest disuse atrophy after immobilization?", "options": {"A": "Fast glycolytic fibers in the gastrocnemius, which fatigue quickly with disuse", "B": "Fast twitch fibers in the hamstrings, because they have the lowest oxidative capacity", "C": "Fast twitch fibers in the tibialis anterior, which are used mostly for speed", "D": "Slow twitch fibers in antigravity muscles such as the soleus and quadriceps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Slow twitch fibers in antigravity muscles such as the soleus and quadriceps): Immobilization causes the most atrophy in slow twitch (type I) fibers of tonic antigravity muscles such as the soleus and quadriceps, because these muscles are active continuously in normal posture and lose that activation under a cast. Fast twitch fibers in the hamstrings, tibialis anterior, and gastrocnemius are used less steadily and lose less relatively. This is why the soleus often shows the most visible loss."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 32-year-old new mother has radial-sided wrist pain from repeatedly lifting her infant. Finkelstein's test reproduces sharp pain over the radial styloid, and mild swelling is visible at the radial side of the wrist. Which pair of tendons is affected in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Extensor pollicis longus and extensor indicis as they pass the third dorsal compartment", "B": "Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis in the second dorsal compartment of the wrist", "C": "Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis within the first dorsal compartment", "D": "Flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus on the volar side of the wrist"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis within the first dorsal compartment): De Quervain tenosynovitis involves the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment at the radial styloid. Finkelstein's test stretches these tendons with thumb flexion and ulnar deviation. The third compartment holds extensor pollicis longus, the second holds the radial wrist extensors, and the volar flexors lie on the opposite side."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 50 year old man reports using a heating pad on low setting over his low back every night for two years. The skin there shows a net-like pattern of brown pigmentation with mild redness at the edges, without pain or warmth. What is the best interpretation and advice?", "options": {"A": "Normal hyperemia from heat, so continue and apply moisturizer afterward", "B": "Erythema ab igne from chronic heat, so stop heating the area and monitor the skin", "C": "Frostnip from cold exposure, so increase layering over the area", "D": "Contact dermatitis from the pad cover, so change the detergent and continue using heat"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Erythema ab igne from chronic heat, so stop heating the area and monitor the skin): A reticulated brown pattern after prolonged low level heat is erythema ab igne, a sign of repeated thermal exposure. The heat source is stopped and the skin monitored. Ordinary hyperemia fades within minutes and does not leave pigmentation, a cover allergy would not form a net pattern, and frostnip is a cold injury."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 40-year-old programmer has a tender palpable trigger point in the upper trapezius with referred pain to the head. The clinician plans low-rate acupuncture-like TENS for 20 minutes. The patient asks where the electrodes should be placed for this mode in particular. Which placement is most consistent with the acupuncture-like approach?", "options": {"A": "One electrode directly over the trigger point and the other over a nearby acupuncture point.", "B": "Both electrodes along the thoracic paraspinals, remote from the trigger point, as in conventional segmental placement", "C": "Both electrodes crossing the painful muscle belly, using high-rate sensory-level current", "D": "One electrode over the trigger point and the other over the painful forehead region"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (One electrode directly over the trigger point and the other over a nearby acupuncture point.): Acupuncture-like TENS is commonly applied over trigger points, motor points or acupuncture points, with intensity high enough to produce local twitching. One electrode over the trigger point and one over a nearby acupuncture point fits that approach. Thoracic paraspinal placement reflects conventional segmental technique. Crossing the muscle belly with high-rate sensory current is also conventional. The forehead is where pain is referred, not the source."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 54-year-old man is in seated cervical traction at 15 degrees of flexion with 12 lb when he reports lightheadedness and blurred vision. His blood pressure is unchanged from the start of the session and his arm symptoms are better. What is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Switch from intermittent to static mode at the same force and continue to monitor", "B": "Increase flexion to 35 degrees to open the foramina further and resume the pull", "C": "Release the pull, lay him supine, and hold further traction until vascular screening", "D": "Reduce the weight to 8 lb and continue, because mild dizziness is expected early"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Release the pull, lay him supine, and hold further traction until vascular screening): Dizziness and visual blurring during neck traction are warning signs of reduced vertebral artery flow, so the pull should be released, the patient placed supine, and further traction withheld pending vascular assessment. The fact that arm symptoms improved does not outweigh a possible vascular sign. Reducing the weight, changing the angle, or switching modes all keep the neck loaded."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 30-year-old runner has a passive straight leg raise limited to 60 degrees by hamstring tightness. The therapist positions the leg at the end of available range and asks for a submaximal isometric hamstring contraction for several seconds, then relaxation, then stretches further. Which neurophysiological mechanism is this hold-relax technique intended to use?", "options": {"A": "Increased muscle spindle sensitivity in the hamstrings that strengthens the stretch reflex response", "B": "Plastic elongation of hamstring connective tissue created by the heat of the contraction", "C": "Reciprocal inhibition of the hamstrings produced by a voluntary quadriceps contraction during the stretch", "D": "Autogenic inhibition from Golgi tendon organ firing in the hamstrings after the contraction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Autogenic inhibition from Golgi tendon organ firing in the hamstrings after the contraction): In hold-relax the therapist has the patient contract the tight muscle isometrically, and the resulting tension excites Golgi tendon organs. Their Ib input inhibits the same muscle, so it relaxes briefly and can be stretched further. Reciprocal inhibition belongs to the agonist contraction method, higher spindle sensitivity would oppose stretch, and a few seconds of effort do not heat connective tissue meaningfully."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A pulsed 905 nm gallium arsenide laser lists a peak power of 10 W, a pulse width of 200 nanoseconds, and a pulse frequency of 10,000 Hz. The chiropractor needs the average power to calculate dose for a patient with knee osteoarthritis. What is the average power output of this device?", "options": {"A": "2 mW, by treating the frequency as 1,000 Hz", "B": "20 mW, by multiplying peak power, pulse width, and frequency", "C": "200 mW, by treating the pulse width as 2,000 nanoseconds", "D": "10 W, by taking the peak power as the average output"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (20 mW, by multiplying peak power, pulse width, and frequency): Average power equals peak power times pulse width times frequency. That is 10 W times 0.0000002 s times 10,000 per second, which equals 0.02 W or 20 mW. Quoting the peak as the average overstates output by a factor of 500, and misreading width or frequency shifts the answer by ten."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 40 year old accountant receives a class 3B 810 nm treatment at 100 mW for 40 seconds per point over the supraspinatus. He feels no warmth and says the machine may not be working. The probe's output indicator shows normal function. How should the chiropractor interpret his experience?", "options": {"A": "The probe is malfunctioning, so power should be raised until warmth is felt", "B": "Absent warmth is expected, since the effect is photochemical and not thermal", "C": "The dose is subtherapeutic, so exposure should be lengthened until warmth is felt", "D": "Lack of warmth shows the beam was reflected from the skin and treatment failed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Absent warmth is expected, since the effect is photochemical and not thermal): At 100 mW and 4 J per point, tissue heating is minimal and the therapeutic effect comes from photochemical changes in the mitochondria. A patient feeling nothing is therefore normal. Using warmth as an endpoint would push the dose into an inhibitory or hazardous range, and reflection is not indicated by missing heat."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A man with psoriasis has had eight UVB sessions at the same dose. Early sessions produced light erythema, but now there is no redness and the plaques have stopped improving. The treatment goal is to maintain an E1 reaction. Which interpretation and action is best?", "options": {"A": "Tolerance has developed, so raise the dose in small steps", "B": "The skin has become photosensitized, so halve the current dose", "C": "The psoriasis is resistant to UVB, so change to psoralen with UVA", "D": "The psoriasis has plateaued, so stop the sessions at this point"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tolerance has developed, so raise the dose in small steps): Loss of erythema at an unchanged dose signals tolerance, so the dose is raised in small steps to restore an E1 reaction. Less redness does not mean more sensitivity, and halving the dose would lose effect. PUVA is a later option, but tolerance should be overcome first. A plateau with persistent plaques is not clearance."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinic owns an 810 nm class 3B probe and a separate 660 nm class 3B probe. The only goggles on the wall are labeled with a high optical density for 780 to 1100 nm. A technician is about to treat a patient's superficial forearm ulcer with the 660 nm probe while wearing those goggles. What is the correct assessment?", "options": {"A": "The goggles are acceptable because red light is less hazardous than near-infrared light", "B": "The goggles are acceptable when the beam is kept below 500 mW on the skin", "C": "The goggles do not cover 660 nm, so matched 660 nm eyewear is needed first", "D": "The goggles are acceptable because high optical density protects at any laser wavelength"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The goggles do not cover 660 nm, so matched 660 nm eyewear is needed first): Optical density ratings apply only across the wavelength range printed on the goggles, here 780 to 1100 nm. A 660 nm beam falls outside that band, so the goggles may transmit it nearly unattenuated. Red class 3B light is still an eye hazard, and a power threshold does not waive wavelength matching."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 50-year-old woman stands on her right leg alone while the clinician observes from behind. Her left iliac crest drops below the level of the right crest and she cannot hold the pelvis level for ten seconds. Which finding does this single-leg stance sign most directly indicate?", "options": {"A": "Weakness of the left tensor fasciae latae, the lifted side hip flexor", "B": "Weakness of the right gluteus medius, the stance side abductor", "C": "Weakness of the right quadratus lumborum acting as a pelvic elevator", "D": "Weakness of the left gluteus medius, the side of the dropped pelvis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Weakness of the right gluteus medius, the stance side abductor): During single-leg stance the stance side gluteus medius holds the pelvis level against gravity. When it is weak the opposite pelvis drops, so a left drop on right stance points to the right gluteus medius. The swing side muscles and lumbar muscles are not the primary stabilizers for this task."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student performs spray and stretch on a trigger point in the upper trapezius, using a vapocoolant spray while a partner holds the neck in gentle passive lateral flexion. The instructor asks about the correct spray pattern to reduce referred pain. How should the vapocoolant be applied?", "options": {"A": "In slow sweeps in one direction, from the trigger point toward its referred pain zone", "B": "In rapid back and forth sweeps over the muscle belly", "C": "Held steady on the trigger point until a layer of frost forms", "D": "From the referred pain zone back toward the trigger point"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (In slow sweeps in one direction, from the trigger point toward its referred pain zone): Spray and stretch uses parallel sweeps in one direction, starting at the trigger point and moving toward the referred zone, while the muscle is passively lengthened. The brief skin cooling lets the muscle stretch with less pain. Holding the spray in place risks frosting the skin, and reversing the direction or sweeping back and forth departs from the technique."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old man with a recent disc herniation asks to resume full sit-ups with his feet anchored, saying he needs abdominal strength for work. The therapist, applying McGill's work on spinal loading, advises replacing them with a different abdominal exercise. Which exercise and reason best fit this advice for him?", "options": {"A": "The curl-up, because anchored sit-ups load the lumbar spine through strong psoas activity", "B": "The straight leg raise, because lifting long levers trains the lower abdominals most effectively", "C": "The crunch on a Swiss ball, because greater lumbar flexion range builds more abdominal strength", "D": "The sit-up with the feet free, because it removes hip flexor involvement from the movement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The curl-up, because anchored sit-ups load the lumbar spine through strong psoas activity): McGill found that full sit-ups load the lumbar spine through the psoas and the flexion cycle, so the curl-up with a neutral lumbar spine is preferred for patients with disc injury. A Swiss ball crunch encourages more flexion range. Straight leg raises load the lumbar spine through the psoas. Freeing the feet does not remove the psoas contribution."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "After eight weeks of steady aerobic training, a 33-year-old man's VO2max has risen only 3 percent, yet his blood lactate at his usual running pace has dropped and he can hold that pace much longer. His coach wonders which adaptation explains the lower lactate. Which is the most likely?", "options": {"A": "A large increase in maximal heart rate, raising oxygen delivery at every pace", "B": "Enlargement of type II fibers, increasing glycolytic enzyme capacity at that pace", "C": "Greater mitochondrial density and oxidative enzyme activity, reducing reliance on glycolysis", "D": "Higher phosphocreatine stores, which spare glycolysis during steady running"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Greater mitochondrial density and oxidative enzyme activity, reducing reliance on glycolysis): Aerobic training increases mitochondrial number and oxidative enzymes such as succinate dehydrogenase and citrate synthase, so more ATP comes from oxidation and less pyruvate is converted to lactate at the same absolute pace. This can occur with little change in VO2max. Maximal heart rate does not rise, and greater glycolytic capacity would raise rather than lower lactate."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "During a neurological screen, a 62-year-old man with long-standing diabetes stands with his feet together and arms at his sides. He is steady with eyes open, but he sways markedly and steps out when he closes his eyes. Which deficit does this positive Romberg finding most directly suggest?", "options": {"A": "Impaired central integration from a lesion in the cerebellar vermis", "B": "Loss of somatosensory input, especially joint position sense from the lower limbs", "C": "Bilateral vestibular hypofunction as the primary cause of the imbalance", "D": "Reduced visual acuity that eye closure has now exposed to the examiner"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Loss of somatosensory input, especially joint position sense from the lower limbs): A Romberg test removes vision, so a patient who was steady with eyes open and unsteady with eyes closed depended on vision to cover lost proprioception from the legs, as in diabetic neuropathy. A cerebellar lesion produces sway with the eyes open as well. Vestibular loss on a firm surface is usually compensated by vision and somatosensation, and a visual deficit would not worsen when the eyes are closed."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old triathlete after a concussion scores 56 out of 56 on the Berg, yet reports unsteadiness in crowded spaces and on uneven ground. The Berg shows a ceiling effect in this patient. Which balance assessment is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "The Berg repeated with the eyes closed on firm floor for each item", "B": "The Timed Up and Go at a comfortable pace", "C": "The BESS using tandem and single-leg stances on foam", "D": "The Tinetti POMA, which assesses gait and balance in older adults"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The BESS using tandem and single-leg stances on foam): A high functioning athlete needs a task that challenges balance beyond the Berg ceiling. The BESS on foam stresses vestibular and somatosensory control. The Timed Up and Go is designed for mobility screening and would likely be normal. The Tinetti was built for frail older adults and also has a ceiling, and repeating the Berg with eyes closed is not a validated, normed test."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 33 year old man is 10 weeks after surgical repair of the ulnar nerve at the elbow, with early tingling advancing distally. He also has elbow tendon soreness and asks for an ice pack. The therapist reviews the history. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Apply ice massage, since it exposes a smaller area to cold", "B": "Apply cold whirlpool at 70 degrees F, a mild temperature", "C": "Avoid cold over the elbow because regenerating nerve may not signal tissue stress", "D": "Apply ice for 20 minutes, since cold protects the repair from swelling at the elbow"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid cold over the elbow because regenerating nerve may not signal tissue stress): Regenerating nerve has unreliable sensation, so cold over it can injure tissue without a warning signal. Cold is therefore avoided. A smaller area or a milder temperature does not change that. Ice for 20 minutes adds a prolonged exposure."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 38 year old graphic designer with Raynaud phenomenon in both hands presents with wrist extensor tendinitis after a deadline week. Her fingers blanch when exposed to cool air. The student must choose a thermal approach for the forearm and wrist that avoids provoking vasospasm. Which choice is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Mild superficial warmth over the forearm, avoiding cold applications to the hands", "B": "Ice massage for 5 minutes, since brief cold limits vasospasm", "C": "Contrast bath beginning with the cold basin to condition the vessels", "D": "Cold whirlpool for the hand at 60 degrees F with a short immersion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mild superficial warmth over the forearm, avoiding cold applications to the hands): Cold triggers vasospasm in Raynaud phenomenon, so cryotherapy to the hands is avoided and gentle warmth is the safer choice. Shortening the cold dose does not remove the trigger. A contrast bath that begins in cold still exposes the hand to cold, so it carries the same problem."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 19-year-old soccer player lands on another player's foot and rolls the ankle into plantarflexion and inversion. That evening she reports lateral ankle pain and swelling and cannot push off normally. Based on the usual mechanism for this injury, which structure is most often damaged first in a lateral ankle sprain?", "options": {"A": "The anterior talofibular ligament on the lateral side of the ankle joint", "B": "The anterior tibiofibular ligament of the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis joint", "C": "The calcaneofibular ligament running along the lateral side of the hindfoot", "D": "The deltoid ligament on the medial side of the ankle mortise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The anterior talofibular ligament on the lateral side of the ankle joint): With the foot plantarflexed and inverted, the anterior talofibular ligament is the primary restraint and is stretched first, so it is the most commonly torn lateral ligament. The calcaneofibular ligament usually fails later, with greater force or in more dorsiflexion. The deltoid ligament resists eversion, and the anterior tibiofibular ligament is injured by external rotation in a high ankle sprain."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "In quadruped, a 32-year-old man lifts his right leg backward and the pelvis rotates while the lumbar spine twists as the foot rises above hip height. He reports no back pain and the hamstrings and hip flexors test with normal length. Which interpretation and progression best fit this bird-dog finding?", "options": {"A": "Tight hip flexors tilting the pelvis, so stretch the iliopsoas in half kneeling", "B": "Poor anti-rotation control, so practice slow leg reaches with a dowel", "C": "Weak rectus abdominis, so add weighted crunches for spinal flexion", "D": "Limited thoracic extension forcing the lumbar spine to twist, so mobilize the thoracic spine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Poor anti-rotation control, so practice slow leg reaches with a dowel): Pelvic rotation and lumbar twist with a rising leg point to poor anti-rotation control, trained with slow leg reaches while holding a dowel. Hip flexor length tested normal, so stretching iliopsoas does not fit. Weighted crunches train flexion, not rotational control, and thoracic extension does not explain a twist at the lumbar spine."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 41-year-old carpenter with chronic low back pain is set up for supine mechanical lumbar traction with a split table and pelvic harness. The therapist places a bolster under his lower legs so that the hips and knees rest at about 90 degrees of flexion. What is the main spinal effect of this positioning during the pull?", "options": {"A": "It increases the lumbar lordosis, which favors opening of the anterior disc space", "B": "It locks the facet joints into extension, which concentrates the pull on the anterior longitudinal ligament", "C": "It reduces friction between the trunk and the table, which lowers the force needed", "D": "It reduces the lumbar lordosis, which favors separation of the posterior vertebral elements"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It reduces the lumbar lordosis, which favors separation of the posterior vertebral elements): Flexing the hips and knees to about 90 degrees tilts the pelvis posteriorly and flattens the lumbar lordosis, which favors opening of the posterior elements and facet joints. The lordosis is decreased, not increased, so the anterior disc space is not the area favored. Friction reduction is accomplished by a split table, not by the leg position. The facets are drawn toward a flexed, open position rather than locked in extension."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 38-year-old teacher received semi-rigid foot orthoses on Monday, wore them all day, and now reports aching in the arches and shins by evening. The orthoses fit the shoes and the arch contour matches her feet. What should the therapist recommend?", "options": {"A": "Raise the arch height by adding more material under the medial longitudinal arch", "B": "Continue all-day wear and add a cushioned insole layer on top for comfort", "C": "Discontinue the orthoses and refer for custom replacement made with a different shell material", "D": "Return to short wear periods and build up gradually, rechecking arch height and fit"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Return to short wear periods and build up gradually, rechecking arch height and fit): New semi-rigid orthoses commonly cause arch and shin aching when worn all day at once, because tissues need time to adapt. The fit and contour are already correct, so the fix is a graded break-in, starting with short periods. Adding arch height or padding changes a correct device, and abandoning or replacing it is premature. Gradual build-up with rechecks is the standard approach."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A student clinician reviews the accepted indications for ultraviolet irradiation in a rehabilitation clinic. The supervising therapist asks which one of the following conditions is a recognized indication for UVB treatment in a patient who is otherwise healthy and takes no photosensitizing medication. Which condition is the recognized indication?", "options": {"A": "Chronic plaque psoriasis with thick scaling over the elbows and knees", "B": "Porphyria cutanea tarda with skin blistering after sun exposure", "C": "Systemic lupus erythematosus with a photosensitive rash across the face", "D": "Xeroderma pigmentosum with extreme sensitivity to ordinary sunlight exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic plaque psoriasis with thick scaling over the elbows and knees): Chronic plaque psoriasis responds to UVB because the radiation slows the overactive skin cell cycle. The other three conditions are photosensitive disorders in which ultraviolet exposure can flare disease or cause severe injury. Lupus can flare with ultraviolet light, xeroderma pigmentosum leaves skin unable to repair ultraviolet damage, and porphyria causes blistering after exposure. Each of these is a contraindication, not an indication."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractic student performs a manual muscle test on a patient's elbow flexors after a nerve injury. The patient completes the full available range of motion against gravity but cannot hold the end position when the examiner applies even slight resistance. Using the standard 0 to 5 grading system, which grade does this finding represent?", "options": {"A": "Grade 1, which is a palpable muscle contraction with no visible joint movement", "B": "Grade 2, which is full range of motion only when gravity is eliminated", "C": "Grade 3, which is full range of motion against gravity with no added resistance tolerated", "D": "Grade 4, which is full range of motion against gravity and moderate manual resistance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade 3, which is full range of motion against gravity with no added resistance tolerated): Grade 3 (fair) means the patient completes the full range against gravity but tolerates no added manual resistance. Grade 2 requires the gravity-eliminated position, so it understates this patient. Grade 4 requires holding against moderate resistance, which this patient cannot do. Grade 1 describes only a palpable flicker without joint motion."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician views a standing patient from the side with a plumb line hanging from the ceiling, a method described by Kendall for judging posture. In ideal alignment, the line passes through the ear lobe and the middle of the shoulder joint. Through which lower body landmarks does it then pass?", "options": {"A": "The sacral promontory, behind the knee axis and behind the lateral malleolus", "B": "The greater trochanter, slightly anterior to the knee axis and the lateral malleolus", "C": "The ischial tuberosity, the popliteal fossa, and the calcaneal tuberosity at the heel", "D": "The iliac crest, the center of the patella, and the posterior heel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The greater trochanter, slightly anterior to the knee axis and the lateral malleolus): Kendall's ideal side view places the plumb line through the ear lobe, shoulder joint, greater trochanter, slightly anterior to the knee axis and slightly anterior to the lateral malleolus. The other options describe landmarks that lie behind or off this line. Deviations from it point to forward head, kyphosis, or pelvic tilt."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 25-year-old soccer player has a grade II medial collateral ligament sprain with valgus stress laxity at 30 degrees of flexion, no cruciate involvement, and is walking pain free. The physician wants early motion while protecting the medial structures. Which brace best matches this goal?", "options": {"A": "A hinged brace with medial and lateral supports and adjustable range of motion stops", "B": "An immobilizer locked in full extension and worn for the next six weeks", "C": "A patellar stabilizing sleeve with a lateral buttress and open popliteal area", "D": "A varus-directed unloader brace that offloads the medial compartment of the knee"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A hinged brace with medial and lateral supports and adjustable range of motion stops): A hinged brace with medial and lateral supports protects the medial collateral ligament from valgus stress, and range of motion stops allow controlled early movement. A locked immobilizer prevents the early motion the physician wants. A patellar sleeve treats patellar tracking, not valgus laxity. A varus-directed unloader brace is for medial compartment arthritis and would not protect the ligament."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 67 year old with diabetes has a shallow neuropathic ulcer on the plantar forefoot, about 2 mm deep, limited to the epidermis and dermis, with no exposed deeper tissue. The chiropractor wants photobiomodulation aimed at the wound bed fibroblasts and keratinocytes. Which wavelength is the best match for this superficial target?", "options": {"A": "660 nm red light, absorbed mainly within the epidermis and superficial dermis", "B": "1064 nm near-infrared light, chosen because it penetrates farthest beyond the wound bed", "C": "810 nm near-infrared light, absorbed mainly within deeper muscle and joint capsule", "D": "980 nm near-infrared light, absorbed mainly by water in deep tissue layers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (660 nm red light, absorbed mainly within the epidermis and superficial dermis): A 2 mm ulcer needs energy absorbed in the skin layers, which is where 660 nm red light deposits most of its energy. The 810 nm wavelength passes through to deeper structures and is chosen for muscle and joints. The 980 and 1064 nm wavelengths shift toward water absorption or deep delivery and are poorly suited to a superficial wound."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 33-year-old receives kinesiology tape on the shoulder for a rotator cuff complaint, and two days later reports intense itching. The skin under the tape shows red, raised vesicles in the exact outline of the strips. What is the best management?", "options": {"A": "Apply hydrocortisone cream under fresh tape and continue the planned wear", "B": "Leave the tape on until the planned five-day wear is complete", "C": "Remove the tape, treat as contact dermatitis and avoid reapplying that tape", "D": "Switch to a hypoallergenic strip applied over the same irritated skin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the tape, treat as contact dermatitis and avoid reapplying that tape): Itching and vesicles in the exact outline of the strips are a classic contact dermatitis reaction to the adhesive, so the tape comes off and is not reapplied. Leaving it on prolongs exposure. A steroid cream under fresh tape continues the same adhesive contact, and a different strip over inflamed skin risks further irritation, so the reaction is treated first and a different support considered later."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinician is applying proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation to the lower extremity of a patient who needs help with the swing phase of gait. Starting with the hip extended, abducted, and internally rotated, the therapist guides the leg into the D1 flexion pattern. Which hip motions does the lower extremity D1 flexion pattern combine?", "options": {"A": "Hip flexion, abduction, and external rotation, finishing with the foot out to the side", "B": "Hip flexion, abduction, and internal rotation, finishing with the leg out and away from the midline", "C": "Hip extension, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the leg behind the trunk", "D": "Hip flexion, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the leg across the midline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hip flexion, adduction, and external rotation, finishing with the leg across the midline): Lower extremity D1 flexion moves the hip into flexion, adduction, and external rotation, which carries the leg across the midline. Flexion with abduction and internal rotation is D2 flexion. Abduction with external rotation does not form a standard diagonal. Extension with adduction and external rotation is the D2 extension pattern."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "While treating a tight hamstring, a clinician applies a constant, low-intensity stretching force with a strap and holds it. After half a minute the same unchanged load has produced a gradual increase in the muscle's length. Which viscoelastic property of connective tissue best describes this behavior?", "options": {"A": "Hysteresis, the energy lost as heat when tissue is loaded and then unloaded", "B": "Creep, the progressive increase in deformation of tissue under a constant applied load", "C": "Plastic deformation, a permanent length change that remains after the applied force is removed", "D": "Stress relaxation, the gradual fall in tension when tissue is held at a fixed length"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Creep, the progressive increase in deformation of tissue under a constant applied load): Creep is the time-dependent increase in length of a viscoelastic tissue under a constant load, which is what the hold with a steady strap load produces. Stress relaxation is the related drop in tension when the tissue is held at a fixed length, so it does not fit a constant load with changing length. Hysteresis describes energy lost over a load and unload cycle. Plastic deformation is the permanent change that may result, not the process observed during the hold."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old carpenter with lateral elbow tendinopathy that has not settled with rest and bracing is scheduled for deep friction massage to the common extensor tendon at its origin. The student asks how to set up the treatment and which direction the strokes should take. Which technique is correct?", "options": {"A": "Transverse strokes across the tendon with the tendon slack and the elbow fully flexed", "B": "Strokes parallel to the tendon fibers with the tendon on a slight stretch", "C": "Transverse strokes across the tendon with the tendon placed on a slight stretch", "D": "Broad kneading of the forearm extensor bulk with the wrist held neutral"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transverse strokes across the tendon with the tendon placed on a slight stretch): Deep friction massage is applied transversely across the tendon fibers with the tendon on a slight stretch, so the tendon is pressed against the underlying bone. Slack tendon reduces effect, parallel strokes do not break adhesions across fibers, and broad kneading of the bulk is general massage."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 60-year-old man with 20 years of type 2 diabetes has a fixed resting heart rate near 98 beats per minute that rises only to 106 during easy cycling. His systolic pressure falls 25 mmHg on standing. He is starting aerobic training. Which monitoring approach is the most reliable for guiding his exercise intensity?", "options": {"A": "Rating of perceived exertion, with blood pressure checks after position changes and at peak effort", "B": "Pulse oximetry readings, aiming to keep saturation above 95 percent throughout the session", "C": "Age-predicted maximum heart rate with a target of 65 to 80 percent of that value", "D": "Heart rate reserve zones set at 50 to 70 percent using his resting heart rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rating of perceived exertion, with blood pressure checks after position changes and at peak effort): Cardiac autonomic neuropathy produces a high, fixed resting heart rate and a blunted response, so heart rate based zones are unreliable. Orthostatic hypotension also makes blood pressure checks around position changes important. Perceived exertion tracks effort independent of heart rate. Oxygen saturation stays normal and does not reflect workload."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has chronic lower leg swelling after a calf strain, with normal vascular studies and no signs of thrombosis. The chiropractor uses motor-level high voltage pulsed current to pump fluid proximally with the leg elevated. Which pulse rate best produces the rhythmic twitch contractions suited to this effect?", "options": {"A": "A pulse rate of about 500 pps that yields no perceptible muscle response", "B": "A low pulse rate of about 10 pps that yields individual twitches", "C": "A pulse rate of about 120 pps that yields smooth tetanic contraction", "D": "A pulse rate of about 60 pps that yields fatiguing tetanic contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A low pulse rate of about 10 pps that yields individual twitches): A muscle pump needs alternating contraction and relaxation, which low pulse rates around 10 pps provide as individual twitches. Rates of about 60 to 120 pps fuse into tetany, which holds the muscle contracted and does not move fluid rhythmically. Very high rates produce no useful muscle response. The elevated leg lets the twitches empty the limb proximally."}, {"id": 725, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 71 year old has a draining 3 cm venous leg ulcer with moderate bacterial bioburden. The chiropractor wants to treat the wound bed with a class 3B 660 nm probe. The probe tip cannot be sterilized between patients. Which technique best addresses infection control without losing meaningful dose at the wound?", "options": {"A": "Irradiate normal skin 10 cm from the wound to stimulate healing remotely", "B": "Press the bare probe tip firmly into the wound bed for good contact", "C": "Hold the bare probe 15 cm above the wound to avoid contamination", "D": "Cover the probe tip with clear plastic film and hold it just above the wound"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cover the probe tip with clear plastic film and hold it just above the wound): A clear barrier film keeps secretions off the tip while passing the red light, and holding the probe close above the wound avoids contact with the bed. Pressing a bare tip into a draining ulcer risks cross contamination. A probe 15 cm away delivers far too little irradiance, and irradiating distant skin does not treat the wound bed."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During Janda's shoulder abduction test, a 40-year-old painter lifts the arm in front of a mirror and the shoulder girdle elevates within the first 60 degrees while the humerus rises slowly. Which muscle pattern does this early elevation suggest, and which strategy fits best?", "options": {"A": "Upper trapezius overactivity, so cue scapular depression and train the lower trapezius", "B": "Serratus anterior weakness, so cue scapular protraction and train the serratus with wall slides", "C": "Middle trapezius overactivity, so cue scapular retraction and train the rhomboids", "D": "Upper trapezius weakness, so strengthen it with resisted shoulder shrugs and overhead pressing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Upper trapezius overactivity, so cue scapular depression and train the lower trapezius): Early shoulder girdle elevation in Janda's abduction test shows the upper trapezius taking over for weak scapular depressors and upward rotators. Cueing depression and training the lower trapezius address that. Serratus weakness tends to cause winging, not a shrug. Middle trapezius work trains retraction, which is not the fault here. The upper trapezius is overactive in this pattern, so shrugs would reinforce the fault."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 36-year-old woman has her right arm in a cast for 5 weeks after a wrist fracture. Her therapist wants to limit strength loss in the casted arm. She is right-hand dominant and works as a dental hygienist. Which approach takes advantage of the cross-education effect?", "options": {"A": "Perform passive stretching of the right elbow and shoulder every two hours", "B": "Apply neuromuscular electrical stimulation to the left arm to prevent atrophy of the right", "C": "Perform heavy isotonic training of the right wrist through the cast opening", "D": "Train the uninjured left arm with resisted exercise to preserve strength in the right arm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Train the uninjured left arm with resisted exercise to preserve strength in the right arm): Cross-education is neural carryover of strength from a trained limb to the untrained opposite limb, so resisted exercise of the uninjured left arm helps preserve right arm strength. Stimulating the left arm does not train the right. Passive stretching does not load muscle. Heavy wrist training through the cast would stress the healing fracture."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinic uses a progressive resistance protocol in which the patient performs three sets of ten repetitions per session. The first set is the heaviest, and each later set is lighter as the muscle fatigues. Which named protocol follows this descending sequence of loads?", "options": {"A": "DAPRE technique, adjusting the working load set by set from the previous performance", "B": "Oxford technique, starting at one hundred percent of 10RM and decreasing each set", "C": "DeLorme technique, starting at fifty percent of 10RM and increasing each set", "D": "Circuit training stations with fixed light loads performed for timed intervals"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Oxford technique, starting at one hundred percent of 10RM and decreasing each set): The Oxford technique reverses DeLorme: 100, 75 and 50 percent of 10RM, so the load drops as fatigue builds. DeLorme rises from 50 to 75 to 100 percent. DAPRE adjusts the working load according to performance from session to session rather than descending within the session. Circuit training uses fixed timed stations and is not a set by set loading protocol."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A physical therapy aide reviews the chart of a 78-year-old woman who lives alone and reports two near falls this month. The therapist scored her on a standardized 14-item balance test in which each item is rated from 0 to 4 points. Which total score on this test is generally accepted as the cutoff for increased fall risk?", "options": {"A": "A combined balance and gait score below 19 out of a possible 28", "B": "A total score below 19 points out of a possible 24 on eight gait tasks", "C": "A total score below 21 points out of the possible 56 on the scale", "D": "A total score below 45 points out of the possible 56"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A total score below 45 points out of the possible 56): The Berg Balance Scale has 14 items scored 0 to 4, for a maximum of 56, and a total below 45 is the commonly used threshold for increased fall risk. The cutoff near 19 belongs to the Tinetti tool and to the Dynamic Gait Index, which use different totals. A score under 21 on the Berg marks only the highest risk band, so it would miss many patients who are already at risk."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 17-year-old has increased thoracic kyphosis. In prone press-up the curve does not reduce, active thoracic extension is minimal, and the spine feels rigid on palpation, unlike a flexible curve that flattens with positioning. Which interpretation and plan fit best?", "options": {"A": "A rigid structural kyphosis, so refer for imaging and use extension mobility with strengthening", "B": "A weak erector spinae pattern, so use heavy loaded extension to correct the curve", "C": "A normal curve for age, so reassure and recheck in one year", "D": "A flexible postural kyphosis, so use pectoral stretching and retractor strengthening alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A rigid structural kyphosis, so refer for imaging and use extension mobility with strengthening): A curve that does not flatten in prone press-up and has little active extension is rigid and may be structural, so imaging and a mobility based program are warranted. Postural exercises alone fit a flexible curve that corrects with positioning. Heavy loaded extension does not suit a rigid curve."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 37-year-old patient reports low back aching after prolonged standing and shows poor endurance of the deep multifidus. A coach suggests heavy lifting while the therapist prefers an approach aimed at the local stabilizing system. Which training prescription best targets these deep stabilizers?", "options": {"A": "Heavy resisted trunk extension performed to failure in three sets of five", "B": "Low load, sustained contractions of about ten seconds, repeated for several repetitions", "C": "Fast ballistic trunk rotations performed with light weights for speed", "D": "Maximal effort contractions held for two seconds with long rest periods between each effort"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Low load, sustained contractions of about ten seconds, repeated for several repetitions): Local stabilizers such as the multifidus work tonically at low load, so training uses low effort, sustained contractions repeated to build endurance. Brief maximal efforts and heavy loads recruit global muscles. Ballistic rotations train speed and power. The patient's problem is endurance of a tonic muscle."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 67-year-old man with severe COPD cannot sustain more than three minutes of treadmill walking at his target pace before reaching a dyspnea rating of 7 out of 10 and stopping. His oxygen saturation stays above 90 percent. Which training format best allows him to accumulate more total work at that intensity?", "options": {"A": "Continuous treadmill walking at target pace with oxygen flow raised until 30 minutes pass", "B": "Continuous walking at a much lower speed until a full 30 minutes is completed", "C": "Inspiratory muscle training seated with a threshold device and no walking component", "D": "Interval training with short work bouts at target pace separated by rest periods"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Interval training with short work bouts at target pace separated by rest periods): Interval training lets him spend more total time at the target pace by alternating short work bouts with rest, which dyspnea prevents in continuous work. Raising oxygen flow does not make continuous work tolerable when saturation is already adequate. Continuous slow walking trades away the intensity. Inspiratory muscle training helps but removes the walking."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with coronary artery disease takes metoprolol daily and begins a supervised walking program. Her heart rate response to increasing treadmill speed is flat, rising only from 64 to 92 beats per minute at a hard effort. Which method should the clinician rely on to judge and prescribe her exercise intensity?", "options": {"A": "Karvonen heart rate reserve method with a target of 60 to 80 percent of reserve", "B": "Resting heart rate plus 20 beats per minute as a fixed ceiling for every training session", "C": "Percentage of age-predicted maximum heart rate calculated as 220 minus age, targeting 70 to 85 percent", "D": "The Borg rating of perceived exertion scale, aimed at about 12 to 14"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Borg rating of perceived exertion scale, aimed at about 12 to 14): A beta blocker lowers resting and exercise heart rate, so heart rate zones from formulas underestimate real effort. Perceived exertion is unaffected by the drug and tracks workload reliably. A target of about 12 to 14 corresponds to somewhat hard effort. The age-predicted and reserve methods both depend on a heart rate response that the drug has flattened, and the resting-plus-20 rule belongs to early inpatient walking."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "An athlete's Functional Movement Screen shows deep squat 2, hurdle step 3 on the right and 2 on the left, in-line lunge 2, and shoulder mobility 2 on the right and 1 on the left, with no pain on any clearing exam. Following standard screen rules, which finding should be addressed first?", "options": {"A": "The left shoulder mobility pattern scoring 1 with a side to side asymmetry", "B": "The right hurdle step pattern scoring 3 with a side to side asymmetry", "C": "The deep squat scoring 2 because it is a bilateral fundamental pattern", "D": "The in-line lunge scoring 2 since it loads both hips and ankles together"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The left shoulder mobility pattern scoring 1 with a side to side asymmetry): The screen guidance is to correct asymmetries and the lowest scores before symmetrical patterns. The shoulder pattern is both the lowest score and asymmetric, so it comes first. The hurdle step is also asymmetric but scores higher, and the squat and lunge are symmetrical."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 66 year old smoker with intermittent claudication has plantar heel pain. The foot is cool and pale when elevated, with weak pedal pulses. The therapist considers either a hot pack or an ice pack for the heel. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Use an ice pack, since cooling lowers metabolic demand in the ischemic foot", "B": "Avoid both, since reduced blood flow cannot meet changing tissue metabolic demand", "C": "Use a hot pack, since heat improves blood supply to ischemic tissue", "D": "Use contrast baths, since alternating temperatures train the vessels"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid both, since reduced blood flow cannot meet changing tissue metabolic demand): A foot with weak pulses and pallor cannot raise blood flow to meet the greater demand heat creates, and it tolerates cold poorly. Both thermal agents are therefore avoided. Heat does not improve supply in diseased arteries, cold carries risk, and contrast baths add the same stresses."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician observes a healthy 30-year-old bending forward to touch the floor with the knees straight, and then describes the normal lumbopelvic rhythm to a student who is learning to recognize altered movement strategies in patients with low back pain. Which sequence best describes this normal rhythm during forward bending?", "options": {"A": "Lumbar flexion contributes early, then anterior pelvic rotation at the hips completes the movement", "B": "Lumbar extension accompanies anterior pelvic tilt through the entire range of forward bending", "C": "The thoracic spine flexes first and the lumbar spine then holds a fixed neutral position", "D": "Anterior pelvic rotation at the hips occurs first, then lumbar flexion completes the movement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lumbar flexion contributes early, then anterior pelvic rotation at the hips completes the movement): In normal forward bending the lumbar spine flexes through roughly the first part of the movement and the pelvis then rotates anteriorly over the femoral heads to finish it. Reversing that order or locking the lumbar spine does not describe healthy rhythm. Departures from this pattern, such as a lumbar spine that carries nearly all the flexion, point to a movement impairment."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old mechanic stands with his trunk visibly shifted to the left of the pelvis and a flattened lumbar spine after acute onset back pain with right thigh pain. He cannot extend his back in standing. Repeated extension in lying is not tolerated yet. Which step should come first?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe repeated lumbar flexion in standing to open the posterior annulus", "B": "Side-glide the trunk further to the left to unload the right side", "C": "Side-glide the trunk toward the painful right side, then add extension", "D": "Start with trunk rotation stretches toward the left to relax the shifted muscles"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Side-glide the trunk toward the painful right side, then add extension): In McKenzie management a visible lateral shift is corrected first, usually by moving the trunk toward the side of pain and the pelvis away from it, until extension becomes tolerable. Only then do extension exercises become effective. Gliding further into the shift or choosing flexion or rotation stretches does not reduce the deformity."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A swimmer has shoulder pain and the examiner notes the shoulder blade is not held firmly against the rib cage during forward reaching. The goal is to strengthen the serratus anterior with high activation. Which exercise best targets this muscle?", "options": {"A": "Push-up plus, adding full scapular protraction at the top of the push-up", "B": "Prone horizontal abduction with thumbs up and arms raised to ear level", "C": "Bent-over row with retraction of the shoulder blades at the top of the movement", "D": "Standing shoulder shrug with dumbbells while elevating both shoulders toward the ears"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Push-up plus, adding full scapular protraction at the top of the push-up): The serratus anterior protracts and upwardly rotates the scapula, and the push-up plus adds extra protraction at the end of a push-up to activate it strongly. Rows and prone horizontal abduction train the retractors and posterior shoulder muscles. Shrugs work the upper trapezius and levator scapulae."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 49-year-old driver has anterior neck and trapezius pain. The chiropractor plans four-pole interferential current and tentatively places one electrode pair over the front of the neck and one pair on the upper trapezius. A classmate objects to the anterior placement. Which is the best revision?", "options": {"A": "Keep the anterior pair but switch to a higher carrier frequency", "B": "Move all electrodes to the posterior neck and shoulder, away from the carotid sinus", "C": "Move the anterior pair to the sternum and keep the posterior pair unchanged", "D": "Keep the anterior pair but lower intensity below the sensory threshold"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Move all electrodes to the posterior neck and shoulder, away from the carotid sinus): Electrodes are not placed over the carotid sinus because stimulation can alter heart rate and blood pressure. The fix is to relocate all pads to the posterior neck and shoulder. Changing carrier or lowering intensity leaves the electrodes over the same vulnerable area. Moving the anterior pair to the sternum places current across the chest near the heart, which is also inappropriate."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 16-year-old gymnast has a grade I isthmic spondylolisthesis at L5 on lateral radiographs, with extension-related low back pain and no neurological deficit. The rehabilitation plan emphasizes dynamic stabilization after the acute pain settles. Which exercise approach best fits the initial stabilization phase for this spinal segment?", "options": {"A": "Resisted trunk rotation with heavy loading at end-range lumbar extension", "B": "Abdominal bracing with transversus abdominis activation in neutral spine", "C": "Repeated prone press-ups into end-range extension to restore lumbar lordosis", "D": "Passive lumbar extension stretching over a stability ball to improve segmental mobility"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Abdominal bracing with transversus abdominis activation in neutral spine): Spondylolisthesis is a problem of segmental instability and anterior shear, so the first phase trains deep trunk muscles to control the neutral position. Bracing and transversus abdominis work add stiffness without end-range loading. Extension and rotation under load stress the pars and the slipped segment. Mobility work is not the deficit here."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "During single-leg stance on his right leg, a 52-year-old man's left pelvis drops 3 centimeters below the right, and his trunk does not lean. He has a mild right hip ache after a long period of inactivity. Which muscle weakness best explains this finding, and which exercise targets it?", "options": {"A": "Right gluteus medius weakness, targeted with side-lying abduction", "B": "Left hip adductor weakness, targeted with standing adduction", "C": "Left gluteus medius weakness, targeted with side-lying hip abduction", "D": "Right gluteus maximus weakness, targeted with bridging exercises"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Right gluteus medius weakness, targeted with side-lying abduction): In single-leg stance the stance-side gluteus medius holds the pelvis level, so weakness on the right allows the left pelvis to drop. Side-lying hip abduction and single-leg stance progressions strengthen it. The left muscle is not bearing weight, and extensor or adductor weakness does not produce this frontal plane drop."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 22-year-old soccer player is seen two days after a grade 2 hamstring strain with swelling, bruising, and pain on gentle stretch. A student is asked about stretching timing relative to tissue healing. Which statement reflects the appropriate approach during the first several days after the injury?", "options": {"A": "Delay all stretching until the remodeling phase is complete, usually about six months later", "B": "Begin vigorous static stretching immediately to prevent scar tissue from shortening the muscle", "C": "Start PNF contract-relax stretching with maximal contractions on day two to restore range", "D": "Withhold stretching during the acute inflammatory phase, then add gentle pain-free stretching after about 72 hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Withhold stretching during the acute inflammatory phase, then add gentle pain-free stretching after about 72 hours): Stretching is withheld during the acute inflammatory phase, then gentle pain-free stretching is added after about 72 hours. Waiting six months delays remodeling guidance unnecessarily. Vigorous stretching or maximal PNF contractions on day two load fragile fibers and risk reinjury."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A student reviewing aerobic metabolism is asked where most ATP is generated when a glucose molecule is fully oxidized in working muscle. Glycolysis produces a small amount of ATP in the cytosol and the Krebs cycle produces a small amount of GTP or ATP directly. Which process accounts for the largest share of the ATP produced?", "options": {"A": "Glycolysis in the cytosol, which yields the largest share when oxygen is available", "B": "The electron transport chain, using NADH and FADH2 to drive ATP synthase", "C": "Beta oxidation of fatty acids in the mitochondrial matrix, which yields most ATP from glucose", "D": "The Krebs cycle itself, which produces most ATP directly from each turn"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The electron transport chain, using NADH and FADH2 to drive ATP synthase): The Krebs cycle generates NADH and FADH2 and only a small amount of ATP or GTP directly. Those reduced coenzymes donate electrons to the electron transport chain, whose proton gradient drives ATP synthase and yields roughly 26 to 28 of the 30 to 32 ATP from a glucose. Glycolysis nets only two, and beta oxidation acts on fatty acids rather than glucose."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 63-year-old man with severe deconditioning after a long hospitalization becomes breathless and stops after eight minutes of continuous walking. His physician has cleared him for aerobic training, and the goal is 20 minutes of activity per session. Which prescription best builds toward that goal given his tolerance?", "options": {"A": "Continuous walking for 8 minutes, held at that duration until he feels ready", "B": "Two continuous 10 minute walks at a moderate pace starting from the first week", "C": "Repeated short walking bouts with rest breaks, lengthening the work portions over weeks", "D": "Interval training with brief near maximal efforts and equal rest, building to 20 minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeated short walking bouts with rest breaks, lengthening the work portions over weeks): He stops at eight minutes, so the program should use repeated short bouts with rest and lengthen the work portions over weeks. This builds toward 20 minutes within his tolerance. Staying at eight minutes gives no progression, and 10 minute continuous bouts exceed what he can do now. Near-maximal intervals are too intense for severe deconditioning."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 66-year-old man with hypertension takes metoprolol daily and begins a walking program. His clinician wants to set a moderate intensity, but the usual age-predicted heart rate targets would overestimate what his blunted heart rate response will allow. Which method is the most reliable way to monitor his exercise intensity?", "options": {"A": "Age-predicted maximal heart rate, adjusted downward by 10 beats per minute", "B": "Karvonen target zone calculated from his current resting heart rate", "C": "Seventy percent of the 220 minus age maximum, held steady during walking", "D": "Rating of perceived exertion, aiming for about 12 to 13 on the 6 to 20 scale"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rating of perceived exertion, aiming for about 12 to 13 on the 6 to 20 scale): Beta blockers lower resting and exercise heart rate, so heart rate based formulas no longer match true effort. RPE reflects perceived work independent of the drug, and 12 to 13 corresponds to moderate intensity. Reducing a predicted maximum by 10 beats or recomputing the Karvonen zone from a medicated resting rate still depends on a blunted heart rate signal. A fixed percent of 220 minus age has the same weakness."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 34-year-old warehouse supervisor develops sudden low back pain after lifting a box, with no leg symptoms, no numbness, normal bowel and bladder function, and no history of cancer or trauma. He asks what to do during the first week. Which advice is most appropriate for him during this acute stage?", "options": {"A": "Rest in bed for several days until pain subsides before resuming normal movement", "B": "Wear a rigid lumbar corset full time for six weeks to support the spine", "C": "Begin a progressive trunk strengthening program with heavy resistance during the first week", "D": "Stay active within pain limits with short walks and daily tasks, avoiding bed rest"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stay active within pain limits with short walks and daily tasks, avoiding bed rest): Nonspecific acute low back pain without red flags recovers best with early return to normal movement, because prolonged rest causes deconditioning and delays recovery. Short walks and daily tasks within tolerance are the baseline advice. Bed rest is tempting but prolongs disability. Heavy strengthening is a later stage goal, and full time bracing weakens trunk muscles without improving outcome."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 72-year-old man with known peripheral artery disease has walked with calf pain at two blocks for years. Over two weeks he now wakes with burning forefoot pain relieved by hanging his leg off the bed, and a small toe ulcer is not healing. The foot is cool and pale. What is the best decision?", "options": {"A": "Withhold the walking program and refer him urgently for vascular evaluation of limb ischemia", "B": "Switch to progressive treadmill intervals at a lower speed with protective footwear", "C": "Add compression bandaging to the calf and continue the walking program at the same dose", "D": "Continue supervised walking to moderate pain and add daily leg elevation at night"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Withhold the walking program and refer him urgently for vascular evaluation of limb ischemia): Rest pain relieved by dependency, a non-healing ulcer, and a cool pale foot are features of critical limb ischemia, which threatens the limb and needs urgent vascular assessment. Exercise therapy for claudication is not appropriate until perfusion is addressed. Elevating the leg lowers perfusion pressure, and compression can further compromise arterial flow."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician uses a direct current device with an active electrode measuring 3 cm by 5 cm. The patient is treated at a current of 30 mA, and the clinician wants to document the current density under the active electrode to compare it with the dispersive pad. What is the current density under the active electrode?", "options": {"A": "3.75 mA per square centimeter beneath the active electrode.", "B": "450 mA per square centimeter beneath the active electrode.", "C": "0.5 mA per square centimeter beneath the active electrode.", "D": "2.0 mA per square centimeter beneath the active electrode."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2.0 mA per square centimeter beneath the active electrode.): The area of the active electrode is 3 times 5, or 15 square centimeters. Current density equals current divided by area, so 30 mA divided by 15 square centimeters equals 2.0 mA per square centimeter. Dividing area by current gives 0.5, and using the sum of the sides, 8 cm, gives 3.75. Multiplying current by area gives 450, which is not a density."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "Before training, a sedentary man has a resting heart rate of 72 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 70 milliliters. After a program of endurance training, his resting heart rate is 60 beats per minute and his stroke volume is 84 milliliters. What happened to his resting cardiac output after training?", "options": {"A": "It rose to about 5.8 liters per minute because stroke volume increased", "B": "It fell to about 4.2 liters per minute because only the heart rate changed", "C": "It fell to about 3.6 liters per minute because heart rate decreased", "D": "It stayed the same at about 5.0 liters per minute because the changes offset"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It stayed the same at about 5.0 liters per minute because the changes offset): Before training, cardiac output was 72 times 70, or 5,040 mL/min. After training it is 60 times 84, which is also 5,040 mL/min, so resting output is unchanged near 5.0 L/min. The lower heart rate and higher stroke volume offset each other. Using only one changed term, or mixing old and new values, produces the 5.8, 3.6, and 4.2 liter answers."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient five weeks after a wrist fracture has cast removal and persistent hand swelling with stiffness. The therapist selects a contrast bath using separate warm and cold basins for the hand to promote alternating vasodilation and vasoconstriction. Which timing ratio of warm to cold immersion is conventionally used in contrast bath protocols?", "options": {"A": "Warm for 3 to 4 minutes, then cold for 1 minute, repeated", "B": "Warm for 1 minute, then cold for 3 to 4 minutes, repeated several times", "C": "Warm for 10 minutes, then cold for 10 minutes, repeated", "D": "Warm for 30 seconds, then cold for 30 seconds, repeated"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Warm for 3 to 4 minutes, then cold for 1 minute, repeated): Contrast baths use longer warm immersions and short cold immersions, typically 3 to 4 minutes warm to 1 minute cold, repeated for several cycles. Reversing the ratio leaves the hand mostly in cold. Equal long immersions are not standard, and 30 second dips are too brief to change vessel behavior."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 68-year-old woman is seen two hours after a rear-end collision with neck pain. She has no midline tenderness, is not intoxicated, and can actively rotate her neck 45 degrees to each side. Applying the Canadian C-spine rule, what is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Apply ice for fifteen minutes and reassess in two days before any imaging decision", "B": "Refer for cervical radiographs because age 65 or older is a high-risk factor", "C": "Perform cervical manipulation and image only if pain increases afterward", "D": "Withhold imaging because the rear-end collision was a simple low-risk mechanism and she is ambulatory"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for cervical radiographs because age 65 or older is a high-risk factor): The Canadian C-spine rule checks high-risk factors first, and age 65 or older mandates radiography. A simple rear-end collision and active rotation of 45 degrees count as low-risk features only when no high-risk factor is present. Waiting for two days, manipulating before imaging, or withholding imaging on the low-risk mechanism all skip the age criterion that decides the pathway here."}, {"id": 752, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "During a demonstration, the instructor places an ultrasound head on dry forearm skin without any gel. The unit shows output but the volunteer feels nothing and the transducer face warms quickly. A student asks why gel is required for therapeutic ultrasound. What is the primary reason a coupling medium is used?", "options": {"A": "It increases the effective radiating area so more tissue is treated", "B": "It lowers the beam frequency so more energy reaches the deeper muscle and joint layers", "C": "It stimulates local circulation so the beam heats the skin evenly", "D": "It removes the air layer that would reflect nearly all energy at the skin surface"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It removes the air layer that would reflect nearly all energy at the skin surface): Ultrasound does not travel through air, and the mismatch between air and tissue reflects almost all of the energy back into the transducer, which then overheats. A water-based gel fills the gap and transmits the beam into the tissue. The gel does not change frequency, circulation or the radiating area."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old bus driver, six weeks after whiplash, has dizziness when turning the neck and is otherwise healthy. Dix-Hallpike testing is negative and positional vestibular tests are normal, but head repositioning accuracy with a head-mounted laser is reduced. Which intervention best targets this problem?", "options": {"A": "Vestibular adaptation exercises with head turns while fixating a target, aimed at vestibular hypofunction", "B": "Cervical proprioceptive training, including laser-guided repositioning and gaze drills", "C": "Cervical segmental mobilization to the upper cervical joints to restore rotation range", "D": "Isometric neck strengthening in neutral with graded loading to reduce dizziness on turning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cervical proprioceptive training, including laser-guided repositioning and gaze drills): Reduced head repositioning accuracy after whiplash with negative vestibular testing points to cervical proprioceptive dysfunction, so laser-guided repositioning and gaze drills are targeted. Vestibular adaptation treats inner ear hypofunction, which testing does not show. Mobilization addresses range rather than joint position sense, and neutral isometric strengthening does not retrain it."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 28-year-old recreational lifter's heels lift off the floor during a bodyweight squat. Passive ankle dorsiflexion measures 5 degrees with the knee extended and 18 degrees with the knee flexed to 90 degrees. Which muscle limits the motion, and which stretch fits best?", "options": {"A": "Soleus, stretched with the knee flexed and the heel on the floor", "B": "Tibialis anterior, stretched with the ankle plantar flexed and the toes tucked", "C": "Talocrural capsule, mobilized with a posterior glide while avoiding calf stretching", "D": "Gastrocnemius, stretched with the knee extended and the heel on the floor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gastrocnemius, stretched with the knee extended and the heel on the floor): Dorsiflexion improves from 5 to 18 degrees when the knee flexes, which slackens the two-joint gastrocnemius, so that muscle is the main limiter and the stretch is done with the knee extended. If the soleus were limiting, range would stay similar with the knee bent. A capsular limit would not change with knee position."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A therapist holds a patient's quadriceps at a steady 30 percent of maximal voluntary contraction until fatigue. Force output is maintained, but surface EMG amplitude recorded over the vastus lateralis steadily rises during the hold. Which interpretation best explains the rising EMG signal?", "options": {"A": "Existing motor units raise their firing rate and no new motor units are recruited", "B": "Additional, higher threshold motor units are recruited to replace fatiguing units and hold the force", "C": "Muscle fibers are increasing in cross sectional area during the hold, which raises signal amplitude", "D": "Motor units are being derecruited from largest to smallest as the contraction becomes less efficient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Additional, higher threshold motor units are recruited to replace fatiguing units and hold the force): During a sustained submaximal contraction the initially active slow units fatigue, so the nervous system recruits additional higher threshold units to keep the force constant, and EMG amplitude rises. Fiber area does not change within one hold, rate coding alone would not add recruited units, and derecruitment would lower rather than raise the signal. This pattern is a standard sign of muscle fatigue on surface EMG."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist fits a 5 ft 8 in patient for a Swiss ball before core exercises. With the patient seated on an inflated ball, the hips are visibly lower than the knees and the knees are bent past ninety degrees. What should the therapist conclude, and what is the best response?", "options": {"A": "The ball is too small, so a larger ball is chosen to put the thighs level", "B": "The ball is too soft, so extra air is added until the knees extend further", "C": "The ball is too large, so a smaller ball is chosen to put the thighs level", "D": "The ball is correct, so the patient proceeds with the hips lower to add difficulty"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The ball is too small, so a larger ball is chosen to put the thighs level): On a correctly sized ball the hips and knees are near ninety degrees and the thighs are parallel to the floor. Hips below the knees mean the ball is too small, so a larger one is needed. A larger ball would not help if the ball were too large. A poor fit is not a sound way to add difficulty, and inflation does not fix the sizing."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "In a physical agents lab, students sort conditions into indications and contraindications for superficial moist heat. One station lists four patients seeking relief of musculoskeletal pain, each requesting a hot pack on the painful area before exercise. Which patient presents a contraindication to superficial moist heat over the treatment area?", "options": {"A": "A patient with diabetic peripheral neuropathy and absent temperature sensation in the area", "B": "A patient with chronic upper trapezius tightness and normal sensation", "C": "A patient with a healed hamstring strain and residual tightness before stretching", "D": "A patient with subacute knee stiffness who cannot reach end range before exercise"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A patient with diabetic peripheral neuropathy and absent temperature sensation in the area): A patient who cannot feel temperature cannot warn the therapist of overheating, so a burn can occur unnoticed. Chronic tightness and residual tightness are classic heat indications. Subacute joint stiffness is a standard indication for heat before stretching."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student studying temperature effects on tissue is told that metabolic rate changes with a Q10 of 2, meaning the rate doubles for each 10 degree C rise and halves for each 10 degree C fall. In a hypothetical model, a cold application lowers tissue temperature by 20 degrees C. What fraction of the original metabolic rate remains?", "options": {"A": "One half of the original rate, since the rate halves once", "B": "One quarter of the original rate, since 20 degrees C equals two halvings", "C": "Three quarters of the original rate, since the change is 25 percent per step", "D": "One eighth of the original rate, since each degree halves it again"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (One quarter of the original rate, since 20 degrees C equals two halvings): A 20 degree C fall is two 10 degree steps, and each step halves the rate. Rate is therefore 1/2 times 1/2, which equals 1/4 of the original. Applying one halving gives 1/2, and compounding per degree or using a 25 percent change both misuse the Q10 definition."}, {"id": 759, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with weak grip asks why she cannot squeeze a dynamometer firmly when her wrist is held in full flexion. The long finger flexors cross both the wrist and the fingers. Which concept explains her loss of grip force in this wrist position?", "options": {"A": "Reciprocal inhibition, because the wrist extensors relax whenever the finger flexors contract", "B": "Concentric overload, because the finger flexors work against excess external resistance", "C": "Active insufficiency, because the finger flexors are shortened over both joints at once", "D": "Passive insufficiency, because the finger flexors are overstretched across both joints at once"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Active insufficiency, because the finger flexors are shortened over both joints at once): A multijoint muscle shortened across all the joints it crosses cannot generate effective tension because the filaments overlap too much, which is active insufficiency. With the wrist flexed, the finger flexors are slack, so grip weakens. Passive insufficiency refers to an overstretched antagonist limiting motion, and no external overload is present."}, {"id": 760, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A researcher records motor neuron activity from the first dorsal interosseous while a volunteer increases pinch force from light to maximal. After essentially every available motor unit is already active, force still rises because each active motor neuron discharges more rapidly. What is this mechanism called?", "options": {"A": "Rate coding, an increase in motor unit firing frequency that raises force through summation", "B": "Spatial summation, in which additional motor units are recruited to add force to the contraction", "C": "Synchronization, in which motor units discharge at the same instant to produce a larger twitch", "D": "Postactivation potentiation, a short lived rise in twitch force after a prior maximal contraction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rate coding, an increase in motor unit firing frequency that raises force through summation): Rate coding is the nervous system's second way to grade force, after recruitment. Increasing the discharge frequency of already active motor units causes twitches to summate toward fused tetanus and raises force. Recruiting more units is spatial summation, synchronization concerns timing between units, and postactivation potentiation is a brief twitch enhancement after prior activity."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Janda described a pattern in which the upper and lower crossed syndromes combine in one patient, producing alternating bands of tight and weak muscle groups from the head down to the pelvis and creating a stacked, layered look to the posture. What is this combined pattern called?", "options": {"A": "Flat back syndrome, a sagittal pattern marked by loss of lumbar lordosis", "B": "Upper crossed syndrome, a head to shoulder pattern with tight pectorals and weak deep neck flexors", "C": "Sway back posture, a sagittal pattern with the pelvis shifted forward of the line", "D": "Layer syndrome, a combination of both crossed patterns in one person"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Layer syndrome, a combination of both crossed patterns in one person): Janda used layer syndrome for the combination of upper and lower crossed syndromes in one person, giving alternating weak and tight layers along the trunk. Upper crossed syndrome is only the upper half of that combination. Flat back and sway back are separate sagittal posture types and are not combined crossed patterns."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 19 year old soccer player twists his ankle during practice and arrives at the clinic two hours later with a swollen lateral ankle, tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament, and mild warmth. The therapist applies a cold pack over a damp towel, with a compression wrap and elevation. How long should the cold application last?", "options": {"A": "About 60 to 90 minutes, followed by a hot pack to restore circulation", "B": "About 2 to 5 minutes, enough for brief skin numbness", "C": "About 30 to 45 minutes for fuller cooling of deep tissue", "D": "About 10 to 20 minutes, repeated several times through the day"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 10 to 20 minutes, repeated several times through the day): For an acute sprain, a cold pack is typically applied for 10 to 20 minutes and repeated through the day with compression and elevation. Longer exposure adds skin and nerve risk without more benefit. Very short applications do not cool enough to limit swelling, and following cold with heat in the acute phase works against it."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 71-year-old man has a stage three sacral pressure ulcer with moderate exudate and a positive wound culture for bacteria. The chiropractor applies high voltage pulsed current to the wound. Which polarity of the active electrode is the usual choice for this wound stage?", "options": {"A": "No polarity choice is needed, as a dispersive-only setup gives an equal effect", "B": "Negative electrode in the wound bed, as cathodal current is bacteriostatic", "C": "Positive electrode in the wound bed, as anodal current promotes epithelial growth", "D": "Alternating polarity every minute, as high voltage current is bacteriostatic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Negative electrode in the wound bed, as cathodal current is bacteriostatic): The standard textbook choice for an infected or inflamed wound is the negative electrode in the wound bed, since cathodal stimulation is described as bacteriostatic. Positive polarity is associated with later epithelial growth, not infection control. A monophasic current does not alternate, and polarity matters in this current."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with a known breast cancer metastasis to the thoracolumbar spine reports worsening mid back pain. A colleague suggests interferential current at sensory intensity, since she has failed analgesics. The chiropractor reviews the contraindication list. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Apply four-pole current at sensory intensity, since sensory level does not produce heat", "B": "Withhold electrical stimulation over the lesion and coordinate pain care with oncology", "C": "Apply current at sensory intensity with electrodes on healthy skin 10 cm from the lesion", "D": "Use interferential current once the patient signs consent acknowledging the tumor related risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Withhold electrical stimulation over the lesion and coordinate pain care with oncology): Known malignancy near the treatment field is a contraindication to electrical stimulation, so the correct step is to withhold it and coordinate pain management with oncology. Sensory intensity may not heat tissue but the contraindication remains. Moving electrodes 10 cm away does not remove the concern in a spine with metastasis. Consent documents risk and does not make an unsafe treatment appropriate."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 65-year-old man with confirmed peripheral artery disease and intermittent claudication begins a supervised walking program on a treadmill. He reports calf discomfort that he rates as 2 on a 4-point claudication scale. What is the recommended management of the walking bout at this point?", "options": {"A": "Stop immediately, rest until the pain resolves, and restart at a slower treadmill speed", "B": "Switch to seated leg exercises because any claudication pain signals dangerous tissue damage", "C": "Continue walking through the pain until it becomes severe, rated 4 of 4, before resting", "D": "Continue walking until the pain reaches moderate, about 3 of 4, then rest until relieved"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue walking until the pain reaches moderate, about 3 of 4, then rest until relieved): The standard claudication protocol has the patient walk until pain reaches a moderate level, about 3 on the 4-point scale, then rest until the pain is relieved and repeat. This repeated ischemic stimulus drives improved walking capacity. Stopping at the first twinge is too conservative and pushing to severe pain is not recommended. Claudication pain reflects reversible ischemia, not tissue damage."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 34-year-old paramedic can exercise only three days per week for 30 minutes each time. He wants to meet the minimum ACSM weekly aerobic target for healthy adults with the schedule he has. Which intensity choice allows his three 30-minute sessions to satisfy the minimum recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Vigorous intensity, since 90 minutes weekly exceeds the 75 minute minimum", "B": "Light intensity, since consistent weekly activity meets the minimum", "C": "Either intensity, since three days per week sets the target", "D": "Moderate intensity, since three sessions of 30 minutes meet the weekly minimum"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Vigorous intensity, since 90 minutes weekly exceeds the 75 minute minimum): Three 30 minute sessions total 90 minutes per week. That exceeds the 75 minute vigorous minimum but falls short of the 150 minute moderate minimum. Light intensity does not count toward the guideline target. Frequency alone does not meet the recommendation, because total weekly time at the right intensity is what is counted."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 17-year-old soccer player is three weeks after a lateral ankle sprain and plays a tournament with three 90-minute matches in two days. No trainer will be available. Zinc oxide tape loosens with exercise. Which support is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "A closed basketweave tape job applied once before the first match", "B": "A lace-up ankle brace that she can adjust herself between halves", "C": "Kinesiology tape applied to the ankle in a fan pattern", "D": "A neoprene sleeve pulled over a sock for the entire tournament"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A lace-up ankle brace that she can adjust herself between halves): Rigid tape loses a large part of its support after about 10 to 20 minutes of exercise, and without a trainer it cannot be reapplied. A lace-up brace maintains support through activity and can be tightened by the athlete. Kinesiology tape and neoprene sleeves offer little mechanical restraint against inversion."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old has a swollen, warm-feeling knee with passive flexion limited to 80 degrees and extension lacking 5 degrees. The end feel is boggy at both limits and pain increases with passive overpressure. A student must decide whether hamstring stretching or another approach is appropriate. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Perform grade IV patellofemoral and tibiofemoral mobilization to break up the capsular swelling", "B": "Avoid forceful stretching, manage the effusion, and restore motion with gentle pain-free active range", "C": "Use PNF contract-relax with maximal hamstring contractions at 80 degrees to increase flexion range", "D": "Apply sustained quadriceps and hamstring stretching with overpressure to push through the boggy end feel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Avoid forceful stretching, manage the effusion, and restore motion with gentle pain-free active range): A warm swollen knee with boggy end feel and pain on overpressure is irritable with effusion, so forceful stretching is avoided and effusion managed while motion returns through gentle active range. Grade IV mobilization, maximal contract-relax and overpressure stretching all force an irritable joint and can increase swelling and pain."}, {"id": 769, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old man is three days into acute low back pain with marked muscle guarding. He cannot tolerate loaded or kneeling positions, yet the therapist needs to begin motor control retraining. Several core exercises could be considered, and bracing increases his pain. Which starting exercise best fits this stage?", "options": {"A": "Abdominal bracing during loaded lifting, using a light box from the floor", "B": "Bird dog on hands and knees, holding each limb lift for ten seconds each", "C": "Side bridge from the feet, holding until fatigue to build lateral endurance", "D": "Transversus abdominis drawing-in in hooklying, with low effort and normal breathing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transversus abdominis drawing-in in hooklying, with low effort and normal breathing): In the acute stage the first step is an unloaded, low effort exercise such as drawing-in in hooklying, which the patient can tolerate and which begins motor relearning. The bird dog and the side bridge are loaded and demand more control. Bracing under a lifting load is a later stage and aggravates pain here."}, {"id": 770, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old man has clumsy hands, trouble buttoning his shirt and an unsteady gait that has developed over several months. Neck rehabilitation is being considered, but the clinician first screens for upper motor neuron signs. Which finding most strongly suggests cervical myelopathy and calls for referral rather than exercise?", "options": {"A": "A positive Spurling test reproducing pain in the ipsilateral arm", "B": "Relief of arm pain when the hand is placed on top of the head", "C": "A diminished biceps reflex with weakness of elbow flexion on one side", "D": "A positive Hoffmann sign with brisk lower limb reflexes and clonus"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A positive Hoffmann sign with brisk lower limb reflexes and clonus): Cervical myelopathy produces upper motor neuron findings such as a positive Hoffmann sign, hyperreflexia below the lesion, clonus, Babinski sign and gait disturbance. Spurling's test, a reduced biceps reflex and the shoulder abduction relief sign all describe a single nerve root problem. Myelopathy needs surgical consultation, not a rehabilitation trial."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 74-year-old man with Parkinson disease is pulled gently backward at the shoulders and takes several tiny rapid steps backward without recovering. He has no orthostatic symptoms and is safe in a harness. Which training emphasis is the most appropriate for this problem?", "options": {"A": "Anticipatory postural adjustment training with self-initiated reaching in standing", "B": "Reactive stepping practice using large cued recovery steps", "C": "Cognitive dual-task walking with serial subtraction to improve attention during gait", "D": "Ankle strategy training with slow sways within the limits of stability"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reactive stepping practice using large cued recovery steps): A backward pull producing tiny rapid steps with no recovery shows poor reactive stepping. Practice with large cued recovery steps trains the response he lacks. Anticipatory reaching trains a different control mode, and dual-task walking trains attention. Ankle strategy sways fit small perturbations, not a pull that exceeds the ankle's reach."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old accountant has forward head posture and a dull neck ache by mid afternoon. She works at a desk with a single monitor and reads documents on screen for most of the day. Which workstation adjustment best supports neutral cervical alignment?", "options": {"A": "Raise the monitor so its top sits 6 inches above eye level", "B": "Lower the monitor near the keyboard so she can look down comfortably", "C": "Place the monitor to one side at 45 degrees from the keyboard", "D": "Set the monitor top at or just below eye level, arm's length away"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Set the monitor top at or just below eye level, arm's length away): A monitor with its top at or just below eye level and about an arm's length away lets the head sit over the shoulders with minimal neck flexion or extension. A screen well above eye level forces extension and one off to the side adds sustained rotation. A screen far below eye level promotes the forward head posture already present."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with a recent DEXA T-score of minus 3.1 has tight hamstrings and a rounded upper back. She asks for a home stretching program. The clinician wants an effective stretch that avoids loaded spinal flexion. Which program is best for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Ballistic bouncing toe touches from standing, performed for ten repetitions each morning", "B": "Long-sitting hamstring stretch reaching toward the toes with the back rounded, held 30 to 60 seconds", "C": "Standing forward bend with the hands to the floor held 30 to 60 seconds", "D": "Supine strap hamstring stretch held 30 to 60 seconds, repeated two to four times"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supine strap hamstring stretch held 30 to 60 seconds, repeated two to four times): Loaded spinal flexion raises vertebral compression fracture risk in osteoporosis, so a supine strap stretch, which keeps the spine unloaded, is safer. Older adults may benefit from holds toward 30 to 60 seconds, repeated two to four times. Standing and long-sitting forward bends load the flexed spine, and ballistic bouncing adds shear and compression risk."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A basketball player is ten days after a grade II lateral ankle sprain. She bears weight without pain and has minimal swelling, but she has not started balance work and her physician has cleared graded loading. Which proprioceptive activity is the best starting point?", "options": {"A": "Bilateral standing on a wobble board with support available", "B": "Single-leg stance on a foam pad with the eyes closed and head still", "C": "Full speed cutting drills on the court with defenders applying pressure", "D": "Single-leg hopping onto a low box and landing with control on both feet"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bilateral standing on a wobble board with support available): Early proprioceptive training after a sprain starts with supported bilateral tasks that restore ankle position sense without overloading healing ligaments. Single-leg foam with eyes closed, hopping, and cutting skip several steps of progression. Skipping steps raises the risk of re-injury."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 61-year-old man with coronary artery disease is cycling at 5 METs when he reports chest discomfort that he rates as 3 on a 4-point angina scale. Heart rate is 118 beats per minute and blood pressure is stable. The discomfort is similar to his usual angina. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Continue for two more minutes to see whether the discomfort fades as he warms up", "B": "Stop the exercise, seat him, and give his prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin per protocol", "C": "Reduce the resistance by half and continue at 3 METs while he reports symptoms", "D": "Switch him to arm ergometry at a low workload to keep the training stimulus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop the exercise, seat him, and give his prescribed sublingual nitroglycerin per protocol): Angina at a rating of 3 on a 4-point scale is a criterion for stopping exercise. He should be seated and given his prescribed nitroglycerin, with symptoms reassessed. Lowering the load, waiting, or changing to arm work all keep a symptomatic heart working. Stable blood pressure does not make continuing safe."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A woman six months postpartum notices a ridge forming down the midline of her abdomen during a curl-up, and palpation shows a gap about three finger widths wide above the umbilicus. The therapist wants a safe initial abdominal program for her. Which approach is best at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Repeated full curl-ups to strengthen the rectus abdominis and close the gap", "B": "Loaded trunk rotation with a medicine ball to stimulate oblique muscle growth", "C": "Transversus abdominis drawing-in with splinting, avoiding full curl-ups and loaded trunk flexion", "D": "Prone plank holds to maximum duration to build global trunk endurance and abdominal strength"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transversus abdominis drawing-in with splinting, avoiding full curl-ups and loaded trunk flexion): A midline ridge and a gap of three finger widths indicate diastasis recti. The first step is low load transversus abdominis activation with splinting, while exercises that increase doming, such as curl-ups and loaded flexion, are avoided. Rotation with a ball and prolonged planks raise pressure on the midline and can worsen the separation."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 72-year-old man has lumbar stenosis with neurogenic claudication that limits him to 150 yards of upright walking. He wants a cardiovascular conditioning plan that lets him train 30 minutes without provoking leg symptoms. His physician has cleared him for moderate exercise. Which aerobic option is most likely to be tolerated?", "options": {"A": "Stair climbing with the trunk extended and no handrail use", "B": "Stationary cycling in a flexed, forward-leaning position at low resistance", "C": "Downhill treadmill walking with long strides and an upright trunk", "D": "Treadmill walking on a steep incline held upright without handrail support"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stationary cycling in a flexed, forward-leaning position at low resistance): Cycling with a flexed trunk keeps the canal and foramina open, so the patient can build aerobic fitness without triggering claudication. Upright incline walking, downhill walking, and stair climbing all increase lumbar extension and typically reproduce symptoms. Cycling also allows duration to build gradually."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist treating a patient after prolonged immobilization wants to improve coordination and smooth transitions between elbow flexors and extensors. The patient should alternate isotonic contractions of agonist and antagonist while the therapist shifts resistance from one direction to the other without a pause for relaxation. Which proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation technique fits this description?", "options": {"A": "Rhythmic initiation, with passive motion progressing toward active assisted movement", "B": "Hold relax, with an isometric contraction followed by relaxation and passive stretch", "C": "Rhythmic stabilization, with alternating isometric holds and no intended joint motion", "D": "Slow reversal, with resistance moving smoothly from one muscle group to its antagonist"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Slow reversal, with resistance moving smoothly from one muscle group to its antagonist): Slow reversal alternates isotonic contractions of antagonists with no relaxation between them, which trains smooth reversals of direction. Rhythmic stabilization uses isometric holds, and hold relax is used to increase range. Rhythmic initiation addresses starting movement. The described alternating resisted motion matches slow reversal."}, {"id": 779, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Janda described a typical faulty pattern in prone hip extension testing for patients with a weak gluteus maximus. A clinician watches a 36-year-old with low back pain lift the leg from the table while palpating the muscles around the pelvis. Which feature characterizes the faulty pattern?", "options": {"A": "Early tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band activation with prolonged gluteus maximus relaxation", "B": "Early gluteus maximus activation with delayed hamstring and lumbar erector spinae activation", "C": "Delayed hamstring activation with early contralateral abdominal wall activation during the lift", "D": "Delayed gluteus maximus activation with early hamstring and lumbar erector spinae activation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Delayed gluteus maximus activation with early hamstring and lumbar erector spinae activation): The faulty pattern shows the gluteus maximus firing late while the hamstrings and lumbar erector spinae take over the lift, often with excess lumbar extension. The early gluteal pattern is the desired one. The abdominal and tensor patterns are not the classic fault in this test."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "On post-myocardial infarction day four, a 59-year-old man with a resting heart rate of 68 beats per minute walks the hospital corridor with a therapist. At 150 feet his heart rate is 96, blood pressure is stable, and he feels fine with a perceived exertion of 11. Which action is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Stop the walk and seat him to rest, then reassess heart rate and symptoms", "B": "Continue only to the next doorway, then stop once heart rate reaches 100 beats", "C": "Continue the walk but slow the pace and add a second therapist for monitoring", "D": "Continue to the end of the corridor, since he is symptom-free with low perceived exertion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop the walk and seat him to rest, then reassess heart rate and symptoms): Inpatient walking after a myocardial infarction commonly uses a heart rate limit of resting plus about 20 beats. His rise of 28 beats exceeds that limit even though he feels well, so activity should stop and he should rest. Low exertion ratings do not override the heart rate criterion. Continuing, even slowly, or setting a higher ceiling ignores the established guideline."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 63-year-old man has had six months of stiff legs and scrapes his toes while walking. He also has clumsy hands, brisk knee reflexes, a positive Hoffmann sign, and an extensor plantar response bilaterally. Which gait interpretation and next step are best?", "options": {"A": "Spastic gait from an upper motor neuron lesion, needing urgent referral", "B": "Parkinsonian gait from basal ganglia disease, needing a medication review and balance training", "C": "Steppage gait from a common peroneal neuropathy, needing orthosis and nerve conduction testing", "D": "Waddling gait from proximal myopathy, needing strengthening and a creatine kinase level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Spastic gait from an upper motor neuron lesion, needing urgent referral): Stiff scraping legs with brisk reflexes, a Hoffmann sign, bilateral extensor plantar responses, and clumsy hands indicate an upper motor neuron lesion, with cervical spondylotic myelopathy a leading concern needing urgent referral. A basal ganglia gait lacks the pyramidal signs. Peroneal neuropathy and proximal myopathy would reduce reflexes or leave the plantar response flexor, so they do not explain this picture."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "An electrical muscle stimulator is programmed for 12 seconds of contraction followed by 48 seconds of rest, and the patient will receive exactly 15 minutes of treatment. The clinician wants to document the duty cycle and the number of contractions the muscle will perform. Which pair of values is correct for this session?", "options": {"A": "An 80 percent duty cycle with 15 contractions in the session.", "B": "A 20 percent duty cycle with 75 contractions in the session.", "C": "A 25 percent duty cycle with 19 contractions in the session.", "D": "A 20 percent duty cycle with 15 contractions in the session."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A 20 percent duty cycle with 15 contractions in the session.): One cycle lasts 12 plus 48 equals 60 seconds, so the duty cycle is 12 divided by 60, or 20 percent. Fifteen minutes is 900 seconds, and 900 divided by 60 equals 15 cycles, so 15 contractions. Using on divided by off gives 25 percent, and dividing 900 by 48 gives 19. Dividing 900 by 12 gives 75, which ignores the rest period."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student reviews contraindications before applying a cold pack to a patient's painful wrist. The intake form lists four conditions, and the student must identify which one is a recognized contraindication to local cooling. Which condition should lead the student to withhold cryotherapy?", "options": {"A": "Acute rheumatoid arthritis flare with warm swollen joints and morning stiffness", "B": "Cryoglobulinemia, in which blood proteins precipitate when exposed to cold", "C": "Type 2 diabetes with intact sensation and normal skin circulation at the wrist", "D": "Chronic tendinosis with tenderness but no sensory loss and warm normal skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cryoglobulinemia, in which blood proteins precipitate when exposed to cold): Cryoglobulinemia is a recognized contraindication because proteins precipitate in cold and can occlude vessels. A warm rheumatoid flare, diabetes with intact sensation and circulation, and tendinosis with normal skin all tolerate local cooling, so none requires withholding cryotherapy."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 57-year-old woman with thyroid disease has shoulder pain and loss of reaching. External rotation is limited to 15 degrees when she moves it and also when the examiner moves it, with a firm end feel. Resisted tests are strong. Which finding best distinguishes her condition from a rotator cuff tear?", "options": {"A": "A positive drop arm test with weakness holding the arm abducted at 90 degrees", "B": "Pain is reproduced by resisted abduction while passive range stays full at end range", "C": "Passive motion is limited about as much as active motion, especially external rotation", "D": "Active motion is limited while passive motion is nearly full in all planes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Passive motion is limited about as much as active motion, especially external rotation): In adhesive capsulitis the capsule limits motion, so passive range is restricted as much as active range, with external rotation lost first and a firm end feel. In a cuff tear, active motion is lost while passive motion is nearly full. A drop arm sign and painful resisted abduction point toward tendon injury."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist is selecting exercises for a patient recovering from a lower extremity injury and wants to explain open versus closed kinetic chain. Which of the following exercises is a closed kinetic chain activity because the distal segment is fixed against a surface and the limb bears the load?", "options": {"A": "Standing leg extension against a weighted cuff while bearing body weight on the opposite limb", "B": "Supine straight leg raise with a cuff weight placed above the knee", "C": "Leg press with the feet planted firmly on the moving platform", "D": "Machine hamstring curl with the ankle pad moving through the full range of knee flexion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Leg press with the feet planted firmly on the moving platform): A closed chain exercise fixes the distal segment against a surface so the limb bears load, as when the feet press on a leg press platform. In a standing extension the working foot moves freely, and standing on the opposite leg does not make it closed. A supine straight leg raise and a machine curl also let the distal segment move freely, so they are open chain exercises."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 19-year-old gymnast with a Beighton score of 8 out of 9 reports hamstring and shoulder tightness during workouts. Passive hip flexion and shoulder rotation exceed normal range, and she has had two shoulder subluxations. She asks for deeper stretches. Which plan best protects the joints?", "options": {"A": "Avoid end-range stretching and prioritize strengthening and motor control within a controlled range", "B": "Apply grade IV joint mobilization to the glenohumeral joint to improve capsular extensibility", "C": "Add prolonged static stretches at the end of range to normalize muscle length", "D": "Use PNF contract-relax with partner overpressure to increase range before each practice session"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid end-range stretching and prioritize strengthening and motor control within a controlled range): A Beighton score of 8 shows generalized hypermobility, and the sensation of tightness is usually muscle protective tension around lax joints. Stretching or mobilizing at end range adds laxity and instability, while strengthening and motor control within a controlled range protect the joints. Partner overpressure and grade IV mobilization push a hypermobile joint beyond a safe range."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 58-year-old man leaves the hospital after a myocardial infarction with a measured functional capacity of about 4 METs. His therapist reviews home activities with him and wants to choose one that stays within that ceiling for sustained effort. Which activity has an energy cost closest to, but not above, his capacity?", "options": {"A": "Cycling outdoors at 12 to 14 miles per hour on a gently rolling route", "B": "Jogging on level ground at about 6 miles per hour for ten minutes", "C": "Level walking at a casual pace of about 3 miles per hour", "D": "Walking briskly uphill at 4 miles per hour on a 5 percent grade for twenty minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Level walking at a casual pace of about 3 miles per hour): Level walking at 3 mph costs about 3 to 3.5 METs, which fits under a 4 MET ceiling. Jogging at 6 mph is close to 10 METs and cycling at 12 to 14 mph is roughly 8 METs. Brisk uphill walking at 4 mph on a 5 percent grade is around 7 METs, well above his capacity, even though walking sounds gentle."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 30-year-old patient performs the drawing-in maneuver in hooklying and holds her breath for the entire ten-second contraction, with the ribs rising and the neck muscles tightening. The therapist wants the transversus abdominis activation sustained for the full hold. Which cue best corrects the problem?", "options": {"A": "Exhale fully and pause with the lungs empty for the duration of the hold", "B": "Continue the gentle abdominal contraction while breathing normally with relaxed ribs", "C": "Take a deep breath in and hold it for the whole contraction to stabilize the trunk", "D": "Draw the ribs down firmly with the upper abdominals to flatten the chest"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue the gentle abdominal contraction while breathing normally with relaxed ribs): The contraction should be sustained at low effort while the patient breathes normally, so the deep muscle works independently of respiration and the superficial muscles stay quiet. Breath holding in or out cannot be sustained for ten seconds and raises tension. Drawing the ribs down firmly recruits the upper abdominals."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 31-year-old man performed 6 weeks of isometric quadriceps training with the knee fixed at 60 degrees of flexion. Testing now shows large strength gains at 60 degrees but modest gains near full knee extension, where he needs strength for stairs. Based on joint angle specificity, which change to his program is best?", "options": {"A": "Add training at 60 degrees with eccentric emphasis, since stair strength depends on eccentric work", "B": "Switch to higher repetitions at 60 degrees, since endurance training improves terminal extension strength", "C": "Stretch the quadriceps and hamstrings daily, since terminal range depends on joint mobility alone", "D": "Add isometric or dynamic training across the range including near terminal extension"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Add isometric or dynamic training across the range including near terminal extension): Isometric strength gains are greatest at or near the joint angle trained and carry over only partly to far angles. Because he needs terminal extension strength for stairs, training must include that part of the range, either isometrically or through full dynamic motion. Eccentric work at the same angle does not change the angle problem. Higher repetitions build endurance, not angle specific strength, and stretching does not address a strength deficit."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A therapist teaches a patient recovering from stroke to rise from a chair using a new strategy. Over several sessions the patient improves. To promote long term retention of the skill rather than just good performance in clinic, how should the therapist adjust the delivery of augmented feedback as practice continues?", "options": {"A": "Give feedback after every repetition throughout training so errors are corrected at once", "B": "Switch to feedback only on correct trials so the patient repeats only successful movements", "C": "Gradually reduce the frequency of feedback so the patient learns to detect errors alone", "D": "Stop all feedback after the first session so the patient relies on internal cues"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gradually reduce the frequency of feedback so the patient learns to detect errors alone): Frequent augmented feedback helps early performance, but constant feedback can create dependence and weaken retention. Gradually reducing its frequency forces the patient to use intrinsic sensory information and detect errors, which improves learning that lasts. Removing feedback after one session is too abrupt, and giving feedback only on correct trials withholds the error information needed."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 55-year-old woman finishes a vigorous 20-minute stationary cycling interval and immediately stands to check her phone. Within seconds she feels dizzy and pale, her heart rate falls abruptly, and she sits down. She has no cardiac history. Which change to her next session best prevents this problem?", "options": {"A": "Add a gradual five to ten minute active cool-down of progressively lighter pedaling", "B": "Extend the warm-up to 20 minutes so the leg muscles are better prepared for effort", "C": "Replace intervals with continuous cycling at the same overall intensity throughout the session", "D": "Increase fluid intake to two liters during the session to expand blood volume"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add a gradual five to ten minute active cool-down of progressively lighter pedaling): Dizziness right after stopping vigorous leg exercise reflects venous pooling once the muscle pump stops, which drops preload and blood pressure. A gradual active cool-down keeps the pump working while heart rate and vessel tone come down. A longer warm-up does not address the post-exercise drop. Switching interval format or drinking large amounts of fluid leaves the abrupt stop in place."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old warehouse worker has worn a lumbosacral corset for eight hours daily for six months after a lumbar strain. He reports pain is mostly controlled but trunk endurance has declined and he fears removing it. What is the most appropriate plan for the corset?", "options": {"A": "Stop the corset immediately and restrict lifting for six weeks to rebuild endurance", "B": "Continue full day wear until he is pain free for a month before starting exercise", "C": "Reduce wear to demanding tasks only while starting a trunk stabilization exercise program", "D": "Change to a rigid chairback brace for stronger support during the workday"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Reduce wear to demanding tasks only while starting a trunk stabilization exercise program): Prolonged corset use can reduce trunk muscle endurance, so wear is cut back to the most demanding tasks while a stabilization program rebuilds muscle support. A more rigid brace adds dependence. Waiting for a month before exercise lets deconditioning continue. Abrupt removal with six weeks of restricted lifting does not rebuild endurance either."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A trainer is about to tape an ankle with rigid zinc oxide tape for an athlete returning after a sprain. Before the first anchor goes on, the athlete must hold the foot in a specific position that avoids limiting motion needed for push-off and avoids taping into a stretched position. Which position is correct?", "options": {"A": "Maximal dorsiflexion, to lock the talus in the mortise and block push-off", "B": "Full plantar flexion, to shorten the lateral ligaments before taping", "C": "Neutral position, with the ankle at about 90 degrees", "D": "Maximal inversion with the toes pulled up, to pre-load the lateral ligaments"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neutral position, with the ankle at about 90 degrees): Taping with the ankle in neutral, near 90 degrees, supports the joint without limiting motion needed for push-off or taping into stretch. End range dorsiflexion restricts function. Full plantar flexion tensions the anterior talofibular ligament and leaves the ankle in a vulnerable position. Inversion pre-loads the lateral ligaments in the injury position and risks further strain."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor's protocol for a small tender point on the medial epicondyle calls for 4 J/cm2. The probe's spot area is 0.25 cm2. Believing the protocol meant 4 J of energy, the clinician delivered 4 J to the point. What is the actual dose delivered, and what is the proper adjustment?", "options": {"A": "The dose was 4 J/cm2, so the treatment matched the protocol as given", "B": "The dose was 16 J/cm2, so reduce to 1 J of energy per point", "C": "The dose was 16 J/cm2, so halve the exposure to 2 J per point", "D": "The dose was 1 J/cm2, so increase the energy to 16 J per point"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The dose was 16 J/cm2, so reduce to 1 J of energy per point): Dividing 4 J by a 0.25 cm2 spot gives 16 J/cm2, four times the intended dose. The protocol needs 4 J/cm2 times 0.25 cm2, or 1 J per point. Equating energy with dose misses the overdose, inverting the area gives an underdose, and halving to 2 J still leaves double the intended dose."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 66-year-old woman is twelve weeks after nonoperative treatment of a proximal humerus fracture. Radiographs show union, she has near-full active motion, and no pain at rest. She has completed the passive and active-assisted phases. Which step is appropriate now?", "options": {"A": "Resume passive motion for another six weeks to protect the callus", "B": "Begin progressive resisted strengthening of the cuff and scapular muscles", "C": "Start joint mobilization with high-velocity thrust to restore end-range external rotation", "D": "Begin heavy overhead lifting with maximal load to test bone strength"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Begin progressive resisted strengthening of the cuff and scapular muscles): With radiographic union, near-full motion, and prior phases complete, progressive resistance for the cuff and scapular muscles is the next step. Returning to passive motion delays recovery. Heavy maximal overhead lifting is too aggressive, and high-velocity thrust near a recently healed fracture in an older patient is inappropriate."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A clinician treating a patient with UVB notices that the lamp beam strikes a sloped area of skin at an angle rather than straight on. The clinic teaches that the angle of incidence changes the dose received at the surface. According to the cosine law, at which angle of incidence is the delivered intensity greatest?", "options": {"A": "When the beam strikes the surface perpendicularly, an angle of incidence of zero degrees", "B": "When the beam strikes at 60 degrees to the line perpendicular to the surface of skin", "C": "When the beam strikes at a grazing angle near 90 degrees to the perpendicular", "D": "When the beam strikes at 45 degrees to the line perpendicular to the surface of skin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (When the beam strikes the surface perpendicularly, an angle of incidence of zero degrees): Intensity at the surface is proportional to the cosine of the angle of incidence measured from the perpendicular. The cosine is largest, equal to one, at zero degrees, so a perpendicular beam delivers the most. At 45 degrees the cosine is about 0.71 and at 60 degrees it is 0.5. Near 90 degrees the cosine approaches zero and almost nothing is delivered."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old long-distance driver reports deep buttock pain and intermittent posterior thigh tingling after prolonged sitting. Lumbar movements are normal, and the straight leg raise is negative. Palpation reproduces pain over the greater sciatic notch region. Which structure does the piriformis muscle most commonly irritate or compress in piriformis syndrome?", "options": {"A": "The sciatic nerve as it passes deep to or through the muscle belly", "B": "The obturator nerve in the obturator canal near the pubic ramus", "C": "The superior gluteal nerve above the muscle as it leaves the greater sciatic foramen", "D": "The L5 nerve root within the lateral recess of the spinal canal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The sciatic nerve as it passes deep to or through the muscle belly): The sciatic nerve passes deep to, or sometimes through, the piriformis, so a tight or spastic muscle can compress it. The obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh and does not pass here. The superior gluteal nerve exits above the piriformis, and an L5 root lesion would be expected to disturb lumbar movement or the straight leg raise."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 41-year-old woman exercises on a cycle ergometer at 100 watts. Before an eight-week program her heart rate at that workload was 150 beats per minute, and afterward it is 134 beats per minute, with unchanged oxygen consumption. The clinician explains what this lower submaximal heart rate reflects. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Increased resting vagal tone alone accounts for the lower rate, with stroke volume unchanged", "B": "Greater stroke volume delivers the same cardiac output at a lower heart rate", "C": "A higher maximal heart rate raises reserve, so submaximal workloads need fewer beats", "D": "Improved mechanical efficiency lowers oxygen consumption at 100 watts, so cardiac output falls"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Greater stroke volume delivers the same cardiac output at a lower heart rate): Training raises stroke volume, so the same cardiac output at 100 watts needs fewer beats. Oxygen consumption was unchanged, which rules out lower oxygen demand. Maximal heart rate does not rise with training. Vagal tone contributes, but it does not account for the change when stroke volume is held fixed."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "During interferential therapy for diffuse mid-thoracic muscle soreness in a patient without implanted devices, the chiropractor selects the vector scan option on a four-pole unit. The patient asks what the setting changes. The student must explain the effect of vector scan on the interference field during treatment. Which explanation is correct?", "options": {"A": "Channel amplitudes vary automatically, shifting the area of maximal interference across a wider region", "B": "The beat frequency varies automatically across a set range, reducing accommodation to a fixed rate", "C": "The carrier frequency steps upward during the session, so current penetrates progressively deeper", "D": "Electrode polarity alternates on a timer, spreading chemical effects beneath each pad"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Channel amplitudes vary automatically, shifting the area of maximal interference across a wider region): Vector scan changes the relative amplitudes of the two channels, which rotates or shifts the orientation of the interference field. The area of maximal modulation therefore moves across a larger region without repositioning pads. Varying the beat frequency is a separate feature called sweep. Interferential current has no polarity, and the carrier is not stepped to change depth."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "During a lecture on cryotherapy, an instructor describes a protective response in fingers and toes exposed to prolonged cold, during which blood vessels periodically open again after initial constriction. A student must name the practical meaning of this cold-induced vasodilation for treatment. Which statement describes it?", "options": {"A": "Histamine-mediated vessel dilation seen in cold urticaria after rewarming", "B": "Immediate vasodilation of deep muscle vessels that increases swelling after acute injury", "C": "Sustained vasoconstriction that continues without interruption throughout any prolonged cold exposure", "D": "Periodic vasodilation during prolonged cooling of the extremities that limits tissue freezing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Periodic vasodilation during prolonged cooling of the extremities that limits tissue freezing): With prolonged cold, vessels in the fingers and toes alternate between constriction and brief dilation, which delivers warm blood and helps protect against freezing. It is a normal protective reflex rather than an allergic reaction. It is not a sustained constriction, and it does not occur as a swelling response in deep muscle."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old woman at 30 weeks gestation has bilateral deep gluteal pain that began two weeks ago. The posterior pelvic pain provocation test and active straight leg raise reproduce her pain, and lumbar motion is normal. She has no fever, neurological loss, or vaginal bleeding. Which management plan is the best fit for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Supine bed rest with legs elevated for most of each day until delivery", "B": "Therapeutic ultrasound over the sacroiliac joints followed by sacral thrust manipulation", "C": "Lumbar traction with a pelvic harness in side-lying to open the sacroiliac joints", "D": "Pelvic belt, load modification, and gentle stabilization exercises"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pelvic belt, load modification, and gentle stabilization exercises): Positive pelvic provocation tests with a normal lumbar examination support pelvic girdle pain, which is treated conservatively with a belt, load management and stabilization. Ultrasound and thrust manipulation are not appropriate in late pregnancy. Lumbar traction targets a spinal problem, not the pelvic joints. Prolonged bed rest worsens deconditioning and does not help."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinic has two patients. Patient one has a chronic hamstring tendon problem at its ischial attachment, an area of about 8 cm2 and 2 cm deep. Patient two has diffuse tightness across both lumbar paraspinal regions, roughly 200 cm2. Which assessment is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Ultrasound suits both, because 1 MHz heats a field of any size in one pass", "B": "Ultrasound suits patient one, while patient two is better served by a hot pack", "C": "Ultrasound suits patient two, because a large area is best treated with a long pass", "D": "Ultrasound suits neither, because it cannot heat tendon or muscle tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ultrasound suits patient one, while patient two is better served by a hot pack): Ultrasound heats only a few times the ERA at a time, so it fits small, localized dense targets such as a tendon attachment. A 200 cm2 field would take an impractical number of sections. A hot pack heats that area better, and ultrasound does heat tendon and muscle effectively."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 45-year-old man with long-standing heavy alcohol use walks with his feet far apart, lurching side to side, with uneven step lengths. He has dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing, and his walking is no worse when he closes his eyes. Which gait pattern is present?", "options": {"A": "Cerebellar ataxia, a wide base with uneven step length", "B": "Parkinsonian gait, with short shuffling steps and stooping", "C": "Vestibular ataxic gait from unilateral labyrinth loss, with veering toward the affected side", "D": "Myopathic waddling gait, with pelvic drop and a wide base from proximal weakness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cerebellar ataxia, a wide base with uneven step length): A wide base, lurching, uneven step length, and dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing point to cerebellar ataxia, a typical result of alcohol related cerebellar degeneration. Vestibular loss produces veering rather than dysmetria. Parkinsonian gait has short shuffling steps, and waddling reflects proximal weakness with pelvic drop, so neither explains the limb incoordination."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 70-year-old woman has a stable T12 anterior wedge compression fracture from osteoporosis and mild pain with standing. The physician wants a brace that prevents forward flexion of the thoracolumbar junction while she begins walking. Which orthosis is the most suitable?", "options": {"A": "A flexible elastic corset that mainly increases intra-abdominal pressure around the waist", "B": "A Williams-type lumbosacral brace that flattens the lordosis and limits extension", "C": "A cervical thoracic orthosis with a sternal pad and an occipital support", "D": "A hyperextension orthosis with three-point pressure to limit thoracolumbar flexion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A hyperextension orthosis with three-point pressure to limit thoracolumbar flexion): Anterior wedge fractures are loaded by flexion, so a hyperextension orthosis with three-point pressure over the sternum, pubis, and thoracolumbar spine prevents flexion at the fracture. A flexible corset gives minimal control at that level. A Williams brace flattens the lordosis and limits extension, which is the opposite requirement. A cervical thoracic orthosis is positioned too high for T12."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A collegiate sprinter pulls up suddenly with posterior thigh pain at top speed during a race, and imaging later confirms a hamstring strain. Considering the usual injury pattern, which muscle is most commonly strained and in which part of the running cycle does the injury typically occur?", "options": {"A": "The semimembranosus, during early stance as it shortens to extend the hip", "B": "The biceps femoris long head, in late swing as it lengthens to slow the leg", "C": "The biceps femoris short head, during initial swing while it accelerates the thigh forward", "D": "The semitendinosus, during toe off as the knee extends against resistance"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The biceps femoris long head, in late swing as it lengthens to slow the leg): The long head of the biceps femoris is the muscle most often strained, usually in late swing when it contracts eccentrically while lengthening to slow the leg before foot strike. The medial hamstrings are involved less often. The short head crosses only the knee, so it does not fit a sprinting overstretch pattern."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A patient's gait speed rose from 0.90 to 1.20 meters per second over four weeks while cadence moved only from 100 to 102 steps per minute. The therapist wants to know what produced the faster speed. Which interpretation is best supported by the numbers?", "options": {"A": "A faster cadence of about 120 steps per minute, with step length unchanged near 0.54 meters", "B": "A longer step length, from about 0.54 meters to about 0.71 meters", "C": "A longer step length, from about 0.54 meters to about 0.60 meters", "D": "A shorter step time, from about 0.60 seconds to about 0.45 seconds, with step length unchanged"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A longer step length, from about 0.54 meters to about 0.71 meters): Speed equals step length times step rate. Cadence barely changed, from 100 to 102 steps per minute, so the gain comes from step length rising from about 0.54 to 0.71 meters. A cadence of 120 or step time near 0.45 seconds contradicts the numbers, and a length near 0.60 meters would not yield 1.20 meters per second."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 64-year-old man starts outpatient cardiac rehabilitation with an ejection fraction of 32 percent, a history of sustained ventricular tachycardia, and a peak capacity of 4.5 METs. A clinician uses standard risk stratification to plan supervision. Which monitoring level is most appropriate for his early exercise sessions?", "options": {"A": "High risk, with continuous ECG monitoring and direct staff supervision for an extended number of sessions", "B": "Not eligible for supervised exercise until the ejection fraction rises above 40 percent", "C": "Moderate risk, with intermittent ECG monitoring and supervision limited to the first two weeks", "D": "Low risk, with brief ECG monitoring and independent home exercise after a few supervised visits"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (High risk, with continuous ECG monitoring and direct staff supervision for an extended number of sessions): An ejection fraction below 40 percent, a history of ventricular tachycardia, and a capacity under 5 METs each place a patient in the high-risk category. High risk requires continuous ECG monitoring and close supervision until stability is shown. Lower categories under-monitor him. A low ejection fraction is a reason to supervise exercise carefully, not to withhold it."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has sharp left anterior chest wall pain worse with deep breathing and twisting. Palpation of the third and fourth costochondral junctions reproduces the pain, with no swelling. She has no fever, a normal resting ECG and no exertional symptoms. Which diagnosis and plan fit best?", "options": {"A": "Tietze syndrome, treated with reassurance, heat and a short course of anti-inflammatory medication", "B": "Costochondritis, treated with reassurance, heat and gentle pectoral stretching", "C": "Intercostal muscle strain, treated with rest, ice and avoidance of twisting for four weeks", "D": "Pericarditis, treated with referral for echocardiography before any exercise or activity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Costochondritis, treated with reassurance, heat and gentle pectoral stretching): Sharp chest wall pain that is reproduced at the costochondral junctions without swelling, with a normal ECG and no systemic signs, fits costochondritis managed with reassurance, heat and gentle stretching. Tietze syndrome is similar but involves swelling, which is absent. Muscle strain would be tender in the muscle belly instead. Pericarditis pain varies with position and often comes with fever, and its work up does not fit this local, reproducible picture."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 41-year-old cyclist has right buttock pain with posterior thigh ache after long rides. Lumbar flexion, extension, and repeated movements do not change symptoms. Straight leg raise is negative, but pain reproduces with resisted hip external rotation and with passive hip flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. Which intervention is the best initial choice?", "options": {"A": "Repeated prone press-ups to centralize the posterior thigh symptoms", "B": "Piriformis stretching in hip flexion and adduction plus rotator strengthening", "C": "Lumbar mechanical traction at 50 percent of body weight with the hips flexed", "D": "Hamstring ballistic stretching with the knee extended until the thigh pain resolves"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Piriformis stretching in hip flexion and adduction plus rotator strengthening): The pain pattern changes with hip movements and not lumbar movements, and the straight leg raise is negative, so the source is the piriformis rather than the spine. Stretching in flexion and adduction lengthens the muscle, and strengthening addresses weakness that overloads it. Lumbar traction and press-ups target a spinal source. Ballistic hamstring stretching misses the deep external rotator."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 70-year-old man with peripheral neuropathy has reduced ankle sensation and plantarflexor weakness. When a therapist nudges him backward at the shoulders, he bends at the waist and swings his hips rather than correcting at the ankles. Which strategy is he using, and why is it adopted in this case?", "options": {"A": "Hip strategy, adopted because distal ankle input and strength are inadequate for control", "B": "Ankle strategy, adopted because it activates muscles proximal to distal in sequence", "C": "Hip strategy, adopted because the surface is firm and the sway is small", "D": "Stepping strategy, adopted because the nudge pushed his center of mass past the base"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hip strategy, adopted because distal ankle input and strength are inadequate for control): Waist bending with hip motion is the hip strategy, which is recruited when ankle torque is limited by weakness, poor sensation, or a narrow or soft surface. A stepping response would move the feet. The ankle strategy works from distal to proximal and dominates on firm surfaces with small sway, which is not what this man is doing."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 48-year-old golfer reports pain at the inside of his elbow that worsens at ball impact. Tenderness is localized just distal to the medial epicondyle, and pain is provoked by resisted wrist flexion combined with forearm pronation. This presentation is consistent with which condition and structure?", "options": {"A": "Distal biceps tendinopathy involving the insertion on the radial tuberosity with painful resisted supination", "B": "Lateral epicondylitis involving the common extensor origin, most often extensor carpi radialis brevis", "C": "Cubital tunnel syndrome involving ulnar nerve compression behind the medial epicondyle with paresthesia", "D": "Medial epicondylitis involving the flexor-pronator origin, most often pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Medial epicondylitis involving the flexor-pronator origin, most often pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis): Golfer's elbow is a tendinopathy at the flexor-pronator origin, and resisted wrist flexion with pronation loads pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis. Lateral epicondylitis is the extensor-side problem. Cubital tunnel syndrome causes ring and small finger paresthesia, and distal biceps problems hurt with resisted supination."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old man with low back pain reports numbness around the groin, difficulty starting urination, and weakness in both legs that developed over two days. His physician has ordered lumbar traction for a presumed disc problem. What is the most appropriate response from the therapist at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Apply gentle supine traction at 25 percent of body weight to unload the sacral roots", "B": "Use prone traction with lumbar extension to centralize the symptoms before referring", "C": "Do not give traction and arrange emergency referral for suspected cauda equina compression", "D": "Defer traction for a week and reassess if the numbness has not resolved"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Do not give traction and arrange emergency referral for suspected cauda equina compression): Saddle numbness, urinary difficulty and progressive bilateral leg weakness are signs of cauda equina syndrome, a surgical emergency where delay risks permanent loss of function. Traction cannot decompress the cauda equina. Centralization strategies and a one week wait both delay urgent surgical evaluation. Light traction force does not change the need for referral."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "During a graded treadmill test in a healthy 26-year-old, workload keeps increasing each minute, but his oxygen uptake changes by less than 150 mL/min across the last two stages and then stays flat until he stops. The clinician must label the result. Which interpretation fits this oxygen uptake pattern?", "options": {"A": "Only a VO2 peak was reached, because the respiratory exchange ratio did not exceed 1.0", "B": "A submaximal result, since oxygen uptake should keep climbing until the final stage", "C": "The test was invalid, since a plateau means the workload was set too low", "D": "A true VO2 max was reached, as oxygen uptake plateaued despite rising workload"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A true VO2 max was reached, as oxygen uptake plateaued despite rising workload): The classic criterion for VO2 max is a plateau in oxygen uptake, usually a rise of less than 150 mL/min or 2.1 mL/kg/min, despite rising workload. A peak value is reported when no plateau appears, and the stem gives no exchange ratio data. Real maximal tests do not need uptake to rise to the end. A plateau is the expected sign of maximal capacity, not evidence that the workload was too low."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 28 year old woman at 16 weeks of pregnancy has two complaints: pubic symphysis pain and plantar heel pain from plantar fasciitis. She asks about laser therapy for these problems. Which region is appropriate for laser treatment under standard pregnancy precautions?", "options": {"A": "The pubic symphysis, delivered at half the standard dose with eye precautions", "B": "The plantar heel, delivered with standard eye safety and dose precautions", "C": "The lower abdominal wall, delivered at a reduced dose with eye precautions", "D": "The anterior pelvis over the iliac fossa, delivered at a low dose"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The plantar heel, delivered with standard eye safety and dose precautions): Laser energy is not applied over the abdomen or pelvic region during pregnancy, so the symphysis, abdominal wall, and iliac fossa are avoided. The heel is remote from the uterus and can be treated with ordinary precautions. Reducing the dose does not turn a contraindicated region into a safe one."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 62 year old retired machinist has chronic right shoulder pain. During examination the chiropractor notices a biopsy-confirmed squamous cell carcinoma, not yet treated, on the skin over the right upper trapezius. Laser therapy is being considered for the shoulder. Which treatment decision is consistent with standard laser contraindications?", "options": {"A": "Use a red 660 nm probe over the lesion because shallow penetration spares deeper tissue", "B": "Cover the lesion with gauze and irradiate the surrounding trapezius at a standard dose", "C": "Avoid irradiating the skin over the untreated carcinoma and keep treatment away from that region", "D": "Treat the upper trapezius at the lowest available dose to avoid stimulating the lesion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid irradiating the skin over the untreated carcinoma and keep treatment away from that region): Photobiomodulation can raise cell energy metabolism, so irradiation over an active malignancy is contraindicated. A low dose still delivers light to the lesion, and gauze does not block the beam. A shallow red wavelength is absorbed in the very skin layers that contain this carcinoma."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A cardiac rehabilitation patient finishes a 30-minute cycling bout at a moderate intensity and wants to sit down immediately to rest. The therapist instead has him pedal slowly against minimal resistance for five to ten minutes. What is the main physiological purpose of this gradual cool-down period after vigorous leg exercise?", "options": {"A": "To speed removal of lactate so that next-day muscle soreness is prevented", "B": "To maintain venous return so blood does not pool in the legs and drop pressure", "C": "To prevent hypoglycemia by keeping the muscles burning glucose after the bout", "D": "To finish repaying the oxygen debt so consumption returns to resting levels within minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To maintain venous return so blood does not pool in the legs and drop pressure): A gradual cool-down keeps the leg muscle pump working so venous return is maintained and blood does not pool, preventing a drop in pressure. Lactate clearance does not prevent next day soreness. Glucose burning is not the purpose and hypoglycemia is not a cool-down concern. Excess oxygen consumption takes much longer than a few minutes to normalize."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old recreational runner recovering from a lumbar strain is cleared to begin return-to-run training. The therapist plans a functional progression of stabilization tasks that moves from stable to unstable, slow to fast, and simple to complex surfaces and movements. Which order follows these principles correctly?", "options": {"A": "Supine isometric brace, standing split stance, quadruped limb lifts, then single-leg hops with rotation", "B": "Single-leg hops with rotation, standing split stance, quadruped limb lifts, then a supine isometric brace", "C": "Quadruped limb lifts, supine isometric brace, single-leg hops, then standing split stance with rotation", "D": "Supine isometric brace, quadruped limb lifts, standing split stance, then single-leg hops with rotation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supine isometric brace, quadruped limb lifts, standing split stance, then single-leg hops with rotation): A functional progression begins in supported, low demand positions and moves toward upright, dynamic tasks, so supine bracing comes first, then quadruped lifts, then standing split stance, and finally hopping with rotation. Putting standing before quadruped skips a step. Hops before split stance raise the demand too soon. Reversing the order begins with the most complex task."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "Three weeks after a painful first session of eccentric hamstring curls, a 24-year-old athlete repeats the identical workout and reports far less soreness and a quicker return of strength. He asks the physiotherapist why the second session was easier on him. Which phenomenon best accounts for the milder response to the second bout?", "options": {"A": "The repeated bout effect, a protective neural, connective tissue and cellular adaptation", "B": "A shift of the working fibers from fast twitch to slow twitch types", "C": "Depletion of the enzymes that cause inflammation after the first session", "D": "Training-induced hypertrophy that fully replaced the damaged fibers within three weeks"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The repeated bout effect, a protective neural, connective tissue and cellular adaptation): A single bout of unaccustomed eccentric exercise protects against damage and soreness from later bouts for weeks to months, which is called the repeated bout effect. Proposed mechanisms include improved motor unit recruitment, stronger connective tissue and cellular remodeling. Fiber type conversion, enzyme depletion and rapid hypertrophy are not accepted explanations for the milder response."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 46-year-old woman is eight weeks after abdominal surgery. The incision is fully closed, dry and pink, but the scar is adherent and pulls when she bends or twists. The surgeon has cleared soft-tissue work. Which massage technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Vigorous hacking directly along the incision to stimulate remodeling", "B": "Cross-fiber friction applied only after waiting a further six months for the scar", "C": "Deep effleurage directly over the scar from the pubic bone toward the sternum", "D": "Skin rolling and small circular friction along and around the healed scar"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Skin rolling and small circular friction along and around the healed scar): Once a scar is closed, dry and cleared by the surgeon, skin rolling and small circular friction mobilize the adherent layers and improve pliability. Hacking transmits force across the fresh scar, and gliding strokes do not engage the adhesion. Waiting six months is not required once the wound is stable."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old retired machinist has had chronic knee osteoarthritis pain for two years. Four-pole interferential current at a 100 Hz beat and sensory intensity gave relief for only an hour after three visits. The chiropractor wants to try a different mechanism of analgesia. Which parameter change is best?", "options": {"A": "Raise the carrier to 8000 Hz at sensory intensity", "B": "Lower the beat to 2 to 10 Hz with intensity giving visible contraction", "C": "Lower the beat to 2 Hz but also reduce intensity to a subsensory level for comfort", "D": "Raise the beat to 150 Hz at the same sensory intensity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower the beat to 2 to 10 Hz with intensity giving visible contraction): Endorphin-mediated analgesia is usually pursued with a low beat, around 2 to 10 Hz, at a strong, comfortable intensity that produces visible contractions. A higher beat at sensory intensity remains in the gate control mechanism that has already given only brief relief. A carrier change does not alter the mechanism, and a subsensory level would not engage the nerves."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A multi-provider chiropractic practice is deciding whether a new 10 W therapeutic laser needs stricter handling than its existing 100 mW probe. The compliance officer asks what hazard feature separates class 4 devices from class 3B devices in the standard laser safety classification. Which feature is the correct distinction?", "options": {"A": "Class 4 beams carry a lower hazard because their larger spot reduces irradiance on skin", "B": "Class 4 beams are pulsed rather than continuous, which produces higher peak irradiance than class 3B", "C": "Class 4 beams can injure eyes and skin from diffuse reflection and can ignite materials", "D": "Class 4 beams are invisible, which removes the ability of the blink reflex to protect"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Class 4 beams can injure eyes and skin from diffuse reflection and can ignite materials): Class 4 begins above 500 mW, and at that output even a diffusely scattered beam can injure the eye or skin, and the beam can start fires. Invisibility depends on wavelength, and pulsing is a delivery mode. Neither defines the class, and a larger spot does not lower the hazard of a high-power beam."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician delivers dexamethasone sodium phosphate by iontophoresis to a patient with lateral epicondylitis. The drug dissociates in solution into a negatively charged active ion. The clinician soaks the drug into one electrode pad and must choose which electrode polarity to use there. Which polarity setting drives the drug into the tissue?", "options": {"A": "Connect the drug pad as the negative electrode over the tendon.", "B": "Place the drug under the dispersive electrode, away from the tendon, as the positive pole.", "C": "Connect the drug pad as the positive electrode over the tendon.", "D": "Connect the drug pad to an alternating output so both polarities drive ions."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Connect the drug pad as the negative electrode over the tendon.): Like charges repel, so a negatively charged ion is pushed away from the negative electrode and into the tissue beneath it. The drug pad over the tendon is therefore connected as the negative electrode. A positive pad would attract the ion back toward the pad. Alternating current reverses polarity and produces no net ion movement, and a remote pad would not deliver drug to the tendon."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A gymnastics coach has athletes bounce rhythmically at the end of their hamstring range with rapid, repeated movements before practice. A student chiropractor reviewing the method asks which neurophysiological response this fast, repeated lengthening most strongly provokes in the stretched muscle. What response is it?", "options": {"A": "Reciprocal inhibition of the hamstrings produced by reflex firing of the quadriceps motor neurons", "B": "Viscoelastic creep producing permanent plastic elongation of the muscle after a few quick repetitions", "C": "Muscle spindle stretch reflex activation producing reflex contraction in the muscle being lengthened", "D": "Golgi tendon organ firing that inhibits the muscle and allows deeper, safer lengthening"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Muscle spindle stretch reflex activation producing reflex contraction in the muscle being lengthened): Rapid, forceful lengthening excites the muscle spindles, and the stretch reflex then contracts the same muscle, which works against lengthening. Golgi tendon organ inhibition needs sustained tension, as in a held contraction or long static hold. Reciprocal inhibition belongs to agonist contraction methods. Creep needs a sustained load over time, so a few quick bounces do not produce it."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractor treats acute ankle swelling after an inversion sprain with high voltage pulsed current and places the negative electrode over the swollen region. A student asks why that polarity is chosen for edema. Which explanation matches the rationale given in standard physical agent textbooks?", "options": {"A": "Negative polarity heats the tissue, which dilates lymphatics and flushes fluid proximally", "B": "Negative polarity attracts positively charged sodium ions, which draw fluid back into vessels", "C": "Negative polarity strongly stimulates the muscle pump, so contraction squeezes fluid proximally", "D": "Negative polarity repels negatively charged plasma proteins, limiting leakage from capillaries into the interstitium"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Negative polarity repels negatively charged plasma proteins, limiting leakage from capillaries into the interstitium): The textbook rationale for negative polarity in acute edema is that negatively charged plasma proteins are repelled from the electrode, so less protein leaks into the interstitium and less fluid follows. Ion attraction does not explain the effect. High voltage pulsed current at sensory level produces no meaningful heat. Muscle pumping needs motor-level contraction, which is not the goal in acute swelling."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 28-year-old woman six weeks postpartum has sacroiliac joint pain that is worse with single-leg stance and rolling over in bed. The therapist fits a pelvic support belt to compress the pelvis. Where should the belt be positioned for best effect?", "options": {"A": "Across the lower ribs at the tenth thoracic level, above the waist", "B": "At the level of the iliac crests, over the fourth lumbar spinous process and lower abdomen", "C": "Just below the anterior superior iliac spines, across the greater trochanters", "D": "Across the midsacrum and gluteal fold, well below the level of the greater trochanters"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Just below the anterior superior iliac spines, across the greater trochanters): A sacroiliac belt works by compressing the pelvic ring, so it is worn low, just below the anterior superior iliac spines and across the greater trochanters. At the iliac crests it acts as a lumbar support and does not compress the sacroiliac joints well. A belt at the tenth thoracic level is a thoracic position. A belt that sits well below the trochanters slips down and misses the pelvic ring."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "An assistant is teaching a chiropractic student how heart rate reserve is used to set training zones for a 45-year-old woman beginning an aerobic program. The woman has a known resting rate of 68 beats per minute, and the assistant wants her target to account for that baseline. Which formula describes the Karvonen method for a target heart rate?", "options": {"A": "Age-predicted maximal heart rate multiplied by the desired intensity fraction alone", "B": "Resting heart rate times the desired intensity fraction, then add the predicted maximum", "C": "Heart rate reserve times the desired intensity fraction, then subtract the resting rate", "D": "Heart rate reserve times the desired intensity fraction, then add the resting rate"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heart rate reserve times the desired intensity fraction, then add the resting rate): The Karvonen method first finds heart rate reserve as maximal minus resting heart rate, multiplies it by the chosen intensity fraction, and then adds the resting rate back. Adding resting rate restores the baseline that was removed when the reserve was calculated. A straight percentage of maximum is a different method that ignores resting rate. Subtracting resting rate or multiplying resting rate by the fraction would give targets that fall below real working heart rates."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient practicing a sit-to-stand transfer reaches the standing position every time, but she pushes off with her arms and leans her trunk forward. The therapist wants to improve movement quality rather than success of the task. Which type of augmented feedback best addresses this need during practice?", "options": {"A": "Knowledge of results, telling her the transfer was completed successfully each time", "B": "Knowledge of results, giving the time taken to complete five transfers", "C": "Knowledge of performance, describing how the trunk and arms moved during the task", "D": "Intrinsic feedback, relying on her own sense of the movement without comment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Knowledge of performance, describing how the trunk and arms moved during the task): Knowledge of performance describes the movement pattern, such as the arm push-off and the trunk lean, and is suited to correcting quality when the goal is already achieved. Knowledge of results reports the outcome and adds nothing here because she succeeds every time. Intrinsic feedback alone has not led her to detect the error."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 58-year-old man has no visible response when the patellar tendon is tapped while he sits relaxed and attentive, with the examiner using a reflex hammer correctly. Strength and sensation in both legs are normal. Before documenting an absent reflex, which maneuver should the examiner perform?", "options": {"A": "Reposition the knee in near full extension and tap again with the flat of the hammer", "B": "Test the other knee and document hyporeflexia if both sides respond equally weakly", "C": "Repeat the tap with reinforcement, such as hooking the clasped hands and pulling them apart", "D": "Grade the reflex as zero and order nerve conduction studies to look for neuropathy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Repeat the tap with reinforcement, such as hooking the clasped hands and pulling them apart): A reflex that is absent may simply be suppressed by tension, so the examiner should use reinforcement before documenting. Hooking the clasped hands and pulling apart is the Jendrassik maneuver and facilitates the reflex. Changing knee position or comparing sides does not facilitate it. Ordering nerve conduction studies before reinforcement skips a simple step."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 26-year-old rugby back has returned to practice after a grade 1 biceps femoris strain, which he has now injured twice in one season. He asks for the exercise with the best evidence for lowering recurrence during the late stage of rehabilitation. Which exercise best fits this goal?", "options": {"A": "Static hamstring stretching held for thirty seconds before each practice session and game", "B": "Prone knee flexion with ankle weights performed concentrically in short sets after practice", "C": "Seated hamstring curls on a machine with light weight and high repetitions each session", "D": "Nordic hamstring curls performed eccentrically with a partner anchoring the heels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nordic hamstring curls performed eccentrically with a partner anchoring the heels): Nordic hamstring exercise loads the hamstrings eccentrically at long muscle length, which matches the usual injury mechanism and has been shown to lower recurrence rates. Light concentric curls and ankle weight work do not create enough eccentric overload. Static stretching improves range but does not protect against reinjury on its own."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old retired teacher with cervical radiculopathy is anxious about neck traction and shows marked muscle guarding when the therapist touches her neck. The plan calls for a first mechanical cervical traction session today. Which initial setup is most appropriate to let the guarding settle and the treatment be tolerated?", "options": {"A": "Seated with the neck extended and a rapid increase to the target force", "B": "Supine with knees supported, low comfortable force, and gradual increases over visits", "C": "Seated upright with a high initial force so the weight of the head is overcome immediately", "D": "Prone with the head over the table edge and a heavy static pull"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Supine with knees supported, low comfortable force, and gradual increases over visits): A supine, supported position lets the cervical muscles relax and removes the weight of the head as a variable, and a low starting force raised over visits builds tolerance. A seated high force would provoke more guarding in an anxious patient. Extension narrows the foramina and a fast ramp increases stress. A prone position with the head unsupported at the table edge is awkward and unsafe."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 22-year-old volleyball player is three weeks past a grade II lateral ankle sprain and is cleared to return to practice. She asks what external support will best lower her risk of repeating the injury during play. Which external support is most appropriate for her return to play?", "options": {"A": "Kinesiology tape applied around the ankle without a brace, renewed after practice", "B": "High-top shoes with extra padding around the malleoli instead of an ankle device", "C": "A lace-up or semi-rigid ankle brace worn inside the shoe during practice and games", "D": "An elastic compression sleeve that covers the ankle but has no lateral stays"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A lace-up or semi-rigid ankle brace worn inside the shoe during practice and games): Lace-up and semi-rigid braces restrict inversion while allowing sagittal motion, and they lower recurrent sprain rates during sport. Kinesiology tape offers mild sensory input but little mechanical restraint. Padded high-top shoes do not control inversion, and an elastic sleeve gives warmth and compression but no lateral support."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with B12 deficiency neuropathy has absent ankle vibration sense and sways more with eyes closed. She walks well in daylight but drifts to one side at night and reports no dizziness. Which training emphasis best compensates for her sensory loss?", "options": {"A": "Fine motor hand coordination activities to improve cortical mapping", "B": "Vestibular habituation with repeated head turns to retrain the labyrinth", "C": "Eyes closed single-leg stance daily to force recovery of proprioception", "D": "Sensory substitution using visual and vestibular cues, with good lighting at home"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sensory substitution using visual and vestibular cues, with good lighting at home): When proprioception is lost, balance training teaches reliance on intact senses, vision and the vestibular system, and makes the environment safer with lighting. Practicing with the eyes closed removes the cue she uses to stay upright. Her vestibular system is not the problem, and hand tasks do not address leg input."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient has a painful ganglion region over the first dorsal compartment of the wrist, an area of about 2 cm by 2 cm with a curved surface. The clinic has a large head with an ERA of 10 cm2 and a small head with an ERA of 1 cm2. Which head is most suitable?", "options": {"A": "The small head, because its ERA matches the small area and it can keep flat contact", "B": "The large head, because a larger ERA delivers more energy to the wrist and surrounding tendons", "C": "The large head, because a larger ERA allows deeper penetration into the first dorsal compartment", "D": "The large head, because a larger ERA lowers the intensity and so protects the tendons here"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The small head, because its ERA matches the small area and it can keep flat contact): The effective radiating area should match the target. The small head fits the 2 cm area and keeps flat contact on a curved surface. A large head overlaps surrounding tissue and loses contact, ERA does not set penetration depth, and a lower intensity does not compensate for poor coupling."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 21-year-old basketball player shows dynamic knee valgus on a single-leg squat. Manual testing shows 5 of 5 hip abduction and external rotation strength, hip range is full, and cueing with a mirror corrects the knee alignment immediately. Which intervention best fits this finding?", "options": {"A": "Foot orthoses fitted to control rearfoot pronation as the primary treatment", "B": "Heavy hip abductor strengthening at maximal loads to build absolute strength", "C": "Motor control retraining with feedback and progressive single-leg task practice", "D": "Stretching of the hip adductors and the iliotibial band before activity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Motor control retraining with feedback and progressive single-leg task practice): Normal strength, full range, and immediate correction with cueing show the problem is movement control rather than capacity, so feedback and graded task practice are the best fit. Maximal strengthening or stretching addresses deficits that were not found. Orthoses do not retrain the movement pattern."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old retired carpenter has groin pain and stiffness after sitting, with radiographs showing joint space narrowing and osteophytes at the right hip. The clinician measures passive motion to look for the classic capsular pattern of hip osteoarthritis. Which group of motions is characteristically most limited in this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation of the hip joint, with abduction preserved", "B": "External rotation, extension, and adduction of the hip joint, with flexion preserved", "C": "Limitation of internal rotation, flexion, and abduction of the hip joint, with extension less affected", "D": "Extension, internal rotation, and adduction of the hip joint, with flexion preserved"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Limitation of internal rotation, flexion, and abduction of the hip joint, with extension less affected): The hip capsular pattern shows greatest limitation of internal rotation, flexion, and abduction, with less limitation of extension and relatively preserved external rotation. This is a typical finding in hip osteoarthritis. The other combinations include motions that remain comparatively free in a capsular restriction."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 44-year-old office manager with mechanical neck pain for three months has stiff mid-thoracic segments with limited extension, no arm symptoms and no red flags on screening. Clinical trials support adding which intervention to a neck exercise program to reduce pain and disability?", "options": {"A": "Therapeutic ultrasound to the upper trapezius added to a neck range of motion program", "B": "Thoracic manipulation or mobilization added to neck and scapular strengthening", "C": "Kinesiology tape applied over the posterior neck and upper thoracic region for four weeks", "D": "Intermittent cervical traction at moderate force added to neck stretching exercises"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Thoracic manipulation or mobilization added to neck and scapular strengthening): For mechanical neck pain with stiff mid thoracic segments, trials support adding thoracic mobilization or manipulation to neck and scapular strengthening. Therapeutic ultrasound is a passive modality with little support for lasting benefit. Kinesiology tape shows little effect on pain or disability in this condition. Intermittent traction added to stretching has limited evidence, so none of them matches the support for thoracic manual therapy."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old warehouse worker has a chronic hamstring strain with scar thickening inside the muscle belly, estimated 4 cm below the skin surface. The chiropractic student wants to heat this deep tissue with continuous therapeutic ultrasound. Which frequency and rationale best match the treatment goal for this patient?", "options": {"A": "3 MHz, because its beam is absorbed within the first two centimeters of tissue", "B": "3 MHz, because its shorter wavelength penetrates farthest into muscle beyond four centimeters", "C": "1 MHz, because its longer wavelength penetrates to about 3 to 5 centimeters", "D": "1 MHz, because its beam is absorbed within the first two centimeters of tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (1 MHz, because its longer wavelength penetrates to about 3 to 5 centimeters): Therapeutic ultrasound at 1 MHz penetrates roughly 3 to 5 cm, so it reaches a muscle belly 4 cm deep. A 3 MHz beam is absorbed in the first 1 to 2 cm and would mostly heat skin and superficial fascia. The wavelength and attenuation both favor the lower frequency for deep targets."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetic peripheral neuropathy has chronic lower leg pain. His feet cannot detect light touch or temperature. The chiropractor plans interferential current and normally sets intensity from the patient's report of tingling. Which approach is best for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Raise intensity until tingling is felt, then inspect the skin at the next visit", "B": "Set intensity by visible response, inspect skin before and after, and use a shorter session", "C": "Use the patient's report as usual, because pain fibers remain intact in neuropathy", "D": "Use a higher carrier frequency, because impaired skin conducts less current"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Set intensity by visible response, inspect skin before and after, and use a shorter session): With impaired sensation the patient cannot report the stimulus reliably, so intensity is guided by visible response and the skin is inspected before and after to detect injury. A shorter session limits risk. Relying on the report or chasing tingling can lead to overdosing. A carrier change does not address reduced sensation."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "Inman described scapulohumeral rhythm during full arm elevation through abduction. A clinician is teaching students the overall ratio of glenohumeral to scapulothoracic motion across the whole range of elevation in a healthy shoulder, as used when judging whether a patient's scapula moves too early or too late. Which ratio is correct?", "options": {"A": "Two degrees of glenohumeral motion for each degree of scapulothoracic motion", "B": "Equal glenohumeral and scapulothoracic motion throughout the entire range of elevation", "C": "One degree of glenohumeral motion for each two degrees of scapulothoracic motion", "D": "Three degrees of glenohumeral motion for each degree of scapulothoracic motion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two degrees of glenohumeral motion for each degree of scapulothoracic motion): Over the full range of elevation the glenohumeral joint contributes about 120 degrees and the scapulothoracic joint about 60 degrees, a 2 to 1 ratio. The reversed or equal ratios do not match measured motion. A larger early ratio near 3 to 1 appears in the first part of the range, not across the whole arc."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 46-year-old woman has sharp pain at the left medial calcaneal tubercle that is worst with her first steps in the morning. She lands on the left forefoot and her left stance is short. Passive ankle dorsiflexion is 10 degrees past neutral. Which interpretation is best?", "options": {"A": "Equinus gait from a tight gastrocnemius limiting ankle dorsiflexion", "B": "Calcaneal gait from weak plantarflexors causing excess dorsiflexion", "C": "Antalgic gait avoiding heel loading because of plantar heel pain", "D": "Steppage gait from weak dorsiflexors causing toe drag in swing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Antalgic gait avoiding heel loading because of plantar heel pain): A short stance, a forefoot landing, and heel pain that peaks with the first steps indicate protective unloading of the painful heel. Passive dorsiflexion of 10 degrees is normal, so a tight gastrocnemius does not explain the forefoot landing. Steppage and calcaneal gaits come from weakness, not pain."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 34-year-old carpenter wears a cast on his right forearm for 5 weeks after a distal radius fracture, with no movement permitted. His therapist wants to limit strength loss in the casted arm. Based on the cross education effect, which approach is best supported by neural adaptation research?", "options": {"A": "Rest both arms for the first 3 weeks so neural pathways are protected from overload", "B": "Use massage on the left forearm daily since cross education depends on increased blood flow", "C": "Train the left arm with light loads for endurance since transfer needs high repetitions", "D": "Train the uninjured left arm with progressive resistance exercise several times each week"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Train the uninjured left arm with progressive resistance exercise several times each week): Cross education is the finding that resistance training one limb raises strength in the opposite, untrained limb, mostly through central neural adaptation. Progressive resistance training of the uninjured arm can therefore blunt strength loss in the casted arm. Resting both arms gives no stimulus, light high-repetition work is a poor dose for transfer, and massage does not drive neural adaptation."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 35-year-old graphic designer has rounded shoulders and tightness across the anterior chest, with limited horizontal abduction. The clinician selects a doorway stretch for the pectoralis major. Which setup places the muscle on stretch while keeping the shoulder joint in a stable, supported position?", "options": {"A": "Elbow bent behind the head with the opposite hand pressing the elbow downward", "B": "Arm swung across the chest at shoulder height and pulled with the other hand", "C": "Wall slide with hands overhead and the shoulders elevated toward the ears", "D": "Forearms on the door frame with elbows at shoulder height, stepping forward through the doorway"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Forearms on the door frame with elbows at shoulder height, stepping forward through the doorway): A doorway stretch with the forearms on the frame and the elbows near shoulder height places the shoulders in horizontal abduction and external rotation, which lengthens the pectoralis major while forearm support reduces stress on the joint. Pulling the arm across the chest stretches the posterior structures. An overhead elbow press is a triceps stretch, and elevating the shoulders during a wall slide does not target the chest."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 69-year-old woman with painful hip osteoarthritis cannot walk a full mile or run but cycles comfortably. Her physician has not cleared her for maximal testing. A chiropractor wants a safe estimate of aerobic capacity using her heart rate response at fixed submaximal workloads. Which test is most appropriate for her?", "options": {"A": "The Rockport one-mile walk test, using finishing time and end heart rate", "B": "The Cooper 12-minute run test, using the total distance covered on a track", "C": "A submaximal cycle ergometer test, extrapolating VO2 max from heart rate at set workloads", "D": "An Astrand bench step test, estimating VO2 max from heart rate after stepping"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A submaximal cycle ergometer test, extrapolating VO2 max from heart rate at set workloads): A submaximal cycle ergometer test uses heart rate at fixed workloads to predict VO2 max and avoids weight bearing through the painful hip. The Rockport test could suit many adults, but she cannot walk a full mile. The Cooper run is impossible for her. A bench step test loads the arthritic hip with repeated stepping and is a poor fit."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old woman has pain at night when lying on her side, aching from the lateral buttock down the thigh and to the ankle, sparing the foot. Straight leg raise is negative and reflexes are intact. A tender taut band is found in the anterior gluteal region. Which muscle is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Tensor fasciae latae, with pain confined to the lateral hip and thigh", "B": "Quadratus lumborum, with pain referring to the iliac crest and trochanter", "C": "Piriformis, with pain centered on the buttock and sacroiliac area", "D": "Gluteus minimus, with a taut band in the anterior gluteal region"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gluteus minimus, with a taut band in the anterior gluteal region): Gluteus minimus trigger points produce a lateral thigh and leg pain pattern that mimics sciatica but spares the foot and lacks neurologic signs. Quadratus lumborum refers to the iliac crest and greater trochanter, piriformis to the sacroiliac area and buttock, and tensor fasciae latae mostly to the lateral thigh and not to the calf. A palpable band in the anterior gluteal region and a pain path to the ankle that spares the foot complete the picture."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A patient has a tight area across the posterior thigh of roughly 25 cm2. The sound head has an ERA of 4 cm2. The clinician wants to produce a thermal effect using standard technique. Which approach best respects the usual relationship between treatment area and ERA?", "options": {"A": "Switch to a higher frequency so that the effective radiating area enlarges to match", "B": "Split the region into fields of about two to three times the ERA and treat each", "C": "Raise the intensity so the whole area is covered within the normal treatment time", "D": "Treat the full 25 cm2 in one pass, moving faster than usual across the thigh"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Split the region into fields of about two to three times the ERA and treat each): Heating requires the energy to be concentrated, so the field should be roughly two to three times the ERA, here about 8 to 12 cm2. A 25 cm2 area is over six times the ERA, so it is split into sections. Raising intensity risks hot spots and a frequency change does not alter the ERA."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 55-year-old woman had a lumbar laminectomy three weeks ago and still has low back and leg pain. The incision is closed. The clinician considers 1 MHz ultrasound to the paraspinal region next to the surgical site. Which choice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use 1 MHz continuous at 1.5 W/cm2, because the skin incision is already closed", "B": "Use 3 MHz pulsed at low intensity, because the shallow beam stops short of the spine", "C": "Avoid ultrasound over the laminectomy area and consider another approach", "D": "Use ultrasound at the site with fast movement, because the beam is not concentrated"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Avoid ultrasound over the laminectomy area and consider another approach): Ultrasound is contraindicated over a laminectomy site because the protective bone is missing, so the beam can reach the neural tissue and heat it. A closed skin incision does not mean the deeper tissue is healed. Frequency and speed do not remove the risk."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 55 year old office worker with chronic low back muscle tightness lies supine while a hydrocollator pack wrapped in six layers of toweling is placed beneath the lumbar region. After a few minutes she says the warmth is becoming strong. What explains the increasing intensity, and what adjustment is appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Sensory adaptation is occurring, so reassure her and continue the full treatment", "B": "Pack moisture is evaporating, so rewet the toweling and continue unchanged", "C": "Body weight compresses the toweling and reduces insulation, so add layers and recheck skin", "D": "Compressed circulation concentrates heat, so remove the toweling and continue five more minutes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Body weight compresses the toweling and reduces insulation, so add layers and recheck skin): When a patient lies on a pack, body weight flattens the toweling and squeezes out the trapped air, so less insulation remains and more heat reaches the skin. The therapist adds toweling and rechecks the skin. Removing layers, wetting the towels or ignoring a report of increasing heat each raise burn risk."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has dizziness and unsteadiness after whiplash that worsen with neck rotation. Dix-Hallpike is negative, there is no nystagmus, and the upper cervical muscles are tight and tender. Hearing is normal and imaging showed no fracture. Which is the best treatment?", "options": {"A": "Gaze stabilization alone with the head held still on a stable base", "B": "The Epley maneuver for posterior canal debris at each visit", "C": "Habituation using repeated positional provocation maneuvers in clinic", "D": "Cervical manual therapy with cervical proprioceptive training and head-eye coordination drills"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cervical manual therapy with cervical proprioceptive training and head-eye coordination drills): Neck-related dizziness after whiplash with a negative positional test points toward the cervical spine. Treatment targets joint and muscle dysfunction and cervical proprioception. The Epley maneuver treats debris that the negative test excluded, and the other options do not address the neck."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 168-lb patient with a lumbar disc protrusion is referred for mechanical traction. The clinic protocol begins at 25 percent of body weight on the first visit and aims to progress toward the 50 percent separation threshold as tolerated. Which pair of values correctly states the first-visit force and the eventual target for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Start at 84 lb and work toward a target of 126 lb", "B": "Start at 42 lb and work toward a target of 84 lb", "C": "Start at 17 lb and work toward a target of 42 lb", "D": "Start at 42 lb and work toward a target of 168 lb"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start at 42 lb and work toward a target of 84 lb): Twenty-five percent of 168 lb is 0.25 x 168 = 42 lb, and 50 percent is 0.50 x 168 = 84 lb, checked again by doubling 42. Starting at 17 lb and ending at 42 lb uses 10 and 25 percent, which is too low. A target of 168 lb is a full body weight pull and is not required. Starting at 84 lb skips the gradual progression and the 126 lb target is 75 percent of body weight."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A patient with weak quadriceps muscles after prolonged immobilization reports that each stimulated contraction starts abruptly and feels uncomfortable. The clinician points to the ramp-up setting on the electrical muscle stimulator. What is the purpose of a ramp-up of one to three seconds on this type of stimulator?", "options": {"A": "It lengthens each pulse gradually, recruiting deeper motor units during the first seconds of contraction.", "B": "It sets the rest interval between contractions so the muscle can recover from fatigue.", "C": "It lowers the carrier frequency gradually so the current meets less skin impedance at onset.", "D": "It raises intensity gradually, producing a smoother and more comfortable contraction onset."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It raises intensity gradually, producing a smoother and more comfortable contraction onset.): Ramp-up is the time over which the stimulator raises output from zero to full intensity at the start of each contraction. A gradual rise avoids a sudden, jerky contraction and improves comfort and tolerance. Recovery between contractions is controlled by the off time. Ramp does not change pulse duration or carrier frequency."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A student uses a goniometer on a still frame of a healthy adult at the instant the heel first touches the ground during normal walking. The student wants to record the typical hip and knee position at this initial contact. Which pair of joint positions is expected?", "options": {"A": "About ten degrees of hip extension with the knee flexed about forty degrees", "B": "About thirty degrees of hip flexion with the knee nearly fully extended", "C": "About sixty degrees of hip flexion with the knee flexed about sixty degrees", "D": "About zero degrees at the hip with the knee flexed about twenty degrees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About thirty degrees of hip flexion with the knee nearly fully extended): At initial contact the hip is flexed about 30 degrees and the knee is almost straight, which gives the long step reach. The hip reaches about 10 degrees of extension later in terminal stance, and the knee reaches about 60 degrees of flexion in swing, not at heel contact."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "During abduction, a patient's arm reaches a total elevation of 150 degrees, measured from the side of the body to the highest point. The clinician applies the classic 2 to 1 ratio of glenohumeral to scapulothoracic motion and wants to know how much of that elevation comes from the scapula moving on the thorax. How many degrees is that?", "options": {"A": "About 50 degrees of upward rotation of the scapula on the thorax", "B": "About 100 degrees of upward rotation of the scapula on the thorax", "C": "About 75 degrees of upward rotation of the scapula on the thorax", "D": "About 30 degrees of upward rotation of the scapula on the thorax"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 50 degrees of upward rotation of the scapula on the thorax): A 2 to 1 ratio divides the total into three parts, so 150 divided by 3 equals 50 degrees for the scapulothoracic joint. The glenohumeral joint gets the other 100 degrees. The 75 degree value assumes an equal split, and 100 degrees is the glenohumeral share."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with painful bilateral knee osteoarthritis cannot kneel on a hard surface for more than a few seconds. Her therapist wants an exercise that trains lumbopelvic control with opposite arm and leg movement, holding the lumbar spine neutral, while keeping her knees unloaded and her trunk supported on her back. Which exercise should the therapist choose?", "options": {"A": "The side bridge on the elbow and feet, holding the pelvis level for sixty seconds", "B": "The dead bug in supine, lowering opposite arm and leg while holding lumbar neutral", "C": "The bird dog on hands and knees, extending opposite arm and leg to horizontal", "D": "The prone opposite arm and leg lift, holding the lumbar spine in extension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The dead bug in supine, lowering opposite arm and leg while holding lumbar neutral): The dead bug in supine trains lumbopelvic control with opposite arm and leg movement and keeps the painful knees unloaded. The side bridge builds lateral trunk endurance but has no opposite limb pattern. The bird dog fits the pattern but loads the knees. The prone lift unloads the knees but holds extension rather than neutral."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with psoriasis is in week 3 of UVB therapy with a stable dose and a good response. Over the weekend the clinic replaced the fluorescent UVB lamp with a new unit of the same model. The scheduled dose was calculated with the old lamp. Which step is best before treating today?", "options": {"A": "Continue the scheduled dose since both lamps are the same model", "B": "Perform a new MED test with the new lamp before continuing the course", "C": "Move the new lamp 5 inches closer to match the old lamp's output", "D": "Increase the scheduled dose by 25 percent to account for the lamp change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Perform a new MED test with the new lamp before continuing the course): Lamp output falls as a lamp ages, so a new lamp usually delivers more intensity than the old one at the same distance. Doses based on the old lamp may then overexpose the patient. A new MED test or radiometer check restores a reliable dose. Raising the dose or moving the lamp closer makes the overexposure worse."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist treating weak hip abductors wants a diagonal that pairs hip flexion with abduction instead of adduction for the swing phase of gait. The leg starts in extension, adduction, and external rotation. Which diagonal and which hip motions does the therapist guide the leg through in this pattern?", "options": {"A": "D1 flexion, which combines hip flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "B": "D1 extension, which combines hip extension, abduction, and internal rotation", "C": "D2 extension, which combines hip extension, adduction, and external rotation", "D": "D2 flexion, which combines hip flexion, abduction, and internal rotation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (D2 flexion, which combines hip flexion, abduction, and internal rotation): Lower extremity D2 flexion begins in extension, adduction, and external rotation and moves into flexion, abduction, and internal rotation. D1 flexion uses adduction and external rotation. D2 extension is the reverse, starting from the finish position of D2 flexion. D1 extension combines extension, abduction, and internal rotation."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 33-year-old soccer player is 3 weeks past a grade 1 hamstring strain. Passive knee extension in supine hip flexion is limited, and a resisted isometric hamstring contraction at end range reproduces sharp pain. The therapist wants a PNF stretch that gains range without loading the injured muscle. Which technique is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Agonist contraction technique, driving the quadriceps to relax the hamstrings by reciprocal inhibition", "B": "Ballistic bouncing of the hamstrings at end range to stimulate the muscle spindle and increase length", "C": "Contract-relax with a concentric hamstring contraction through range, then a passive stretch", "D": "Hold-relax with a maximal isometric hamstring contraction before each stretch to induce autogenic inhibition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Agonist contraction technique, driving the quadriceps to relax the hamstrings by reciprocal inhibition): Because contracting the hamstrings hurts, the best option contracts the opposing quadriceps instead. Quadriceps activation reduces hamstring excitability through reciprocal inhibition and lets the therapist add range without loading the injured tissue. Hold-relax and contract-relax both require the injured muscle to contract, and ballistic bouncing stresses healing tissue and provokes the stretch reflex."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 47 year old with neck pain receives moist heat to the posterior neck, with the pack wrapped in six layers of toweling. At the five minute check she reports a burning sensation, and the skin shows bright red blotches over the pack margin. What should the therapist do next?", "options": {"A": "Explain that mild burning is normal and continue for the full duration", "B": "Remove the pack, inspect the skin, and resume with added toweling if appropriate", "C": "Shorten the session to ten minutes but leave the toweling unchanged", "D": "Place a cold pack directly on the red area to cool the skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Remove the pack, inspect the skin, and resume with added toweling if appropriate): Burning with blotchy redness signals that too much heat is reaching the skin, so the pack is removed and the skin inspected. Treatment resumes only if the skin is acceptable and more insulation is added. Continuing unchanged risks a burn, and putting a cold pack directly on irritated skin adds a second thermal insult."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 15-year-old at Risser stage 4, whose menses began three years ago, has a Cobb angle of 18 degrees on a standing radiograph and no pain. She plays club volleyball and asks what is needed. Which management is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A surgical consult for fusion planning at this curve size and skeletal age", "B": "Scoliosis-specific exercise, continued sport and a recheck in 6 to 12 months", "C": "Stopping volleyball and other sport for six months until the curve is reassessed", "D": "A rigid brace worn twenty hours a day until skeletal maturity is reached"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Scoliosis-specific exercise, continued sport and a recheck in 6 to 12 months): A curve under 25 degrees in a nearly mature adolescent has low progression risk and is managed by observation, with scoliosis-specific exercise offered. Bracing is for curves of about 25 to 40 degrees in the growing child, and surgery is considered near 45 to 50 degrees. Sport does not cause progression and can continue."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 31-year-old graphic designer has forward head, rounded shoulders, and shoulder pain with overhead reaching. Palpation shows tight pectorals and upper trapezius, and manual testing shows weak lower trapezius and deep neck flexors. Which exercise plan best corrects the imbalance?", "options": {"A": "Strengthen the pectorals and upper trapezius, and stretch the rhomboids", "B": "Strengthen the levator scapulae with shrugs, and stretch the serratus anterior", "C": "Stretch the pectorals and upper trapezius, and strengthen the lower trapezius and deep neck flexors", "D": "Stretch the lower trapezius and deep neck flexors, strengthen the pectorals"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stretch the pectorals and upper trapezius, and strengthen the lower trapezius and deep neck flexors): Correcting the upper crossed pattern means lengthening the overactive pectorals and upper trapezius and strengthening the inhibited lower trapezius, rhomboids, serratus anterior, and deep neck flexors. Strengthening the tight muscles deepens the posture. The other plans stretch inhibited muscles or load an already overactive one, which reinforces the imbalance."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician plans iontophoresis with dexamethasone for a patient with lateral epicondylitis. The treatment is delivered at a constant direct current of 4 mA for a duration of 10 minutes. Iontophoresis dosage is expressed in milliampere-minutes. What dose does this treatment deliver to the patient?", "options": {"A": "The delivered dose equals 14 milliampere-minutes of direct current.", "B": "The delivered dose equals 2.5 milliampere-minutes of direct current.", "C": "The delivered dose equals 40 milliampere-minutes of direct current.", "D": "The delivered dose equals 0.4 milliampere-minutes of direct current."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The delivered dose equals 40 milliampere-minutes of direct current.): Iontophoresis dose is current in milliamperes multiplied by time in minutes, so 4 mA times 10 minutes equals 40 mA-min. Adding gives 14, which has no meaning as a dose. Dividing 10 by 4 gives 2.5, and dividing 4 by 10 gives 0.4, both inverting the product relationship."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with diminished foot sensation wears thick, soft-soled slippers around the house and has fallen twice on the kitchen floor in the past year. She reports that her feet feel numb on the floor. Which footwear advice most reduces her fall risk?", "options": {"A": "High heeled dress shoes to improve her posture", "B": "Barefoot walking on smooth floors to improve foot feedback", "C": "Thick cushioned soles to absorb impact and protect the heels", "D": "Firm, thin, non-slip soles with a low heel and ankle support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Firm, thin, non-slip soles with a low heel and ankle support): Thin firm soles with a low heel keep the foot closer to the ground and preserve sensory feedback, while non-slip tread and ankle support add stability. Thick soft soles reduce feedback and bare feet on smooth floors slip. High heels raise the center of mass and narrow the base."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 20-year-old runner has lateral patellar tracking and anterior knee pain. Her therapist reviews the function of the vastus medialis oblique, whose fibers run at about 50 to 55 degrees to the long axis of the femur. What is the primary role of the VMO at the patellofemoral joint?", "options": {"A": "Assisting hip flexion through its origin on the anterior inferior iliac spine", "B": "Producing lateral patellar glide during the final range of knee extension", "C": "Initiating knee extension through the first fifteen degrees before the other quadriceps heads", "D": "Dynamic medial stabilization of the patella against the lateral pull of the quadriceps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dynamic medial stabilization of the patella against the lateral pull of the quadriceps): The vastus medialis oblique gives dynamic medial stabilization of the patella against the lateral pull of the rest of the quadriceps. Hip flexion through the anterior inferior iliac spine belongs to the rectus femoris. The VMO does not pull the patella laterally, and it does not selectively initiate the first degrees of knee extension."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 21-year-old basketball player with chronic lateral ankle instability has been taped before every game and practice. A two hour practice is planned and the athletic trainer notes the tape loosens quickly once he begins sprinting and cutting. Which support is best for this long session?", "options": {"A": "A lace-up brace, because adhesive tape loses much of its restraint early in exercise", "B": "A double layer of adhesive tape over underwrap applied before the practice", "C": "A neoprene sleeve, because compression improves joint position sense during long sessions", "D": "An elastic bandage wrap, because it permits proprioceptive input through the skin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A lace-up brace, because adhesive tape loses much of its restraint early in exercise): Adhesive tape can lose much of its restraint within the first 10 to 20 minutes of vigorous activity. A lace-up brace keeps its support through a long session of sprinting and cutting. Extra layers of tape still loosen, and a neoprene sleeve or elastic wrap add sensation without real restraint. The brace is the best match for a two hour practice."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A therapist has just completed vapocoolant spray and stretch to the levator scapulae of a 42-year-old patient, and neck rotation toward the painful side has improved by roughly 20 degrees. The skin over the muscle is now cool. What is the next step in the standard sequence?", "options": {"A": "Give deep friction at the tender point to consolidate the release", "B": "Apply an ice pack for fifteen minutes to maintain the analgesia", "C": "Apply moist heat to rewarm the skin, then take the neck through active motion", "D": "End the session and tell the patient to avoid neck motion until tomorrow morning"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply moist heat to rewarm the skin, then take the neck through active motion): After spray and stretch the skin is cool, so moist heat is used to rewarm it, and active range of motion through the new range helps the nervous system accept it. Additional ice, deep friction and rest all prolong cooling or irritate the muscle and do not consolidate the gain in motion. This sequence ends treatment with a gain the patient can use in daily movement."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 63-year-old with hip pain has passive internal rotation of 5 degrees, flexion of 90 degrees, and mild limitation of abduction, with a firm capsular end feel. Muscle length tests are unremarkable. A clinician considers aggressive hip flexor stretching versus joint-based treatment. Which plan best fits the capsular pattern?", "options": {"A": "Piriformis stretching with the hip flexed and adducted, held for sixty seconds, three times daily", "B": "Ballistic hamstring stretching in long sitting to increase hip flexion and rotation range", "C": "Aggressive static hip flexor stretching in half-kneeling to restore internal rotation and flexion", "D": "Graded long-axis distraction and capsular mobilization, followed by pain-limited internal rotation range work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Graded long-axis distraction and capsular mobilization, followed by pain-limited internal rotation range work): Limited internal rotation, flexion, and abduction with a firm capsular end feel and normal muscle length tests fit the capsular pattern of the hip, so treatment targets the capsule with graded distraction and mobilization and pain-limited range work. Aggressive or ballistic muscle stretching does not change capsular restriction and can irritate the joint. Long piriformis holds target a different tissue."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 40-year-old electrician has subacromial pain with overhead work. The therapist is selecting rotator cuff exercises. Elevating the arm to 90 degrees with the thumb pointing down narrows the subacromial space and often aggravates symptoms. Which exercise is the better choice for supraspinatus work?", "options": {"A": "Lateral raise with the thumb up and the arm in the frontal plane to shoulder height", "B": "Upright row with a heavy barbell lifted to chin height with elbows high", "C": "Scaption with the thumb up, raising the arm in the scapular plane to shoulder height", "D": "Side-lying external rotation with the elbow at the side, using a light dumbbell"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Scaption with the thumb up, raising the arm in the scapular plane to shoulder height): Scaption with the thumb up in the scapular plane keeps the greater tuberosity clear of the acromion while activating the supraspinatus. Elevating in the frontal plane brings more compression, and upright rows load the shoulder in internal rotation at height. Side-lying external rotation is a valid cuff exercise, but it targets the infraspinatus and teres minor more than the supraspinatus."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old teacher uses a home TENS unit for chronic low back pain. She reports that the unit works well for about 20 minutes, after which the same settings no longer relieve her pain even though the electrodes remain well attached. Which adjustment is most appropriate to counter this loss of effect from nerve accommodation?", "options": {"A": "Lower the intensity below the sensory threshold so the nerves recover their responsiveness.", "B": "Switch to larger electrodes to reduce current density and prevent fatigue of the nerve.", "C": "Reverse electrode polarity each session so charge does not accumulate beneath one pad.", "D": "Use amplitude or frequency modulation, or periodically raise the intensity during the session."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use amplitude or frequency modulation, or periodically raise the intensity during the session.): Accommodation occurs when the nervous system adapts to a constant stimulus, so modulating amplitude or frequency, or raising intensity as the sensation fades, keeps the input changing and restores effect. Dropping below the sensory threshold removes the stimulus. Larger electrodes lower current density and weaken the input. TENS uses balanced biphasic or pulsed waveforms, so no charge accumulates beneath one pad."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 50-year-old sedentary accountant cannot find one 30 minute block in her workday but will walk briskly in short sessions. She asks whether short walks count toward her weekly aerobic goal. According to classic ACSM guidance of the 2008 era, what is the shortest bout that can be accumulated?", "options": {"A": "Bouts of at least 10 minutes each can be accumulated toward the goal", "B": "Only bouts of at least 20 minutes count toward the weekly total", "C": "Bouts of at least 5 minutes each can be accumulated toward the goal", "D": "Only a single continuous session of 30 minutes counts toward the weekly total"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bouts of at least 10 minutes each can be accumulated toward the goal): The classic ACSM guidance and the 2008 federal guidelines allowed exercise to be accumulated in bouts of at least 10 minutes, so three brisk 10 minute walks can add up to a 30 minute day. Newer guidance, such as the 2018 Physical Activity Guidelines, counts bouts of any length, which is why this question names the classic standard. A 20 minute minimum or a requirement for one continuous 30 minute session is stricter than either version. Five minute bouts fall below the classic minimum."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician positions a 41-year-old runner side-lying with the lower hip and knee flexed, then abducts and extends the upper leg with the knee bent before releasing it. The leg stays abducted and does not drop toward the table. Which tissue shortness does this Ober test result implicate?", "options": {"A": "Shortening of the hip adductors and gracilis at the medial thigh", "B": "Shortening of the piriformis and obturator internus at the posterior hip", "C": "Shortening of the tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band", "D": "Shortening of the rectus femoris and iliopsoas at the anterior hip"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shortening of the tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band): A leg that stays abducted during the Ober test indicates shortening of the tensor fasciae latae and iliotibial band on the lateral thigh. The adductors would restrict abduction, the deep rotators are tested with other positions, and the rectus femoris and iliopsoas are assessed with the Thomas test."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A physical therapy assistant is stretching the tight right hamstrings of a 24-year-old sprinter using the PNF hold-relax method. The leg has been positioned passively at the end of available hip flexion range with the knee extended. What should the athlete do next, before the therapist takes up the new range?", "options": {"A": "Stay fully relaxed while the therapist maintains a sustained passive stretch for sixty seconds", "B": "Move the leg through its range concentrically against moderate resistance, then relax and allow the stretch", "C": "Contract the quadriceps actively to reciprocally inhibit the hamstrings while the therapist holds the leg still", "D": "Press into the therapist's hand in an isometric hamstring contraction for several seconds, then relax"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Press into the therapist's hand in an isometric hamstring contraction for several seconds, then relax): Hold-relax uses a brief isometric contraction of the tight muscle at end range, followed by relaxation and a new passive stretch. The contraction is held without joint motion, usually for about 6 to 10 seconds. Contracting the quadriceps describes the agonist contraction method, and moving through range against resistance describes contract-relax. Staying passive for a full minute is simple static stretching with no PNF contraction."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A table lists tissue heating of 0.3 degrees C per minute for 1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 and 0.6 degrees C per minute for 3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2. A superficial tendon needs a 3 degree C rise in the shortest safe time. Which choice and time fit best?", "options": {"A": "1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 for about 5 minutes, since the higher intensity doubles the rate", "B": "1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2 for about 10 minutes, since 3 divided by 0.3 equals 10", "C": "3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 for about 5 minutes, since 3 divided by 0.6 equals 5", "D": "3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 for about 2 minutes, since 3 times 0.6 is under 2"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (3 MHz at 1.0 W/cm2 for about 5 minutes, since 3 divided by 0.6 equals 5): Dividing 3 degrees by 0.6 degrees per minute gives 5 minutes for the 3 MHz setting, versus 10 minutes with 1 MHz at 1.5 W/cm2. Multiplying gives a meaningless figure and the table does not support a doubled rate for 1 MHz. A superficial target also favors 3 MHz."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 46 year old man with Fitzpatrick type VI skin is treated over the infraspinatus with a stationary 810 nm class 3B probe at 400 mW. At 30 seconds he reports burning heat and flinches. The chiropractor sees no visible skin damage. What is the best immediate response?", "options": {"A": "Stop emission, then resume at lower power with the probe kept moving over the area", "B": "Continue to complete the planned time because heat confirms effective absorption", "C": "Switch to a 405 nm probe because melanin absorbs less at shorter wavelengths", "D": "Press the probe harder to displace blood and cool the skin surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stop emission, then resume at lower power with the probe kept moving over the area): Heavily pigmented skin absorbs more of the beam in melanin, so a stationary 400 mW probe can produce heating that is not therapeutic. The safe step is to stop, lower the power, and keep the probe moving to spread the energy. Burning heat is a warning, pressure does not cool the skin, and melanin absorbs more strongly at shorter wavelengths."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 40-year-old marathon runner is placed on strict rest for six weeks after a stress fracture, and the clinician counsels him about what to expect on return. He asks whether the aerobic fitness he built over several years will remain intact during that rest. Which statement about detraining during complete rest is accurate?", "options": {"A": "VO2 max declines only after about three months, with stroke volume unchanged until then", "B": "Plasma volume expands during rest, so VO2 max improves for the first two weeks", "C": "Mitochondrial enzyme activity falls within days, but VO2 max remains unchanged for several months", "D": "VO2 max begins to decline within a few weeks of stopping training"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (VO2 max begins to decline within a few weeks of stopping training): Aerobic adaptations are reversible, and VO2 max begins to fall within roughly two to four weeks of stopping training, driven first by lower plasma volume and stroke volume. A three month delay does not occur. Plasma volume falls rather than expands with rest. Mitochondrial enzymes do decline early, but VO2 max declines alongside them, so it does not stay constant for months."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 44-year-old with right C6 radicular arm pain is in supine mechanical cervical traction at 25 degrees of flexion and reports increased arm pain. When the head is lowered to about 5 degrees of flexion the arm pain eases. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Increase flexion to 40 degrees to open the joints further and then resume", "B": "Move the neck into slight extension to compress the facets and hold the pull", "C": "Return to 25 degrees and raise the force because stretch pain is expected during treatment", "D": "Keep the lower flexion angle and reassess, letting symptom response guide the angle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the lower flexion angle and reassess, letting symptom response guide the angle): The best traction angle is the one that reduces symptoms, so if 5 degrees eases the arm pain the therapist keeps it and reassesses. Returning to 25 degrees with more force ignores the response. Greater flexion is the direction that already aggravated symptoms. Extension narrows the foramina and can worsen radicular pain."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 22-year-old basketball player in late-stage rehabilitation begins box jump drills. The therapist coaches him to rebound quickly off the floor so the stretch reflex and stored elastic energy add to the jump. Which feature of the stretch shortening cycle is the therapist emphasizing?", "options": {"A": "A deep eccentric squat held for two seconds to build maximal elastic loading", "B": "A long amortization phase so the spindle reflex can reach full strength", "C": "A short amortization phase between the eccentric loading and the concentric push off", "D": "A heavy concentric lift with a full stop before each jump to build power"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A short amortization phase between the eccentric loading and the concentric push off): In the stretch shortening cycle a rapid eccentric stretch loads the muscle and spindles, and a very short amortization phase lets stored elastic energy and the stretch reflex add to the concentric push off. A held squat or a long amortization phase lets that energy dissipate. A full stop before each jump removes the stretch reflex contribution. Short ground contact is the hallmark of good plyometric technique."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A research-minded chiropractor reviews a probe specification listing a continuous output of 200 mW through a circular spot with an area of 0.4 cm2. The clinic's protocol sheet also lists power density, sometimes called irradiance, in mW per cm2. What irradiance does this probe deliver at the skin surface?", "options": {"A": "0.5 mW/cm2, found by dividing 0.2 W by 0.4 cm2", "B": "500 mW/cm2, found by dividing 200 mW by 0.4 cm2", "C": "80 mW/cm2, found by multiplying 200 mW by 0.4 cm2", "D": "1,250 mW/cm2, found by dividing 200 mW by 0.16 cm2"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (500 mW/cm2, found by dividing 200 mW by 0.4 cm2): Irradiance is power divided by area, so 200 mW divided by 0.4 cm2 equals 500 mW/cm2, the same as 0.5 W/cm2. Multiplying by area is the wrong operation. Leaving the answer as 0.5 mW/cm2 loses the unit conversion, and squaring the area overstates the density."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old software developer presents with forward head, rounded shoulders, and protracted scapulae after years of desk work. He reports chest tightness and neck stiffness by evening, and a postural screen shows no scoliosis. Using Janda's upper crossed syndrome model, which muscle group is typically tight and shortened in this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Deep cervical flexors, rhomboids, middle trapezius, and lower trapezius fibers", "B": "Serratus anterior, middle trapezius, and deep cervical flexors", "C": "Teres minor, infraspinatus, posterior deltoid, and the long head of triceps", "D": "Pectoralis major and minor, upper trapezius, and levator scapulae"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pectoralis major and minor, upper trapezius, and levator scapulae): Janda's upper crossed syndrome pairs tight pectorals, upper trapezius, and levator scapulae with weak deep neck flexors, rhomboids, middle and lower trapezius, and serratus anterior. The tight muscles pull the head forward and round the shoulders. The remaining options describe the weak group or muscles outside the pattern."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 47-year-old has neck pain with numbness in the thumb and index finger, a diminished biceps reflex and a positive Spurling test. The upper trapezius also has a tender taut band on palpation. He is otherwise healthy. What is the best plan?", "options": {"A": "Treat the trigger point with ischemic compression, since it explains the symptoms", "B": "Evaluate for cervical radiculopathy first and treat the trigger point only as an adjunct", "C": "Use spray and stretch of the upper trapezius as the primary treatment", "D": "Use deep friction to the trapezius, expecting the numbness to resolve"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Evaluate for cervical radiculopathy first and treat the trigger point only as an adjunct): Dermatomal numbness, a reduced reflex and a positive Spurling test point to nerve root involvement, which a trapezius trigger point cannot explain. Evaluation of the radiculopathy takes priority, and myofascial treatment can be added for the coexisting muscle tenderness. Treating the muscle as the cause would delay needed care."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old nurse with back pain lies in hooklying, and the therapist needs to find her lumbar neutral spine position before core stabilization training begins. She can move her pelvis through anterior and posterior tilt without pain and understands the pelvic clock instructions. How does the therapist locate the neutral position for her?", "options": {"A": "Have her arch to the end of anterior tilt where the back curve is greatest", "B": "Have her place the pelvis where the anterior superior iliac spines sit at the same height", "C": "Have her flatten the lumbar spine firmly against the table and hold that position", "D": "Have her tilt the pelvis through its full range and settle in the comfortable middle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Have her tilt the pelvis through its full range and settle in the comfortable middle): Neutral is the pain-free midrange position found by moving the pelvis between anterior and posterior tilt and resting between the extremes. A flat back is the end of posterior tilt, not neutral. The maximum lumbar arch is the end of anterior tilt. The height of the anterior superior iliac spines is not used to find midrange."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinic stores hydrocollator packs in a thermostatically controlled stainless steel tank between uses. A new student asks what water temperature the unit should hold so that the packs are ready for the standard moist heat treatment with towel layers. Which setting is the range commonly specified for a hydrocollator tank in physical agent textbooks?", "options": {"A": "Roughly 185 to 200 degrees F (about 85 to 93 degrees C)", "B": "Roughly 105 to 115 degrees F (about 41 to 46 degrees C), a warm bath", "C": "Roughly 160 to 170 degrees F (about 71 to 77 degrees C)", "D": "Roughly 130 to 140 degrees F (about 54 to 60 degrees C)"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Roughly 160 to 170 degrees F (about 71 to 77 degrees C)): Hydrocollator tanks are kept around 160 to 170 degrees F so the silica gel packs store enough heat for a 20 to 30 minute treatment through toweling. Water near 130 to 140 degrees F would leave packs too cool to be effective. Water near boiling is hotter than the pack and cover are designed for. Tissue temperature goals are far lower than tank temperature, so the 105 to 115 range misreads the two."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 28-year-old runner has anterior knee pain on stairs. During a single leg squat her knee drifts medially and her pelvis drops, while knee extension strength is normal and the patella tracks centrally. Which program best addresses the likely contributing impairment in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening with squat movement retraining", "B": "Iliotibial band stretching with foam rolling as the primary treatment", "C": "Patellar bracing with rest from squatting until pain has been gone for a month", "D": "Heavy open chain knee extensions through the full range to build quadriceps strength"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hip abductor and external rotator strengthening with squat movement retraining): Medial knee drift and pelvic drop on a single leg squat point to weak hip abductors and external rotators with poor frontal plane control, which raises patellofemoral stress. Her quadriceps strength and patellar tracking are normal, so those are not the priority. Strengthening the proximal muscles and retraining movement addresses the cause, while bracing and rest do not."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student reviewing thermal dosing learns that superficial heating raises blood flow and collagen extensibility but becomes harmful when tissue gets too hot. Textbooks describe a therapeutic tissue temperature window for rehabilitation heating, below the point where tissue damage risk climbs. Which tissue temperature range is generally cited as that therapeutic window?", "options": {"A": "Between 98 and 102 degrees F (37 to 39 degrees C) in the treated tissue", "B": "Between 104 and 113 degrees F (40 to 45 degrees C) in the treated tissue", "C": "Between 125 and 140 degrees F (52 to 60 degrees C) in the treated muscle tissue", "D": "Between 113 and 122 degrees F (45 to 50 degrees C) in the treated tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Between 104 and 113 degrees F (40 to 45 degrees C) in the treated tissue): Raising tissue to about 104 to 113 degrees F increases blood flow, metabolism and collagen extensibility. Tissue above roughly 113 degrees F risks cell injury, so that range is not therapeutic. Temperatures near 98 to 102 degrees F barely exceed normal body temperature and produce little change. The 125 to 140 degree range would cause burns."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 38-year-old office worker with acute paraspinal muscle spasm after shoveling snow is treated with a handheld mechanical vibrator applied over the lumbar muscles at a comfortable intensity for several minutes. What is the main therapeutic rationale for this treatment?", "options": {"A": "Breaking down myofascial scar by mechanical shearing of collagen cross links", "B": "Sensory stimulation that reduces pain perception and promotes muscle relaxation", "C": "Strengthening of the muscle through repeated maximal motor unit recruitment", "D": "Deep heating of the muscle to raise tissue temperature by several degrees"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sensory stimulation that reduces pain perception and promotes muscle relaxation): Vibration provides a large, fast sensory input that competes with pain signals at the spinal level and encourages relaxation of the muscle. It does not meaningfully heat deep tissue like ultrasound, does not train muscle, and does not shear collagen the way deep friction is intended to. It is most useful as a comfort measure early in a program, before active work begins."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 71-year-old man is brought in by his daughter. He walks with a bent trunk and neck, takes tiny steps that seem to speed up as he goes, and his arms hang nearly still at his sides. Which pathological gait pattern is he showing?", "options": {"A": "Cerebellar ataxic gait, a wide base with irregular unsteady steps", "B": "Magnetic gait of normal pressure hydrocephalus, with feet seeming stuck to the floor", "C": "Parkinsonian gait, with a bent trunk and tiny shuffling steps", "D": "Hemiplegic gait, with a stiff leg swung outward and one arm held flexed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Parkinsonian gait, with a bent trunk and tiny shuffling steps): A stooped posture, tiny accelerating steps and reduced arm swing identify Parkinsonian gait. Cerebellar gait is wide based and irregular. Normal pressure hydrocephalus gives a shuffling magnetic gait but not a bent trunk or festination. Hemiplegic gait is one sided with a circumducting leg and a flexed arm."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "During interferential treatment for acute lumbar pain, a patient first reports a faint tingling at 12 mA and a visible muscle twitch beneath the electrodes at 26 mA. The goal is low-intensity sensory-level analgesia with a 100 Hz beat, set just above the sensory threshold. Which intensity setting best matches the plan?", "options": {"A": "About 26 mA, at the level where the first twitch appears", "B": "About 20 mA, just below the level where the twitch appears", "C": "Slightly above 12 mA, where tingling is clear and no twitch occurs", "D": "About 35 mA, above the twitch threshold, to produce a strong contraction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Slightly above 12 mA, where tingling is clear and no twitch occurs): Sensory-level treatment sits between the sensory threshold and the motor threshold, so tingling is clear and no twitch occurs. Slightly above 12 mA satisfies that. At 20 mA the intensity nears the motor threshold and is stronger than a sensory goal requires. At 26 mA the muscle is already contracting, and 35 mA gives a strong motor response, which belongs to low-rate protocols."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A therapist compresses a tender point in the infraspinatus of a patient with shoulder pain and the patient exclaims that this is the exact deep shoulder ache that brought her to the clinic. How should this trigger point be classified?", "options": {"A": "Secondary, because the muscle is stretched by the neighboring one", "B": "Latent, because the point is tender only when pressure is applied", "C": "Satellite, because it lies in the referral zone of another muscle", "D": "Active, because pressure reproduces her familiar pain complaint"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Active, because pressure reproduces her familiar pain complaint): An active trigger point causes spontaneous pain and, when compressed, reproduces the patient's recognized complaint. A latent point is tender locally but does not reproduce the patient's pain. Satellite and secondary points develop in other muscles because of an existing point and are not defined by this finding."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 30-year-old competitive swimmer shows delayed activation of the lower trapezius on scapular control testing, with the scapula elevating during overhead reach. The therapist wants to use kinesiology tape to facilitate that muscle. Her shoulder is pain free at rest. How should the strip be applied?", "options": {"A": "From the origin toward the insertion, but with the muscle fully lengthened and the tape unstretched", "B": "Across the muscle belly perpendicular to the fibers with maximal stretch", "C": "From the insertion toward the origin, with strong tension and tensioned ends", "D": "From the muscle origin toward the insertion, with light tension and untensioned ends"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (From the muscle origin toward the insertion, with light tension and untensioned ends): The standard teaching is that facilitation tape runs from origin to insertion, applied with light tension and with the end anchors laid with no stretch. The reverse direction is used for tone reduction. A strip across the fibers has no directional intent, and heavy tension on the ends can lift the adhesive and irritate skin."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A patient with quadriceps weakness tolerates a maximum amplitude of 60 mA with biphasic pulses of 200 microseconds, and the resulting contraction is weaker than the clinician wants. Without raising amplitude, the clinician increases the pulse duration to 400 microseconds. What is the expected effect of lengthening the pulse duration at constant amplitude?", "options": {"A": "Greater charge per phase, recruiting additional motor units and producing a stronger contraction.", "B": "Selective stimulation of sensory fibers only, with no additional motor unit recruitment.", "C": "Higher pulse rate, because longer pulses shorten the interval between successive pulses.", "D": "Lower charge per phase, which reduces discomfort but also weakens the contraction."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Greater charge per phase, recruiting additional motor units and producing a stronger contraction.): Charge per phase is amplitude multiplied by phase duration, so doubling duration at the same amplitude doubles the charge. More charge depolarizes more and deeper nerve fibers and recruits more motor units, strengthening the contraction. Pulse rate is set separately by frequency. Short phase durations favor sensory fibers, so lengthening the phase moves away from sensory-only stimulation."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A therapist observes a patient learning a new home exercise. The patient makes large, inconsistent errors, concentrates intensely on each step, and needs frequent verbal cues to finish the movement. Using the Fitts and Posner model of motor skill acquisition, which stage is the patient demonstrating?", "options": {"A": "The retention stage, where the learner recalls the skill after a period without practice", "B": "The associative stage, where errors shrink and the learner refines timing and movement patterns", "C": "The cognitive stage, where the learner is still working out what the whole task requires", "D": "The autonomous stage, where the movement is performed with little conscious attention and few errors"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The cognitive stage, where the learner is still working out what the whole task requires): In the Fitts and Posner model the first stage is cognitive. The learner must understand the task, so errors are large and variable, attention demand is high, and cues are needed often. In the associative stage performance is more consistent, and in the autonomous stage the skill needs little attention. Retention is a test of learning after a delay and is not one of the three stages."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist tests a patient's MED with patches exposed for 30, 60, 90, 120 and 150 seconds. The 30 and 60 second patches show no change, the 90 second patch shows the faintest barely visible redness, and the longer patches show stronger redness. The goal is an E1 reaction at 2.5 times the MED. Which exposure time should be set?", "options": {"A": "An exposure of 225 seconds at the tested lamp distance", "B": "An exposure of 150 seconds at the tested lamp distance", "C": "An exposure of 180 seconds at the tested lamp distance", "D": "An exposure of 450 seconds at the tested lamp distance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An exposure of 225 seconds at the tested lamp distance): The MED is the shortest patch with minimal visible redness, which is 90 seconds. An E1 dose is 2.5 times that, and 90 times 2.5 equals 225 seconds. Using the 150 second patch ignores the calculation. Five times the MED gives 450 seconds, which is an E2 dose, and doubling gives only 180."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician records a patient's gait cycle time as 1.10 seconds on both limbs. Right stance time is 0.58 seconds and left stance time is 0.72 seconds. The patient has pain in one leg. Which limb is most likely painful, and why?", "options": {"A": "The right limb, because its stance time is shorter than the left", "B": "The left limb, because its stance time is longer than the right", "C": "The left limb, because 0.72 seconds is above sixty percent of the cycle", "D": "Neither limb, because a cycle time of 1.10 seconds is normal"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The right limb, because its stance time is shorter than the left): Right stance is 0.58 of 1.10 seconds, about 53 percent, while left stance is 0.72 seconds, about 65 percent, and the asymmetry shows the right limb is protected by unloading it sooner. Antalgic gait shortens stance on the painful side. A normal total cycle time does not remove an asymmetry between the limbs."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor's protocol for chronic tendinopathy calls for 8 J/cm2 using a 100 mW probe with a 1 cm2 spot. The assistant treated each point for 10 seconds and recorded that the protocol was followed. The chiropractor checks the arithmetic and wants to correct the record. What exposure time per point would actually meet the protocol?", "options": {"A": "40 s, found by dividing 4 J by 0.1 W", "B": "8 s, found by dividing 8 J by 1 W", "C": "80 s, found by dividing 8 J by 0.1 W", "D": "800 s, found by dividing 8 J by 0.01 W"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (80 s, found by dividing 8 J by 0.1 W): The protocol needs 8 J/cm2 over 1 cm2, which is 8 J. Dividing 8 J by 0.1 W gives 80 s, so the 10 second exposure delivered only 1 J/cm2. Treating the output as 1 W reproduces the error, halving the energy gives half the dose, and a decimal slip overshoots tenfold."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 38-year-old office worker with chronic neck pain lies supine and lifts her head. Her chin juts forward and the sternocleidomastoid muscles bulge while a chin tuck cannot be held. Which muscle group is weak, and which exercise addresses it first?", "options": {"A": "Deep neck flexors, trained with low load craniocervical flexion before any head lift", "B": "Sternocleidomastoid, trained with resisted head lifts against a hand placed on the forehead", "C": "Upper trapezius, trained with heavy shoulder shrugs using handheld dumbbells", "D": "Cervical extensors, trained with prone head lifts against gravity held ten seconds"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deep neck flexors, trained with low load craniocervical flexion before any head lift): Chin jutting with prominent sternocleidomastoids shows that the superficial flexors are dominating because the deep flexors cannot stabilize the neck. The deep flexors are trained first with low-load craniocervical flexion before head lifts. Strengthening the already overactive sternocleidomastoid worsens the imbalance. The upper trapezius and cervical extensors are not the failing muscles."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 68-year-old woman 5 months after a stroke walks a 6-meter marked course in 10 seconds using a cane on level ground. Her therapist wants to classify her walking speed with the commonly used community ambulation categories. What classification fits her speed?", "options": {"A": "Household ambulator, since the speed is below 0.4 meters per second", "B": "Limited community ambulator, since the speed is 0.6 meters per second", "C": "Full community ambulator, since the speed is above 0.8 meters per second", "D": "Typical healthy adult, since the speed is near 1.3 meters per second"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Limited community ambulator, since the speed is 0.6 meters per second): Speed is 6 meters divided by 10 seconds, or 0.6 meters per second, double checked as 0.6 times 10 equals 6. Perry's categories treat speeds under 0.4 meters per second as household ambulation, 0.4 to 0.8 as limited community, and above 0.8 as full community. Healthy adults average about 1.3 meters per second."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 70-year-old man after knee arthroplasty cannot lift his leg from a seated position against a 2 lb cuff but can lower the leg slowly under control from full extension. He is motivated and attends therapy three times weekly. Which strengthening approach takes advantage of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Open chain hamstring curls to build the antagonist before quadriceps training", "B": "Isometric quadriceps sets at full extension held ten seconds without any lowering practice", "C": "Slow eccentric lowering from full extension, assisted up by the therapist", "D": "Isokinetic testing at fast speed to measure power output of the leg"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Slow eccentric lowering from full extension, assisted up by the therapist): He cannot lift against a 2 lb cuff but controls lowering, so eccentric loading from full extension with help on the way up uses the strength he has. Hamstring curls train the antagonist, not the weak quadriceps. Isometric sets alone skip the usable eccentric strength, and fast isokinetic testing is a measure, not a strengthening approach."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 50-year-old man walks with his trunk thrown backward at the moment his right heel touches down, then straightens up again. His knee is stable and strong, but he cannot extend the right hip against resistance. Which weakness explains this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Right gluteus medius, which normally levels the pelvis in single support", "B": "Right quadriceps, which normally prevents the knee from collapsing in stance", "C": "Right gluteus maximus, which normally controls hip flexion at initial contact", "D": "Right iliopsoas, which normally flexes the hip to start the swing phase"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right gluteus maximus, which normally controls hip flexion at initial contact): Gluteus maximus keeps the trunk upright by controlling hip flexion at initial contact and loading. When it is weak, the patient throws the trunk back to hold the center of mass behind the hip, producing a posterior trunk thrust. The stem rules out quadriceps weakness, and abductor weakness would show lateral pelvic drop or lean."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractor needs a beat frequency of 100 Hz for an interferential treatment. The unit holds channel one at 4000 Hz and allows adjustment of channel two only. Instructed to set channel two below channel one, at what frequency should the therapist set channel two?", "options": {"A": "4100 Hz, which is 100 Hz above the channel one carrier", "B": "4000 Hz, which matches the channel one carrier exactly", "C": "3990 Hz, which is 10 Hz below the channel one carrier", "D": "3900 Hz, which is 100 Hz below the channel one carrier"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3900 Hz, which is 100 Hz below the channel one carrier): The beat equals the difference between the carriers, so a 100 Hz beat needs the two channels 100 Hz apart. With channel two set below 4000 Hz, the value is 3900 Hz. A setting of 4100 Hz would also give 100 Hz but violates the instruction to set below. A 3990 Hz setting gives only a 10 Hz beat, and equal carriers give zero beat."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 48-year-old woman wants a cardio session that maximizes the percentage of energy drawn from fat. She can either walk briskly at 50 percent of VO2max or run intervals at 90 percent of VO2max for the same total energy cost. Which statement about substrate use best guides the advice?", "options": {"A": "Fat contribution is greatest at 90 percent of VO2max since catecholamines maximize lipolysis", "B": "Lower intensity gives a larger fat fraction, while higher intensity shifts toward carbohydrate", "C": "Fat fraction is identical at both intensities when total energy expenditure is matched", "D": "Fat becomes the main fuel only once glycogen is depleted after thirty minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lower intensity gives a larger fat fraction, while higher intensity shifts toward carbohydrate): Fat contributes a larger share of energy at low to moderate intensity, and the share falls as intensity rises while carbohydrate takes over. Matching total energy does not match substrate mix. Catecholamines raise lipolysis at high intensity, but carbohydrate use rises more. Fat is used from the start of exercise, so waiting for depletion is wrong."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient recovering from a quadriceps strain performs seated knee extension through the full range against gravity. The examiner then applies resistance at the ankle and the patient holds the end position against moderate pressure, but the limb gives way as the examiner increases to maximal resistance. What grade is recorded?", "options": {"A": "Grade 2, poor, because the quadriceps gave way under the examiner's maximal resistance", "B": "Grade 5, normal, because the patient completed the full range against gravity", "C": "Grade 3, fair, because the patient completed the range against gravity unaided", "D": "Grade 4, good, because moderate resistance was tolerated but maximal resistance was not"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade 4, good, because moderate resistance was tolerated but maximal resistance was not): Holding against moderate resistance but not maximal resistance is grade 4 (good). Grade 5 requires holding against full examiner resistance. Grade 3 describes a patient who completes the range against gravity but tolerates no added resistance. Grade 2 describes motion with gravity eliminated, which is far below this performance."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 55-year-old accountant sits most of the day and has an underactive, lengthened hip extensor group. Using Janda's tonic and phasic classification, the clinician asks which muscle is phasic and therefore expected to weaken and inhibit rather than shorten with prolonged sitting. Which muscle is it?", "options": {"A": "The iliopsoas muscle at the front of the hip and pelvis", "B": "The gluteus maximus muscle at the back of the hip and pelvis", "C": "The rectus femoris muscle at the front of the thigh and knee", "D": "The tensor fasciae latae muscle at the lateral hip and upper thigh"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The gluteus maximus muscle at the back of the hip and pelvis): The gluteus maximus is phasic and tends toward inhibition and weakness when the hip stays flexed for long periods. The iliopsoas, rectus femoris and tensor fasciae latae are tonic muscles that tend to shorten. Sitting therefore produces tight hip flexors paired with a weak gluteus maximus."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A biopsy of a patient's soleus shows small diameter fibers with dense mitochondria, high myoglobin, abundant capillaries and slow myosin ATPase activity. The patient is choosing activities to rebuild postural endurance after a long illness. Which activity would these fibers most likely support best?", "options": {"A": "Repeated maximal vertical jumps with long rests, calling for rapid peak force", "B": "Sustained standing and slow walking lasting many minutes with low force demands", "C": "Repeated sets of ten loaded squats near maximum effort with two minute rests", "D": "Moderate intensity interval work of thirty seconds at near maximal speed, repeated eight times"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sustained standing and slow walking lasting many minutes with low force demands): Small fibers with dense mitochondria, myoglobin, capillaries, and slow ATPase are Type I fibers, suited to fatigue resistant, low force, long duration work such as standing posture and slow walking. Maximal jumps, near maximal squat sets, and thirty second all out intervals recruit larger fast motor units and rely on Type II fibers. Matching the fiber properties to the task demands is the key step."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "During the first lumbar traction trial for a 33-year-old nurse with L5 radiculopathy, she reports that the pain which was in her calf before treatment has now moved down into her foot. The therapist is monitoring her response at five minutes into the session. How should the clinician classify this response and what should happen to the treatment?", "options": {"A": "Peripheralization of symptoms, so the force or position should be changed or traction stopped", "B": "Centralization of symptoms, so the current dose is effective and should continue", "C": "Peripheralization of symptoms, which is expected in the first trial, so continue and reassess next visit", "D": "Referred pain from stretching of the sciatic nerve, so add a nerve glide and continue traction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Peripheralization of symptoms, so the force or position should be changed or traction stopped): Pain moving from the calf into the foot is peripheralization, an adverse response, so the force or position should be changed or traction stopped. Centralization would favor continuing. Peripheralization is not an expected early response, and adding nerve glides while continuing would risk further aggravation."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 44-year-old woman drinks a sugary sports beverage 20 minutes before a 40-minute walk and then complains that her fat burning seems minimal on the metabolic cart. Her ratio is 0.93 at an easy pace where she normally reads 0.82. Which hormonal change best explains the shift toward carbohydrate use?", "options": {"A": "Elevated cortisol blocks entry of fatty acids into mitochondria, forcing dependence on glucose", "B": "Elevated epinephrine stimulates lipolysis, which increases fat oxidation at the easy pace", "C": "Raised insulin suppresses lipolysis and promotes glucose uptake, shifting fuel use to carbohydrate", "D": "Elevated growth hormone activates hormone-sensitive lipase and raises fat oxidation at rest"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raised insulin suppresses lipolysis and promotes glucose uptake, shifting fuel use to carbohydrate): Carbohydrate intake shortly before exercise raises insulin, which inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and lipolysis and promotes glucose uptake, so the respiratory exchange ratio rises toward 1.0. Epinephrine and growth hormone favor fat mobilization and would lower the ratio. Cortisol does not block fatty acid entry into mitochondria."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A student is learning closed basketweave ankle taping on a classmate and is told to place the first strips on the lower leg and the midfoot before any supporting strips. What is the main purpose of these initial anchor strips?", "options": {"A": "To compress the swollen tissue by tightening circumferentially around the malleoli", "B": "To resist inversion and eversion directly, as the main stabilizing strips", "C": "To provide fixed attachment points for later strips, applied without tension", "D": "To limit plantar flexion by tightly encircling the foot at the arch"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To provide fixed attachment points for later strips, applied without tension): Anchors are applied on the proximal leg and the midfoot to give the supporting strips a stable base to adhere to. They are laid without much tension, because a tight circumferential anchor can impair circulation. Stirrups provide the main medial and lateral stability, so the anchors themselves do not restrict inversion or eversion."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 58-year-old retired electrician is referred for neck pain with arm radiation, and the clinician is screening for cervical traction contraindications before the first session. The intake review lists four findings from the chart and examination. Which of the following findings is a recognized contraindication to applying cervical traction?", "options": {"A": "Cervical radiculopathy with a positive Spurling test that eases with manual distraction", "B": "Mild degenerative disc space narrowing at C5 and C6 with painful limited neck rotation", "C": "Cervical facet joint stiffness with hypomobility and no neurological signs or arm symptoms", "D": "Suspected vertebrobasilar insufficiency with dizziness and visual blurring on neck positioning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Suspected vertebrobasilar insufficiency with dizziness and visual blurring on neck positioning): Dizziness and visual blurring on neck positioning suggest vertebrobasilar insufficiency, and traction and positioning can reduce vertebral artery flow, making traction unsafe. Radiculopathy that eases with distraction predicts a good response. Mild disc narrowing with limited rotation is a common reason to use traction. Facet hypomobility without neurological signs is also a typical indication, so none of those is a contraindication."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A strength coach asks a physiotherapist why prolonged, intense training combined with poor sleep can reduce lean mass. The physiotherapist points to a stress hormone released by the adrenal cortex that rises during exercise and, when chronically high, favors tissue breakdown. Which hormone and primary metabolic action fit this description?", "options": {"A": "Growth hormone, which suppresses protein synthesis and promotes glycogen storage in muscle", "B": "Aldosterone, which promotes protein catabolism to raise blood glucose during long sessions", "C": "Epinephrine, which increases amino acid uptake and raises muscle protein synthesis", "D": "Cortisol, which promotes protein catabolism and gluconeogenesis from amino acids"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cortisol, which promotes protein catabolism and gluconeogenesis from amino acids): Cortisol is released from the adrenal cortex and mobilizes amino acids from muscle for gluconeogenesis, so chronic elevation favors protein loss. Aldosterone is also cortical but regulates sodium and potassium. Growth hormone supports protein synthesis, and epinephrine comes from the adrenal medulla and mainly drives glycogenolysis and lipolysis."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 49-year-old teacher has had low back pain for 14 months, avoids bending and lifting because she fears damage, and has normal neurological testing and benign imaging. She reports passive modalities help only briefly. Which approach best fits current rehabilitation guidance for her?", "options": {"A": "Rest and lumbar bracing for six weeks, then a gradual return to lifting", "B": "Graded activity and exercise with pain education, using modalities as brief adjuncts", "C": "Repeat lumbar MRI and consider epidural injection before beginning any active program", "D": "More frequent passive modality sessions to build lasting relief before any exercise starts"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Graded activity and exercise with pain education, using modalities as brief adjuncts): Fear avoidance sustains chronic pain, and graded exposure with education and exercise is the evidence supported response. Passive modalities give short relief and serve only as adjuncts. Rest and bracing reinforce avoidance of movement. Repeat imaging and injection are not indicated for nonspecific pain with benign imaging and normal neurological findings."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 77-year-old woman with congestive heart failure, on a diuretic, has bilateral pitting leg edema that worsens by evening. She asks the chiropractor to treat both legs with high voltage pulsed current to reduce the swelling before a family trip. Which response is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Treat both legs with sensory-level cathodal current in an immersion basin, rechecking next visit", "B": "Treat one leg at a time with motor-level pumping contractions at 10 pps", "C": "Defer electrical treatment and refer back to the physician for the cardiac failure", "D": "Treat with elevation and positive polarity to draw fluid out"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defer electrical treatment and refer back to the physician for the cardiac failure): Bilateral pitting edema in congestive heart failure is a systemic problem, and local electrical treatment does not address its cause. Pumping fluid toward a failing heart can also worsen cardiac load. The appropriate step is to defer treatment and refer the patient to the physician for medical management."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old woman uses conventional TENS for chronic shoulder pain at 100 pps with a pulse duration of 300 microseconds. Before pain relief appears, she feels uncomfortable muscle contractions at the shoulder and wants only a comfortable tingling. The clinician wants to keep the pulse rate. Which adjustment best produces selective sensory stimulation?", "options": {"A": "Lower the pulse rate to 4 pps so that fewer pulses reach the muscle.", "B": "Lengthen the pulse duration to 500 microseconds and lower the intensity slightly.", "C": "Increase the intensity until contractions fuse, as tolerance to twitching develops with time.", "D": "Shorten the pulse duration to about 50 to 100 microseconds and adjust intensity to tingling."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Shorten the pulse duration to about 50 to 100 microseconds and adjust intensity to tingling.): Short phases of about 50 to 100 microseconds preferentially excite large sensory fibers before motor fibers, so tingling can be produced without contraction. A 300 microsecond phase recruits motor fibers at lower amplitude. Lengthening it further worsens contractions, and lowering the rate changes the mode, not selectivity."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A chiropractic student sets up high voltage pulsed current for a swollen foot using the immersion technique, with the foot placed in a basin of water. The instructor asks where the two electrodes belong. Which arrangement correctly describes electrode placement in this technique?", "options": {"A": "Active electrode on the proximal thigh, dispersive electrode submerged in the basin beside the foot", "B": "Both electrodes on dry skin of the lower leg, with the water acting as a ground", "C": "Active electrode submerged in the basin near the foot, dispersive electrode on the proximal leg", "D": "Both electrodes submerged in the basin on opposite sides of the foot"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Active electrode submerged in the basin near the foot, dispersive electrode on the proximal leg): In the immersion technique the active electrode goes into the water bath, which acts as a large conductive interface around the foot. The dispersive electrode is placed on the skin of the proximal leg to complete the circuit through the limb. Placing both in the water bypasses the tissue, and reversing the roles changes the intended polarity effect. Water is a conductor in the circuit, not a ground."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A runner with chronic Achilles tendinopathy soreness after a long run is placed in a cold whirlpool for the lower leg. The therapist sets the water temperature according to standard guidelines for cold immersion in the rehabilitation setting, aiming for effective cooling without the severe discomfort of near freezing water. Which range is typically used?", "options": {"A": "Approximately 40 to 45 degrees F (about 4 to 7 degrees C)", "B": "Approximately 65 to 75 degrees F (about 18 to 24 degrees C)", "C": "Approximately 50 to 60 degrees F (about 10 to 15 degrees C)", "D": "Approximately 92 to 98 degrees F (about 33 to 37 degrees C)"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Approximately 50 to 60 degrees F (about 10 to 15 degrees C)): Cold whirlpools are typically set at about 50 to 60 degrees F (10 to 15 degrees C), which cools effectively and stays tolerable. Water at 40 to 45 degrees F is closer to ice bath temperatures and is poorly tolerated. Water at 65 to 75 degrees F is only cool and gives little cooling. Water at 92 to 98 degrees F is neutral warm and is not used for cryotherapy."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 33-year-old runner repeats an incremental treadmill test after ten weeks of endurance training. At 9 miles per hour, her blood lactate was 4.5 mmol/L before training and is now 2.0 mmol/L, and her VO2 max rose only slightly. The clinician interprets the change in the lactate curve. What does it indicate?", "options": {"A": "Her lactate threshold shifted to a lower percentage of her VO2 max", "B": "The lactate threshold shifted to a higher percentage of her VO2 max", "C": "Her VO2 max fell, because lactate production dropped at that speed", "D": "Her lactate clearance worsened, which reduces endurance at submaximal speeds"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The lactate threshold shifted to a higher percentage of her VO2 max): Lower lactate at the same absolute speed means the curve moved right, so the threshold now occurs at a higher fraction of VO2 max and a higher running speed. This is a common endurance adaptation and can occur with only a small rise in VO2 max. A leftward shift would raise lactate, a falling VO2 max would not lower lactate in this pattern, and worse clearance would raise it."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A student reviewing phototherapy notes asks why clinical lasers for musculoskeletal conditions emit between roughly 600 and 1000 nm instead of in the blue or ultraviolet range. A chiropractor explains that this span is called the optical window for tissue penetration. What best explains why wavelengths inside this span reach deeper tissue?", "options": {"A": "Shorter wavelengths are scattered less by skin collagen, so ultraviolet and blue light penetrate farthest", "B": "Tissue contains no chromophores in this span, so light passes through without interaction", "C": "Hemoglobin and melanin absorb weakly above 600 nm while water absorbs weakly below 1100 nm", "D": "Longer wavelengths carry more energy per photon, so they drive deeper into tissue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hemoglobin and melanin absorb weakly above 600 nm while water absorbs weakly below 1100 nm): Hemoglobin and melanin absorb strongly in the blue and green range, and water absorption rises steeply beyond about 1100 nm. Between these limits absorption is low enough that light can travel several millimeters or more. Photon energy actually rises as wavelength shortens, and ultraviolet and blue light are absorbed in the superficial skin layers."}, {"id": 914, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A strength coach screens a college athlete with the Functional Movement Screen before the preseason and wants to record the total composite score correctly on the intake form. How many fundamental movement patterns are scored, and what is the highest total composite score that can be earned?", "options": {"A": "Five patterns scored 0 to 3 each, for a maximum composite of 15", "B": "Seven patterns scored 0 to 3 each, for a maximum composite of 21", "C": "Seven patterns scored 0 to 5 each, for a maximum composite of 35", "D": "Ten patterns, seven scored and three clearing tests, for a maximum composite of 30"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seven patterns scored 0 to 3 each, for a maximum composite of 21): The Functional Movement Screen scores seven fundamental patterns from 0 to 3, so the highest composite is 21. Five patterns would give only 15, and a 0 to 5 scale would raise the maximum to 35. Clearing tests record pain only and are not added to the composite."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 34-year-old warehouse worker has acute low back pain after lifting, rated 7 of 10, with no motor weakness. The chiropractor chooses interferential current to apply gate control analgesia over the paraspinals using a four-pole setup. Which beat frequency and intensity best match this goal?", "options": {"A": "A beat of about 100 Hz at an intensity strong enough to produce tetanic contraction", "B": "A beat of about 100 Hz at sensory intensity with comfortable tingling and no contraction", "C": "A beat of about 1000 Hz at an intensity just below the sensory threshold", "D": "A beat of about 2 Hz at an intensity strong enough to produce visible contraction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A beat of about 100 Hz at sensory intensity with comfortable tingling and no contraction): Gate control analgesia is usually delivered with a beat in the range of roughly 80 to 150 Hz at a sensory intensity, giving tingling without contraction. A 2 Hz beat with visible contraction is the strategy aimed at endorphin-mediated relief, typically for chronic pain. A 1000 Hz beat is far outside the usual beat range, and a subsensory level would not stimulate the afferent fibers. Tetanic contraction at 100 Hz adds fatigue and discomfort to an acute injury."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 25-year-old basketball player receives high voltage pulsed current with the ankle in a basin the day after a sprain. After the therapist increases intensity for comfort, the foot muscles contract visibly at a pulse rate of 120 pps. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Lower intensity below the motor threshold, since contraction is not the aim here", "B": "Switch to positive polarity so the contractions fade", "C": "Lower the pulse rate to 10 pps so contractions turn into gentle pumping of the ankle", "D": "Move the dispersive electrode into the basin to spread the current"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower intensity below the motor threshold, since contraction is not the aim here): In acute edema control the intensity stays below the motor threshold, because contraction can increase bleeding and swelling in a fresh injury. Reducing intensity is the direct fix. A low pulse rate pump belongs to later, chronic swelling. Electrode position and polarity do not control contraction."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 68-year-old former smoker reports calf cramping after walking two blocks that eases within minutes of standing still. Her physical therapist measures ankle and brachial systolic pressures and calculates the ankle-brachial index. Which value of this index is the accepted threshold that supports a diagnosis of peripheral artery disease?", "options": {"A": "An index at or below 0.40 measured immediately after treadmill walking", "B": "An index at or below 0.90 measured while the patient rests supine", "C": "An index above 1.40, which signals stiff and incompressible vessel walls", "D": "An index between 1.00 and 1.40 measured while the patient rests supine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An index at or below 0.90 measured while the patient rests supine): The ankle-brachial index is the ratio of ankle to brachial systolic pressure, and a resting value of 0.90 or lower indicates peripheral artery disease. Values of 1.00 to 1.40 are normal. Readings above 1.40 suggest noncompressible, calcified arteries and are unreliable rather than diagnostic. Values near 0.40 reflect severe disease, and post-exercise testing is used as an additional measure, not the diagnostic threshold."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 31-year-old runner asks whether to carbohydrate load before a 45-minute 10 km race and before a 3-hour trail marathon the following month. Muscle glycogen supercompensation is the tool being considered. For which situation does glycogen loading offer the most expected performance benefit?", "options": {"A": "Both events, since glycogen loading raises stores enough to help any race beyond 30 minutes", "B": "The 10 km race, because high intensity depends heavily on stored muscle glycogen", "C": "The 3-hour trail marathon, where effort exceeds about 90 minutes at moderate to high intensity", "D": "Neither event, because glycogen loading mainly benefits ultra events lasting over six hours"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The 3-hour trail marathon, where effort exceeds about 90 minutes at moderate to high intensity): Glycogen loading helps when effort exceeds about 90 minutes at moderate to high intensity, which describes a 3-hour trail marathon. A 45-minute race rarely depletes stores, so loading adds little. High intensity does use glycogen, but not enough to run out. The benefit does not wait for ultra events lasting several hours."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A student explains to a classmate why therapists often apply moist heat to a stiff joint capsule or tight muscle before beginning sustained stretching. The explanation centers on how elevated tissue temperature changes the mechanical behavior of connective tissue. Which physiological effect of heating best supports stretching afterward?", "options": {"A": "Greater extensibility of collagen, which allows more length gain with sustained stretch", "B": "Constriction of cutaneous vessels, which shunts blood into deep muscle", "C": "Reduced capillary permeability, which lowers tissue stiffness from edema", "D": "Lower local metabolic rate, which reduces tissue resistance to elongation during sustained stretch"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Greater extensibility of collagen, which allows more length gain with sustained stretch): Warm collagen becomes more extensible, so the same stretching force produces more length change with less tissue stress. Heat increases rather than decreases capillary permeability and metabolic rate. Heat also dilates cutaneous vessels instead of constricting them."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A therapist applies a gel cold pack to a patient's sore quadriceps after exercise. The gel pack was removed from a freezer and will be placed against the skin through a layer of cloth. Which interface material allows the cold to transfer most effectively to the tissue while remaining comfortable?", "options": {"A": "A sheet of plastic wrap sealed against the dry skin", "B": "A single layer of damp toweling between the pack and skin", "C": "A thick wool blanket wrapped around both pack and thigh", "D": "Several layers of dry toweling folded between the pack and skin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A single layer of damp toweling between the pack and skin): Moisture improves conduction, so one layer of damp toweling lets cold pass efficiently and also protects the skin from direct contact with the frozen gel. Dry toweling and wool insulate and slow cooling, which is the purpose of layers in heat treatments. Plastic wrap sealed on dry skin adds little conduction."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A therapist plans quadripolar interferential treatment for an arthritic knee and sets out the electrodes. A classmate asks what defines the four-pole technique compared with other interferential setups, since the unit lists several options. Which description correctly states the equipment arrangement used in the four-pole technique?", "options": {"A": "Four electrodes on two separate circuits whose current paths cross inside the treated tissue", "B": "Two electrodes on one circuit delivering current already modulated at the beat frequency", "C": "Four electrodes on one circuit with two active electrodes and two return electrodes", "D": "Three electrodes sharing one large dispersive pad and two small active electrodes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Four electrodes on two separate circuits whose current paths cross inside the treated tissue): The four-pole technique uses two independent circuits, each with its own pair of electrodes, positioned so the paths cross in the target tissue. The beat forms in the tissue where the two carriers interfere. Two electrodes on one circuit describes the premodulated technique. A single circuit with four pads or a shared dispersive pad cannot create tissue interference."}, {"id": 922, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A therapist treats a 42 year old tennis player with lateral epicondylalgia using ice massage from a frozen foam cup over a palm sized area. The patient has normal sensation and no cold sensitivity. The therapist moves the ice in slow overlapping circles. What is the usual endpoint and typical duration for this application?", "options": {"A": "Until the skin turns bright red, usually within about 1 to 2 minutes", "B": "Until the patient reports aching and burning sensations, usually within about 2 to 3 minutes", "C": "Until twenty minutes of circular passes are completed, matching the standard ice pack duration", "D": "Until the skin is numb, usually within about 5 to 10 minutes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Until the skin is numb, usually within about 5 to 10 minutes): Ice massage continues until the skin is numb, which usually takes about 5 to 10 minutes over a small area. Redness appears early, before analgesia, and aching and burning are intermediate sensations that come before numbness. Twenty minutes is the dose for a cold pack, and ice massage over that long risks excessive cooling."}, {"id": 923, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "During a clinic workshop, an instructor explains that one diagonal reproduces the movement of drawing a sword from its scabbard. The instructor starts the patient's arm extended, adducted, and internally rotated across the opposite hip and guides it up and away. Which shoulder motions complete this upper extremity D2 flexion pattern as taught in proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation?", "options": {"A": "Shoulder flexion, abduction, and internal rotation, ending with the arm overhead and the palm facing forward", "B": "Shoulder flexion, adduction, and external rotation, ending with the arm crossing in front of the face", "C": "Shoulder flexion, abduction, and external rotation, ending with the arm overhead and out to the side", "D": "Shoulder extension, abduction, and internal rotation, ending with the arm down and away from the body"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shoulder flexion, abduction, and external rotation, ending with the arm overhead and out to the side): D2 flexion starts in extension, adduction, and internal rotation and ends in flexion, abduction, and external rotation, so the arm finishes overhead and out to the side. Ending across the face describes D1 flexion, which keeps adduction. Pairing abduction with internal rotation does not match either flexion diagonal. The downward finish is the D1 extension pattern."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A therapist photographs a healthy walker at the instant the right heel strikes the ground and checks for reciprocal arm swing coordinated with the legs and trunk. Which arm and shoulder position is expected at this instant of right initial contact?", "options": {"A": "Right arm forward and left arm back, shoulders turned with the pelvis", "B": "Left arm forward and right arm back, shoulders turned against the pelvis", "C": "Left arm forward and right arm back, shoulders turned square to the walkway", "D": "Right arm forward and left arm back, shoulders turned against the pelvis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Left arm forward and right arm back, shoulders turned against the pelvis): At right initial contact the left arm swings forward and the right arm back, while the shoulders rotate opposite to the pelvis. Reversed arms or shoulders turning with the pelvis break reciprocal coordination, and shoulders held square omit the counter-rotation."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 74-year-old man scores 38 out of 56 on the Berg Balance Scale at the start of rehabilitation after a hospital stay for pneumonia. He lived independently before admission and walked without a device. Using the commonly cited score bands, which classification and mobility recommendation best fits this result?", "options": {"A": "Negligible fall risk, with balance training not indicated at this stage", "B": "High fall risk, with wheelchair use recommended for household distances", "C": "Low fall risk, with independent community walking and no device", "D": "Elevated fall risk, with walking assisted or aided by a device"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated fall risk, with walking assisted or aided by a device): On the Berg scale, 41 to 56 suggests independent mobility, 21 to 40 suggests walking with assistance or a device, and 0 to 20 suggests wheelchair mobility. A score of 38 falls in the middle band with elevated fall risk. The other options place the patient in the top or bottom band."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A student treats a clean heel ulcer with high voltage pulsed current. Twenty minutes in, the patient reports a sharp burning at the wound. Inspection shows the gauze around the active electrode has dried out, and the intensity has not changed. What is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Reverse polarity so the burning sensation shifts to the dispersive pad", "B": "Replace the saline gauze with ultrasound gel and continue unchanged", "C": "Increase intensity slightly to keep delivering the same sensation through the dry gauze", "D": "Pause treatment, re-moisten the gauze with saline, and reset intensity before resuming"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pause treatment, re-moisten the gauze with saline, and reset intensity before resuming): Dry gauze raises impedance and concentrates current, causing burning. The treatment is paused, the gauze re-moistened with saline, and intensity reset to a comfortable level before resuming. Raising intensity worsens the problem, gel is not the standard interface in a wound, and reversing polarity changes the biology of the treatment while ignoring the dry interface."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 50-year-old patient has limited shoulder external rotation. Testing shows a firm, capsular end feel and reduced accessory glenohumeral glides, with muscle length testing of the surrounding shoulder muscles normal. A student chiropractor asks which treatment most directly addresses restricted motion from a tight joint capsule. Which is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Static stretching of the pectoralis major with the arm in a doorway position", "B": "PNF contract-relax stretching of the subscapularis through the available external rotation range", "C": "Graded joint mobilization applied to the glenohumeral joint capsule to restore accessory glides", "D": "Ballistic stretching of the shoulder external rotators using rapid bouncing movements"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Graded joint mobilization applied to the glenohumeral joint capsule to restore accessory glides): A firm capsular end feel with reduced accessory glides indicates a joint restriction, so graded mobilization of the capsule is the direct treatment. Muscle stretching techniques act on contractile tissue and are chosen when muscle length tests are abnormal. Here muscle length is normal, so stretching the pectoralis, using bouncing, or using PNF on the subscapularis would not restore the lost glide."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "On a video frame of the left limb, the left heel is raised off the floor and the forefoot remains on the ground. The right foot is still swinging forward and has not yet touched the floor. Which stance subphase is the left limb in?", "options": {"A": "Terminal stance, which ends when the right foot makes contact", "B": "Loading response, which ends when the right foot leaves the floor", "C": "Midstance, which ends when the left heel begins to rise", "D": "Preswing, which begins when the right foot makes contact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Terminal stance, which ends when the right foot makes contact): Terminal stance begins with heel rise and ends when the opposite foot contacts the floor. The raised heel excludes midstance, and the right foot still in the air means that double support has not begun, which excludes preswing. Loading response is the first part of stance."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 27-year-old cyclist is two days out from internal fixation of a tibial plateau fracture. The surgeon's order reads touchdown weight bearing on the right leg, and the therapist teaches crutch gait before discharge. Which instruction correctly applies this weight bearing status during walking?", "options": {"A": "The right leg may carry about half of the body weight, as with partial weight bearing", "B": "The right leg may carry full body weight as long as pain remains tolerable during stance", "C": "The right leg is kept lifted off the ground during every phase of gait", "D": "The right foot touches the floor for balance and carries almost no body weight"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The right foot touches the floor for balance and carries almost no body weight): Touchdown, or toe touch, weight bearing lets the foot contact the floor for balance and proprioception while the limb supports almost no load. Partial weight bearing allows a defined percentage of body weight, non weight bearing allows no floor contact, and weight bearing as tolerated allows load up to pain limits. The fixation needs protection from axial load while early healing occurs."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 56-year-old farmer needs electrical stimulation for pain over the thick, calloused skin of his palm. A low-voltage stimulator gives little sensation at tolerable intensity. The chiropractor considers high voltage pulsed current instead. What feature of high voltage pulsed current justifies the switch?", "options": {"A": "A high peak voltage drives current through skin resistance while brief pulses keep it comfortable", "B": "A high average current heats the callus and lowers the skin resistance", "C": "A high carrier frequency passes through the callus like a capacitor", "D": "A high charge per pulse overwhelms the callus and forces tetanic contraction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A high peak voltage drives current through skin resistance while brief pulses keep it comfortable): The high peak voltage can push current through the resistance of thick skin, and the very short pulse duration keeps charge per pulse low so the treatment stays comfortable. Average current is low, not high, so it does not heat the callus. High voltage pulsed current has no carrier, and its charge per pulse is small, not large."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician applies a microcurrent device to a patient's ankle at 800 microamperes, and the patient reports a distinct tingling sensation at the electrode sites within one minute. The treatment goal was sub-sensory stimulation for tissue healing, and the device is working normally with good pad contact. How should the clinician interpret this finding?", "options": {"A": "Expected finding, because microcurrent is meant to produce tingling that confirms electrode contact.", "B": "The device has switched into TENS mode, so amplitude is now displayed in milliamperes.", "C": "Output exceeds this patient's sensory threshold, so amplitude should be lowered until no tingling remains.", "D": "Skin impedance has risen, so amplitude should be raised to 1.2 mA for sub-sensory delivery."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Output exceeds this patient's sensory threshold, so amplitude should be lowered until no tingling remains.): Microcurrent therapy is intended to be sub-sensory, so tingling means this patient's threshold is below the delivered amplitude. The clinician should reduce amplitude until the sensation disappears. Tingling is not required to confirm contact, a device does not switch modes on its own, and raising output to 1.2 mA moves beyond the microampere range and increases sensation."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "The label on a therapeutic ultrasound transducer lists a head diameter of 2.5 cm and an effective radiating area of 4.0 cm2. A new student asks what the effective radiating area represents and why it does not match the physical face of the metal sound head. Which statement best defines ERA?", "options": {"A": "The depth of tissue penetration reached at the selected frequency and intensity", "B": "The total skin area that should be treated in one session, set by the clinician", "C": "The portion of the transducer face that emits ultrasound, which is smaller than the metal head", "D": "The ratio of peak intensity to average intensity across the sound beam"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The portion of the transducer face that emits ultrasound, which is smaller than the metal head): ERA is the area of the crystal and head face that actually radiates sound, measured by the manufacturer, and it is always less than the area of the metal head. It is used to convert power in watts into intensity in W/cm2. The treatment field and the penetration depth are different concepts, and the peak to average ratio is the BNR."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 58-year-old house painter has shoulder pain with overhead reaching and a painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees of abduction. Imaging reports describe wear where a cuff tendon passes beneath the acromion and coracoacromial arch. Which rotator cuff tendon is most commonly affected by subacromial impingement?", "options": {"A": "The supraspinatus tendon, which runs under the acromial arch to the greater tuberosity", "B": "The subscapularis tendon, which crosses the anterior humeral head to the lesser tuberosity", "C": "The infraspinatus tendon, which crosses the posterior humeral head to the greater tuberosity", "D": "The teres minor tendon, which runs below the infraspinatus along the posterior capsule"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The supraspinatus tendon, which runs under the acromial arch to the greater tuberosity): The supraspinatus passes through the subacromial space and is the tendon most often compressed and degenerated in impingement. The painful arc between 60 and 120 degrees fits its position. The subscapularis, infraspinatus, and teres minor are cuff muscles but lie outside the narrowest part of the space."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 29-year-old man starts a leg strengthening program after a long period of inactivity. After three weeks his one repetition maximum has risen 22 percent, yet thigh girth has not changed. Which explanation best accounts for the early strength gain?", "options": {"A": "Increased tendon length that improved the muscle length-tension relationship", "B": "Neural adaptation, including improved motor unit recruitment and firing coordination", "C": "Muscle fiber hypertrophy from increased myofibrillar protein synthesis in the thigh muscles", "D": "Hyperplasia, meaning new muscle fibers formed by splitting of existing fibers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neural adaptation, including improved motor unit recruitment and firing coordination): In the first several weeks of resistance training, strength rises mainly through neural adaptation: more motor units are recruited and they fire more synchronously. Hypertrophy usually becomes the major contributor after about six to eight weeks, so unchanged girth fits a neural cause. Hyperplasia is not an established response, and tendon lengthening does not explain early gains."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinic's older interferential unit offers a two-pole premodulated mode, and a new student does not see how interference can occur without two crossing circuits. The student asks the supervising chiropractor where the beat frequency is actually created when this mode is selected. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "Within the tissue, where two separate circuits cross beneath the electrodes of the treated limb", "B": "In the lead wires, which act as capacitors that mix the two carriers", "C": "Inside the unit before delivery, so one circuit carries current already amplitude modulated", "D": "At the skin surface, where each electrode adds its own carrier frequency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inside the unit before delivery, so one circuit carries current already amplitude modulated): In premodulated mode the two carriers are combined inside the stimulator, so a single circuit delivers an already amplitude-modulated current through two electrodes. No crossing paths exist, so no interference occurs in the tissue. The four-pole setup is the technique that depends on tissue interference. Lead wires only conduct the current to the pads."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A clinic nurse practitioner asks the physical therapist to run the Timed Up and Go on an 80-year-old man before his discharge home. The therapist cues him to stand from a standard armchair at the word go and walk at a comfortable pace. What distance is walked before turning back, and what is measured?", "options": {"A": "Walk 10 meters in a straight line; gait speed is recorded", "B": "Walk 3 meters, turn, return, and sit; total time is recorded", "C": "Walk 6 meters, turn, return, and sit; the total time is recorded", "D": "Walk 3 meters, turn, return, and sit; the step count is recorded"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Walk 3 meters, turn, return, and sit; total time is recorded): The Timed Up and Go measures the seconds needed to rise from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down again. Doubling the distance or counting steps changes the validated procedure and the cutoff values no longer apply. A straight 10 meter walk with speed in meters per second is a separate gait speed test."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician programs pulsed ultrasound with a peak output of 6 watts at a 20 percent duty cycle, delivered during one session lasting 10 minutes. The student must estimate the total energy delivered in joules. What is the correct estimate?", "options": {"A": "3,600 joules, from 6 W times 600 seconds", "B": "720 joules, from 6 W times 0.20 times 600 seconds", "C": "120 joules, from 6 W times 0.20 times 100 seconds", "D": "7,200 joules, from 6 W divided by 0.20 times 240 seconds"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (720 joules, from 6 W times 0.20 times 600 seconds): Average power is 6 W times 0.20, or 1.2 W, and 10 minutes is 600 seconds, so energy is 1.2 times 600, which is 720 J. Ignoring the duty cycle overstates it at 3,600 J. Check: 720 divided by 600 seconds returns 1.2 W."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A chiropractic student assesses lateral standing alignment of a 30-year-old patient using a plumb line hung from the ceiling. In ideal posture viewed from the side, the line should pass through which sequence of landmarks, beginning at the head and ending at the foot?", "options": {"A": "Ear lobe, acromion, greater trochanter, just anterior to knee axis, anterior to lateral malleolus", "B": "Mastoid process, acromion, iliac crest, posterior to knee axis, behind the lateral malleolus", "C": "Ear lobe, clavicle midpoint, anterior superior iliac spine, patella center, fifth metatarsal head", "D": "Ear lobe, acromion, greater trochanter, directly through the knee axis, behind the lateral malleolus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ear lobe, acromion, greater trochanter, just anterior to knee axis, anterior to lateral malleolus): In the lateral view the plumb line should fall through the ear lobe, the acromion, the greater trochanter, slightly in front of the knee axis, and in front of the lateral malleolus. Landmarks such as the iliac crest or a line behind the malleolus describe other views or deviations. Deviation of any segment from the line helps identify the muscle imbalance responsible."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 26-year-old lifter's lumbar spine rounds near the bottom of a deep squat, but only below parallel. Supine passive hip flexion measures 95 degrees with the knee pulled to the chest, compared with a typical 120 degrees, and the glutes activate normally in a bridge. Which approach best fits?", "options": {"A": "Lumbar flexion stretching to improve depth of the squat", "B": "Heavy glute strengthening since the pattern fails below parallel", "C": "Core bracing with higher loads to hold the lumbar spine still through every rep", "D": "Hip flexion mobility work to expand available range before deep squatting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hip flexion mobility work to expand available range before deep squatting): Passive hip flexion of 95 degrees is well below the typical 120, so the hip runs out of range and the lumbar spine rounds to finish the squat. Gaining hip range addresses the cause. Normal glute activation argues against a strength problem, and more lumbar flexion would worsen the rounding."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A clinician compares four ultrasound settings by temporal average intensity: continuous at 0.5 W/cm2, pulsed at 20 percent with a 3.0 W/cm2 peak, pulsed at 20 percent with a 2.5 W/cm2 peak, and pulsed at 50 percent with a 1.4 W/cm2 peak. Which setting delivers the highest average?", "options": {"A": "Pulsed at 20 percent duty with a 3.0 W/cm2 peak, averaging 0.6 W/cm2 per period", "B": "Pulsed at 50 percent duty cycle with a peak of 1.4 W/cm2, averaging 0.7 W/cm2", "C": "Pulsed at 20 percent duty cycle with a peak of 2.5 W/cm2, averaging 0.5 W/cm2", "D": "Continuous output with a steady 0.5 W/cm2, averaging 0.5 W/cm2"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pulsed at 50 percent duty cycle with a peak of 1.4 W/cm2, averaging 0.7 W/cm2): Average intensity is peak times duty cycle: 3.0 times 0.20 is 0.6, 2.5 times 0.20 is 0.5, 1.4 times 0.50 is 0.7 and the continuous setting is 0.5. The 50 percent setting is highest despite its lower peak. Peak value alone is a poor guide to thermal dose."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A therapist, standing behind a harnessed patient, pulls the harness backward with enough force that the center of mass moves behind the heels. The patient takes a quick step backward and regains balance. Which statement best explains why this response was triggered?", "options": {"A": "Ankle plantar flexor torque was insufficient, so a hip strategy replaced the step", "B": "Postural muscle responses had a delayed onset because proprioceptive input from the feet was reduced", "C": "The vestibular system detected head acceleration and automatically triggered a protective stepping reflex", "D": "The center of mass moved past the limits of the base"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The center of mass moved past the limits of the base): A step is triggered when a perturbation moves the center of mass beyond the limits of the base of support, so ankle and hip responses cannot restore balance without moving the feet. Weak plantar flexor torque would produce a hip strategy attempt, not a step into a larger base. Delayed onset from reduced sensation is not described here. The step is driven by the size of the displacement rather than by a vestibular reflex."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 61-year-old man reports bilateral hand numbness, clumsiness with buttons, and unsteady walking over several months. Examination shows hyperreflexia in the legs and a positive Hoffmann sign on both sides. The referring physician requested cervical traction for neck stiffness. What should the therapist do?", "options": {"A": "Start traction at 10 lb and monitor the Hoffmann sign during each visit", "B": "Proceed because the bilateral findings rule out a single nerve root cause of symptoms", "C": "Use manual traction only, because it can be stopped immediately if symptoms worsen", "D": "Defer traction and refer for urgent imaging because the signs suggest cervical cord compression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Defer traction and refer for urgent imaging because the signs suggest cervical cord compression): Upper motor neuron signs such as hyperreflexia and a positive Hoffmann sign, with gait disturbance and clumsiness, indicate cervical myelopathy, and traction could further stress the cord. Imaging and medical review come first. A low force or manual technique still loads the neck, and bilateral findings point to the cord, not away from it."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 31-year-old runner presents 18 hours after an inversion ankle sprain with warm, swollen tissue. The chiropractor can use high voltage pulsed current either for sensory-level edema control or for motor-level pumping to move fluid. Which choice fits this stage?", "options": {"A": "Motor-level pumping contractions at 10 pps with the ankle in a dry, elevated position", "B": "Sensory-level anodal current with the ankle immersed in warm water", "C": "Sensory-level cathodal current with the ankle immersed in cool water", "D": "Motor-level pumping contractions with the ankle elevated and immersed in warm water"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sensory-level cathodal current with the ankle immersed in cool water): At 18 hours the injury is acute, so the goal is to limit swelling with sensory-level cathodal current and cool immersion. Motor-level pumping and warm water increase blood flow and swelling risk. Anodal current is not the polarity chosen to limit protein leakage in acute edema."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A therapist secures an ice bag with an elastic wrap over the lateral knee of a patient with a lateral collateral ligament sprain and leaves the patient alone for a long period. Afterward, the patient shows foot drop and numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve was most likely injured by prolonged cold and compression?", "options": {"A": "The tibial nerve, as it crosses behind the knee in the popliteal fossa", "B": "The common peroneal nerve, where it wraps around the fibular head", "C": "The saphenous nerve, running along the medial joint line of the knee", "D": "The femoral nerve, as it passes beneath the inguinal ligament into the thigh"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The common peroneal nerve, where it wraps around the fibular head): The common peroneal nerve lies superficially at the fibular head, so prolonged cooling with compression can cause neurapraxia with foot drop and dorsal foot numbness. The tibial nerve lies deep in the popliteal fossa, the femoral nerve is in the groin, and the saphenous nerve runs medially. None of those sit under a lateral knee ice bag."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 78-year-old woman with a fear of falling walks slowly with a slightly wide base, short steps, and a prolonged double support time. She turns in several small steps. Neurological examination is normal, with no tremor, rigidity, or ataxia. What is the best classification?", "options": {"A": "Parkinsonian gait, with festination and rigidity of the trunk and limbs", "B": "Cautious gait, slow short steps with long double support", "C": "Cerebellar ataxic gait, with irregular steps and dysmetria in the limbs", "D": "Sensory ataxic gait, with stamping steps worsened by eye closure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cautious gait, slow short steps with long double support): A cautious gait is an adaptive response to perceived instability: slow speed, short steps, a slightly wider base, and extra double support, with normal neurological findings. Parkinsonian gait needs rigidity or tremor, cerebellar ataxia needs irregularity and dysmetria, and sensory ataxia needs proprioceptive loss."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A physical therapist designs a program for a firefighter who must climb stairs quickly carrying equipment. She explains that the body's adaptations follow the speed, movement pattern and energy system that training actually loads. Which principle justifies selecting training that closely mimics the job task?", "options": {"A": "Variation, which rotates volume and intensity to prevent staleness and plateaus in adaptation", "B": "Progressive overload, which raises the training dose gradually as the tissue adapts to load", "C": "Specificity, also called the SAID principle, meaning adaptations are tied to the imposed demands", "D": "Diminishing returns, in which gains slow as a trainee approaches a genetic ceiling for performance"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Specificity, also called the SAID principle, meaning adaptations are tied to the imposed demands): Specificity, expressed as the SAID principle (specific adaptations to imposed demands), means training effects are largest for the movement pattern, velocity and energy system practiced. That is why a stair climbing job calls for task like stair training. Progressive overload, diminishing returns and variation are real training concepts but none of them selects the type of training to match a task."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old cyclist with patellofemoral pain has a positive Ely test on the right. The clinician teaches a prone quadriceps stretch and notes the patient arches the lower back and lifts the right hip as the heel approaches the buttock. How should the technique be corrected?", "options": {"A": "Brace the abdomen to hold the pelvis flat and flex the knee only to first tension", "B": "Let the lower back arch to increase the pull and accept the hip lift as normal", "C": "Move to a side-lying stretch with the hip flexed to 90 degrees and the knee bent", "D": "Pull the heel to the buttock harder with a strap so the hip extends fully"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brace the abdomen to hold the pelvis flat and flex the knee only to first tension): Lumbar arching and hip lift show the pelvis is tilting, which takes tension off the rectus femoris, so the abdominals hold the pelvis flat while the knee flexes only to first tension. Pulling harder with a strap increases the compensation. Accepting the arch wastes the stretch. A hip flexed to 90 degrees slackens the rectus femoris, which crosses the hip."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has chronic shoulder pain of eight months from rotator cuff tendinopathy. Sensory-level high voltage pulsed current at 100 pps over three visits gave only brief relief, and the chiropractor wants to try an endorphin-mediated approach instead. Which parameters fit?", "options": {"A": "A pulse rate of 2 pps at subsensory intensity with the electrodes immersed in water", "B": "A pulse rate of 120 pps at sensory intensity for longer sessions", "C": "A pulse rate of 100 pps at intensity producing tetanic contraction", "D": "A low rate of 2 to 10 pps with intensity strong enough for visible twitching"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A low rate of 2 to 10 pps with intensity strong enough for visible twitching): Endorphin-mediated analgesia is pursued with a low pulse rate, about 2 to 10 pps, and an intensity high enough to produce visible twitching. A higher rate at sensory intensity repeats the gate control approach that has already helped only briefly. Tetanic contraction at 100 pps fatigues the muscle, and a subsensory low rate gives no effective stimulus."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 79-year-old community-dwelling woman completes the Timed Up and Go in 16 seconds without a device. She reports no falls but feels unsteady on stairs and in crowds. Based on commonly used cutoff values for community-dwelling older adults, how should the therapist interpret this time?", "options": {"A": "Normal for her age, because times under 20 seconds indicate independent mobility", "B": "Severe impairment, because any time over 14 seconds means dependent mobility", "C": "Increased fall risk, because the time exceeds the commonly cited cutoff near 13.5 seconds", "D": "Mild deficit needing no action, because fall risk applies only after a documented fall"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased fall risk, because the time exceeds the commonly cited cutoff near 13.5 seconds): For community-dwelling older adults a Timed Up and Go time above about 13.5 seconds is associated with increased fall risk, so 16 seconds is abnormal and fits her unsteadiness. Times under 20 seconds describe independent basic transfers in frail groups, a different use. Dependent mobility is suggested by times above 30 seconds. Fall risk can be present before any fall has occurred."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 25-year-old man is eight weeks after hamstring graft ACL reconstruction. He has a persistent 10 degree quadriceps lag on straight leg raise despite doing daily quad sets, and the knee has no effusion. Which addition is most likely to improve voluntary quadriceps activation in this patient?", "options": {"A": "High frequency TENS over the quadriceps during rest periods between exercise sets", "B": "Neuromuscular stimulation of the quadriceps during isometric contractions near sixty degrees", "C": "Therapeutic ultrasound over the quadriceps tendon at a thermal dose before exercise", "D": "Interferential current at a beat frequency of 100 Hz delivered for pain relief"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Neuromuscular stimulation of the quadriceps during isometric contractions near sixty degrees): Persistent quadriceps lag after ACL surgery reflects arthrogenic muscle inhibition, and neuromuscular electrical stimulation combined with a voluntary isometric contraction recruits more motor units than exercise alone. The knee at about sixty degrees protects the graft. TENS, thermal ultrasound, and interferential pain currents do not generate the contraction needed to improve activation."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A patient with an inflamed tendon asks how an 810 nm laser can change tissue behavior without heating it noticeably. The chiropractor explains the leading photobiomodulation mechanism, in which light is absorbed by a specific mitochondrial chromophore and triggers downstream cellular effects. Which chromophore and resulting effect best match the accepted mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Water absorbs the photons, raising tissue temperature enough to accelerate local enzyme reaction rates", "B": "Hemoglobin absorbs the photons, producing deep vasodilation through direct smooth muscle heating", "C": "Cytochrome c oxidase absorbs the light, raising ATP output and releasing inhibitory nitric oxide", "D": "Collagen absorbs the photons, causing immediate contraction and remodeling of the tendon matrix"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cytochrome c oxidase absorbs the light, raising ATP output and releasing inhibitory nitric oxide): The accepted photobiomodulation mechanism begins with absorption by cytochrome c oxidase in the mitochondrial respiratory chain. This raises ATP output and displaces nitric oxide that had been inhibiting the enzyme. Heating of water, hemoglobin, or collagen describes thermal modalities and does not explain the effect at typical therapy doses."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A 34 year old woman with acne receives a UVB test exposure. At 20 inches from the lamp, a 2 minute exposure produced the target response. The clinician now moves the lamp to 30 inches to treat a larger area and wants the same dose delivered. Which new exposure time is correct?", "options": {"A": "Increase the exposure to 3 minutes at the 30 inch distance from the lamp", "B": "Increase the exposure to 6.75 minutes at the 30 inch distance from the lamp", "C": "Decrease the exposure to about 53 seconds at the 30 inch distance", "D": "Increase the exposure to 4.5 minutes at the 30 inch distance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase the exposure to 4.5 minutes at the 30 inch distance): Intensity falls with the square of distance, so time must rise with the square of the ratio. The ratio is 30 over 20, or 1.5, and 1.5 squared is 2.25, so 2 minutes times 2.25 equals 4.5 minutes. A linear scaling gives only 3 minutes, which underdoses. Inverting the ratio gives about 53 seconds and cubing it gives 6.75 minutes."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with stable coronary artery disease repeatedly develops mild exertional chest tightness within the first three minutes of brisk walking, which fades as she continues at the same pace. Her physician has confirmed no change in her pattern. What exercise modification best reduces this early symptom?", "options": {"A": "Lengthen the warm-up to 10 to 15 minutes of gradually increasing low-intensity activity", "B": "Lower the training heart rate to 40 percent of reserve for the entire session", "C": "Replace the warm-up with static stretching held 60 seconds per muscle group", "D": "Begin directly at the training heart rate and rest briefly each time the tightness appears"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lengthen the warm-up to 10 to 15 minutes of gradually increasing low-intensity activity): Early exertional symptoms that fade with continued effort show warm-up ischemia, and a longer gradual warm-up of 10 to 15 minutes lets coronary flow adapt before the target pace. Reducing intensity for the whole session sacrifices training and does not target the early minutes. Static stretching does not raise heart rate gradually. Starting at target pace worsens the early ischemia."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 19-year-old soccer player is three days out from a grade I lateral ankle sprain, with mild swelling and no deformity. The therapist wants a massage approach that limits swelling while respecting the stage of healing. Which technique is the best choice?", "options": {"A": "Effleurage from distal to proximal with the limb elevated above heart level", "B": "Deep petrissage across the swollen tissue to wring fluid from the ankle", "C": "Vigorous tapotement over the swollen lateral ankle to disperse the fluid quickly", "D": "Proximal to distal strokes along the limb to move fluid away from the heart"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Effleurage from distal to proximal with the limb elevated above heart level): Light effleurage directed toward the heart with the limb elevated lets gravity and the stroke work together to clear edema without stressing healing ligament. Percussion and deep kneading risk re-injury and bleeding in recently injured tissue. Strokes directed away from the heart push fluid against venous flow."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A therapist plans a program for a 41-year-old with upper crossed syndrome who has a tight pectoralis major and upper trapezius and a weak lower trapezius. Following Janda's approach to tonic and phasic imbalance, which order of interventions is best for the first phase of care?", "options": {"A": "Begin with general aerobic conditioning, then address the muscle imbalance after four weeks", "B": "Strengthen the weak phasic muscles first, then stretch the tight tonic muscles in a later phase", "C": "Release or stretch the tight tonic muscles first, then retrain the weak phasic muscles", "D": "Strengthen all muscle groups together at high load to restore balance in a single phase"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Release or stretch the tight tonic muscles first, then retrain the weak phasic muscles): Janda's approach releases the overactive tonic muscles first, since their tightness inhibits their antagonists, and then retrains the weak phasic muscles. Strengthening a muscle that is reciprocally inhibited by a tight antagonist is less effective. Training everything at once or delaying correction does not follow the sequence."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician reviews the tissue effects of continuous direct current delivered to intact skin through two electrodes of opposite polarity. A student asks what happens beneath the negative electrode, the cathode, when direct current flows for an extended period. Which chemical reaction and tissue effect occur under the cathode?", "options": {"A": "Acidic reaction from hydrochloric acid formation, with tissue hardening and protein coagulation.", "B": "Neutral reaction with no net chemical change, as acid and base products cancel each other.", "C": "Alkaline reaction from sodium hydroxide formation, with tissue softening and liquefaction.", "D": "Acidic reaction from hydrochloric acid formation, with tissue softening and liquefaction."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alkaline reaction from sodium hydroxide formation, with tissue softening and liquefaction.): Under the cathode, sodium ions combine with water to form sodium hydroxide, an alkaline reaction that softens and liquefies tissue. Under the anode, hydrochloric acid forms and tissue hardens. Mixing the acid reaction with the softening effect is inconsistent. Chemical products do not cancel in direct current because each pole produces its own product."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A barefoot hiker steps on a sharp stone with the right foot. She instantly lifts the right leg while the left leg stiffens to support her body weight. A student must name the protective response that explains the left leg's action. Which reflex is responsible for the left leg extension?", "options": {"A": "The flexor withdrawal reflex, which shortens the limb away from the noxious stimulus", "B": "The tonic neck reflex, which extends limbs on the side the head is turned toward", "C": "The inverse myotatic reflex, which relaxes the left leg muscles as tension rises in them", "D": "The crossed extensor reflex, which extends the opposite limb so it can bear body weight"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The crossed extensor reflex, which extends the opposite limb so it can bear body weight): A painful stimulus triggers flexor withdrawal on the same side, and the crossed extensor reflex simultaneously excites extensors of the opposite limb so the body is supported on the other leg. Flexor withdrawal explains the right leg only. The tonic neck reflex is a postural reflex, and the inverse myotatic reflex relaxes a muscle rather than stiffening it."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old patient with deep gluteal muscle tightness 4 cm below the skin was treated with 1 MHz continuous at 0.5 W/cm2 with a moving head for 5 minutes. He felt no warmth and the technique was correct. What is the best adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the treatment area to the size of the head and keep the settings", "B": "Hold the head stationary at the same intensity to concentrate energy", "C": "Switch to 3 MHz at the same intensity, because it heats superficial and deep tissue faster", "D": "Increase intensity toward 1.5 W/cm2 and allow adequate time, keeping the head moving"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Increase intensity toward 1.5 W/cm2 and allow adequate time, keeping the head moving): A dose of 0.5 W/cm2 at 1 MHz heats very slowly, so no warmth is expected. The target is deep, so 1 MHz stays, and intensity is raised toward the thermal range with enough time. A 3 MHz beam is absorbed superficially and a stationary head risks hot spots."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 48 year old patient with chronic Achilles tendinopathy receives ice massage before loading exercises and reports that exercise is more tolerable for about an hour. The student asks which neurophysiological effects of brief intense cold best explain this pain relief. Which explanation is best?", "options": {"A": "Slowed conduction in pain fibers and sensory gate control reduce perceived pain", "B": "Reduced tissue metabolism that limits secondary hypoxic injury in the tendon sheath", "C": "Cold-induced vasodilation that washes algesic chemicals from the tendon within minutes", "D": "Reduced muscle spindle firing that relaxes the calf and unloads the tendon"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Slowed conduction in pain fibers and sensory gate control reduce perceived pain): Brief intense cold slows conduction in pain fibers and engages gate control, so perceived pain falls and exercise is easier. Lowered metabolism protects tissue in acute injury but does not explain relief in chronic tendinopathy. Cold-induced vasodilation is a minor effect. Spindle changes affect tone, not the pain relief reported here."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 45-year-old long-haul truck driver, after eleven years behind the wheel, shows increased lumbar lordosis, an anterior pelvic tilt, and a protruding abdomen with no neurologic findings. According to Janda's lower crossed syndrome, which muscle groups are typically shortened and overactive in this pattern, forming one diagonal of the cross?", "options": {"A": "The hip adductors and vastus medialis together with the transversus abdominis", "B": "The hamstrings, gluteus maximus and rectus abdominis acting as one overactive group", "C": "The rectus abdominis and gluteus maximus together with the gluteus medius", "D": "The iliopsoas and rectus femoris together with the thoracolumbar erector spinae"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The iliopsoas and rectus femoris together with the thoracolumbar erector spinae): Lower crossed syndrome pairs tight hip flexors and lumbar extensors on one diagonal with weak abdominals and gluteals on the other. Tight iliopsoas and rectus femoris tip the pelvis forward while the erector spinae deepen the lordosis. The abdominals and gluteal muscles are the inhibited arm, so listing them as overactive reverses the pattern."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "Two sound heads have the same ERA, and a clinician uses each at 1.0 W/cm2 continuous on the same patient on different days. The patient reports a focal burning under head B but not head A. Which explanation is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Head B had a lower beam nonuniformity ratio, which raised its average intensity at the skin", "B": "Head B has a larger ERA, which spreads the power over more tissue", "C": "Head B has a higher beam nonuniformity ratio, producing hot spots of peak intensity", "D": "Head B emits a higher frequency, which penetrates deeper and burns the muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Head B has a higher beam nonuniformity ratio, producing hot spots of peak intensity): With the same ERA and the same displayed intensity, a difference in sensation points to the beam profile. A head with a high BNR has peaks several times the average, which feels like focal burning. A lower BNR means a more even beam, so it would reduce hot spots rather than cause them, and the displayed average intensity is the same for both heads. The stem states that the ERAs are equal, and frequency is not tied to one head here."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A clinician needs to find the optimal electrode site on a weak tibialis anterior before electrical muscle stimulation. The patient is sitting with the leg relaxed. A pen electrode connected to a low-intensity pulsed current is available. Which method best locates the motor point of this muscle for electrode placement?", "options": {"A": "Palpate the tendon and place the pad at the musculotendinous junction at high intensity.", "B": "Measure to the geometric center of the muscle belly and place the pad there.", "C": "Place pads over bony prominences nearest the muscle, where current meets the least resistance.", "D": "Probe the skin and mark the site giving the strongest twitch at the lowest current."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Probe the skin and mark the site giving the strongest twitch at the lowest current.): The motor point is found functionally by moving a pen electrode over the skin and marking where the lowest current produces the strongest twitch. This identifies the site with the lowest nerve threshold. The tendon junction and belly center are not reliable substitutes, and bony prominences are chosen by neither resistance nor nerve location."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 61-year-old on long-term oral prednisone for asthma has tight shoulder muscles and wants vigorous stretching to regain overhead range. Skin is thin with easy bruising, and she had a rib fracture last year from a minor fall. Which stretching approach is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Partner-assisted ballistic stretching to take advantage of the elastic recoil of the shoulder tissues", "B": "Gentle low-load static stretching without manual overpressure, with attention to bone and skin fragility", "C": "Grade IV joint mobilization combined with maximal PNF contractions to restore overhead range quickly", "D": "High-load prolonged stretching with manual overpressure to maximize permanent connective tissue lengthening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gentle low-load static stretching without manual overpressure, with attention to bone and skin fragility): Long-term corticosteroids weaken skin, tendon, muscle and bone, so stretching should be gentle, low-load and free of manual overpressure. High loads, ballistic partner stretching and end-range mobilization with maximal contractions increase the risk of tears, bruising and fracture. Range can still improve gradually with low-load holds."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "During a Thomas test on a 41-year-old office worker, the patient lies supine and hugs the left knee to the chest. The right thigh rises off the table while the right knee stays flexed near 90 degrees. Which muscle shortening is most directly indicated by this finding?", "options": {"A": "Shortened iliopsoas limiting full extension of the tested hip", "B": "Shortened tensor fasciae latae limiting adduction of the tested hip", "C": "Shortened rectus femoris limiting knee flexion while the hip is extended", "D": "Shortened gluteus maximus limiting flexion of the tested hip"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Shortened iliopsoas limiting full extension of the tested hip): In the Thomas test, a thigh that lifts off the table while the knee stays flexed points to shortened iliopsoas, because the muscle holds the hip in flexion. Rectus femoris shortening shows instead as the knee extending, since the muscle crosses the knee. Tensor fasciae latae shortening shows as abduction or lateral drift. Gluteus maximus is a hip extensor, so its shortening would restrict hip flexion rather than lift the thigh."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "A 19-year-old sprinter asks her physiotherapist which energy system supplies most of the ATP during the first 5 to 8 seconds of an all-out 60 m dash. She has heard that oxygen delivery to the working legs has not yet caught up at that point. Which system is the dominant source of that energy?", "options": {"A": "Phosphocreatine breakdown, which rapidly rebuilds ATP through the creatine kinase reaction", "B": "Aerobic oxidation of fatty acids within mitochondria, supplying ATP at a high steady rate", "C": "Oxidative metabolism of muscle glycogen through the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain", "D": "Anaerobic glycolysis, converting blood glucose to pyruvate and lactate within the cytosol of the working fibers"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Phosphocreatine breakdown, which rapidly rebuilds ATP through the creatine kinase reaction): Phosphocreatine donates its phosphate to ADP through creatine kinase, regenerating ATP within a fraction of a second and without oxygen. This system dominates maximal efforts lasting up to roughly 10 seconds. Glycolysis is slower to ramp up and takes over as phosphocreatine falls, while fat and glycogen oxidation depend on oxygen delivery and supply ATP at a much lower rate."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "An 82-year-old man fell twice when rising from bed. His Timed Up and Go is 15 seconds. Supine blood pressure is 140/80 and after three minutes standing it is 108/66 with lightheadedness. He takes tamsulosin and furosemide. Which action is the best next step?", "options": {"A": "Begin progressive single-leg balance training on foam three times weekly", "B": "Prescribe a wheeled walker and discharge him with a home exercise handout", "C": "Start vestibular habituation exercises using repeated head turns", "D": "Contact his physician about orthostatic hypotension and medications, and teach staged rising"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contact his physician about orthostatic hypotension and medications, and teach staged rising): A systolic drop of 32 mmHg with symptoms on standing meets the criterion for orthostatic hypotension, and his alpha blocker and diuretic are likely contributors. The falls occurred on rising, so the physician needs to review the medications while the patient learns slow staged transitions. Balance, vestibular, or device measures leave the cause of the falls in place."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 50-year-old teacher receives interferential current for neck pain. Ten minutes into the session she reports the tingling has faded at the same intensity, although the unit has not changed. The therapist wants a built-in setting that discourages this response during the remaining time. Which setting should be turned on?", "options": {"A": "Vector scan, which moves the interference area but holds the beat constant", "B": "Automatic beat frequency sweep, such as varying between 1 and 100 Hz", "C": "Polarity reversal, which switches the electrodes between positive and negative", "D": "A carrier of 10,000 Hz, which lowers skin resistance during treatment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Automatic beat frequency sweep, such as varying between 1 and 100 Hz): A beat sweep automatically varies the beat frequency across a range, so the nerves do not adapt to a single constant stimulus. Interferential current is alternating, so polarity reversal is not an available strategy. Vector scan shifts where interference is greatest but keeps the beat the same. Changing the carrier alters skin impedance and does not prevent accommodation."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 26-year-old runner has medial shin pain. Examination shows a flexible flat foot with a navicular drop of 14 mm and excessive rearfoot eversion in stance that corrects when she rises onto her toes. Which orthotic design best addresses these findings?", "options": {"A": "A lateral heel wedge with soft cushioning to promote supination", "B": "A medial longitudinal arch support with a medial rearfoot post", "C": "A soft cushioned heel cup with a shock absorbing forefoot insole for impact reduction", "D": "A full-length rigid orthotic with a lateral forefoot post for a rigid cavus foot"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A medial longitudinal arch support with a medial rearfoot post): A flexible flat foot with excessive rearfoot eversion and a navicular drop responds to an arch support that raises the medial longitudinal arch together with a medial rearfoot post that limits calcaneal eversion. A lateral heel wedge shifts load the wrong way, cushioning absorbs impact but does not control pronation, and a rigid lateral forefoot post suits a rigid cavus foot."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A patient in an outpatient conditioning program is taught to rate how hard she is working with the original Borg rating of perceived exertion scale, which pairs numbers with verbal anchors such as very light and very, very hard. The student asks what numeric range that scale covers. What range does the original Borg RPE scale span?", "options": {"A": "6 to 20, so that a rating multiplied by ten approximates heart rate", "B": "10 to 100, with each rating equal to the percent of maximal heart rate", "C": "1 to 20, with the lowest rating representing a resting heart rate of 60", "D": "0 to 10, with descriptive anchors from nothing to maximal effort"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (6 to 20, so that a rating multiplied by ten approximates heart rate): The original Borg scale runs from 6 to 20 so that a rating times ten roughly matches the heart rate of a healthy young adult, with 6 near rest and 20 near maximum. The 0 to 10 scale is the revised category ratio version with different anchors. A scale starting at 1 or one measured as a percent of maximum is not the original instrument."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 35-year-old carpenter fell from a ladder 2 days ago and has an uncomplicated rib contusion with severe pain on breathing and moving. He needs rapid relief that he can control while working through the day. A clinician considers TENS options for a home unit. Which TENS setting is best suited to his need?", "options": {"A": "Conventional settings near 100 pps with short pulses at sensory intensity, worn during activity.", "B": "Brief intense settings with long pulses at maximum tolerable intensity for 8 hours daily.", "C": "Acupuncture-like settings near 4 pps at visible twitch intensity for 30 minutes once daily.", "D": "Burst settings at about 2 bursts per second with intensity high enough to twitch the ribs."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conventional settings near 100 pps with short pulses at sensory intensity, worn during activity.): Conventional TENS has a rapid onset and can be worn through the day because it uses sensory intensity without contraction. Acupuncture-like settings need 20 to 30 minutes to work and cause twitching that would aggravate painful rib movement. Brief intense mode is meant for short procedures, not all-day wear. Burst settings at twitch intensity would also move the injured ribs."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 30-year-old woman completes a verified maximal treadmill test with a peak heart rate of 172 beats per minute, although her age-predicted maximum is 190. Her resting heart rate is 60. The clinician prescribes 70 percent of heart rate reserve using the Karvonen method. Which target heart rate is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 151 beats per minute, from predicted maximum and resting rate in the reserve formula", "B": "About 138 beats per minute, from measured maximum and resting rate in the reserve formula", "C": "About 133 beats per minute, from 70 percent of the predicted maximum alone", "D": "About 120 beats per minute, from 70 percent of the measured maximum alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About 138 beats per minute, from measured maximum and resting rate in the reserve formula): A verified maximal test result is more accurate than a prediction, so reserve is 172 minus 60, or 112. Seventy percent of 112 is about 78, and adding the resting rate of 60 gives about 138 beats per minute. Using the predicted maximum gives 151. Taking a simple percent of either maximum gives 120 or 133 and is not the Karvonen method."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient with chronic low back pain performs the dead bug in supine, lowering one straight leg toward the table. On the third repetition her lumbar spine arches and she cannot keep a neutral position. The therapist wants to reduce difficulty while keeping the same motor control goal for the trunk. Which modification is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Lower the leg more slowly over five seconds while keeping the same range and arm position", "B": "Place a small towel roll under the low back to guide the spine and continue", "C": "Shorten the movement to a heel slide with the knee bent, keeping the arms still", "D": "Lower both bent legs together with the arms overhead to spread the load evenly"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shorten the movement to a heel slide with the knee bent, keeping the arms still): Shortening the movement to a heel slide with the knee bent reduces the lever so she can keep a neutral spine while practicing the same motor control. A slower lowering over the same range raises difficulty, a towel roll substitutes for control, and overhead arms with both legs raise the demand."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A 47-year-old golfer reports low back pain during the downswing. Lead hip internal rotation measures 10 degrees while the trail hip measures 35 degrees, and the examiner sees excess lumbar rotation during a practice swing. Considering regional interdependence, what is the best first focus?", "options": {"A": "Strengthening the abdominals with high repetition sit-ups before the swing", "B": "Restoring lead hip internal rotation to relieve compensatory lumbar rotation", "C": "Improving thoracic flexion range to unload the lumbar segments during rotation", "D": "Stretching the lumbar rotators to increase motion in the painful lumbar region"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Restoring lead hip internal rotation to relieve compensatory lumbar rotation): Lost hip rotation forces the lumbar spine to supply the missing rotation, so restoring lead hip range reduces lumbar stress. Stretching the lumbar rotators would add motion where it is already excessive. Sit-ups and thoracic flexion do not address the stiff hip."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "functional_assessment", "question": "A clinician applies the concept of regional interdependence when examining a 44-year-old patient reporting pain in one body region and wants to explain the idea to a chiropractic intern at the bedside. Which statement best defines the concept for musculoskeletal practice?", "options": {"A": "Local tissue findings at the painful site should guide treatment, so remote regions are skipped", "B": "Referred pain follows dermatomal patterns, so the spinal level matches the symptomatic limb", "C": "Pain felt in the periphery must be screened for systemic and visceral causes first", "D": "Impairments in a remote body region can contribute to the patient's presenting symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Impairments in a remote body region can contribute to the patient's presenting symptoms): Regional interdependence holds that impairments in a remote region, such as a stiff thoracic spine or weak hips, can contribute to symptoms elsewhere. Examination therefore includes regions beyond the painful one. Treating only the local site ignores this idea. Dermatomal referral describes how pain is felt, and screening for visceral causes is red flag practice, so both are real concepts that are not this definition."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 48-year-old has limited glenohumeral abduction caused by a tight inferior capsule. Applying the convex-concave rule, the therapist mobilizes the convex humeral head on the concave glenoid fossa. The patient lies supine with the arm supported and reports minimal pain. Which accessory glide direction best improves abduction?", "options": {"A": "Posterior glide of the humeral head on the glenoid fossa", "B": "Superior glide of the humeral head on the glenoid fossa", "C": "Inferior glide of the humeral head on the glenoid fossa", "D": "Anterior glide of the humeral head on the glenoid fossa"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Inferior glide of the humeral head on the glenoid fossa): When a convex surface moves on a concave surface, the glide occurs in the direction opposite the bone movement. In abduction the humeral head rolls superiorly, so it glides inferiorly, and an inferior glide mobilization restores the missing motion. A superior glide matches the roll and would jam the head upward. Anterior and posterior glides address rotation and flexion or extension limits rather than abduction."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A therapist positions an ultraviolet lamp over a patient's back and needs to predict how a change in lamp height alters the intensity arriving at the skin. The clinic teaches a physical law that relates the distance from the lamp to the radiant intensity. Which statement correctly gives the inverse square law for this lamp?", "options": {"A": "Intensity at the skin varies inversely with the distance from the lamp", "B": "Intensity at the skin varies inversely with the cube of the distance from the lamp", "C": "Intensity at the skin varies directly with the square of the distance from the lamp tube", "D": "Intensity at the skin varies inversely with the square of the distance from the lamp"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intensity at the skin varies inversely with the square of the distance from the lamp): For a point source the radiant energy spreads over an area that grows with the square of distance, so intensity falls with the square of distance and doubling the distance cuts it to one quarter. A simple inverse relationship underestimates the drop. A cube law overstates it. A direct relationship reverses the physics."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "neuromuscular_rehabilitation", "question": "A 77-year-old man reaches forward 5 inches in the Functional Reach test while standing with his arm flexed to 90 degrees and feet shoulder width apart. He lives alone and has not fallen yet. How should the therapist interpret this reach distance?", "options": {"A": "Substantial fall risk, since a reach of 6 inches or less is abnormal", "B": "Moderate risk only, since reaches of 6 to 10 inches indicate mild fall risk", "C": "Normal for a man of his age, since reaches above 7 inches are expected", "D": "Borderline result needing a repeat on a second day before any conclusion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Substantial fall risk, since a reach of 6 inches or less is abnormal): In Functional Reach a distance of 6 inches or less is associated with markedly increased fall likelihood, and 6 to 10 inches indicates moderate risk. A 5 inch reach is therefore abnormal and substantial. Placing him in the moderate band understates the finding. Published norms for older men are well above 7 inches. Repeating the test does not change the clear result."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 25-year-old athlete is 5 weeks after a partial meniscectomy. He is otherwise healthy. Strength is 4 out of 5, but a small effusion persists and the knee aches after walking. He asks to begin jump training. What is the best recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Delay plyometrics and continue strengthening until the effusion resolves and strength is adequate", "B": "Start single-leg hopping on a trampoline to improve joint position sense", "C": "Start low intensity bilateral jumps, since strength has reached four out of five on testing", "D": "Start depth jumps from a low box to build eccentric tolerance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delay plyometrics and continue strengthening until the effusion resolves and strength is adequate): Persistent effusion and post-activity aching show the joint is still irritable, so plyometric loading should wait until the effusion resolves and strength and control are adequate. Strength of four out of five alone does not clear him. Depth jumps and single-leg trampoline hopping impose even greater impact and instability demands."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 44 year old runner has deep buttock pain from suspected piriformis muscle tightness. The muscle lies several centimeters beneath the gluteal fat and muscle. The therapist wonders whether a hydrocollator pack applied for 30 minutes will substantially warm the piriformis. Which statement is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Moist heat penetrates deeply because water conducts heat better than dry toweling", "B": "Moist heat warms deep muscle more than skin when towel layers are added", "C": "Moist heat warms mainly superficial tissue, so deep muscle heating needs a different modality", "D": "Moist heat penetrates about 5 cm within 30 minutes, adequately warming the deep piriformis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Moist heat warms mainly superficial tissue, so deep muscle heating needs a different modality): Hydrocollator packs heat by conduction and raise temperature only in the superficial centimeter or so of tissue. A deep structure such as the piriformis needs a modality that reaches deeper tissue. Extra toweling reduces heat delivery and never makes deep tissue warmer than skin."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 45-year-old warehouse supervisor weighing 180 lb is scheduled for mechanical lumbar traction for a posterolateral disc protrusion. Before setting the dial, the clinician reviews the literature on the minimum tension needed to produce measurable separation of the lumbar vertebral bodies. Which force is generally cited as the threshold for lumbar intervertebral separation?", "options": {"A": "About 100 percent of body weight, which is roughly 180 lb for this patient", "B": "About 10 percent of body weight, which is roughly 18 lb for this patient", "C": "About 50 percent of body weight, which is roughly 90 lb for this patient", "D": "About 25 percent of body weight, which is roughly 45 lb for this patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 50 percent of body weight, which is roughly 90 lb for this patient): Separation of lumbar vertebral bodies is generally reported to need tension near 50 percent of body weight, which is 90 lb for a 180-lb patient. About 25 percent of body weight is the usual starting force and mostly overcomes friction of the lower body on the table. Ten percent is too low to overcome table friction, let alone separate segments. A pull equal to full body weight is not required for separation and would raise the risk of an adverse reaction."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 33 year old man is four weeks after a lateral ankle sprain, with stiffness and mild lingering soreness after activity but no warmth or visible swelling. He plans a session of ankle mobility exercise today. Which pairing of thermal modalities best fits his stage and goals?", "options": {"A": "Ice before exercise and heat afterward, as cold preserves the comfort of later mobility", "B": "Moist heat before mobility exercise and ice afterward if soreness or swelling follows", "C": "Ice for 20 minutes before exercise and moist heat afterward", "D": "Moist heat after exercise, with a cold pack applied before the mobility work"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Moist heat before mobility exercise and ice afterward if soreness or swelling follows): At four weeks without warmth or swelling, heat before exercise increases tissue extensibility and comfort, and ice afterward addresses soreness or swelling if it appears. Cooling first would stiffen tissue before mobility work. Avoiding heat is an acute stage rule that no longer applies, and heat afterward with cold before reverses the useful order."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A patient has weakness of elbow extension and wrist flexion, a diminished triceps reflex, and numbness of the middle finger after a neck injury in a rugby tackle. The biceps reflex and thumb sensation are normal. Which nerve root is most likely compressed?", "options": {"A": "The C7 root, exiting at the C6-C7 intervertebral foramen", "B": "The C8 nerve root, exiting at the C7-T1 intervertebral foramen", "C": "The C5 nerve root, exiting at the C4-C5 intervertebral foramen", "D": "The C6 nerve root, exiting at the C5-C6 intervertebral foramen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The C7 root, exiting at the C6-C7 intervertebral foramen): The C7 root supplies the triceps, wrist flexors, the middle finger and the triceps reflex, and exits at the C6-C7 foramen. C6 would affect the biceps reflex and thumb, C8 the finger flexors and little finger, and C5 the deltoid. The normal biceps reflex and thumb sensation exclude C6."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "electrotherapy", "question": "A 29-year-old runner has persistent hand and forearm swelling three weeks after a distal radius fracture, with the cast removed and no signs of infection or clot. The chiropractor wants interferential current to assist fluid return through muscle contraction, with the arm elevated. Which parameter set best suits this goal?", "options": {"A": "A beat of 100 Hz at sensory intensity that produces tingling but no contraction", "B": "A beat of 5000 Hz delivered just below the sensory threshold to avoid contraction", "C": "A beat of 100 Hz at a strong intensity that holds the muscles in tetany", "D": "A beat of 1 to 10 Hz with intensity giving visible rhythmic contractions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A beat of 1 to 10 Hz with intensity giving visible rhythmic contractions): A muscle pump needs rhythmic, intermittent contractions, which a low beat of about 1 to 10 Hz at motor intensity provides. Elevation helps gravity assist the fluid return. A sensory-level 100 Hz beat gives analgesia without contraction. Tetany holds the muscle contracted and does not alternate squeeze and relaxation, and a 5000 Hz beat is not a realistic setting."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A chiropractor sets a probe to a continuous output of 250 mW and delivers it to one point on a patient's lateral epicondyle for 40 seconds. A student asks for the energy delivered to that point, since the treatment record lists energy in joules. What energy value should be recorded for this point?", "options": {"A": "10 J, found by multiplying 0.25 W by 40 s", "B": "10,000 J, found by multiplying 250 W by 40 s", "C": "1 J, found by multiplying 0.025 W by 40 s", "D": "100 J, found by multiplying 2.5 W by 40 s"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (10 J, found by multiplying 0.25 W by 40 s): Energy in joules equals power in watts multiplied by time in seconds. A 250 mW output is 0.25 W, and 0.25 W times 40 s is 10 J. The other values come from misplacing the decimal point or skipping the milliwatt to watt conversion."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 44-year-old recreational tennis player with chronic midportion Achilles tendinopathy asks why the therapist placed a half inch heel lift in both shoes during the early phase of treatment. What is the main mechanical reason the lift can reduce symptoms?", "options": {"A": "It shifts the center of pressure forward so the metatarsal heads absorb more load", "B": "It increases dorsiflexion at heel strike so the tibialis anterior shares the work", "C": "It lengthens the calf musculotendon unit so the tendon is stretched with each step", "D": "It holds the gastrocnemius and soleus slightly shortened, lowering tensile strain on the tendon"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It holds the gastrocnemius and soleus slightly shortened, lowering tensile strain on the tendon): Raising the heel places the ankle in slight plantar flexion, which holds the gastrocnemius and soleus slightly shortened and lowers tensile strain on the Achilles tendon. It does not lengthen the calf unit. A heel lift may shift some pressure forward, but that is not the mechanism of symptom relief. Dorsiflexion demand at heel strike is reduced, not increased, so the tibialis anterior is not asked to share the work."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "During a cardiac rehabilitation lecture, a chiropractic student reviews how exercise intensity is expressed in metabolic equivalents for adults in an outpatient clinic. The instructor asks the group to state the resting oxygen consumption value that defines one MET when the measurement is normalized to body mass. What value defines 1 MET?", "options": {"A": "35 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute", "B": "1.0 liter of oxygen per minute for an average adult", "C": "0.35 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute", "D": "3.5 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (3.5 milliliters of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute): One MET is defined as the resting oxygen uptake of about 3.5 mL per kg per minute, so any activity can be expressed as a multiple of rest. The value of 35 is ten times too large and resembles a typical VO2 max of a fit adult rather than rest. The value of 0.35 is ten times too small. An absolute liter per minute figure cannot be applied across people of different size, which is why the definition is normalized to body weight."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "thermotherapy", "question": "A 61 year old woman finished radiation for breast cancer four months ago and now has thoracic paraspinal muscle tightness within the old radiation field. The skin is dry and slightly darkened. She asks for a hot pack. Which decision is best?", "options": {"A": "Avoid heat over the irradiated skin and choose a gentler, nonthermal approach", "B": "Use a hot pack over the area, since the course of cancer treatment is now complete", "C": "Use moist heat with eight towel layers for 20 minutes over the thoracic paraspinals", "D": "Use moist heat for just five minutes with frequent checks of the skin and comfort"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Avoid heat over the irradiated skin and choose a gentler, nonthermal approach): Irradiated skin and underlying tissue tolerate heat poorly, and heat over a field treated for malignancy is avoided. A nonthermal approach treats the tightness without that risk. Insulation, short time and completion of treatment do not restore normal heat tolerance."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A home phototherapy lamp delivers 8 mW/cm2 to the skin at a distance of 20 inches. The patient's psoriasis plaque is on the back of the thigh, and the patient moves the lamp to 40 inches to cover more area. Assuming the beam strikes the skin perpendicularly, what intensity now reaches the skin?", "options": {"A": "An intensity of 16 milliwatts per square centimeter at the skin surface", "B": "An intensity of 4 milliwatts per square centimeter at the skin surface", "C": "An intensity of 1 milliwatt per square centimeter at the skin", "D": "An intensity of 2 milliwatts per square centimeter at the skin"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An intensity of 2 milliwatts per square centimeter at the skin): Doubling the distance cuts the intensity to one quarter because intensity follows the inverse square of distance. Eight divided by 4 equals 2 mW/cm2. A simple inverse relationship would give 4, and an inverse cube would give 1. Intensity cannot rise when the source moves away, so 16 is wrong."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 63-year-old retired mail carrier has medial compartment knee osteoarthritis with a varus alignment and pain on walking. She wants a brace that reduces the load across the painful part of the joint. Which device best fits her presentation and describes its mechanism?", "options": {"A": "An unloader brace applying a varus-directed force that shifts load toward the medial compartment", "B": "A patellar stabilizing brace with a lateral buttress that guides the patella in the groove", "C": "An unloader brace applying a valgus force that shifts load toward the lateral compartment", "D": "A rigid functional brace that limits anterior tibial translation during pivoting activities"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An unloader brace applying a valgus force that shifts load toward the lateral compartment): For medial compartment osteoarthritis with varus alignment, an unloader brace applies a three-point valgus force that moves load toward the lateral compartment. A varus-directed force would increase medial loading. A patellar stabilizing brace treats patellofemoral tracking, and a functional brace limiting tibial translation is for ligament deficiency, so neither addresses compartment overload."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 16-year-old swimmer with an increased thoracic curve lies prone and extends over a pillow, and the curve flattens fully. Radiographs taken at the clinic show normally shaped vertebral bodies with no wedging and no endplate irregularity. Which diagnosis and plan fit best?", "options": {"A": "Structural scoliosis, treated with Schroth rotational breathing exercises and bracing", "B": "Postural kyphosis, treated with postural strengthening and thoracic mobility exercise", "C": "Congenital kyphosis, treated with an urgent surgical referral for vertebral malformation", "D": "Scheuermann kyphosis, treated with bracing and an evaluation for spinal fusion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Postural kyphosis, treated with postural strengthening and thoracic mobility exercise): A curve that corrects with extension and normal vertebral shapes indicate postural kyphosis, which responds to strengthening of the scapular and extensor muscles and thoracic mobility work. Scheuermann disease is stiff and shows wedging, and congenital kyphosis shows a malformed vertebra. Scoliosis is a lateral curve and is not described here."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A chiropractor with dry needling privileges plans to treat an upper trapezius trigger point in a thin 29-year-old patient. The point lies in the part of the muscle that sits directly over the lung apex region. Which technique choice best limits the risk of pneumothorax?", "options": {"A": "Insert perpendicular to the skin to reach the deepest part of the taut band", "B": "Insert perpendicular with a short needle while the patient holds a deep breath", "C": "Pass a fine needle quickly through the full muscle thickness to reach the deepest fibers", "D": "Pinch the muscle and angle the needle tangentially away from the chest"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pinch the muscle and angle the needle tangentially away from the chest): Pinching the muscle and angling the needle tangentially away from the chest keeps the tip in the muscle and away from the pleura. A perpendicular approach over the apex risks puncture. A deep breath lifts the apex closer to the needle. Passing through the full muscle thickness brings the tip toward the pleura even with a fine needle."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "A 41-year-old man is three weeks after open reduction of a tibial plateau fracture, with the surgeon restricting him to touchdown weight bearing for eight weeks. He wants to begin light leg press to keep his legs strong. Which exercise choice is acceptable within the order?", "options": {"A": "Single leg squats on the operative limb to a comfortable depth with hands supported", "B": "Treadmill walking with full weight bearing in a hinged knee brace set for protection", "C": "Leg press at twenty five percent of body weight on the operative limb with light resistance", "D": "Quadriceps sets, straight leg raises, and seated knee motion in the allowed range without axial loading"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Quadriceps sets, straight leg raises, and seated knee motion in the allowed range without axial loading): Touchdown weight bearing prohibits meaningful axial load through the healing plateau, so exercises that do not load the joint, such as quad sets, straight leg raises, and range of motion, are used to preserve strength. Leg press, single leg squats, and treadmill walking all load the fracture beyond the order. A brace does not change the restriction."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "phototherapy", "question": "A multi-doctor practice buys a 10 W class 4 laser. The compliance consultant says goggles alone will not satisfy laser safety practice for this class. Staff must decide what additional administrative control is expected for a class 4 treatment room. Which additional control is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Set up a controlled area with signs and restricted entry", "B": "Post a notice on the device and allow staff to enter freely during emission", "C": "Switch the device to pulsed mode, which converts it to a lower class", "D": "Have patients sign a consent form and rely on that form for staff protection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Set up a controlled area with signs and restricted entry): Class 4 devices call for a controlled area with signs and limits on who may enter while the beam is on, in addition to eyewear. A label alone does not stop people from walking in. Consent forms do not protect staff, and pulsing changes the delivery pattern without changing the class of the device."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A 52-year-old with chronic supraspinatus tendinopathy has a painful arc and a positive empty can test. The therapist plans deep transverse friction to the tendon. Which arm position best exposes the tendon from under the acromion so that it can be palpated?", "options": {"A": "Arm resting at the side with the shoulder in neutral rotation", "B": "Hand behind the back, with the shoulder in extension and internal rotation", "C": "Hand on the opposite shoulder, with the arm in horizontal adduction", "D": "Arm abducted to 160 degrees with the elbow straight overhead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hand behind the back, with the shoulder in extension and internal rotation): Placing the hand behind the back extends and internally rotates the shoulder, moving the supraspinatus tendon forward from under the acromion where the therapist can reach it. At the side, the tendon is covered, and overhead abduction jams it under the arch. Horizontal adduction exposes the posterior cuff region instead."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "mechanotherapy", "question": "A therapist treats an active trigger point in the upper trapezius of a 39-year-old office worker with ischemic compression. The patient is anxious about discomfort and asks how hard the therapist will press and when the pressure will stop. Which description of the technique and its endpoint is correct?", "options": {"A": "Rapid oscillating pressure for 5 seconds, repeated until the muscle fatigues", "B": "Deep stripping strokes along the muscle fibers, repeated ten times at a steady speed", "C": "Maximal pressure that produces sharp pain, released as soon as the pain peaks", "D": "Sustained pressure at tolerable discomfort, held until the tenderness eases"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sustained pressure at tolerable discomfort, held until the tenderness eases): Ischemic compression applies sustained pressure at tolerable discomfort and holds it until the tenderness eases. Rapid oscillation and deep stripping are different techniques that do not hold a point. Maximal pressure to sharp pain is not tolerable and is likely to increase guarding in an anxious patient."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "exercise_physiology", "question": "After a stroke, a 64-year-old woman shows greater resistance to passive elbow extension when the therapist moves her arm quickly than when moving it slowly. At slow speeds the elbow moves through full range with little resistance and no fixed joint limitation. Which finding best explains this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Golgi tendon organ hypersensitivity, which causes sudden inhibition of the muscle at high speeds", "B": "Spasticity, a velocity dependent increase in muscle spindle mediated stretch reflex activity", "C": "Lead pipe rigidity, a velocity independent resistance present throughout the full passive range", "D": "Fixed contracture, a structural shortening of the muscle and joint capsule that limits passive range"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Spasticity, a velocity dependent increase in muscle spindle mediated stretch reflex activity): Resistance that rises with the speed of passive movement is the defining feature of spasticity, an exaggerated spindle driven stretch reflex after an upper motor neuron lesion. Rigidity is velocity independent, and a fixed contracture would limit range at slow speeds as well. Tendon organ hypersensitivity would produce relaxation, not rising resistance."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 24-year-old soccer player is seen 2 days after a grade II lateral ankle sprain. There is swelling, warmth, and pain with weight bearing. The goal is to begin strengthening while respecting the inflammatory phase of tissue healing. Which strengthening approach is most appropriate at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Double-leg balance board training with eyes closed to restore proprioception", "B": "Submaximal, pain-free isometric contractions of the ankle muscles at several joint angles", "C": "Eccentric calf raises on a step through full available range with progressive load", "D": "Resisted elastic band eversion and inversion in three sets to fatigue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Submaximal, pain-free isometric contractions of the ankle muscles at several joint angles): Two days after a grade II sprain the tissue is in the inflammatory phase, so loading must be gentle. Submaximal pain-free isometrics activate the muscles without stressing the ligament. Balance board work with eyes closed is a later-phase task. Heavy eccentric calf raises load the wrong structure too early, and resisted band work to fatigue provokes the inflamed ligaments."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "muscle_rehabilitation", "question": "A 37-year-old swimmer lies supine with arms at the sides. The right shoulder sits forward and the posterior acromion rests well off the table. The scapula appears tilted forward and downwardly rotated. Which muscle shortening is most likely, and which stretch corrects it?", "options": {"A": "Serratus anterior, stretched by protracting the scapula with the arm pushed forward", "B": "Lower trapezius, stretched by elevating and upwardly rotating the scapula overhead", "C": "Rhomboids, stretched by retracting the scapula and squeezing the shoulder blades together", "D": "Pectoralis minor, stretched by retracting and posteriorly tilting the scapula while the arm is supported"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pectoralis minor, stretched by retracting and posteriorly tilting the scapula while the arm is supported): A shortened pectoralis minor pulls the coracoid process forward and down, tilting the scapula anteriorly and downwardly rotating it, so the shoulder sits forward off the table. It is stretched by retraction and posterior tilt of the scapula. The serratus anterior and lower trapezius do not produce this pattern, and retraction shortens the rhomboids."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "endurance_training", "question": "A 38-year-old office worker asks how much aerobic activity current guidelines recommend for general health. The clinician is quoting ACSM and federal guidance for healthy adults who perform moderate-intensity activity rather than vigorous activity. The worker wants one weekly target, expressed in minutes, that adults should reach or exceed. What is the minimum weekly amount?", "options": {"A": "At least 90 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity", "B": "At least 300 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity", "C": "At least 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity", "D": "At least 75 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (At least 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity): ACSM and federal guidelines set the minimum at 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity activity. The 75 minute figure is the minimum for vigorous activity, which counts double. The 300 minute figure is the higher target associated with additional health benefit, not the minimum. A 90 minute total is not a guideline value."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "disorder_specific_rehabilitation", "question": "While testing a 37-year-old nurse with sciatica, a clinician performs 10 repetitions of standing lumbar extension. After the set, the pain that was in the buttock now reaches the lateral calf, and it stays there at rest. Which action should the clinician take?", "options": {"A": "Continue standing extension for 10 more repetitions because pain after exertion is expected", "B": "Advance to prone press-ups since a stronger extension force centralizes pain", "C": "Add manual overpressure to the extension to force the pain back centrally", "D": "Stop standing extension and test another direction, such as flexion or a side-glide"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop standing extension and test another direction, such as flexion or a side-glide): Distal spread of symptoms that remains afterward is a sign the direction is unfavorable. The clinician should stop it and test flexion or a lateral component. Continuing, adding overpressure, or advancing the same direction risks aggravating nerve root irritation."}];
